    "id": "cdb95c8a-5ce7-45ca-aec1-29572e867b9e",
    "title": "Washington State Statute and Guidance related to ELs (Part 2/2)",
    "content": "ademic standards; and\n\n(2) guide school districts in providing the most effective and efficient practices when implementing supplemental instruction and services to assist students who are not meeting academic standards.\n\nThe LAP Guide indicates that activities for implementation of LAP requirements may include training in a number of areas, including, “training and coaching to staff and partners so supports are delivered in a coordinated and integrated manner”; “how to collect, enter, and analyze data consistently”; “training and support to create a positive learning environment and increase fidelity of implementation”;\n\nLAP FAQ\n\nQuestion A.4 indicates that it is allowable to use LAP funds for professional learning. “To plan, deliver, and monitor supplemental instruction and services effectively and efficiently, schools may use LAP funds to purchase related intervention curricula and materials, diagnostic and progress monitoring assessments, professional learning, and family engagement in participating students’ native language and/or language of instruction.”\n\nQuestion B.2, includes examples activities that are aligned with the intent and purpose of LAP and the Washington Integrated Student Supports Protocol (RCW 28A.300.139) which include: “Consultants and coaches: Hiring staff and out-of-school organizations with necessary training to provide professional learning (training and coaching) on the effective planning, implementation, and monitoring of supplemental instruction and services through the Washington Integrated Student Supports Protocol.” And, “Professional learning: Funding ongoing, job-embedded professional learning (training and coaching) on planning, implementing, and monitoring supplemental instruction and services through the Washington Integrated Student Supports Protocol.”\n\nBeginning Educator Support Team (BEST) Grants\n\nOSPI’s BEST webpage\n\nLEAs and ESDs (Educational Service District) must apply annually to receive these funds. This may be a source of funding that could be used for CARE Coaching, but LEAs and ESDs would need to include CARE Coaching as part of the activities they intend to implement in their grant applications. Marketing content should be distributed during the application period for these grants. The next grant cycle opens in Spring 2026.\n\nTeacher license/certification renewal requirements\n\nTo maintain their certification, educators are required by law to complete continuing education credits throughout their career. These credits are offered through “clock hours,” which are units of credit assigned to educators at a professional development or training event. Clock hour rules are available in Chapter 181-85 WAC\n\nDocumentation required is discussed at WAC 181-85-107 and says, “(3) Any official correspondence from an approved in-service education agency that verifies completion of one or more clock hours continuing education credit hours.” The requirement is for the educator, when filing a report to document clock hours, to retain documentation (records) to support the clock hours earned. For WCEPS, this could be registration for CARE Coaching accompanied by a certificate of completion that includes the number of clock hours earned.\n\nWashington requires that the Professional Educator Standards Board (PESB) approve providers of continuing education for educators. Approval of agencies as providers of clock hours occurs annually. NOTE: In addition to the PESB list of approved providers, all Washington public school districts and compact schools are approved providers of teacher recertification clock hours. ESDs (Education Service Districts) are also approved providers as noted in this link, along with other automatically approved agencies.\n\nClock hours are only accepted from approved providers. See the OSPI approved providers webpage. WCEPS should apply to become an approved provider during the next open cycle for these applications. WIDA might consider doing the same. See the OSPI Become an Approved Provider webpage.",
