    "id": "0a3e3e17-7985-4dea-ab08-ade3faa8f292",
    "title": "Washington State Statute and Guidance related to ELs (Part 1/2)",
    "content": "Washington State Statute and Guidance related to ELs\n\nTransitional Bilingual Instruction Program\n\nWA statute 28A.180 RCW\n\nRCW 28A.180.030(4) is the legal definition for “Transitional bilingual instruction” in WA State as included in the definitions section of this WA statute. It is important to note this definition says under point (b), “(b) In those cases in which instruction in two languages is not practicable as established by the superintendent of public instruction and unless otherwise prohibited by law, an alternative system of instruction which may include English as a second language and is designed to enable the pupil to achieve competency in English.”  RCW 28A.180.030(4)(b)\n\nSince TBI can include regular ESL in certain circumstances, this means that these funds may be used to support EL program teachers / educators in schools where it isn’t feasible to establish a TBI program, but where typical ESL type programs are used. So, if an LEA receives these funds and it isn’t practicable to start a TBI program, the funds can be used to support other EL programs.\n\nRCW 28A.180.040 – List school board duties related to TBI programs funded with TBIP funds.\n\nPD requirement – Every School District board of directors shall: RCW 28A.180.040(1)(f) “Provide in-service training for teachers, counselors, and other staff, who are involved in the district's transitional bilingual program. Such training shall include appropriate instructional strategies for children of culturally different backgrounds, use of curriculum materials, and program models”\n\nRCW 28A.180.040(2) requires that all classroom teachers assigned using TBIP funds for TBI programs must hold an endorsement in bilingual education, or ELL, or both. NOTE: since this is a requirement to use TBIP funds for teachers, the teachers should have the endorsement before being assigned using TBIP funds – SO, they would need to find other funding to earn the endorsement, like general funds or personal funds. A district could potentially use their general funds for PD to help teachers attain this endorsement.\n\nRCW 28A.180.0490(4) – OSPI can provide LEAs with technical assistance and support in selecting PD for program staff. I think the best way for WCEPS to take advantage of this would be by becoming an approved PD provider (more later).\n\nTBIP Washington State Policies and Practices Guide – Provides guidance on required policies and practices for TBIP programs: There are eight chapters in this guide with the fourth chapter being “Staffing and Professional Learning”. This chapter indicates (page 68) that professional learning is required by state and federal law and includes a list of potential professional learning activities, which is copied and pasted below:\n\nProfessional Learning Activities\n\nProfessional learning activities may include:\n\n• Effective services for multilingual learners and AI/AN students\n\n• WIDA English Language Development standards and assessments\n\n• Second language acquisition\n\n• Instructional strategies for English language development and accessible content\n\n• Dual language research, program development, and instruction\n\n• Culturally and linguistically responsive instruction for multilingual learners\n\n• Core content or skills needed for ELL/Bilingual endorsed teachers to support content instruction for MLs (i.e., foundational skill instruction for ELD teachers working with PK–3 students, content for ELD teachers doing co-teaching)\n\n• Other topics related directly to services for multilingual learners\n\nProfessional learning activities, including conferences or workshops, should be designed as part of a comprehensive plan that will directly impact staff members’ ability to support improved educational outcomes for multilingual learners.\n\nTransitional Bilingual Instruction Program Guidance from OSPI.\n\nWashington Administrative Code (WAC) outlining requirements for Transitional Bilingual Programs – WAC 392-160\n\nWAC 392-160-010(3) – School district board of directors duties section – Indicates that school district board the directors must provide effective professional development to EL educators.\n\n“(3) Shall provide effective professional development training of sufficient duration and depth for administrators, teachers, counselors, and other staff on bilingual program models, and/or district's alternative instructional program, appropriate use of instructional strategies and assessment results, and curriculum and instructional materials for use with culturally and linguistically diverse students.”\n\nLearning Assistance Program (LAP) –\n\nLaw - 28A.165 RCW\n\nRegulations and Rules – WAC 392-162\n\nLAP OSPI webpage\n\nLAP Guide\n\nThe LAP Guide says LAP is designed to:\n\n(1) promote the use of data when developing programs to assist students who are not meeting academic standards; and\n\n(2) guide school districts in providing the most effective and efficient practices when implementing supplemental instruction and services to assist students who are not meeting",
