    "id": "73a83732-53a9-4866-9c62-bcbe4fbedc1f",
    "title": "Florida State Statute, Rules and Guidance related to ELs and school improvement grant information (Part 2/2)",
    "content": "administrators, and guidance counselors.\n\nPPT says, All instructional personnel who work with ELLs in a school, regardless of the grade level they teach should integrate all the strands, standards, and performance indicators into their daily curriculum.\n\nNOTE: Knowing how WCEPS training can address the strands, standards, and performance indicators could allow WCEPS to create customized training for FL educators. All EL PD WCEPS offers should be tied to a component of the FL Teacher Standards for ESOL Endorsement if possible.\n\nOther professional learning provided by the state:\n\nThe FL DOE website English Language Learners/Professional Learning page provides information only about professional learning available from WIDA. The FL DOE Student Achievement through Language Acquisition Bureau (SALA) also offers professional learning via an online series of webinars.\n\nLaws and Regulations\n\nFL Rules and Legislation ELL webpage\n\nRule 6A-6.0904(2)(g) requires that “Basic ESOL instruction shall be provided by appropriately qualified personnel”\n\nRule 6A-6.0907 includes the requirements outlined above for the different categories of educators completing 60 or 18 hours of ESOL training.\n\nOriginal 1990 FL Consent Decree – Outlines requirements for providing EL programs in FL by legal Civil Rights Court Decree. It establishes PD requirements for ESOL endorsement and requirements for content teachers.\n\nFL Consent Decree Modification (2003) (link on this page) – establishes ESOL training requirements for administrators and guidance counselors.\n\nThe original FL Consent Decree included requirements related to Personnel Certification and In-service (page 22). This is where the requirements for an ESOL endorsement must include 300 hours of training (Specifically, 15 semester hours, 1 semester hour = 20 hours of training, thus 15 X 20 = 300 hours of training)\n\nThe original Consent Decree also requires in-service training as documented starting on page 22 with specific requirements on page 24-28. The important thing to note is that the Consent Decree requires in-service training (PD) and the amount of training (number of hours) is also specified in the Consent Decree (and aligns with the info in the ESOL endorsement section of this document).",
