    "id": "15336f33-c4fc-4d29-9e41-763ab3a7278e",
    "title": "Florida State Statute, Rules and Guidance related to ELs and school improvement grant information (Part 1/2)",
    "content": "Florida State Statute, Rules and Guidance related to ELs and school improvement grant information\n\nSchool Improvement grants are called Comprehensive Support Grants (CSGs)\n\nThere are three categories to which schools can be identified for school support and improvement under Title I of ESSA. These are Comprehensive Support and Improvement (CSI); Targeted Support and Improvement (TSI), and Additional Targeted Support and Improvement (ATSI). ESSA requires that 7% of a state’s Title I funds be set aside for grants to schools identified in one of these categories. Florida calls grants to CSI, TSI, and ATSI schools its, Comprehensive Support Grant (CSG), and information about CSG grants is available in a FL DOE PowerPoint, slides 116 to 122. Funding available is discussed on Slide 120 and is $39,282,943, or about $79,000 per eligible school. One of the areas identified where these funds can be used is “Professional Learning Activities”. There is also funding for Underperforming Subgroups, which would include ELs. I think Raydel told Yvonne there was $14 million set aside for this.\n\nYvonne reported that the communication she had with Raydel related to the fact that many CSI school’s applications for the CSG funds did not include sufficient PD related to the needs of teachers working with ELs. Raydel recommended focusing efforts to contact CSI schools to suggest they amend their CSG grant applications to include CARE Coaching.\n\nApplications for these funds were initially due Nov. 14th. This is why the FL DOE said that districts would need to amend their application to include CARE Coaching if they wanted to use these funds to purchase CARE Coaching. Current marketing should target schools whose PD portion of their CSG application is in need of improvement.\n\nSchools identified for CSI, TSI, and ATSI is available (see FederalIndex25.xls spreadsheet, column BK has EL info). Communications should target not only the administration at each school, but Federal Programs, Title I Directors, and EL Coordinators at the district level.\n\nEducator Certification Renewal\n\nEducator Certification Renewal Requirements can be found on the FLDOE website. Six semester hours of college credit are required. One semester hour equals 20 in-service points.\n\nTeachers must apply for renewal by navigating the FL DOE website’s Educator Certification section. Educators must submit official transcripts or other certification documents to the Bureau of Educator Certification. NOTE: This is where it may be important for CARE Coaching to provide certificates.\n\nEducator Certification Laws and Rules\n\nESOL (English for Speakers of Other Languages) Endorsement\n\nFL Teacher Standards for ESOL Endorsement\n\nFL DOE PPT ESOL Endorsement Matrix\n\nStarting in October, 2024, Florida DOE went through a process to update the Florida Teacher Standards for ESOL Endorsement. The new standards were approved by the FL State Board of Education on August 20, 2025. Some important information regarding how CARE Coaching and other WCEPS PD could be impacted.\n\nThere are five ESOL Endorsement strands comprised of Standards and Performance Indicators\n\nThere are two entities that can offer this endorsement to teachers - State-approved teacher preparation programs; and School districts\n\nIf school districts want to create the training to offer an ESOL endorsement, they must submit their ESOL endorsement matrix to the FL DOE by June 1, 2026 for review and approval\n\nRequired hours EL teachers need to earn the ESOL endorsement or for meeting requirements for other educators is as follows:\n\nTo earn an ESOL Endorsement – 60 hours per ESOL endorsement strand (5 strands X 60 = 300 hours)\n\nContent-area teachers are required to complete 60 hours of training\n\nSchool-based administrators and Guidance Counselors are required to complete 60 hours\n\nAll other areas are required to complete 18 hours, which includes P.E., Music, Media Specialists, SLPs, Psychologists, Social Workers, World Languages, ROTC, Occupational and Physical Therapists\n\nWhat is important to note is that no matter which category an educator falls into, if they have to complete 300, 60, or 18 hours of training and that training must cover all five FL ESOL endorsement Teacher Strands.\n\nOPPORTUNITY FOR WCEPS: Since schools must basically create their own ESOL Endorsement training, now is the time to review the Strands and determine what WCEPS training could align with these Strands, Standards, and Performance Indicators, then market to LEAs the potential to contract with WCEPS for PD that could meet the requirements for training. If WCEPS could create a 60 hour course that covers all Strands, Standards, and Performance Indicators, it could offer this to LEAs to meet the training requirements for Content-area teachers, school-based administrators, and guidance counselors.\n\nPPT says, All instructional personnel who work with ELLs in a school, regardless of the grade level they teach should integrate all the strands, standards, a",
