    "title": "2025 Session slides - Selecting an effective Interim copy (Part 2/3)",
    "content": "es Interim Use:  Fall testing to gauge student progress between annual ACCESS administration Progress Monitoring :  Fall and spring testing to understand if students are making appropriate progress during the academic year Supplemental Use:   Administer as needed to make decisions related to a student’s education\n\nWIDA MODEL: A Closer Look Let's look at some features of MODEL together and connect them to the critical categories. For each MODEL feature, we will ask you to consider which of the seven categories it exemplifies. Could be more than one!\n\nFeature 1:  MODEL and the WIDA ELD Standards\n\nAlignment with WIDA ELD Standards\n\nGrade Cluster : 6-8 Standard:  Language for Science Target Proficiency Level:  3-4\n\nAssessment Inventory Activity\n\nGrade Cluster : 3-5 Standard:  Language for Language Arts Target Proficiency Level:  3\n\nWhich of the seven categories does this exemplify? Theoretical foundation Content validity Technical quality Score reports and score interpretations Practicality and inclusivity Relationship to performance on the summative test (ACCESS) Consequential validity\n\nFeature 2:  MODEL's Relationship with ACCESS\n\nMODEL and ACCESS Predictive study conducted in 2019: Study tracked scores from students who took both MODEL and ACCESS Overall composite MODEL scale scores predict overall composite ACCESS PLs A concordance table shows which MODEL scale scores predict which ACCESS PLs\n\nMODEL x ACCESS Score Relationships Relationships are strongest between the Overall Composite scores R=0.88 This indicates a very strong, positive relationship between MODEL and ACCESS Overall Composites\n\nLeah, Grade 3 MODEL score: 340 ACCESS PL: ?\n\nDario, Grade 7 MODEL score: 301 ACCESS PL: ?\n\nWhich of the seven categories does this exemplify? Theoretical foundation Content validity Technical quality Score reports and score interpretations Practicality and inclusivity Relationship to performance on the summative test (ACCESS) Consequential validity\n\nFeature 3: WIDA MODEL and Instructional Planning\n\nFocus Bulletin:  Using WIDA MODEL to Support Instructional Planning for Multilingual Learners\n\nAssessment Inventory REFLECT:  What assessments do you use in your school/district to monitor students’ content knowledge growth? What assessments do you use to monitor students’ language growth?  Assessments Content Language • Reading A-Z • MAP testing   • WIDA ACCESS\n\nUsing MODEL Scores to Inform Instruction Mario 4 th  Grade\n\nQuestion 1 How do I use the information in my students score reports? What information in the report is most important to me?\n\nwida.wisc.edu/teach/can-do/descriptors Scan to access the full set\n\nWIDA Can Do Descriptors Provide  rich  detail about students' language development Look up what students may be expected to do with their English language development, based on the following criteria: Student’s grade level  Language domain Key Uses of language\n\nMario’s Oral Language Development Mario 4 th  Grade\n\nMario's Oral Language Development PL 1.9 in Oral Language Mario 4 th  Grade\n\nQuestion 2 How could I plan a lesson to focus on using examples to support statements in a conversation? How might this lesson also be relevant to students at slightly lower and higher proficiency levels?\n\nWhich of the seven categories does this exemplify? Theoretical foundation Content validity Technical quality Score reports and score interpretations Practicality and inclusivity Relationship to performance on the summative test (ACCESS) Consequential validity\n\nIn Summary Interim assessments can provide valuable information about the language development of multilingual learners and their progress. If you decide to use an interim assessment, please consider carefully what you and your students need. WIDA provides guidance on how to choose an effective interim assessment that can benefit multilingual learners and their teachers. We believe that WIDA MODEL works well as an interim assessment, particularly so in states that administer ACCESS.\n\nInterim assessment adoption criteria\n\nAssessment Terminology: Summative, Interim &amp; Formative\n\nThank you!!\n\nLearn More about WIDA MODEL Visit our website wida.wisc.edu/assess/model Stop by the  WIDA Booth Ask to speak to someone about WIDA MODEL Request an overview demo presentation bit.ly/MODELOverviewDemo\n\nTerminology: Summative, Interim, &amp; Formative Typically administered one time per year Activate your assessment knowledge:  Based on your understanding of each assessment, sort and categorize the descriptors in your envelope.  Summative Interim Formative\n\nSmall-scale (duration is a few seconds, a few minutes, certainly less than a class period) Embedded within a lesson or learning activity Predictive of summative performance Designed to inform decisions at both the classroom and beyond the classroom level, such as the school or district level Terminology: Summative, Interim, &amp; Formative 1 -Summative 2 -Interim 3- Formative Usually used as part of an accountability program or to o",
