    "id": "b9d57b56-c7b5-491f-b9d6-38b9095f9b06",
    "title": "2025 Session slides - Selecting an effective Interim copy (Part 1/3)",
    "content": "Beyond the summative assessment:  Selecting an effective interim ELP assessment  Suzanne Johar, Mark Chapman, David MacGregor, Katie Schultz\n\nHow to Select an Interim Assessment Look for (1) evidence the assessment meets your needs \t    (2) evidence of technical quality Guidance from the Literature Guidance from WIDA\n\nFive Key Questions  ( Perie  et al., 2007) What do we want to learn from this assessment? What purpose will it serve? Who will use the information gathered from this assessment?  What action steps will be taken as a result of this assessment? What professional development or support structures need to be in place to ensure the action steps are taken and are successful? How will student learning improve as a result of using this interim assessment? Will the interim assessment improve student performance more than an alternative option or investment?\n\nSix criteria to evaluate quality  ( Li et al., 2010) Test purpose and use ​ Test development and documentation ​ Administration and inclusion ​ Test scores and reports ​ Test utility ​ Practicality and logistics  Is there evidence that the test is valid and reliable?  Are the test results useful?  Is the test easy to administer? Are results immediate and data manageable?\n\nWIDA’s seven critical categories Theoretical foundation Content validity Technical quality Score reports and score interpretations Practicality and inclusivity Relationship to performance on the summative test (ACCESS for ELLs) Consequential validity\n\nCritical Category 1: Theoretical Foundation Is the content on the test based on a sound theory of language development? Examples of evidence: Written explanation of the interim assessment’s theoretical foundation\n\nCritical Category 2: Content Validity Is the content on the test relevant to the content taught in the classroom? Examples of evidence Report describing content development and review steps, including how educators were involved\n\nCritical Category 3: Technical Quality Does the interim assessment have an acceptable level of technical quality? Examples of evidence Documentation about the reliability of the test Reports on inter-rater reliability This information is usually found in a technical report, which should be  publicly  available\n\nCritical Category 4: Score Reports &amp; Score Interpretations Do the score reports from the interim assessment clearly explain what the scores mean? Examples of evidence Examples of score reports available in public Support materials that help explain the scores and how they can be used\n\nCritical Category 5: Practicality and Inclusivity (I)  Practicality: Does the interim assessment offer flexible administration options (paper and online)? Is it simple to set up and administer the test?  Examples of evidence Test administration manuals that provide clear guidance Technology guidance documents\n\nCritical Category 5: Practicality and Inclusivity (II) Is the interim assessment fair and accessible to diverse student populations? Examples of evidence Documentation of bias and sensitivity (fairness) reviews Explanations of accessibility tools for all students and accommodations that are available to students with disabilities\n\nSession Overview Assessment inventory activity Assessment terminology: Summative, interim, and formative  The value of interim assessment data Criteria for selecting an effective interim assessment Working through an example: WIDA MODEL\n\nCritical Category 6: Relationship to Summative Assessment Are the interim assessment test scores predictive of summative test performance? Examples of evidence Reports of statistical analyses between scores on the two tests Simple guidance on how to understand these relationships between the interim and summative assessments\n\nCritical Category 7: Consequential Validity Can the interim assessment outcomes be helpful in making instructional planning decisions? Examples of evidence Testimonials Webinars or documents on connections between the test and instruction\n\nWIDA’s seven critical categories What is important to you when choosing a test for your students? Which ones would you prioritize? Theoretical foundation Content validity Technical quality Score reports and score interpretations Practicality and inclusivity Relationship to performance on the summative test (ACCESS for ELLs) Consequential validity\n\nExample: WIDA MODEL\n\nWIDA MODEL, Interim Assessment On-demand  English language proficiency assessments for grades K-12 May be administered at any time during school year Available in online and paper format for 1-12; K in paper format Test items developed based on the WIDA ELD Standards statements Reports same kinds of scores as ACCESS for ELLs (scale scores and PLs) Locally purchased, administered, and scored\n\nWIDA MODEL Implementation Use Cases Interim Use:  Fall testing to gauge student progress between annual ACCESS administration Progress Monitoring :  Fall and spring testing to understand if students are making appropriate progress du",
