    "content": "ates a canvas course to collaborate with w/LEA (data, feedback, information) Schedule initial training for staff based on the leadership action plan. Plan for coaching check-in with the leadership team Analyze data from baseline Learning Walks. Coach leadership team in providing job-embedded feedback. Support creating conditions for success Present the LEA topic to the selected audience. Set a compelling vision* Data & Planning Retreat* Facilitation of leadership retreats for district and school leaders to clarify goals and align them with instructional priorities. Coordinate training to build sustainability based on action plan* Continuous support through instructional coaching cycles scaffolding teachers’ ability to implement new practices. Virtual follow-ups with the leadership team to check in on the action plan Analyze data from learning walks to measure progress. Plan for opportunities to reflect on mindshifts that support a vision, communication and cultural shift. Credit courses will require office hours to follow up on assignments. Re-administer Needs Assessment Data retreat to conduct an in-depth data analysis comparing needs and measuring growth Recognition systems to celebrate progress include showcasing teacher innovations or district achievements in instructional shifts. Ensuring professional learning communities (PLCs) to build a sense of ownership and collaboration. Summarize communication about how instructional changes benefit students, with data to back claims. Next steps are planning and prioritizing Educators and students are empowered to take ownership of their roles in driving and sustaining instructional improvements. Collect qualitative data and testimonials. For teachers, survey results will show increased knowledge and skills in the targeted area. For administrators, survey results will show increased knowledge and skills in observing effective practices in the target area. Action Plans will guide areas of improvement For school districts, learning walk data will show an increased use of target skills in classrooms. For the school community, a fully executed action plan is planned. Share student data from educators that attended all 3 years with administrators, coaches, or grade level chair. Add-ons: Curriculum, State Standard, and Assessment Alignments (DOK, MLL) Add-ons: Needs Assessments Tool Add-ons: WCEPS Badges* Add-ons: Coaching, 1 to 3-year Plans * Based on the Knoster Model for Managing Complex Change *The Knoster Model for Managing Complex Change outlines essential components required for successful change, highlighting how their absence can lead to specific challenges. When applying this model to professional development for instructional shifts in schools, your organization can structure its offerings around three key levels: 1. Vision Level: Strategic Leadership- Purpose: Helps stakeholders understand the \"why\" behind the change, fostering buy-in and commitment across all school system levels. 2. Skills Level: Capacity Building- Purpose: Ensures educators and administrators have the knowledge and competencies to enact the instructional shifts. 3. Incentive Level: Motivation and Support - Purpose: Motivates by connecting the shifts to tangible rewards, whether professional (improved outcomes, recognition) or personal (impact on students). These levels align with the Knoster Model by addressing the critical need for vision, skills, and incentives, ensuring stakeholders are equipped, motivated, and aligned to achieve sustainable instructional change.",
