    "id": "897038b4-33f9-4454-b5d4-83f9237194e6",
    "title": "June 2025 DOK Session_Slides Used.pptx (Part 11/11)",
    "content": "points\nSQUARE: Something that really “squared” with your thinking\n\nAs you think about implementation, take into account that how we envision learning……so implementation will likely need to address \n\nConsider building from any existing uses.\nConsider using existing structures and systems\n\n\nDOK is an evaluative tool that helps us talk effectively and consistently about complexity of cognitive engagement.\nDifficulty and complexity are related but different.\nThere are several common misunderstandings and misrepresentations of DOK.\nSuccessful application of the language of DOK within a team is an ongoing process.\nDOK can inform standards interpretation, lesson and assessment development and evaluation practices.\nDOK informs alignment efforts; alignment is a necessary component of a standards based education system.\nTakeaways:\n\nPlease complete this evaluation form!\n\n\nDOK Framework\nBloom’s Taxonomy\nBased in cognitivism and contemporary understandings about the nature of learning\nBased in behaviorism, with revisions intended to reflect current understandings of learning.\nBased on a constructivist model of learning. Reflects a contemporary understanding of learning as a process of linking to networks of ideas. All categories of complexity are considered valuable as they combine to build proficiency.\nStructure implies that the higher the level of complexity, the more valuable the performance.\nAssignment of DOK takes into account actions as well as the structure of the content area, the content students engage with, students’ prior knowledge, and students’ existing conceptual ecology. \nFocused on using actions (verbs) to assign levels of complexity.\nDefinitions for each DOK category are tailored to different subjects, recognizing the inherent differences in learning processes, approaches, goals, etc. between and among disciplines.\nDoes not distinguish between subject areas. \nDifferentiates complexity from difficulty (and from other related factors)\nDoes not differentiate between difficulty and complexity.\nHas a rationale for the number of levels. (Necessary and sufficient) \nHas no rationale for the number of levels. \nDid not include this in Seattle but the topic came up\n\nAs the field of content alignment analysis was emerging in the education world, there was pushback against use of Blooms for purposes of content alignment analysis – it wasn’t looking to be a good fit for content alignment analysis, which was the need in the context of a standards-based education system. \n\nSome of the issues with Blooms – at the point of the development of DOK – were general, not specific to content alignment analysis – for example, people were concerned because we had moved on from a behaviorist perspective on learning, but Blooms was grounded in Behaviorism – and some of Bloom’s grad students were also concerned about that, Krathwol and Anderson –and they revised Blooms to try to address that, by adding the cognitive process dimension – but it was a retrofit. But there were other issues, too, that folks wanted to see addressed with a system that would be developed specifically for purposes of content alignment evaluation. \n\nRead An Inside Look at Webb’s Depth of Knowledge\nWhat resonates with you and connects with your experiences? \nWhat does the article bring up that you have questions about or that you’d like to explore more? \n\n  \nMicro-Book Club\nDid not include this in Seattle\n\n\nRead article. What does it surface for you that connects with what we have already talked about and what does it bring up that you still want to talk about? \n\n113\n\nWORD\nPHRASE\nSENTENCE\nRead through the DOK definitions for at least two subject areas.\n\nFind a word, phrase and sentence that resonates with you for each of DOK 1, 2, 3, and 4. \n\nNo need to rewrite, can use “+1”\nDOK 1\nDid not include this in Seattle\n\nThis is a chart paper activity that I have used sometimes.",
