    "title": "June 2025 DOK Session_Slides Used.pptx (Part 10/11)",
    "content": "late their own annual food waste? \nPresent your findings in the format of your choosing (5-min max).\nModified from: https://m3challenge.siam.org/practice-problems/2018-challenge-problem-better-ate-never-reducing-wasted-food\nDOK 4\nhttps://www.tcfoodjustice.org/food-waste-insecurity-justice\nAnother example specific to an AZ context: there’s some efforts in the Phoenix area to reduce food waste, and to get some of this potential food waste to people who could use it. This example is more relevant to the older elementary students, but the idea applies across grades. \n\nSo if this is a context that I know is relevant and meaningful to my students, I might share this infographic with my with students, about TC Food Justice, a local organization -– to introduce my students to this topic. This graphic makes a claim that we could use the food waste to feed people and that there are environmental cosequences too – and more\n\nSO I could structure a task to have my students figure out how many people could be fed using rescued food waste in Arizona. And then I could have them make a mathematical model that households could use to calculate their own annual food waste. And a task like this could wrap in many math standards, literacy standards….\n\nSo this is just an example of  a way that I could start with DOK 1 and 2 math standards and then build those into a DOK 4 task because we know that providing high complexity learning opportunities that are appropriate and relevant help to engage and motivate learning. \n\n99\n\nWe conduct a content analysis, using the DOK lens…\n…to make an inference…\n…about the cognitive complexity required for successful task completion.\nDOK helps us operationalize rigorous standards. \n\nPurposes of Today’s Meeting\nContextualize DOK within an aligned (coherent) education system\nWork toward a common understanding of cognitive complexity\nDifferentiate difficulty from complexity (and from other factors)\nReview common misinterpretations of DOK\nPractice using DOK to promote calibration\nIdentify specific goals for implementation or improvement in use of DOK in your practice / context.\nStrategize approaches for meeting these goals\nA lot of the specific applications relate to these ideas of alignment or coherence. \n\n\nDepth of Knowledge\u000b\nis a powerful language system for talking about cognitive complexity.\nDOK helps us differentiate between and among different levels or types of complexity of cognitive engagement.\n102\n\nSteps to Successful Implementation of DOK:\nDevelop a calibrated understanding of complexity and the DOK language system within the team. \nPractice to ensure that interpretation of the DOK of learning expectations, tasks, and questions is reasonably consistent.\nTHEN, apply to promote alignment; achieve intended goals and outcomes. \n\nHow we envision learning as relates to difficulty and complexity influences what types of learning opportunities we provide to students, how we measure learning, and how we interpret and use the results of assessments. \nWho cares? \nThe sentence at top is the big “who cares” (in my opinion). \n\nIn response to low interim scores, instructional modifications tend to be remedial. \n(Goertz, et al., 2009)\n\n\n“The fact that background knowledge is important for higher level problem solving does not mean that “basic skills” must be taught by rote” before more complex engagement. “In fact, posing challenges to learners … created more contextualized understanding and ultimately led to better recall and use of information presented later than did approaches that simply taught novices the relevant facts or formulas.” \u000b\u000b\t\t\t\t(Darling-Hammond, et al, 2020)\u000b\nExample of a quote that can be used as a sort of “mini-book club” \n\nLinda Darling-Hammond, Lisa Flook, Channa Cook-Harvey, Brigid Barron & David Osher (2020) Implications for educational practice of the science of learning and development, Applied Developmental Science, 24:2, 97-140, DOI:\n10.1080/10888691.2018.1537791\n\n\n[T]he term “higher order” skills is probably itself fundamentally misleading, for it suggests that another set of skills, presumably called “lower order,” needs to come first. This assumption . . . justifies long years of drill on the “basics” before thinking and problem solving are demanded.\nNRC, 1987\nExample of similar idea from nearly 40 years ago. \nSort of discouraging, honestly, that we are still struggling with this same issue but…\n\nPlanning Time: Specific goals & strategies for implementation and/or improvement? \nHow we envision learning as relates to difficulty and complexity influences what types of learning opportunities we provide to students, how we measure learning, and how we interpret and use the results of assessments. \nCIRCLE: Anything that’s still circling in their heads\nTRIANGLE: Three most important points\nSQUARE: Something that really “squared” with your thinking\n\nAs you think about implementation, take into account that how we envision learning……so implementation will likely need to address \n\nC",
