    "id": "a88b6ea2-5e81-4aa2-8bc4-380ca8651986",
    "title": "June 2025 DOK Session_Slides Used.pptx (Part 9/11)",
    "content": "e reader.  \nDOK 3\nDOK 1\nDOK 1\nDOK 3\nDOK 3\nDevelop an argument for why you think the author chose to repeat these words.  \nThe DOK lens adds clarity to the work of promoting coherence / alignment. \n\nI’m not sure I actually went through this in Seattle? \n\n\n\n\n91\n\nRL.CS.2.1.3 Analyze the rhetorical role or roles of a literary device in a text. \nDOK 3\nDOK -\nDOK 1\nDOK -\nDOK 3\nRepetition is a literary device. What do you think repetition means? \n\nHighlight all the words you see repeated in the story. \n\nWhy do you think the author chose to repeat these words?\n\nHow does the repeating of words affect the message of the story? Give examples to support your answer.\nDefine repetition as a literary device.\nFind examples of repetition in the story Jonny Chuck….\nDescribe the effect of the repetition of words on the reader. \nDevelop an argument for why you think the author chose to repeat these words.  \n \nMight use this set of slides in MKE b/c they are working in revising unit plans. \n\n\n92\n\nRL.CS.2.1.3 Analyze the rhetorical role or roles of a literary device in a text. \nDOK 3\nDOK 3\nDOK 1\nDOK 3\nComplete this sentence starter: \nThe repeated words makes the reader feel…_____________ _____________________________________________________________________\nbecause_____________________________________________________________\n____________________________________________________________________\n\nWork with your partner to develop an argument for why you think the author chose to repeat the words….\n\nWhat is the literary device called repetition?\nDefine repetition as a literary device.\nFind examples of repetition in the story Jonny Chuck….\nDescribe the effect of the repetition of words on the reader. \nDevelop an argument for why you think the author chose to repeat these words.  \n \n93\n\nRL.CS.2.1.3 Analyze the rhetorical role or roles of a literary device in a text. (**grade level appropriate terminology) \t\nDefine repetition as a literary device.\nFind examples of repetition in the story Jonny Chuck….\nExplain the meaning of passages that use repetition as a literary device.\nDOK 3\nDOK 1\nDOK 1\nDOK 2\n\n\n94\n\nRL.CS.2.1.3 Analyze the rhetorical role or roles of a literary device in a text. \nDOK 3\nDOK -\nDOK 1\nDOK 2\nRepetition is a literary device. What do you think repetition means? \n\nHighlight all the words you see repeated in the sentences below. \nThey didn’t hurry now for they were so very, very tired! And they were cross—oh so cross!\n\nRead the sentences above. Explain what is happening in the story.\n\nWhat is the literary device called repetition?\n\nDefine repetition as a literary device.\nFind examples of repetition in the story Jonny Chuck….\nExplain the meaning of passages that use repetition as a literary device. \nDOK 1\n\n\n\n95\n\nMany teaching strategies help promote more complex engagement by design.\nGrouping or bundling expectations can allow for lower complexity tasks to be embedded within more complex and overarching tasks. E.g., through\nProject-based learning\nProblem-based learning\nPhenomenon-based learning\n5E model\nAuthentic Intellectual Work\nTheme-based units\nAnd others!\n\n\n\n96\n\nUse algebra and geometry to calculate the relative probability of making a successful bank shot from different positions on the court, keeping the distance to the hoop constant. \nDOK 4\nTask 3: \nThen test your predictions with a scale model. \nPresent your findings in the format of your choosing (5-min max).\n\nDon’t think I used this example in Seattle. \n\nI could wrap this work with trigonometric ratios and the Pythagorean theorem into a larger project, that has students use a broader scope of math concepts and skills to figure out the probability of making a bank shot from different positions on the court. And I could have them test their predictions, their findings with a scale model and synthesize and organize their findings into a presentation.\n97\n\nhttps://theconversation.com/the-math-behind-the-perfect-free-throw-91727\nSo I might start by having students read this article about the math behind the perfect free throw. Get them thinking about how algebra and geometry and other math can be used in the context of basketball shots! And share some actual research about the topic. \n\nThen I might group students and provide some additional structure to get them started into their work. Over the course of days or maybe weeks, depending, I would make sure that groups have the resources needed to develop scale models and test predictions, and check in with groups on their plans for presentations, I’d be a facilitator for their work. \n98\n\nMath Standards\nLiteracy Standards\nScience Standards\nEconomics Standards\nCitizenship Standards\nGeography Standards\nCould food waste in the Twin Cities feed people in need of food? \nDevelop a mathematical model that a household could use to calculate their own annual food waste? \nPresent your findings in the format of your choosing (5-min max).\nModified from: https://m3challenge.siam.org/practice-problems/2018-challenge-problem-better-ate-nev",
