    "id": "f94dd486-c759-4b23-adf6-cef3e7b95609",
    "title": "June 2025 DOK Session_Slides Used.pptx (Part 8/11)",
    "content": "oks like. To develop appropriately complex educational materials, we really have to know what the standards look like. \n\n(Circles and colors are not intended to represent anything in particular)\n82\n\nStandards\nEducational Materials\n\nAlignment is not a property of a curriculum or of an assessment – it is a relationship between two or more components of a system. \n\nThis slide shows a relationship between standards and “educational materials” – which could be a assessment, a quiz, an activity, a curriculum – anything. For now, just thinking broadly. \n\n\nStandards\nEducational Materials\nEven though the educational materials were “good” in the last example, here they are NOT good. \n\n…which is intended to show that alignment is NOT a judgement related to being “good” or “bad” but rather it is a description of the relationship between different components. – are our materials doing what we want them to do? \n\n\n\nStandards\nEducational Materials\nAlignment – -- can be strengthened by a change to the standards, and change to the materials, or both.\n\n\n\nStandards explicate not just what students are expected to know but also specify expectations for the complexity of cognitive engagement with disciplinary concepts and ideas.\nOur academic standards specify not just a topic but also the complexity of engagement with academic topics. So if we are working toward a coherent or aligned system, our first task is to make sure we are interpreting the standards in the same way – that our work is anchored in the same understanding of the expectations within the standards (could also be be a set of competencies, they may be a set of performance expectations or entrance requirements or other sets of learning targets or outcomes. But in all the different education contexts, we are typically guided by a carefully considered plan that identifies what we expect students to know and be able to do. \n\nFor alignment or coherence, the complexity of cognitive engagement within those standards has to play out in the classroom – students must be provided opportunities to engage at the intended level(s) of complexity. And if we want to learn about how students are doing in terms of meeting these learning expectations, then we need to use assessments that allow students to demonstrate those same complexities of cognitive engagement. \n\nWe often work with groups to 1) interpret and identify the complexity of a set of learning expectations and then 2) self-evaluate our curriculum and assessment materials to ensure that these parts are all working together as we expect.\n\n\n\nDOK 3\nDOK 3\nDOK 3\nFor example, if a DOK 3 standard, then we want to see similar DOK 3 -- opportunity to engage in the classroom and to demonstrate KSAs.\n\n…But it’s not really like this – it’s not like we just do exactly the same thing in all parts of the system. This is not realistic, nor is it what is intended / expected. \n\n\n\n\nDOK 3\nDOK 3\nDOK 3\nDOK 2\nDOK 2\nDOK 2\nDOK 2\nDOK 2\nDOK 3\nDOK 3\nDOK 3\nDOK 1\nDOK 1\nDOK 1\n(It’s not necessarily a direct one-to-one relationship.)\nDOK helps us to work with greater intentionality.\nMore realistically, there’s a lot of professional judgements and decisions that go into all of this. If we have a DOK 3 standard – for example -- , it doesn’t mean that everything we do has to be DOK 3 – but we want to make sure that the DOK 3 aspects of the standards carries through to our learning objectives, our learning opportunities presented in the classroom, and our assessments. \n\nKey is that we are actually doing what we are INTENDING to be doing. Overall,  I might have good reasons to include tasks as different levels of DOK but I must be sure that I’m offering adequate opportunity for students practice and demonstrate DOK 3 engagement, consistent with the expectations of the standard. \n\nYes, all levels of DOK are important. But usually we need to put the time and effort in to make sure that we are getting at those higher DOK levels. Planning learning opportunities and prompts and activities and questions that get at higher DOK levels takes work.\n\n\nALIGNMENT\nWe might build something more elaborate out of the standards – and for good reasons – but we still see the standards carrying through. Same with assessment – it might look different (and we probably cannot thoroughly assess absolutely everything) but we should still be able to see the standards carrying through. \n\n89\n\nSelf-Reflection Stoplight\nThis extended or affirmed my thinking.\nI need to work on this a little more. \nHold up. I need a better map. \n\nRL.CS.2.1.3 Analyze the rhetorical role or roles of a literary device in a text. (**grade level appropriate terminology) \t\nDefine repetition as a literary device.\nFind examples of repetition in the story Jonny Chuck….\nDescribe the effect of the repetition of words on the reader.  \nDOK 3\nDOK 1\nDOK 1\nDOK 3\nDOK 3\nDevelop an argument for why you think the author chose to repeat these words.  \nThe DOK lens adds clarity to the work of promoting coherence / alignment. \n\nI’",
