    "id": "185c10c8-85a2-4432-986c-a84d72e34dab",
    "title": "June 2025 DOK Session_Slides Used.pptx (Part 7/11)",
    "content": "imals. \nIf learning goal is to use standard algorithm to multiply two three-digit decimals, then necessary difficulty – there is a need for decimals. \nWhether task difficulty is necessary or excessive depends on our goals – what we want students to learn, what we want students to be able to demonstrate – so we always need to check is the difficulty here necessary, is it inherent to what we want students to learn or demonstrate.\n73\n\nAlas, all the good intentions that I \u000bhad had had had \u000bno effect on the intended outcomes. \nSource: [common “English is hard” memes]\nIt is okay to ask students to do difficult things. We expect our students to do difficult things – like to spell and use conventions of standard English.\n\nBut we want to make sure that we are not including excess difficulty and that when we are aiming to have students engage in complex tasks, that we are really giving them opportunities that are complex and not just more difficult. \n\n74\n\nItem C What is the name of this plant? \n \nIs one of these items more difficult than the other? \nItem D What is the name of this plant? \nCarrot\nCorn\nPotato\nTomato\nAmerican sycamore\nBox elder\nSwamp maple\nSweet gum\nOpportunity to learn (OTL) influences difficulty. \n\nFamiliarity makes things easier. When we haven’t had the opportunity to learn, it makes things harder. \n\nThis is why many people would not recognize the sweet gum tree on the left but would recognize the tomato plant on the right. \n\n75\n\nItem E What brands are shown here? \n \nIs one of these more difficult than the other? \nItem F What brands are shown here? \nThis was supposed to represent cultural aspects of exposure and experience – that it is harder to identify brands that you aren’t familiar with. \n\nAgain, the idea is just that we know that cultural factors, exposure, experience all affect difficulty.\n76\n\nWhat makes this question difficult? \nWhich of the following is least likely to not be partially incorrect? \nWhat does that even mean??\nWhile there is some inherent difficulty to things we learn, there is also a category of out of out of place difficulty– we need to keep a lookout for that, too – we want to flag any instances where we see difficulty obstacles that might interfere with students showing what they know. \n\n\n\n77\n\nMany factors influence task difficulty. \u000b\u000bWe can be intentional about factors within the realm of curriculum and instructional design.\nWe have a lot of tools – \nUDL \nBias and sensitivity review \nAccommodations \n\nWe can be intentional within our practice to think about difficulty separate from complexity\n\nDOK helps with content analysis and with separating difficulty from complexity as we design and use educational materials and make pedagogical decisions. \n\n\n\n\nComplexity OF a text is different from complexity of engagement WITH the text. \nK.RI.5\nRecognize common types of informational text; identify the front cover, back cover, and title page of a book. \n1\n1.RI.5\nKnow and use various text features (e.g., headings, tables of contents, glossaries, electronic menus, icons) to locate key facts or information in a text. \n1\nThese are Kinder and G1 expectations – could apply to text of any complexity. \n\n\n\n7.RL.6\n\nAnalyze how an author develops and contrasts the points of view of different characters or narrators in a literary text. \n3\n8.RL.6\nAnalyze how differences in the points of view of the characters and the audience or reader (e.g., created through the use of dramatic irony) create such effects as suspense or humor.\n3\nComplexity OF a text is different from complexity of engagement WITH the text. \nCan also have complex engagement with low complexity text. \n\n\n\n\nSelf-Reflection Stoplight\nThis extended or affirmed my thinking.\nI need to work on this a little more. \nHold up. I need a better map. \nWHAT ARE YOUR TAKEAWAYS AT THIS POINT? \n\nDOK is a language system that helps us talk effectively and consistently about complexity of cognitive engagement with academic content.\nDifficulty and complexity are related but different.\nUsed to promote alignment\n\nEducational Materials\nStandards\nDepth of Knowledge (DOK) Consistency\nThe component of educational materials (objective, question, prompt, task, etc.) elicits work that is as cognitively demanding as the expectations in the corresponding [learning] targets.\nDOK consistency / intentional process of designing aligned materials/opportunities/etc – we want to be making purposeful decisions about the content of our educational materials, informed by the complexity of the learning targets, -- we want to provide learning opportunities that are as cognitively demanding as the expectations in the corresponding learning targets. \n\nWe are looking at a relationship –but we can’t compare our educational materials to something if we don’t know what that something looks like. To develop appropriately complex educational materials, we really have to know what the standards look like. \n\n(Circles and colors are not intended to represent anything in particular)\n82\n\nS",
