    "id": "e7c49726-6f21-4297-be39-c3ab119d596c",
    "title": "June 2025 DOK Session_Slides Used.pptx (Part 6/11)",
    "content": "Which of the following is an equation of a line that passes through the point (0, 5) and has a negative slope? \n\ny = 5x\ny = 5x − 5\ny = 5x + 5\ny = −5x − 5\ny = −5x + 5*\nHARD\nLOW\nHow does this compare with what you expected? \nLOW complexity per test framework AND per DOK: a student is expected to have learned the meaning of “slope” and know y = mx + b. Thus, this task requires recognizing a specific example of y = mx + b. \n\nAlthough low complexity, high difficulty. \n\nExamples of tasks and questions that are difficult but not complex.\nWhat is the term for “fear of cooking”? \n(Mageirocophobia)\nRecite the first 50 digits of Pi.\nhttps://c1.staticflickr.com/3/2304/4514253818_42fbd3fa98_b.jpg\nWhat is the scientific name for this tree? \n(Liquidambar styraciflua )\n\nDifficulty and complexity are related but different topics. \nDEBRIEF at your table: In what ways is complexity different than difficulty?\nOne way of thinking about it is amount of effort vs degree of processing of concepts and skills\n\nOne thing in particular that is important to keep in mind is that tasks that are difficult are not always complex.\n\nMost certainly, some things may be harder for some students than for others. Separating perceptions of difficulty from complexity is helpful, for example, to ensure access to the complex cognitive demands of the standards is maintained, including when a variety of accommodations and supports are needed.\n\nFor teachers of multilingual learners (?) What strategies do you use to help students engage with complex academic work when curriculum and instruction is in English language and students are working on developing English language proficiency? \n\n\n\n\nDefinitions in a Nutshell: Complexity of Engagement\nKey factors that influence expectation / task complexity\n \n\nLow complexity\t\t\t                   High complexity\nConcrete ideas and concepts ---------------------------------------------Abstract/Hypothetical ideas and concepts\nDiscrete facts or ideas in isolation--------------------------------Intricacies/dependencies between/among ideas\nLow processing of concepts and skills---------------------------------------High processing of concepts and skills\nRoutine/typical problem type------------------------------------------------------------Non-routine/novel problem type\nStraightforward/literal thinking---------------------------------------------------------------Inferential/figurative thinking\nNo transfer--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------Far transfer\nNo metacognitive demands----------------------------------------------------------Extensive metacognitive demands\nThis is not an exhaustive list –but it’s some key factors. \n\nImportantly, complexity looks different in different disciplines - we have different ways of knowing (different epistemological practices) in each discipline. So we use DOK definitions that are specific to a particular content area – but these are some broader factors that represent a conceptualization of complexity. \n\nNOTE: Complexity is influenced by task context (e.g. complexity of a phenomenon, text, or other stimulus) but depends on the way(s) in which students must engage with the task context. \n\n\n\n\n\nDefinitions in a Nutshell: Difficulty (that is inherent to a task)\n                     \nBased on these conceptual models of complexity and difficulty, tasks that are complex are likely difficult– because they require a lot of processing of concepts and skills and involve interconnected parts and abstract ideas. But tasks that are difficult are not necessarily complex, and many simple tasks can still be very difficult. \n\nDifficulty is often conflated with complexity. But we would argue that they are different concepts – and also that our learning expectations, across content areas, emphasize complexity, not difficulty. So sorting out difficulty from complexity is really important to help us “do what  we are already trying to do.”\n\nLike complexity, difficulty is influenced by multiple factors, including the amount of effort required –it also can be related to the opportunity for error, opportunity to learn.\n\nIn the context of education we want to be very intentional with difficulty – We DO have some expectations for students to work through difficult tasks. There are things that we know are hard and also think it’s important to learn. One way to think of that is “inherent” difficulty. And of course we try to avoid “excess”  difficulty – this is difficulty that is NOT directly related to the learning target and that could present an obstacle for students. \n\n\n\nWhat makes this question difficult? \n   0.82\nX 1.34\nIf learning goal is to use standard algorithm to multiply two three-digit numbers, then excess difficulty – no need for decimals. \nIf learning goal is to use standard algorithm to multiply two three-digit decimals, then necessary difficulty – there is a need for decimals. \nWhether task difficulty is necessary or excessive",
