    "title": "June 2025 DOK Session_Slides Used.pptx (Part 5/11)",
    "content": "ts have the opportunity to learn and demonstrate DOK 3 expectations then we need to use DOK 3 tasks – just as an example, but overall we need to give opportunities that correspond to the complexity explicit in the standard.\n\nSelf-Reflection Stoplight\nThis extended or affirmed my thinking.\nI need to work on this a little more. \nHold up. I need a better map. \n\nKey observations about the complexity of cognitive demands as expressed in our academic standards:\nThe standards are not ordered by complexity. \n\nThe distribution of complexity within academic standards varies by grade and by subject area.\n\nThere are lower and higher complexity expectations in all grades. \nSometimes, particularly in mathematics, higher complexity expectations are purposefully introduced before lower complexity expectations  - because of what we know about learning – the standards are represent our best understanding of effective and efficient teaching and learning. \nWe don’t have the same distribution of the complexity of expectations in each subject area or within each grade. \n\nThese observations are consequential for “why this matters.”\n\nStandard / Assessment Target\nDOK \n5.NBT.5 Fluently multiply multi-digit whole numbers using the standard algorithm. \n5.NF.6 Solve real world problems involving multiplication of fractions and mixed numbers, e.g., by using visual fraction models or equations to represent the problem. \nConsensus process: \nAssign DOK to each of the expectations shown in the table below. \nThis is to share an example of the type of work we might do with a group – go through a set of standards. \n\nStandard / Assessment Target\nDOK \n5.NBT.5 Fluently multiply multi-digit whole numbers using the standard algorithm. \nConsensus process: \nAssign DOK to each of the expectations shown in the table below. \nDOK 1\nUsing the full definitions for consistency, calibrating on interpretations, etc. \n\nStandard / Assessment Target\nDOK \n5.NF.6 Solve real world problems involving multiplication of fractions and mixed numbers, e.g., by using visual fraction models or equations to represent the problem. \nConsensus process: \nAssign DOK to each of the expectations shown in the table below. \nDOK 2\n\n\n\nRI.8.2 Determine two or more central ideas of a text and analyze their development over the course of the text, including how they interact and build on one another to provide a complex analysis; provide an objective summary of the text.\n\nDOK 3\nDOK 2\nThis is an example of a standard that is mostly DOK 3 but a part that is DOK 2. \n\nMight not always make sense to assign a single DOK to a whole standard. Depends on context.\n\nDOK Calibration with Test Items\n\n\n \nDOK 1\nrequires recall of information, use of a taught procedure or protocol, routine tasks, etc.\nDOK 2\ninvolves use of underlying conceptual understanding, connections between and among ideas and relationships, etc.\nDOK 3\nrequires heavy reasoning, abstract thinking, may involve novel solutions, etc.\nDOK 4\napplies to high complexity tasks that require extended time to complete, for example a research-based argument or an authentic science investigation, etc.\nDepth of Knowledge (Webb, 1997)\nThis is just a very brief summary that fits onto a single slide, but for consistency, should use the full definitions as a guide. \n\nWork through the problem as a whole group.\nOn your own, assign a DOK.\nDiscuss at your table.\nDiscuss as whole group.\nNOTE: The following items are not posted publicly. \n\nWhich of these means about the same as the word gauge?\n\nbalance\n\nmeasure\n\nselect\n\nwarn\nGrade 4\n61\n\nGrade 8\n62\n\nGrade 8\n63\n\nhttps://www.michigan.gov/documents/mde/2018_M-STEP_Annotated_Sample_Items_test_614886_7.pdf\n64\n\nNone of the above\nA)\nB)\nC)\nD)\nGrade 4\n65\n\nIt’s still a level 1\n(AP Photo/Matt Rourke)\nIn 2007, Marc Umile became the North American record-holder by reciting from memory over 15,000 digits of Pi.   \n“Recite Pi” = DOK 1. \n\nYou could ask other questions – e.g. Why did you decide to memorize Pi instead of the dictionary? How did you choose your methods for memorizing Pi? What approach for memorizing Pi is most efficient? How are people’s approaches to memorizing Pi similar and different? Etc. But the task of reciting Pi = DOK 1. There’s one correct digit after another. \n66\n\nWith tablemates, rate this item by DIFFICULTY and by COMPLEXITY for the average 8th grader.\nWhich of the following is an equation of a line that passes through the point (0, 5) and has a negative slope? \n\ny = 5x\ny = 5x − 5\ny = 5x + 5\ny = −5x − 5\ny = −5x + 5\nEASY\nHARD\nLOW\nMEDIUM\nMIDDLE\nHIGH\nDifficulty\nComplexity\n\nThis is a released NAEP item. \nWe have empirical difficulty data (they sort into buckets of easy, medium, and hard). \nWe have the NAEP complexity rating (low, middle, high). \nAnd we can think about complexity through the lens of DOK. \n\nWhich of the following is an equation of a line that passes through the point (0, 5) and has a negative slope? \n\ny = 5x\ny = 5x − 5\ny = 5x + 5\ny = −5x − 5\ny = −5x + 5*\nHARD\nLOW\nHow does this compare w",
