    "content": "udents really have to think about this one. \nIt’s pretty rigorous. \nI don’t think it’s hard.\nMy students struggle with this. \nSource: PPT Clipart\nWe need this tool because if we are going to effectively evaluate and interpret and compare and communicate about complexity – and do this consistently -- we need to have a common way of talking about that complexity. \n \nWe find that people – even highly experienced educators and assessment writers and other stakeholders – all bring very different ideas about complexity to the table – then we can all be trying to talk about the complexity of an expectation or task-- and coming at it from different angles, looking through different lenses. Even when we are all working toward the same goals, this can interfere with achievement of those goals. \n\n40\n\nDOK 2!\nDOK 2!\nDOK 2!\nDOK 2!\nDOK 2!\nSource: PPT Clipart\nIf we are trying to evaluate complexity, for the purpose of evaluating (and promoting) alignment, as well as for the purpose of working toward goals of educational equity and student achievement, we need a common language system.\n\n This image is oversimplified, of course. We don’t actually expect everyone to completely agree on everything. In reality this is a dynamic consensus, that involves lots of listening and discussion and reflection and interaction. \n\n41\n\ndailymail.co.uk\n42\n\nAcademic Content Standards and DOK\nWhat distribution of DOK do we see in standards? \n43\n\nExample: Mathematics CCSS categorized by type of cognitive demands \nHS.VM.1 Recognize vector quantities as having both magnitude and direction. Represent vector quantities by directed line segments, and use appropriate symbols for vectors and their magnitudes (e.g., v, |v|, ||v||, v).\nHS.IF.2 Use function notation, evaluate functions for inputs in their domains, and interpret statements that use function notation in terms of a context.\nHS.MD.5 Evaluate and compare strategies on the basis of expected values.\nHS.MG.3 Apply geometric methods to solve design problems (e.g., designing an object or structure to satisfy physical constraints or minimize cost; working with typographic grid systems based on ratios).\nEXAMPLE: In high school, we have math expectations that are DOK 1, 2, 3, and 4\n\nDOK 1: Definitional understanding of vectors\nDOK 2: Using underlying conceptual understanding, interpreting mathematical ideas in context - thinking about relationships between ideas and applying ideas. \nDOK 3: This expectation is more abstract. We have to use hypothetical thinking and make reasoned judgments. \nDOK 4: This last task is highly complex but it requires iterative thinking over time to authentically complete. \n\n\nExample: ELA & Literacy CCSS categorized by type of cognitive demands\nL.9–10.2 Demonstrate command of the conventions of standard English capitalization, punctuation, and spelling when writing.\nRI&RL.9–10.1 Cite strong and thorough textual evidence to support analysis of what the text says explicitly as well as inferences drawn from the text. \nRL.9–10.5 Analyze how an author’s choices concerning how to structure a text, order events within it (e.g., parallel plots), and manipulate time (e.g., pacing, flashbacks) create such effects as mystery, tension, or surprise. \nW. 9–10.1 Write arguments to support claims in an analysis of substantive topics or texts, using valid reasoning and relevant and sufficient evidence. \nEXAMPLE: In high school, we have ELA expectations that are DOK 1, 2, 3, and 4\n\n46\ntotal is > 100% b/c a DOK “range” was assigned.\n\nDon’t need details -- KEY point = most of the math CCSS are DOK 1 and DOK 2, some DOK 3 (most of these in GEOM). The change from old standard 🡪 CCSS was largely a shift to more DOK 2, to a greater focus on skills and concepts and not just memorization/rote \n\n\n46\n\nDOK of All Mathematics Common Core State Standards (based on WestEd data)\n(Total > 100% because ranges of DOK were assigned.)\nPercent of standards\n47\n\n48\nWhat distribution of DOK 3 tasks is appropriate for an assessment?– it depends on the targeted standards. \n48\n\n49\nKEY POINT: totally different ballgame for ELA. CCSS are heavy on the 3 end and there’s as many DOK 4 as DOK 1!\n49\n\nDOK of All Literacy Common Core State Standards (based on WestEd data)\n(Total > 100% because ranges of DOK were assigned.)\nPercent of standards\n\nMath\nLiteracy\nShould the difference in DOK distribution affect curriculum, instruction, and assessment? \nBig picture is that the distribution should reflect the standards. That said, there are very good reasons to make pedagogical decisions that intentionally wrap many different tasks into a larger problem or project – so we could purposefully craft DOK 4 tasks and embed opportunities within that address a variety of standards at all different DOK levels – but if we want to make sure students have the opportunity to learn and demonstrate DOK 3 expectations then we need to use DOK 3 tasks – just as an example, but overall we need to give opportunities that correspond to the complexity ex",
