    "content": "gement with hypothetical or abstract constructs, evaluative tasks, heavy reasoning.\nDOK 4: Applies to high complexity, iterative tasks that require extended time to complete with greater metacognitive demands.\nAcademic expectations can be categorized by type.\nClassification systems are helpful tools, used in many different contexts. \n\nIf a categorization framework is grounded in meaningful distinctions, then the use of the framework, even though a simplification of the more elaborate construct, the use of the framework can help to enrich understandings of the fuller construct. \n\nDOK 1: Does it focus on recall of facts or reproduction of taught processes?\nDOK 2: Does it focus on relationships between concepts and ideas or the use of underlying conceptual understanding?\nDOK 3: Does it focus on abstract inferencing, non-routine problem-solving, or require authentic evaluative and argumentative processes?\nDOK 4: Does it have at least the complexity of DOK 3 tasks, but also require iterative and extended time to complete?\n\nLike anything else that we categorize, this does not mean that there are four naturally occurring categories - these are just categories that are useful for particular purposes – here – for interpreting academic content consistently - to inform and evaluate alignment in support of a coherent system. \n\n\n\n\nDOK 4!\nUse of DOK can help clarify and communicate about how we are interpreting a standard (or any other learning expectation).\nImage: PPT Clipart\nDOK 3!\n\nSo this is why it is so important to start with an interpretation of the standards– to make sure that we have a shared understanding of what we are expecting students to know and be able to do. And DOK is a tool that can help us with that. \n\nSo we could agree on an interpretation and then NAME this expectation as DOK 1 and that could help us all interpret the standard in the same way. \n\nOr of course we might agree on the other interpretation. \n\nNow to be clear – this is not to suggest that you could just interpret a standard however you want -- \n\n\n\n35\n\nWhat do we mean by DOK levels? \nThey ARE levels of increasing complexity.\nThey ARE NOT levels of importance. \nThey ARE NOT levels of progression or a prescribed sequence.\n\nDOK 1\nDOK 2\nDOK 3\nDOK 4\nLearning does not progress strictly from low to high complexity. \nThere are many representations of DOK as a staircase, suggesting that a student might sort of progress “UP” the steps. This is NOT the intended message of DOK. DOK levels do not represent a leveled sequence of instruction or progression of learning or performance. \n\nLearning does not take place strictly from low complexity to high complexity. If learning progressed, as a rule, from low complexity to higher complexity, then we would expect low complexity expectations in the academic standards for lower grades and high complexity expectations in higher grades. Instead, we have different types of expectations–including high complexity expectations–for students across all grade levels. This reflects a contemporary understanding of learning as a process that follows non-linear and interacting sequences. \n\nAs teachers we know that engaging students with high complexity tasks can improve outcomes, including as relates to basic skills (Darling-Hammond, et al. 2020; Agarwal, 2019; Smith, et al., 1996). And we also know that starting with low complexity tasks, such as memorization, can sometimes be a very inefficient approach. \n\n“Rigid hierarchial models of learning, where basic skills are presumed to be the foundation of more complex skills, are outmoded and inconsistent with what we know about how people learn.” Consortium for Policy Research In Education – CPRE (1991) \n\n(An underlying idea of UDL is that an individual’s learning trajectory is as unique as a fingerprint. )\n\n\n\nLearning involves an interconnected network of ideas that get restructured and elaborated as needed.\nDOK 1\nDOK 2\nDOK 3\nDOK 4\n\nIn some cases, learning may likely progress from low to high complexity - but it is not a general rule. Learning involves an interconnected network of ideas that get restructured and elaborated as needed\n\nProject based learning approaches, for example, start with a complex task and establish a need to know for more simple tasks. \n\nIn general, learning could progress in many ways – we might make decisions related to which way makes most sense, taking into account resource constraints (time, materials, etc), student population and context (interest, preferences, themes, current events, etc), best understanding of teaching and learning (efficiency / effectiveness, etc), and many other factors. \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSelf-Reflection Stoplight\nThis extended or affirmed my thinking.\nI need to work on this a little more. \nHold up. I need a better map. \n\nIt’s Challenging\nStudents really have to think about this one. \nIt’s pretty rigorous. \nI don’t think it’s hard.\nMy students struggle with this. \nSource: PPT Clipart\nWe need this tool because if we are going to effective",
