  {
    "id": "9ef0f0c8-c30c-4140-8e42-26e8ef044676",
    "title": "June 2025 DOK Session_Slides Used.pptx (Part 2/11)",
    "content": "is the complexity of cognitive engagement. \n\nWarm Up: Which expectation is most complex?\n1. Interpret symptoms of common car problems to suggest likely repair needs.\n\n2. Correctly install a car battery.\n\n3. Propose defensible systemic changes to improve automobile safety regulations. \nCourse: Automotive Technology and Transportation Systems\n20\n\n\nSelf-Reflection Stoplight\nThis extended or affirmed my thinking.\nI need to work on this a little more. \nHold up. I need a better map. \n\nSort the cards by task complexity \nAs you sort, discuss WHAT ARE THE FACTORS that help you sort these tasks by complexity? Try to really pin down what you see in each task that helps you differentiate different levels or types of complexity. \n\n\nOur perceptions of complexity are based on conceptual constructs that may vary. \n\nA grounding assumption of a standards-based system is that the language of the standards communicates the expected complexity of cognitive engagement with disciplinary content – for\u000ball students. \nWe must be able to use a content analysis…\n…to make an inference…\n…about the cognitive complexity required to successfully meet an expectation or for successful task completion.\n\n\"If teachers are inconsistent in the way they interpret standards, then students’ opportunity to learn will be varied according to their teachers’ understanding.\" (Dickenson, et al., 2020)\nInconsistent interpretations affect curriculum and assessment development and evaluation, pedagogical decision-making, etc. \nA coherent standards-based system requires consistency in interpretation, communication, and evaluation of expectations.\n\nWe have many important tools and considerations that all work together to support students’ achievement of educational goals. \n\nDistinguishing between and among DOK 1, 2, 3, and 4 requires using the DOK definitions.\nFor consistency we use the DOK definitions\n28\n\nSolve the linear equation. \nCorrectly spell 10 terms. \nDefine the word “liminal.” \nDescribe the characteristics of a right triangle. \nDraw a circle, square, rectangle, and triangle. \nMatch the carnivore with its prey. \n\nSummarize the main idea of the article. \nCompare and contrast the roles of a president and the roles of a prime minister. \nDraw a floor plan of this room (to scale). \nDefine the difference between a short story and an essay. \n\nProvide a rationale for your solution to the problem. \nDescribe how the story would be different if it were set in the year 2015 instead of 1915. \nDiscuss what you think the author of Story A would think about Story B. \nDraw a 5-10 panel cartoon that presents an alternative ending to the story you just read. \nDefine the terms and conditions under which children less than 15 years old should be allowed to work. \n\nDevelop a research-based argument for whether or not the U.S. government should provide money to fund space exploration. \n\nDOK 1\nDOK 3\nDOK 2\nDOK 4\nPLEASE DO NOT SHARE OUTSIDE OF WCEPS. \n\n\n29\n\nWe don’t endorse this. (We didn’t make this.)\nThis is sometimes called the Wheel of Misfortune, we have heard. It’s a good thesaurus, but it’s not very helpful for determining the complexity of an expectation or task. \n\nNote that this has been widely distributed in the form of an 8 ½” x 11” handout with a table underneath and a citation to Norman Webb. The table is, indeed, from Norman’s work – but the citation on the same page as the wheel gives the impression that the wheel is also Norman’s work (it is not). \n\nThus, many, many, many, many people have (very reasonably) assumed that the Wheel was Norm’s work. It is not. \n\n30\n\nOne way to define cognitive complexity in the context of a standards-based system is by categorizing the types of cognitive demands within the academic standards themselves. \nConduct a content analysis of the academic standards documents.\nIdentify patterns, themes, categories (requires input from subject area experts).\nDerive underlying ideas about the factors that influence complexity of engagement, as represented by the standards for each subject area.\nThis is how the Depth of Knowledge (DOK) framework was developed.\n\nCredit: Motion Pictures Association\nWe categorize all sorts of things to make sense of elaborate constructs and help communicate about them. \n\nThis is a very familiar example. \n\nA categorization system is a type of tool that adds clarity to communication about something that is much more involved and complex. When we see that a movie is rated G or PG-13 – we know more or less what that means. \n32\n\n33\n\nDOK 1: Requires recall and reproduction of taught facts, concepts, procedures, protocols, etc; Routine tasks; fluency.\nDOK 2: Involves underlying conceptual understanding of disciplinary ideas, application of concepts, relationships and connections between and among ideas.\nDOK 3: Requires engagement with hypothetical or abstract constructs, evaluative tasks, heavy reasoning.\nDOK 4: Applies to high complexity, iterative tasks that require extended time to complete with greater metacognitive",
