    "id": "da8aca64-3ab4-46fc-bb93-b7435840d58f",
    "title": "June 2025 DOK Session_Slides Used.pptx (Part 1/11)",
    "content": "Unlocking Rigor: Practical Applications of Webb’s Depth of Knowledge (DOK)\n\nSara Christopherson, Director of WebbAlign\nsara@wceps.org\n\t\n\n1\n\n9:00 am – 3:00 pm\nLunch break at noonish.\nToday’s Schedule\nPlease sign in using the QR code of the link below!\n ​\nbit.ly/CARESIGN\n\u000b\u000b\n\nIntroductions\n\nSelect a spokesperson for your table.\n\nNames, home bases, professional role(s)?\n\nDo you currently use DOK in your classroom, school, or district? If so, how? \n\nAny particular questions about DOK?\n\nPurposes of Today’s Meeting\nContextualize DOK within an aligned (coherent) education system\nWork toward a common understanding of cognitive complexity\nDifferentiate difficulty from complexity (and from other factors)\nReview common misinterpretations of DOK\nPractice using DOK to promote calibration\nIdentify specific goals for implementation or improvement in use of DOK in your practice / context.\nStrategize approaches for meeting these goals\nHere are the goals we advertised: \nParticipants will:\nDifferentiate DOK levels, addressing common misconceptions. \nApply the DOK framework to curriculum, instruction, and assessment contexts.\nDevelop an action plan for implementation or improvement.\n.\n\n\n\n\n\n\nReflex Response:\u000b\u000b\u000b\u000b\u000b\u000b\u000b\nWhat are the first few words or phrases that come to mind…\n...when you hear the word....\n“TEST”\n5\n\n\nIn general, we ask students questions and use their responses to make inferences about what they know and are able to do. \nWe can use DOK to make sure that our questions allow for the intended inferences. \n\n\u000bWhat is (are) the purpose(s) of academic content standards?\u000b\u000bTalk with your table.\u000bThen, share out.\u000b\u000b\nWe are always trying to find out what student know….as relates to the academic standards, linking back to the academic standards. \n8\n\n\nAcademic standards guide…\nequitable educational opportunity\nassessment development\ncurriculum development \nteachers’ practices\ndevelopment of learning objectives\nunit/lesson development\nteacher professional development\nteacher education\nand more\n\n\n“Alignment refers to how well all policy elements in a system work together to guide instruction and, ultimately, student learning.” \n(Webb, 1997)\nSource: WCEPS/WebbAlign, 2023\n\n\nAlignment: A powerful tool for focusing instruction, curricula, and assessment\n\nAlignment is the degree to which expectations, curriculum, and assessments are in agreement and serve in conjunction with one another to guide the system toward students learning what is expected.\n(Webb, 1997)\n\nThere were (and are) multiple goals of standards-based reform. \u000b\n“…depth of understanding, and complex thinking” …“knowledge construction through analysis and synthesis of real life problems” …“production of knowledge, rigor in thinking, and sustained intellectual effort”  vs “isolated facts and skills” and “broad coverage of unconnected facts.” \n(Smith and O’Day, 1988)\n\n\nCognitive  Social and Emotional  Dispositional  Collaborative  Behavioral\n\n\nA core purpose is to set “higher-level cognitive goals” for all students.\n\nFor example…“developing students’ self-worth and promoting collective responsibility”…\n\nRIGOR\n\n“…alignment and cognitive rigor…based on rigorous standards…aligned with rigorous standards…access to rigorous coursework…rigorous and of higher quality…”\nFrom: https://oese.ed.gov/files/2020/10/implementing_the_every_student_succeeds_act-1.pdf\n\nWhat is RIGOR?\nSource: Merriam Webster\n15\n\nStrictness and hard work\nQualifier of the evidentiary basis in educational research and evaluation \nCompiled by Gavin Fulmer\nGeneral environment\nAcademic Content\nEvaluative determination\nRigor is used in various ways, to mean various things, as relates to various topics. \n\nRigorous standards are those that “ include high-level cognitive demands by asking students to demonstrate deep conceptual understanding through the application of content knowledge and skills to new situations.” \nThese types of high-level cognitive demands involve thinking skills like “reasoning, justification, synthesis, analysis, and problem-solving.” \n(CCSS development criteria)\nWithin standards-based education, rigor is typically closely linked with cognitive complexity.\n\nStandards do not just list topics that students should know “about.” Instead, they specify expectations for the complexity of cognitive engagement with disciplinary concepts and ideas.\n\nBut wait…what is complexity?\nCOMPLEXITY as relates to the PROCESS OF LEARNING?\nCOMPLEXITY as relates to the PROCESS OF TEACHING?\nCOMPLEXITY as relates to the PROCESS OF SOLVING A PROBLEM?\nCOMPLEXITY as relates to STUDENT PERFORMANCE?\nCOMPLEXITY as relates to ACADEMIC TOPICS?\nCOMPLEXITY as relates to TEXTS - text complexity?\nCOMPLEXITY as relates to LANGUAGE and ELP Development - linguistic complexity?\nCOMPLEXITY as relates to an EXPECTATION or TASK?\n\n\nOkay, so rigor is the complexity of cognitive engagement. \n\nWarm Up: Which expectation is most complex?\n1. Interpret symptoms of common car problems to suggest likely repair needs.\n\n2. Correctly install a car batter",
