  {
    "id": "4a61a87e-947b-4e5d-8a93-214e554a31ac",
    "title": "ESEA/ESSA Federal Funding for EL Professional Development",
    "content": "File: Title Funding for PD.docx\nTitle: ESEA/ESSA Federal Funding for EL Professional Development\nTags: Cntext for Colorado and the Federal title programs. If someone asks about Colorado funding sources this is helpful.\n\nContent: ESEA/ESSA Federal Funding for EL Professional Development\n\nThis document includes information and specific statutory citations for where there are federal Title funding requirements to specifically provide PD for EL educators (EL teachers, content teachers, administrators, etc.) using Title funds – This can be used for marketing in any state and the BIE. NOTE: in Colorado, local education agencies are called local education providers. Other states may have alternate vocabulary for this and other terms and marketing should ensure that appropriate vocabulary is employed.\n\nFederal Funding from The Elementary and Secondary Education Act (ESEA) as amended by The Every Student Succeeds Act (ESSA)\n\nESEA/ESSA Title II – Requirements to Address the Needs of ELs\n\nESEA/ESSA Title II, Section 2103 includes the requirements to support English learners with Title II funds. Title II funds must be used “to develop, implement, and evaluate comprehensive programs and activities described in subsection (b)” which says that programs and activities funded with Title II funds “shall address the learning needs of all students, including children with disabilities, English learners, and gifted and talented students;” (Sec. 2103(b)(2)). Title II also allows the use of Title II funds for the development of “programs and activities that increase the ability of teachers to effectively teach . . . English learners” (Sec. 2103(b)(3)(F)).\n\nESEA/ESSA Title III – Professional Development for EL Educators Required\n\nESEA/ESSA Title III includes the requirement to use Title III funds “to provide effective professional development to classroom teachers (including teachers in classroom settings that are not the settings of language instruction educational programs), principals and other school leaders, administrators, and other school or community-based organizational personnel” (Sec. 3115(c)(2)). Any local education agency receiving Title III funds must use a portion of these funds to provide professional development to its educators working with ELs.\n\nPurchasing CARE Coaching, which is an allowable expenditure using Title II and Title III funds, is one way to meet the compliance requirements highlighted in these statutory citations.\n\nNOTE: Professional development for EL educators is allowed under Title I in several ways. Title I, however, does not specifically say that Title I funds must be used for PD for EL educators.",
