  {
    "id": "58375d6b-ceec-46fb-b39a-dcf9a294af83",
    "title": "Colorado Statute and Guidance related to ELs",
    "content": "File: CO statute and info.docx\nTitle: Colorado Statute and Guidance related to ELs\nTags: Cntext for Colorado and the Federal title programs. If someone asks about Colorado funding sources this is helpful.\n\nContent: Colorado Statute and Guidance related to ELs\n\n“Colorado funds English Language Learners (ELLs) through a combination of federal and state sources, including Title III funds and state categorical programs like the English Language Proficiency Act (ELPA).” Additionally, some ELL students are included in the state's \"at-risk\" funding formula\n\nEnglish Language Proficiency Act: CDE Guidance/flyer (https://www.cde.state.co.us/cde_english/elpa-overview) This funding includes both a component to provide EL programs and a second component to provide professional development and student support.\n\nStatute: CO statute 22-24-101 through 22-24-108\n\nLink to statute language on Justia\n\nRegulations: https://www.sos.state.co.us/CCR/GenerateRulePdf.do?ruleVersionId=7752&amp;fileName=1%20CCR%20301-10\n\nNOTE: Colorado uses the term “local education provider” instead of “local education agency”\n\nCO statute citations for supporting EL teacher professional development\n\n22-24-102(1)(d) “(d) The state and local education providers must enhance all educators' effectiveness in supporting English language development and in enabling English language learners to achieve and maintain grade-level performance in academic content areas;”\n\n22-24-102(1)(e) “(e) The state must develop an educator workforce that can support the educational approach and goals of local education providers to help ensure that English language learners are postsecondary and workforce ready at graduation;”\n\n22-24-102(1)(f) “(f) The state must appropriate and allocate moneys to local education providers to help ensure that English language learners are postsecondary and workforce ready at graduation;”\n\n22-24-108(1)(b) “(b) Provide effective professional development activities related to teaching English language learners for all educators who may work with English language learners in the classroom;”\n\nEnglish Learner Educator EL PD requirements for certain Content Teachers from the CO SBOE Rules: (https://ed.cde.state.co.us/educatortalent/elpdeducators) “educators with elementary, math, science, social studies and English language arts (and any middle-level) endorsements to complete Culturally and Linguistically Diverse (CLD) Education training or professional development (PD).” Requirement is 45 clock hours of professional learning aligned with CLD standards.\n\nAt-Risk Funding: See link (https://cosfp.org/wp-content/uploads/edbuild_-co_-_ell_-_final.pdf) The CO At-Risk funding is a weighed formula that adds a percentage of the adjusted base per pupil funding to the total per pupil funding a local education provider receives. However, at-risk funding includes eligibility for free-reduced lunch. A student can only be included in at-risk funding once, so if an EL is also free-reduced lunch eligible, they only are included once in the at-risk funding, basically generating no extra funding unless they only qualify as an EL. There is also a sliding scale for at-risk funding that starts at 12% (added to base) and can go as high as 30% based on the concentration of ELs in a district.\n\nCDE offered Mentor Grants on a competitive basis in the past. While the website says that there are no current plans for future rounds of funding for Mentor Grants, these may be something to watch out for since they include allowable costs for PD do develop mentors (https://ed.cde.state.co.us/educatortalent/mentorgrant)\n\nAppropriations (https://content.leg.colorado.gov/sites/default/files/documents/2024A/bills/2024a_1183_enr.pdf) see page 7 – Looks like about $34 million for ELPA from State funding\n\nWCEPS is an approved PD provider (https://ed.cde.state.co.us/educatortalent/elpdprograms)",
