  {
    "id": "121c659b-abc9-4dfe-9386-3a3358bc2917",
    "title": "Potential Colorado Marketing Content",
    "content": "File: Potential Colorado Marketing Content.docx\nTitle: Potential Colorado Marketing Content\nTags: Cntext for Colorado and the Federal title programs. If someone asks about Colorado funding sources this is helpful.\n\nContent: Potential Colorado Marketing Content\n\nHow to fund CARE Coaching Professional Development (PD) in Colorado\n\nProfessional Development for EL Educators Requirements\n\nState Funding\n\nColorado English Language Proficiency Act – Required Professional Development\n\nThe Colorado English Language Proficiency Act (ELPA) includes requirements to provide EL programs, professional development for educators, and supports for EL students (CO Statute 22-24-101 through 22-24-108). The ELPA includes funding that is separate from the Colorado school funding formula’s At-Risk funding. ELPA funding is distributed to local education providers (districts, charter schools, etc.) to provide, among other things, “effective professional development activities related to teaching English language learners for all educators who may work with English language learners in the classroom” (CO statute 22-24-108(1)(b)). It is important to note that the statute says “all educators” who work with ELs, not just educators involved in teaching English language acquisition. WCEPS professional development is an approved professional development provider by the Colorado Department of Education (CDE). This makes purchasing CARE Coaching an allowable expenditure using ELPA professional development funds.\n\nColorado School Funding Formula – At-Risk Funding\n\nColorado’s school funding formula includes additional funds for at-risk students. These funds can be used to support professional development for educators. The CO At-Risk funding is a weighed formula that adds a percentage of the adjusted base per pupil funding to the total per pupil funding a local education provider receives for eligible students. However, at-risk funding includes eligibility for both EL students and students who qualify for the free lunch program. A student can only be included in at-risk funding once, so if an EL is also free lunch eligible, they are only included in the at-risk funding formula once, generating no extra funding for local education providers unless the student only qualifies as an EL and not is not eligible for the free lunch program. At-risk funding is calculated using a sliding scale that starts at 12% (added to base) and can go as high as 30% based on the concentration of ELs in a district.\n\nState Professional Development Requirements\n\nColorado State Board of Education EL Professional Development Rules\n\nEnglish Learner Educators Professional Develop in Colorado include the requirement that “educators with elementary, math, science, social studies and English language arts (and any middle-level, such as middle school math (grades 6-8) or MIDL) endorsements [must] complete Culturally and Linguistically Diverse (CLD) Education training or professional development (PD).” WCEPS is an Approved English Learner Professional Development Program for Colorado and can be purchased to help educators meet this requirement.",
