    "content": "File: mm25_chunk_16.mp4\nTitle: mm25_chunk_16\nTags: multimodal workshop good content\n\nContent: I think the key is thinking of scaffolds is not as a consistent thing that you're doing the same thing for the students every time, that it's always changing and evolving and you're growing in the scaffolds that you're giving your students so that they are moving in the direction of the goal of a great level standard. And that way it's not just the same scaffold every time. And then at the end, at the end of the day, the students are working independently. You know, that's the goal of the scaffold, is to, you know, it's not something we just, it's a knee-jerk reaction or it's not something that's planned the same way every time. But it's something that's the right place, right time, and eventually pulled away so the students can do it independently. Now, I was a little worried about time, so we'll go through the next three like this. So this next section is around action and expression. So the goal is expert learners who are strategic and goal-directed. And this first section is we are intentionally designing options for interaction. So we want to vary the different ways to get students' responses and optimize access to materials. Now this also includes assistive and accessible technologies and tools. We could go, we could probably talk for an hour on this, but it goes for multilingual learners, this goes back to what are we putting in front of our students and is it accessible? Is it challenging? Is it the right amount of challenge, right? Is it, or is it going to be too boring for them or create too much anxiety?\n",
