    "content": "File: mm25_chunk_15.mp4\nTitle: mm25_chunk_15\nTags: multimodal workshop good content\n\nContent: our new learning, looking for patterns, features, the big ideas, relationships, multiple ways of knowing and making meaning, and then transfer and generalization. So you know, this, I'm just going to kind of skip, looking at the scaffolding piece. Those last two bullet points there, they introduce graduated scaffolds that support, oh, I'd I did it backwards. Looking at graduated scaffolds that support processing, and then progressively releasing the information. So this, we're going to get back into scaffolding, see the connection here between UDL and scaffolding. That last bullet there is so, so important. You know, we're going to provide, scaffolding is meant to be temporary, right? It's meant to be a temporary support, but eventually it needs to be taken away. You know, that's one of the most important concepts is, you know, you're working with the more meaningful other, both are committed to the learning process. We're building the student up, providing the scaffolds, but there has to be that release at the end where the students, so the students can do it independently. So here's a quick example, I'll give you a moment to read through this. And what this was meant to show is the graduated scaffolds. So the scaffolds that the students are using, that's going to build them up. And then we see that scaffold being taken away.\n",
