    "id": "a8c7f0f0-7451-4905-8a92-7219761789d9",
    "title": "mm25_chunk_01",
    "content": "File: mm25_chunk_01.mp4\nTitle: mm25_chunk_01\nTags: multimodal workshop good content\n\nContent: because there's only we don't. Sorry, I always forget something. So we do. Anyway, we have very unique classrooms, very unique school districts. Everybody's on their own path. So this idea of scaffolding really provides that equitable access that I saw one of you mention earlier. It comes from the work of, I guess, Vygotsky's mostly associated with it. We have a lot of people that have built on these ideas. One thing I didn't realize was that his work was really from the early 1900s. It didn't quite become mainstream until the constructivist movements and things like that in the 70s. It's sidebar conversation. But Vygotsky came up with this. The idea is that learners are completing small, manageable steps in order to reach a goal. He referred to working within the zone with a more knowledgeable other. So that more knowledgeable other could be the teacher. It could be another peer, maybe a parent, just somebody who knows a little bit more than they do to help them make connections between concepts, between things they've already experienced, to the new knowledge. And they grow in their zone of proximal development. They become more confident and practice new tasks with the social support around them. Now, this graphic is an attempt to show what that zone is. So on the y-axis, you see the level of challenge grow. Or the level of challenge on the x-axis, we see the level of confidence.\n",
