    "content": "File: mm25_chunk_11.mp4\nTitle: mm25_chunk_11\nTags: multimodal workshop good content\n\nContent: the culture, but there's so much more. So I always think that food is always that entry to a conversation. But somebody wrote newspaper artifacts from home countries. That's such a great one. And now with the internet, it certainly allows for more virtual locker and students are able to include their culture. Math students get to compare maps of their home location. I think you did that, Ryan. Yes. I work in Korea and have a newcomer from Poland. So when we were reading a story about immigration to the US, I asked about his experience moving to Korea. I also found a story. Yes. Connecting to their stories and or allowing, I don't know, this is an old saying, but I'll use it in this case, windows and mirrors. When you give an opportunity for your students to see their cultures represented in your classrooms in a variety of ways, not just with a map or information, but in the lessons and the math unit, even using names of people in word problems that are similar to their culture, that is where you're giving them an opportunity for a mirror and windows is when other students get to see other cultures represented. So it's almost in the IB world. For those of you who have been in IB programs, it's really looking at that in this area is allowing that space for continuing the windows and mirrors. I find candy from different locations and show them. Teresa, I bring candy. I bring Mexican candy to all my workshops so that everyone could be exposed to the spicy candy. Well, and Madeline, I love how you use the word cultural exchange there. Oh, gosh. There could be so many cultural exchanges, you know, not just the food, but also, you know, just talking about, oh, I've had a lot over just what is school, what's the purpose of school, the, you know.\n",
