    "content": "File: mm25_chunk_10.mp4\nTitle: mm25_chunk_10\nTags: multimodal workshop good content\n\nContent: of you to respond. I think it'll help also, Ryan, because those of you who have heard about it versus the ones that are using it in their classroom and the ones that are first time hearing about it, if you're using it in your classroom, this is a great opportunity to share some of the successes, especially if you are finding that some of the areas in UDL are more effective than others, because there is like that balance when you're looking at it from a practical lens, what has been the most effective for multilingual learners. Those of you who are first time hearing it, great, because it's such a great framework. It's good for all students, as Ryan mentioned it. First time hearing it or I've heard about it. So I think we have 68 responses. We're still needing like 20 something, but let's end the poll and share it so that everybody sees the results. So as Ryan's asking you, where do you see this represented? If it's new to you, think of ways that you would consider in your current context what this would look like. Those of you who are using UDL, how are you instilling this framework in your everyday? And then you have some responses of the clothing or religious articles brought in social studies classes, newspaper artifacts from home countries for historic events, food we share and often talk about and share recipes. That is awesome. That is awesome. One of my favorite things about working with multilingual learners is the food. Ryan. Well, you can't say no when they bring it to you, you know. I always laugh because I remember in secondary how they, I would say to the teachers, do you know your students' cultures? And they're like, yeah, they're, you know, I eat their tamales. And so I know that they have great food. I'm like, yeah, there it is.\n",
