  {
    "id": "5b49866c-8f3c-4a08-b751-babe54860f51",
    "title": "mm25_chunk_12",
    "content": "File: mm25_chunk_12.mp4\nTitle: mm25_chunk_12\nTags: multimodal workshop good content\n\nContent: So, clubs and activities that kids can do and it's available to them, really helping the families adjust to life in school in the U.S. And we can learn a lot from them as well, so that's great. So the next part of this one is the language and symbols. So we're looking at vocabulary symbols and language structure, how we decode text, mathematical notation. You can see them here. I'll highlight this last one here, illustrating through multiple media. We have a lot of options these days, you know, really a lot of options and that really gets at the heart of multimodal instruction as well. So just presenting key concepts beyond the traditional sense, where we're either standing and talking, giving out notes maybe, but finding different ways to show the information. It doesn't just help with the visual processing of the content, but your language learners need that repetition. They need to hear those things multiple times in different contexts. So break out those illustrations, the charts, diagrams, infographics, you know. It really helps solidify that learning. I think most of us have probably seen this, but choice boards go a long way. I think the hardest thing for me is actually figuring out all the different ways to do it. You know, but there's a lot, you know, illustrations, storyboards. There are tools now where you can make animations, which are really fun.\n",
