  {
    "id": "bb8ca5fe-07ac-44f6-a0b5-ea88d93a4fd2",
    "title": "mm25_chunk_06",
    "content": "File: mm25_chunk_06.mp4\nTitle: mm25_chunk_06\nTags: multimodal workshop good content\n\nContent: is to honor the culture, the home life, the way that they communicate at home, and just making a clear distinction between that's still communication, what we're learning as academic ways to engage in communication, and it's not better than or less than the way you communicate at home. That is really where translanguaging is speaking to that practice that bilinguals have, but it's also in reference to all our students. Yes, great. So yeah, the sociocultural component, learning is very much a social process. There's a lot that goes into it. With our multilingual students, we have an added layer too, right? We have, we're working within our zone of proximal development, learning, you know, language arts, math, whatever the subject area may be, but we also have language, okay? So whenever we are looking in that, now this is the WIDA framework. We have people from across the country and around the world really that may be using a different framework. I just want to point that out, but we also have to think about the zone of proximal development as it relates to language proficiency and what they are, what is comprehensible to the student. Now you see this progression from our beginning, our earliest stages, the journey students take in their path to, or their journey to English proficiency. We also need to be thinking about, you know, the texts that we put in front of students, the types of language we're expecting out of them, and keeping that in the zone too. You know, we've got to keep that in the zone too and be very mindful of what we put in front of students.\n",
