    "title": "mm25_chunk_07",
    "content": "File: mm25_chunk_07.mp4\nTitle: mm25_chunk_07\nTags: multimodal workshop good content\n\nContent: You know, if you were to, I did study German in college and I feel like I can speak a little bit but I'm not going to be reading a chemistry chapter in German and understand it. So AI can help with that these days too, but I don't want to stay on topic. So this is especially true for our newcomers and our students at the earliest stages of language acquisition. Now this just refers to English, right? Like if you have multilingual resources and things like that, we're able to keep, you know, keep that language growing too. But also we need to be thinking about, you know, the experience of our newcomers, making sure that they're confident and comfortable in our schools using the language that is stretching them, not necessarily snapping them. And that gets us into UDL. So I'm going to copy this link. I'm going to post the UDL guidelines in the chat so you can open that up and explore as you will later. I'm going to give you an overview of the framework. So UDL provides the opportunity for all students to access, participate in, and by reducing barriers to instruction, including the language barrier. The UDL framework values diversity. Now this is for all students. This UDL framework is, you'll notice that it can apply to about anyone. And it's meant to provide access to the course.\n",
