  {
    "id": "d2396f9f-80a8-4b80-b889-a4757d9c29a5",
    "title": "mm25_chunk_13",
    "content": "File: mm25_chunk_13.mp4\nTitle: mm25_chunk_13\nTags: multimodal workshop good content\n\nContent: a comic strip, letting them make a model, dioramas type things, or an infographic, but giving them choice on how they would like to prove their knowledge, the show me type things. And I mentioned something about this. Yes. And I always mentioned it with, we don't have time for this conversation. But if I, I know choice boards have been a part of instruction for many years now. It is even more important now because of this generation of learners who are used to the immediate TikTok responses and, or being able to multitask with their technology. They don't have the stamina to sit in a lesson. We have to build that. And so the best way to attend to that is to have choice boards that are representing the many different ways that students are learning today. Before we used to call them multiple intelligences. And I'm sure that there is a evolution now of this in regards to technology. And there is a balance of too much technology in the classroom as well as in home life. But that is, this idea of choice board is not just about your multilingual learners. It's essential for them. But our learners today are growing up in a very different world than the way that we grew up and the way that we've been taught to teach. So be aware that it is probably one of the most valuable pieces of this UDL framework is to think about representation through choice and letting them see how do they learn best? Like, what do they need to learn best so that they can start communicating to you? Ms. Williams, I need a photo. Ms. Williams, I need a visual. Ms. Williams, can I talk to a partner? Like, they need to tell us what they need to learn in the classroom, because if we go by what we think, we could potentially bore them, which would...\n",
