  {
    "id": "9718746c-071d-4c37-9ae2-90271882e53d",
    "title": "Bridging Content and Language: A Cohesive Approach to Multilingual Learner Success (Part 2/3)",
    "content": "Instruction is both differentiated and goal-oriented, allowing multilingual learners to access content in a way that meets their needs while working toward high expectations.\n\nStudents receive timely feedback that guides their progress and fosters independence in learning.\n\nEducators implement flexible supports that promote both engagement and autonomy, ensuring students are both supported and empowered in their learning journey.\n\nAssessment and instruction are seamlessly integrated, making learning more transparent, equitable, and effective for multilingual learners.\n\nBy participating in both workshops, educators will gain a holistic approach to designing instruction and assessment that not only makes learning accessible but also ensures that students have the tools and confidence to monitor their progress, set goals, and take ownership of their academic growth.\n\nTo receive credit for attending these two workshops, choose one of the assignments below.\n\nAssignment C: Designing Multi-Modal Instruction to Meet the Needs of English Learners Reflection Essay OR\n\nAssignment D: Promoting Autonomy through Formative Assessment Practices Reflection Essay\n\nDescription: Write a reflection essay based on the learning from one of the two workshops above, focusing on personal growth, new insights, and how the knowledge gained can be applied to your practice.\n\nLength &amp; Format: A 3-5 page, double-spaced, 12-point font (≈750-1250 words) Word document or PDF.\n\nContent:\n\nIntroduction: Briefly summarize the content of the workshop, including foundational principles, enduring understandings, and main takeaways.\n\nReflection:\n\nWhat was the most valuable content from the workshop?\n\nWhat did you learn about the topic, yourself, or your professional practice?\n\nHow will you apply what you learned in future work or personal development?\n\nConclusion: Summarize your overall learning experience.\n\nTITLE: Building Capacity to Bridge Content and Language\n\nCOURSE DESCRIPTION:\n\nThis course is designed to help educators explore the intersections between grade-level content standards and the components of an English Language Development framework. Participants will learn to connect content learning with language development by examining key aspects of the framework. The course will focus on: 1) identifying Key Language Uses associated with summative assessments, 2) using Language Functions &amp; Language Features to establish clear and effective learning goals, and 3) analyzing student work using Proficiency Level Descriptors. Educators will develop practical strategies for integrating language development into content areas to enhance learning for multilingual learners.\n\nSTUDENT LEARNING OUTCOMES:\n\nUpon completion of this course, the student will be able to:\n\nIdentify key components of the framework and their relationship to grade-level content standards.\n\nUse Language Functions &amp; Language Features to establish language learning goals that align with content standards.\n\nAnalyze student work using Proficiency Level Descriptors to assess language development.\n\nTITLE: Empowering Newcomers for Academic Success\n\nCOURSE DESCRIPTION:\n\nThis session equips educators with practical strategies to support newcomers in academic classrooms. Participants will explore elements of effective scaffolding, learn to make grade-level content accessible, develop tailored language supports, and leverage students’ strengths, interests, and preferences. Through hands-on activities and collaboration, participants will design scaffolded lessons that integrate content and language learning while fostering a supportive and inclusive environment for newcomers.\n\nSTUDENT LEARNING OUTCOMES:\n\nUpon completion of this course, the student will be able to:\n\nDescribe elements of effective scaffolding for newcomers.\n\nIdentify strategies to make grade-level content accessible for multilingual learners.\n\nDevelop lessons that scaffold content and language development.\n\nIdentify ways to leverage students’ strengths, interests, and preferences to enhance learning.\n\nTITLE: Designing Multi-Modal Instruction to Meet the Needs of English Learners\n\nCOURSE DESCRIPTION:\n\nThis course explores the principles of Universal Design for Learning (UDL) as a framework for creating differentiated instruction that supports English learners in accessing and engaging with grade-level content. Participants will learn how to apply UDL principles to design and implement instruction that is flexible, inclusive, and tailored to the diverse needs, interests, and strengths of all learners. Through practical strategies and hands-on activities, participants will develop instruction that differentiates how content is presented, how learners process new content and make-meaning, and how they demonstrate their understanding. Application of the principles of UDL will ensure that differentiated instruction considers students’ readiness, interests, learning preferences, and English language proficiency.",
