    "content": "File: chunk_27.mp4\nTitle: chunk_27\n\nContent: when we're using the other language. And so once again, reminder, it's not that it's wrong, it's that they're applying the rules of one language to the language that they're learning. So I love seeing how it's done in your language. Let me show you what it looks like in English, is really what we need to respond there. Posting those multilingual vocabulary charts, we spoke a little bit about that with images, with definitions, with examples, non-examples, also very powerful. And then affirming their identities, listen, ask questions, and always recognize that the opinions that they bring to their classroom stem from their culture, stem from where they come from, how they were raised, what values they truly, as I always say, hold close to their hearts. All right, so we've got about six minutes left according to my time. I'm gonna ask you to take part in your very last elevation activity tonight. This is called hanging hashtags. And a hashtag, as you all know, and I should have written an example there, I apologize, but a hashtag, our kids are very familiar with it. And so I'm gonna ask you all to write a hashtag that describes one thing that you learned today, or maybe you confirmed it, or maybe it was an aha moment, you just realized that today. And I'm gonna write mine here in the chat. Hashtag I am. Dayton, I love that one. Hashtag respect is the welcoming door. Wordless books rock, that's great. Language is fluid, most definitely.\n",
