    "id": "7d8a15c1-1f5c-4cae-b0fb-202fdcaf26cf",
    "title": "chunk_25",
    "content": "File: chunk_25.mp4\nTitle: chunk_25\n\nContent: Thank you, Cindy. Coming in. You're cooking your family and dog. Carolyn, you've got quite a situation matters. Absolutely, absolutely. And I know responses are going to continue. So, yes, I could be cooking my family and my dog. But what I really want our students to know is that, yes, punctuation does matter. And so we do need those those commas. But when you compare, you let kids compare and have fun with language that strengthens their language development. It improves their comprehension. It allows them to start thinking flexibly about how to use language and what language means in context there. So thanks to those of you who engaged in this one. Some other suggestions, and I'm going to start talking New York City fast, but some other suggestions is exposing your English learners to idioms, to poetry, to sayings. Yet every time, just as I did, you dictate a line of poetry or any figurative language or any of the idioms that we were all using, proverbs, you're introducing the English learners not only to the language, but to the culture behind that. They begin to appreciate the sociocultural aspect of language. They start to learn about morphology. They start to listen to historical aspects of language. Why do we no longer say thy? And what happened? When did that change? So reflect a little on this. We would have taken part in another quick activity, but I don't think we have time.\n",
