  {
    "id": "35059f6a-6567-4f44-8eb6-0693fe4ca9a4",
    "title": "chunk_24",
    "content": "File: chunk_24.mp4\nTitle: chunk_24\n\nContent: And it's like, oh my goodness, I'm so sorry. I was thinking in Spanish and I just went off on Spanish. But it happens to our kids as well. So as your students continue to learn new content and they share all that they bring, their culture and their language, our role is to bring it all together. We need to bring their linguistic worlds together. And there's two ways that you can. These are just two. There's plenty, but I'm going to go into two. Two ways that you can do that. Implementing strategies that increase the metalinguistic awareness and ensuring that they make cross-linguistic connections between their languages. So metalinguistic awareness, it didn't pop up, but it's the ability to think and discuss language. How are they the same? How are they different? And then those cross-linguistic connections, our kids are understanding and making use of the differences and similarities between the language. So they think, and then they start to implement. Okay, so we have enough time for this activity. One form of metalinguistic awareness is understanding that words and sentences can have more than one meaning. It happens in vocabulary, simple words. It happens sometimes in a phrase, in a sentence, right? Listen, write, correct is another elevation activity that you can use. And you don't need to have elevation to be able to use it, but it's gonna help your English learners understand ambiguity in language. And it's also gonna help them understand how language works. So we're gonna practice. I'm gonna say a statement. You're gonna go ahead and write it in the chat. I find inspiration in cooking, my family, and my dog. I find inspiration in cooking, my family, and my dog.\n",
