  {
    "id": "125c02e6-6841-4d58-9652-a7593b0c0e6f",
    "title": "chunk_19",
    "content": "File: chunk_19.mp4\nTitle: chunk_19\n\nContent: perspectives of others is through storytelling using authentic texts in our classroom and some of us I saw somebody and I don't remember who it was she said she had no books in her room I'm not exactly sure why that stood out to me in context I think there was more to that but um store even oral storytelling kids can scribe to each other kids can scribe and the other one can draw that interpretation of what they're hearing stories represent culture and when we maintain those oral traditions when our kids feel comfortable coming into our classrooms and being able to share a story that they heard that was read to them the night before they start to share their fears they start to share their dreams they start to share little snippets of life at home right life lessons that perhaps are discussed at home or they've even learned and when we start to preserve a group's heritage and customs and we can engage our learners then in making cultural comparisons another way I guess is through wordless books wordless books I tend to see them only in our elementary grades usually our k-2 pre-k-2 I don't usually see them in our older grades and I always wondered why not especially for our English learners because at any age wordless books not only allow our kids to use all of their linguistic repertoires but they can also use their learned vocabulary they can also work with a peer and develop that language they can create a bilingual story that they can present later on they can use idioms our older ones can use figure we go back to figurative language idioms metaphors personification\n",
