  {
    "id": "d71e2f0f-72bf-4b54-bb02-394557d537fb",
    "title": "chunk_21",
    "content": "File: chunk_21.mp4\nTitle: chunk_21\n\nContent: here, who helped them along the way, what have they learned. And if we just give them that opportunity, they have so much to share with us. So one way of uniting their cultural identities with their linguistic identities is to involve them in an activity where they can reflect a little bit and describe an important event that might have changed the way they react to the world, might have changed them personally. And we have an activity called My Life Map. It was adapted from Dr. Jose Medina. And it is a visual representation of important events that have changed us. But I'm going to show you how to perhaps adapt that. If you've seen this before, take note. If you have not, you will have an opportunity in just a bit to download the template and use it. OK, there we go. All right, so what you see before me is My Life Map. This is a life map prior to the age of 17, events that impacted my life, my future at that moment, and shaped the values that I have today. I could present these orally. I could present them in written form. But it's an impactful way to show But it's an impactful way in which our students can tell us about an obstacle they faced, a value they hold close to their heart, a special person maybe in their lives who impacted them, an experience that shaped them. I mean, this is the true intent of the map. But even then, quite adaptable. We could even take this map from the moment that I am approaching, for example, high school graduation. And what are my dreams in two years, in four years, in five years? So very adaptable.\n",
    "document_type": "mp4",
    "file_name": "chunk_21.mp4",
    "file_path": "1764879191547_chunk_21.mp4",
    "metadata": {
      "size": 6007346,
      "processed_at": "2025-12-04T20:13:27.551Z",
