  {
    "id": "48f64381-3506-49ad-a934-105f61da2c7a",
    "title": "chunk_20",
    "content": "File: chunk_20.mp4\nTitle: chunk_20\n\nContent: There's so much that our older kids can write about and do with language when you put a wordless book in their hands. They can use hypothetical questions, for example. They can write poetry. I mean, the list goes on and on. Then you can also use a simple image, a historical image, a moment in time captured in a photo or in a painting, and it provides them with the opportunity to tell you what they know. Maybe it relates to something they've experienced before. Maybe they've read something or heard something about that topic in the past. But it gives you the opportunity to let them tell you what they know and what perhaps they're still wondering about. And then global discussions. I think the most beautiful thing about global discussions is that it starts to bring families into the conversations as well. What are they hearing as a topic, as it relates to the country where they're from? And I'll take just our whole electoral process now not to go into politics at all. But I am interested. What are they saying? I just came back from Puerto Rico, as a matter of fact, and so I was reading up. What are they saying about our politics here? I want to know. That's where I'm from. That's where my ties are. So bringing families into that conversation so that kids can come and talk about that amongst themselves and begin to compare is powerful, especially for our older ones. And so I remind you that they all have stories and we can never underestimate the power of their own story. It's the chance for them to tell you where they come from, how they got here.\n",
    "document_type": "mp4",
    "file_name": "chunk_20.mp4",
    "file_path": "1764879174606_chunk_20.mp4",
    "metadata": {
      "size": 5585131,
      "processed_at": "2025-12-04T20:13:10.578Z",
      "original_word_count": 289
