  {
    "id": "24d6dd74-78d3-4e51-a48d-cf218992d053",
    "title": "chunk_22",
    "content": "File: chunk_22.mp4\nTitle: chunk_22\n\nContent: We could also look at it through the lens of a content area. This is my math life map. And about two years ago, I had to do a session in math. And all I kept thinking was, you know, I used to like math. When did I stop liking math? And as I went through my own life map, I understood the relationship that I had with this content. As I started out in elementary, moving into algebra, then loved, I absolutely loved geometry. But then I was required to take statistics to graduate as a teacher. And that was just, that broke me. That was it, I was done. But think about it, if you're a content area teacher, right? If you ask your students, what has been your relationship with this topic? Very insightful. And now you can all see why I don't help my granddaughter with math homework. We also could do a language life map. This is my own language life map. Maybe you wanna know where, when, how do your students learn all of the languages that they know? And rather than having them verbally tell you, maybe you could ask them to write you a life map that walks you through not only the languages that were dominant at that time, but what experiences, what was happening in their lives as they were acquiring this language or perhaps learning a new one, tapping into one that they already had that they had to dust off. It's gonna give you insight into their language dominance. It's going to tell you a little bit more about your kids and the experiences that they've had, the languages that they speak, because those are resources that are walking into your classroom. We talked about cognates. Imagine a student when I did know Italian.\n",
