  {
    "id": "937d1ec4-7be8-44a1-abbf-74a5d9397c92",
    "title": "chunk_18",
    "content": "File: chunk_18.mp4\nTitle: chunk_18\n\nContent: start saying some of these, your kids will turn around. I guarantee you, your kids are going to turn around and say, Oh, my house, they say this. So they are rich in figurative language, maybe not in English, but they're rich in figurative language. And, and when you stop and think about it, it's pretty abstract. And it's difficult sometimes for our language learners to grasp. So as you hear and you share figurative language, make sure that all of your kids can connect with it. Because there will be some sort of words of wisdom, we'll call them, where they can connect their experiences and their past and their culture with the language that they're learning. Because obviously, we all know how important it is for them to understand figurative language as they're going through their reading. So I always say language and culture are intertwined. In an environment where kids will feel safe and respected and valued and appreciated, multilingual learners know that they can use their full linguistic repertoire. They don't even have to ask. A lot of you did it in your chat. Nobody asked me, can I write this in Spanish, Spanglish in my language? No, it was you, right? And we need to make sure that our kids feel that comfortable in using the language that they bring. We also know that they feel comfortable presenting different perspectives. And they start thinking about relationships. We start noticing similarities, not only in language, but similarities in maybe upbringing, similarities in experiences, similarities in values. And they start to affirm their identities. So one way that we can help our students increase their language development and knowledge of their culture, and learn about their culture, is to use the language that they bring with them. And that is to use the language that they bring with them.\n",
