    "id": "b4dc774b-ce8a-4541-a8ed-e5497fe7c168",
    "title": "chunk_13",
    "content": "File: chunk_13.mp4\nTitle: chunk_13\n\nContent: I was waiting for that one. All right, so regardless, as those answers continue to come in, regardless of where our students are from, and we can never generalize and think that we, that they all share the same linguistic repertoire. Because as you just noticed, four different images, and check out how many different ways each one of these very common items are called so differently. So for me to tell the child, you don't say pajita, you say absorbente. At that point, I have taken away any feelings that he might've felt of being appreciated, of being valued, of bringing his language, his culture into the classroom. All right, I'm going to move on. This was something that I walked into a classroom a couple of years ago, and they had an entire wall dedicated to languages that our kids brought into the classroom. One of them was those regional variations. How do you say it in your country? Maybe it's just how do you say it at home, right? Because some of our kids are born here in the United States, but the language that they speak at home, which also has to be honored, is what they bring into our classrooms. So you could most definitely have a wall with regional variations. You could also have a wall in which they start to work with language.\n",
