    "id": "9f118a89-11f7-4b27-9ae8-ed0b06ffa148",
    "title": "chunk_14",
    "content": "File: chunk_14.mp4\nTitle: chunk_14\n\nContent: I call it playing with language, right? So I'm from New York, I said, I learned use. More than one person, use. Use are all in my way. Obviously, I don't say it like that anymore. I've been in Florida now for a very long time. But I also don't say y'all like a lot of my Southerner friends do. For social and formal, you might hear you guys. And as we continue to have our kids engage with language and think about the purpose that they're using language, think about, someone told me once, reading the room. Who are they speaking to? What are they using language for? What's the purpose? I could still say use when I'm with my sisters, but I would never say use when I'm in elevation working or presenting a session such as this. So again, analyzing language, allowing kids to bring that language in and then working through, how would you say that in a different environment, in a different setting for a different reason? I'm gonna stop for a moment and just go quickly through the chat. Autobus and guagua. Lunch, almuerzo. There's plenty of them. Ahora vamos a la alfombra. Yeah, Heather used a beautiful word, invalided. That's how they feel, invalidated. When I tell them, we don't use that word here. Okay. So you can do this in any class. It doesn't matter what you're teaching, but in classes such as math or science, create a similar chart. And maybe you wanna use cognates in that chart as well. So they can begin to analyze language and the similarities between the languages that they bring.\n",
