  {
    "id": "7d70a1f1-2ebd-499d-b51c-911282773011",
    "title": "chunk_15",
    "content": "File: chunk_15.mp4\nTitle: chunk_15\n\nContent: All right, so I'm going to ask someone, Amy, would you like to pick someone to read our quote? Erica? You shared a little bit from Pittsburgh, Yins, went to high school in Johnstown, so Erica, you want to share this? Oh, okay. Yeah, thanks. Yins, want to read this? Sorry. A natural language is the archive where the experiences, knowledge, and beliefs of a community are stored. All right, so take a moment to visualize this quote, an archive. I envision this cabinet, this filing cabinet with this endless drawer that I'm pulling out, full of experiences, and stories, and knowledge, and beliefs that our kids hold so close to their hearts. And when you look, when you think about it that way, and you envision it that way, you have to start to think about all the possibilities that we have, all of the funds of knowledge that are coming into our classroom every single day, and maybe we're not really tapping into the way we should. So, let's take a look at another elevation activity. This one's called Go Figure, Figura Fin, and it engages students in figurative language through proverbs and idioms. And so, when I was much younger, my dad always used to tell me, Dime con quien andas y te diré quien eres. He didn't have to say anything else. I showed up, again, growing up in New York, I'd walk home from school, and if he saw me with anybody new, that's all he had to say. And that put me in check, making me realize, okay, hmm, what he was trying to tell me was that people with similar interests or characteristics tend to spend time together, right? And we pick up on each other's habits.\n",
