  {
    "id": "53ec8eb4-2872-4d7f-bd68-7fa4cec5ff3c",
    "title": "chunk_17",
    "content": "File: chunk_17.mp4\nTitle: chunk_17\n\nContent: You get what you get, and don't throw it. Let's see what else we've got. Es mejor precaver que tener que prevenir. You can't compare apples and oranges. Some of these are translated, and they translate similarly, we'll say, in some languages, but the majority of them don't, because they bring down generations of wisdom with them. Things were different when these proverbs were perhaps begun and stated, and then they became, you know, I'm going to say a common phrase in some of our homes, like mine did. But they're passed on from generation to generation, and there's experiences and stories, as my father always reminded me, attached to them. And so many of them can be found in our literature, and I saw a couple there as I was reading. Many of them can be found in our literature. I got stuck for a minute, because Valerie says, life in the pursuit of happiness with a glass of wine. I don't know that I'd use that one in the classroom as an example, but thank you. And also, I went to a session a couple years ago, and there was this whole analysis on how proverbs actually connect to our real world. They connect to nature. They connect to science and social studies curriculum. And when you start to pull these apart, you realize that that is definitely the case. A lot of times you can read these in almanacs. You can read them throughout the years in a lot of more of our classic literature. They all represent a learning experience for our multilingual learners. So if you go through this list in the chat, and we...\n",
