    "content": "File: chunk_11.mp4\nTitle: chunk_11\n\nContent: Whether we're doing Spanish, English, or any language, that's not the way it's said. Because I can tell you that early on in my career, as a Spanish dual language admin, I can tell you that I realized that the message that I was sending was that the knowledge that they brought into our classrooms and into our schools wasn't welcomed. By marking them incorrectly, I was diminishing the importance of their prior knowledge. What they had been taught before, be it from a family member, a former teacher, their environment, I was telling them, that's not done. We don't say it like that. So I want us to think about that as we take a moment. I did see we had a lot of people who speak Spanish, so I'm going to play this game with our Spanish speakers, but I want you to consider that all languages have regional variations. You can be in the same country, just as we did with soda and pop, and even in a language such as Spanish that is spoken in so many languages around the world, even in the same country, you're going to see variations. So I'm going to ask our Spanish speakers to get ready, put your fingers on that keyboard, and when the image comes up, and those of us, soda pop was a formal term, ooh, Robert, I'm going to have to investigate that. When the image comes up, I want you to go ahead and tell me, what do you call it? What do we call this? Besides popcorn.\n",
