ct. work to meet the needs of your specific situation, including this one. This newcomer series we do offer is a full-day session. Today, we're going to do three of the sections that we generally do in the full-day workshop, but there are more. Today we'll be talking about who are our newcomers, creating a safe and thriving environment, and instructional strategies. Let's just dive right in. One thing to remember, I think we all probably know this, but it's a very big puzzle. Working with newcomers, working with any student, there's a lot of things that go into it. There are no magic bullets with working with newcomers. This is just one piece of a much larger puzzle. A little bit about me. My name is Ryan Rumpf. I've been in education for 21 years. I've worked as a teacher and coordinator in public schools in Missouri and Colorado. I also worked in an international school in Vietnam. I was a state director of English development in Missouri, and most recently I served as a principal of a newcomer center in Kansas City called the Global Academy, and now I work with YSEPs. I am bilingual, although I don't think I speak any language that well. I do have my doctorate in educational leadership. Something a little different, or not different, but unique about me is that I did grow up in rural Missouri where there wasn't much diversity. I think that's one of the reasons why I'm so engaged and fascinated by all of the different cultures around the world now, because I didn't see that much growing up. This is an example of a trading card. Trading cards are great ways to get to know your students. You can use these in a variety of ways in the classroom so they can get to know each other. One idea that you may share is this is a welcoming ritual. Rituals are very important for students as they start their journey in your school. In this example, there's a large map right in the middle of the hallway where every new student, as they come in, can put their trading card up on the wall and they can make connections to other students who may be from that area or just see the diversity that's in your school. In this particular example, the student who's putting the card there now made a quick connection with someone in her class, found another student from Puerto Rico, and she could make an immediate connection to that student. You only have one time to make a first impression. What you do whenever the students first arrive in your building really matters and helps create this sense of community. Other ideas to get to know your newcomers. There's an I Am From poem that we generally do in the full-day workshop. TikTok, Flipgrid, although I hear Flipgrid has gone away for anybody that doesn't use Microsoft or Microsoft Teams, but there's some other cool online things you can do. The trading cards, as I mentioned before, social media type posts, or even a story, a dual-language My Journey story is a very powerful way for students to introduce themselves to you, to the community, but also to help them process maybe some of the things that happened on their journey. Getting to know your newcomers. Who are our newcomers? I think there's a range of people in this webinar. The term newcomer I've come to realize means different things in different school districts. It's an umbrella term that includes a wide variety of students. It's a very diverse mix, and no one newcomer is alike, so grouping them all together is interesting. Oftentimes, newcomers are any students who recently immigrated to the United States, but there are a lot of countries around the world where English is the official language. For example, we had a student from Guyana. The teacher really wanted them to come to a newcomer center, but the student's English was their native language. How do I say that right? Some multilingual students may need help integrating into life and school in the United States. School looks differently in different parts of the world. Big yellow school buses aren't always the norm, so helping them adjust to life and school in your area is important. Some students may have access to a newcomer center, and some students may be mainstreamed with ELD support. It's very important that you also think about the structures available to help students in their journey. That's very blurry, but hopefully we can still see. Is that all right, Margie? You can see it. This chart shows growth patterns. There was some research, especially around when ESSA was coming out. It just seemed like a few years ago, but it's been a while now, around growth rates. Newcomers in particular studied third, fifth, and eighth graders. Newcomers had tremendous growth in their first few years while in the States compared to students who started higher on the scale. Newcomers tend to come, and they grow very rapidly and then start to level off. They say that lower is faster, higher is slower, and that is true for grade and proficiency level. Students who are in lower grades and lower proficiency level grow faster than students who are in the higher grades and the higher proficiency levels. I will tell you that the range for newcomers is much bigger than the other grade levels and other proficiency levels, which means that what schools do really matters. How they address the needs of newcomers really matters. If you do a good job at addressing their needs, you're going to give them a nice jump start in their English journey. Ryan, there's a question in the chat that says, it seems like the older newcomer scores in the SCRAF actually went down. Is that right? Well, that would be year one, two, three, four. It looks like from year three to four, the newcomer scores went down. This would be an eighth grade student, and that would have happened at the high school age. Yes, but generally what we should look in this first year, you see how it just shoots up there as compared to students who started, say, at a level two, a level three, or a level four. Can you all hear me okay? Yes. All right. Okay, so again, the growth rates for newcomers can be very high, and they can grow very quickly, but we need to pay attention to the need, but also a reminder about having labels. Labels limit learning. If we lump them all together into one group, we're simplifying the complexity of each child. So it's really important to get to know the student, their family experiences, schooling history, and know them on an individual level rather than lumping them all together under the term newcomer. So that leads us to creating a safe and thriving environment. This is a place attachment theory has been around for a while, but this PPP framework, I'm very intrigued by it. If you're not familiar with it, Brian Mann out of the University of Kansas has put together this framework as it relates to immigrants and refugees and what their experience is like in school and in the community. The three P's are the person, the psychological processes, and the place. So this framework gives us kind of guidance or a background to think about all the different ways that folks make an attachment to where they are and to where they're being. I think about the school being like a second home or school feeling like a second home or even individual classrooms within that school or even the school library being a place that feels like they belong. And there's things that we can do to welcome that, to take advantage of that feeling, and there's also things that we can do to push students away. Let's look at the first scenario or the first component of the framework, the person. The person is really reflecting the cultural and historical recognition of groups and individual care or personal development. Take a look at this poster. This is just one example. This is something that you can buy on Amazon or what have you that shows the greatest mathematicians of all time. And here we have a list of French and Greek, British mathematicians that really made an impact on the world. But if you have students from Syria, for example, where they may have a different set of historical figures that really made contributions to math, for example, and I'm going to butcher the names because I can't ask if I'm saying it right. But Al-Khwarizmi was the father of algebra. We can thank this man for the classes we had to take in school. And he made very, very important contributions to the world of math. Al-Biruni, we can thank him for trigonometry and spherical geometry. Al-Qaraji, polynomials and factorization. All this to say that the images, the things that we put up, the cultural groups that we recognize in schools matters. So if you have students that they may have a connection to some people who are not generally represented in the posters and things we can find here. That stuff matters. So some strategies for individual care. Before the first day, if you can have an enrollment center or a welcome center to collect information, to give a school tour that helps prepare the students and the staff for their time, for the beginning of their journey. Especially how to pronounce a student's name, understanding their English proficiency level. School tours are very important. The kids are generally pretty nervous to start school. Show them the important places. Show them where their classroom is, where the office is. Some important people, especially those that speak their language. Introduce them to their teacher. Those are really good ways to start the year off right or to start their time off right in your school. And when they get there, maybe have a peer mentor. One that speaks the language of the student if possible. But if not, find a willing and able peer mentor. An old friend of mine who's familiar with the work that I do, her daughter found out that she was in first grade at the time, found out that a new student would be joining the class. And they were just beginning their journey in English. That child spoke Spanish. Whenever I went over to her house, she was on Duolingo learning some Spanish words because she was going to have a new classmate. Wanted to be able to help. I also recommend giving the gift of time. So give the students some time to adjust. We don't have to give them a, you know, just because a test is scheduled doesn't mean they have to take it on the first day. And some students need time for breaks, you know. Spending all day, a full day in a classroom where English is the language of instruction and you're just beginning your journey is tiring. So give some time for breaks, especially if there is someone who's there that speaks the language. And set small goals. Goal setting can happen with newcomers as far as language, participation, academics. And these things are all super important because you've got to give attention to these things. Otherwise, the rest won't really matter. So in the chat, what are some things that you all do to welcome students and to get to know them? That's awesome, Luce. You're on call all day in case they need something. I'm sure that's taxing on you. Meet them a week before and give them a tour. Connect the students with other school tours and a buddy student. It's great. Picture books. I love that idea. Giving students pictures of important places in the room or in the school. Finding books with their cultural background and language. More on that later, yes. Someone's also saying a snack social or like a lunchtime, you know, like a particular gathering. Yeah, that's a great idea. Home country projects. Flagboard. Yeah, more on some of these later. All About Me Kahoot. Oh, man, I haven't played a kahoot in a while. A school tour for parents. That's great. All right, thank you all. So the next part of the framework is the psychological processes. These are intended to foster happiness, pride, and a love of the place. As far as cognition, we're talking about building memories, knowledge, schemas, and specifically around behavior, addressing the feelings of the place, and how to address the feelings of the place. And specifically around behavior, addressing the feelings of displacement and homesickness, because those are very real. We don't know how the students are feeling about their transition to life in school in the U.S. until we've sat down and talked with them. Some of the students are thrilled and couldn't be happier to be here. Some students didn't want to leave. And you have a range of things in between those two. So it's really important that we help the students process those, because that leads to other things. Having a counselor and a trauma therapist, a trained trauma therapist, to be available, and we didn't need them for everyone, but the ones that we did need them for was very, very valuable to help with their transition to school here. Let's take a quick moment to just read through this vignette. It's about a Syrian student who fled Syria during the civil war. And this is from a soon-to-be-published book by Brian Mann, as I was talking about. Just take a minute and read through this. All right. All right. So this, it's a controversial topic, prayer in schools, right? Now, students are, of course, allowed to pray. The school just can't mandate it.",
