    "content": "WEBVTT\r\n\r\n1\r\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000\r\nRyan Rumpf: The rosemary.\r\n\r\n2\r\n00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:46.230\r\nYvonne Williams: We have about 2 min before we begin. We wanna make sure we start on time. So we're gonna wait for all the colleagues across the us to join us. In the meantime, please keep adding.\r\n\r\n3\r\n00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:52.099\r\nYvonne Williams: where you're from, what you teach anything you'd like to share in the chat while we wait.\r\n\r\n4\r\n00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:34.070\r\nYvonne Williams: I'm happy to see Nevada. We hardly see you all, Leslie. Thank you for joining us.\r\n\r\n5\r\n00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.730\r\nRyan Rumpf: There's 1 from North Dakota.\r\n\r\n6\r\n00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.280\r\nYvonne Williams: Oh, wonderful!\r\n\r\n7\r\n00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:39.370\r\nRyan Rumpf: Yeah.\r\n\r\n8\r\n00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:40.829\r\nYvonne Williams: New York.\r\n\r\n9\r\n00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:43.970\r\nAmy King: San Francisco, couple from Texas.\r\n\r\n10\r\n00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:46.530\r\nYvonne Williams: Wonderful welcome Texas.\r\n\r\n11\r\n00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:51.090\r\nAmy King: Iowa.\r\n\r\n12\r\n00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:52.900\r\nYvonne Williams: Any from California yet.\r\n\r\n13\r\n00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.120\r\nAmy King: Yeah. One from San Francisco.\r\n\r\n14\r\n00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.309\r\nYvonne Williams: Oh, that's right, San Francisco.\r\n\r\n15\r\n00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:02.969\r\nYvonne Williams: I was in Reno, Nevada, so I feel San Francisco is like part of Nevada. I spent so much time. There.\r\n\r\n16\r\n00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.110\r\nYvonne Williams: Hi, Erin, nice to see you.\r\n\r\n17\r\n00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:07.460\r\nRyan Rumpf: Principle in here.\r\n\r\n18\r\n00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:08.959\r\nYvonne Williams: Wonderful.\r\n\r\n19\r\n00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:10.879\r\nYvonne Williams: That's a plus\r\n\r\n20\r\n00:02:12.169 --> 00:02:15.749\r\nYvonne Williams: to those of you who are administrators. Jamie. I think.\r\n\r\n21\r\n00:02:16.209 --> 00:02:23.329\r\nYvonne Williams: Margie, I'm not sure if you have the link. We have a free webinar specific for administrators that is coming up.\r\n\r\n22\r\n00:02:24.679 --> 00:02:28.519\r\nYvonne Williams: Amy, I mean Margie, if you want to send it to Jamie.\r\n\r\n23\r\n00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:32.670\r\nMargie Marrero: Look for that. Yes, I'll look for that, and I can share it.\r\n\r\n24\r\n00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.720\r\nYvonne Williams: Might be good to have one on newcomers. Yeah, you're welcome.\r\n\r\n25\r\n00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:43.271\r\nYvonne Williams: Oh, there's Pasadena Yay, Gloria.\r\n\r\n26\r\n00:02:47.250 --> 00:03:04.319\r\nRyan Rumpf: All right. It's 5 o'clock, so I think we'll go ahead and get started. It's nice to see such a variety of folks here, different roles from across the country, all joining us for the 1st tips and dips on newcomers.\r\n\r\n27\r\n00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:13.249\r\nRyan Rumpf: I think, from what we're hearing and seeing, a lot of districts are seeing. An increase in the student population\r\n\r\n28\r\n00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.700\r\nRyan Rumpf: and a lot of interest in\r\n\r\n29\r\n00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:27.550\r\nRyan Rumpf: designing, you know, an effective system and strategies for helping them. My name's Ryan Rumpf. I'm the lead newcomer coach at Yseps.\r\n\r\n30\r\n00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:39.440\r\nRyan Rumpf: For those of you who don't know. Yceps began about 14 years ago.\r\n\r\n31\r\n00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:42.869\r\nRyan Rumpf: and gradually got.\r\n\r\n32\r\n00:03:48.030 --> 00:03:48.799\r\nYvonne Williams: Ryan, you're muted.\r\n\r\n33\r\n00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.369\r\nMargie Marrero: Got muted for some reason right.\r\n\r\n34\r\n00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.620\r\nYvonne Williams: This time it wasn't me, Brian.\r\n\r\n35\r\n00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:55.694\r\nMargie Marrero: Into that, either. I don't know what happened.\r\n\r\n36\r\n00:03:56.930 --> 00:04:02.350\r\nRyan Rumpf: Anyway. Care coaching started in about in 2021,\r\n\r\n37\r\n00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:16.380\r\nRyan Rumpf: and there's a range of offerings for in-person and webinars that that Yseps can do to support your district. It's very customized work to meet the needs of of your specific situation\r\n\r\n38\r\n00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:23.720\r\nRyan Rumpf: and including this one. So this newcomer series is a we do offer as a full day session.\r\n\r\n39\r\n00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:31.009\r\nRyan Rumpf: Today, we're going to do 3 of the sections that we generally do in the full day workshop. But just there are more.\r\n\r\n40\r\n00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:38.139\r\nRyan Rumpf: And today we'll be talking about who are our newcomers and creating a safe and thriving environment\r\n\r\n41\r\n00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.590\r\nRyan Rumpf: and instructional strategies.\r\n\r\n42\r\n00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:43.319\r\nRyan Rumpf: And so let's just dive right in\r\n\r\n43\r\n00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:59.460\r\nRyan Rumpf: one thing to remember. I think we all probably know this, but this is, it's a very big puzzle working with newcomers working with any student. There's a lot of things that go into it. There are no magic bullets with working\r\n\r\n44\r\n00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:06.570\r\nRyan Rumpf: with newcomers. So this is just one piece of an overall of a much larger puzzle\r\n\r\n45\r\n00:05:07.480 --> 00:05:22.989\r\nRyan Rumpf: a little bit about me. My name's Ryan Rumpf, I said. I've been in education for 21 years. I've worked as a teacher and coordinator in public schools in Missouri and Colorado. I also worked in an international school in Vietnam.\r\n\r\n46\r\n00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:31.639\r\nRyan Rumpf: I was a State Director of English development in Missouri, and most recently I served as the principal of a newcomer center in Kansas City\r\n\r\n47\r\n00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:33.569\r\nRyan Rumpf: called the Global Academy.\r\n\r\n48\r\n00:05:33.910 --> 00:05:37.049\r\nRyan Rumpf: And now I work with Yseps\r\n\r\n49\r\n00:05:37.826 --> 00:05:43.663\r\nRyan Rumpf: I am bilingual although I don't think I speak any language that well,\r\n\r\n50\r\n00:05:45.340 --> 00:06:01.399\r\nRyan Rumpf: I have. I do have my my doctorate in in educational leadership, and something a little different or not different, but unique about me is that I did grow up in rural Missouri, where there wasn't much diversity.\r\n\r\n51\r\n00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:02.300\r\nRyan Rumpf: And\r\n\r\n52\r\n00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:13.039\r\nRyan Rumpf: yeah, I think that's 1 of the reasons why I'm so engaged and fascinated by all of the different cultures around the world. Now, because I didn't see that much growing up.\r\n\r\n53\r\n00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:20.610\r\nRyan Rumpf: This is an example of a trading card. Trading cards are great ways to get to know your students.\r\n\r\n54\r\n00:06:21.130 --> 00:06:31.860\r\nRyan Rumpf: and you can use these in a variety of ways in the classroom, so they can get to know each other. One idea that that you may share is\r\n\r\n55\r\n00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:41.300\r\nRyan Rumpf: this is a welcoming ritual. Rituals are very important for students as you as they start their journey in your school.\r\n\r\n56\r\n00:06:41.420 --> 00:06:48.759\r\nRyan Rumpf: In this example there's a large map right in the middle of the hallway, where every new student, as they come in.\r\n\r\n57\r\n00:06:48.920 --> 00:06:58.199\r\nRyan Rumpf: can put their trading card up on the wall, and they can make connections to other students who may be from that area, or just see\r\n\r\n58\r\n00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:10.659\r\nRyan Rumpf: the diversity that's in your school. In this particular example, the student who's putting the card there now made a quick connection with someone in her class found another student from Puerto Rico.\r\n\r\n59\r\n00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:15.490\r\nRyan Rumpf: and she could make an immediate connection to that student.\r\n\r\n60\r\n00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:29.970\r\nRyan Rumpf: These you only have one time to make a 1st impression. So what you do whenever the students 1st arrive in your building really matters and and help create this sense of community.\r\n\r\n61\r\n00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:49.470\r\nRyan Rumpf: Other ideas to get to know your newcomers. There's an I am from poem that we generally do in the full day workshop, tick, tock, flipgrid, although I hear Flipgrid has gone away for anybody that doesn't use Microsoft or Microsoft teams.\r\n\r\n62\r\n00:07:49.620 --> 00:08:00.779\r\nRyan Rumpf: But there's some other cool online things you can do. The trading cards. As I mentioned before social media type posts, or\r\n\r\n63\r\n00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:07.030\r\nRyan Rumpf: even a story, a dual language. My journey story\r\n\r\n64\r\n00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:10.450\r\nRyan Rumpf: is a very powerful way for students to\r\n\r\n65\r\n00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:17.770\r\nRyan Rumpf: introduce themselves to to you to the community, but also to help process help them process maybe\r\n\r\n66\r\n00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:21.029\r\nRyan Rumpf: some of the things that happened on their journey.\r\n\r\n67\r\n00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:27.509\r\nRyan Rumpf: But getting to know your newcomers. So who? Who are our newcomers?\r\n\r\n68\r\n00:08:28.094 --> 00:08:34.000\r\nRyan Rumpf: I think there's a range of range of people in this webinar.\r\n\r\n69\r\n00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:43.109\r\nRyan Rumpf: And so the the term newcomer, which I've come to realize means different things in different different school districts.\r\n\r\n70\r\n00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:51.610\r\nRyan Rumpf: It's an umbrella term that includes a wide variety of of students. It's a very diverse mix.\r\n\r\n71\r\n00:08:52.325 --> 00:08:56.019\r\nRyan Rumpf: And no one newcomer is alike.\r\n\r\n72\r\n00:08:56.030 --> 00:09:07.219\r\nRyan Rumpf: So grouping them all together is is interesting. Sometimes newcomers are any students who recently immigrated to the United States.\r\n\r\n73\r\n00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:16.553\r\nRyan Rumpf: But there are a lot of countries around the world where English is. The is in the is the official language. For example,\r\n\r\n74\r\n00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:23.259\r\nRyan Rumpf: we had a student from Guyana. Teacher really wanted them to come to a newcomer center.\r\n\r\n75\r\n00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:25.030\r\nRyan Rumpf: but to the student\r\n\r\n76\r\n00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:27.560\r\nRyan Rumpf: English was their native language. So\r\n\r\n77\r\n00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:31.360\r\nRyan Rumpf: most\r\n\r\n78\r\n00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:41.610\r\nRyan Rumpf: well, how do. How do I say that right? Some multilingual students may need help integrating into life and school in the United States? So school looks differently\r\n\r\n79\r\n00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:44.840\r\nRyan Rumpf: in different parts of the world. You know.\r\n\r\n80\r\n00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:49.469\r\nRyan Rumpf: big yellow school buses aren't always the the norm. So\r\n\r\n81\r\n00:09:50.031 --> 00:09:56.029\r\nRyan Rumpf: helping them adjust to life in school, in in your area is important.\r\n\r\n82\r\n00:09:56.450 --> 00:09:59.750\r\nRyan Rumpf: Some students may have access to a newcomer center.\r\n\r\n83\r\n00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:04.660\r\nRyan Rumpf: and some students may be mainstreamed with eld support.\r\n\r\n84\r\n00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:13.070\r\nRyan Rumpf: And so it's very important that you also think about the structures available to help students in their journey.\r\n\r\n85\r\n00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:18.149\r\nRyan Rumpf: and that's very blurry. But hopefully we can still see.\r\n\r\n86\r\n00:10:18.631 --> 00:10:20.639\r\nRyan Rumpf: Is that all right, Margie?\r\n\r\n87\r\n00:10:21.750 --> 00:10:33.939\r\nRyan Rumpf: You can see it. This chart shows growth patterns. Okay? So there's some. There was some research, especially around when Essa was coming out. It seemed\r\n\r\n88\r\n00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:37.189\r\nRyan Rumpf: just seemed like a few years ago, but it's been a while now.\r\n\r\n89\r\n00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:40.820\r\nRyan Rumpf: Around growth rates and\r\n\r\n90\r\n00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:47.680\r\nRyan Rumpf: newcomers in particular. Here was they studied 3, rd 5, th and 8th graders.\r\n\r\n91\r\n00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:51.530\r\nRyan Rumpf: Newcomers had tremendous growth\r\n\r\n92\r\n00:10:51.560 --> 00:11:02.520\r\nRyan Rumpf: in their 1st few years, while in the, you know, while in the in the States, compared to students who started higher on the scale.\r\n\r\n93\r\n00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:09.449\r\nRyan Rumpf: and so newcomers tend to come, and they grow very rapidly and then start to level off.\r\n\r\n94\r\n00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:14.020\r\nRyan Rumpf: They say that what is it?\r\n\r\n95\r\n00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:17.500\r\nRyan Rumpf: Lower is faster, higher is slower\r\n\r\n96\r\n00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:33.539\r\nRyan Rumpf: and that is true for grade and proficiency level. So students who are lower in lower grades and lower proficiency level grow faster than students who are in the higher grades and the higher proficiency levels.\r\n\r\n97\r\n00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:44.409\r\nRyan Rumpf: Now I will. I will tell you that the range for newcomers is is much bigger than the other the other grade levels and other proficiency levels.\r\n\r\n98\r\n00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:47.430\r\nRyan Rumpf: Which means that what schools do\r\n\r\n99\r\n00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:52.680\r\nRyan Rumpf: really matters like, how they address the needs of newcomers really matters.\r\n\r\n100\r\n00:11:52.710 --> 00:12:00.919\r\nRyan Rumpf: So if you do. If you do a good job at addressing their needs, you're going to give them a nice jumpstart in their English journey.\r\n\r\n101\r\n00:12:01.130 --> 00:12:09.059\r\nYvonne Williams: Ryan. There's a question in the chat that says it seems like the older newcomer scores in this graph actually went down. Is that right?\r\n\r\n102\r\n00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:19.460\r\nRyan Rumpf: Well, that would be year 1, 2, 3, 4, so it looks like from year 3 to 4. The newcomers scores went down.\r\n\r\n103\r\n00:12:19.550 --> 00:12:25.640\r\nRyan Rumpf: This would be an 8th grade student, and that would have happened at the high School age. Yes.\r\n\r\n104\r\n00:12:25.830 --> 00:12:27.819\r\nRyan Rumpf: but generally what\r\n\r\n105\r\n00:12:27.990 --> 00:12:31.349\r\nRyan Rumpf: what we should look in this 1st year.\r\n\r\n106\r\n00:12:31.420 --> 00:12:33.850\r\nRyan Rumpf: You see how it just shoots up there\r\n\r\n107\r\n00:12:34.470 --> 00:12:40.320\r\nRyan Rumpf: as compared to students who started, say, at a level 2, a level 3, or a level 4.\r\n\r\n108\r\n00:12:44.880 --> 00:12:46.549\r\nRyan Rumpf: Can you all hear me? Okay.\r\n\r\n109\r\n00:12:46.780 --> 00:12:47.540\r\nYvonne Williams: Yes.\r\n\r\n110\r\n00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:48.410\r\nRyan Rumpf: All right.\r\n\r\n111\r\n00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:54.065\r\nRyan Rumpf: Okay,\r\n\r\n112\r\n00:12:55.500 --> 00:13:05.080\r\nRyan Rumpf: So again, the growth rates for newcomers can be very high and they can grow very quickly. But we need to do, or, you know, pay attention to the need.\r\n\r\n113\r\n00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:11.549\r\nRyan Rumpf: but also a reminder about having labels so labels limit learning.\r\n\r\n114\r\n00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:18.320\r\nRyan Rumpf: If we lump them all together into one group, you know, we're\r\n\r\n115\r\n00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:31.170\r\nRyan Rumpf: it's we're we're simplifying the complexity of each of each child. So it's really important to get to know the child or the student their family experiences, schooling history.\r\n\r\n116\r\n00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:38.579\r\nRyan Rumpf: and know them on an individual level rather than lumping them all together under the term newcomer.\r\n\r\n117\r\n00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:45.709\r\nRyan Rumpf: So that leads us to creating a safe and thriving environment.\r\n\r\n118\r\n00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:51.849\r\nRyan Rumpf: this is a place. Attachment theory has been around for a while.\r\n\r\n119\r\n00:13:52.656 --> 00:13:57.220\r\nRyan Rumpf: But this Ppp framework. I'm very intrigued by it\r\n\r\n120\r\n00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:15.899\r\nRyan Rumpf: if you're not familiar with it. Brian Mann, out of the University of Kansas, has put together this framework, as it relates to immigrants and refugees, and what their experience is like in school and in the in the community.\r\n\r\n121\r\n00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:22.860\r\nRyan Rumpf: The the 3 P's are the person, the psychological processes, and the place.\r\n\r\n122\r\n00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:24.500\r\nRyan Rumpf: So\r\n\r\n123\r\n00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:42.310\r\nRyan Rumpf: this framework gives us kind of guidance or a background to think about all the different ways that folks make an attachment to where they are and to where they they're being. And I think about, you know, the\r\n\r\n124\r\n00:14:42.590 --> 00:15:09.639\r\nRyan Rumpf: school being like a second home or school, feeling like a second home, or even individual classrooms within that school, or even the school library being a place that feels like that they belong. And there's things that we can do to to welcome, that to take advantage of that feeling. And there's also things that we can do to\r\n\r\n125\r\n00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:12.300\r\nRyan Rumpf: to push students away.\r\n\r\n126\r\n00:15:12.510 --> 00:15:18.989\r\nRyan Rumpf: So let's look at the 1st scenario or the 1st component of the framework. The person.\r\n\r\n127\r\n00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:20.866\r\nRyan Rumpf: another person,\r\n\r\n128\r\n00:15:21.730 --> 00:15:26.240\r\nRyan Rumpf: is really reflecting the cultural and historical recognition of groups.\r\n\r\n129\r\n00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:28.720\r\nRyan Rumpf: an individual care\r\n\r\n130\r\n00:15:28.900 --> 00:15:31.199\r\nRyan Rumpf: or personal development.\r\n\r\n131\r\n00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:43.519\r\nRyan Rumpf: Take a look at this poster. This is just one example. This is something that you can buy on Amazon, or what have you that shows the greatest mathematicians of all time. And here we have\r\n\r\n132\r\n00:15:43.530 --> 00:15:49.410\r\nRyan Rumpf: a list of French and Greek.\r\n\r\n133\r\n00:15:49.600 --> 00:15:54.500\r\nRyan Rumpf: British mathematicians that really made an impact on the world.\r\n\r\n134\r\n00:15:54.670 --> 00:15:57.524\r\nRyan Rumpf: But if you have students from\r\n\r\n135\r\n00:15:58.130 --> 00:15:59.960\r\nRyan Rumpf: Syria, for example.\r\n\r\n136\r\n00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:00.950\r\nRyan Rumpf: where?\r\n\r\n137\r\n00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:09.950\r\nRyan Rumpf: they. They may have a different set of historical figures that really made contributions to math.\r\n\r\n138\r\n00:16:10.310 --> 00:16:11.703\r\nRyan Rumpf: For example,\r\n\r\n139\r\n00:16:12.410 --> 00:16:19.750\r\nRyan Rumpf: and I'm gonna butcher the names, because I can't ask if it's if I'm saying it right. But I'll query queries me\r\n\r\n140\r\n00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:24.799\r\nRyan Rumpf: was the father of algebra. We can thank this man for the classes we had to take in school.\r\n\r\n141\r\n00:16:25.975 --> 00:16:26.960\r\nRyan Rumpf: And\r\n\r\n142\r\n00:16:27.670 --> 00:16:32.750\r\nRyan Rumpf: he made very, very important contributions to the world of math.\r\n\r\n143\r\n00:16:33.458 --> 00:16:38.739\r\nRyan Rumpf: Alberuni. We can thank him for trigonometry and spherical geometry.\r\n\r\n144\r\n00:16:39.831 --> 00:16:43.890\r\nRyan Rumpf: Al Karaji, polynomials and factorization.\r\n\r\n145\r\n00:16:45.400 --> 00:17:05.939\r\nRyan Rumpf: all this to say that you know the the images, the things that we put up, the the cultural groups that we recognize in schools matters. So if you have students that they may have a connection to to some people who are not generally represented\r\n\r\n146\r\n00:17:05.950 --> 00:17:10.029\r\nRyan Rumpf: in the posters and and and things we can find here\r\n\r\n147\r\n00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:12.829\r\nRyan Rumpf: that that stuff matters.\r\n\r\n148\r\n00:17:13.500 --> 00:17:16.659\r\nRyan Rumpf: So some strategies for individual care\r\n\r\n149\r\n00:17:17.299 --> 00:17:21.610\r\nRyan Rumpf: before the 1st day, if you can have a\r\n\r\n150\r\n00:17:21.670 --> 00:17:28.990\r\nRyan Rumpf: an enrollment center or a welcome center to collect information, to give a school tour\r\n\r\n151\r\n00:17:29.190 --> 00:17:35.309\r\nRyan Rumpf: that helps prepare the students and the staff for their time for their the beginning of their journey.\r\n\r\n152\r\n00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:41.230\r\nRyan Rumpf: especially how to pronounce a student's name, understanding their English proficiency level.\r\n\r\n153\r\n00:17:43.160 --> 00:17:46.380\r\nRyan Rumpf: school tours are very important.\r\n\r\n154\r\n00:17:46.410 --> 00:18:00.810\r\nRyan Rumpf: The kids are generally pretty nervous to start school, show them the important places, show them where their classroom is, where the office is. Some important people, some especially those that speak their language.\r\n\r\n155\r\n00:18:00.830 --> 00:18:03.230\r\nRyan Rumpf: introduce them to their teacher.\r\n\r\n156\r\n00:18:03.540 --> 00:18:09.090\r\nRyan Rumpf: Those are really good ways to start the year off right, or to start their time off right in your school.\r\n\r\n157\r\n00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:16.510\r\nRyan Rumpf: and when they get there maybe have a peer mentor, one that speaks the language of the student, if possible.\r\n\r\n158\r\n00:18:16.530 --> 00:18:21.279\r\nRyan Rumpf: but if not, find some, you know, find a willing and able\r\n\r\n159\r\n00:18:21.320 --> 00:18:22.850\r\nRyan Rumpf: peer Mentor.\r\n\r\n160\r\n00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:35.919\r\nRyan Rumpf: an old friend of mine, who's familiar with the work that I do. Her daughter found out that she was in 1st grade at the time, found out that a new student would be joining the class.\r\n\r\n161\r\n00:18:35.960 --> 00:18:45.410\r\nRyan Rumpf: and they were just beginning their journey in English. That child spoke Spanish. Whenever I went over to her house she was on Duolingo, learning some Spanish words to.\r\n\r\n162\r\n00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:49.300\r\nRyan Rumpf: because she was going to have a new classmate wanted to be able to help.\r\n\r\n163\r\n00:18:50.028 --> 00:19:04.650\r\nRyan Rumpf: I also recommend giving the gift of time, so give the students some time to adjust. We don't have to give them a, you know, just because a test is scheduled doesn't mean they have to take it on the 1st day.\r\n\r\n164\r\n00:19:05.980 --> 00:19:09.013\r\nRyan Rumpf: and some students need time for breaks, you know.\r\n\r\n165\r\n00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:28.509\r\nRyan Rumpf: spending all day a full day in a classroom where English is the language of instruction, and you're just beginning. Your journey is is tiring, so give some time for breaks, especially if there is someone who's there that speaks the language\r\n\r\n166\r\n00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:37.519\r\nRyan Rumpf: and set small goals. Goal setting can happen with newcomers as far as language, participation academics.\r\n\r\n167\r\n00:19:39.620 --> 00:19:43.730\r\nRyan Rumpf: and these things are all super important, because.\r\n\r\n168\r\n00:19:44.182 --> 00:19:50.860\r\nRyan Rumpf: you've you've got to give give attention to these things. Otherwise the rest won't really matter.\r\n\r\n169\r\n00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:51.735\r\nRyan Rumpf: So\r\n\r\n170\r\n00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:59.960\r\nRyan Rumpf: in the chat. What are some things that you all do to to welcome students? And to get to know them.\r\n\r\n171\r\n00:20:05.990 --> 00:20:12.199\r\nRyan Rumpf: That's awesome, Luce, you're on on call all day in case they need something that's I'm sure that's taxing on. You.\r\n\r\n172\r\n00:20:14.590 --> 00:20:17.199\r\nRyan Rumpf: Meet them a week before, and give them a tour.\r\n\r\n173\r\n00:20:22.550 --> 00:20:27.319\r\nRyan Rumpf: connect the students with other school tours and a buddy student. It's great\r\n\r\n174\r\n00:20:27.910 --> 00:20:35.720\r\nRyan Rumpf: picture books. I love that idea, giving students pictures of important places in the in the room\r\n\r\n175\r\n00:20:36.550 --> 00:20:37.820\r\nRyan Rumpf: or in the school.\r\n\r\n176\r\n00:20:40.560 --> 00:20:45.300\r\nRyan Rumpf: finding books with their cultural background and language more on that later. Yes.\r\n\r\n177\r\n00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:50.039\r\nMargie Marrero: Someone's also saying a snack social, or like a lunchtime, you know, like\r\n\r\n178\r\n00:20:50.430 --> 00:20:51.440\r\nMargie Marrero: gathering.\r\n\r\n179\r\n00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:54.010\r\nRyan Rumpf: Yeah, that's a great idea. Home country\r\n\r\n180\r\n00:20:54.290 --> 00:20:55.340\r\nRyan Rumpf: projects\r\n\r\n181\r\n00:20:56.380 --> 00:20:59.480\r\nRyan Rumpf: flagboard. Yeah, more on some of these later.\r\n\r\n182\r\n00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:06.709\r\nRyan Rumpf: All about me. Cahoot. Oh, man, I haven't played a cahoot in a while.\r\n\r\n183\r\n00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:11.720\r\nRyan Rumpf: A school tour for parents. That's great.\r\n\r\n184\r\n00:21:13.130 --> 00:21:14.750\r\nRyan Rumpf: all right, thank you all.\r\n\r\n185\r\n00:21:21.160 --> 00:21:30.929\r\nRyan Rumpf: So the next part of the framework is the psychological processes. These are intended to foster happiness, pride, and a love of the place.\r\n\r\n186\r\n00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:36.520\r\nRyan Rumpf: As far as cognition. We're talking about building memories, knowledge, schemas.\r\n\r\n187\r\n00:21:36.640 --> 00:21:44.349\r\nRyan Rumpf: and specifically around behavior, addressing the feelings of displacement and homesickness. Because those are very real.\r\n\r\n188\r\n00:21:45.950 --> 00:21:59.869\r\nRyan Rumpf: we don't know how the students are feeling about their transition to life in school in the Us. Until we've we've sat down and talked with them. Some of the students are thrilled and couldn't be happier to to be here.\r\n\r\n189\r\n00:21:59.890 --> 00:22:05.690\r\nRyan Rumpf: Some students didn't want to leave. And you have a range of things\r\n\r\n190\r\n00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:16.020\r\nRyan Rumpf: in between those 2. So it's it's really important that we that we help the students process those because that leads to other things.\r\n\r\n191\r\n00:22:16.768 --> 00:22:20.479\r\nRyan Rumpf: Having a counselor and a trauma\r\n\r\n192\r\n00:22:20.510 --> 00:22:32.970\r\nRyan Rumpf: trauma therapist trained trauma therapists to be available, and we didn't need them for everyone but we, the ones that we did need them for, was very, very valuable to help with their transition to school. Here.\r\n\r\n193\r\n00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:37.789\r\nRyan Rumpf: let's take a quick moment to just read through this vignette.\r\n\r\n194\r\n00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:43.580\r\nRyan Rumpf: It's about a Syrian student who fled Syria during the Civil War.\r\n\r\n195\r\n00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:50.349\r\nRyan Rumpf: and this is from a soon to be published book by Brian Mann as I was talking about.\r\n\r\n196\r\n00:22:50.600 --> 00:22:53.339\r\nRyan Rumpf: Just take a minute and read through this\r\n\r\n197\r\n00:23:20.280 --> 00:23:26.353\r\nRyan Rumpf: alright. So this it's a controversial topic. Prayer in schools right?\r\n\r\n198\r\n00:23:27.090 --> 00:23:35.940\r\nRyan Rumpf: Now, students are, of course, allowed to pray. The school just can't mandate it. Any student from any religious background\r\n\r\n199\r\n00:23:36.040 --> 00:23:38.319\r\nRyan Rumpf: can do\r\n\r\n200\r\n00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:49.329\r\nRyan Rumpf: you know what they, if they want to do it on their own, they're they're able to. Yusuf is a 15 year old, and in his religion he's required to pray 5 times a day.\r\n\r\n201\r\n00:23:49.620 --> 00:24:01.569\r\nRyan Rumpf: I've seen schools handle this in so many different ways. But if we're talking about placemaking and finding and and making making a connection with school.\r\n\r\n202\r\n00:24:01.630 --> 00:24:07.648\r\nRyan Rumpf: This is one very hot button issue, or it could be\r\n\r\n203\r\n00:24:08.220 --> 00:24:11.060\r\nRyan Rumpf: that could really make or break this connection.\r\n\r\n204\r\n00:24:11.190 --> 00:24:13.590\r\nRyan Rumpf: So let's take a look through\r\n\r\n205\r\n00:24:14.740 --> 00:24:26.670\r\nRyan Rumpf: the 1st in the 1st scenario. He didn't. He did not know who he could trust to to ask the question. He knew he didn't. He hadn't thought about it before, because in his school.\r\n\r\n206\r\n00:24:26.710 --> 00:24:30.409\r\nRyan Rumpf: in his former school he didn't have to think about it.\r\n\r\n207\r\n00:24:30.450 --> 00:24:34.559\r\nRyan Rumpf: They all prayed together, and it just wasn't something he had to think of before.\r\n\r\n208\r\n00:24:34.660 --> 00:24:42.239\r\nRyan Rumpf: but he didn't know who to ask, so he should have a go-to person or people in situations like these.\r\n\r\n209\r\n00:24:43.720 --> 00:24:51.029\r\nRyan Rumpf: So in Syria he and his classmates always prayed together in one space it suddenly occurred to him that he did not know where to pray.\r\n\r\n210\r\n00:24:51.680 --> 00:24:57.240\r\nRyan Rumpf: Is there some way we could capitalize on this, knowing what his former experience was.\r\n\r\n211\r\n00:24:57.817 --> 00:25:00.140\r\nRyan Rumpf: Is there something we could do.\r\n\r\n212\r\n00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:06.880\r\nRyan Rumpf: you know? Perhaps. He and any other Muslim student.\r\n\r\n213\r\n00:25:07.830 --> 00:25:13.270\r\nRyan Rumpf: could help design an area in the school where they could go. Pray\r\n\r\n214\r\n00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:17.400\r\nRyan Rumpf: help them know which. Yes, help them know which direction to face.\r\n\r\n215\r\n00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:20.677\r\nRyan Rumpf: and there yes.\r\n\r\n216\r\n00:25:22.750 --> 00:25:27.600\r\nRyan Rumpf: and how does praying by a staircase make use of feel?\r\n\r\n217\r\n00:25:29.660 --> 00:25:36.600\r\nRyan Rumpf: Does it make him feel like he's part of the school, or is he being pushed to the side to take care of his business while everybody else does their thing?\r\n\r\n218\r\n00:25:38.880 --> 00:25:42.454\r\nRyan Rumpf: and so I love. Yeah. Humiliated.\r\n\r\n219\r\n00:25:43.060 --> 00:25:53.460\r\nRyan Rumpf: He knew that kids were whispering. Everybody knows what a whisper is, you know you may not understand the words they were saying, but everybody understands what a what a whisper is!\r\n\r\n220\r\n00:25:55.090 --> 00:25:58.030\r\nRyan Rumpf: So things like this!\r\n\r\n221\r\n00:25:58.990 --> 00:26:06.989\r\nRyan Rumpf: Well, in here! When he was finished praying he was tardy to his class, and the teacher called him out in front of everybody.\r\n\r\n222\r\n00:26:07.230 --> 00:26:10.830\r\nRyan Rumpf: And so, you know, policies do matter.\r\n\r\n223\r\n00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:31.349\r\nRyan Rumpf: And as we're thinking about this, you know what could have been done before he started school to really get in front of this. This shouldn't be an issue. That's, you know, really, that foreign to to folks. But our words also matter. You know this was a teacher that didn't have patience\r\n\r\n224\r\n00:26:31.420 --> 00:26:38.790\r\nRyan Rumpf: that didn't have you know, rather than calling him out in front of the class. She, he or she could have.\r\n\r\n225\r\n00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:44.200\r\nRyan Rumpf: you know. Help! Help figure this out for the student.\r\n\r\n226\r\n00:26:44.990 --> 00:26:51.379\r\nRyan Rumpf: you know, and and one way creates a connection with the student. The other way pushes them off, pushes them away.\r\n\r\n227\r\n00:26:53.010 --> 00:27:08.680\r\nRyan Rumpf: The last part of place attachment theory is addressing the physical space itself. So social symbols, murals, flags include, and then just an inclusive and welcoming design.\r\n\r\n228\r\n00:27:08.840 --> 00:27:27.870\r\nRyan Rumpf: And here's some, you know. I showed the picture of the the map before. These are always fun to watch or for. Watch how they develop over the year, because there is no start date, for you know newcomers aren't all. They don't all come in August, and and in may they come throughout the year? So\r\n\r\n229\r\n00:27:28.030 --> 00:27:30.209\r\nRyan Rumpf: it's fun watching these develop.\r\n\r\n230\r\n00:27:31.066 --> 00:27:38.019\r\nRyan Rumpf: I'm glad we have a librarian in here, because I'm a big, big, big proponent of multilingual libraries.\r\n\r\n231\r\n00:27:38.987 --> 00:27:45.210\r\nRyan Rumpf: Having books in the students. Native languages is a very great way to to let them know they belong\r\n\r\n232\r\n00:27:45.340 --> 00:27:50.400\r\nRyan Rumpf: as well as flags, although sometimes I think we can overdo\r\n\r\n233\r\n00:27:51.192 --> 00:27:53.299\r\nRyan Rumpf: overdo the flags.\r\n\r\n234\r\n00:27:53.560 --> 00:27:56.459\r\nRyan Rumpf: There's some other things, but\r\n\r\n235\r\n00:27:56.620 --> 00:28:08.459\r\nRyan Rumpf: this particular example shows a personal flag project where students got to choose the colors that they felt represented them, or colors and designs that they felt best represented\r\n\r\n236\r\n00:28:08.520 --> 00:28:09.530\r\nRyan Rumpf: them.\r\n\r\n237\r\n00:28:10.235 --> 00:28:12.699\r\nRyan Rumpf: Yes, a Swahili collection.\r\n\r\n238\r\n00:28:14.090 --> 00:28:16.239\r\nRyan Rumpf: yeah, Karibu, Kansas City.\r\n\r\n239\r\n00:28:16.450 --> 00:28:19.150\r\nRyan Rumpf: Some of my favorite words are in Swahili.\r\n\r\n240\r\n00:28:19.310 --> 00:28:24.239\r\nRyan Rumpf: This particular library had 3,000 books in 14 languages.\r\n\r\n241\r\n00:28:24.937 --> 00:28:31.020\r\nRyan Rumpf: So any any student could go and find a book in their language.\r\n\r\n242\r\n00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:36.194\r\nRyan Rumpf: Some of the strategies you could use is just show\r\n\r\n243\r\n00:28:38.043 --> 00:28:42.869\r\nRyan Rumpf: Who said that? Asante Aaron Mapunda, kilakitu nisawa\r\n\r\n244\r\n00:28:44.130 --> 00:28:46.040\r\nRyan Rumpf: do a little Swahili here.\r\n\r\n245\r\n00:28:47.420 --> 00:28:48.015\r\nRyan Rumpf: great!\r\n\r\n246\r\n00:28:51.390 --> 00:28:55.408\r\nRyan Rumpf: But in I'm sorry I I got thrown off there.\r\n\r\n247\r\n00:28:56.180 --> 00:29:10.100\r\nRyan Rumpf: But you can search for blogs of of famous, you know, preteen books in whatever language. There's a lot of people writing about them, and then search for search for those titles on Amazon.\r\n\r\n248\r\n00:29:10.658 --> 00:29:16.339\r\nRyan Rumpf: and 9 times out of 10 you'll either get a new one or a used version of those books.\r\n\r\n249\r\n00:29:16.350 --> 00:29:20.840\r\nRyan Rumpf: So Google and technology can help us find these things.\r\n\r\n250\r\n00:29:23.680 --> 00:29:30.290\r\nRyan Rumpf: murals. Another great way to do it. You can get community partners to pitch in and help\r\n\r\n251\r\n00:29:30.310 --> 00:29:38.890\r\nRyan Rumpf: in this example. It's in an elementary school. The the clothes the kids are wearing represent their flag.\r\n\r\n252\r\n00:29:39.560 --> 00:29:48.110\r\nRyan Rumpf: There was a Afghan student walking through and pointed out this girl right away and said, This is me.\r\n\r\n253\r\n00:29:48.140 --> 00:29:50.959\r\nRyan Rumpf: and that's what place attachment is all about.\r\n\r\n254\r\n00:29:51.540 --> 00:30:00.089\r\nYvonne Williams: Brian, sorry to interrupt you. I know that you probably have an answer to this. Anyone have resources to books\r\n\r\n255\r\n00:30:00.710 --> 00:30:05.459\r\nYvonne Williams: like, did you use a specific resource to find the variety of books.\r\n\r\n256\r\n00:30:05.460 --> 00:30:06.063\r\nRyan Rumpf: So\r\n\r\n257\r\n00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:19.209\r\nRyan Rumpf: my myself. Now, language lizard is one I don't language. Lizard has a lot of books, and and they're dual language books. They have pen readers that go with them.\r\n\r\n258\r\n00:30:19.836 --> 00:30:26.440\r\nRyan Rumpf: But like I said a lot of it, you can search on Barnes and noble by language.