170 --> 00:38:52.800\r\nRyan Rumpf: let's we've already thought and reflected.\r\n\r\n349\r\n00:38:53.670 --> 00:38:59.190\r\nRyan Rumpf: Let's take a look at some instructional strategies. They're effective instructional practices\r\n\r\n350\r\n00:38:59.730 --> 00:39:00.609\r\nRyan Rumpf: and\r\n\r\n351\r\n00:39:01.640 --> 00:39:10.350\r\nRyan Rumpf: go back to this adaptation from Albert Einstein's quote that everyone is a genius. But if you judge a fish by its ability to climb a tree.\r\n\r\n352\r\n00:39:10.530 --> 00:39:13.650\r\nRyan Rumpf: it will live its whole life, believing that it can't swim.\r\n\r\n353\r\n00:39:13.730 --> 00:39:19.549\r\nRyan Rumpf: And I'm a, you know, coming from a newcomer center. I'm very aware of the\r\n\r\n354\r\n00:39:20.026 --> 00:39:29.600\r\nRyan Rumpf: the assessment culture that we have in the United States, and so many decisions are are based on the results of an assessment.\r\n\r\n355\r\n00:39:30.132 --> 00:39:34.567\r\nRyan Rumpf: But just know that, you know, if we're wanting\r\n\r\n356\r\n00:39:35.370 --> 00:39:37.390\r\nRyan Rumpf: the language of the test\r\n\r\n357\r\n00:39:37.400 --> 00:39:42.230\r\nRyan Rumpf: really makes a difference, and the purpose of the test really matters.\r\n\r\n358\r\n00:39:42.320 --> 00:39:51.100\r\nRyan Rumpf: If we're gauging English proficiency or gauging how much English they've acquired, we're going to. That's what we're going to assess.\r\n\r\n359\r\n00:39:51.290 --> 00:39:59.500\r\nRyan Rumpf: But for newcomers who are just beginning their journey in English, you know, taking a reading or a math test in English right away.\r\n\r\n360\r\n00:39:59.530 --> 00:40:09.509\r\nRyan Rumpf: really isn't going to tell you much about their knowledge of language, arts, or math. It's going to tell you that they really don't understand the test questions yet.\r\n\r\n361\r\n00:40:09.850 --> 00:40:11.270\r\nRyan Rumpf: but they will.\r\n\r\n362\r\n00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:13.010\r\nRyan Rumpf: Eventually.\r\n\r\n363\r\n00:40:14.057 --> 00:40:23.350\r\nRyan Rumpf: As far as what does those? What are those 1st stages look like? If you look at the the plds for for Wida\r\n\r\n364\r\n00:40:24.216 --> 00:40:29.339\r\nRyan Rumpf: those are generally, or those are descriptions of the end of a level.\r\n\r\n365\r\n00:40:29.790 --> 00:40:33.869\r\nRyan Rumpf: So for newcomers, what does it look like before that?\r\n\r\n366\r\n00:40:34.590 --> 00:40:37.610\r\nRyan Rumpf: Well, they come, or the students come in\r\n\r\n367\r\n00:40:37.800 --> 00:40:43.100\r\nRyan Rumpf: and they're they're in what's what the research community calls the silent period.\r\n\r\n368\r\n00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:44.553\r\nRyan Rumpf: There are,\r\n\r\n369\r\n00:40:45.930 --> 00:41:04.090\r\nRyan Rumpf: you know, different opinions around the use of that term. But there's a reluctant use of in our case. English. If anybody's been in a world language course before, and your 1st experience, you know, having to speak to your teacher, maybe for a test.\r\n\r\n370\r\n00:41:04.190 --> 00:41:11.560\r\nRyan Rumpf: There's some anxiety there. There's some affective variables. You may not have wanted to talk in front of your friends\r\n\r\n371\r\n00:41:11.590 --> 00:41:15.130\r\nRyan Rumpf: because the the confidence just wasn't there.\r\n\r\n372\r\n00:41:15.700 --> 00:41:20.469\r\nRyan Rumpf: The good news about the silent period is, it's it can be relatively quick.\r\n\r\n373\r\n00:41:20.520 --> 00:41:25.639\r\nRyan Rumpf: depending on personality, and how we work with the students in that level.\r\n\r\n374\r\n00:41:25.780 --> 00:41:36.469\r\nRyan Rumpf: We don't want to to force it, but we want to build confidence. We want to give them words. We want to use repetition\r\n\r\n375\r\n00:41:36.750 --> 00:41:45.690\r\nRyan Rumpf: immediately after the silent period. It's generally memorized chunks of language. This is language that we give to them things that they hear all all day.\r\n\r\n376\r\n00:41:45.700 --> 00:41:47.679\r\nRyan Rumpf: They hear frequently.\r\n\r\n377\r\n00:41:48.115 --> 00:41:58.899\r\nRyan Rumpf: And you know common words that daily interactions in class and experiences in class 9 times out of 10. The 1st words that the students are using are\r\n\r\n378\r\n00:41:58.910 --> 00:42:06.270\r\nRyan Rumpf: are meant to convey, like a need, a survival, a survival situation like needing to go to the bathroom or some water, or\r\n\r\n379\r\n00:42:06.460 --> 00:42:16.230\r\nRyan Rumpf: that there's a challenge, but those are generally the first, st and you never know when a student's ready to come out of the silent period, I had a\r\n\r\n380\r\n00:42:16.390 --> 00:42:24.480\r\nRyan Rumpf: a 1st grader who seemed like she was in the silent period for 4 or 5 months, and we were wondering what's going on. Nobody\r\n\r\n381\r\n00:42:24.510 --> 00:42:26.569\r\nRyan Rumpf: takes this long to\r\n\r\n382\r\n00:42:26.810 --> 00:42:35.859\r\nRyan Rumpf: to speak before, and we were walking out to the bus one day, and she just darts back inside, and I'm like, Where are you going? And she turned around and said, I got to go to the bathroom.\r\n\r\n383\r\n00:42:35.960 --> 00:42:38.330\r\nRyan Rumpf: Yeah, full sentence. Just like that.\r\n\r\n384\r\n00:42:38.902 --> 00:42:41.497\r\nRyan Rumpf: So you you never really know\r\n\r\n385\r\n00:42:42.020 --> 00:42:51.980\r\nRyan Rumpf: but with the with the students who are coming out of this, the the affective side of that is very real, the the confidence, the\r\n\r\n386\r\n00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:57.000\r\nRyan Rumpf: the self-esteem, the fear of embarrassment.\r\n\r\n387\r\n00:42:57.030 --> 00:43:03.090\r\nRyan Rumpf: things like that really matter. So it's up to us to build build them up\r\n\r\n388\r\n00:43:03.170 --> 00:43:12.590\r\nRyan Rumpf: and and and help them feel like they're that they're in a safe space to to try to use the the language that they have.\r\n\r\n389\r\n00:43:14.760 --> 00:43:31.880\r\nRyan Rumpf: And here's another slide about the development. Just as recently as 2020, there was a study showing that newcomers advanced more quickly over time than non newcomer English learners, despite having lower initial English proficiency levels\r\n\r\n390\r\n00:43:32.250 --> 00:43:46.170\r\nRyan Rumpf: the 4 years that was impacted the most was with students who are entering schools at the higher grades showed slower growth over the 1st 4 years than by students in the younger grade.\r\n\r\n391\r\n00:43:46.320 --> 00:44:03.910\r\nRyan Rumpf: So how can we leverage this rapid growth we should build on their language skills to support this growth, be ready to jump on those teachable moments, praise them, or give them praise whenever they're stretching themselves.\r\n\r\n392\r\n00:44:04.120 --> 00:44:13.650\r\nRyan Rumpf: And there are promising programs to support this this development bilingual programs, translanguaging\r\n\r\n393\r\n00:44:14.336 --> 00:44:21.430\r\nRyan Rumpf: ideas and strategies to help them interact with the with the grade level curriculum\r\n\r\n394\r\n00:44:21.610 --> 00:44:27.680\r\nRyan Rumpf: course placement policies. You know, I've seen schools where\r\n\r\n395\r\n00:44:27.830 --> 00:44:35.490\r\nRyan Rumpf: every Ela or English Language arts class was substituted with an Esl class. So a student would grad could\r\n\r\n396\r\n00:44:35.530 --> 00:44:39.829\r\nRyan Rumpf: theoretically graduate without ever having taken language arts.\r\n\r\n397\r\n00:44:40.817 --> 00:44:42.929\r\nRyan Rumpf: and then, of course.\r\n\r\n398\r\n00:44:43.940 --> 00:44:51.939\r\nRyan Rumpf: if we're going to, the more students have opportunities to engage with the language to use the language among their peers, the better.\r\n\r\n399\r\n00:44:52.200 --> 00:44:57.489\r\nRyan Rumpf: as much as we pride ourselves in the work that we do. The kids are going to\r\n\r\n400\r\n00:44:57.580 --> 00:45:03.819\r\nRyan Rumpf: learn more language with each other, so we need to give them opportunities to do that.\r\n\r\n401\r\n00:45:06.650 --> 00:45:12.040\r\nRyan Rumpf: moving out of the silent period. Again, attention to that affective side\r\n\r\n402\r\n00:45:12.250 --> 00:45:26.449\r\nRyan Rumpf: lots of repetition. So we should be working with the same text from Monday to Friday. Gradually very teacher led on Monday, and gradually moving it to more student led on Friday.\r\n\r\n403\r\n00:45:26.520 --> 00:45:28.980\r\nRyan Rumpf: The point is, we get a lot of repetition.\r\n\r\n404\r\n00:45:29.010 --> 00:45:31.389\r\nRyan Rumpf: memorize chunks of words.\r\n\r\n405\r\n00:45:31.480 --> 00:45:46.259\r\nRyan Rumpf: And then this idea of total physical response. Total physical response has been around, I think, since the sixties. It's a very popular technique in the world, languages in the\r\n\r\n406\r\n00:45:47.690 --> 00:45:53.440\r\nRyan Rumpf: learning, a language in like like many of us, did Spanish or French in in school.\r\n\r\n407\r\n00:45:53.700 --> 00:46:02.249\r\nRyan Rumpf: where there's a an oral prompt from the teacher, followed by motion of some kind with the students.\r\n\r\n408\r\n00:46:02.330 --> 00:46:07.509\r\nRyan Rumpf: So what are some strategies that you do already? As you're working with students in the silent period.\r\n\r\n409\r\n00:46:11.690 --> 00:46:27.820\r\nMargie Marrero: So, Ryan, while we wait for some of those responses to come through there, there was a comment, and I know that trans languaging can be a a concept for a full day, but I think it was new to some people. So they somebody was asking if you could just elaborate\r\n\r\n410\r\n00:46:27.940 --> 00:46:32.180\r\nMargie Marrero: a little bit more, if that's possible, because I know it's a complex\r\n\r\n411\r\n00:46:32.860 --> 00:46:36.640\r\nMargie Marrero: concept to just to explain that a little bit more.\r\n\r\n412\r\n00:46:36.640 --> 00:46:49.480\r\nRyan Rumpf: Yeah. And we are developing a translanguaging workshop, too. So that that's another. But in essence, translanguaging is giving the students the opportunity to use their full linguistic repertoire\r\n\r\n413\r\n00:46:50.100 --> 00:46:54.689\r\nRyan Rumpf: as they interact with the content and interact with each other.\r\n\r\n414\r\n00:46:55.309 --> 00:46:59.259\r\nRyan Rumpf: What I immediately think of is, whenever I would walk into\r\n\r\n415\r\n00:46:59.270 --> 00:47:09.119\r\nRyan Rumpf: a front office and had a couple bilingual folks working up there, and if you heard them talking it's just going back and forth between English and Spanish\r\n\r\n416\r\n00:47:10.694 --> 00:47:12.240\r\nRyan Rumpf: effortlessly\r\n\r\n417\r\n00:47:12.300 --> 00:47:25.219\r\nRyan Rumpf: as, and it was just common, and it it used to be looked down upon. Right like they were. This type of code switching was was frowned upon, and\r\n\r\n418\r\n00:47:25.320 --> 00:47:34.669\r\nRyan Rumpf: for many that went to school, you know, 1020 years ago, where they may have stumbled upon an English only policy or so.\r\n\r\n419\r\n00:47:35.668 --> 00:47:42.400\r\nRyan Rumpf: But we found that that's normal. And children adults\r\n\r\n420\r\n00:47:42.410 --> 00:47:54.270\r\nRyan Rumpf: do that. And it's it's beneficial not only to their their place and their their sense of belonging, but also their academic growth and language growth.\r\n\r\n421\r\n00:47:54.270 --> 00:48:03.629\r\nMargie Marrero: Yeah. And I think, like someone mentioned on the chat, too, they learn faster because they're not kind of inhibited to only try to say words in a certain way. They're allowed to use\r\n\r\n422\r\n00:48:03.700 --> 00:48:05.330\r\nMargie Marrero: like you said they're full\r\n\r\n423\r\n00:48:05.340 --> 00:48:06.590\r\nMargie Marrero: repertoire.\r\n\r\n424\r\n00:48:06.920 --> 00:48:09.859\r\nRyan Rumpf: And I saw some people talking about songs on here.\r\n\r\n425\r\n00:48:11.390 --> 00:48:20.020\r\nRyan Rumpf: music chance, I think, somewhere around 4, th 5th grade. We generally tend tend to move from music to chance.\r\n\r\n426\r\n00:48:20.150 --> 00:48:24.110\r\nRyan Rumpf: I don't know what it is about kids not wanting to sing along with\r\n\r\n427\r\n00:48:24.120 --> 00:48:26.440\r\nRyan Rumpf: their peers at that age, but\r\n\r\n428\r\n00:48:27.574 --> 00:48:36.450\r\nRyan Rumpf: when this when you start getting some reluctance to sing chants would be a great way.\r\n\r\n429\r\n00:48:36.750 --> 00:48:41.669\r\nRyan Rumpf: Gosh! I've had this if anybody's listened to the doors, I don't know if\r\n\r\n430\r\n00:48:42.085 --> 00:48:50.019\r\nRyan Rumpf: I'm going to date any of us here. But there's a song out there. That's a Hello! I love you. Won't you tell me your name.\r\n\r\n431\r\n00:48:50.050 --> 00:48:54.240\r\nRyan Rumpf: I just changed the words to Hello, how are you? What is your name?\r\n\r\n432\r\n00:48:54.290 --> 00:49:03.609\r\nRyan Rumpf: And that was just play that along with the kids. And right away they learned they learned how to introduce themselves to each other.\r\n\r\n433\r\n00:49:03.690 --> 00:49:07.560\r\nRyan Rumpf: but songs, chants\r\n\r\n434\r\n00:49:08.090 --> 00:49:18.299\r\nRyan Rumpf: very powerful, in fact, sometimes the songs they sing them so much. Then you're singing them all weekend, too. You can't get them out of your head, which is kind of the point.\r\n\r\n435\r\n00:49:20.690 --> 00:49:31.240\r\nRyan Rumpf: good stuff. There are some variations to total physical response. Tpr circles. So after practicing the movements, you can all get in the big circle\r\n\r\n436\r\n00:49:31.370 --> 00:49:35.319\r\nRyan Rumpf: last one to do the movements has to sit down.\r\n\r\n437\r\n00:49:35.680 --> 00:49:37.590\r\nRyan Rumpf: and last one standing wins.\r\n\r\n438\r\n00:49:37.790 --> 00:49:51.530\r\nRyan Rumpf: although the person who sat down 1st should probably get more practice. But there's a Simon says is is Tpr. That is, someone giving an oral prompt, and the students responding through movement.\r\n\r\n439\r\n00:49:51.650 --> 00:49:55.823\r\nRyan Rumpf: Tpr sounds, there's slap vocabulary. I've seen\r\n\r\n440\r\n00:49:56.890 --> 00:50:13.679\r\nRyan Rumpf: It's a picture laying down flashcards on the ground or on a table. Somebody says the word, and the 1st one to slap it gets the card. Whoever has the most cards at the end wins now after trial and error.\r\n\r\n441\r\n00:50:13.730 --> 00:50:20.980\r\nRyan Rumpf: Fly swatters work a lot better than the hands, because then you won't. The kids won't be hitting each other.\r\n\r\n442\r\n00:50:21.100 --> 00:50:28.089\r\nRyan Rumpf: and even Bingo arguably, is a type of total physical response. You have an oral, prompt\r\n\r\n443\r\n00:50:28.180 --> 00:50:30.870\r\nRyan Rumpf: students have to make a connection in their brain.\r\n\r\n444\r\n00:50:31.050 --> 00:50:33.549\r\nRyan Rumpf: They they have a movement afterwards.\r\n\r\n445\r\n00:50:35.160 --> 00:50:39.160\r\nYvonne Williams: Brian can. Do you mind if I quickly answer a question.\r\n\r\n446\r\n00:50:39.400 --> 00:50:51.809\r\nYvonne Williams: there's a question, how do we know when a newcomer is relying on translanguaging to a detrimental point, only seeking out bilingual staff members and refusing to interact with monolingual staffs and students.\r\n\r\n447\r\n00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:56.280\r\nYvonne Williams: Before I give my answer, do you have one.\r\n\r\n448\r\n00:50:56.737 --> 00:51:01.770\r\nRyan Rumpf: Well, I would say, translanguaging and translating are 2 different things.\r\n\r\n449\r\n00:51:02.690 --> 00:51:03.890\r\nRyan Rumpf: So\r\n\r\n450\r\n00:51:04.230 --> 00:51:08.050\r\nRyan Rumpf: translating, everything is.\r\n\r\n451\r\n00:51:09.466 --> 00:51:19.939\r\nRyan Rumpf: really reluctance to engage in in the target language. Right? It's a reluctant but translanguaging is\r\n\r\n452\r\n00:51:20.250 --> 00:51:22.260\r\nRyan Rumpf: is using both.\r\n\r\n453\r\n00:51:24.740 --> 00:51:31.339\r\nRyan Rumpf: so this there is some confusion around translation versus translanguaging.\r\n\r\n454\r\n00:51:33.470 --> 00:51:44.359\r\nYvonne Williams: So my, my response is, of course, what Ryan mentioned that translanguaging doesn't mean just translating Spanglish is now an acceptable\r\n\r\n455\r\n00:51:45.090 --> 00:51:52.399\r\nYvonne Williams: way for students to make meaning of content, and engage in making meaning and conversations with other students.\r\n\r\n456\r\n00:51:52.440 --> 00:52:10.859\r\nYvonne Williams: The idea is that they're seeing it as a scaffold or support, so that they know when and how it's necessary, and it's not something that they are going to do consistently all the time. We do want them to be exposed to the use of English, and or how they would write in the in the content area.\r\n\r\n457\r\n00:52:11.270 --> 00:52:14.619\r\nYvonne Williams: The practice of translanguaging is allowing them\r\n\r\n458\r\n00:52:14.650 --> 00:52:17.100\r\nYvonne Williams: to use it when they need\r\n\r\n459\r\n00:52:17.480 --> 00:52:36.269\r\nYvonne Williams: to engage in understanding or clarification, so that they they know that it is something in their toolbox, and not necessarily. That's all they're going to do, and that's a difficult message for students when you're letting them use their home language and they feel most comfortable using it. I'll tell you that it's\r\n\r\n460\r\n00:52:36.380 --> 00:52:40.499\r\nYvonne Williams: it's something that for me, as an El student.\r\n\r\n461\r\n00:52:40.690 --> 00:52:59.010\r\nYvonne Williams: I had the opposite effect. Now, as an English speaker, I feel uncomfortable speaking Spanish, and I'd rather engage in English, but I know that I want to continue to practice my Spanish. And so those are the conversations we need to have with students when you're going to engage in translanguaging. There was somebody that said that their students are moving\r\n\r\n462\r\n00:52:59.010 --> 00:53:11.199\r\nYvonne Williams: quite rapidly because they're now doing translanguaging practices in their classroom. I encourage you to read Ophelia Garcia and or Susani Vara. Johnson's book on Translanguaging. We need to know how to communicate\r\n\r\n463\r\n00:53:11.410 --> 00:53:22.839\r\nYvonne Williams: what the purpose is in this research on translanguaging, because it's more more than just letting them speak Spanish or letting them speak their home language. It involves this cultural piece, and\r\n\r\n464\r\n00:53:22.990 --> 00:53:37.469\r\nYvonne Williams: windows and mirrors which is allowing them to see other cultures represented in your classroom and their own cultures as well. So we will try to make a trans languishing webinar for the next tips and dips, because I think there's a lot to discuss, and I don't. Wanna\r\n\r\n465\r\n00:53:37.930 --> 00:53:39.540\r\nYvonne Williams: did this give you a little bit.\r\n\r\n466\r\n00:53:40.520 --> 00:53:47.710\r\nRyan Rumpf: Yes, and we have. I will say we have 9 min left, and there's a really cool activity I want to do at the end, if you don't mind.\r\n\r\n467\r\n00:53:47.780 --> 00:54:09.389\r\nRyan Rumpf: but just some things on when to translate, especially safety and emergency situations we want. We don't want there to be a language barrier there. So you know, in Missouri we have tornadoes, we want to know, and we have drills. So we want the kids to know right away what to do in case of an emergency\r\n\r\n468\r\n00:54:09.700 --> 00:54:16.430\r\nRyan Rumpf: communication with parents and guardians who don't have access, maybe to an an Esl type class\r\n\r\n469\r\n00:54:16.460 --> 00:54:21.279\r\nRyan Rumpf: to grow their own English. They need to be feel like they're part of the community\r\n\r\n470\r\n00:54:21.480 --> 00:54:37.719\r\nRyan Rumpf: emotional support. If the student is having a rough day and needs to process something with a counselor. They ideally should do that in their native language, either through a language line or through bilingual staff.\r\n\r\n471\r\n00:54:37.780 --> 00:54:42.550\r\nRyan Rumpf: And it's behavior issues you want\r\n\r\n472\r\n00:54:42.710 --> 00:54:52.820\r\nRyan Rumpf: as a former building leader. Resolving behavior concerns in the native language is very, very helpful\r\n\r\n473\r\n00:54:53.000 --> 00:54:55.590\r\nRyan Rumpf: as far as instruction.\r\n\r\n474\r\n00:54:55.900 --> 00:55:04.979\r\nRyan Rumpf: Instructional clarity is what I like to say. If we've tried 3 times to half the battle is getting the kids to understand the objective.\r\n\r\n475\r\n00:55:05.070 --> 00:55:09.219\r\nRyan Rumpf: and if they just seem lost and we've tried A, B and C,\r\n\r\n476\r\n00:55:09.800 --> 00:55:14.950\r\nRyan Rumpf: let's just get it out of the way, so we can move on to the learning and what we want the kids to to do.\r\n\r\n477\r\n00:55:15.100 --> 00:55:20.380\r\nRyan Rumpf: That's instructional clarity explaining the objectives, and then we move back.\r\n\r\n478\r\n00:55:20.996 --> 00:55:25.163\r\nRyan Rumpf: And clarification kind of on the flip side of that\r\n\r\n479\r\n00:55:25.870 --> 00:55:33.790\r\nRyan Rumpf: If there's a misconception, perhaps that could be, or a teachable moment that we can do real quick and in the moment.\r\n\r\n480\r\n00:55:33.820 --> 00:55:40.479\r\nRyan Rumpf: and then we move on. We don't want to live there, because translation will be become a crutch.\r\n\r\n481\r\n00:55:40.580 --> 00:55:48.249\r\nRyan Rumpf: And if this, if you give too much, the students will rely on the translation. And that's not what we want. Okay.\r\n\r\n482\r\n00:55:50.690 --> 00:55:52.130\r\nRyan Rumpf: 7 min.\r\n\r\n483\r\n00:55:52.240 --> 00:55:57.690\r\nRyan Rumpf: So this idea of comprehensible input there's going to be a lot of strategies I throw at you.\r\n\r\n484\r\n00:55:57.850 --> 00:56:04.699\r\nRyan Rumpf: But this idea of comprehensible input are techniques that we use to convey information to students.\r\n\r\n485\r\n00:56:04.820 --> 00:56:12.030\r\nRyan Rumpf: And this comes from Stephen Crash. And it's been around since the the 19 seventies where we talk about\r\n\r\n486\r\n00:56:12.090 --> 00:56:18.170\r\nRyan Rumpf: their their input plus one. So the zone of proximal development. We've all heard of that\r\n\r\n487\r\n00:56:19.086 --> 00:56:23.320\r\nRyan Rumpf: crashes comprehension hypothesis centers around.\r\n\r\n488\r\n00:56:23.360 --> 00:56:35.910\r\nRyan Rumpf: We're stretching students to the next level. We're making meaningful jumps and stretching them linguistically to a zone or to the proficiency level that's within their reach.\r\n\r\n489\r\n00:56:37.690 --> 00:56:40.050\r\nRyan Rumpf: so as we're looking at newcomers.\r\n\r\n490\r\n00:56:40.170 --> 00:56:44.700\r\nRyan Rumpf: and we're who are coming in at a level Pre level one.\r\n\r\n491\r\n00:56:44.750 --> 00:56:47.221\r\nRyan Rumpf: these level one skills.\r\n\r\n492\r\n00:56:47.890 --> 00:56:59.789\r\nRyan Rumpf: we're looking at short phrases, simple sentences. That's those are the things that's within their reach. They get there very quickly, and then we need to start stretching them to a 2 and then to a 3, and then to a 4.\r\n\r\n493\r\n00:57:00.247 --> 00:57:07.190\r\nRyan Rumpf: We can't be living it again. Same with translation. We don't want to live in one area the entire time.\r\n\r\n494\r\n00:57:07.860 --> 00:57:14.940\r\nRyan Rumpf: There are several. I got 5 min. There are several ways that we can provide comprehensible input\r\n\r\n495\r\n00:57:16.780 --> 00:57:29.220\r\nRyan Rumpf: thinking about speech. If we're working with newcomers, we don't want to use idioms, we want them to understand what the words really mean before we throw in figurative language and idioms\r\n\r\n496\r\n00:57:29.310 --> 00:57:35.050\r\nRyan Rumpf: speak slowly. It's amazing how fast teachers can talk when they're standing in front of 30 kids\r\n\r\n497\r\n00:57:35.340 --> 00:57:47.000\r\nRyan Rumpf: interaction give students opportunities to interact with one another, not just because because it helps their language. But, culturally speaking, they're used to working in groups.\r\n\r\n498\r\n00:57:47.620 --> 00:57:59.620\r\nRyan Rumpf: visuals, gestures, using our hands, making connections, helping the kids make connections between what's being said and and and the concept that we're learning\r\n\r\n499\r\n00:58:00.780 --> 00:58:09.240\r\nRyan Rumpf: technology. There's a lot of cool stuff out there anymore. In fact, we could probably do a full day session on all the tech tools.\r\n\r\n500\r\n00:58:09.862 --> 00:58:21.347\r\nRyan Rumpf: But there are some really cool things that can help break down the language barrier. You, you see, Google translate on here. But again, we don't want to live there.\r\n\r\n501\r\n00:58:22.530 --> 00:58:26.875\r\nRyan Rumpf: there's also some really cool video dubbing programs out there.\r\n\r\n502\r\n00:58:27.560 --> 00:58:32.569\r\nRyan Rumpf: that if you wanted to flip a classroom, so to speak. You know, help the kids\r\n\r\n503\r\n00:58:32.830 --> 00:58:41.820\r\nRyan Rumpf: understand what they're going to learn. The next day we'll send them home with a native language video. They can watch the night before. Maybe they can watch it with their parents, and they can talk about it, and.\r\n\r\n504\r\n00:58:42.200 --> 00:58:46.759\r\nRyan Rumpf: you know, bring up their prior learning, or build some background around it.\r\n\r\n505\r\n00:58:47.470 --> 00:58:53.440\r\nRyan Rumpf: Clear expectations, especially visual expectations or directions.\r\n\r\n506\r\n00:58:54.380 --> 00:59:01.580\r\nRyan Rumpf: Again, just helping the kids understand exactly what we want them to to do is half the battle.\r\n\r\n507\r\n00:59:01.870 --> 00:59:06.310\r\nRyan Rumpf: If we can have pictures or modeling or demonstrations\r\n\r\n508\r\n00:59:06.330 --> 00:59:16.340\r\nRyan Rumpf: to help them. Now, this isn't, you know. This is keeping it rigorous, keeping it challenging. We don't want to give them too much, but we want to give them just enough to stretch them\r\n\r\n509\r\n00:59:18.439 --> 00:59:25.150\r\nRyan Rumpf: scaffold scaffolding the the not only the presentation, but the product.\r\n\r\n510\r\n00:59:27.050 --> 00:59:33.390\r\nRyan Rumpf: using these manipulatives, models, graphic organizers think alouds.\r\n\r\n511\r\n00:59:33.990 --> 00:59:39.229\r\nRyan Rumpf: and then also scaffolding the content. And there's some really cool options with AI there, too.\r\n\r\n512\r\n00:59:39.400 --> 00:59:42.699\r\nRyan Rumpf: to to help scaffold some of these things.\r\n\r\n513\r\n00:59:43.560 --> 00:59:45.890\r\nRyan Rumpf: So that's the idea of comprehensible\r\n\r\n514\r\n00:59:46.560 --> 00:59:51.870\r\nRyan Rumpf: input. Now, there's also this idea of comprehensible output.\r\n\r\n515\r\n00:59:51.940 --> 00:59:57.840\r\nRyan Rumpf: And with this one I'm going to have to trust everyone to follow some directions, even though we are\r\n\r\n516\r\n00:59:57.920 --> 01:00:00.219\r\nRyan Rumpf: in a in a virtual setting.\r\n\r\n517\r\n01:00:00.740 --> 01:00:11.140\r\nRyan Rumpf: So we are going to do a little activity. We're we're going to read. Read this sentence. Okay, so I'm going to say it.\r\n\r\n518\r\n01:00:11.150 --> 01:00:16.880\r\nRyan Rumpf: And if you just say it with me, and I'll pretend we're doing it corally, like we're all doing it together. Okay.\r\n\r\n519\r\n01:00:17.570 --> 01:00:20.010\r\nRyan Rumpf: so 3, 2, 1\r\n\r\n520\r\n01:00:20.020 --> 01:00:25.769\r\nRyan Rumpf: language learners improve their abilities when they are pushed to produce language. That is clear.\r\n\r\n521\r\n01:00:26.970 --> 01:00:30.639\r\nRyan Rumpf: All right. That's the idea behind comprehensible output.\r\n\r\n522\r\n01:00:31.290 --> 01:00:36.289\r\nRyan Rumpf: Now I'm going to push you to produce language that is clear. Let's read this together with me again.\r\n\r\n523\r\n01:00:37.380 --> 01:00:42.079\r\nRyan Rumpf: Language learners improve their abilities when they are pushed to produce language.\r\n\r\n524\r\n01:00:43.760 --> 01:00:45.999\r\nRyan Rumpf: Hopefully, you finish that sentence.\r\n\r\n525\r\n01:00:46.000 --> 01:00:47.930\r\nYvonne Williams: That is clear.\r\n\r\n526\r\n01:00:47.930 --> 01:00:49.359\r\nRyan Rumpf: There we go ready. Let's try it.\r\n\r\n527\r\n01:00:49.360 --> 01:00:50.635\r\nRosemary Trumbo: That is clear\r\n\r\n528\r\n01:00:51.210 --> 01:00:53.639\r\nRyan Rumpf: Let's try again. Ready language learner.\r\n\r\n529\r\n01:00:53.640 --> 01:00:58.349\r\nRuth Cahill: Language learnings improves their abilities when they are pushed to produce\r\n\r\n530\r\n01:00:58.950 --> 01:01:01.240\r\nRuth Cahill: language that is clear.\r\n\r\n531\r\n01:01:01.340 --> 01:01:03.729\r\nRyan Rumpf: Very good. All right. Let's keep going.\r\n\r\n532\r\n01:01:04.800 --> 01:01:06.779\r\nMiad Shamass: Language learners are programmed\r\n\r\n533\r\n01:01:07.600 --> 01:01:11.239\r\nMiad Shamass: when they are pushed to give\r\n\r\n534\r\n01:01:14.930 --> 01:01:15.840\r\nMiad Shamass: here.\r\n\r\n535\r\n01:01:15.840 --> 01:01:17.380\r\nRyan Rumpf: Very good. All right. Let's keep.\r\n\r\n536\r\n01:01:18.970 --> 01:01:19.490\r\nMelissa Chiofalo: Language.\r\n\r\n537\r\n01:01:21.710 --> 01:01:23.749\r\nPALOMA RODRIGUEZ: When they are.\r\n\r\n538\r\n01:01:23.750 --> 01:01:25.130\r\nTanya Harmon: Reduce language.\r\n\r\n539\r\n01:01:27.740 --> 01:01:28.830\r\nRyan Rumpf: Alright\r\n\r\n540\r\n01:01:29.420 --> 01:01:31.000\r\nRyan Rumpf: language learners.\r\n\r\n541\r\n01:01:31.620 --> 01:01:32.140\r\nRyan Rumpf: Oh.\r\n\r\n542\r\n01:01:32.140 --> 01:01:33.170\r\nMiad Shamass: Improve their.\r\n\r\n543\r\n01:01:33.360 --> 01:01:37.860\r\nTanya Harmon: Their ability when they are pushed to produce language that is clear.\r\n\r\n544\r\n01:01:38.360 --> 01:01:40.810\r\nRyan Rumpf: Let's see if we can do a total one this time.\r\n\r\n545\r\n01:01:45.110 --> 01:01:46.050\r\nMelissa Chiofalo: There, of course.\r\n\r\n546\r\n01:01:46.050 --> 01:01:47.730\r\nTanya Harmon: Lose their ability\r\n\r\n547\r\n01:01:50.180 --> 01:01:52.019\r\nTanya Harmon: to translate language.\r\n\r\n548\r\n01:01:52.020 --> 01:01:53.160\r\nRuth Cahill: It's clear.\r\n\r\n549\r\n01:01:54.570 --> 01:01:58.850\r\nRyan Rumpf: Language, there we go that is called a vanishing close activity.\r\n\r\n550\r\n01:01:59.190 --> 01:02:07.119\r\nRyan Rumpf: Now, if you work with middle school or high schoolers, then I would think of a different name. But that's a great example of comprehensible output.\r\n\r\n551\r\n01:02:07.520 --> 01:02:17.719\r\nRyan Rumpf: We are pushing the kids to to do this with that, said, Thank you all for joining us for this quick 1 h. Newcomer tips and dips.\r\n\r\n552\r\n01:02:18.200 --> 01:02:23.639\r\nYvonne Williams: Now I'm gonna answer a few questions. Ryan has another webinar, so he's gonna be leaving us shortly.\r\n\r\n553\r\n01:02:23.700 --> 01:02:32.880\r\nYvonne Williams: But thank you, Ryan, I wanna answer 2 questions. The 1st one is in regards to secondary high school students being placed in\r\n\r\n554\r\n01:02:32.910 --> 01:02:33.940\r\nYvonne Williams: a\r\n\r\n555\r\n01:02:34.130 --> 01:02:38.628\r\nYvonne Williams: if you have to go, the the webinar is over, I'm just gonna answer some questions.\r\n\r\n556\r\n01:02:38.910 --> 01:02:41.259\r\nMargie Marrero: We can stay for Q. And A, yeah.\r\n\r\n557\r\n01:02:41.260 --> 01:03:09.819\r\nYvonne Williams: The high school question. I think it's so important for us to understand that students can, regardless of their proficiency level, engage in grade level content expectations in their classes. It's actually more effective for them to be with their peers and to prepare for the scaffolds that are necessary for them to engage with that said, should we be translating everything? Probably not. So. The idea behind translating and translanguaging is.\r\n\r\n558\r\n01:03:09.880 --> 01:03:28.049\r\nYvonne Williams: there's these strategies we can use like preview Review. Can we give them the content, just like a syllabus, that they are going to be expected to engage in in English before we introduce the content? So. Yes, we can give them that. We can give them that as scaffolds and support.\r\n\r\n559\r\n01:03:28.050 --> 01:03:42.610\r\nYvonne Williams: and then start introducing the English so that they're making connections with what they understood in their home language and what they are hearing in English. So it has to be very strategic. There's too much. And somebody said it. Translation happening\r\n\r\n560\r\n01:03:42.610 --> 01:04:11.569\r\nYvonne Williams: that is, in many ways hindering the transition of understanding to then using English and understanding English. That's why the scaffolds are so important. We might have a scaffolding tip and dip next after we do a translanguaging one, because I think the scaffolds are key into ensuring that we don't just rely on trans translating, because that's the easy way to ensure that they're understanding. But we are here to ensure that they're becoming bilingual\r\n\r\n561\r\n01:04:11.690 --> 01:04:17.569\r\nYvonne Williams: in their home language and in English proficiency. So there needs to be that English piece\r\n\r\n562\r\n01:04:18.060 --> 01:04:20.539\r\nYvonne Williams: that we are needing. We need to be very\r\n\r\n563\r\n01:04:20.730 --> 01:04:28.200\r\nYvonne Williams: aware of. So that we're not just translating, because it is. It is difficult in many cases, to try to balance the 2,\r\n\r\n564\r\n01:04:28.480 --> 01:04:34.019\r\nYvonne Williams: and I think that was those were the only 2 questions that I saw that I thought were important to address.\r\n\r\n565\r\n01:04:34.020 --> 01:04:41.021\r\nMargie Marrero: We're still looking for. Who had information about the what grant? Because when you try Googling, what what grant.\r\n\r\n566\r\n01:04:41.390 --> 01:04:42.560\r\nAmy King: I was. Gonna say.\r\n\r\n567\r\n01:04:42.650 --> 01:04:45.080\r\nAmy King: I think, someone added.\r\n\r\n568\r\n01:04:45