1:17:54.110\r\nMargie Marrero: Yes.\r\n\r\n668\r\n01:17:54.110 --> 01:17:55.499\r\nAmy King: Something like that.\r\n\r\n669\r\n01:17:55.820 --> 01:18:09.159\r\nMargie Marrero: Calling it out, the teachers giving that meta metacognition, meta, linguistic awareness. Because that's that's where the learning happens. And I think that makes a key difference between not enabling, but rather pushing them, to to be empowered.\r\n\r\n670\r\n01:18:09.340 --> 01:18:09.740\r\nStacie Campbell: Okay.\r\n\r\n671\r\n01:18:10.120 --> 01:18:11.530\r\nStacie Campbell: thank you so much.\r\n\r\n672\r\n01:18:11.530 --> 01:18:12.899\r\nAmy King: You and your your\r\n\r\n673\r\n01:18:13.550 --> 01:18:18.472\r\nAmy King: knowledge. Hungry dog, had something that you wanted to ask about.\r\n\r\n674\r\n01:18:18.920 --> 01:18:37.030\r\nJulie Lowery: So Perry Perry's very inquisitive. There's 3 of them running around. He's just the lap one but yes, so I don't know if you're familiar with the benchmark curriculum. I'm in Georgia. And so it just got written into law that we are required to use one of their pre-approved Ela curriculums, and I like\r\n\r\n675\r\n01:18:37.370 --> 01:18:49.530\r\nJulie Lowery: aspects of the curriculum. I do like the text are great. It's these magazines. It teaches them to annotate and to underline and to circle and to do. And it's really writing heavy, which is where I like to focus on Esol. K. 5,\r\n\r\n676\r\n01:18:50.175 --> 01:18:53.509\r\nJulie Lowery: but so my main issue is\r\n\r\n677\r\n01:18:53.550 --> 01:18:57.980\r\nJulie Lowery: one thing that I don't like is the pacing of it like they are doing.\r\n\r\n678\r\n01:18:58.480 --> 01:19:25.229\r\nJulie Lowery: They have to do 3 lessons a day, and they have 90 min blocks, and if they finish we're lucky. And that's even just the English speaking. Kids can't keep up, and the teachers are struggling to even fit it all in. There's all these pieces and parts. They're required to do all of it. And so, you know, I've got 5 newcomers, actually 6 that are having to do this. From second to 5th grade. There's these just rigorous, intense, like end of, or there's\r\n\r\n679\r\n01:19:25.250 --> 01:19:44.960\r\nJulie Lowery: 2 end of week and one end of unit assessment, and they're aligned to be like Georgia milestones or like the standardized test. So it's a lot of reading and like reading passages or multiple passages and comparing paragraphs. And you know all this, and it's great. It's good, and I'm sure after a year or so of doing it all year, like we will see a lot of growth.\r\n\r\n680\r\n01:19:45.040 --> 01:20:00.129\r\nJulie Lowery: My current issue is, I don't know how to support my newcomers with this, like, there's just, there's so many pieces, and there's so many parts that it's even like the normal scaffolds and stuff that I would do like. There's just not enough time to prepare that\r\n\r\n681\r\n01:20:00.380 --> 01:20:05.772\r\nJulie Lowery: across 4 grade levels for all of those kids, and I'm struggling. If you have any advice.\r\n\r\n682\r\n01:20:06.900 --> 01:20:09.270\r\nYvonne Williams: But, Julie, this is something that\r\n\r\n683\r\n01:20:09.290 --> 01:20:17.849\r\nYvonne Williams: I stand on a soapbox, on, often with teachers, with regards to pacing. I wanna have the norm array union that says no pacing.\r\n\r\n684\r\n01:20:17.920 --> 01:20:25.789\r\nYvonne Williams: because it it really makes no. And and I don't want to go against any decisions made at the State level.\r\n\r\n685\r\n01:20:25.790 --> 01:20:26.660\r\nJulie Lowery: I know.\r\n\r\n686\r\n01:20:26.660 --> 01:20:38.840\r\nYvonne Williams: Pacing where you're not. The students are not moving in the direction of the pace is really not giving them time to truly understand the purpose. Behind each of the lessons\r\n\r\n687\r\n01:20:39.540 --> 01:20:51.770\r\nYvonne Williams: there are a lot of ideas I can share the most important, I think, if your administrator is really enforcing the pacing. And it's because the idea is, if you start to go\r\n\r\n688\r\n01:20:52.080 --> 01:20:55.559\r\nYvonne Williams: with the lessons where the students eventually will level out.\r\n\r\n689\r\n01:20:56.110 --> 01:21:09.350\r\nYvonne Williams: But a lot of curriculum does not allow for that kind of extra time to do it. So how can we ensure that the students are are getting some of the information that is within the window of the pace.\r\n\r\n690\r\n01:21:09.480 --> 01:21:23.949\r\nYvonne Williams: and we have to be very strategic and ensuring that they're getting those pieces ideally. That is not the right way to do it. But if we have to stick to the pacing, it's what can we do to ensure? They're capturing the most important\r\n\r\n691\r\n01:21:23.950 --> 01:21:40.889\r\nYvonne Williams: purpose behind this lessons. What is the purpose? What is the? And it's almost like what Amy said, thinking backwards at the end of this lesson, what is the most important concept that my students really need to understand in order for me to move on without all these other pieces, because there's probably a lot.\r\n\r\n692\r\n01:21:40.930 --> 01:21:43.599\r\nYvonne Williams: So you have to almost dissect\r\n\r\n693\r\n01:21:43.700 --> 01:21:51.500\r\nYvonne Williams: the lesson that's going to be in that pace to ensure that you, as a teacher, are saying, this is where my students at this proficiency level.\r\n\r\n694\r\n01:21:51.700 --> 01:22:02.739\r\nYvonne Williams: need support that they can achieve this goal and then communicate that to the students. Because if the students feel like you've moved on and they don't understand these other pieces they need to know.\r\n\r\n695\r\n01:22:02.750 --> 01:22:09.949\r\nYvonne Williams: you know. These pieces will come and see if you can incorporate those pieces into the next lesson. It's it's extra work.\r\n\r\n696\r\n01:22:10.200 --> 01:22:13.999\r\nJulie Lowery: That's the thing like it doesn't even like teach the same thing like one day you'll be doing grammar.\r\n\r\n697\r\n01:22:14.400 --> 01:22:16.020\r\nJulie Lowery: You'll be doing this the next day you'll be doing this.\r\n\r\n698\r\n01:22:16.020 --> 01:22:16.829\r\nMargie Marrero: Like there's no.\r\n\r\n699\r\n01:22:16.830 --> 01:22:17.829\r\nJulie Lowery: There's no, it's.\r\n\r\n700\r\n01:22:18.650 --> 01:22:20.469\r\nMargie Marrero: You don't feel that it's coherent.\r\n\r\n701\r\n01:22:20.470 --> 01:22:26.270\r\nJulie Lowery: Curriculum. That's like $800 per grade level that's made for Els that corresponds with it. But did we buy it.\r\n\r\n702\r\n01:22:27.630 --> 01:22:33.109\r\nYvonne Williams: I wonder if if so, something for you all to know that Weda created a\r\n\r\n703\r\n01:22:33.450 --> 01:22:48.849\r\nYvonne Williams: way to review curriculum. So if you ever are in a position to advocate Weda. Prime allows publishers to go through a review process where they look to see if all the components within the framework and their content. Grade level\r\n\r\n704\r\n01:22:49.170 --> 01:22:55.130\r\nYvonne Williams: curriculum is aligned to what Weda saying students need to do now with genre based learning.\r\n\r\n705\r\n01:22:55.400 --> 01:22:58.180\r\nYvonne Williams: If they have the prime seal, that means that they have\r\n\r\n706\r\n01:22:58.200 --> 01:23:03.999\r\nYvonne Williams: gone through the process. So if you ever are in that position to say, Do they have a weta prime seal?\r\n\r\n707\r\n01:23:04.050 --> 01:23:19.070\r\nYvonne Williams: Please do so because publishers are aware of the seal, and it would help them. In many cases. Some publishers that didn't get the seal are told why, and they go back and they fix it, and then they come back. So it is effective for publishers to go through it.\r\n\r\n708\r\n01:23:19.150 --> 01:23:22.850\r\nYvonne Williams: I think, as you mentioned. If the 1st lesson is grammar.\r\n\r\n709\r\n01:23:24.480 --> 01:23:31.370\r\nYvonne Williams: Skip ahead. See what the second lesson is. Can you kind of merge grammar with what's happening in the second lesson?\r\n\r\n710\r\n01:23:31.660 --> 01:23:37.650\r\nYvonne Williams: It like? I said, it's gonna require probably a Plc conversation of let's look at what's being required, and\r\n\r\n711\r\n01:23:37.670 --> 01:23:42.860\r\nYvonne Williams: and plan that way so that you can ensure that you're moving with the pace, but not\r\n\r\n712\r\n01:23:43.010 --> 01:23:43.830\r\nYvonne Williams: with all.\r\n\r\n713\r\n01:23:43.830 --> 01:23:47.820\r\nJulie Lowery: Stay on, stay on topic, but kind of slow it down for them.\r\n\r\n714\r\n01:23:48.180 --> 01:23:49.459\r\nAmy King: Yeah, as much as.\r\n\r\n715\r\n01:23:49.460 --> 01:23:51.610\r\nMargie Marrero: Possible with the planning.\r\n\r\n716\r\n01:23:52.080 --> 01:24:02.310\r\nAmy King: I can't remember who said this, and whoever said it said it much more eloquently than me. But when they were talking about the variables in learning. You know, you have students, you have curriculum. But the only thing that you can actually control\r\n\r\n717\r\n01:24:02.400 --> 01:24:03.770\r\nAmy King: is time.\r\n\r\n718\r\n01:24:03.940 --> 01:24:15.589\r\nAmy King: And yet we tell teachers they can't control time. Here's your scope and sequence document. Here's your pacing guide, you know, and that just doesn't make sense in terms of just the way people learn and the way people learn differently. So\r\n\r\n719\r\n01:24:15.620 --> 01:24:32.160\r\nAmy King: I'm I'm again with Yvonne like thinking about what's what's the most important thing? Or if it's a lot of reading. Are they reading? Because we're trying to teach comprehension strategies? Are they just reading to learn something? If that's the case, can I give it to them in their native language. Can I give it to a way that makes more sense\r\n\r\n720\r\n01:24:32.300 --> 01:24:32.900\r\nAmy King: cause? It's.\r\n\r\n721\r\n01:24:32.900 --> 01:24:34.459\r\nYvonne Williams: Do I have to do this one?\r\n\r\n722\r\n01:24:34.870 --> 01:24:36.300\r\nYvonne Williams: Do I have to do this.\r\n\r\n723\r\n01:24:36.300 --> 01:24:36.970\r\nMargie Marrero: Planning.\r\n\r\n724\r\n01:24:36.970 --> 01:24:38.130\r\nAmy King: Exactly. Yeah.\r\n\r\n725\r\n01:24:38.130 --> 01:24:55.770\r\nJulie Lowery: It's it's kind of all the things like they'll have multiple passages per week and per unit. And it's like they'll have one passage that like explains. So, for example, 3rd grade, they were doing animal adaptations for unit one. So it's like one passage is talking about camouflage. Well, then, one passage is talking about like outer coverings, and one passage is talking. And so\r\n\r\n726\r\n01:24:55.780 --> 01:24:59.150\r\nJulie Lowery: like they have to read each passage for comprehension.\r\n\r\n727\r\n01:24:59.250 --> 01:25:20.929\r\nJulie Lowery: to work on those skills. But then, as the unit moves forward, then they're having to compare and contrast the passages, or like pull information from the passages in order to write things about them. And like I just you know, and I know my kids are smart, and I know that they come to me with a vast array of knowledge in their 1st language. And so, you know, I mean, I try to translate at least so they understand what's going on, or let them listen to it the 1st time\r\n\r\n728\r\n01:25:21.170 --> 01:25:30.169\r\nJulie Lowery: in their 1st language, and then have them go back and listen to it again in English, so that they can start making connections, and like that one to one correspondence between the academic vocabulary.\r\n\r\n729\r\n01:25:30.230 --> 01:25:35.497\r\nJulie Lowery: But like, it's just, I mean, it's like it, snowballs. And it just it's it's out of control.\r\n\r\n730\r\n01:25:35.790 --> 01:25:39.140\r\nRosemary Trumbo: I'm curious to. I actually wanted to.\r\n\r\n731\r\n01:25:39.140 --> 01:25:39.770\r\nAmy King: Snowball gets.\r\n\r\n732\r\n01:25:39.770 --> 01:25:41.200\r\nRosemary Trumbo: Oh, sorry sorry go ahead.\r\n\r\n733\r\n01:25:41.200 --> 01:25:43.