    "title": "Translanguaging in Action: Strategies for Biliteracy & Beyond (Part 1/1)",
    "content": "Translanguaging in Action: Strategies for Biliteracy & Beyond  Wisconsin Center for Education Products and Services (WCEPS)  Please sign in using the QR code of the link below! bit.ly/CARESIGN  © 2024 CARE Coaching  CARE Coaching  Wisconsin Center for Education Products and Services  • Understanding translanguaging as  a language practice. Objetivos  • Exploring its role as a pedagogy: of the  Session  Stance, Design and Shift.  • Explore a strategy that enhances  biliteracy development.  Introductions  Logistics: Getting to Know You  1.  Middle: Name  2.  Bottom left: Years in Education  3.  Bottom right: Position  4.  Top right: How familiar with the Framework  1. Huh? | 2. Heard of them | 3. Explored | 4. Used | 5. Give me that clicker!  Getting to Know You  Setting the Stage  How familiar are you with this topic?  1  – I am curious and here to learn about translanguaging  2  – I have heard a bit about it but not sure I quite understand  3  – I have heard about it, I may use it myself as a language practice, but I am not sure what it means as a pedagogical practice.  4  – I have some training and understanding of translanguaging as a language practice and pedagogical practice  5  – I apply this in my classroom/school setting and can share some expert insights  Translanguaging as a Language Practice of Bi-Multilingual People  What is Translanguaging?  Community Building-La Carrera  In 60 seconds write down every noun you know, using your whole linguistic repertoire, to name the entity with wheels in this picture.  Debrief  How might your students respond to this same prompt? What linguistic resources might they draw from?  Activity:  ❖  Compare your lists and reflect on how your linguistic repertoires shaped your choices.  ❖  Discuss how to incorporate similar identity-affirming warm-ups in the classroom.  Linguistic Repertoire – 1 Image  Car -standard  Automobile – more formal or old fashioned  Ride -nice ride-slang  Bucket – another word for older car.  Coche — common in Spain, parts of Mexico.  Carro — popular in much of Latin America (Mexico, Colombia, Venezuela, etc.). Vehicle -general  Auto — more common in Argentina, Chile, Uruguay.  Hooptie – worn-out or old car  Automóvil — the formal term, used everywhere.  Buggy – historical reference  Máquina — sometimes used informally in Cuba and other Caribbean countries (\"la máquina\" = the car).  What is Translanguaging Ofelia Garcia?  Translanguaging is the act performed by bilinguals of accessing different linguistic features or various modes of what are described as autonomous languages, in order to maximize communicative potential. Ofelia García (2009: 140) Translanguaging is about communication, not about language itself.  What is Translanguaging?  http://bit.ly/TREERoots  With a partner or at you tables:  What is  Translanguaging?  ➢  Create a summary of translanguaging  ➢  A question you still have about translanguaging  ➢  Or what stood out from our exploration of the meaning of translanguaging  Classroom or Real Life Application  Translanguaging in Action  Classroom Vignette:  Ms. Rivera is leading a 5th-grade English science lesson on ecosystems. During discussion, Carla shares:  “So, los animales que viven en el desierto tienen adaptaciones… like, they store water in their bodies. Porque no hay mucha lluvia, y necesitan conservar el agua para sobrevivir. It's like a cactus — también tiene esa adaptación.”  Ms. Rivera responds:  “Yes! Great use of your full language skills to explain adaptations. Can anyone build on Carla’s idea about conserving water?”  Translanguaging in Action  Connecting to Theory  •  This is translanguaging: not switching between languages but blending linguistic features fluidly to make meaning.  •  The teacher affirms and builds on this use, promoting deeper engagement and conceptual clarity.  •  The teacher uses strengths from one language to encourage use in another language.  Translanguaging in Action  Connecting to Theory  •  Carla uses her unitary linguistic repertoire (García & Li Wei, 2014) to express understanding.  \"Unitary linguistic repertoire\" refers to the idea that a multilingual person has one single, flexible set of language resources, not separate, isolated languages in their mind.  Instead of thinking, \"Here is my English box, and here is my Spanish box,\" the unitary view sees everything: words, grammar, styles, tones — as part of one integrated system that the person moves through depending on the situation, audience, and purpose.  Reflect  Share an experience when you or a student used more than one language to communicate, connect ideas, or show understanding.  With a partner or at you tables:  Reflect: Classroom or Real-Life Application  ➢  Create a summary of translanguaging in context  ➢  An example of a real-life scenario  ➢  Or what stood out from our exploring real-life examples and their implications  Understanding Translanguaging as a Language Practice of Bi-Multilingual People  Terms to Keep in Mind when Discussing Translanguaging  Dual Language Education  Programs that teach students in two languages and  who’s goal are bilingualism and biliteracy  Language Allocation Policy  A systematic plan for language development in schools to determine language of instruction, curriculum and assessment  Named  Languages  A term coined by Otheguy, Garcia and Reid (2015-2018) that describes groups of features socially recognized and defined as “a language” (i.e.  Spanish, English, etc)  Translanguaging  The way that bi-multilingual people use features of their unitary linguistic system that do not necessarily align with socially recognized and defined named languages  Both languages share a deeper cognitive & linguistic foundation & are interdependent of each other.  Reference:  Cummins, J. (1981). The role of primary language development in promoting educational success for language minority students. In California State Department of Education (Ed.), Schooling and language minority students: A theoretical framework (pp. 3–49). Los Angeles: California State University, Evaluation, Dissemination and Assessment Center  Translanguaging in Dual Language Education  While DLE programs have grown in popularity, so too have more dynamic understandings of bilingualism that challenge conceptualizations of bilingual speakers as two monolinguals in one (García, 2009; García & Li Wei, 2014).  This view of bilingualism posits that rather than having two separate linguistic systems that correspond to two “named languages,” such as English, Spanish or Mandarin,  bilingual and multilingual people have one complex, unitary linguistic system from which they use different features to communicate and make meaning (Otheguy,  García & Reid, 2015, 2018).  Translanguaging in Dual Language  Depending on the context, bi-and multilingual people may use features of their linguistic repertoire in ways that align with what we recognize as “monolingual” uses of English or Spanish or Mandarin.  However, among bi-and multilingual friends and families and in bi-and multilingual neighborhoods, people may engage in translanguaging using English and Spanish or English and a home language fluidly in ways that blur the boundaries of one named language and another. (Sánchez, M. T., García, O., & Solorza, C. (2017). Reframing language allocation policy in dual language bilingual education. Bilingual Research Journal, 1-15.)  Discuss: Common Confusion  Translation  Interpretation  Translanguaging  Code-Switching  Converting written text from one language to another while maintaining the original text’s content, style and context.  Orally convey spoken or signed language from one language to another, either simultaneously or consecutively.  Moving between languages based on need “in the moment.”  One way, among many, to use language. Adjusting how you use language based on who you are around.  Example  Example  Example  Example  Giving students a version of The Little Prince in the home language so they can read the same book as their English-speaking classmates.  A teacher pausing instruction so the paraprofessional can explain the content of the lesson into Swahili. Then continues with the lesson.  A student is working on an essay and does all of the brainstorming and planning in Spanish. Then writes the essay in English.  A student says  “Miss, I can’t finish  the worksheet porque tengo que ir al bathroom.”  A word from Jose Medina:  Bridging, Translation & Interpretation  What is Translanguaging?  https://www.translanguagingeducation.org/translanguagingpedagogy  What is Translanguaging?  The bottom of the tree highlights the \"why\" behind translanguaging: it’s not just about switching languages for convenience, it’s about honoring identity, promoting justice, and enabling deeper learning.  These roots support the visible classroom practices shown in the top half of the tree, such as using home language groups, mixing school/home languages, and encouraging students to use their full linguistic repertoires.  bit.ly/WABERoots  https://www.translanguagingeducation.org/translanguagingpedagogy  Two Hands -One Drum  Roots/Hands & Identity – Principles  Branches/Drum & Sound – Strategies & Activities  Reflect: Understanding Translanguaging as a Language Practice of Bi-Multilingual People  With a partner or at you tables:  ➢  Share an image of how translanguaging affects Bi-Multilingual Learners  ➢  An example the roots of the tree  ➢  Or what stood out from our discussions on translanguaging as a practice  Translanguaging as a Pedagogy  Framing Translanguaging as a Pedagogy  •  Translanguaging as a pedagogical approach is inherently asset-based and culturally responsive.  •  Translanguaging is an approach for implementing language learning with a mindset of learning as a process to teach students HOW to think versus WHAT to think.  •  Translanguaging is a research-based approach to acquiring and maintaining multiple languages (versus using “interventions” to teach English).  Pedagogical vs. Spontaneous  Pedagogical translanguaging -teachers intentionally plan activities that integrate multiple languages for learning  Spontaneous translanguaging -naturally occurs in everyday bilingual communication.  Cenoz and Gorter (2017, 2020)  Stance, Design, and Shift -The Pedagogy  Centers students' holistic linguistic system and provides opportunities to use their entire linguistic repertoire, rather than just specific named languages.  Affirms both the bi-multilingual practices of the community and those supported by the school community.  Stance, Design, and Shift -The Pedagogy  The Translanguaging Classroom Framework  Three Strands of the Translanguaging Classroom: Stance  •  “The philosophical, ideological, or belief system that teachers draw from to develop their pedagogical framework” (p.27).  •  JUNTOS/TOGETHER –  •  Home and school language practices  •  Family, community, and school  •  Teachers and students in a democratic classroom space  Stance  Three Strands of the Translanguaging Classroom: Design  •  “How we strategically plan instruction to work within the translanguaging corriente…the design is flexible; it intentionally connects bilingual students’ home and community language practices and identities to the language practices and identities deemed appropriate for school settings, while working to address social justice” (p.61).  •  A translanguaging design for:  •  The classroom space  •  The instruction  •  The assessment  Stance Design  Three Strands of the Translanguaging Classroom: Shifts  •  “…those unplanned moment-by-moment decisions that teachers make in response to the flow of the translanguaging corriente in their classrooms” (p.77).  •  Shifts “respond to content and language needs and interests that are not built directly into the translanguaging design, but which students need to be successful” (p.77).  Stance Design Shifts  A Central Concept: The Translanguaging Corriente  •  Refers to the diverse, fluid language and cultural practices that flow through classrooms, even when invisible  •  The characteristics of the translanguaging corriente in any classroom reflects the language repertoires of the bilingual students and teachers.  •  “The translanguaging corriente is ever-changing, shaped by the repertoires of both students and teachers.” García, Johnson & Seltzer (2017)  I Translanguaging �-�· Home Language V 3□� om Lan Cultural Experiences in Schools] c es  Design Your Class Translanguaging River  Sketch your classroom's language flow:  ✓  Label tributaries (sources of language input)  ✓  Identify obstacles (barriers to fluid language use)  ✓  Mark supports (teacher practices, school norms, policies)  Discuss in PLC’s, your grade level or administrators:  How can you strengthen the flow? What supports can you build into your instruction?  What does it sound and look like in the DL classroom?  Cultivating the Pedagogy of Translanugaging fo rk-12 Transformative Education, Chapter 7 Transformative Translanguaguing Teacher-Student Interactions in a Third Grade Dual-language Bilingual Education Classroom  With a partner or at you tables:  Reflect: Translanguaging as a Pedagogy  ➢  Share what you understand about translanguaging as a pedagogy  ➢  An example of the Corriente  ➢  Or what stood out from our understanding of stance, design and shifts  Translanguaging as a Pedagogy:  A Math lens  ¿ Que observas? ¿ Que te preguntas?  What’s My Value?  = 19 = 6 = 14 + + + + + +  Why Translanguage for Mathematical Reasoning?  What is procedural fluency?  Procedural fluency is an essential component of equitable teaching and is necessary to developing mathematical proficiency and mathematical agency. Each and every student must have access to teaching that connects concepts to procedures, explicitly develops a reasonable repertoire of strategies and algorithms, provides substantial opportunities for students to learn to choose from among the strategies and algorithms in their repertoire, and implements assessment practices that attend to all components of fluency. -NCTM Position  •  Students must reason and language through substantial opportunities for connections and strategies with agency.  Mathematical Language Routines  https://tinyurl.com/MathLangRoutinesInfoGraphic  Support Sense Making  Que rol tiene el transleguaje en esta rutina?  Que rol tiene el transleguaje en esta rutina?  •  Apoya organizar informacion en una forma acesible  •  “crowdsource language” collaboratively  •  Multiple opportunities to express ideas  Let’s Recap Translanguaging is:  •  A language practice which sometimes even enacted within one language.  Ex: Shakespearean English to modern English or changes in register or formality such as with  Korean or Spanish (also varies by country).  •  An inherently asset-based approach to teaching bi-multilingual students.  •  A mindset or framework for dual language and bilingual education  •  A fluid, dynamic, and holistic approach to empowering students to use  their full linguistic repertoires for learning content and multiple languages.  •  A pedagogical approach with three strands to enact theory: Stance,  Design, & Shifts.  With a partner or at you tables:  Reflect: Translanguaging Pedagogy – Math Application  ➢  What stood out about applying it in a Math context?  ➢  What will you take away when attending to math concepts?  ➢  What is something new that made you think or say “a-ha”?  Role of Translanguaging in Biliteracy  An Example  The teacher and her students read the book Dear Primo by Duncan Tonatiuh, in which two cousins, Charlie and Carlitos, use English and Spanish to write letters to each other about their lives in Mexico and the U.S. The teacher knew that, like Charlie, most of her students had cousins and other family members in countries outside the U.S. and designed a class project that best aligns to their identity.  An Example  ✓  Design a bilingual interview protocol that asked a family member outside the U.S. about different parts of their life in their home country  ✓  Conduct the bilingual interview with the family member (via email, social media, or phone) and transcribe excerpts from the interview  ✓  Create their own versions of Dear Primo in which they use the interviews to construct a back and forth “letter” comparing their lives to the lives of family members outside the U.S.  Once students had written their letters, the teachers invited their families and communities into the classroom to watch their performances and give their feedback, which was given using both English and their home languages.  She also compiled students’ letters into a book that became part of the whole-class library.  Detectives Lingüísticos  ❖  Regardless of the language of the day, every two days Juana conducts an activity which she calls “Language Detectives/Detectives Lingüísticos.”  ❖  During this short activity, Juana focuses on a single feature or single use of English and español. She writes on the whiteboard an example in both languages and gives linguistically heterogeneous groups of students a magnifying glass to engage them as language detectives or detectives lingüísticos.  ❖  Students are free to use all their language practices to discuss among themselves how the two languages differ. They then come up with other examples along similar lines.  With a partner or at you tables:  Reflect: Role of Translanguaging in Biliteracy  ➢  What was a new idea when thinking of biliteracy?  ➢  How does this apply to your context?  ➢  What is something you will change or enhance about your instruction and biliteracy through translanguaging?  Reflect: Role of Translanguaging in Biliteracy, an example  Survey  https://tinyurl.com/WCEPSSurvey",
