Celebrate Each Other  Using the PLDs  Using the WIDA PLDs  Criteria  End of Level 1  End of Level 2  End of Level 3  End of Level 4  End of Level 5  Level 6  DISCOURSE  Organization of language  Understand how coherent texts (spoken, written, multimodal) are created... I I  around topics (all about pandas) with repetition, rhyming, and common language patterns  around topics (all about pandas) with short sentences  around topics (habitat, diet, behavior) through multiple related simple sentences  to meet a purpose in a series of extended  sentences  to meet a purpose in a short text (to inform, narrate, entertain)  to meet a purpose I through generic I  I  (not genre-specific) I  3  organizational patterns I in texts (beginning, I middle, end) ' I  DISCOURSE  Cohesion of  language  Understand how ideas are connected across a whole text through... I I  patterned language with repetitive words, phrases, and sentences  repetitive chunks of meaning across a text  (Brown bear, brown bear, what do you see?)  some frequently used cohesive devices  (demonstratives: these, those, that, this)  a few different types of cohesive devices  (repetition, pronoun referencing, etc.)  multiple types of cohesive devices  (synonyms, antonyms, (We are all alike. We are all different.)  a variety of cohesive I devices that connect I  I  I  larger meaningful  3  chunks of text (class/ I subclass: animals like  I  dogs, cats, tigers) I  I  DISCOURSE  Density of language  Understand how ideas are elaborated or condensed through... I I  frequently used single word noun groups  (dinosaurs)  frequently used multi-word noun groups (big tall dinosaurs)  multi-word noun groups with connectors (a big and hungry dinosaur)  expanded noun groups with classifiers  (the biggest meat-eating dinosaurs)  expanded noun groups with prepositional phrases (the meat-eating dinosaurs in the jungle)  expanded noun groups { with embedded :  I  clauses (the biggest meat-eating dinosaurs,  I  who lived at that time) I I  SENTENCE  Grammatical  complexity  Understand how meanings are extended or enhanced through... I I  words, pictures, phrases, and chunks of language (turtle eggs)  chunks of language  (turtles swimming)  simple sentences (Turtles swim in the ocean.)  related simple sentences (Turtles are reptiles. They like warm water.)  multiple related simple sentences (There are many types of turtles.  Some live in the ocean.  Other turtles live in lakes and rivers.)  '  simple and compound I  '  sentences with familiar ways of  3  combining clauses 3 (using coordinating 3  '  '  conjunctions: Some I turtles eat only sea I  '  grasses, but others I  I  eat shrimp, crabs, and I I small fish.) I I  I  I  I  WORD,  PHRASE  Precision of  language  Understand how precise meanings are created through everyday, cross-disciplinary, and technical language through...  repealed words and phrases in familiar contexts and topics  (classroom helpers)  frequently used words and phrases in familiar contexts and topics  (Would you like to share... ?)  situation-specific words and phrases  (Plant the seeds in this pot)  an increasing number of words and phrases  (Tell me about your picture on the left)  a growing number of words and phrases in a variety of contexts  (How many red triangles are there?)  an expanding number ' I of words and phrases, y  '  '  including idioms and I collocations (plus and I  : '  minus)  What is Reading & Research Background  What is reading?  ▪  Tehre is mcuh dbetae aobut waht mkaes good radenig intsuriton. But goo_ read_ use man_ strate_ to unders_ writ_ tex_.  ▪  The moon revolves around the _____.  Semantic Knowledge - Knowledge of the world  ▪  This is called a smochter. This is another smochter. There are two ______.  Syntactic Knowledge - Knowledge of the language  ▪  A wicket consists of three ____ and two ____.  Cultural Capital  Stages of Literacy Development (L1)  ▪  Stage 1: Pre-reading (Birth-Age 6)  ▪  Stage 2: Decoding  ▪  Stage 2: Confirmation & Fluency  ▪  Stage 3: Reading for Learning  ▪  Stage 4: Reading from Multiple Viewpoints  ▪  Stage 5: Construction & Reconstruction  Chall  on Stages of Reading  Development  New Learning Online  Chall (1983) -  Scarborough’s Rope  National Reading Panel Report (2000)  ▪  Over a 2-year period, the panel of 14 experts focused on five key areas (in no particular order):  •  Phonemic Awareness  •  Phonics  •  Fluency  •  Vocabulary  •  Comprehension  ▪  This continues with the Science of Reading.  Information Gap Activity  ▪  Find a partner and designate each as partner A and partner B.  ▪  Find the folder labeled A or B and put up the barrier to where you can safely put your paper out of sight from your partner.  ▪  Without showing your partner your paper, work together to complete the missing information on your paper.  ▪  When you are finished, remove the barrier and check for accuracy.  ▪  Finally, write your main takeaways in layman’s terms in the box on the bottom of your paper.  Phonological Processing  Phonological Memory:  Process and recall sounds in short term memory  Phonological Naming: Efficiently retrieve words from long term memory using known sounds  ▪  Cross-language transfer exists in phonological awareness between languages (Branum-Martin et al., 2012)  ▪  Literacy transfer from one language to another depends on the similarities and differences between their writing systems, including the units of speech. (Peregoy & Boyle, 2000)  ▪  Phonemic awareness is about listening to and distinguishing sounds of language; it is about understanding and manipulating the sounds orally and it is best internalized when it moves from whole to part, known to unknown (Gibbons, 2015)  ▪  English proficiency is not a necessary precursor to phonemic awareness although vocabulary must also be explicitly taught through context (Brown & Ortiz, 2014).  Phonological Awareness: Understand and manipulate the sound structure of oral language  Rhyming  Onset-Rime Alliteration Syllable Awareness Phoneme Identification Phoneme Blending Phoneme Deletion Phoneme Manipulation Phoneme Segmentation  Phonological Awareness Strategies  ▪  Thumbs Up, Thumbs Down (Rhyming)  ▪  What Word is This?  ▪  What Sounds Can You Hear  Phonics  Letter Recognition: Identify & name letters of the alphabet Grapho-phonemic Knowledge: articulate a sound associated with a letter  Decoding: Translate a word from print to speech  Morphological Awareness: Understand meaning of word parts  ▪  Both national panels found a positive correlation between explicit and systematic phonics instruction. However, the panel for MLs noted special considerations must be made for oral language proficiency, background knowledge and attention to new sounds of the language.  ▪  Learn Languages  -  Grammar and Vocabulary (mylanguages.org)  Teachers need to be aware of similarities between languages and use them to their advantage.  ▪  Phonics instruction can begin with letter naming and the corresponding sound (Cardenas-Hagan, 2020).  ▪  “Children in the early stages of learning to read should never be expected to simply sound out words they have never met and can’t say….” (Gibbons, 2015)  Phonics Strategies  ▪  Alphabet book  ▪  Progressive Words  ▪  Memory Reading Game  Fluency  Accuracy:  Reading words correctly  Rate:  Reading effortlessly  Prosody:  Reading with expression  ▪  The relationship between fluency and comprehension is moderated by English oral language skills (Crosson & Lesaux, 2010).  ▪  Engaged and repetitive reading practices with systematic support is most effective at developing reading fluency (Mathes et al., 2005).  ▪  Students need a strong foundational base in phonological and writing skills to support the development of fluency (Cardenas-Hagan, 2020).  Fluency Strategies  Vocabulary  Meaning:  Literal or colloquial meaning in a given context  Grammatical Construction: Placement of a word in a sentence  Morphological Variations: Root word and derivations  Semantic  Variations:  Synonyms and  antonyms  ▪  Vocabulary knowledge is a critical component in all children’s language development and plays an important role in gaining conceptual knowledge, building background knowledge and the overall comprehension of text.  ▪  A focus on the word level encountered in content-areas classes facilitates communication during academic tasks and builds an ELs academic language use and academic thinking.  ▪  Children’s first words are reflective of their lived experiences and consequently come to school with differences in the depth and breadth of their vocabulary knowledge.  ▪  Native language support and maintenance should be prioritized as ELs engage in adult-child conversations using linguistic and cultural capital that are more readily available to stimulate oral language and knowledge development in culturally relevant ways.  Vocabulary Strategies  ▪  Key Principles  •  Make connections to prior learning (and especially social equivalents)  •  Provide multiple and varied opportunities to say and hear new words  •  Include opportunities for adult-student interaction  •  Promote native language vocabulary learning  Vocabulary Development  ▪  Tiers of Words • Level 1 – basic words  o  Line, table, run, numbers  •  Level 2 – general academic words  o  Equation, difference, analyze, determine  •  Level 3 – content-specific words  o  Photosynthesis, algorithm, mitosis  Most experts say to focus on Tier II words, but MLs may need instruction at all levels depending on their ELP level.  ▪  Strategies:  •  Graphic Organizers (Word Detectives)  •  Vocabulary Journals  •  Charades/Movement  •  Semantic mapping  •  Word parts  Comprehension  Summarizing:  A reader synthesizes the important ideas of a text in a clear sequence  Story Structure: Beginning, middle, end; characters, setting, problem/solution, etc.  Monitoring: Students question and self-assess their understanding of the story  ▪  Educators must adjust and adapt instruction to meet the unique needs of ELs, including an emphasis on building oral language and taking advantage of a student’s native language.  ▪  ELs often encounter concepts or events that are not consistent with their lived experiences or cultural background, but they can grasp these concepts by making connections or noting differences.  ▪  A crucial practice that promotes better and faster comprehension is by taking advantage of the native language by bringing explicit attention to cognates.  Comprehension Strategies  ▪  Principles  •  Provide instruction for transfer of skills across languages.  •  Combine approaches involving direct instruction, student-teacher and student to student interaction.  •  Utilize before, during and after reading strategies  •  Teach, model and practice routines  ▪  Pre-Reading Strategies  •  Preview the text  o  Predict from Title  o  Predict from Images  o  Sequence Illustrations  o  Reader Questions  o  Pre-read in native language (dual language books)  ▪  During Reading  •  Apply context clues  •  Modeled Reading  •  Pause & Predict  •  Shadow Reading  •  Re-reading for detail  ▪  After Reading  •  Reader’s Theater  •  Story maps  •  Timelines  •  Graphic Organizers  •  Cooperative Learning  Break  Transferring Reading to an Additional Language  “Literacy in a second language develops as in the first – globally, not linearly, and in a variety of rich contexts.” – Pat Rigg & Virginia Allen  National Reading Panel (ELL Edition)  ▪  ELs benefit from the five areas described in the original report, but adjustments are necessary especially with oral language development.  ▪  Oral language proficiency and literacy in the L1 aids development of literacy in English  ▪  Individual differences contribute significantly: age, prior learning, cognition, oral language proficiency.  ▪  Adequate assessments are necessary to determine strengths and weaknesses.  ▪  There is limited evidence about the effectiveness in determining whether MLs require a reading intervention.  ▪  Home language experiences have a positive impact on literacy.  ▪  There is little evidence of sociocultural factors and student literacy outcomes.  Common Underlying Proficiency  Native Language Literacy  •  The Common Underlying proficiency model is a part of Cummins’ Interdependence Principle.  •  It argues that our approach to literacy (and education in general) and the native language determines a student’s ultimate success.  •  Students who drop their native language in favor of the 2nd language results in students who are semi-lingual and semi-literate in two languages.  Student characteristics  Students at or above grade level in their home language Developing readers in the home language Beginning readers  Reading in a 2nd Language  El verano es una estación del año; empieza el 21 de junio. Los días son muy largos. Hace mucho calor y siempre esta soleado. Muchas familias se van de vacaciones. A los niños les encanta nadar, ir a la playa y jugar en el parque. Usan pantalones cortos, sandalias y lentes de sol. Comen muchos helados.  1)  Cómo es el clima en el verano?  2)  Cuándo empieza el verano?  3)  Qué hacen los niños en el verano?  Some phonics discussions  ▪  Spanish is a phonetic language – if you see a letter, say the letter!  ▪  Pure vowel sounds!  • A – ah – ma  • E – a – me  • I – e – mi  • O – oh – mo  • U – oo – mu  ▪  Accented vowels – the right emphasis on the right syllable!  ▪  ñ – like the ny in canyon  ▪  There are others – ie. v is a b sound  Some vocabulary  ▪  Verano  Summer  ▪  Estación  Season  ▪  Calor  Heat  ▪  Soleado  Sunny  ▪  Niños  Children  ▪  Encanta  Love  ▪  Playa  Beach  ▪  Lentes  Glasses  ▪  Helados  Ice Cream  Reading in a 2nd Language  El verano es una estación del año; empieza es 21 de junio. Los días son muy largos.  Hace mucho calor y siempre esta soleado. Muchas familias se van de vacaciones. A los niños les encanta nadar, ir a la playa y jugar en el parque. Usan pantalones cortos, sandalias y lentes de sol. Comen muchos helados.  1)  Cómo es el clima en el verano?  2)  Cuándo empieza el verano?  3)  Qué hacen los niños en el verano?  El verano es una estación del año; empieza es 21 de junio. Los días son muy largos. Hace mucho calor y siempre esta soleado. Muchas familias se van de vacaciones. A los niños les encanta nadar, ir a la playa y jugar en el parque. Usan pantalones cortos, sandalias y lentes de sol. Comen muchos helados.  1)  Cómo es el clima en el verano?  2)  Cuándo empieza el verano?  3)  Qué hacen los niños en el verano?  El verano es una estación del año; empieza es 21 de junio. Los días son muy largos. Hace mucho calor y siempre esta soleado. Muchas familias se van de vacaciones. A los niños les encanta nadar, ir a la playa y jugar en el parque. Usan pantalones cortos, sandalias y lentes de sol. Comen muchos helados.  1)  What is the weather like in the summer?  2)  When does summer begin?  3)  What do children do in the summer?  Learning to Read in a 2nd Language  Stages of Literacy Development (L1)  ▪  Stage 1: Pre-reading (Birth-Age 6)  ▪  Stage 2: Decoding  ▪  Stage 2: Confirmation & Fluency  ▪  Stage 3: Reading for Learning  ▪  Stage 4: Reading from Multiple Viewpoints  ▪  Stage 5: Construction & Reconstruction  Chall  on Stages of Reading  Development  New Learning Online  Chall (1983) -  ▪  Stage 1: Pre-reading  Oral Language Development, Background Building, Cultural Connections  ▪  Stage 1: Pre-reading  Students need time to learn foundational phonemic awareness and phonics skills and to develop automaticity.  ▪  Stage 2: Decoding  ▪  Stage 2: Decoding  ▪  Stage 2: Confirmation & Fluency  ▪  Stage 2: Confirmation & Fluency  ▪  Stage 3: Reading for Learning  ▪  Stage 3: Reading for Learning  ▪  Stage 4: Reading from Multiple Viewpoints  ▪  Stage 4: Reading from Multiple Viewpoints  ▪  Stage 5: Construction & Reconstruction  ▪  Stage 5: Construction & Reconstruction  Stages of Literacy Development (L1)  ▪  Stage 1: Pre-reading  Immediate attention to key sight words, both common and “first words”  ▪  Stage 2: Decoding  ▪  Stage 2: Confirmation & Fluency  ▪  Stage 3: Reading for Learning  5:  Construction & Reconstruction  ▪  Stage 4: Reading from Multiple Viewpoints  ▪  Stage  ▪  Stage 1: Pre-reading  Immediate attention to key sight words, both common and “first words”  ▪  Stage 2: Decoding  ▪  Stage 2: Confirmation & Fluency  ▪  Stage 3: Reading for Learning  5:  Construction & Reconstruction  ▪  Stage 4: Reading from Multiple Viewpoints  ▪  Stage  ▪  Stage 1: Pre-reading  ▪  Stage 2: Decoding  ▪  Stage 2: Confirmation & Fluency  ▪  Stage 3: Reading for Learning  Provide audio options for students to participate in mainstream classrooms  ▪  Stage 4: Reading from Multiple Viewpoints  ▪  Stage 5: Construction & Reconstruction  Quotes on SIFE Reading or Video  Lunch  Energizer – Running Dictation  Form teams of 2  Identify one as A and one as B  Take a look at your sheet. On your paper are a series of quotes, but parts are missing.  You will find those quotes on the wall outside.  A will run outside, find quote 1, run back and tell partner B, who fills in the missing piece.  Then it is B’s turn. The process repeats until all four quotes are complete.  The winner gets bragging rights and mad respect!  Learning Language Through Reading  Creating the Opportunity to Read  ▪  “Given appropriate texts, learners develop their language skills in the course of reading itself, because the patterns of language are ‘open to notice’ in written language in a way they are not in spoken language. So the more fluently and widely EL students read, the more they will gain exposure to the second language.” –Pauline Gibbons  ▪  “Many years ago, a group of researchers tried to sort out the factors that helped children to remember what they’d been reading. They found that how interested the students were in the passage was thirty times more important than how ‘readable’ the passage was.” -Alfie Kohn  ▪  “An obvious way to increase reading achievement: increase access to books by investing more in library holdings and services….” -Stephen Krashen  Vocabulary Development Reading Comprehension  La Masia is more than just a soccer pitch, it is a complex. It includes a five-story building plus training grounds, fields, locker rooms and cafeterias. There is also a dormitory to house players who are not from Barcelona and thus can’t commute to the academy from their parents’ home. It is a serious place, but for a soccer-loving kid, it is also paradise.  Vocabulary Development Reading Comprehension  Direct Context Clues  La Masia is more than just a soccer pitch, it is a complex. It includes a five-story building plus training grounds, fields, locker rooms and cafeterias. There is also a dormitory to house players who are not from Barcelona and thus can’t commute to the academy from their parents’ home. It is a serious place, but for a soccer-loving kid, it is also paradise.  Vocabulary Development Reading Comprehension  Inferred Context Clues  La Masia is more than just a soccer pitch, it is a complex. It includes a five-story building plus training grounds, fields, locker rooms and cafeterias. There is also a dormitory to house players who are not from Barcelona and thus can’t commute to the academy from their parents’ home. It is a serious place, but for a soccer-loving kid, it is also paradise.  Vocabulary Development Reading Comprehension  La Masia is more than just a soccer pitch, it is a complex. It includes a five-story building plus training grounds, fields, locker rooms and cafeterias. There is also a dormitory to house players who are not from Barcelona and thus can’t commute to the academy from their parents’ home. It is a serious place, but for a soccer-loving kid, it is also paradise.  More Abstract  Teaching Context Clues  ▪  Sequence (Biemiller, 2010)  •  Before first reading: Introduce 3 words essential for comprehension.  •  Immediately after 1st read: Explicitly teach 4-5 word meanings.  o  Show students how to understand meaning based on context and pictures.  •  Subsequent readings: teach 4-5 additional words.  •  Last reading: review each word taught during the week.  Native Language Literacy  ▪  All students deserve to enjoy a good book and develop a love of reading. The students will read in English one day, but while they are learning they should still have access to a good book to read for pleasure.  ▪  A multilingual library sends a powerful message and supports a comprehensive home literacy program.  Oral Language Development  Oral Language Development  ▪  Think about how children learn their first language. How does that happen?  Total Physical Response  TPR Practice  Elementary: Total Physical Response Variations  ▪  TPR Circles - After practicing the movements, students get in a circle. The teacher says a word and the last person that does it is out. It proceeds until there is one winner.  ▪  Simon Says – TPR used with the rules of Simon Says.  ▪  TPR Sounds – After practicing the movements, the teacher adds sounds. The two methods can then be used interchangeably.  ▪  Slap Vocabulary – similar to slap jack, the teacher (or another student) places 2 or more vocabulary cards on the floor. The teacher says the name and the two competing students slap the card. Whoever has the most cards at the end wins.  ▪  Vocabulary BINGO – Students play bingo with vocabulary images or even numbers.  Listening Key Ideas  Two-way  Interpersonal Topics  Quadrant A  Participating in:  A conversation at lunch A conversation on the bus FaceTime/Video chat  Quadrant C  Participating in:  An academic conversation Collaborative work Giving/receiving instructions  Quadrant B  Listening to:  Someone telling a joke Someone telling a story (anecdote/narrative)  Quadrant D  Listening to:  A YouTube video  A lecture  A recording  One-way  Information- based Topics  Nunan, 1990 & Gibbons, 2015  The Last Word  ▪  Each table has a set of before, during or after reading strategies  ▪  Number off 1-4  ▪  Take 1 minute to read and understand your strategy.  ▪  Number 1 summarizes the strategy only. No additional thoughts or opinions. Then each team member offers an opinion or potential use for the strategy. Number 1 gets the last word and offers a final opinion or potential use.  ▪  Then number 2 goes in the same format, then number three and finally number 4.  Key Ideas on Developing Speaking  ▪  Is there any way to get this video embedded?  ▪  Doing and Talking Math and Science  (wceruw.org)  Productive Talk Moves  ▪  Chapin (2009)  •  Revoicing – “so you’re saying that it’s an even number.”  •  Repeating – “can you repeat what you just said.”  •  Reasoning – “do you agree or disagree”  •  Adding on – “would someone like to add something to what ___ said?”  •  Waiting – “take your time, I’ll wait.”  ▪  Zweirs (2014)  •  Clarifying – “What else do we need to consider”  •  Analyzing – “How do all these pieces fit together”  •  Stating an opinion – What is your opinion on what she described”  •  Supporting a point with evidence – “what evidence would support this point?”  Productive Talk Moves  Doing and Talking Math & Science  Activity  ▪  Find a partner and grab the talk move cards  •  You will use these to navigate through a 3-minute discussion about the topic.  ▪  Read the Gibbons passage  ▪  Wait for instructions  What points from this passage affirm your current practice? What is the most important learning for you?  Email address  QR Code",
