    "content": "As you enter, please create a name tent & sign in with the QR code  1.  Middle of tent: your first name  2.  Top left: Place you hope to visit 3. Bottom right: years in education  4.  Bottom left: role or grade level  Engaging in Reflective Practice through Classroom-Based Action Research and Data Analysis  © 2024 CARE Coaching  Opening & Welcome  Care Coach Information  jessica.fundalinski@wcepspl.org  Overview of Workshop  Description: The purpose of this workshop is for teachers to engage in an action research process focused on exploring how to enhance their equitable instructional practices. Teachers will work to positively impact outcomes for MLs through a shift in their practice using standards for equitable pedagogy.  Upon completion of this workshop, participants will be able to:  ▪  Reflect on instructional practices  ▪  Enact a change in practice and gather evidence  ▪  Analyze the evidence to identify patterns and  ▪  Consider implications and plan next steps  2-Day Workshop  Inquire  In February, we INQUIRED and PLANNED.  For the last 8 weeks, we ACTed in our classrooms and collected evidence of the impact of our changes in teaching practice  Today, we will collaboratively ANALYZE the evidence we collected & REFLECT for ongoing exploration of the impact you can make for Multilingual Learners  Agenda  •  Opening & Welcome  •  Part 1: Reviewing Action Research  •  Part 2: Evidence Analysis Protocol  •  Lunch  •  Part 3: Reflecting on Action Research  •  Part 4: Planning for Next Steps  •  Closing  Learning Agreements  •  Take space/make space  •  What is shared here, stays here; what is learned here, leaves here  •  Approach with curiosity  •  Assume positive intent  •  Attend to personal needs  Some Feedback  Some Actionable Feedback: ❑More examples of action research  ❑Broader collaboration, beyond just table groups ❑More movement ❑More visuals/videos  Teacher Resources  Notetaker  Personal Learning Goal  Check-In, Reflect & Share  Part 1: Review Day 1  Characteristics of Action Research  Commit to Equity  … equity [for multilingual learners] equates to scaffolding up content rather than watering it down. The goal is to create a rich instructional environment that challenges all students to inquire, negotiate meaning, and articulate complex disciplinary concepts. This means creating the conditions for multilingual students to become protagonists of their own learning.  Focus on Equity for MLs  •  Developing Language and Literacy across the Curriculum  •  Teaching through Conversation  •  Teaching Complex Thinking  Equity-Focused Situations  1)  Within your power to change  2)  Actionable [through pedagogy]  3)  Measurable  4)  Focused on equity and purposeful language use connected to content [Think CREDE Standards!]  Planning  So... what if I don’t have evidence to share?  Reconnect  Stand Up, Hand Up, Pair Up.  Mix around the room. When the music stops, find a person with their hand up.  ❑  Take turns sharing your action plans.  ❑  Feel free to take notes on any of their plans you might want to adopt in future action planning cycles  EXAMPLES: Shifts in Practice  •  Teaching students to make connections between their home languages and English. Model making connections and describing WHY specific language is important.  •  Students discuss or explain ideas [cooperative learning] before turning in writing.  •  Integrating supports that get students engaged in academic conversations, e.g. models of language needed (modeling and teaching discourse moves)  •  Using language-focused rubrics, individual student writing conferences, and having students assess peers and themselves on language use  Your Examples: Shifts in Practice  Part 2: Evidence Analysis  Protocol  Part 2 Overview  Reflect on Action Plan using protocol  ✓  Reflecting on current practices  ✓  Preparing to analyze and share evidence  ✓  Sharing evidence through Notice & Wonder  ✓  Discussion – Give One, Get One  ✓  Draw Conclusions/Implications  ✓  Gallery Walk  ✓  LUNCH (somewhere in this time!)  Shifting Approach to Analyzing Evidence  In your small groups, use a round robin protocol to discuss the following question:  ❑  How do you talk about your instructional practice (e.g., as an individual, a grade-level team and/or school-level)?  ❑  How do you get feedback on your instructional practice?  Evidence Analysis Protocol  Share Context Notice: Record & Share Wonder: Record & Share Discuss Draw Conclusions  Share Context Notice: Record & Share Wonder: Record & Share Discuss Draw Conclusions Preparation  Prepare to Analyze & Share Evidence  Step 1: Record Hypothesis / Hypothesis  Step 2: Organize and/or Code Evidence  EXAMPLE: Organize Evidence  Evidence:  1.  Student Speaking Examples  2.  Student Survey Responses  Coding Data  Qualitative coding is the process of systematically assigning descriptive labels (codes) to segments of qualitative data, like interview transcripts or audio recordings, to identify patterns and themes within the information. These data can then be interpreted into meaningful categories for analysis.  Steps (Deductive Coding):  1.  Read through data thoroughly: Familiarize yourself with the content to identify potential themes.  2.  Develop codes: Based on your research question/hypothesis, develop descriptive labels (codes) that capture the key meaning of the text.  3.  Assign Codes: Highlight relevant sections of the data that correspond to the code.  EXAMPLE: Code Evidence  Steps:  ❑  Read through the data thoroughly.  ❑  Develop Codes:  •  Blue = Math Language  •  Green = Direct from teacher think-aloud  •  Yellow = Explanation  Language (sequencing)  ❑  Assign Codes  EXAMPLE: Code and Organize  ML  TA  EL  Total  ZW  3  3  2  8  JS  1  1  2  4  EM  0  5  3  8  Key:  ML  = Math Language  TA = directly from think aloud EL = Explanation (sequence)  EXAMPLE: Organize Quantitative Results  Data Collection:  •  Pre-Intervention (Think Alouds)  •  Post-Intervention (3x)  Connect with a Colleague  How might you organize, code, or represent your evidence to easily share with your group?  YOUR TURN: Organize and/or Code Evidence  Evidence Analysis Protocol  Share Context Notice: Record & Share Wonder: Record & Share Discuss Draw Conclusions  •  Show hypothesis  •  Reason for evidence source selected  •  Describe evidence source in detail  Select an Evidence Source to Share  Which evidence source do you want to share with your group?  •  New source of evidence for you?  •  Confirming?  •  Puzzling?  •  Exciting?  Evidence Analysis Protocol  Share Context Notice: Record & Share Wonder: Record & Share Discuss Draw Conclusions  (Revisiting) The Ladder of Inference  Working with a partner…  •  Read the statements.  •  Determine whether each statement is an observation or a conclusion (inference).  •  Provide justification  •  Be ready to share with the whole groups.  Notice & Wonder Protocol  What do you notice about how this team is interacting with each other and the evidence they’re analyzing?  Notice & Wonder Protocol  Norms:  •  Observing the results and the evidence/tool itself  •  One person speaks at a time at a time  •  No discussion  •  Using sentence frames:  •  I notice that…  •  I wonder if…  EXAMPLE: Justine’s Hypothesis  How can I teach complex thinking in order to help my students be more effective mathematical problem solvers?  If I routinely engage in think alouds during instruction, then, my MLs will:  1.  Explain how they solve multi-step word problems,  2.  Have greater confidence as mathematicians, and;  3.  Solve multi-step word problems with greater accuracy.  EXAMPLE: Justine’s Evidence Sources  Part of Hypothesis  Evidence Source  If I routinely engage in think alouds during instruction…  • Peer Observation  1.  Explain how they solve multi-step word problems  • Student speaking samples  2.  Have greater confidence as mathematicians  • Student Survey  3.  Solve multi-step word problems with greater accuracy  • Student Work  EXAMPLE: Justine & Colleagues  Justine: 7th grade Math  Larry: ELL Specialist  Kim: 7th Grade Math  Maxine: Teammate ELA  EXAMPLE: Student Speaking  EXAMPLE: Student Survey  Evidence Analysis Protocol  Share Context Notice: Record & Share Wonder: Record & Share Discuss Draw Conclusions  •  Share one statement at a time.  •  Consider the results and tool.  •  No discussion.  •  Take notes as others share about your evidence  •  Repeat the process for each member.  Share Context Notice: Record & Share Wonder: Record & Share Discuss Draw Conclusions  Suggested Timeframe:  •  1 minute to introduce the evidence  •  3 minutes to review evidence and silently write noticings & wonderings on index card  •  3-5 minutes to go around the circle with noticings  •  3-5 minutes to go around the circle with wonderings  •  5 minutes to discuss  Total Time = 20 minutes  Select a new timekeeper for each round!  Share Context Notice: Record & Share Wonder: Record & Share Discuss Draw Conclusions  LUNCH (LIKELY)  Give One, Get One  What are your “take aways” from the evidence analysis protocol tasks and discussion?  Evidence Analysis Protocol  Share Context Notice: Record & Share Wonder: Record & Share Discuss Draw Conclusions  Drawing Conclusions  •  Reflect on data: Teachers examine the data they gathered through observation, interviews, student work, etc., to identify patterns and trends.  •  Interpret findings: Beyond just summarizing the data, teachers analyze what the information means in the context of their classroom and teaching strategies.  •  Make connections: Teachers link their findings to relevant educational theories and research to provide a deeper understanding of their observations.  •  Formulate next steps: Based on their conclusions, teachers develop actionable steps to modify their teaching practices to address the identified issues and improve student learning.  EXAMPLE: Draw Conclusions  How can I teach complex thinking in order to help my students be more effective mathematical problem solvers?  If I routinely engage in think alouds during instruction, then, my MLs will:  1.  Explain how they solve multi-step word problems,  2.  Have greater confidence as mathematicians, and;  3.  Solve multi-step word problems with greater accuracy  Conclusions after gathering peer observation feedback, student work & surveys:  •  Each student used aspects of the think-aloud or other language expectations in their own explanations.  •  Students reported feeling more confident.  YOUR TURN: Draw Conclusions  Step 1: Independently, develop some conclusions/ reflections from your analysis.  Step 2: As a group, use round-robin protocol to share your conclusions / reflections.  Note: After each person shares, there should be a discussion about the response. For example, what group members agree/disagree with, other implications or suggestions.  Repeat for the second question.  Action Research Outcomes  You will create a poster to share your groups’ collective impacts on teaching and learning.  •  The format and organization are up to you!  •  We will use a gallery walk to share out our results.  Positive Impacts on Equity  Gallery Walk  Part 3: Reflecting on Action  Research  Part 3 Overview  Reflecting on the Action Research Cycle  ✓  Short Video on Reflection  ✓  Example Reflection: Justine  ✓  15-Minute Independent Reflection  ✓  Share Out  Why Reflect?  EXAMPLE: Reflection  Reflect  Take 15 minutes to reflect, draw, doodle on the following questions:  Was your action plan successful or successful enough to continue refining and doing?  •  Has your research question been answered?  •  Have more questions emerged?  What are the implications for your practice?  •  What changes have you made in your teaching approaches?  •  What have you learned about your teaching practices?  •  What have you learned about student learning?  Reflect with a Colleague  Find a partner to share your findings with. Share your results and be spontaneous with what emerges as you share aloud. Practice intentional listening.  (More) Planning for Action Research  Part 4 Overview  (More) Planning for Action Research  ✓  Reviewing CREDE Standards  ✓  Regrouping for new Action Research  ✓  Planning for Action Research Cycle  ✓  Sharing Plans  CREDE Standards in Action  •  Developing Language and Literacy  •  Teaching through Conversation  •  Teaching Complex Thinking  Regroup for New Perspectives  Content  Grade-Level  Other CREDE Standard  Position  New Group Share Outs  1.  Share your hypothesis.  2.  Share lessons learned from Round 1 of your action plan.  3.  Share general ideas for a new or refined action plan:  ◦  Now what?  ◦  What is next?  EXAMPLE: Plan Next Steps  Plan Next Steps  Complete a new Action Planner  Prepare for “Check-in Chunks”  ◦  Identify your Focus: Who, Why, What, How  ◦  Craft of your Plan: Implementation and Evidence Collection  Share Out!  What you did Round 1 What you learned What you are planning for Round 2  Closing & Thank You  2-Day Workshop Check In  Today we INQUIRED & PLANned.  For 8 weeks, we ACTed in our classrooms and collected evidence of the impact of our changes in teaching practice  Today, we will collaboratively ANALYZEd the evidence we collected & REFLECTed for ongoing exploration of the impact you can make for Multilingual Learners  Continue to Collaborate & Connect  Collaborate with colleagues through reflective prompts.  - Peer Observation  - Student Surveys  - Any Additional Evidence  ☺  Feedback Survey & Thanks",
