    "title": "Supporting Students with Limited or Interrupted Formal Education (SLIFE) (Part 1/1)",
    "content": "Supporting Students with Limited or Interrupted Formal Education (SLIFE)  Outcomes  By the end of this professional learning session, staff will be able to:  •  Define and recognize characteristics of a SLIFE label  •  Determine how to engage SLIFE students in grade level instruction  •  Use data to advocate with leaders on making informed decisions about instructional programming for SLIFE students or to discuss the unique learning needs of SLIFE  Introduction  Coaches enter introduction here  463fe9ed-6305-4c90-abc6- 39428e2e5461  Logistics  •  Padlet  •  Attendance  •  Bathrooms  https://padlet.com/tamishalsampson1/slife-resources-imu7ljucudwncwev  Session Description  •  This coaching session is all about understanding SLIFE (Students with Limited and Informal Education). This session will help you recognize who these students are, explore supports for classroom and instructional support, and use data to make informed instructional decisions.  Who’s in the Room?  Getting to Know You  1. Middle of tent: your first name (front fold) 2.Bottom left: Your position  3. Bottom right: years in education  4. Bottom left: role  5. Middle top: response to prompt  On a scale of Cat, how are you feeling today?  © 2024 CARE Coaching  Working Agreements  What does a SLIFE label mean?  Who are Students with Limited or Interrupted Formal Education (SLIFE)?  Questions to Ponder  1.  Can a SLIFE label apply to a newcomer?  2.  Can a newcomer also be labeled as SLIFE?  3.  Can an ML be labeled both as a newcomer and SLIFE?  4.  Has SLIFE been influenced by the COVID years?  Who Are Newcomer Students?  “Newcomers” refers to any foreign-born students and their families who have recently arrived in the United States.  Throughout our country’s history, people from around the world have immigrated to the United States to start a new life, bringing their customs, religions, and languages with them.  The United States is, to a great extent, a nation of immigrants. Newcomers play an important role in weaving our nation’s social and economic fabric, and U.S. schools play an important role in helping newcomers adapt and contribute as they integrate into American society.  www2.ed.gov/about/offices/list/oela/newcomers-toolkit/chap1.pdf  Definition of Multilingual Learner  As part of its asset-based belief system, WIDA uses the term “multilingual learners” to describe all students who come in contact with and/or interact in languages in addition to English on a regular basis.  They include students who are commonly referred to as English language learners (ELLs), dual language learners (DLLs), newcomers, students with interrupted formal schooling (SIFE), long-term English learners (L-TELs), English learners with disabilities, gifted and talented English learners, heritage language learners, students with English as an additional language (EAL), and students who speak varieties of English or indigenous languages.  WIDA Standards Framework, 2020  Definition of SLIFE*  •  The student has been identified as an English Learner.  •  The student Is 8 to 21 years old.  •  Entered a United States school after grade 2, or Exited the United States for six months or more and did not attend school during that time.  •  Prior exposure to formal schooling is characterized by one of the following:  •  no formal schooling.  •  interruptions in formal schooling, defined as at least two or fewer years of schooling than their typical peers.  •  consistent, but limited formal schooling.  •  Functions two or more years below expected grade level in native language literacy relative to typical peers.  •  Functions two or more years below expected grade level in numeracy relative to typical peers.  *Criteria developed based on research conducted by the SLIFE guidance team. Information drawn from state and federal law, Colorin Colorado and research conducted by Andrea DeCapua and Helene Marshall.  State Definitions of SLIFE  Each state has a slightly different definition of SLIFE.  Find the document in the Padlet and  see how your state’s definition differs from others.  Tabletop Discussion  Questions to Discuss:  •  How do the state SLIFE demographics match the demographics of your classroom?  •  How does understanding of SLIFE characteristics impact how you think about your students when planning?  Each table be prepared to share out your findings.  Reflection  1.  Can a SLIFE label apply to a newcomer?  2.  Can a newcomer also be labeled as SLIFE?  3.  Can an ML be labeled both as a newcomer and SLIFE?  4.  Has SLIFE been influenced by the COVID years?  Why or why not?  Supporting the Whole Child  Let this sink in…  “...while taking a remedial approach may seem intuitive, such instruction does not match the reality of SLIFE who are not relearning what they failed to grasp before, but rather engaging in formal classroom learning, many for the first time or after a significant interruption” (DeCapua & Marshall, 2015)  Strengths Focus: Reframe!  Asset Mindset vs. Deficit Mindset  SLIFE students bring a variety of strengths to learning, including the ability to navigate multiple cultures, and resilience skills resulting from experiencing significant challenges such as civil unrest, discrimination, lack of access to education, and the immigration process.  Two Third Grade Students  Student A  Student B  •Parents are immigrants  •Dad is a retired doctor  •Speaks Spanish  •Mom a university professor  •Dad works nights  •Mom volunteers often  •Does not S, R, or W English  •Reads at home in home language  •Learning to read in Spanish  •Motivated to learn English  •Two grade levels behind  •Showing progress toward goals  Strengths and Challenges of SLIFE  Strengths  Challenges  ●  Possess a wealth of life experiences and extensive pragmatic knowledge  ●  Can be motivated to learn  ●  Are adept at hands-on work  ●  Collaborate well with others  ●  Can learn at a rapid rate  ●  May be fluent in multiple languages  ●  Face significant time constraints to learn English, acquiring grade-level academic skills and knowledge, and preparing for standardized tests  ●  May not be able to read or write in their home language  ●  May have experienced immigration-related trauma  ●  Face deficit-based educator mindset that views learning through an abstract, literacy-based lens  What might be other strengths or challenges you have experienced not listed here?  Directions for Scenario Group Work  •  Break up into groups of 5  •  Take a look at the scenario that your group is given and look for the strengths that each student brings to the classroom  •  Share out  Andre  Andre was four when war started in his country of Afghanistan. He was playing outside one day when someone began shooting at him. If his aunt had not rushed out and dragged him inside, he might have been killed. When life became too dangerous Andre's father took the family away from the house to hide in the forest; the next day their house was burned and their neighbors murdered. They left with only what they could carry, walking in deep snow. Andre's feet froze to his boots, and he could not take them off for two days. The family of five walked over the mountains in winter, hiding from soldiers, to reach a safe place. Finally, someone hid them, but Andre's father had to pay the homeowner not to turn them in. They made their way into another country, where his father worked illegally, saving money to get a visa. Eventually they made their way into the United States, with nothing more than his father's life savings stitched into the pocket of his coat. The family settled in the mountains that reminded his father of home.  After three years of moving, the family settled in a medium-sized city, and Andre arrived at school with a very scattered and disrupted school history. He could read little in any language. His records showed that he had made very little progress since his arrival in the West and that he was getting Fs in everything. Each year he missed between 25 and 30 days of school.  Emilio  Emilio is a fourth grade student who moved from El Salvador 2 years ago and is living with his grandmother. His English is broken but he can comprehend most words, especially when accompanied with gestures. When he first arrived at school he was very excited and liked being included in the classroom atmosphere. He quickly began to withdraw and become frustrated and depressed. The teacher started to have a difficult time getting Emilio to participate in class activities. Emilio's teacher recognized that he was experiencing the rejection stage of culture shock. She did her best to make him feel comfortable in the class by assigning him peer buddies . After this rejection stage passed, he continued to struggle with vocabulary, especially contextually.  Beverly  Beverly has been here for 3 months and is from Thailand. She understands much of what she hears in class, and reads and writes at a much higher level than you would think, because she is very reluctant to participate in any way in classroom dialogue. She is also very cautious about making mistakes in front of the class and when she is in a group. She simply will not try if there are any risks. When you observe her in a one-on-one with another English-speaking student, you can see she understands what the other student is saying, but she takes a long time to respond, because she is always looking for the exact word.  Bayan  Bayan is a Kurdish refugee and has observed violence toward her family. She did not attend formal school in Iraq. She seems sad and withdrawn; some of her teachers suspect she has post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). Her family arrived last year without basic belongings and lives well below the poverty level. In terms of language, Bayan answers formulaic questions with memorized statements and can participate in academic conversations only minimally. She often uses incomplete sentences and phrases orally to convey complete thoughts but is only able to write with considerable effort because she’s not literate in Kurdish.  Jesus  Jesus's Mexican family owns a successful local restaurant that employs extended family members. Though his sister came to the United States with her father 5 years ago, Jesus and his mother arrived in the United States last year when, after interrupted schooling, he started to attend middle school.  Core Stressors  US Department of Education Newcomer Toolkit  Best practices for engaging SLIFE  ●  Teach students about school culture.  ●  Build conditions for learning.  ●  Use oral language activities to scaffold written work  and build individual accountability.  ●  Scaffold academic tasks using familiar language and content.  ●  Provide intensive literacy and numeracy instructions (as needed).  Decapua & Marshall, 2013, 2017  Activity  •  At your tables, discuss some explicit ideas (stories) for what these ”look like”:  ●  Teach students about school culture.  ●  Build conditions for learning.  ●  Use oral language activities to scaffold written work and build individual accountability.  ●  Scaffold academic tasks using familiar language and content.  ●  Provide intensive literacy and numeracy instructions (as needed).  •  Create a Google doc, they could use those ideas in planning for a future lesson  Reflect at your table  •  Knowing strengths and challenges for SLIFE, how might you use this information to help you plan instruction for your students?  •  What is an aspect of school culture or routines that SLIFE may not be familiar with?  •  How can you help students make connections to their own lives?  Take a Break–10 minutes  Engaging SLIFE Students in Grade Level Instruction–Outcome #2  The Iceberg Concept of Culture  Find the blank Iceberg Concept of Culture document on the Padlet  • Surface Culture  • Explicit/Deep Culture  • Norms and Values  • Assumptions and Beliefs  Iceberg of Concept Culture JPEG -Without Words downloaded from PBS LearningMedia, https://www.pbslearningmedia.org/. Rights to use this asset do not expire. Asset Copyright: 2017 Media Credits  Iceberg of Concept Culture JPEG -Without Words downloaded from PBS LearningMedia, https://www.pbslearningmedia.org/. Rights to use this asset do not expire. Asset Copyright: 2017 Media Credits  The Iceberg Concept of Culture  Iceberg of Concept Culture JPEG -Without Words downloaded from PBS LearningMedia, https://www.pbslearningmedia.org/. Rights to use this asset do not expire. Asset Copyright: 2017 Media Credits  Iceberg of Concept Culture JPEG -Without Words downloaded from PBS LearningMedia, https://www.pbslearningmedia.org/. Rights to use this asset do not expire. Asset Copyright: 2017 Media Credits  •  Awareness of Cultural Complexity  •  Avoiding Stereotypes and Assumptions  •  Cultural Sensitivity and Respect  •  Effective Communication  •  Building Relationships and Trust  Iceberg of Concept Culture JPEG -Without Words downloaded from PBS LearningMedia, https://www.pbslearningmedia.org/. Rights to use this asset do not expire. Asset Copyright: 2017 Media Credits  Take a Break-Lunch 1 hr.  Differentiation  Differentiation for MLs involves how these students’ English Language Proficiency (ELP) levels in Listening, Speaking, Reading, and Writing influences what individual students can be expected to do with content.  What is Grade Level Instruction and why is it important for ALL students?  •  What is Grade Level Instruction in your context?  •  Why is it important?  •  What are some ways you ensure that students receive instruction at the grade level standard?  What is Grade Level Instruction and why is it important for All students?  What do SLIFE need in order to engage in grade level content?  •  Literacy Skills  •  Background knowledge  •  Learning discourse practices in formal school setting  Differentiation of Instruction for SLIFE Students  •  Multimodal Instruction  •  Amplify not Simplify Language  •  Visual Supports  •  Differentiated Assignments  •  Collaborative Learning/Grouping  Multimodal Instruction  •  incorporate a variety of learning styles  •  provide  •  visual aids  •  manipulatives  •  realia  •  hands-on activities to support comprehension  Differentiation of Instruction for SLIFE  •  Amplify not Simplify Language  •  use graphic organizers  •  translanguaging  •  use clear and concise language when providing instructions  •  scaffold complex sentence structures  •  use visuals and gestures  •  scaffold instruction by breaking down complex tasks/concepts into smaller parts  • Visual Supports  •  charts  •  diagrams  •  pictures  •  videos  •  Differentiate Assignments by Language Proficiency  •  allow choice for how students can demonstrate understanding  •  allow students to communicate in different ways -(i.e. visual, hands-on, orally, language they are most comfortable with)  (<https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vtiIZNNXUbqJghACai9EOOWxCx1gnV1xPBBjg6TldEQ/edit>)SLIFE, Newcomer, and/or ML?  •  Collaborative Learning and Grouping  •  homogeneous grouping for SLIFE students allow for student to build confidence have a sense of belonging  •  heterogeneous grouping for SLIFE students allow for practicing language to build language skills and create a more inclusive classroom  Use data to make informed decisions about instructional programming for SLIFE Outcome  #3  “... ELLS do not have the luxury of waiting until they are fluent in English to engage in essential learning of content material. From the first day in a U.S. school, each ELL plays ‘catch up’ with non-ELL peers [...]. Teachers must, through competent instruction and assessment, empower their students to take charge of their own learning, join their non-ELL peers in achievement, and reach their full potential.”  (Fairbairn & Jones-Vo, 2019, p.100)  Application of New Learning  Activity: Part 1  Number off by 4s  All odd numbers will read and take notes on Level 1 Student  Descriptors, Assignment and Instructional  Strategies  All even numbers will read and take notes on Level 2 Student Descriptors, Assignment and Instructional Strategies  Level 1  Level 1 -Student Descriptors  Note: this applies to new language, and not to make assumptions  (Fairbairn & Jones-Vo, 2022, p. 91)  Level 1 -Assignment/ Assessment Strategies  (Fairbairn & Jones-Vo, 2022, p. 103)  Level 1 -Instructional Strategies  (Fairbairn & Jones-Vo, 2022, p. 113)  Level 2  (Fairbairn & Jones-Vo, 2022, p. 113)  (Fairbairn & Jones-Vo, 2022, p. 113)  Level 2 -Instructional Strategies  I  Speaking  Reading  •  Use simplified, correct language, repeating or paraphrasing as needed  •  Allow sufficient wait time  •  Model the completion of graphic organizers to build higher-order thinking  •  Employ think-alouds to model both process and language  (Fairbairn & Jones-Vo, 20  Encourage Involvement in discussions by eliciting phrases or simple sentences  Scaffold content and academic language by providing appropriate sentence frames Concentrate on students' meaning rather than on grammatical correctness Reward all attempts to communicate  Ł  Implement high-quality, researched-based, culturally, and linguistically appropriate rendingdevelopmerkprogram  •  Use extensive visual supports so that studertscan derve meaning from print  •  Supportgrade-levelcontent using comprehensible reeding materials with octensevisual  support  •  Tesch early reading skills  •  Bulldbackgroundand help students make connections te prior leamlng experiences  •  Read or sing visually supported stories or texts to students, using props and acting to Increase comprehension and develop oral language skills necessary for rending  •  Useshared, shared-to-guided and guided meding  •  Implement language experience stories  •  Model the use of graphic  organizers  •  Scaffold content and academic language by providing sentence frames  •  Incorporate modeled, shared, and guided writing activities  •  Accept phrases or simple sentences in lieu of grade-level writing expectations  •  Focus on correct answers instead of errors and omissions  •  Correct and use identity texts  •  Model the use of graphic organizers  © 2024 CARE Coaching  Application of New Learning Activity: Part 2  You will number off by 5.  •  You will read the scenario based on your number  •  When directed, you will find the corresponding group number indicated by the numbers posted around the room  •  What do you notice about the students’ English proficiency level in each individual language domain (listening, speaking, reading, and writing)?  •  What other factors mentioned in the profiles might influence how you differentiate assignments/ assessments and instruction?  Application of New Learning Activity: Part 2  Within your groups, make notes on the poster about the following:  •  What do you notice about the students’ English proficiency level in each individual language domain (listening, speaking, reading, and writing)?  •  What other factors mentioned in the profiles might influence how you plan to differentiate assignments/ assessments and instruction?  •  Use your knowledge of the levels from the previous section.  (<https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vtiIZNNXUbqJghACai9EOOWxCx1gnV1xPBBjg6TldEQ/edit>)SLIFE, Newcomer, or EL? Scenario #1  Andre  Andre was four when war started in his country of Afghanistan. He was playing outside one day when someone began shooting at him. If his aunt had not rushed out and dragged him inside, he might have been killed. When life became too dangerous Andre's father took the family away from the house to hide in the forest; the next day their house was burned and their neighbors murdered. They left with only what they could carry, walking in deep snow. Andre's feet froze to his boots, and he could not take them off for two days. The family of five walked over the mountains in winter, hiding from soldiers, to reach a safe place. Finally, someone hid them, but Andre's father had to pay the homeowner not to turn them in. They made their way into another country, where his father worked illegally, saving money to get a visa. Eventually they made their way into the United States, with nothing more than his father's life savings stitched into the pocket of his coat. The family settled in the mountains that reminded his father of home.  After three years of moving, the family settled in a medium-sized city, and Andre arrived at school with a very scattered and disrupted school history. He could read little in any language. His records showed that he had made very little progress since his arrival in the West and that he was getting Fs in everything. Each year he missed between 25 and 30 days of school.  Emilio  Emilio is a fourth grade student who moved from El Salvador 2 years ago and is living with his grandmother. His English is broken but he can comprehend most words, especially when accompanied with gestures. When he first arrived at school he was very excited and liked being included in the classroom atmosphere. He quickly began to withdraw and become frustrated and depressed. The teacher started to have a difficult time getting Emilio to participate in class activities. Emilio's teacher recognized that he was experiencing the rejection stage of culture shock. She did her best to make him feel comfortable in the class by assigning him peer buddies . After this rejection stage passed, he continued to struggle with vocabulary, especially contextually.  Beverly  Beverly has been here for 3 months and is from Thailand. She understands much of what she hears in class, and reads and writes at a much higher level than you would think, because she is very reluctant to participate in any way in classroom dialogue. She is also very cautious about making mistakes in front of the class and when she is in a group. She simply will not try if there are any risks. When you observe her in a one-on-one with another English-speaking student, you can see she understands what the other student is saying, but she takes a long time to respond, because she is always looking for the exact word.  Bayan  Bayan is a Kurdish refugee and has observed violence toward her family. She did not attend formal school in Iraq. She seems sad and withdrawn; some of her teachers suspect she has post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). Her family arrived last year without basic belongings and lives well below the poverty level. In terms of language, Bayan answers formulaic questions with memorized statements and can participate in academic conversations only minimally. She often uses incomplete sentences and phrases orally to convey complete thoughts but is only able to write with considerable effort because she’s not literate in Kurdish.  Jesus  Jesus’s Mexican family owns a successful local restaurant that employs extended family members. Though his sister came to the United States with her father 5 years ago, Jesus and his mother arrived in the United States last year when, after interrupted schooling, he started to attend middle school.  Share Out  •  What do you notice about the students’ English proficiency level in each individual language domain (listening, speaking, reading, and writing)?  •  What other factors mentioned in the profiles might influence how you plan to differentiate assignments/ assessments and instruction?  Differentiated Assignment Template  (Adapted from Fairbairn & Jones-Vo, 2022, p. 19)  Application of New Learning Part 3  Each group will choose 1 Exemplar of a completed Differentiated Template.  Talk about what do you notice and what do you wonder?  •  K 12 Mathematics Example  - •  Elementary Differentiated Template/English  Language Arts (ELA)  •  Secondary Differentiated Template/ELA  (<https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sDt8waPmEidVVZWReKwMbnVBkuRQD14I4R0IDENr3Z8/edit>)K(<https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sDt8waPmEidVVZWReKwMbnVBkuRQD14I4R0IDENr3Z8/edit>)-(<https://docs.google.com/document/d/1sDt8waPmEidVVZWReKwMbnVBkuRQD14I4R0IDENr3Z8/edit>)12 Mathematics Example Differentiated Assignment Template  (Adapted from Fairbairn & Jones-Vo, 2022, p. 19)  Elementary English Language Arts (ELA)  Example  Differentiated Assignment Template  (Adapted from Fairbairn & Jones-Vo, 2022, p. 19)  Secondary ELA Example  Differentiated Assignment Template  (Adapted from Fairbairn & Jones-Vo, 2022, p. 19)  Take a Break–10 minutes  Differentiated Assignment Template  Find the Blank Differentiated Template on your Padlet, download to your computer. You will need this for the next section.  19)  It is Your Turn!  Using the student data scenarios on the Padlet you will develop one differentiated lesson plan based on your student’s ELP Levels.  With your group, choose one assignment that you would like to use to develop your differentiated template.  Assignment 1: Describe, compare, and explain features of the rain forest before and after deforestation.  Assignment 2: Describe shapes and their attributes by defining and visually representing a rhombus, rectangle and a square.  Assignment 3: Create and present a speech describing an assigned country using multimedia technology.  Planning and Instructional Considerations  Planning and instructional considerations support the SLIFE’s transition to academic success. Effective SLIFE instructional models should provide:  1.  Thematically coordinated literacy and content courses;  2.  Appropriate ESOL classes;  3.  Intensive, consistent, and age-appropriate sheltered content or bilingual instruction, and ESOL;  4.  Trained ESOL or bilingual endorsed teachers that teach age-level literacy and numeracy skills for their students;  5.  Enrichment opportunities that accelerate both their language acquisition and overall socio-emotional adjustment;  6.  Supplemental support programs before or after school, and during the summer to accelerate academic progress and language acquisition; and  7.  For secondary students, pathways to pursue career, post-secondary, vocational, and technical education, a High School Equivalency, Testing Program or other alternative educational programs.  Massachusetts Students with Limited or Interrupted Formal Education (SLIFE) Definition and Guidance, 2019  Share Out!  ●  What assignment did your group choose?  ●  What steps did you take to begin this template?  ●  Each group share the process you went through to develop your activities, language expectations, and assessment that is differentiated for your students.  a � � / »I � � r loa Ea r � ii 1 . O ea ii ■I I . , ■ ■I bl ■  © 2024 CARE Coaching",
