    "title": "Developing Discourse in Expressive Language (Part 1/1)",
    "content": "Developing Discourse in Expressive Language  Building multilingual learners’ capacity in speaking and writing  Logistics  •  Something goes here  •  And here  Session objectives  •  Understand WIDA’s functional approach to language development.  •  Examine the interconnectedness of the 2020 WIDA Standards Framework components.  •  Design a unit sketch to map ELD standards framework components to content-area instruction  •  Explore ways to implement teaching of language functions and features within grade-level content lessons.  •  Create a discourse anchor chart for a content-area expressive language task  Getting to Know Me  ESL educator for 27 years!  WIDA presenter for 10 years!  adjunct instructor at WPU!  a  past president of NJTESOL/NJBE!  Getting to Know You  Let’s talk…  What do we teach when we teach language?  THINK - PAIR - SHARE  Big Ideas  Four Big Ideas anchor the standards and are interwoven throughout the framework:  • Equity of opportunity and access  Ł  This is essential for multilingual learners' preparation for college, career and civic lives.  • Integration of content and language  •  Academic content is the context for language learning, and language is the means for learning academic content.  ■  Collaboration among stakeholders  Ł  Stakeholders share responsibility for educating multilingual learners.  • Functional approach to language development  Ł  This approach helps educators focus on the purposeful use of language.  Systemic Functional Linguistics  (an underlying theory in the WIDA 2020 Standards Framework, Appendix F)  ●  Focuses on mastery of language and syntactic features of language  ●  Facilitates full participation in academic content and language by building competency.  ●  Attention given to specific patterns of use, linked to purpose  ●  Context shapes language choices  ●  Enables construction of text in a variety of ways  ●  Includes intentional teaching of language structures as they relate to KLU language patterns  ●  Identifies the language demands of a specific task  ●  Language becomes a vehicle for learning  Source: “English Language Development: Implementation at Grades Six Through Twelve” (Dutro and Kinsella 2010)  Create an answer  What is the functional approach to language development?  Use these words to create a working answer with a partner:  language choices  syntax  academic content  patterns of  use  intentional  language demands  Write your response in a connected sentence!  Quote  “Raising teachers’ and students’ awareness of how linguistic choices make the texts of schooling the kinds of texts they are can enable participation in the contexts of learning that those texts help create.”  Schleppegrell (2004) The Language of Schooling  Gather Resources  https://wida.wisc.edu/teach/standards/eld/2020  Full Framework (400 pages)  Grade level cluster editions (in WIDA online Resource Library)  https://wida.wisc.edu/resources?keys=materials&field_category%5B10%5D=10&  field_type_target_id%5B18%5D=18  https://satchel.commongoodlt.com/97c883b4 - 8590 - 454f - b222 - f28298ec9a81/97c883b4 - 8590 - 454f - b222 - f28298ec9a81  Standards Satchel (standards digital interface)  WIDA Implementation Guide (for district and curriculum planning, collaboration)  https://wida.wisc.edu/sites/default/files/resource/Implementation - Guide - WIDA - ELD - Standards - Framework.pdf  Practice your slang:  Choose Three  Right on!  Word!  No doubt!  Totally!  100%  Straight up!  Facts!  ✓  REMEMBER THEM.  ✓  LET’S PRACTICE  ✓  GET READY!  Intentional Planning  AGREE AGREE  WIDA Standards Framework Section 4: Resources, p. 249  WIDA Framework Components  Science & ELD Standards: How does energy flow through an ecosystem?  KLU Explain  Expectation: Explain a phenomenon:  Energy from the sun gets passed along from producers to consumers and eventually decomposers.  Function: Describe evidence about a phenomenon, establish neutral stance, show reasoning with evidence to support claims, summarize  Features: abstract nouns, relational verbs, present tense, passive voice, objective language, connectors, conditional clauses  WIDA Standards Framework Section 4: Resources, p. 246  Discourse in the WIDA Framework  Proficiency Level Descriptors (PLDs) are tables that describe multilingual learners’ language growth across levels of English language proficiency (PL), from PL1 to PL6. PLDs describe how multilingual learners use both interpretive and expressive language toward the end of each language proficiency level until they reach PL6.  Based on the dimensions of academic language:  DISCOURSE (organization-cohesion-density)  SENTENCE  WORD/PHRASE  2020 WIDA Standards Framework, p.32  Interconnectedness  What I need to explicitly teach in my lesson-through modeling, direct instruction, • • I Mini-lesson At the level of goals & objectives Linked to the KLUs used in the lesson-these frame the lessons  Language Expectations (taken from Appendix C) building blocks/not exclusive  9-12 paraphrase central  ideas  refute a claim  6-8 maintain formal style  contextualize topic/phenomenon  4-5 act on feedback share solutions  convey initial thinking offer alternatives  refine claims  2-3 add details  justify concluding steps obtain and combine information  distinguish between evidence  and opinion  create conjecture engage audience  generalize commonalities establish a neutral tone  signal logical relationships  1 identify central message select relevant information  develop story events  orient audience analyze evidence  introduce topics  offer suggestions  evaluate sources  K share observations connect stories with images ask questions recount ideas discuss next steps elaborate on ideas define concepts revise opinions describe characteristics sort, summarize ideas clarify ideas compare & contrast information support opinions defend thinking  Language Functions address organizational structure  ●  Teach the structure of the KLU for expressive communication modes.  ●  Create and use a checklist for students during production.  ●  Provides a structure that aligns to expectations of the genre.  ●  Use this for scoring and analyzing student responses, as well as student self-evaluation.  Where can you find these?  WIDA Framework p. 124  Where can you find these?  Functions = highlight Features = squares  WIDA Framework p. 125  Language Features -the bits & pieces  Blue = kinder Red = 4-5 Relating verbs: be, have Nouns that show concepts: investigation Simple sentences Articles, demonstrative pronouns Adjectives Expanded noun groups Prepositional phrases Dialog verbs: told, whispered Pronouns, renaming of nouns Dependent clauses to add details.  Action verbs: cooking, dancing  Causal connectors:  so, because  Concluding statements  sampled from WIDA 2020  Standards Framework  Evaluative word choices:  awesome, scared, mean  Literary devices: simile, metaphor  Quick Talk: What’s the difference?  Sort these terms by language type: bits & pieces, clauses, frame the lesson, goals, pronouns, scientific explanation, structure, interact with language, unit focus  Expectations  Functions  Features  Answers  Expectations  Functions  Features  goals  interact with language  structure  frame the lesson  bits & pieces clauses pronouns  scientific  explanation  unit focus  Take a Break  How are you feeling right now?  Visualize a feeling. Draw it on a post it.  Name it.  Share why.  Prioritizing Key Language Uses  Order of Prevalence across the Framework and in assessment  Most prevalent  least prevalent  Kinder:  Inform in all content areas, narrate (SI and LA), explain (SCI)  1st:  Inform in all content areas, narrate (SI and LA), explain (SI and SCI), argue (SS)  2-3:  explain and argue (SI, MA, SCI, SS), narrate and inform (SI and LA) 4-12: argue and explain in all content areas, narrate and inform (SI and LA)  Instructional progression  WIDA Framework p.237  WIDA Framework Guiding Questions  CONTENT What do students need to learn? INTERACT & USE LANGUAGE How will students use language? THINKKLU LANGUAGE FOCUS Which expectations are selected? FUNCTIONS & FEATURES NEEDED  Which bits & pieces will be needed?  Gulding Questions  What content (e.g, disciplinary practices, concepts, topics) are students expected to learn?  • How are students being asked to use language In the unit? • What Key Language Uses best reflect how students will Interact with language? What Language Expectations best reflect the language focus of the unit?  What Language Functions and Features are essential for meeting content and language goals and the end-of-unit assessment?  ŁŁ WCEPS Ł CARE Coaching  Unit sketching  Unit sketch template  State  How do we interpret language?  Modes of Communication  How do we express language?  A Unit Sample: 5th grade science  Wetlands support a rich Ł food web, from microscopic algae and dragonfly larvae to alligators, and black boars. -I ŁŁ Ł 72€7! 2 :± 6e ±  Standards coorelation  https://www.nextgenscience.org/pe/5 - ps3 - 1 - energy  NGSS  Use models to describe that energy in animals’ food (used for body repair, growth, and motion and to maintain body warmth) was once energy from the sun. [Clarification  Statement: Examples of models could include diagrams, and flow charts.]  WIDA ELD Standards  ELD-SC.4-5.Explain.Expressive Multilingual learners will construct scientific explanations that  *  Describe observations and/or data about a phenomenon  *  Establish neutral or objective stance in communicating results__  *  Develop reasoning to show relationships between evidence and claims__  *  Summarize and/or compare multiple solutions to a problem __based on how well they meet the criteria and constraints of the design solution__  Unit Sketch – Language for Science  INPUT  In 5th grade science, when learning how energy flows through ecosystems, multilingual learners will explain using the language for learning in  •  describing observations about a phenomenon  •  maintaining a neutral stance  •  developing reasoning to show relationships between evidence and claims  •  summarizing  ⇪ THIS is discourse!  Language Standard  Key Language Use  Language Expectation  1 1  ELD-SC.4-5.Explain.Interpretive  Multilingual learners will interpret scientific explanations by  •  Defining investigable questions or design problems based on observations, data, and prior knowledge about a phenomenon  •  Obtaining and combining evidence and information to help explain how or why a phenomenon occurs  •  Identifying evidence that supports particular points in an explanation  DALE-CN.4-5.explicar.interpretativo  Por medio de su repertorio linguistico y utilizando artefactos multimodales, los estudiantes bi/multilingues podran interpretar explicaciones para...  •  *procesar las observaciones y/o evidencias sobre un fen6meno  •  * reconocer posturas neutrales u objetivas en las descripciones de teorias o conceptos  •  entender el razonamiento para mostrar las relaciones entre las evidencias y las afirmaciones  •  identificar y/o comparar multiples soluciones a problemas  •  evaluar el analisis de datos  •  precisar las relaciones entre modelos cientificos y sistemas y fenomenos naturales  INPUT:  Interpretive Mode  Students listen, read, view to make meaning and notice functions and features of text  OUTPUT:  Expressive Mode  Students learn discourse structure and features of text, explicitly plan and prepare spoken or written response  Input -making meaning  Multisemiotic support for Building Background  . Experience table demonstration/co-learning  . Apply learning to \"reading an image.\" How does the your food chain show this? Let's label the parts.  . How does the energy flow through your food chain?  Science standard: Using a model to describe  Food Chain  Visual/audio input  Ed Puzzle: Food Webs  Posted Mar 27  No due date  11  Turned in  Assigned  Ł Ł :a csz4 Ł A*!  Edpuzzle -5th Grade Sci...  naps://edpuzzte.com/assignme  View instructions  I  Review work  I  Input: Vocabulary in context  Vocabulary: use these words to complete the sentences. animals (3x) dead job living plants (2x) produce mushroom organisms  1.  the__and  An ecosystem is all of nonliving things in an environment.  2.  Each living or non-living thing has a_, or job, in an ecosystem. 3. Producers are plants that make, or their own food that is eaten byŁ 4. These are animals that consume, or eat, _P or other C Io 5. Decomposers are living organisms that eat decomposing, or decaying _p _ or a 6. A fungi is a type of organism, like a_, that helps to break down and consume dead o  7.  Bacteria is another kind of organism that we cannot see, but it breaks down Ł organisms.  The Functional Approach: Developing awareness of discourse  Interpretive DECONSTRUCT TEXT * * * * * Expressive CO-CONSTRUCT TEXT  How can you take a functional approach during instruction?  ●  Examine model texts -deconstruct together  ●  Use Color-coding (see Framework’s Legend for Annotated Texts)  ●  Mini-lessons on target language features: adjectives, verb tenses, pronouns, prepositions  ●  Anchor charts for discourse structure  ●  Student practice in context: Cloze sentence/paragraphs  ●  Explicit planning of oral/written response  Noticing language bits & pieces using text annotation (designed for reading text) WIDA 2020 Standards Framework  Input: Text annotation as close read  Ł Directions: Read and annotate. Use the color chart to annotate the text.  Most plants are@roducers) They)produce, or make, their own food. Animals are consumers. Animals cannot  son.  of the oil or ie  becor.ie par.  nimals become art ani...is  make their own food. They consume, or eat, plants or other animals. Fungi and bacteria decompose, or break down, dead plants and animals. They help dead plants and  Nouns= red Verbs = green Prepositional phrases = blue Cohesion)= circled  How does language function to make meaning in text?  DOER ACTION  Utilize the Standards Satchel  https://satchel.commongoodlt.com/97c883b4 - 8590 - 454f - b222 - f28298ec9a81/  How does energy flow through an ecosystem? 5th grade language arts and science  5-PS3-1.  Use models to describe that energy in  animals’ food  (used for body repair, growth, and motion and to maintain body warmth) was once energy from the sun.  ELD-SI.4-12.  Explain  ELD-SC.4-5.  Explain.Interpre tive  ELD-SC.4-5.  Explain.Express ive  Explain  Interpret and construct scientific explanations about how energy flows through a food chain  Describe observations and/or data about a phenomenon. Maintain a neutral stance. Develop reasoning to show  relationships…  abstract nouns, cohesion, relational verbs, connectors, conditional clauses  Unit Sketching  1)  Use the link to make your own copy of the Unit Sketch template.  2)  With a partner, select a science topic for your grade level. Complete the top box describing your unit essential question.  3)  Add to your sketch by locating content  and ELD standards.  NGSS standards topics overview  1)  Identify the KLU.  2)  Use the Standards Satchel website to locate language expectations, functions and features.  3)  Be selective-don’t choose everything!  Table presentations  Presenters:  Unit Sketching  ●  You will table-present your unit sketch.  ●  Answer questions and give clarifications about your choices.  Listeners:  ●  Listen to your colleague's present  ●  Ask 1 question How does language…?  ●  Offer 1 suggestion What do you think about…?  Quote  “Students must be taught to use subject-specific academic language; students also need to see others use and rehearse using such language in their own literacy tasks. Such practices enhance students’ ability to develop their comprehension alongside their science-based academic language.”  Brisk and Zhang Wu (2016)  OUTPUT  Oral explanation assessment  Three Dimensions of Language  Shaped by Key Language Uses Organizational Patterns and Language Features  Discourse  Sentence  Organization, Cohesion, and Density of Language by Key 