other animals or plants. First, for example, the algae, which is a producer, gets energy from sunlight, soil, and water.  After that, a consumer, such as shrimps, eat the algae and get 10% of the energy. When the shrimp gets eaten by another consumer, the energy goes to the animal. After the organism dies, the decomposer, such as worm and bacteria breaks down the animal. The progress begins again when the consumer becomes a part of the soil and another consumer eats it.  Answers  How does energy flow through a food chain? A food chain is a chain of animals that eats another animal. [O] Each organism gets their energy by eating other animals or plants. [P] First, for example, the algae, which is a producer, gets energy from sunlight, soil, and water. [D] After that, a consumer, such as shrimps, eat the algae and get 10% of the energy. [D] When the shrimp gets eaten by another consumer, the energy goes to the animal. [S] After the organism dies, the decomposer, such as worm and bacteria breaks down the animal. [D] The progress begins again when the consumer becomes a part of the soil and another consumer eats it. [C]  Title  •  Text  •  Text  •  Text  References  Brisk, M. E., & Zhang-Wu, Q. (2016). Academic language in K–12 contexts. In Handbook of research in second language teaching and learning (pp. 82-100). Routledge.  Derewianka, B., & Jones, P. (2016). Teaching language in context (2nd ed.) Oxford.  Dutro, S., & Kinsella, K. (2010). English language development: Issues and implementation in grades 6-12. In Improving education for English learners: Research-based approaches. California Department of Education.  Schleppegrell, M. (2004). The language of schooling: A functional linguistics perspective. Erlbaum.  WIDA (2020). WIDA English Language development standards framework, 2020 edition: Kindergarten-grade 12. Board of Regents of the University of Wisconsin System.  Images  https://free images.com/display/new_jersey_outline_shaded.html  - New Jersey  https://