    "content": "Understanding Identification, Programming & Proficiency Levels  Identification  Demographics  Proficiency Levels  Collaborative Teaching  C2IT Strategies  Introductions  Getting to Know You  Virtual: In the chat, add your favorite season and why it’s your favorite.  In Person: Have a name tent or name tags and have them share their favorite season or hobby.  Working Agreements  ▪  See yourself and other with kind eyes  ▪  Maintain productive dialogue  ▪  Speak your truth  ▪  Advocate for your needs  A single conversation across the table with a wise man is better than ten years mere study of books.  - Henry Wadsworth Longfellow  Identification  Identification Process  How Are Potential ELs Identified?  •  Home Language Survey  •  Initial evaluation  Why Do We Identify?  •  Title VI, Civil Rights Act, 1964  •  Elementary & Secondary Act 1965  •  Bilingual Education Act, 1968  •  Lau v. Nichols, 1974  •  Casteñeda v. Pickard, 1981  •  Plyler v. Doe, 1982  •  No Child Left Behind Act 2001  Demographics  National Demographics  District Demographics  Demographic Trends  Enrollment Increase  1991-2005  ELL Low Income  Dropout  ELL  Non- ELL  PreK-5  6-12  Latino  Non- Latino  100%  15%  68%  60%  43%  6%  ELL Population by Race  Latino  Asian  African, Caribbean, European or Other  Native American, Alaskan & Hawaiian Native  80%  13%  4.5%  2.5%  Source: National Center for Educational Statistics (2006)  English Language Proficiency Levels  Features of Academic Language  2020 Board of Regents of the University of Wisconsin SystemWIDA is housed within the Wisconsin Center for Education Research at the University of Wisconsin-Madison  Four Language Domains  Listening  Process, understand, interpret and evaluate spoken language in a variety of situations  Speaking  Engage in oral communication in a variety of situations for a variety of purposes and audiences  Reading  Process, understand, interpret and evaluate written language, symbols and text with understanding and fluency  Writing  Engage in written communication in a variety of situations for a variety of purposes and audiences  Modes of Communication  2020 Board of Regents of the University of Wisconsin SystemWIDA is housed within the Wisconsin Center for Education Research at the University of Wisconsin-Madison  Levels of English Proficiency  Demands of Academic Language  Level 5 Bridging Level 4 Expanding Level 3 Developing Level 2 Emerging Level 1 Entering Organization, Cohesion, Density Discourse Level Precision of Language Word/Phrase Level Grammatical Complexity of Language Sentence Level Lower is Faster and Higher is Slower  Interdependent  Provide Access to Grade-Level Concepts  ELLs Learning a New Language Learning IN a New Language Language Content  Develop Academic Language Skills  English Language Proficiency Scores  Types of Scores  Raw Score  Tally-Derived  7 out of 10  Scale Score  Psychometrically-Derived  237  Proficiency Level Score  Socially-Derived  4.2  Raw Scores  •  Total number of correct responses  •  Not appropriate for tracking progress  •  Rough indicators of student performance  Scale Scores  •  Range from 100 to 600 (above 500 is rare)  •  Consistent across all grades (within a single language domain)  •  Useful for monitoring progress  Proficiency Level Scores  •  Interpretations of scale scores within the WIDA language proficiency levels  •  Range from 1.0 to 6.0  •  Whole Number = Proficiency Level  •  Decimal = How far, in tenths, a student is progressing toward the next level  “Translating” Scale Scores  Grade  Scale  Score  Proficiency  Level  9  370  4.2  10  370  3.9  11  370  3.7  12  370  3.5  Interpretation of Scale Scores  355 Scale Score in Listening Grade 2 Proficiency Level 6.0 Grade 6 Proficiency Level 3.7 Grade 8 Proficiency Level 3.2 At higher grades, expectations increase  Domain Scores  Domain Scores Listening Speaking Reading Writing  Composite Scores  Speaking 50% Listening 50%  Oral Language  Writing 50% Reading 50% Literacy  Reading 70% Listening 30%  Comprehension  Overall Score  Listening 15%  Speaking 15%  Reading  35%  Writing  35%  Writing Rubric of the WIDA\" Consortium Grades 1-12  Level  Linguistic Complexity  Vocabulary Usage  Language Control  6  Reaching*  A variety of sentence lengths of varying linguistic complexity in a single tightly organized paragraph or in well-organized extended text; tight cohesion and organization  Consistent use of just the right word in just the right place; precise Vocabulary  Usage in general, specific or technical language.  Has reached comparability to that of Englsih proficient peers functioning at the  \"proficient\" level in state-wide  35Se5S1entS.  A variety of sentence lengths of varying linguistic complexity in a single organized paragraph or in extended text; cohesion and  organization  Usage of technical language related to the content area; evident facility with needed vocabulary.  Approaching comparability to that of Englsih proficient peers; errors don't impede comprehensibility.  4  Expanding  A variety of sentence lengths of varynig linguistic complexity; emerging cohesion used to provide detail and clarity.  Usage of specific and some technical language related to the content area; lack of needed vocabulary may be occasionally evident.  Generally comprehensible at all times, errors don't impede the overall meaning; such errors may reflect first language interference.  3  Developing  Simple and expanded sentences that show emerging complexity used to provide detail.  Usage of general and some specific language related to the content area; lack of needed vocabulary may be evident.  Generally comprehensible when writing in sentences; comprehensibility may from time to time be impeded by errors when attempting to produce more complex text.  2  Beginning  Phrases and short sentences; varying amount of text may be copied or adapted; some attempt at organzaition may be evidenced.  Usage of general language related to the content area; lack of vocabulary may be evident.  Generally comprehensible when text is adapted from model or source text, or when original text is limited to simple text; comprehensibility may be often impeded by er7Or5.  1  Entering  Single words, set phrases or chunks of simple language; varying amounts of text may be copied or adapted; adapted text contains original language.  Usage of highest frequency vocabulary from school setting and content areas.  Generally comprehensible when text is copied or adapted from model or source text; comprehensibility may be significantly impeded in original text.  Writing Rubric of the WIDA\" Consortium Grades 1-12  Level  Linguistic Complexity  Vocabulary Usage  Language Control  6  Reaching*  A variety of sentence lengths of varying linguistic complexity in a single tightly organized paragraph or in well-organized extended text; tight cohesion and organization  Consistent use of just the right word in just the right place; precise Vocabulary  Usage in general, specific or technical language.  Has reached comparability to that of Englsih proficient peers functioning at the  \"proficient\" level in state-wide  35Se5SMentS.  A variety of sentence lengths of varying linguistic complexity in a single organized paragraph or in extended text; cohesion and  organization  Usage of technical language related to the content area; evident facility with needed vocabulary.  Approaching comparability to that of English proficient peers; errors don't impede comprehensibility.  4  Expanding  A variety of sentence lengths of varying linguistic complexity; emerging cohesion used to provide detail and clarity.  Usage of specific and some technical language related to the content area; lack of needed vocabulary may be occasionally evident.  Generally comprehensible at all times, errors don't impede the overall meaning; such errors may reflect first language interference.  Developing  Simple and expanded sentences that show emerging complexity used to provide detail.  Usage of general and some specific language related to the content area; lack of needed vocabulary may be evident.  Generally comprehensible when writing in sentences; comprehensibility may from time to time be mipeded by errors when attempting to produce more complex text.  2  Beginning  Phrases and short sentences; varying amount of text may be copied or adapted; some attempt at organzai tion may be evidenced.  Usage of general language related to the content area; lack of vocabulary may be evident.  Generally comprehensible when text is adapted from model or source text, or when original text is limited to simple text; comprehensibility may be often impeded by  er7or5.  1  Entering  Single words, set phrases or chunks of simple language; varying amounts of text may be copied or adapted; adapted text contains original language.  Usage of highest frequency vocabulary from school setting and content areas.  Generally comprehensible when text is copied or adapted from model or source text; comprehensibility may be significantly impeded in original text.  Considering Language  Social v. Academic  Social Language  Academic Language  Short segments  Longer segments  Words and Phrases  Sentences  Everyday Vocabulary  Specialized Vocabulary  Simple grammar  Complex grammar  Face-to-Face Interaction  Lectures and Textbooks  Immediate Feedback  Limited Feedback  Facial Expressions, Gestures  No Visual Cues  Informal  Formal  L1 v. L2  First Language  Second Language  Learned at home  Learned at school  Learned at a young age  Learned at an older age  Learned to communicate with family  Learned to communicate with teachers and peers in school  Largely unconscious  Partly “learned”  Partly “acquired”  Freedom to learn  Pressure to learn  What’s Being Assessed?  The Universal Language?  Nezd meg az alabbi abrat. AC ugyanolyan hosszusagu, mint BC, ACB szog ketszer akkora mint CBD szog, and DBE derekszoget zar be. Mekkora az Y szog?  Megoldas:  A) Y = ketszer X  C) Y = negyszer X  B) Y = X fele  D) Y = negyed X  Magyarazd:  Does This Help?  derekszog  right angle  ketszer akkora  twice the measure  negyed  1/4 (one-fourth)  fele  1/2 (one-half)  mekkora  how big  szog  angle  ketszer  two times  negyszer  four times  ugyanolyan hosszusagu  equals  Megolda & Magyarazd?  Nezd meg az alabbi abrat. AC ugyanolyan hosszusagu, mint BC, ACB szog ketszer akkora mint CBD szog, and DBE derekszoget zar be. Mekkora az Y szog?  Megoldas:  A) Y = ketszer X  C) Y = negyszer X  B) Y = X fele  D) Y = negyed X  Magyarazd:  derekszog – right angle  ketszer akkora – twice the measure  negyed – 1/4 (one-fourth)  fele – 1/2 (one-half)  mekkora – how big  szog – angle  ketszer – two times  negyszer – four times  ugyanolyan hosszusagu -equals  Question 10: Location and climate are major factors that affect the economies of various states. Due to these factors, different regions of the United States have different sources of income. Some regions produce many crops, while others rely on tourism for most of their income and do not produce any crops at all. States with coastlines on the Atlantic or Pacific Oceans harvest fish. In the warm Southern states, citrus fruits such as oranges and grapefruit are produced. Florida is a Southern state on the Atlantic Ocean.  PAUSE.  Based on what you heard, which is probably true for Florida?  [_] It depends mostly on tourism. [] It grows a wide variety of crops. [[] It has a good location and climate for corn. [ ] Citrus crops and fishing are probably very important.  From the WIDA CLIMBS Course Module 4: Language Assessment & Feedback  Dan and Jen are making chocolate chip cookies for a bake sale. The chocolate chip recipe is shown below. Step A Chocolate Chip Cookies Ł cup butter 1 egg 4 cup sugar 1 4 cups flour Ł teaspoon salt Ł teaspoon vanilla Ł cup chocolate chips This recipe makes 50 cookies. Dan and Jen need to make 150 cookies for the bake sale. How many cups of flour will they need in order to make 150 cookies? Answer:cups of flour  From the WIDA CLIMBS Course Module 4: Language Assessment & Feedback  Step B Using the same recipe and what you know about fractions and whole numbers, write a similar word problem. The number of cookies needed must be 50 to 150. Your word problem should ask for the number of cups of sugar that should be used. Solve your problem and show all your work.  From the WIDA CLIMBS Course Module 4: Language Assessment & Feedback  Teaching Models  ESL Push-In  Focus: English proficiency & academic achieveme nt in content areas  •  Students remain in classroom  •  Does not require bilingual staff  •  All students learn same material  •  Requires time for collaboration between ESL teacher and mainstream teacher  ESL Pull-Out  Focus: English acquisition  •  Most commonly used  •  Often disconnected from classroom  •  Does not require bilingual staff  •  Fits into school schedule  •  Research shows to be least effective  Collaborative Teaching  Me or Us?  Your Collaboration Journey  •  Single: Not co-teaching but willing to try.  •  Engaged: Proposed to co-teach but have not yet actually gone through with it.  •  Arranged Marriage: Told to co-teach but not sure if you are really doing it or how long it will last.  •  Happily Ever After: Currently co-teaching and looking forward to the next anniversary.  •  Divorce?  Co-Planning: Before Instruction  •  Identify content and language standards  •  Align content and language objectives  •  Determine essential background knowledge  •  Select instructional materials and adapt as necessary to reduce linguistic load  •  Develop differentiated instructional activities and assessments  Co-Teaching: During Instruction  •  Introduce content and language objectives  •  Make connections to prior knowledge and build necessary background  •  Engage students in differentiated instructional activities and assessments  Co-Assessing: After Instruction  •  Identify successes and analyze struggles  •  Evaluate student work  •  Outline next steps  One Teach, One Observe  Two Teach Together  Two Teach Apart  Rotate  C2IT Strategies  C2IT Strategies  In order to improve English language proficiency and increase academic achievement in the core subject areas, you must “see to it” that students are:  •  Comprehending  •  Connecting  •  Interacting  •  Thinking  Comprehending  Begin at the End  What is It?  •  Comprehension strategy  •  Reversed sequence of instruction  Traditional  Reversed  1. Read the material  1. Do extension activity  2. Answer study questions  2. Discuss material  3. Discuss material  3. Preview study questions  4. Do extension activity  4. Read the Material  Adapted from: “Teach the Text Backwards”, Judith H. Jameson, Center for Applied Linguistics and “Reading in Reverse”, Jodi Reiss, 102 Content Strategies for English Language Learners  Why it Works?  •  Begins with oral language  •  Contextualizes vocabulary  •  Moves from Whole  Part  •  Activates prior knowledge  •  Provides scaffolding  •  Presents new material in hands-on activity  •  Enables Concrete  Abstract transfer  How? Start by Doing  •  Science: Conduct the experiment first  •  Math: Have students create math problems that relate to their contexts and perspectives  •  Communication Arts: Watch the movie before reading the novel  •  Social Studies: Draw a family tree before doing a unit on immigration  Begin at the End  How? Discuss Activity  •  Have students create questions (and answers)  •  Students answer each others' questions  (Can you stump your friends?)  •  Introduce key vocabulary during discussion  •  Demonstrate relationships  •  Develop “schema”  How? Preview Study Questions  •  Identify main ideas and concepts  •  Set a purpose for reading  •  Identify text features in order to:  •  Demonstrate how text is organized  •  Identify where to look for specific information  How? Read the Text  •  Jigsaw text to reduce the amount of reading  •  Provide leveled texts  •  Use translated texts  •  Provide audio recordings of text  •  Use multiple reading strategies  •  Read-alouds  •  Shared reading  •  Choral reading  Connecting  Connecting to Students  What are some ways you build connections in your classrooms?  •  Background Knowledge  •  Classroom Community  •  Scaffolding  •  Cognates  Community Quilt: Create a Class Quilt  Connecting with Cognates  What is It?  •  Vocabulary development strategy  •  Reading comprehension strategy  Why it Works?  •  Makes connections to prior knowledge  L1 Knowledge L2 Knowledge Content in L1 Content in L2  Native Culture New Culture  How?  •  Read-Alouds  ◦  During read-alouds, have students raise their hands when they think they hear a cognate  •  T-Charts  ◦  Give students pairs of cognates to match  •  Cognate Notebooks  ◦  Have students keep notebooks of cognates they encounter during reading  •  Woops! Lists  ◦  Create a word wall of “false” cognates (embarazada)  Activity: Fun with Cognates  •  Explore the “Glossaries of Cognates” section by following the link in the chat box.  (Be sure to pick a language you DON’T speak!)  •  Answer the following questions in the chat box: Which cognate surprised you? What is one cognate you knew already?  Interacting  Classroom Conversations  What is It?  •  Scaffolded discussions of content  •  Written support for oral communication  Why it Works?  •  Promotes routine use of academic language and vocabulary in the classroom  •  Increases language development and content understanding simultaneously  How it Works?  1.  Present a conversation question  2.  Brainstorm with class after modeling an appropriate response orally  3.  Provide sentence frame for written response  4.  Demonstrate an appropriate written response  5.  Provide language for interaction  6.  Set a listening task  The roles of teachers and students is different, but the students and teachers are not alone as they shift these roles. Instead of the teacher interacting with each student individually, they interact with groups of students as they are meaning making through language with one another.  ٥٣ -٩ة •  ي«ألتهم ؤعق 5TU0ENT DISCOURSE MOWES  ．.&.  'TEACHER DISCOUR'SE M10VES ，雀．一呻＇，一···Il-,·一·一－－．．一·一｀；．归谝畴．，血阻回国IId酝．喟J',TlL，志l置盲-....-__酝l...旦．,., ..遍,_-__醴l一，“-，晒l圈匾叩··一一，0 一···一臧 Talk Move ( -) Say More/Elaborate Rephrasing/Restating (Asking for Evidence � or Reasoning � / Adding On/ Elaborate 'Applying Your OwnJ Reasoning � Revising Your Thinking � Teacher Talk So what you're saying is � What I hear you saying is_ Are you saying. Con you soy more obout�hol? What do you mean by that? Can you repeat what she/he said in � your own words? \\_Who can explain what she/he just said? � ( w-..,,, do yov ,,.,;n,.. thotf Wnat's your evidence? How doe; that idea compare to_'s example? Would somebody like to add on? Does anyone have a connection to this idea? Do you agree/disagree? Why? Do you support/oppose this idea? # Would you like to revise your thinking? I wonderif_ What do you think about what she said? Why? Student Talk r Yes, tho1 is who! I said. =J No, I didn't say that. I want to clarify my idea. I said_. � □ I mean to say_ An example is--l- Sh_ e_ /_He-so-:_i-d--lh-:..o-:..t-:..-:...-:... -_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_-_..,.J I think because My evidences__ I would like to add on to what _ said. I connect to what was said because_-( I agree because I disagree because� tf wont lo change my idea ecause want to revise my thinking. I think hink it might be false. m starting to wonder.  Activity: Exploring Student Interaction Routines  •  Find Column #16, Item #1 “Classroom Routines”  •  Explore the resources in Section #2 “Student Interaction Routines”  •  Share your ideas in the chat box using the following sentence starters:  I notice…  I think that…  I wonder why…  Thinking  Picture It in Words  What is It?  •  Strategy to promote higher-level thinking  •  Method of scaffolding reading and writing  Why it Works?  •  Provides a visual means of organizing content  •  Scaffolds transfer from graphic representation to written language  How it Works?  1.  Select a graphic organizer appropriate to the content being studied  2.  Have students complete the graphic organizer  3.  Develop a writing task based on the content and vocabulary  4.  Determine what linguistic support students need  5.  Have students complete the writing task  CoBallT., \"\"  The W!lJI Jtld W!>t ol COI  l°\"lrud)on.1I IAodJlcs  ..  lllaaliiillmlŁlar C9I  Overview of Common Graphic Organizers  w,,w, ŁOflJ\"'llt••· .,.. .-.nOOAt ..,., eW, lhirtklng 1klll$ ond l,Jngllfll• t,lruc;IUrH C,0 Clley i.rvtl?  eEIOW G a lrSt cr coov,,on g,&phlc c,ganr.n,s Ł., tiA-. oean de111Ł afld ll1at art .,i.lll>lf 1br Łou 1D Łe_  l)rtŁlllO<l-t;  l' 1/q,t )·our-Ł-.rlltt Ł Ł/lllll Ł111 tneltfl eolr.:110 -,i ilboUlŁ....cll llc-wegt 51:lb lllt)'QnOIStlil>tQel CIO (lfl ea<:110M or Ł 10 ttam tnO<t 600>.t 111ti Ł M!I!! Ł 1,-u<::.,.s 111a1 can be eomlWltcl 1,,111 Ł 1.41  21lt1',0tlf fflOUSlfOI-tne llvll.nQ..., biedWI tne f\\<Jlllcolu,m IOOŁ lftidHcC#lldl gr,lpluc CtglWlCZers•• beSUS10Clllld •illl tnelft  lmtgril\".llgŁ  Ui;,,oSUtloidno  USllQOrŁŁ  IIC!ldJcliotl Ł OfflVWw  s:eps Ł  TŁ  Con:eXlllaŁar.nmar  Pfo, °\"'9 Mtfflng:.il 1.np,.'  ŁŁOull)UI  ŁŁFeec:c.ct;  ŁŁŁŁ  5Wl  4IMMIH6-Setklog  CIIIM .nd E.lr«t now Cllirt  1111Jyzlno  CI-.tnd E.11«1: 11.ull,c)le C4'1SH  1>Btn11oon1no  Coml)lrl,011 & Cor11n11 Cl'oan  ultOOIWno  CClclttpt laddtf  ca,uie.'tn«t  DeclslOIIŁ• flow C\"8n  C0111111nnocori1nwn11  Facruat orid eon.,1e1 QUtiliOll  OKlsloii-makln,g  llle Fl1h1Jollt  tvolllibl'IO  Tbtfr41M  gallltrlng  G\\ltlltrlnQGnd  lnfernno  lflŁlr1IIO  PMl  WIYIWOIIJIIO  Pfedle!loft Tr•  111lll0  Probllm SOIW'tllg CIWII  prediCtlllg  OutllJOII MlilllX  p!lof,iy.sealnQ  TM $Qlff  ..._......-Ł ,.._,_..__  pŁpo•lng  Picture It in Words  Organizer  Language Needed  Compare  Contrast  And  However  Also  But  Similarly  Although  Both  Neither  In the same way  On the other hand  As…as  In contrast (to)  Similar (to)  Different (from)  Sentence Frames:  Both _____ and _____  have _____.  However, only _____ has _____.  Setting Goals  Considerations  Start with a language assessment: Assess the student's current language abilities in listening, speaking, reading, and writing in both the primary language and English.  Set realistic goals: Based on the assessment, set language goals that are realistic, challenging, and aligned with grade-level standards.  Consider the student's needs: Consider the student's individual language needs, strengths, and weaknesses when setting goals.  Focus on both academic and social language: Set goals for academic language (e.g., reading comprehension, writing) and social language (e.g., interaction, participation).  Incorporate cultural and linguistic diversity: Consider the student's cultural and linguistic background when setting goals and choose appropriate strategies to support language development.  Include measurable objectives: Ensure that language goals are specific, measurable, and tied to observable behaviors or performance.  Provide appropriate accommodations: Provide appropriate accommodations and support services to help the student reach their language goals.  Monitor progress: Regularly assess the student's progress towards their language goals and make modifications to the goals or support services as needed.  Students will:  (language function) (content topic) using (grammatical structure(s)) and (target vocabulary) in (domain) with/using (support(s)).  ©2013 Language Learning Matters, LLC  Example: Food Chains  Content-Required  Students will describe consumers in a food chain using simple sentences in present tense and the terms herbivore, carnivore, omnivore in oral statements using word cards.  “An herbivore is a plant eater.”  “A carnivore eats meat.”  ©2013 Language Learning Matters, LLC  Content-Related  Students will describe consumers in a food chain using negative statements and the terms herbivore, carnivore, omnivore in written statements using sentence frames.  Herbivores do not eat meat. Carnivores do not eat plants.  ©2013 Language Learning Matters, LLC  Content-Related  Students will explain consumers in a food chain using wh-questions and responses in complete sentences with pronoun subjects and the terms herbivore, carnivore, omnivore in oral statements in pairs using visuals.  herbivores  “What do eat?”  “They eat plants.”  ©2013 Language Learning Matters, LLC  &C Ł, , ·Ł\" lJ_ l1J Q ? \\3> • ,.Ł ' '? Ł\\Ł ? ·'\\) ' ,, ŁŁ Ł . Ł \\:",
