  {
    "id": "45e0acb4-403e-4ab1-9e99-01a16a27a4aa",
    "title": "Bridging Oracy and Literacy: Professional Learning Series Follow-up Walkthrough",
    "content": "Title: Bridging Oracy and Literacy: Professional Learning Series Follow-up Walkthrough\n\nContent: Bridging Oracy and Literacy: Professional Learning Series Follow-up Walkthrough  Observer:  Teacher:  Grade:  Date:  Time:  Component  Classroom Snapshot  Glows & Grows  Any  Scaffolding strategies and supports are used to enhance access to grade-level content.  ●  Asset-based language  ●  Safe learning environment  ●  Identities affirmed and represented  ●  Visual and graphic supports  ●  Linguistic supports  ●  Peer interaction  ●  Translanguaging  Glows:  Grows:  Building Knowledge  Instructional activities focus on building knowledge and expertise around a given topic through multiple modalities  ●  Ts activate prior learning through various structures (e.g., K-W-L charts, etc.)  ●  Ts build knowledge using various structures and modalities (e.g., virtual field trips, etc.)  ●  Ss have opportunities to use, hear, and see the language associated with the topic  Glows:  Grows:  Supporting Reading  Reading protocols are used to continue to knowledge using carefully selected texts to support students in identifying the purpose, organizational structure, and language features of a specific genre  ●  Ts use mentor texts to highlight purpose, organizational structure & language features.  ●  Ts use modeled or shared reading to:  ○  ask comprehension questions  ○  demonstrate comprehension strategies  ○  explore authors’ language choices  ○  highlight relevant language features  ●  Ts use reading groups and independent reading to provide differentiated support by reinforcing, practicing, or extending skills  ●  Ts use collaborative reading to provide repeated exposure to a text and to deepen comprehension by:  ○  identifying main idea & supporting details  ○  annotating the text  ○  analyzing the author’s craft, and  ○  summarizing & drawing conclusions  Glows:  Grows:  Exploring a Genre  Mentor texts are used to examine a genre and investigate ways authors use language to communicate a message to a particular audience.  ●  Ts use a variety of mentor texts to:  ○  highlight the purpose, organization, and key language features of a particular genre  ○  engage in a close analysis of the language characteristics of a given genre  ○  facilitate language-focused conversations to analyze texts for specific characteristics  ○  explore ways authors use language to communicate their messages to their intended audiences  Glows:  Grows:  Supporting Writing  Writing protocols are used to explore the writing process and its iterative nature  ●  Ts use modeled writing to turn ideas into written form as they make their thinking and writing processes visible through a think-aloud as they construct a text  ●  Ts use joint construction or guided writing to scaffold the co-construction of text through questions, think-alouds, etc.  ●  Ts use collaborative writing for interaction, cooperation, and consensus-building in the co-construction of a text  Glows:  Grows:  Using a Genre  The writing process is used to enable students to review, reflect on, and revise their own writing  ●  Ts have established writing routines that:  ○  allow time for independent construction  ○  enable students to present their writing and ask for targeted feedback  ○  engage students in rounds of critiques with the teacher, a peer, or in a small group  ○  promote the use of success criteria to review, reflect on, and revise their writing  ○  enable students to publish their writing  Glows:  Grows:  Additional Notes: ",
