    "id": "1df1367a-ccae-455e-ae68-7e154b732a5b",
    "title": "Multi-Modal-Daniel-Slides (Part 2/2)",
    "content": "Logistics Padlet  link: bit.ly / CAREMultimodal Session Goals:  October 11: Starting with your students &amp; Who is in your school November 8: Scaffolding &amp; Academic Language Defined December 13: Content &amp; Process January 10: Product &amp; Environment February 14: Planning for Interaction March 13: Understanding Scores &amp; Putting it all together\n\nScaffolding the Product\n\nExpressive Modes\n\nProduct Options\n\nOther Options maps diagrams journals speeches infographics videos skits storyboards This Photo  by Unknown Author is licensed under  CC BY-NC-ND\n\nGraphic Supports\n\nLinguistic Supports\n\nPresentations  Share what you created\n\nLearning Environment\n\nConsider Culture All instruction is culturally responsive. The question is: to which culture is it currently oriented?  - Gloria Ladson-Billings\n\nModel Making Connections Whose stories do we study?  What are their perspectives?  How do I connect with this topic/text? How can we make connections between different experiences (that honors everyone’s perspective)?  Sims Bishop, R. (1990). Mirrors, windows, and sliding glass doors.\n\nMulti-Modal Instruction  to Meet the Needs of English Learners Session 2: Scaffolding &amp; Academic Language\n\nMotivate and Engage through Responsive Instruction  Teachers have the opportunity to honor and celebrate the uniqueness of the backgrounds of all students, creating a climate with a strong sense of belonging, safety and confidence that diversity is a source of pride.  Maslow’s Hierarchy of Needs, 1943\n\nPlanning for Interaction\n\nStrategy: Peer Interaction\n\nTalk Moves\n\nGroup Students Intentionally Consider student strengths and needs within 4 language domains.  Reading vs. Speaking Heterogeneous skills for language modeling Homogeneous skills for sense-making Heterogeneous cultural backgrounds to practice collaboration skills or highlight potentially different perspectives\n\nStrategy: Model Critical Thinking Model with think-alouds using comprehension strategies solving math problems creating a hypothesis reflecting on perspectives Ask questions and provide time for students to think between each step as appropriate. Respond to the questions to model critical thinking and important language needed that students can repeat, replay and practice on their own.\n\nPlanning for Multiple Supports Which of these have you experienced or discussed today? Which do you still wonder about?\n\nStrategies Exploration\n\nExploring Scaffolding\n\nEveryone is a genius. But if you judge a fish by its ability to climb a tree, it will live its whole life believing that  it can’t swim . ─ Adapted from Albert Einstein\n\nA Scaffolding Definition To help learners achieve independence,  Vygotsky  outlined scaffolding as a tool for growth. Learners complete small, manageable steps in order to reach the goal. Working in collaboration with a skilled instructor or more knowledgeable peers help students make connections between concepts. As learners grow within their zone of proximal development and become more confident, they practice new tasks with the social support that surrounds them.   Vygotsky defined scaffolding instruction as the “role of teachers and others in supporting the learner's development and providing support structures to get to that next stage or level” (Raymond, 2000)\n\nA Scaffolding Definition Vygotsky maintains that learning occurs through purposeful, meaningful interactions with others and the teacher is central to learning; society and culture influence the attitudes and beliefs of a student towards learning and education;  language is the primary tool  used in the development of  learning,including  the transfer of sociocultural influences; and students benefit greatly in programs that are student-led, as they can use the social interaction to grow towards their potential level of development.  Vygotsky defined scaffolding instruction as the “role of teachers and others in supporting the learner's development and providing support structures to get to that next stage or level” (Raymond, 2000)\n\nA Scaffolding Image  Vygotsky defined scaffolding instruction as the “role of teachers and others in supporting the learner's development and providing support structures to get to that next stage or level” (Raymond, 2000)",
