    "id": "29c73919-6bc0-4e47-a138-5520be85fada",
    "title": "Multi-Modal-Daniel-Slides (Part 1/2)",
    "content": "Multi-Modal Instruction  to Meet the Needs of  English Learners Session 1: Starting with your Students &amp; Who is in Your School\n\nComponents of Scaffolding Relationships Concept Goals Beliefs Zone Interaction Sociocultural Why  Blue ? Why  Green ?  Why  Orange ?\n\nComponents of Differentiation Process Content Product Learning Preference Readiness Interests Sociocultural Why  Blue ? Why  Green ?  Why  Orange ?\n\nActivity Before Concept  Leaving scientific words for specific learning and focusing on the words that would enhance their learning and personal vocabulary  after  the experience\n\nMulti-Modal Instruction  to Meet the Needs of English Learners Session 3: Content &amp; Process\n\nPresentation of Content\n\nContent = WHAT Scaffolds = HOW Content is PRESENTED Standards remain the same for all students Scaffolding of content refers to making the same content more accessible Scaffolding Content\n\nProviding access to grade-level materials across proficiency levels (comprehensible input)  Representing information in multimodal ways (variety of types of input)  Keeping the task and content the same but adjusting for language proficiency Presentation of Content\n\nVideo: Audio &amp; Visual Support\n\nVaried Visuals\n\nWhy Multimodal?  Text: a unit of meaning with a purpose in a particular context. Meaning can be created with different meaning-making systems: art, words, sound, symbols, color, movement. Texts can be written, oral (conversations or presentations), digital (websites or news broadcasts), visual (diagrams, art, posters, advertisements), or a combination of all these types. This document defines texts as multimodal; they can contain symbols, maps, timelines, drawings, and other modalities.\n\nLanguage Supports Sentence Frames*  When  ________,  then  ________.  _________  causes  the Greenhouse Effect.  The Greenhouse Effect  happens/occurs when _____.  _______becomes trapped by _____.    *General cause and effect words are bolded for emphasis.  dangerous gases unhealthy heat sun’s rays same as a bubble one carbon damage/destroy caught by breathe oxygen part of the atmosphere traps hot oxygen the ozone temperatures escalate Word/Phrase Bank\n\nScaffolding Process\n\nResponsive Process Selecting strategies and  learning experiences  that align with  student learning needs\n\nMore Ideas Domain Speaking  (strength)  Listening  (strength)  Reading  (strength)  Writing (strength)  Speaking (growth focus) … Have Ss repeat some of the key language chunks and information they hear.  Have Ss read before they share their thinking verbally.  Have Ss write their ideas before sharing verbally.  Listening (growth focus) Draw attention to key language Ss should listen for during conversations.  … Have Ss read before they need to listen for information to learn.  Have Ss jot some ideas about a topic before they listen to information.  Reading (growth focus) Have Ss practice speaking about the topic they will read about. Make connections to key words used verbally to what they will look like in the text.  Have Ss listen to text to build background before they read.  … Have Ss jot some ideas about the topic before they read.  Writing (growth focus) Allow Ss to talk and share ideas before they write. Remind students to use frames and anchor charts from discussions.  Have Ss practice writing information and key language chunks they hear.  Use texts as mentors (pointing out the language skills and functional language).  …\n\nIntentional Grouping Consider strengths and needs in four domains Heterogeneous cultural backgrounds to practice collaboration or highlight different perspectives Heterogeneous skills for language modeling Homogeneous skills for sense-making Intentional check-ins Check for understanding  (Multimodal)\n\nBuild Literacy through Oracy\n\nStrategy: Model Critical Thinking Model with think- alouds using comprehension strategies solving math problems creating a hypothesis reflecting on perspectives Ask questions and provide time for students to think between each step as appropriate. Respond to the questions to model critical thinking and important language needed that students could repeat, replay and practice on their own.\n\nStrategy: Cooperative Learning\n\nStrategy: Peer Interaction Hear and practice discipline-specific language Repeat key language features and forms Support literacy development (oral before written) Increase retention  Builds thinking skills, peer relationships, engagement, and motivation                                                  Zwiers &amp; Crawford, 2011\n\nStrategy: Processing Time Provide additional processing time as needed Provide sample requests for more time*:  May I please have some more time to think?  May I ask a friend for help?  Would you please repeat?  May I please have some more information?  *Return to the student at an appropriate time and support the student’s response as needed.",
