    "id": "d5d5a6e7-40c7-4d06-83fb-1d05075e5e47",
    "title": "Oracy-Daniel-Slides (Part 3/4)",
    "content": "INPUT The Effects of Global Warming Global warming is the gradual increase in air temperature around the globe. It is caused by air pollutants, such as carbon dioxide and methane. The rise in air temperatures can lead to higher extinction rates and an increased risk of disease. It may also have political repercussions, such as wars over available resources.  Extinction of Important Species The biggest risk of global warming comes from habitat change. For example, polar bears rely on cold, snowy regions. As these regions shrink, polar bears will no longer have a place to live. Extinction affects the food chain and puts stress on the surviving animals to find adequate food.  Health Issues As temperatures rise, so do heat-related illnesses such as heatstroke. Increased air pollution correlates with the number of lung-related illnesses, such as asthma and lung cancer. Rising temperatures and unstable climates can also lead to rapid spread of disease.  Political Conflict As sea levels and temperatures rise, access to resources such as clean water and land decreases. This could raise the threat of political conflicts. Extreme drought can lead to fighting over the limited resources of water and food.  TITLE Definition Cause CONSEQUENCES Consequence 1 Consequence 2 Impacts Consequence 3\n\nSequential Explanation Phenomenon Identification Explanation Sequence 1 2 3 4 5\n\nHoney is a sweet, thick, sugary solution made by bees. The first step in making honey begins when field bees fly from flower to flower collecting the sweet nectar from the flowers. With their tongues, the nectar is sucked out by the field bees and stored in sacs within their bodies. After filling their sacs with these sweet juices, the field bees fly back to the beehive and the stored nectar is regurgitated into the mouths of house bees. These house bees then add enzymes from their bodies to the nectar. Lastly, the nectar is stored in a cell of a honeycomb. Overtime, the nectar ripens and becomes honey. When the honey is ready, it is collected by the beekeeper from the beehive and put into jars that are later delivered to the shops. Phenomenon Identification Explanation Sequence 1 2 3 4 5\n\nSESSION 5\n\nPrinciple 5: Foster an Inclusive School  Learning Environment     Bridging Oracy and Literacy for Multilingual Learners   Session 5 of 6\n\nTwo-Step Detective Process Noticing and Naming  – Reading like writers to identify and describe specific features that authors use in a particular genre. Lifting and Borrowing  – Highlighting  specific language features so they can be appropriated and incorporated into student writing.\n\nExamining Language Features Explanation Text Language Features  Card\n\nNotice and Name Adapted from:  Gibbons, P. (2009).  English learners, academic literacy, and thinking: learning in the challenge zone . Heinemann. Notice and name as many  examples of the sample language features as you can. Use the highlighter to distinguish between the different language features. Be prepared to share. https://tinyurl.com/Language-Features-Rainforest\n\nLift and Borrow Adapted from:  Gibbons, P. (2009).  English learners, academic literacy, and thinking: learning in the challenge zone . Heinemann. Look at the text again. Consider what you might lift and borrow. Add ideas to the Jamboard. https://tinyurl.com/Language-Features-Lift-Borrow\n\nAdditional Consideration “Nobody is born with a style or a voice. … We learn by copying. We’re talking about practice, not plagiarism. Plagiarism is trying to present the work of someone else as your own. Copying is about reverse-engineering.  It’s like a mechanic taking apart a car to see how it works.” -Steal Like an Artist,  Austin  Kleon How would you explain the difference between “lifting and borrowing” as an instructional practice, separate and distinct from the concept of plagiarism?\n\nWhat Lies Ahead Page 3 .\n\nSupporting Writing\n\nThree Approaches Modelled  Collaborative  Joint Construction p25\n\nWhat is Modeled Writing? Adapted from: Literacy Teaching Toolkit. Victoria State Government Education and Training. https://www.education.vic.gov.au/school/teachers/teachingresources/discipline/english/literacy/writing/Pages/teachingpracmodelled.aspx M odelled writing provides an opportunity for students to observe a proficient turning ideas into a written form as teachers make their own thinking and writing processes visible, so students can clearly see how th e text is constructed. Students do not offer ideas or suggestions during  the modelling, rather they observe strategies they will eventually incorporate into their own independent writing.",
