  {
    "id": "3e25b166-a684-4faa-89b7-629fcfc05b7a",
    "title": "Oracy-Daniel-Slides (Part 4/4)",
    "content": "The Role of the Teacher Adapted from: Literacy Teaching Toolkit. Victoria State Government Education and Training. https://www.education.vic.gov.au/school/teachers/teachingresources/discipline/english/literacy/writing/Pages/teachingpracmodelled.aspx Teacher is in complete control.  T eacher makes explicit exactly what is being modelled and why. Teacher may engage students in discussion after the modelling to clarify the expectation that the modelled actions and strategies will be incorporated into the students’ own independent writing.\n\nThe Role of Students Adapted from: Literacy Teaching Toolkit. Victoria State Government Education and Training. https://www.education.vic.gov.au/school/teachers/teachingresources/discipline/english/literacy/writing/Pages/teachingpracmodelled.aspx Students observe the actions, strategies and decisions modelled during the writing process. Students are actively engaged through questioning by the teacher that encourages them to: identify the  strategies being employed consider why they are being used, and determining whether they are effective Students are expected to  incorporating the actions and strategies in their own writing.\n\nWhat is Collaborative Writing? Collaborative  writing  is a process  for co-constructing  text in which all members contribute. It involves: Interaction  among group members throughout the process (e.g., brainstorming, drafting, and revising) Consensus-building  so that all members are actively engaged in decisions related to the text (e.g., organizational structure, word choice, etc.) Cooperation  in the development of a single text\n\nWhy Collaborate? Collaboration helps students: understand writing as a process develop a sense of audience better understand the conventions of academic discourse recognize that academic writing conventions are not simply arbitrary rules, but reflect readers’ expectations explain and sometimes defend their writing choices Adapted from: Vanderbilt University. https://www.vanderbilt.edu/bold/tools/collaborative-writing/#:~:text=Collaborative%20or%20team%20writing%20is,how%20the%20group%20will%20function .\n\nWhat is Joint Construction? Joint construction involves the teacher and students working together to collaboratively construct a text. Teachers scaffold the co-construction of text through questions, think-alouds, and explanations, etc. This approach is best supported when student have: already build knowledge of a topic been explicitly taught purpose, organization and language features of a particular genre\n\nBenefits for MLLs P rovides a context for  talking and learning about organizational structures and language features Supports students in understanding how purpose and audience impact language choices  S caffolds the students through the writing process  Allows students to write a text that they might not be able to write independently in a supportive context  Offers a model students can use during independent construction of genre-specific writing tasks\n\nSESSION 6\n\nPage 3     p7\n\nPrinciple 5: Foster an Inclusive School  Learning Environment     Bridging Oracy and Literacy for Multilingual Learners   Session 6 of 6\n\nUsing a Genre\n\nUsing a Genre Adapted from:  Derewianka , B. (n.d.).  A teaching and learning cycle .  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf Engaging students in language-rich experiences Finding out what students know about a topic Developing shared understandings\n\nIndependent Construction p30\n\nReview, Reflect, Revise p30\n\nFraming Questions  p31\n\nPage 3 .     p7\n\nSESSION 2\n\nBridging Oracy and Literacy for Multilingual Learners   Session 2 of 6",
