    "id": "89e65f74-d7a7-4400-a90e-153f189fcbc5",
    "title": "Oracy-Daniel-Slides (Part 1/4)",
    "content": "Principle 5: Foster an Inclusive School Learning Environment Bridging Oracy and Literacy for Multilingual Learners   Session 1 of 6\n\nTeaching &amp; Learning Cycle\n\nThe Teaching and Learning Cycle Page 5 Adapted from:  Derewianka , B. (n.d.).  A teaching and learning cycle .  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf\n\nThe Teaching and Learning Cycle Page 5 Gradual Release of Responsibility Increasing Student Independence Adapted from:  Derewianka , B. (n.d.).  A teaching and learning cycle .  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf\n\nAn Introduction Page 5 pp9-10\n\nExploring the Cycle In your breakout rooms number off 1-5. Read about your stage: 1=Building Knowledge 2=Supporting Reading 3=Exploring a Genre 4=Supporting Writing 5=Using a Genre Share highlights as a group. Be prepared to share with the whole group. Page 5 pp9-10\n\nBuilding Knowledge\n\nBuilding Knowledge Adapted from:  Derewianka , B. (n.d.).  A teaching and learning cycle .  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf Engaging students in language-rich experiences Finding out what students know about a topic Developing shared understandings\n\nSESSION 3\n\nPrinciple 5: Foster an Inclusive School  Learning Environment     Bridging Oracy and Literacy for Multilingual Learners   Session 3 of 6\n\nSupporting Reading\n\nIntended Outcomes After completing this workshop, participants will be able to: build shared understanding of content so students are equipped to write knowledgeably use mentor texts to explore the purposes, organizational structures, and language features of specific genres jointly construct a text with students based on what was learned from various mentor texts support students as they apply their learning to their independent writing engage students in drafting, reviewing, and revising to increase the effectiveness of their writing p2\n\nSupporting Reading Adapted from:  Derewianka , B. (n.d.).  A teaching and learning cycle .  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf In order to write, students need to gather information from texts. Most students, however, will need support in how to read the increasingly complex texts of school.\n\nSupporting Reading Adapted from:  Derewianka , B. (n.d.).  A teaching and learning cycle .  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf p14\n\nModeled Reading Adapted from:  Derewianka , B. (n.d.).  A teaching and learning cycle .  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf The whole country about the bay is agreeable beyond description and, if properly cultivated, would be a most fertile spot. The hills are covered with beautiful flowering shrubs, which perfume the air, making it odiferous.  The natives, in canoes, invited them hither, and behaved very amicably.  A chief entertained us military exercises, which are performed with the  patoo-patoo  and the lance.  “When I start reading a text, I skim it to see what it will be about.”  Explanation: it is usually easier to understand a text if its purpose can be predicted. “I wonder why the natives were in canoes?” Explanation: Active readers interact with the text by asking questions and being curious. “If I close my eyes, I can picture the scenery in my mind.” Explanation: Visualization can aid comprehension. “How can I understand the meaning of this word?” Explanation: Clues from the text can help define unfamiliar words. “I wonder which one is the  patoo-patoo ?” Explanation: Good readers look for the relationship between the written text and the accompanying images.\n\nShared Reading Adapted from:  Derewianka , B. (n.d.).  A teaching and learning cycle .  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf Teachers read text to the class, engaging students by: asking questions defining vocabulary in context explaining the relationship between written text and images interpreting key messages demonstrating comprehension strategies (e.g., previewing, predicting, questioning, visualizing, etc.) drawing attention to relevant language features (e.g., simple sentences for effect, tightly crafted sentence to express complex ideas, nominalization, etc.)",
