    "id": "a52a3123-4c8a-4e0b-97e9-b475b9d3afa4",
    "title": "Oracy-Daniel-Slides (Part 2/4)",
    "content": "Small Group Instruction Reading Adapted from:  Derewianka , B. (n.d.).  A teaching and learning cycle .  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf Teachers group students based on ability (e.g., reading levels, English language proficiency) to provide differentiated support and engage students in specific aspects of reading texts. Certain students might be having difficulty following cohesive relationships in a text, so the teacher would work with that group to identify how language creates links across the text.  Some students might need support in decoding words in a mentor texts while others might need help understanding how ideas are elaborated or condensed in texts.\n\nCollaborative Reading Adapted from:  Driving Deep Reading Comprehension in K–5 . (n.d.). Edutopia.  https://www.edutopia.org/article/driving-deep-reading-comprehension-k-5 Collaborative reading is a blend of shared reading – students read a text together, guided by the teacher – and close reading – students in small groups to analyze a text. One five-phase approach emphasizes repeated exposure to the text to deepen comprehension by: understanding the main idea annotating the text identifying key ideas and details analyzing author’s craft, and  summarizing and drawing conclusions\n\nIndependent Reading Adapted from:  Driving Deep Reading Comprehension in K–5 . (n.d.). Edutopia.  https://www.edutopia.org/article/driving-deep-reading-comprehension-k-5 To support the development of academic writing, students choose from a range of texts intentionally selected by the teacher to: 1) continue to “build the field,” and 2) exemplify characteristics of the focus genre. Independent reading can be accompanied by activities that reinforce skills that have already been introduced, such as: determining purpose drawing conclusions making connections between text and accompanying visuals\n\nExploring a Genre\n\nExploring a Genre Adapted from:  Derewianka , B. (n.d.).  A teaching and learning cycle .  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf Highlight the purpose, organization, and key language features of a particular genre Use mentor texts to engage in a close analysis of the language characteristics of a given genre Facilitate language-focused conversations to support mentor text analysis\n\nWhy Explore Genre? Adapted from:  Derewianka , B. (n.d.).  A teaching and learning cycle .  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf Since students will be expected to write in ways that are characteristic of given genres, close analysis of mentor texts and accompanying language-focused discussions provide critical support for all students. Use of mentor texts enables student to learn from exemplary writing that effectively achieves the purpose of a given genre and provides them with an expanded repertoire of language choices that they can then draw from as writers.\n\nBelieving In Students\n\nEssentials for Exploring a Genre Students understand what genres are and why people use a specific genre for a particular  purpose . Students engage in activities that allow them to discover  organizational structures  of genres. Students learn to “notice and name”  critical language features  and how to “lift and borrow” from mentor texts.\n\nJIGSAW:  Considering Mentor Texts Adapted from Dorfman &amp; Cappelli (2007) Independently read your section. Share main ideas. p17 Room 1:  What  is a Mentor Text? Room 2:  How to Select  a Mentor Text Room 3:  How to Use  a Mentor Text\n\nSESSION 4\n\nPrinciple 5: Foster an Inclusive School  Learning Environment     Bridging Oracy and Literacy for Multilingual Learners   Session 4 of 6\n\nDis tinct Characteristics of Genre Adapted from:  Derewianka , B. (n.d.).  A teaching and learning cycle .  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf\n\nPurpose\n\nIdentifying Purpose Adapted from:  Gibbons, P. (2009).  English learners, academic literacy, and thinking: learning in the challenge zone . Heinemann. Skim the text on the following slide. Select the purpose. Be prepared to defend your position.\n\nStructure\n\nIdentifying Organization Structure Adapted from:  Gibbons, P. (2009).  English learners, academic literacy, and thinking: learning in the challenge zone . Heinemann. Input Sequential Explanation Cyclical Explanation Consequential Explanation Causal Explanation Factorial Explanation\n\nPage 3 “One of the greatest gifts we can give others is to see in them what they cannot yet see in themselves. To fuel that until they own it, because when you help people realize they are much stronger than they think, it can give them a self confidence that can last the rest of their lives.”  The Power of Believing in Others  Paul Castagno TEDx Talk",
