  {
    "id": "08a4fa43-64f7-4f9c-a196-598163ecc759",
    "title": "Bridging Oracy and Literacy: Professional Learning Series Follow-up Walkthrough",
    "content": "Title: Bridging Oracy and Literacy: Professional Learning Series Follow-up Walkthrough\n\nContent: Bridging Oracy and Literacy: Professional Learning Series Follow-up Walkthrough\n\nGrade-level\n\nEngaging\n\nAffirming\n\nMeaningful\n\nObserver:\n\nTeacher:\n\nGrade:\n\nDate:\n\nTime:\n\nLesson:\n\nContent:\n\nTotal # Sts\n\n# ML Sts:\n\n# Dual ID Sts:\n\nComponent\n\nClassroom Snapshot\n\nGlows &amp; Grows\n\nAny\n\nScaffolding strategies and supports are used to enhance access to grade-level content.\n\nAsset-based language\n\nSafe learning environment\n\nIdentities affirmed and represented\n\nVisual and graphic supports\n\nLinguistic supports\n\nPeer interaction\n\nTranslanguaging\n\nGlows:\n\nGrows:\n\nBuilding Knowledge\n\nInstructional activities focus on building knowledge and expertise around a given topic through multiple modalities\n\nTs activate prior learning through various structures (e.g., K-W-L charts, etc.)\n\nTs build knowledge using various structures and modalities (e.g., virtual field trips, etc.)\n\nSs have opportunities to use, hear, and see the language associated with the topic\n\nGlows:\n\nGrows:\n\nRationale: Please provide justification for the overall level assessed:\n\nNot at all evident\n\nSomewhat evident\n\nEvident\n\nExemplary\n\nSupporting Reading\n\nReading protocols are used to continue to knowledge using carefully selected texts to support students in identifying the purpose, organizational structure, and language features of a specific genre\n\nTs use mentor texts to highlight purpose, organizational structure &amp; language features.\n\nTs use modeled or shared reading to:\n\nask comprehension questions\n\ndemonstrate comprehension strategies\n\nexplore authors’ language choices\n\nhighlight relevant language features\n\nTs use reading groups and independent reading to provide differentiated support by reinforcing, practicing, or extending skills\n\nTs use collaborative reading to provide repeated exposure to a text and to deepen comprehension by:\n\nidentifying main idea &amp; supporting details\n\nannotating the text\n\nanalyzing the author’s craft, and\n\nsummarizing &amp; drawing conclusions\n\nGlows:\n\nGrows:\n\nRationale: Please provide justification for the overall level assessed:\n\nNot at all evident\n\nSomewhat evident\n\nEvident\n\nExemplary\n\nAdditional Notes:\n\nExploring a Genre\n\nMentor texts are used to examine a genre and investigate ways authors use language to communicate a message to a particular audience.\n\nTs use a variety of mentor texts to:\n\nhighlight the purpose, organization, and key language features of a particular genre\n\nengage in a close analysis of the language characteristics of a given genre\n\nfacilitate language-focused conversations to analyze texts for specific characteristics\n\nexplore ways authors use language to communicate their messages to their intended audiences\n\nGlows:\n\nGrows:\n\nRationale: Please provide justification for the overall level assessed:\n\nNot at all evident\n\nSomewhat evident\n\nEvident\n\nExemplary\n\nAdditional Notes:\n\nSupporting Writing\n\nWriting protocols are used to explore the writing process and its iterative nature\n\nTs use modeled writing to turn ideas into written form as they make their thinking and writing processes visible through a\n\nthink-aloud as they construct a text\n\nTs use joint construction or guided writing to scaffold the co-construction of text through questions, think-alouds, etc.\n\nTs use collaborative writing for interaction, cooperation, and\n\nconsensus-building in the co-construction of a text\n\nGlows:\n\nGrows:\n\nRationale: Please provide justification for the overall level assessed:\n\nNot at all evident\n\nSomewhat evident\n\nEvident\n\nExemplary\n\nAdditional Notes:\n\nUsing a Genre\n\nThe writing process is used to enable students to review, reflect on, and revise their own writing\n\nTs have established writing routines that:\n\nallow time for independent construction\n\nenable students to present their writing and ask for targeted feedback\n\nengage students in rounds of critiques with the teacher, a peer, or in a small group\n\npromote the use of success criteria to review, reflect on, and revise their writing\n\nenable students to publish their writing\n\nGlows:\n\nGrows:\n\nRationale: Please provide justification for the overall level assessed:\n\nNot at all evident\n\nSomewhat evident\n\nEvident\n\nExemplary\n\nAdditional Notes:\n\n1\n\n1",
