  {
    "id": "b7977d36-63f5-41b9-b299-7bc7218803ba",
    "title": "This version is streamlined for field-based walkthroughs, focusing on how teachers: (Part 1/2)",
    "content": "“Promoting Autonomy through Formative Assessment Practices” Learning Walk Observation Tool that aligns with this session’s focus on enhancing student agency through formative assessment and feedback cycles.\n\nThis version is streamlined for field-based walkthroughs, focusing on how teachers:\n\nEstablish clear learning goals, intentions, and success indicators for content and language.\n\nDesign meaning-making tasks that scaffold learning and foster collaboration.\n\nUse constructive feedback practices that are specific, actionable, and growth-oriented.\n\nSupport student agency by empowering learners to monitor progress, reflect, and make choices in their learning.\n\nLearning Walk Tool: Promoting Autonomy through Formative Assessment Practices\n\nPurpose:To observe how teachers implement formative assessment practices that promote autonomy and ownership of learning among multilingual learners. The focus is on identifying how instructional design and classroom interactions foster self-monitoring, reflection, and the use of feedback to drive continuous improvement in both content understanding and language development.\n\nTeacher Name\n\nDate of Observation\n\nObserver Name\n\nLearning Goals, Learning Intentions, and Success Indicators\n\nFocus Area\n\nLook Fors\n\nEvidence/Notes\n\nLearning expectations for both content and language are explicitly highlighted.\n\nTeachers communicate and display clear content and language expectations that guide student learning.\n\nLearning intentions are scaffolded toward the learning goal.\n\nInstruction is sequenced in manageable steps with supports that build toward mastery of the learning goal.\n\nSuccess indicators provide specific examples of the language needed to meet the learning intentions.\n\nSuccess criteria specify the language features, structures, or vocabulary students need to demonstrate learning.\n\nStudents use learning expectations to monitor their progress.\n\nStudents reference learning goals or success indicators to self-assess and reflect on their progress.\n\nTeacher models effective language use.\n\nThe teacher intentionally demonstrates academic language through think-alouds, examples, and feedback.\n\nMeaning-Making Tasks\n\nFocus Area\n\nLook Fors\n\nEvidence/Notes\n\nInstructional tasks engage students with content and language.\n\nStudents participate in tasks that integrate content understanding with purposeful language use.\n\nTasks are scaffolded to meet diverse educational experiences and proficiency levels.\n\nTasks include differentiated supports that make grade-level content accessible to all learners.\n\nTasks promote interaction and collaborative problem-solving.\n\nStudents engage in structured peer interactions to negotiate meaning and co-construct understanding\n\nTasks require students to analyze, synthesize, or evaluate information.\n\nStudents apply higher-order thinking to interpret, connect, and extend learning.\n\nTasks are supported by sentence frames, word banks, or graphic organizers.\n\nStudents use visual and linguistic supports to organize ideas and communicate effectively.\n\nTasks provide opportunities for peer discussion and collaborative work.\n\nStudents collaborate through dialogue and shared tasks to deepen comprehension and language use.\n\nConstructive Feedback\n\nFocus Area\n\nLook Fors\n\nEvidence/Notes\n\nTeacher and peers provide feedback that is specific, actionable, and connected to success indicators.\n\nFeedback from teachers and peers identifies next steps aligned to clear success criteria.\n\nStudents are given opportunities to use feedback to revise their work.\n\nStudents apply feedback to make targeted revisions and demonstrate improved understanding.\n\nFeedback points to specific improvements or strengths.\n\nFeedback highlights concrete strengths and areas for growth linked to learning goals.\n\nStructures exist for peer feedback or self-assessment.\n\nStudents engage in structured opportunities for reflection and peer-to-peer feedback.\n\nStudents use feedback to make corrections or enhancements.\n\nStudents use feedback to refine their thinking, language use, or task performance.\n\nRubrics or checklists are used by students to self-assess or peer-assess.\n\nStudents use rubrics or checklists to evaluate their progress and provide constructive input to peers.\n\nStudent Agency\n\nFocus Area\n\nLook Fors\n\nEvidence/Notes\n\nStudents monitor their progress using learning intentions and success indicators.\n\nStudents track their learning by referencing posted goals and success criteria during tasks.\n\nStudents reflect on their learning and identify next steps.\n\nStudents engage in reflection to recognize growth areas and plan next steps for improvement.\n\nStudents make choices in how they demonstrate understanding.\n\nStudents exercise choice in products, processes, or tools to show their learning in meaningful ways.\n\nOptional Rating Scale\n\nDomain\n\n1 -\n\nEmerging\n\n2 -\n\nDeveloping\n\n3 -\n\nApplying\n\n4 -\n\nExtending\n\nLearning Goals, Learning Intentions, and Success Indicators\n\nMeaning-Making Tasks\n\nConstructive Feedback",
