  {
    "id": "863e5851-2581-4bbb-b2db-4a0764968b83",
    "title": "Engaging English Learners in Science Classrooms",
    "content": "Title: Engaging English Learners in Science Classrooms\n\nContent: Engaging English Learners in Science Classrooms\n\nLearning Walk Observation Tool\n\nTeacher Name\n\nDate of Observation\n\nObserver Name\n\nClassroom Environment\n\nThe classroom environment supports language-rich science instruction.\n\nComments\n\nCollaborative and Inclusive Atmosphere\n\nStudents are actively engaged in science discussions in groups or pairs.\n\nTeacher encourages student-to-student dialogue and peer support.\n\nVisual Supports for Academic Language\n\nWord walls, anchor charts, or science vocabulary displays are accessible.\n\nDiagrams, charts, and graphic organizers support comprehension.\n\nAccess to Background Knowledge\n\nTeacher activates students’ prior knowledge and leverages their assets.\n\nStudents are encouraged to bring their cultural, linguistic, and background experiences into learning discussions.\n\nLanguage and Content Integration\n\nScience content and academic language expectations are integrated.\n\nComments\n\nAcademic Language within Science Practices\n\nTeacher models and emphasizes language specific to Science and Engineering Practices (e.g., explaining, justifying, hypothesizing).\n\nConnection between Language and Content\n\nTeacher provides explicit links between science concepts and associated language (e.g., using specific vocabulary or sentence frames related to scientific genres).\n\nSupport for Diverse Learners\n\nLanguage supports such as sentence starters, graphic organizers, or partner work are available.\n\nSupports are tailored to English learners’ needs and strengths.\n\nAcademic Discourse and Genres\n\nOpportunities for academic discourse, particularly within scientific genres.\n\nComments\n\nGenre Awareness\n\nTeacher introduces or builds on specific science genres (e.g., explanations, procedures, argumentation).\n\nStudents practice these genres in their discussions and writing.\n\nStructured Academic Talk\n\nTeacher provides clear expectations for academic language during discussions (e.g., using evidence, comparing hypotheses).\n\nOpportunities for Language Practice\n\nStudents practice expressing scientific ideas using targeted vocabulary and structures.\n\nTeacher provides feedback that supports academic language development.\n\nCultural and Linguistic Responsiveness\n\nStudents' cultural, linguistic, and racial identities are seen as assets that are valued and incorporated into the learning process.\n\nComments\n\nAcknowledgment of Students' Backgrounds\n\nTeacher draws on students' cultural, linguistic, and life experiences to enrich learning.\n\nCulturally and Linguistically Relevant Examples\n\nTeacher incorporates diverse perspectives and examples that reflect students' backgrounds and experiences in science.\n\nInclusive Language Choices\n\nLanguage and content are framed inclusively, reflecting a respect for diverse linguistic and cultural identities.",
