  {
    "id": "3f76d56b-cd91-417f-a22b-c2121fa8fbbf",
    "title": "Engaging English Learners in Math Classrooms",
    "content": "Title: Engaging English Learners in Math Classrooms\n\nContent: Engaging English Learners in Math Classrooms\n\nLearning Walk Observation Tool\n\nTeacher Name\n\nDate of Observation\n\nObserver Name\n\nClassroom Environment and Engagement\n\nThe classroom environment supports language-rich math instruction.\n\nComments\n\nCollaborative Learning Environment\n\nStudents are actively participating in math discussions, including group and partner work.\n\nTeacher encourages collaborative problem-solving.\n\nVisual and Language Supports\n\nMath-specific word walls, anchor charts, and visual aids for vocabulary and key phrases are visible.\n\nGraphic organizers are used to support understanding.\n\nAccess to Prior Knowledge\n\nTeacher activates and values students’ prior knowledge and personal experiences as they relate to math concepts.\n\nAcademic Language in Math\n\nAcademic language is integrated with math content.\n\nComments\n\nAcademic Language within Mathematical Practices\n\nTeacher models and emphasizes the language specific to Standards for Mathematical Practice (e.g., reasoning, explaining, constructing arguments).\n\nLanguage Expectations for Mathematical Concepts\n\nClear language objectives are shared alongside math content objectives\n\nTeacher highlights and reinforces math-specific vocabulary and syntax.\n\nAcademic Language Supports for Diverse Learners\n\nLanguage supports (e.g., sentence frames, key phrases, graphic organizers) are used to assist English learners in understanding and using math language.\n\nOpportunities for Language Amplification\n\nTeachers provide opportunities for students to amplify language use in math discussions, explanations, and problem-solving.\n\nComments\n\nStructured Math Talk and Discussion\n\nTeacher encourages structured academic talk (e.g., think-pair-share, small group discussions) that emphasizes math vocabulary and precise language use.\n\nMath Genres and Language Structures\n\nTeacher explicitly teaches and supports math-specific genres (e.g., explanations, justifications, arguments).\n\nStudents practice these genres.\n\nOpportunities to Extend Academic Language\n\nStudents are given opportunities to expand on their ideas, justify answers, and explain processes, using appropriate math language and sentence frames.\n\nConnections to Language Development Standards\n\nTeacher links math instruction with language development standards to support academic language development.\n\nComments\n\nAlignment with Language Standards\n\nTeacher connects math language objectives with language development standards, especially for English learners.\n\nLanguage Amplification Techniques\n\nTeacher uses techniques to amplify language, such as paraphrasing, modeling complex sentences, and highlighting math-related language structures.\n\nFeedback on Academic Language Use\n\nTeacher provides targeted feedback that supports students’ use of math language, helping them refine academic vocabulary and expressions.\n\nAcademic Discourse and Genres\n\nOpportunities for academic discourse, particularly within scientific genres.\n\nComments\n\nGenre Awareness\n\nTeacher introduces or builds on specific science genres (e.g., explanations, procedures, argumentation).\n\nStudents practice these genres in their discussions and writing.\n\nStructured Academic Talk\n\nTeacher provides clear expectations for academic language during discussions (e.g., using evidence, comparing hypotheses).\n\nOpportunities for Language Practice\n\nStudents practice expressing scientific ideas using targeted vocabulary and structures.\n\nTeacher provides feedback that supports academic language development.\n\nCultural and Linguistic Responsiveness\n\nStudents' cultural, linguistic, and racial identities are seen as assets that are valued and incorporated into the learning process.\n\nComments\n\nAcknowledgment of Students' Backgrounds\n\nTeacher draws on students' cultural, linguistic, and life experiences to enrich learning.\n\nCulturally and Linguistically Relevant Examples\n\nTeacher incorporates diverse perspectives and examples that reflect students' backgrounds and experiences in science.\n\nInclusive Language Choices\n\nLanguage and content are framed inclusively, reflecting a respect for diverse linguistic and cultural identities.",
