    "title": "1.2 .Applying the Framework to Instructional Planning",
    "content": "Title: 1.2 .Applying the Framework to Instructional Planning\n\nContent: “Applying the Framework to Instructional Planning”, Learning Walk Observation Tool that aligns with this session’s focus on implementing the WIDA ELD Standards Framework (2020) during Unit Design and Instructional Planning.  This version is streamlined for field-based walkthroughs, focusing on how teachers:  •  Apply Steps 1 & 2 of the instructional planning process,  •  Use Key Language Uses (KLUs) meaningfully,  •  Integrate content and language objectives, and  •  Engage multilingual learners in authentic academic discourse.  Learning Walk Tool Applying the WIDA Framework to Instructional Planning  Purpose:  To observe how teachers apply the WIDA ELD Standards Framework during instructional planning and classroom instruction, specifically focusing on identifying content standards, key language uses (KLUs), and language expectations that align to lesson goals.  Teacher Name  Date of Observation  Observer Name  1. Teacher Practices: Framework Application  Focus Area  Look Fors  Evidence/Notes  Step 1: Identify the Content  Lesson objectives include both content standards and language goals aligned with WIDA ELD Standards.  Step 2: Identify the Key Language Use (KLU)  Teacher connects learning outcomes to one of the four KLUs: Narrate, Explain, Inform, or Argue.  Integration of ELD Standards  ELD Standards are referenced in planning or displayed in lessons; tasks promote simultaneous development of content and language.  Language Expectations (Functions & Features)  Teacher highlights how students will use language — e.g., connectors, sentence stems, vocabulary, or grammatical structures to meet content goals.  Modeling & Scaffolding  Teacher models language for content supports. (mentor text)  tasks, provides frames, or structured dialogue   visuals, sentence  Collaborative Learning  Students engage in discussions, peer exchanges, or group problem-solving using academic language.  2. Student Evidence of Language Use  Focus Area  Look Fors  Evidence/Notes  Use of KLU in Tasks  Students use genre of narrate, explain, inform, or argue as part of classroom activities (oral, written, or multimodal).  Authentic Use of Language  Students use language to complete meaningful tasks tied to content and language objectives aligned to the standards (e.g., explaining a science process, arguing from evidence).  Support for Emerging Proficiency  Students at different language levels receive differentiated supports (visuals, modeling, sentence stems).  Engagement in Modes of Communication  Students demonstrate multiple modes: interpretive (listening, reading, viewing) and expressive (speaking, writing, representing).  3. Reflection & Next Steps  Prompt  Notes  How did the teacher connect content and language learning?  Which WIDA components were evident (Standards, KLUs, Functions, Features)?  How were multilingual learners supported to participate in academic discourse?  What additional supports or coaching could strengthen the integration of WIDA ELD practices?  4. Optional Rating Scale  Domain  1 -  Emerging  2 -  Developing  3 -  Applying  4 -  Extending  Content-Language Integration  Use of KLUs  Differentiation for MLs  Student Engagement with Language  Alignment with Instructional Planning Process  Observer Information  ●  Observer: ___________________________  ●  Date: ___________________________  ●  Grade Level / Content Area: ___________________________  ●  Observation Length: ___________________________ ",
