  {
    "id": "e6f1db4b-779a-479e-aba5-31b9ae52a1c3",
    "title": "This version is streamlined for field-based walkthroughs, focusing on how teachers:",
    "content": "Title: This version is streamlined for field-based walkthroughs, focusing on how teachers:\n\nContent: “Applying the Framework to Instructional Planning”, Learning Walk Observation Tool that aligns with this session’s focus on implementing the WIDA ELD Standards Framework (2020) during Unit Design and Instructional Planning.\n\nThis version is streamlined for field-based walkthroughs, focusing on how teachers:\n\nApply Steps 1 &amp; 2 of the instructional planning process,\n\nUse Key Language Uses (KLUs) meaningfully,\n\nIntegrate content and language objectives, and\n\nEngage multilingual learners in authentic academic discourse.\n\nLearning Walk Tool\n\nApplying the WIDA Framework to Instructional Planning\n\nPurpose:To observe how teachers apply the WIDA ELD Standards Framework during instructional planning and classroom instruction, specifically focusing on identifying content standards, key language uses (KLUs), and language expectations that align to lesson goals.\n\n1. Teacher Practices: Framework Application\n\nFocus Area\n\nLook Fors\n\nEvidence/Notes\n\nStep 1: Identify the Content\n\nLesson objectives include both content standards and language goals aligned with WIDA ELD Standards.\n\nStep 2: Identify the Key Language Use (KLU)\n\nTeacher connects learning outcomes to one of the four KLUs: Narrate, Explain, Inform, or Argue.\n\nIntegration of ELD Standards\n\nELD Standards are referenced in planning or displayed in lessons; tasks promote simultaneous development of content and language.\n\nLanguage Expectations (Functions &amp; Features)\n\nTeacher highlights how students will use language — e.g., connectors, sentence stems, vocabulary, or grammatical structures to meet content goals.\n\nModeling &amp; Scaffolding\n\nTeacher models language for content tasks, provides visuals, sentence frames, or structured dialogue supports. (mentor text)\n\nCollaborative Learning\n\nStudents engage in discussions, peer exchanges, or group problem-solving using academic language.\n\n2. Student Evidence of Language Use\n\nFocus Area\n\nLook Fors\n\nEvidence/Notes\n\nUse of KLU in Tasks\n\nStudents use genre of narrate, explain, inform, or argue as part of classroom activities (oral, written, or multimodal).\n\nAuthentic Use of Language\n\nStudents use language to complete meaningful tasks tied to content and language objectives aligned to the standards (e.g., explaining a science process, arguing from evidence).\n\nSupport for Emerging Proficiency\n\nStudents at different language levels receive differentiated supports (visuals, modeling, sentence stems).\n\nEngagement in Modes of Communication\n\nStudents demonstrate multiple modes: interpretive (listening, reading, viewing) and expressive (speaking, writing, representing).\n\n3. Reflection &amp; Next Steps\n\nPrompt\n\nNotes\n\nHow did the teacher connect content and language learning?\n\nWhich WIDA components were evident (Standards, KLUs, Functions, Features)?\n\nHow were multilingual learners supported to participate in academic discourse?\n\nWhat additional supports or coaching could strengthen the integration of WIDA ELD practices?\n\n4. Optional Rating Scale\n\nDomain\n\n1 - Emerging\n\n2 - Developing\n\n3 - Applying\n\n4 - Extending\n\nContent-Language Integration\n\nUse of KLUs\n\nDifferentiation for MLs\n\nStudent Engagement with Language\n\nAlignment with Instructional Planning Process\n\nObserver Information\n\nObserver: ___________________________\n\nDate: ___________________________\n\nGrade Level / Content Area: ___________________________\n\nObservation Length: ___________________________",
