  {
    "id": "a5d14758-3a5f-4cc8-b7d1-81971d9656c3",
    "title": "Continuous Learning* (Part 1/4)",
    "content": "Continuous Learning*\n\nProgram Overview\n\nCARE Coaching is a professional learning program, offered through the Wisconsin Center for Education Products and Services, designed to ensure that district- and school-based educators have the knowledge and skills to provide equitable educational opportunities for multilingual learners. The CARE Coaching approach cultivates engagement in professional learning that is: Collaborative, Actionable, Responsive, and Empowering.\n\nC\n\nA\n\nR\n\nE\n\nCollaborative\n\nActionable\n\nResponsive\n\nEmpowering\n\nCARE Coaches value a culture of collaboration within and across the learning community.\n\nCARE Coaches focus on actionable changes in practice that can quickly enhance learning.\n\nCARE Coaches are responsive to the needs of educators and the learners they serve.\n\nCARE Coaches empower learning partners to be reflective, self-directed, and responsive.\n\nCARE Coaches foster meaningful relationships and develop productive partnerships that elicit and leverage the strengths, knowledge, and expertise of all participants across the learning community.\n\nThe Three Cs of CARE Coaching\n\nContinuous\n\nLearning\n\nContinuous learning opportunities enable educators to meet the needs of all learners with an emphasis on the unique needs of multilingual learners across K-12 classrooms.\n\nConsulting\n\nCARE Coaches consult directly with school- and district-level administrators to ensure the success of all students through various forms of technical assistance and program evaluation.\n\nCoaching\n\nWhether onsite or online, CARE Coaches meet you where you are to get you want to go through guiding questions, reflection, problem-solving, and goal setting.\n\nVersion 8.15.24\n\n*All continuous learning sessions can be customized to meet your needs and include ongoing coaching and/or a learning walk tool that can be used to measure real-world application of learning.\n\n1-Foundational Principles\n\n1.1-Building Capacity to Bridge Content Learning and Language Development\n\nDescription: Participants explore connections between grade-level content standards and components of an English Language Development framework. They examine Language Expectations to guide the creation of learning goals and Proficiency Level Descriptors to consider student work.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nConnect content learning and language development\n\nUse Language Functions &amp; Features to establish learning goals\n\nAnalyze student work using Proficiency Level Descriptors\n\n1.2-Applying the Framework to Instructional Planning\n\nDescription: Participants build on their understanding of the components of an English Language Development framework through a guided planning process designed to support content learning and language development for multilingual learners.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nConnect content and language development standards\n\nIdentify the Key Language Use of a summative task\n\nPrioritize Language Functions &amp; Features to scaffold instruction\n\n1.3-Promoting Equity through a Culturally Responsive Pedagogy\n\nDescription: Participants examine responsive pedagogical practices to meet the needs of culturally and linguistically diverse students.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nBuild a shared understanding of \"equity\"\n\nRecognize the impacts of culture on learning\n\nDescribe components of culturally responsive pedagogy\n\n1.4-Maintaining a School-Wide Focus on English Learner Success through Collaboration\n\nDescription: Participants explore components of a collaborative culture that support partnerships across the school community.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nDefine types of educator collaboration\n\nExplore collaborative practices\n\nConsider challenges and explore solutions\n\n1.5-Understanding Identification, Programming &amp; Proficiency Levels (for Mainstream Teachers)\n\nDescription: Participants learn how English learners are identified and what programs support language development. They examine the different types of English proficiency level scores and how they can be used to inform instruction and assessment.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nDescribe how English learners are identified\n\nDefine different language development program models\n\nExplain English proficiency level scores\n\n2-Pedagogical Practices\n\n2.1-Designing Multi-Modal Instruction to Meet the Needs of English Learners\n\nDescription: Participants examine the principles of Universal Design for Learning as a framework for classroom-based differentiation aimed at engaging all English learners in grade-level content learning.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nDevelop and deliver instruction that differentiates the presentation of content, the meaning-making process, and the means of demonstrating learning\n\nAddress readiness, especially based on English proficiency levels\n\nLeverage learners' strengths, interests, and preferences",
