  {
    "id": "9aaa9e13-e266-4980-ab95-9e088a770465",
    "title": "Continuous Learning* (Part 3/4)",
    "content": "Description: Participants will be introduced to definitions and descriptions of SLIFE. This session will help educators recognize who these students are, explore supports for classroom and instructional support, and use data to make informed instructional decisions.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nIdentify ways to engage and encourage these students\n\nAdvocate for making informed decisions about instructional programming and the unique learning needs of this group\n\n4.4-Exploring Best Practices Support Dual Language Learners and their Families in Pre-Kindergarten\n\nDescription: Participants will be introduced to eight essential actions for Dual Language Learners (DLLs) which represent a language-focused approach that promotes dynamic language and cultural practices\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nSupport DLLs in the school, home, and community\n\nPlan steps for developing or enhancing Early Years programming\n\nAdvocate for culturally and linguistically sustaining practices\n\n4.5-Opportunities to Support Multilingual Learners Classified as Long-Term English Learners\n\nDescription: Participants will deepen their understanding of how to effectively support long-term English learners (LTELs) through discussions and activities that explore the essential conditions necessary for fostering the success of all multilingual learners.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nDescribe beliefs, actions, and labels and their impact on student identity and achievement\n\nIdentify strategies to strengthen LTELs linguistic repertoires\n\n5-Family Engagement\n\n5.1-Building Community with Multilingual Learners through Family Engagement\n\nDescription: Participants explore the value of building community and examines the components of effective family partnerships. Content includes creating an environment that leverages families as reliable resources for engaging students and honoring culture to foster an inclusive learning community.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nDefine family engagement\n\nConsider research that supports family engagement\n\nExamine cultural proficiency\n\nIdentify ways to extend learning\n\n6-Literacy Development\n\n6.1-Enhancing Writing Proficiency through a Literacy Learning Cycle\n\nDescription: Participants examine a a genre-based approach, known as the Literacy Learning Cycle, that uses reading to develop writing skills. Writing instruction is scaffolded through each stage of the cycle. This cycle can be incorporated into different grade levels and content areas, enabling English learners to become effective and informed writers across the curriculum. NOTE: One- and two-day workshops available.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nShare understanding of how students write knowledgeably\n\nUse mentor texts to explore the purposes, organizational structures, and language features of specific genres\n\nPlan for opportunities to deconstruct and reconstruct texts\n\nSupport students with their independent writing\n\nEngage students in drafting, reviewing, and revising\n\n7-Discourse\n\n7.1-The Discussion Project\n\nDescription: This highly interactive professional learning course provides research-based techniques to design and implement equitable, inclusive, and engaging classroom discussion. The course provides in-depth learning, focused on designing, implementing, and facilitating high-quality classroom discussion.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nBuild and maintain community\n\nStructure discussions and craft questions\n\nFacilitate whole class interactions\n\nPlan and assess discussion\n\nWCEPS provides the Discussion Project with permission of the University of Wisconsin-Madison. The Discussion Project and logo are trademarks of the Board of Regents\n\n8-Bilingual Offerings (delivered in Spanish)\n\n8.1-La lmportancia de la ludica a traves de estrategias de ensenanza\n\nDescription: Este taller retoma enfoques de diversos teóricos que hablan acerca del juego como actividad central para el aprendizaje de los niños, también centraremos nuestra atención en estrategias pedagógicas lúdicas que apoyan nuestra práctica docente y en cómo al conocer de qué manera aprenden nuestros estudiantes nosotros podremos intervenir en su aprendizaje. A través de discusiones en grupo y actividades prácticas, aprenderemos a recrear actividades lúdicas que sean atractivas, divertidas, estimulen la participación activa de los niños y promuevan su desarrollo cognitivo, emocional y social.\n\nAl termino de este taller los participantes podran:\n\nPromover la ensenanza ludica mediante estrategias divertidas de rigor y relevancia para estudiantes bi/multilingues\n\nAplicar estrategias pedag6gicas en el aula bajo un enfoque ludico centrado en el contexto sociocultural y lingufstico de los estudiantes bi/multilingues\n\nCambiar aspectos de la ensenanza tradicionalista a una ensenanza ludica de empoderamiento\n\nContact educators@wceps.org or call 877-272-5593",
