  {
    "id": "b3be200d-548e-4817-896d-cdd42b511e8a",
    "title": "Continuous Learning* (Part 2/4)",
    "content": "2.2-Promoting Autonomy through Formative Assessment Practices\n\nDescription: Participants explore effective classroom-based formative assessment practices that enable teachers to gauge growth for students at various English proficiency levels and with wide-ranging educational backgrounds. This growth can be measured against learning goals and success criteria, which students can also use to monitor their progress and self-assess.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nUse learning goals and success criteria to clarify expectations for content learning and language use\n\nEngage students in meaning-making tasks that support content learning and academic language development\n\nFoster constructive feedback that promotes student agency\n\n2.3-Integrating Content Learning &amp; Language Development in Lesson Planning\n\nDescription: Participants learn how to develop instruction that promotes student engagement, ensures equitable access to grade-level concepts, and accelerates content learning and language development.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nArticulate learning goals and success criteria\n\nDevelop activities that engage students in multiple modes of communication and purposeful language use\n\nIntegrate varied supports based on student needs\n\n2.4-Engaging in Reflective Practice through Classroom-Based Action Research and Data Analysis\n\nDescription: Participants identify a classroom condition they would like to change and determine a change in practice that might improve that condition and enhance student achievement. This session enables teachers to gather, analyze, and reflect on classroom-based data to promote growth that will positively impact outcomes for English learners. NOTE: This is a two-day workshop.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nIdentify a change in practice\n\nDetermine what type of data to collect\n\nDevelop an action plan\n\nA follow-up workshop enables participants to: analyze data to identify patterns, determine impact, and plan next steps\n\n3-Content-Based Instruction\n\n3.1-Engaging English Learners in Science Classrooms\n\nDescription: Participants develop a common understanding of academic language expectations in science and explore strategies to foster a collaborative and language-rich environment. Through analysis of the academic language embedded in the Science and Engineering Practices, participants will have a deeper understanding of how to actively engage English learners in content learning and language development.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nDescribe the role of collaboration in science\n\nConsider students’ assets for learning\n\nRecognize the impact of race, culture, and language on learning\n\nLink content knowledge and academic language\n\nFocus on genres to support academic discourse\n\n3.2-Engaging English Learners in Math Classrooms\n\nDescription: Participants develop a common understanding of academic language expectations in math and explore strategies to foster a language-rich environment. Through analysis of the academic language embedded in the Standards for Mathematical Practices, participants will have a deeper understanding of how to actively engage English learners in content learning and academic English language development.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nDescribe the academic language expectation in math\n\nMake connections between language development standards and the Standards for Mathematical Practice\n\nIdentify opportunities for language amplifications in a math lesson\n\nApply language amplifications to instruction in math\n\n4-Special Populations\n\n4.1-Creating an Inclusive Learning Environment (Focus on Students with Individualized Educational Plans)\n\nDescription: Participants explore principles for making grade-level content accessible to students at all levels of English language proficiency with a focus on dually-identified students.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nIncorporate Universal Design for Learning principles in planning\n\nLeverage learners' strengths, interests, and preferences\n\n4.2-Empowering Newcomers for Academic Success\n\nDescription: This session focuses on ways to empower newcomers for success in academic classrooms. Topics covered include identifying elements of effective scaffolding, incorporating strategies to make grade-level content accessible, developing language supports, and leveraging learners' strengths.\n\nAfter this workshop, participants will be able to:\n\nDescribe elements of effective scaffolding for Newcomers\n\nIdentify strategies to grade-level content is accessible\n\nDevelop lessons that scaffold content and language development\n\nIdentify ways to leverage strengths, interests, and preferences\n\n4.3-Supporting Students with Limited or Interrupted Formal Education (SLIFE)",
