  {
    "id": "787d4454-51b2-4472-97fa-c16e0922c97a",
    "title": "Maintaining a Schoolwide Focus on English Learner Success through Collaboration",
    "content": "Title: Maintaining a Schoolwide Focus on English Learner Success through Collaboration\n\nContent: Maintaining a Schoolwide Focus on English Learner Success through Collaboration\n\nObservation Tool\n\nTeacher Name\n\nDate of Observation\n\nObserver Name\n\nDefined Collaboration Types\n\nCollaboration exists along a continuum.\n\nComments\n\nCollaboration type (e.g., planning, instruction, assessment, reflection) is explicitly identified.\n\nParticipants can articulate the goals and purpose of the collaboration.\n\nTeams consistently engage in a wide range of collaborative practices.\n\nEfforts to integrate multiple collaboration types (e.g., reflection tied to planning) are evident.\n\nCollaborative Planning\n\nLeverages a range of expertise\n\nComments\n\nGroup members contribute equitably to idea generation and decision-making.\n\nTeams use structured protocols (e.g., agenda, roles, timelines) to guide planning.\n\nJoint planning considers the needs of diverse stakeholders (e.g., students, families, educators).\n\nEvidence of co-created resources, lesson plans, or shared tools is observed.\n\nCollaborative Instruction\n\nTeachers use various models of co-teaching to best meet the needs of students.\n\nComments\n\nCo-teaching roles are clearly defined and executed (e.g., lead/support, parallel teaching, etc.).\n\nTeachers demonstrate flexibility and adaptability in their roles during instruction.\n\nStudents actively engage with and benefit from collaborative teaching approaches.\n\nInstructional strategies incorporate feedback or input from previous collaboration sessions.\n\nCollaborative Assessment\n\nTeachers evaluate student work collectively to inform instruction.\n\nComments\n\nTeam members use consistent and agreed-upon criteria to evaluate student work or outcomes.\n\nCollaborative discussions focus on identifying trends, strengths, and areas for growth in student data.\n\nFeedback provided to students reflects shared perspectives and decisions made collaboratively.\n\nEvidence of using assessment findings to inform collaborative planning or instruction is present\n\nCollaborative Reflection\n\nTeachers engage in reflections on pedagogical practices.\n\nComments\n\nTeams engage in reflective practices that are guided by structured questions or protocols.\n\nReflection includes diverse perspectives, ensuring all voices are heard.\n\nParticipants evaluate the effectiveness of the collaborative process, identifying what worked well and areas for improvement.\n\nReflection outcomes are documented and used to inform future collaborations.\n\nAddressing Challenges\n\nWork identifies solutions for challenges.\n\nComments\n\nTeams identify challenges collaboratively and prioritize them for resolution.\n\nParticipants use problem-solving protocols or strategies to address barriers to collaboration (e.g., time constraints, communication breakdowns).\n\nEvidence of compromise or consensus-building is observed during conflict resolution.\n\nSolutions are revisited and refined during future collaborations to ensure effectiveness.",
