  {
    "id": "07560dcb-8559-4b6c-a55a-01467ca9a74a",
    "title": "Learning Walk Observation Tool",
    "content": "Title: Learning Walk Observation Tool\n\nContent: WIDA English Language Development (ELD) Standards Framework (2020) and implementing the CARE Coaching approach that supports multilingual learners.\n\nLearning Walk Observation Tool\n\nBuilding Capacity to Bridge Content Learning and Language Development\n\nPurpose:To collect evidence of how teachers integrate language development with content instruction, using the WIDA ELD Standards Framework and Key Language Uses (KLUs) as a guide.\n\nObservation Focus:\n\nHow teachers plan and deliver instruction that reflects an asset-based view of multilingual learners.\n\nHow students use language to narrate, explain, inform, and argue within content learning.\n\nEvidence of language functions, features, and proficiency scaffolds aligned to students’ levels (PLDs).\n\n1. Teacher Practices\n\nFocus Area\n\nLook Fors\n\nEvidence/Notes\n\nAsset-Based Philosophy\n\nTeacher references students’ linguistic and cultural backgrounds as resources for learning.\n\nContent-Language Integration\n\nLesson objectives or tasks explicitly connect content goals with language goals.\n\nUse of WIDA Framework Components\n\nTeacher references or plans with Key Language Uses, Language Functions, or Features (e.g., sentence stems, graphic organizers).\n\nStudent Engagement with Language\n\nStudents use language for meaningful purposes—explaining, reasoning, discussing, or justifying ideas.\n\nUse of Visuals/Multimodal Texts\n\nTeacher incorporates visuals, models, or multimodal supports to scaffold language and content understanding.\n\nFeedback Aligned to PLDs\n\nTeacher differentiates supports and feedback based on students’ proficiency levels.\n\n2. Student Evidence of Language Use\n\nFocus Area\n\nLook Fors\n\nEvidence/Notes\n\nKey Language Use (KLU)\n\nStudents demonstrate one or more KLUs: • Narrate – retelling events, describing experiences• Explain – describing how/why• Inform – giving factual information• Argue – supporting claims with evidence\n\nDiscourse Dimension\n\nStudents organize their ideas coherently across sentences or multimodal texts (oral, written, visual).\n\nSentence Dimension\n\nStudents use increasingly complex grammatical structures (e.g., connectors, clauses).\n\nWord/Phrase Dimension\n\nStudents use precise, content-related, and academic vocabulary.\n\n3. Differentiation by Grade Band\n\nGrade Band\n\nPossible Classroom Look-Fors\n\nExample Evidence\n\nElementary (K–5)\n\n• Visuals and manipulatives to support meaning.• Sentence frames for describing or sequencing.• Collaborative talk to narrate or explain.\n\n“Students used drawings and simple sentences to explain the water cycle.”\n\nMiddle School (6–8)\n\n• Structured argumentation with evidence.• Scaffolded writing to inform or explain.• Opportunities for oral rehearsal before writing.\n\n“Students justified claims in science using sentence starters like ‘because…’ and ‘therefore…’.”\n\nHigh School (9–12)\n\n• Sophisticated use of connectors and nominalizations.• Integration of multimodal texts (charts, models, essays).• Student-led discussions connecting academic concepts and real-world issues.\n\n“Students presented counterclaims using academic language and evidence.”\n\n4. Reflection &amp; Next Steps\n\nPrompt\n\nNotes\n\nWhat evidence of content and language integration was observed?\n\nWhich WIDA components were most visible (KLUs, Functions, Features, PLDs)?\n\nHow are teachers scaffolding language for diverse proficiency levels?\n\nWhat professional learning or coaching might support next steps?\n\n5. Optional Rating Scale (for consistency across observers)\n\nDomain\n\n1 - Emerging\n\n2 - Developing\n\n3 - Applying\n\n4 - Extending\n\nAsset-Based Practice\n\nContent-Language Integration\n\nStudent Language Use\n\nDifferentiation by PLD\n\nMultimodal Supports\n\n🔍 Implementation Notes\n\nUse during brief classroom visits (5–10 minutes).\n\nCollect low-inference evidence—write what you see and hear rather than judgments.\n\nDebrief with teachers to reflect on language and content integration.\n\nLink findings to CARE Coaching cycles and professional learning communities (PLCs).",
