  {
    "id": "08dff267-5db8-41e5-9b37-4b8f2fecfbaf",
    "title": "Empowering Newcomers for Academic Success",
    "content": "Title: Empowering Newcomers for Academic Success\n\nContent: Empowering Newcomers for Academic Success\n\nLearning Walk Observation Tool\n\nTeacher:\n\nSubject:\n\nGrade:\n\nDate:\n\nClassroom Environment and Engagement\n\nThe classroom provides an inclusive, supportive environment for newcomers to engage with grade-level content.\n\nComments\n\nWelcoming and Inclusive Atmosphere\n\nClassroom reflects diverse backgrounds and includes visuals or materials that represent students’ cultures and languages.\n\nStudents are engaged in positive interactions.\n\nCollaborative Learning Opportunities\n\nStudents are encouraged to participate in group activities that promote peer support and interaction.\n\nTeacher facilitates inclusive groupings for newcomers.\n\nAccess to Visual and Interactive Supports\n\nUse of visual aids, interactive activities, or hands-on materials to support comprehension.\n\nAccessible resources are provided for newcomer students.\n\nEffective Supports\n\nSupports make grade-level content and language accessible to newcomers.\n\nComments\n\nSupports for Academic Language\n\nSentence frames, word banks, or graphic organizers are provided for language support.\n\nTeacher models key academic language in accessible ways.\n\nStep-by-Step Instruction and Support\n\nContent is broken into manageable steps, with clear instructions and checks for understanding.\n\nSupports are reduced as students show understanding.\n\nUse of Multimodal Instruction\n\nLessons incorporate visuals, gestures, and varied formats (e.g., videos, demonstrations) to aid comprehension.\n\nContent Accessibility and Engagement with Grade-Level Standards\n\nGrade-level content is made accessible to newcomers and they are actively engaged with the material.\n\nComments\n\nAccess to Grade-Level Concepts\n\nTeacher uses simplified language or visuals to explain complex ideas while maintaining grade-level rigor.\n\nContent-related vocabulary and concepts are pre-taught or reinforced through repetition and varied contexts.\n\nEngaging with Rigorous Content\n\nNewcomers are encouraged to express understanding of content in different ways (e.g., drawings, first-language responses, graphic organizers).\n\nLeveraging Students’ Strengths, Interests, and Preferences\n\nTeacher draws upon students’ strengths, interests, and preferences to support learning and engagement.\n\nComments\n\nRecognizing and Connection to Students’ Strengths and Interests\n\nTeacher incorporates students’ cultural or linguistic backgrounds as assets in learning activities.\n\nLessons include examples, topics, or projects that relate to students’ real-world experiences or interests.\n\nProviding Choices and Encouraging Autonomy\n\nStudents are given options for activities, assignments, or group roles that reflect their preferences or strengths.\n\nFeedback and Language Development\n\nTeacher provides feedback that supports both content comprehension and language development for newcomers.\n\nComments\n\nConstructive and Supportive Feedback\n\nTeacher offers specific, encouraging feedback on content understanding and language use.\n\nTeacher provides corrective feedback on language use that builds confidence.\n\nOpportunities for Practice and Growth\n\nStudents have time and space to revise responses or participate in practice activities for both language and content learning.\n\n© 2024 CARE Coaching",
