  {
    "id": "030e9d0e-1a04-4151-b517-6ab30e466db2",
    "title": "2.2_Learning_Walk_Reformatted (Part 1/1)",
    "content": "“Promoting Autonomy through Formative Assessment Practices” Learning Walk Observation Tool that aligns with this session’s focus on enhancing student agency through formative assessment and feedback cycles.  This version is streamlined for field-based walkthroughs, focusing on how teachers:  •  Establish clear learning goals, intentions, and success indicators for content and language.  •  Design meaning-making tasks that scaffold learning and foster collaboration.  •  Use constructive feedback practices that are specific, actionable, and growth-oriented.  •  Support student agency by empowering learners to monitor progress, reflect, and make choices in their learning.  Learning Walk Tool: Promoting Autonomy through Formative Assessment Practices Purpose:  To observe how teachers implement formative assessment practices that promote autonomy and ownership of learning among multilingual learners. The focus is on identifying how instructional design and classroom interactions foster self-monitoring, reflection, and the use of feedback to drive continuous improvement in both content understanding and language development.  Teacher Name  Date of Observation  Observer Name  Learning Goals, Learning Intentions, and Success Indicators  Focus Area  Look Fors  Evidence/Notes  Learning expectations for both content and language are explicitly highlighted.  Teachers communicate and display clear content and language expectations that guide student learning.  Learning intentions are scaffolded toward the learning goal.  Instruction is sequenced in manageable steps with supports that build toward mastery of the learning goal.  Success indicators provide specific examples of the language needed to meet the learning intentions.  Success criteria specify the language features, structures, or vocabulary students need to demonstrate learning.  Students use learning expectations to monitor their progress.  Students reference learning goals or success indicators to self-assess and reflect on their progress.  Teacher models effective language use.  The teacher intentionally demonstrates academic language through think-alouds, examples, and feedback.  Meaning-Making Tasks  Focus Area  Look Fors  Evidence/Notes  Instructional tasks engage students with content and language.  Students participate in tasks that integrate content understanding with purposeful language use.  Tasks are scaffolded to meet diverse educational experiences and proficiency levels.  Tasks include differentiated supports that make grade-level content accessible to all learners.  Tasks promote interaction and collaborative problem-solving.  Students engage in structured peer interactions to negotiate meaning and co-construct understanding  Tasks require students to analyze, synthesize, or evaluate information.  Students apply higher-order thinking to interpret, connect, and extend learning.  Tasks are supported by sentence frames, word banks, or graphic organizers.  Students use visual and linguistic supports to organize ideas and communicate effectively.  Tasks provide opportunities for peer discussion and collaborative work.  Students collaborate through dialogue and shared tasks to deepen comprehension and language use.  Constructive Feedback  Focus Area  Look Fors  Evidence/Notes  Teacher and peers provide feedback that is specific, actionable, and connected to success indicators.  Feedback from teachers and peers identifies next steps aligned to clear success criteria.  Students are given opportunities to use feedback to revise their work.  Students apply feedback to make targeted revisions and demonstrate improved understanding.  Feedback points to specific improvements or strengths.  Feedback highlights concrete strengths and areas for growth linked to learning goals.  Structures exist for peer feedback or self-assessment.  Students engage in structured opportunities for reflection and peer-to-peer feedback.  Students use feedback to make corrections or enhancements.  Students use feedback to refine their thinking, language use, or task performance.  Rubrics or checklists are used by students to self-assess or peer-assess.  Students use rubrics or checklists to evaluate their progress and provide constructive input to peers.  Student Agency  Focus Area  Look Fors  Evidence/Notes  Students monitor their progress using learning intentions and success indicators.  Students track their learning by referencing posted goals and success criteria during tasks.  Students reflect on their learning and identify next steps.  Students engage in reflection to recognize growth areas and plan next steps for improvement.  Students make choices in how they demonstrate understanding.  Students exercise choice in products, processes, or tools to show their learning in meaningful ways.  Optional Rating Scale  Domain  1 -  Emerging  2 -  Developing  3 -  Applying  4 -  Extending  Learning Goals, Learning Intentions, and Success Indicators  Meaning-Making Tasks  Constructive Feedback  Student Agency  Observer Information  ●  Observer: ___________________________  ●  Date: ___________________________  ●  Grade Level / Content Area: ___________________________  ●  Observation Length: ___________________________",
