  {
    "id": "9bd29509-8d83-4a74-b7fd-d7eae5ec6080",
    "title": "Teacher Name",
    "content": "Title: Teacher Name\n\nContent: Building Community with Multilingual Learners through Family Engagement Learning Walk Observation Tool\n\nTeacher Name\n\nDate of Observation\n\nObserver Name\n\nDefine Family Engagement\n\nEvidence of a common definition of family engagement.\n\nComments\n\nTeacher can articulate a definition of family engagement that includes collaboration and reciprocity.\n\nEvidence of two-way communication between families and the classroom (e.g., feedback forms, family surveys, parent-teacher discussions).\n\nExamples of practices that actively involve families in student learning or classroom decision-making.\n\nConsider Research That Supports Family Engagement\n\nEngagement practices are based on research.\n\nComments\n\nClassroom practices reflect evidence-based strategies, such as regular family-teacher communication or involvement in school activities.\n\nTeacher references specific research-informed strategies during discussions or planning.\n\nVisible displays or documentation of how family involvement is linked to student achievement (e.g., charts, posters).\n\nExamine Cultural Proficiency\n\nClassroom environment shows evidence diversity is honored.\n\nComments\n\nClassroom displays and resources (books, visuals, materials) represent diverse cultures, languages, and perspectives.\n\nTeacher actively incorporates students’ cultural and linguistic backgrounds into lessons and discussions.\n\nEvidence of classroom norms promoting respect for diversity (e.g., student discussions about cultural identity or values).\n\nIdentify Ways to Extend Learning\n\nLearning extends beyond the classroom\n\nComments\n\nAssignments or activities explicitly designed for family participation (e.g., home projects, interviews with family members).\n\nCommunication tools provide families with guidance on supporting learning at home (e.g., handouts, translated instructions, digital platforms).\n\nEvidence that families are encouraged to share their expertise or contribute to classroom learning (e.g., family presentations or story-sharing sessions).\n\nFoster Inclusive Communication\n\nCommunication is collaborative and reciprocal.\n\nComments\n\nCommunication methods (newsletters, emails, announcements) are available in multiple languages and formats.\n\nTeacher actively seeks input from families about preferred communication methods.\n\nEvidence of regular updates and accessible communication about classroom activities and student progress.\n\nRecognize Families as Co-Educators\n\nLearning is connected to families’ funds of knowledge\n\nComments\n\nActivities or lesson plans explicitly incorporate family knowledge and experiences.\n\nFamilies are invited to participate in classroom activities (e.g., guest speaking, skill demonstrations).\n\nEvidence that students’ work reflects collaboration with family members (e.g., shared family stories, cultural artifacts).",
