    "id": "8a2e61e3-db05-4665-b14c-55205fec0fb4",
    "title": "ANNOTATED_NGSS_DOK_Categories of Engagement_2023",
    "content": "Title: ANNOTATED_NGSS_DOK_Categories of Engagement_2023\n\nContent: Considerations for selection of a framework to evaluate the complexity of expectations, tasks,  etc : Is there an underlying theory or conceptualization of the framework? Does the framework differentiate difficulty from complexity from sophistication of thinking from cognitive load from dimensionality from learning progression from ,  etc ? How is complexity defined/understood by the framework and is that consistent with how it is defined/understood by stakeholders? What is the purpose of the framework? Is it intended for item evaluation, content analysis, lesson planning,  etc ? What level of precision is needed? How many categories are used and why? What is the rationale?  Does the framework draw on or resonate with contemporary learning theory? Do people find the framework to be useful?  How reliably can the framework be used?\n\nSAIC High School Item Cluster Prototype, 2015 DOK Category 1 Engagement\n\nSAIC High School Item Cluster Prototype, 2015 DOK Category 1 Engagement\n\nSAIC High School Item Cluster Prototype, 2015 DOK Category 1 Engagement\n\nhttps:// www.michigan.gov /documents/ mde /2018_M-STEP_Annotated_Sample_Items_test_614886_7.pdf DOK Category 1 Engagement\n\nSource:  https://snapgse.stanford.edu/snap-assessments/short-performance-assessments Item requires DOK Category 2 Engagement\n\nDOK – Category of Engagement of High School NGSS  (based on educator/content panel review, 2019) Percentage of standards\n\nThe Structure of the AIR Clusters Source: AIR, October 2018\n\nCreative Commons https://www.svgrepo.com/svg/120913/duck Creative Commons: https:// www.svgrepo.com / svg /12257/duck https:// commons.wikimedia.org /wiki/File:Rubber_Duck_Front_View_in_Fine_Day_20140107.jpg Dimensionality is different from cognitive complexity.  http:// maramattiaart.blogspot.com /2009/02/watercolor-painting-of-mallard- ducks.html\n\nSophistication of thinking  and  complexity  are related but different. Source: NGSS Appendix E\n\nIt’s Challenging Students really have to think about this one.  It’s pretty rigorous.  I don’t think it’s hard. My students struggle with this.  Source: PPT Clipart\n\nWe don’t endorse this. (We didn’t make this.)\n\nDOK 2! DOK 2! DOK 2! DOK 2! DOK 2! Source: PPT Clipart\n\nDifficulty  and  complexity  are related but different.\n\nExamples of tasks and questions that are  difficult  but not complex. What is the term for “fear of cooking”?  (Mageirocophobia) Recite the first 50 digits of Pi. https://c1.staticflickr.com/3/2304/4514253818_42fbd3fa98_b.jpg What is the scientific name for this tree?  ( Liquidambar  styraciflua   )\n\n.  Appendix A, Conceptual Shift #4:  “The NGSS focus on  deeper understanding  of content as well as  application  of content.” Appendix C:  “The shift in what the world needs and values requires that K–12 science education undergo a huge transition, from a focus on knowledge itself to a focus on  putting…knowledge to use —a transition that in and of itself necessitates a corresponding  leap in rigor .”  “ Both the Framework and the NGSS reflect current thinking about the need for  greater depth and rigor  in K–12 science…” “ …the NGSS focus on  understanding  rather than memorization…” Framework:  “… give time for students to  engage in scientific investigations and argumentation  and to  achieve depth of understanding  of the core ideas presented.” “… conceptual understanding  is linked to the ability to develop explanations of phenomena and to carry out empirical investigations in order to  develop or evaluate…knowledge claims.” Current K-12 science standards were designed to promote a systemic shift toward more complex engagement with science concepts.\n\nSource:  https://www.doe.k12.de.us/\n\nSource: CSAI Whitepaper – Cognitive Load Source:  https://www.doe.k12.de.us/ Items require DOK Category 1 Engagement\n\nSAIC High School Item Cluster Prototype, 2015\n\nSAIC High School Item Cluster Prototype, 2015",
