  {
    "id": "303039b7-7392-409a-8050-647726570480",
    "title": "Title: (Part 1/2)",
    "content": "Title:\n\nAlignment Analysis of a Framework-Based Statewide Summative Science Assessment with Corresponding Standards (such as NGSS): Results and Recommendations\n\n90-minute symposium\n\nSubmitter – Sara Christopherson, WCEPS\n\nPresenters:\n\nTimothy Butcher\n\nBrian Reiter\n\nJon Cohen\n\nSara Christopherson\n\nTopic Strands:\n\nHigh-quality assessments\n\nEquitable accountability\n\n[other options are: Effective uses of data; continuous evaluation and improvement; meaningful communication of student and school outcomes]\n\nProposal Summary (147 words; 150 words max):\n\nReleased and exemplar tasks for Framework-based science assessments suggest a wide range of interpretation regarding the expectations of the corresponding science standards. Without valid alignment criteria to ensure that all students are being supported in meeting the same high standards, the NGSS and other Framework-based standards risk failing to achieve the desired impact on advancing science education for all students (Fulmer, et al, 2018). As states begin to implement various summative NGSS and other Framework-based assessment programs, suitable alignment criteria must be established to ensure that all students are similarly expected to meet an appropriate depth and breadth of the standards, as required by ESSA. One approach to Framework-based alignment analysis was implemented in a WebbAlign third-party evaluation of AIR’s science assessment with corresponding standards (such as NGSS). The approach and overall results will be presented in this session along with evaluative reflection on the methodologies used.\n\nAbstract: (499 words; 500 words max) The abstract needs to define the issue(s) that will be presented, the relevance of the topic, what will be shared, and how the presentations relate to overall session.\n\nThe three-dimensional structure, contents, and complexity of the Next Generation Science Standards (NGSS) and other Framework-based standards pose a challenge for the development of aligned statewide summative assessments. This NGSS “alignment challenge” is highlighted in many current conversations in the field, including in the September, 2018 special issue of the Journal of Research in Science Teaching (JRST) titled A Critical Examination of the Next Generation Science Standards as well as in multiple presentations at NSCA 2018.\n\nFramework-based standards, such as NGSS, outline a necessary shift to ensure that K-12 students are provided opportunities to engage with what matters – to meet a corresponding shift in “what the world needs and values” as relates to science education (Appendix C). Without valid alignment criteria to ensure that all students are being supported in meeting the same high standards, Framework-based standards risk failing to achieve the desired impact on advancing science education for all students (Fulmer, et al, 2018). As states begin to implement various summative NGSS and other Framework-based assessment programs, suitable alignment criteria must be established to ensure that assessments measure what matters, as defined by today’s science standards. Appropriate alignment criteria are also necessary to promote accountability for equity, such that all students are similarly expected to meet an appropriate depth and breadth of the standards, as required by ESSA.\n\nOne approach to Framework-based alignment analysis was implemented in a WebbAlign third-party evaluation of AIR’s science assessment with corresponding standards (such as NGSS). The approach and overall results will be presented in this session along with evaluative reflection on the methodologies used – from the perspective of two states, the test developer, and the alignment study director. Timothy Butcher, of West Virginia Department of Education, and Paul Dumas from Hawaii Department of Education will offer state perspectives. Jon Cohen of the American Institutes for Research (AIR) will describe the alignment study and assessment design. Sara Christopherson of Wisconsin Center for Education Products and Services (WCEPS) WebbAlign program will describe the alignment study results and overall lessons learned as relates to alignment methodologies for summative assessments intended to target Framework-based science standards.",
