  {
    "id": "be746f56-f429-4c78-b2ed-4bdef036d9b3",
    "title": "WebbAlign DOK PD for Fayette County CTE: March 15, 2022 Session Evaluation Responses (Part 1/2)",
    "content": "WebbAlign DOK PD for Fayette County CTE: March 15, 2022 Session Evaluation Responses\n\nTotal responses: 49\n\nTeachers reported that they learned about item writing and test development. They felt much more comfortable and confident with the concepts after the sessions. This PD was offered online and participants thought the online format was managed very well.\n\n8. Which part of the March 15 PD session was most helpful for you? Explain why.\n\nDOK\n\nHelping to understand the program standards.\n\nAligning assessment questions to the standards\n\nUsing DOK to promote an aligned (coherent) education system\n\nReminders about multiple choice questions - measuring what you wanted to measure and unintended bias in questions\n\nExplanations and examples given in the session\n\ncollaboration with colleagues… learning how to interpret what we all do in a common format to make our job easier and to get a clearer picture of what our students know and can do\n\ncollaborating with other JROTC Programs\n\nCreating a unit assessment was very helpful. It gave me the opportunity to pick the brains of my peers and feel more confident about beginning the year with a strong sense of what I'd give my students.\n\nCollaboration\n\nSpeaking with people who teach the same thing as me is always helpful.\n\nAir Force JROTC, the Opportunity to network and discuss curriculum standards with Aviation\n\nGoing through our test and matching up to the standards\n\nassessment mapping\n\nWorking with colleagues in my pathway to form common assessments, because it was helpful to get different perspectives on the DOK levels and questions. Also, developing the blueprint helped to assure we were covering all of the standards.\n\nwork with co workers\n\nTime with Team\n\nmeeting with my peers in small group\n\nExamples of the different questions to see if we need to revisit our questions.\n\nThe Blueprint was very user friendly.\n\nItem evaluation: It allowed us as pathway teachers to examine an existing assessment and reevaluate each item.\n\nBuilding assessments\n\nHaving a form available to complete\n\nOther pathway teacher communication\n\nSharing with my peers\n\nThe clarification of what is expected in these assessments.\n\nTime to collaborate with other teachers across the district for PLC\n\nThe assessment template and explanation was superb!\n\nTalking about DOK in reference to standards and assessments helped pull the concepts together.\n\nFurther explanation of DOK, and explanation of the Elements\n\nWorking together on the standard and DOK assignment to test questions to unit test.\n\nGetting together with my colleague that teaches the same subject and collaborating\n\nWorking through the DOK\n\nUnderstanding the levels of DOK and how we can improve with assessments.\n\nI did the evaluation on the first test I wrote for my safety portion. I found several faults/or ways that questions could be worded better.\n\nJROTC, sharing knowing among instructors.\n\nMaking clear assessment questions\n\nIt is always helpful to meet with other teachers who teach the same subject as I do! We were able to break down the standards and look at the individual elements and consider how to best assess the learning of those individual elements. We continued discussing the DOK levels used that to begin developing a common assessment. Hearing the insight and input of my colleagues often clarifies things for me!\n\nHaving the opportunity to work with my counterparts\n\nThe group discussion that clarified our unique situation in CTE\n\nCollaboration with colleagues on standards and assessment questions.\n\nGroup breakout with actual questions\n\n9. What aspects of the March 15 PD session helped affirm your existing understanding of educational assessment?\n\nDOK\n\nWorking with other technology teachers\n\nIt was a good refresher\n\nThe visuals, time to discuss with my pathway colleagues.\n\ncreating a blueprint from common assessment\n\nExamples of different content\n\nI have been through multiple trainings so it was good to see consistency and helped for me to think the next type of level on the performance piece.\n\nExplanations while collaborating about DOK\n\nReevaluating my use of standards and adjusting my curriculum to fit the appropriate elements.\n\nConfirmation from colleagues\n\nDOK overview was helpful\n\nThe concept of knowing the standards, creating questions, and the important of creating the right assessments.\n\nyou need to start with the standards before developing the test.\n\ngroup discussions\n\nDOK Levels\n\nIt help confirm that we were on task from the last session.\n\nAssessment Blueprint\n\nHearing how other teachers, even in different pathways, implement DOK levels.\n\nThe slides on DOK\n\nThat we are in the right path to doing what needs to be completed\n\nStandards\n\nWhen the groups put the sticky notes on the board. It allowed me to confirm that my mindset is similar to others.\n\nAids to keep everyone on the same page regarding goals and having common assessments",
