    "id": "28c9801a-e9f4-4df2-80a1-e4d433c5a4c5",
    "title": "Applied Measurement in Education (Part 1/1)",
    "content": "Applied Measurement in Education  www.tandfonline.com/journals/hame20  ISSN: (Print) (Online) Journal homepage:  Using Content Relevance and Representativeness Indices in Instrument Revision  Anne Traynor & Sara C. Christopherson  10.1080/08957347.2024.2347518  To cite this article: Anne Traynor & Sara C. Christopherson (04 May 2024): Using Content Relevance and Representativeness Indices in Instrument Revision, Applied Measurement in Education, DOI:  https://doi.org/10.1080/08957347.2024.2347518  To link to this article:  Submit your article to this journal  View related articles  View Crossmark data  Published online: 04 May 2024.  Full Terms & Conditions of access and use can be found at (<https://www.tandfonline.com/action/journalInformation?journalCode=hame20>)https://www.tandfonline.com/action/journalInformation?journalCode=hame20  APPLIED MEASUREMENT IN EDUCATION            https://doi.org/10.1080/08957347.2024.2347518  Using Content Relevance and Representativeness Indices in Instrument Revision  a  b  Anne Traynor (<http://orcid.org/0000-0003-2662-9184>)and Sara C. Christopherson aDepartment of Educational Studies, Purdue University; bWebbAlign Program, Wisconsin Center for Education Products and Services  ABSTRACT  Combining methods from earlier content validity and more contemporary content alignment studies may allow a more complete evaluation of the meaning of test scores than if either set of methods is used on its own. This article distinguishes item relevance indices in the content validity literature from test representativeness indices in the subsequent content alignment literature. We then demonstrate that setting minimum thresholds for both types of indices in a sequential process can affect conclusions about test content representativeness, which are integral to validity evaluation. We illustrate this outcome using test item-to-target domain objective match data from subject-matter expert panelists who reviewed sixteen US state achievement tests. Although the illustration incorporates educational achievement test data, the process has broader applicability to development of survey questionnaires, workplace tests, and other instruments.  Professional testing standards explain that evidence from test content analyses delimits test score interpretations that can be considered plausible and bears on test fairness (American Educational Research Association et al., (<>)2014). For this reason, processes for establishing an instrument’s items as representative of the intended score reporting categories are critical to validation (Lissitz & Samuelsen,  (<>)2007). Validity theory has long included two categories of content-related evidence that characterize the degree of correspondence between an instrument’s item set, and a domain operationalizing the attribute that the instrument is meant to measure: relevance and representativeness evidence (Chapelle, (<>)2020; Fitzpatrick, (<>)1983; Sireci, (<>)1998). We define item content relevance as the extent to which an item’s observable responses are expected (by content experts) to allow inference about test- takers’ mastery or perceptions of a specific objective in the target domain. “Item” may refer to many types of prompts or tasks presented to test-takers. We define test content representativeness as the extent to which the items presented to a test-taker adequately sample the content elements of the entire target domain. Throughout, we use the term “content relevance and representativeness” (content R&R) to refer collectively to these two evidence types. The purposes of this article are to distinguish item content relevance from test content representativeness indices ((<>)Table 1), to outline a sequential process for their use ((<>)Table 2), and to demonstrate that setting minimum thresholds for both types of indices may affect conclusions about test content representativeness.  Content analysis methods require panels of independent subject-matter experts to match each item of an educational test, survey questionnaire, or observational checklist to elements of a prespecified target domain, or rate each item’s relevance to particular domain elements (e.g., McCoach et al., (<>)2013; Wise et al., (<>)2015). Early approaches to quantifying content relevance suggested summarizing experts’ item ratings using various index calculations (Crocker et al., (<>)1989; Hambleton, (<>)1984). Item relevance  CONTACT Anne Traynor atraynor@purdue.edu Department of Educational Studies, Purdue University, West Lafayette, IN  47907, USA  © 2024 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC  Table 1. Examples of item content relevance and test content representativeness indices.  Item Content Relevance Index  Test Content Representativeness Index  ● Mean or median expert rating on a relevance scale (e.g., McCoach et al., (<>)2013; Penfield, (<>)2003)  ● Construct comprehensiveness index (Cizek et al.,  (<>)2018)  ●  Transformations of the mean expert rating (V, Aiken, (<>)1985; content concentration index; Cizek et al., (<>)2018)  ●  Proportion of experts rating an item as “relevant” or “topical” (Herman et al., (<>)2007; content validity ratio; Lawshe, (<>)1975)  ●  Item-objective congruence index (Hambleton, (<>)1984)  ● Curriculum coverage index (Cizek et al., (<>)2018)  ●  Surveys of Enacted Curriculum overall test-to- standards alignment index (Porter, (<>)2002)  ●  Balance-of-Representation index (Webb, (<>)2007a)  ●  Range-of-Knowledge Correspondence index  (Webb, (<>)2007a)  Note. The question prompts and relevance scale value labels that raters use to judge item relevance vary across content evaluation methods, as noted in the “Phase 1” description. Two test content analysis studies may use the same item content relevance index (i.e., formula), without necessarily having posed the same question prompt to raters. Choosing the question prompt, the rating scale value labels, and the content R&R indices and thresholds are separate decisions.  Examples of question prompts include: “Is the skill (or knowledge) measured by this item: ‘Essential,’ ‘Useful but not essential,’ or ‘Not necessary’ to the performance of the job?” (Lawshe, (<>)1975, p. 567). “How comprehensively does this item measure the focal aspects of the intended content standard?. . .Does not match . . . poor match . . . moderate match . . . strong match . . . fully matches” (Cizek et al., (<>)2018, p. 500). “Rate the centrality of the item to the topic it addresses, using the following rating scale: . . . not essential . . . of moderate importance . . . of central importance” (Herman et al., (<>)2007, p. 105).  Table 2. Phases of item content relevance and test content representativeness evaluation.  Phase  Activities  1  Collect item content relevance ratings from subject-matter experts.  2  Compute item relevance indices to inform decisions about item retention.  (a)  Select an item relevance index.  (b)  Identify a minimum threshold for the index, given situational considerations. The threshold can be set for the index value (effect size) only, or can account for its statistical uncertainty.  (c)  Compute the item relevance index.  (d)  Determine whether each item meets the minimum threshold for content relevance. Mark any items that do not meet the threshold to be revised or removed from the instrument (or item pool).  (e)  Use subject-matter experts’ qualitative comments, if available, to judge whether each marked item should be revised, or removed.  3  Compute test content representativeness index(es) to inform a judgment about whether the content distribution is adequate.  (a)  Select test content representativeness index(es).  (b)  Identify a minimum threshold for each test content representativeness index, given situational considerations.  (c)  Compute the test content representativeness index(es) with and without any items that did not meet the minimum threshold for content relevance in Phase 2.  (d)  Determine the extent to which the instrument’s items adequately represent the target domain, and revise the instrument if warranted. Document the decisions made.  indices continue to be used to guide revision during test development efforts (e.g., Colquitt et al., (<>)2019; Lambie et al., (<>)2017). In recent decades, alignment indices that characterize an instrument’s representation of its intended domain have been proposed, and computed primarily for achievement testing programs (e.g., Cizek et al., (<>)2018; Martone & Sireci, (<>)2009; Porter et al., (<>)2013; Webb, (<>)1997, (<>)2007). Some contemporary content alignment analysis methods identify items that were judged not to be adequately relevant (e.g., Christopherson & Webb, (<>)2013), but these items are not necessarily revised or removed from the instrument, as recommended (Sireci & Faulkner-Bond, (<>)2014).  If test users focus only on content representativeness indices for the test as a whole, without consideration of item relevance, information about poorly-suited items that merit removal from the instrument or item pool may be lost. Conversely, earlier content validity methods lacked consistently interpretable, standardized indices to evaluate a particular test’s representation of the target domain (Crocker et al., (<>)1989). Choosing content R&R indices that effectively operationalize these two concepts, and then applying them in sequence may strengthen the set of validity evidence supporting a given test score use. After classifying existing content R&R indices ((<>)Table 1), we outline a sequential  process for their use (summarized in (<>)Table 2). The process applies liberal minimum threshold values in both the item relevance and test representativeness phases of evaluation. This work elaborates the relationship between item relevance and test representativeness, making use of existing indices.  2. Phases of Content Relevance and Representativeness Evaluation  We define the target domain for an instrument as the set of all possible test-taker actions on which test users want to characterize their performance status or response patterns. The target domain con-cretizes the attribute(s) measured by an instrument (e.g., creativity, oral reading fluency, application of science practices, patient care), and is typically more expansive than all the measurement conditions observed (Kane, (<>)2011). Choices must be made about how finely to parse the domain for test development purposes (e.g., Fulmer et al., (<>)2018; Webb, (<>)2007a). Target domain elements could include, for instance, learning objective statements in an educational curriculum, tasks identified by a job analysis, behaviors implied by a construct definition, or other clearly defined content units. We will use the term “objectives” to denote the target domain elements. Decisions about the domain elements intended for assessment are codified in test blueprints and other test framework documents, including clear descriptions of test purpose(s) and intended score use(s). These documents should inform design decisions for an R&R evaluation appropriate to a particular test (e.g., CCSSO, (<>)2002).  Broadly, test content R&R evaluation provides evidence for claims about the relationship between a test’s content and that of the target domain (Sireci & Faulkner-Bond, (<>)2014). We assume the test development process has defined a focused test purpose and clear content specifications for sampling objectives from the target domain, consistent with the purpose (American Educational Research Association et al., (<>)2014). Domain inferences are required early in the logical argument supporting any score interpretation that extends beyond observed responses to the tasks administered, which necessitates reference to content evidence (Chapelle, (<>)2020). Data from independent experts’ content judgments can corroborate documentation of content characterizations used in the test development process (e.g., a test blueprint, item specifications; Perie & Huff, (<>)2016), as well as identify discrepancies that may be addressed by instrument revision. All of these types of validity evidence are needed to support inferences about test score meaning (e.g., Beck, (<>)2020; Ebel, (<>)1956). Ultimately, the test scores can appropriately be used if the score meaning(s) that are plausible, given the content evidence and other evidence, offer pertinent information for a planned application.  In many measurement contexts, the tested item domain intentionally does not sample from the full domain indicative of test-takers’ status on the target construct, for example, when some objectives are deemed not to be measurable within the testing period. Such incomplete coverage is expected to be a source of systematic error in test score interpretations made with respect to the full target domain, even if items are well matched to the instrument specifications (e.g., Kane, (<>)2011). However, if test score users simply limit their score interpretations to the tested item domain, incongruity between the test specifications and broader target domain should not be a source of bias. An intentional decision should be made by the investigator conducting content R&R evaluation (Fulmer, Tanas, & Weiss,  (<>)2018) about whether the test content representativeness index(es) will be computed with respect to objectives: (i) in the full target domain, (ii) that will be tested, and/or (iii) in a subset of the target domain that extends beyond the tested objectives. This decision should be consistent with the test score interpretation(s) that are planned (c.f., the generalization inference; Kane, (<>)2013).  To make decisions about the adequacy of content R&R based on indices derived from experts’ item ratings, specific rules should be identified (Kane, (<>)2011). Threshold values for different content R&R indices (i.e., cutoffs for acceptability) have been proposed in the literature, as will be detailed in the “Selecting a minimum threshold” sections. It is widely recognized that any threshold values identified should depend on the type of instrument, test specifications, intended score use, and features of the evaluation procedure, including the number of raters (Popham, (<>)1992; Webb, (<>)2007a). Popham ((<>)1992) explained a general logic for determining content R&R threshold values that is widely applicable. However, test technical manuals seldom contain thorough documentation of setting minimum  threshold values for both item relevance and test content representativeness indices, or report how these indices were used to make decisions during instrument development. In the sections that follow, we detail three main procedural phases for evaluating the content R&R relationships between an instrument and its target domain ((<>)Table 2).  2.1. Phase 1: Collect Item Content Relevance Ratings from Subject-Matter Experts  Judgmental ratings of test item content relevance are the data from which content R&R indices are computed. We assume the ratings have been collected consistent with contemporary professional testing standards. Particularly, the recruited subject-matter experts should be knowledgeable about the intended content domain (Beck, (<>)2020), have sustained experience working with the test-taker population (Herman et al., (<>)2007), be independent of the test developer (Aiken, (<>)1985; Davis-Becker & Buckendahl,  (<>)2013; Dickinson et al., (<>)2020), and complete individual practice with the item coding process during training (Bhola et al., (<>)2003; Traynor & Merzdorf, (<>)2018). Numbers of reviewers recommended for test content R&R evaluation range from about four (Porter et al., (<>)2013) to ten (Sireci & Faulkner-Bond,  (<>)2014). In some cases, R&R evaluation is conducted at the same time as item bias review.  Expert reviewers’ item content relevance judgments can be elicited as ratings of content correspondence between each item and particular objectives identified a priori, as ratings of each item’s salience to all objectives in the instrument specifications (a time-consuming method, e.g., Turner & Carlson, (<>)2003), or as matches of each item to the objective(s) it is perceived to measure (D’Agostino et al., (<>)2008). Questions about item fit to the target domain overall, or specific elements of it, can be posed to reviewers in different ways, and the precise wording of the prompt (e.g., to determine “relevance,” “importance,” “essentiality,” “coverage” of the full scope of an objective, “what can be inferred” from a response) can affect the results obtained (e.g., Crocker, Miller, & Franks, (<>)1989). When a reviewer finds that an item does not correspond to any specific objective, some alignment processes allow the reviewer to code that item as related to a broader content topic category within the target domain.  Rating the relevance of an item to a particular objective on an ordinal scale requires independent experts to either match each item to one or more objectives before rating it for relevance, or rate all items on all possible objectives in the domain. Frisbie ((<>)2003) and McCoach, Gable, and Madura ((<>)2013) argue the independent experts should match items to target domain objectives, rather than relying on the test developer’s existing content classifications, while Anderson et al. ((<>)2015) and Cizek, Kosh, and Toutkoushian ((<>)2018) propose using existing item-objective matches made by the item writers to constrain item relevance rating by the independent experts. Methods intended to reduce experts’ confirmation bias include having them match items to a content classification table rather than directly to the target domain objectives (Porter, (<>)2002), or comparing all items’ pairwise similarity (D’Agostino et al., (<>)2011; Sireci & Geisinger, (<>)1992). In the simplest special case of item relevance rating, items may be matched to objectives, indicating a binary (yes/no) rating of relevance without further quantifying the extent of correspondence. As noted previously, the intended interpretation of test scores should be taken into consideration when selecting an approach for rating item relevance because key decisions (e.g., appropriate unit for content analysis, wording of rating prompts, rater training requirements) may affect the results’ utility as validity evidence.  Because test items may be intended or empirically shown to measure multiple objectives (e.g., Leighton, (<>)2017), content R&R data collection designs that allow experts to rate each item’s relevance to multiple objectives may be particularly appropriate (e.g., Porter et al., (<>)2013). Then, indices that directly reflect one item’s relevance to multiple objectives may be useful (e.g., Turner & Carlson, (<>)2003). We implement one such index in the Illustration section.  2.2. Phase 2: Compute Item Relevance Indices to Inform Decisions About Item Retention  While specific procedures used to make decisions about item relevance vary across applied studies, drawing conclusions from the item relevance data has been viewed as a necessary step before  proceeding to evaluate global content representativeness of the instrument or its subscales (e.g., Hambleton, (<>)1984). One or more item relevance indices are computed, and the index values are then either compared to a pre-set threshold value, or collated with experts’ written comments and interpreted qualitatively (e.g., Lambie et al., (<>)2017). The evaluation of item relevance may result in certain items being marked for omission, revision, or further review by subject-matter experts before their continued operational use. Even when items have previously passed editorial reviews, content experts may identify as-yet-unnoticed discrepancies between the response process most likely to be elicited by an item, and its corresponding objective.  Examples of indices that quantify individual items’ relevance to elements of a target domain are listed in (<>)Table 1. When an item rating scale has only two categories, all indices in (<>)Table 1 except the item-objective congruence index will be numerically equivalent. When an item rating scale has more than two categories, the rating scale mean or median may be a more suitable relevance index than collapsing rating categories to obtain a single proportion, which results in loss of information (e.g., Polit et al., (<>)2007).  Popham ((<>)1992) argued that setting minimum threshold values for interpretation of content R&R indices should be a regular validation practice. Analogous to interpreting score reliability coefficients, factor analysis model fit statistics, or other psychometric indices by comparing them to acceptable values identified before data collection, interpreting content R&R index values by comparing them to pre-set thresholds can help to identify necessary revisions to an instrument. Although any threshold should not be applied too rigidly, neglecting to establish objective criteria for item relevance and test representativeness indices may allow an instrument’s scores to stray from their intended meaning. Many instrument development papers and technical manuals report on parts of the process summarized in (<>)Table 2, but complete examples seem to be scarce.  Because recommendations about minimum item relevance index values vary situationally, we review them next. While some investigators argue that the effect size of an item relevance index should be the basis for item retention decisions (Dunn et al., (<>)1999), these indices contain both sampling and measurement error (Herman et al., (<>)2007). For this reason, choosing a threshold value that accounts for sampling error in the index has been suggested, particularly given the relatively small number of experts who typically examine the instrument’s items (Aiken, (<>)1985; Penfield, (<>)2003). We describe both types of methods, and briefly compare them in the illustration that follows.  2.2.1. Selecting a Minimum Threshold for Item Relevance Based on Index Effect Size Values  Any item relevance index chosen should be interpreted with reference to the rating scale category labels; for example, ratings indicating an item is “essential” or “relevant” to an objective within the domain may be interpreted as favorable judgments of alignment. It is recognized that subject-matter experts vary in strictness when applying relevance rating scales, and sometimes err in judgment about the meaning of items (DeVellis, (<>)2017). For this reason, questions about rater reliability in item content relevance rating are an ongoing source of controversy (Welch & Dunbar, (<>)2020, p. 8). Thus, item relevance thresholds rarely require consensus among expert raters. If multiple raters indicate problems with an item, however, it should be considered for revision or exclusion, particularly if they describe similar types of problems (e.g., McCoach et al., (<>)2013).  If an item retention criterion is based on the proportion of raters marking an item as “essential,” values exceeding 0.50 may indicate “some degree of content validity” (Lawshe, (<>)1975, p. 567). If the threshold is tied to the proportion of raters marking an item as “relevant,” instead, McCoach, Gable, and Madura ((<>)2013) suggest a minimum value of 0.80 is acceptable. When raters are directed to match items to a list of objectives, Herman, Webb, and Zuniga ((<>)2007, p. 111) suggested 0.65 or “a clear majority” of raters agreeing on the item topic, while Turner and Carlson ((<>)2003) suggest 75% of raters identifying the intended primary objective should be a minimum threshold. Overall, the recommendations suggest a more liberal minimum item relevance index threshold value if qualitative rater data will also be used systematically in the item selection process (e.g., McCoach, Gable, & Madura, (<>)2013),  and a more conservative (i.e., higher) minimum value if the intended test score use has long-term consequences.  2.2.2. Selecting a Minimum Threshold for Item Relevance That Accounts for Statistical Uncertainty  More rigorous thresholds have been proposed to account for variability in the expected item relevance index values if different same-size expert rater panels could be repeatedly drawn from the rater population. Aiken ((<>)1985) suggested testing whether his V relevance index, the item- specific mean of all judges’ item-objective correspondence ratings, was significantly different from a pre-set threshold value. Penfield and Giacobbi ((<>)2004) developed a score-based confidence interval for Aiken’s V index that could be compared to a threshold value (Penfield & Giacobbi,  (<>)2004). In the illustration presented later, we compare the relevance index effect size and confidence interval approaches to item retention.  To summarize, in Phase 2, an item content relevance index is computed for either all items, or all observed item-objective match combinations. Then, a minimum threshold value for the index may be judiciously applied. Phase 2 should end with explicit determinations of the content adequacy of each item by subject-matter experts (e.g., Beck, (<>)2020). Items with relevance index values that do not meet the minimum threshold should be reviewed for revision or removal (e.g., Lawshe, (<>)1975). Raters’ qualitative notes may provide narrative feedback that explains the source of any item content problems. If flagged items will be modified or replacement items will be added to the instrument, then Phase 2 should be repeated with the revised or new items. If some items do not meet the minimum threshold value, subsequent calculation of test representativeness indices should either exclude, or both include and exclude, the flagged items to aid decision-making about instrument revision (Herman et al., (<>)2007). This is particularly important if the investigators conducting the content evaluation are not the test developers who will determine the operational item set. Ultimately, tentatively excluding items from an instrument during content R&R evaluation is most useful if it occurs before operational instrument administration (Cizek et al., (<>)2018) or before the instrument will be used again.  2.2.3. Phase 3: Compute at Least One Test Content Representativeness Index to Inform a Judgment About Whether the Content Distribution Is Adequate  To interpret instrument scores, score users rely on claims that a test-taker’s item responses are a random or representative sample from the target content domain (Lissitz & Samuelsen, (<>)2007), or a defined subset of the domain (i.e., the universe of generalization; Kane, (<>)2011). Early quantitative content validity methods emphasized evaluating the relevance of individual items, but gave less attention to characterizing the sampling adequacy of the test as a whole (Fitzpatrick,  (<>)1983). The mean of all item relevance indices for an instrument often has been taken as a summary of the instrument’s overall content quality (e.g., Aiken, (<>)1985; McCoach et al., (<>)2013).  Although establishing the relevance of an instrument’s items is necessary, it is not sufficient to assure their representativeness of the target domain, as a set. It is not sufficient because even when the mean of item relevance index values is computed for each score reporting category, such mean relevance indices do not necessarily decrease when the proportions of items corresponding to particular domain elements are inconsistent with the domain definition. Certain alignment indices ((<>)Table 1) directly operationalize the concept of content representativeness: their values measure differences between content distributions of the test items and target domain objectives (or a chosen subset of the target domain objectives). These indices may be particularly suitable for Phase 3, during which content representativeness of the item set for each score reporting category is examined. Arguing for use of any specific content R&R indices is not an aim of this paper, but (<>)Table 1 classifies existing indices from content alignment methods that are described elsewhere (e.g., Council of Chief State School Officers, (<>)2002; Martone & Sireci, (<>)2009).  After the chosen content representativeness index(es) are computed for each score reporting category, they should be compared to threshold values that have been set in advance (Dickinson et al., (<>)2020) based on index values for existing instruments (Popham, (<>)1992; Webb, (<>)2007a). If certain items have been flagged as questionable in Phase 2, and test representativeness indices have been computed with and without those items, both could be interpreted with respect to the threshold value. If the minimum value is not reached, the test user should again consider revisions to the instrument.  2.2.4. Selecting a Minimum Threshold for Test Content Representativeness Index Values  A challenge for setting minimum values for test representativeness indices is that they are subject to criticism as partly arbitrary (Cizek et al., (<>)2018), unless they have a well-established empirical basis (Popham, (<>)1992). For example, 0.50 is a commonly used value for Webb’s ((<>)1997) Range-of-Knowledge Correspondence index. This value is based on a logic argument that any inference about what a student knows and is able to do as relates to a target domain should be based on a sample of the knowledge and skills from at least half (0.50) of the objectives identified for assessment within that target domain (Webb, (<>)2007a). Traynor, Li, and Zhou ((<>)2020) have evaluated this commonly used threshold, finding that it appears reasonable for existing data from state achievement tests, because it was neither above nor far below the mean of the Range-of-Knowledge Correspondence index distribution in a sample of alignment study reports. However, it is important that stakeholders consider multiple contextual factors to determine an appropriate threshold value for use in any particular study (e.g., Christopherson & Webb, (<>)2013; Webb, (<>)2007a). Appropriate threshold values for Porter’s test- to-standards alignment index depend on the numbers of test items and domain objectives, and can be established by simulation (Porter, Polikoff, Barghaus, & Yang, (<>)2013). While further study is needed, the existing results show promise that meaningful thresholds for test content representativeness indices can be established for a given test score use situation. Regardless of the particular test representativeness index and threshold applied, Phase 3 should end with an explicit determination of the extent to which the instrument’s item set adequately represents the target domain and, if warranted, an inventory of any revisions made to the instrument.  Using content R&R indices iteratively during the test development process, rather than only to produce a summary evaluation after an instrument is finalized or operational, can benefit instrument quality (Cizek et al., (<>)2018; Davis-Becker & Buckendahl, (<>)2013). Bridging methods from earlier content validity and contemporary content alignment studies may allow a more complete evaluation of the fundamental criteria of item content relevance and test content representativeness than if either set of methods is used alone. The content R&R evaluation process illustrated here is generalizable across a range of data collection methods for item relevance ratings, and can be implemented with different choices of item- and test-level indices. As we demonstrate in the illustration that follows, setting a minimum threshold value for an item relevance index and using it to remove unacceptable items before computing test representativeness indices can affect those values, and decisions about whether the instrument content adequately represents the target domain.  3. Illustration  3.1. Phase 1  To illustrate, we utilize Phase 1 item relevance data from 16 alignment study reports for Grade 3–12 achievement tests: five in English language arts, eight in mathematics, and three in science. Tests were from 12 US states, dating from 2003 to 2013. State achievement test items are designed to measure mastery of specific objectives from state academic standards documents that are included in the test specifications. The standards documents were unique to each state. States made different choices about the specificity of their objectives, and whether to prioritize a few fundamental objectives or list all objectives of any importance at a given grade level. The number of objectives in each standards  document that were identified as eligible to be assessed ranged between 11 and 98 (M = 34). In general, the English language arts standards documents tended to contain fewer objectives (M = 22.4) than other documents did. Objectives were grouped under broader content topics; the average number of objectives per topic across the standards documents ranged widely from 2.4 to 13.5, with little evident pattern by subject area. Data from two other Grade 3–12 achievement tests, an alternate assessment for students with the most significant cognitive disabilities, and an English language proficiency (ELP) assessment, were excluded from the analysis. These data were excluded because alternate and ELP test structures may differ from those of other achievement tests in ways that affect the methods for collecting item relevance ratings (e.g., Flowers, Wakeman, Browder, & Karvonen, (<>)2009). The data sources, listed in Appendix A1, are publicly available. We have also made the complete dataset available in the openICPSR data repository.  The item relevance data were judgmental item-objective correspondence data recorded by subject- matter experts using Webb ((<>)1997, (<>)2005) content alignment methodology. Experts worked independently in subject-matter panels led by seven different facilitators. In total, 106 expert raters, the majority of whom were experienced grade-level classroom teachers, evaluated the relationship between 883 items’ content and the content of the corresponding learning objectives identified for assessment. If an item was found not to address a specific objective but to relate to a broader content topic within the standards, panelists were instructed to code the item to that broad category. Between two and twelve experts judged each item.  The average pairwise agreement proportion for item-objective matches between all possible pairs of raters for every item ranged from 0.29 to 0.92, which we would characterize as “weak” to “strong,” with a median value of 0.56. We interpret the range of interrater agreement statistics as demonstrating realistic, varying degrees of test content quality, and of uncertainty among raters in identifying precisely the objective measured by each test item. To examine the robustness of our results, we will also present results for the subset of alignment studies with the highest agreement about item-objective matches.  3.2. Phase 2  We first computed Aiken’s ((<>)1985) V item relevance index – here, the proportion of experts matching the given item to a particular objective – and used either the index value or its confidence interval (Penfield & Giacobbi, (<>)2004) to identify items that did not meet a minimum threshold for item retention. The data collection tool allowed raters to identify up to three objectives that they judged to be measured by each item (Webb, Alt, Ely, Cormier, & Vesperman, (<>)2005). Thus, an item relevance index that could accommodate matching to multiple target domain elements was required. Because most items written for state achievement tests during this timeframe were intended to measure a single or primary objective, we regarded matches to secondary objectives that were not indicated by the majority of experts as minimally informative about the content measured by the item (Beck, (<>)2020; Polit, Beck, & Owen, (<>)2007) and possibly reflecting rater error (McCoach, Gable, & Madura, (<>)2013). We computed V for all observed item-objective match combinations, and interpreted its maximum value, Vmax, as an item relevance index. This index intentionally excludes item-objective matches that are less common among raters. Each item’s Vmax indicates the extent to which the raters could identify a primary objective(s) measured by that item.  For purposes of illustration, we applied a liberal threshold value of 0.50 (Lawshe, (<>)1975; see also Popham, (<>)1992) to flag items that potentially should be revised or removed from an instrument, based on their maximum V value. The 0.50 threshold represents at least half of experts agreeing about one primary objective measured by an item (regardless of whether it was the objective intended by the item writer), except in special cases when only two raters have participated. We interpreted items with lower Vmax values as having no clear relevance to any specific objective within the target domain for the given state’s test. To compare a more conservative threshold, we also marked items for which the  Figure 1. Bean plots of maximum Aiken’s V Item Content Relevance Index (a), and lower bound of 80% confidence interval for maximum Aiken’s V index (b) (n = 883 test items). Note. Points mark values of Vmax index, or its confidence interval lower bound, on the vertical axis. Gray density plot summarizes distribution of Vmax or its lower bound values. Horizontal lines denote median Vmax and lower bound values of 0.84 and 0.55, respectively. (Horizontal spacing of points is for visibility, only.) Nineteen items that many raters coded to a broad content category, rather than a specific objective in the standards document, so that their Vmax did not pertain to a specific objective are not shown in this plot, but were included in the Phase 2 analysis.  lower bound of the 80% confidence interval for Vmax (Penfield & Giacobbi, (<>)2004) was less than 0.50 as having questionable relevance to the target domain.  Figure 1  shows density plots of values for the Vmax item content relevance index, and lower bound of its 80% confidence interval, for the 883 test items. Results for Phase 2 showed the Vmax values for 2.2% of the items pertained to broad content topics rather than specific objectives within the standards, indicating raters tended not to view them as relevant to specific objectives. Sixteen of these “generic” items came from three tests. These items were excluded from (<>)Figure 1 and the Vmax index summary statistics. Median values for the Vmax index and its confidence interval lower bound were 0.84 and 0.55, respectively. Overall, 8.4% of the items had Vmax proportion values less than 0.50, indicating low rater agreement about the primary learning objective represented by each of those items. Across the 16 test forms, between 0 and 32.3% of the items, with a median of 4.6%, were flagged by our liberal minimum threshold for Vmax. Four test forms, all in mathematics, had no items with uncertain content relevance identified.  Applying the more stringent threshold requiring the lower bound of an 80% confidence interval for V to exceed 0.50, we found 35.8% of items, a considerable percentage, were marked as having questionable content relevance. Items flagged ranged between 6.8 and 78.5% (median 30.7%) across test forms. The median width of the confidence interval for V was 0.37, which is sizable given the index’s range between 0.0 and 1.0. Confidence interval width was negatively correlated with the number of experts who examined the item (−0.36), as expected given its construction (Penfield & Giacobbi, (<>)2004). The magnitude of the correlation was moderate, however, implying that enlisting additional expert judges will only sometimes yield greater certainty about an item’s content relevance. For the 83 state achievement test subtests included in this analysis, setting an item retention threshold based on the 80% confidence interval for Vmax in Phase 2 would have resulted in rejection of 316 out of 883 items, which may not be reasonable for practice. Even when we restricted the sample to alignment studies for which pairwise agreement about item-objective  matches exceeded the median value (0.56); that is, studies with relatively high interrater agreement, using the lower bound of the confidence interval for Vmax as an item retention threshold still flagged 18.6% of all items as having questionable content relevance. The more liberal item retention threshold of Vmax greater than 0.50 appeared useful for identifying items with clearly inadequate content relevance, however.  3.3. Phase 3  After verifying item relevance, a subtest-level alignment index characterizing content representativeness, Webb’s ((<>)2007a) Range-of-Knowledge Correspondence index, ROK, was computed for the item sets matched to every broad content topic (i.e., conceptual category or score reporting category) included in the test specifications. The 83 subtests included 18 in English language arts, 47 in mathematics, and 18 in science. We report distributions of ROK values for the subtests under three conditions:  (1)  assuming no item removal using findings from Phase 2, and  (2)  excluding items based on the effect size of Vmax or (3) its confidence interval.  ROK is the mean proportion of objectives per content topic that have at least one corresponding item, with the mean taken across expert raters. It is expressed:  where p is the proportion of objectives for a given content standard to which at least one item has been matched by a particular rater, and J is the total number of raters. The index values range between 0.0 and 1.0, inclusive. This index of test representativeness does not capture differences among raters in  Figure 2. Observed distribution of test content representativeness (range-of-knowledge) index values under three minimum thresholds for item relevance (n = 83 subtests).  the specific items matched to a given topic, only in the number of items matched. By definition, ROK excludes items that were not matched to a specific objective by any rater. Although ROK has previously been computed only for achievement tests (e.g., Russell & Moncaleano, (<>)2020), it could be applied to other types of instruments if objectives were understood as elements of a target domain. The purpose of this illustration is not to suggest the ROK index is always preferable or should solely be used, but to demonstrate how values of a test representativeness index may change depending on earlier decisions about item relevance.  (<>)Figure 2 illustrates the distribution of Range-of-Knowledge index values in this sample of 83 subtests under three item retention conditions: no item removal, item removal if Vmax was less than 0.50, or further item removal if the lower bound of the 80% CI for Vmax was less than 0.50. With no item removal, the mean of the subtests’ ROK index distribution could be characterized as moderate (M = 0.67, SD = 0.25), given that index’s range. Twenty-one of the subtests (25.3%) did not meet the threshold value used for the ROK index (0.50; Webb, (<>)2007a), even when all items were retained. These subtests spanned the three subject areas: 8 from English language arts, 10 from mathematics, and 3 from science. With item removal using the Vmax value, the subtests’ ROK indices had a lower mean (M = 0.64, SD = 0.27). Five additional subtests’ ROK indices did not meet the threshold, which might affect decisions about whether those subtests adequately represented their target domains. Excluding items with less evident content relevance also left two subtests with no remaining items, so ROK could not be computed. When additional items were excluded from the ROK index because the CI for their Vmax value fell below 0.50, some of the ROK index values decreased, reducing their mean to 0.57 (SD = 0.30). Nine of the subtests had no remaining items.  To examine the empirical relationship between the ROK test content representativeness index and subtest mean Vmax item relevance index values, as suggested by a reviewer, we computed their correlation. The correlation between the two indices was 0.61 when all items were retained, and 0.67 when a minimum threshold for the Vmax item relevance index was used to remove items from the instrument, demonstrating that there is a positive (linear) relationship between these aspects of test content quality, but the indices carry different information.  Overall, decisions about test content representativeness in Phase 3 were sometimes sensitive to removal of a few items from a subtest, which occurred when a minimum threshold for the Vmax item relevance index was applied in Phase 2. Using a confidence interval for the mean of experts’ item relevance ratings to make instrument revision decisions appeared to be somewhat impractical, because it flagged a median of 31% of the 16 subtests’ items as having uncertain content relevance. However, if the number of expert raters could be increased without compromising the expertise of the panel (e.g., to 10; Sireci & Faulkner-Bond, (<>)2014), this item content screening method might potentially be useful, particularly for high-stakes tests.  4. Discussion  Test content representativeness evidence bears on the validity of instrument score interpretations and uses, and on fairness (e.g., Welch & Dunbar, (<>)2020). Evaluating item relevance first checks whether each item is likely to elicit responses related to one of the target domain objectives, before judging test content representativeness, and finalizing the instrument. Our illustration results show that item content relevance and test content representativeness index values have a moderate positive correlation, but are conceptually and empirically distinct, so they should not be treated as interchangeable types of validity evidence. Although many instrument technical manuals and development papers report content-related evidence, applying a sequential process for use of minimum thresholds for both item relevance and test representativeness indices can support decision making about instrument revision during a development cycle. In some instances, the target domain definition or test specifications may need to be adjusted, rather than the items themselves (Sireci & Faulkner-Bond, (<>)2014). Ultimately, a thorough evaluation of test content R&R indices is intended to reduce construct  irrelevant variance in test scores by marking for revision any items that appear to require test-taker actions from outside the target domain.  Our illustration results suggest the timing of content evaluation within the test development cycle may be consequential for the quality of the instrument produced. Content R&R evidence is likely to be most useful when time is available to reexamine the items before they are used on an operational test. Timing considerations are particularly important when a test content evaluation by independent subject-matter experts is required for regulatory purposes. Content R&R indices from a near-final version of a test, supplemented by information about how the test developer addressed any problems with item relevance or test representation arguably provides stronger validity evidence than content R&R indices from an operational test that has many items flagged as lacking relevance. The illustration shows that incorporating the phased approach described here during content alignment evaluation and before a test is final may not only identify items that should be revised, but also yield test representativeness indices that are more accurate, because they have not been distorted by including items with low relevance to the target domain. When multiple rounds of content evaluation by SMEs are feasible, calculating content R&R indices for the final version of the test is advised. Before concluding, we address considerations for calculating test content R&R indices in more complex test design situations.  4.1. Evaluating Correspondence of Test Items to the Target Domain and Instrument Specifications  Determination of test content R&R requires intentional decisions about an appropriate referent against which to compare items and the test (Fulmer et al., (<>)2018). When both broader and narrower interpretations of scores from the same instrument are likely, when existing tests are adopted for new purposes (e.g., Camara et al., (<>)2019), or when test content is being evaluated for an assessment system of multiple instruments, separate processes may be needed to check correspondence among the item set, instrument specifications, and target domain (AERA et al., 2014, p. 196; Forte, (<>)2017). Forte proposes that a few independent subject-matter experts conduct and report on a qualitative pre- review to characterize the degree of correspondence between the test specifications and full target domain, and evaluate the rationale for excluding some domain elements from the instrument. Evaluating items’ correspondence to the test specifications may improve the feasibility of using standard minimum thresholds for test content representativeness indices (Dickinson et al., (<>)2020) in Phase 3 of the process we outline. Matching items to the test specifications may also resolve questions about assigning equal weight to the domain elements during test content R&R evaluation (e.g., Lawshe, (<>)1975; Polikoff et al., (<>)2020), because the specifications may have an embedded weighting of the target domain elements: certain objectives are designated to be assessed by more items per measurement occasion, or more frequently across measurement occasions (e.g., Forte, (<>)2017). Further, because the weighting of target domain elements in the test specifications should be related to the intended meaning of test scores, computing content R&R indices with respect to the test specifications can strengthen the logic supporting domain inferences (Chapelle, (<>)2020). Ultimately, content R&R evaluation will produce the most useful validity evidence if its referent, the test specifications or a broader target domain, is chosen to relate as closely as possible to the score interpretations that are planned.  4.2. Sampling Approaches to Content Relevance and Representativeness Evaluation  All the judgmental item-objective match data used in the illustration came from one or more intact forms of 16 fixed-form achievement tests. For more complex test designs such as adaptive tests (Porter et al., (<>)2013), “amalgamated” tests (Forte, (<>)2017, p. 19), through-course assessment (Way et al., (<>)2011), and test form sets that fully sample a target domain over time (e.g., Cizek et al., (<>)2018), content R&R  evaluation approaches that systematically sample items for review from an item bank are often required (Crotts et al., (<>)2012). Kannan et al., ((<>)2015) review matrix sampling procedures for items in the context of test performance standard (i.e., cut score) setting, which may also be relevant to content R&R evaluation design. The test content R&R review process can be applied to sets of items drawn from an item bank (e.g., simulated adaptive test forms for test-takers at different performance levels), provided that the item set size is limited to prevent serious fatigue among the raters. As automated methods of content analysis continue to be developed, comparing instrument content R&R indices from independent expert review, content classifications made by the item writers, and text mining procedures (Anderson et al., (<>)2020) may appraise whether they provide equally useful information about instruments’ content quality.  Acknowledgments  We appreciate the thoughtful responses of eight state education agency assessment leaders who replied to data requests by the first author. We gratefully acknowledge productive criticisms and insights from the editor and three anonymous reviewers of this work. Norman Webb also raised several questions after reading an earlier version that helped 