    "id": "7f3953a8-f456-4c0f-b206-0de63bdec714",
    "title": "NSCA 2023 Content Alignment - Interim Assessment_ANNOTATED SLIDES (Part 3/4)",
    "content": "Content  alignme nt  can help ensure that pedagogical decisions do not negatively affect students’ opportunity to learn. Specific Inferences about Student Learning Specific Instructional Guidance “[T]he teacher may choose different levels of the assessment for different students in the class. For example, … the teacher can choose to administer a Levels 1–2 assessment for some students and a Levels 3–4 assessment for other students.”  (Kannan, et al., 2008) Expectations In response to low interim scores, instructional modifications tend to be remedial.  (Goertz, et al., 2009)\n\nContent Alignment for Interim Assessments:  Key Takeaways For purposes of peer review, focus of a validity argument is on the use of scores for accountability. Peer Review critical elements do not specify evidence for claims related to instructional utility. More broadly, professional standards expect all assessment claims to be supported by evidence.  Compared with summative assessment, interim assessment claims necessitate a more systemic perspective on alignment.  Claims related to  instruc ti onal  utility require evidence of alignment of multiple components of standards, curriculum, and assessment.\n\nReferences and Bibliography  Causa, O. and  Chapuis , C. (2010) Equity in Student Achievement Across OECD Countries: An Investigation of the Role of Policies. OECD Journal: Economic Studies Vol. 2010/1 Crane, E. (2010) Building an Interim Assessment System: A Workbook for School Districts. Council of Chief State School Officers. Available May 2023 at  https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED528642.pdf Dadey , N. and Gong, B. (2017) Using Interim Assessments in Place of Summative Assessments? Consideration of an ESSA Option. Council of Chief State School Officers. Available May 2023 at  https://ccsso.org/sites/default/files/2017-12/ASR_ESSA_Interim_Considerations-April.pdf Dadey , N., Evans, C.M., and  Lorié , W (2023). Through-Year Assessment: Ten Key Considerations. Center for Assessment. Available May 2023  at https ://www.nciea.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/Ten-Key-Considerations-Through-Year-Assessment-Report-March2023-F.pdf\n\nGoertz, M.E.,  Oláh , L.N., and  Riggan , M. (2010) From Testing to Teaching: The Use of Interim Assessments in Classroom Instruction. Consortium for Policy Research in Education (CPRE) CPRE Research Report #RR-65 Herman, Joan (2017) Interim Assessments in Brief. National Center for Research on Evaluation, Standards, and Student Testing (CRESST). The Center on Standards &amp; Assessment Implementation.  WestEd . Available May 2023 at  https://compcenternetwork.org/sites/default/files/archive/InterimAssessmentsBrief.pdf Kane, M.T. (2013) Validating the Interpretations and Uses of Test Scores. Journal of Educational Measurement 50(1), 1-73 Kannan, P. Bryant, A.D., Shao, S., and Wylie, E.C. (2021) Identifying Teachers’ Needs for Results From Interim Unit Assessments. ETS Research Report 21-08 Marchitello , M. and Wilhelm, M. (2014) The Cognitive Science Behind the Common Core. Center for American Progress.\n\nPerie , M;, Marion, S., Gong, B., and  Wurtzel , J., (2007)The Role of Interim Assessments in a Comprehensive Assessment System. The Aspen Institute. Available May 2023 at  https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED551318.pdf Webb, N. L. (1997). Criteria for alignment of expectations and assessments in mathematics and mathematics education. Council of Chief State School Officers and National Institute for Mathematics Education Research Monograph No. 6. Madison: University of Wisconsin, Wisconsin Center for Education Research. OECD (2012), Equity and Quality in Education: Supporting Disadvantaged Students and Schools, OECD Publishing. http:// dx.doi.org /10.1787/9789264130852-en WIDA (2018) Choosing an Interim Assessments: Guidelines for Stakeholders. Available May 2023 at  https://wida.wisc.edu/sites/default/files/resource/Choosing-Interim-Assessment-Guidelines-Stakeholders.pdf\n\nThe Elementary and Secondary Education Act (ESEA) Assessment Requirements A key purpose of Title I of the ESEA is to promote educational excellence and equity so that all students master the knowledge and skills, by the time they graduate high school, that they need in order to be successful in college and the workforce.   A State accomplishes this, in part, by  adopting challenging academic content standards that define what the State expects all students to know and be able to do, developing and administering assessments aligned to those standards and adopting academic achievement standards aligned to the academic content standards t o define levels of student achievement on the assessments.     ESEA allows for the use of  “multiple statewide interim assessments”  for purposes of accountability  provided these assessments meet appropriate technical criteria , including that they yield “valid, reliable, and transparent information on student achievement or growth” (ESEA, §1111(b)(2)(B)(viii)).",
