    "id": "40db6b75-feeb-4b96-abd8-792ea304fe26",
    "title": "NSCA 2023 Content Alignment - Interim Assessment_ANNOTATED SLIDES (Part 4/4)",
    "content": "ESEA Assessment Requirements: What Are “Appropriate Technical Criteria”? States are expected to build a validity argument for test-related claims.  Evidence of content alignment between the assessment and the assessment targets (content standards, achievement standards) is central to a validity argument.\n\nHow do interim tests differ from summative assessments? Source:  (Perie, M;, Marion, S., Gong, B., and Wurtzel, J., 2007)\n\nESEA Assessment Requirements: What Are “Appropriate Technical Criteria”? States are expected to build a validity argument for test-related claims.  Evidence of content alignment between the assessment and the assessment targets (content standards, achievement standards)  is central to a validity argument. Compared with summative assessments, some additional considerations are necessary when evaluating content alignment of interim assessments.  Importantly, the Department of Education’s Assessment Peer Review Process is agnostic regarding the type/design of assessment; the Peer Review Criteria are focused on the validity and technical quality appropriate for the use of assessment results for purposes of accountability.\n\nHow does  test purpose  differ for interim vs summative assessments? Source:  (Perie, M;, Marion, S., Gong, B., and Wurtzel, J., 2007) evaluative; accountability predictive instructional Test Purpose",
