    "id": "a6258104-d5ce-4f88-ac1e-3dd67c48a3a4",
    "title": "NSCA 2023 Content Alignment - Interim Assessment_ANNOTATED SLIDES (Part 1/4)",
    "content": "How Do Alignment Considerations Differ for Interim  vs  Summative Assessments?  (And Why Should We Care?)\n\nPlanning considerations for alignment analysis: Define purpose of alignment analysis (e.g. peer review, to inform ongoing improvements,  etc ) Identify test purpose(s), including the  specific  intended claims and inferences Determine appropriate: scope of alignment analysis units of analysis evaluative processes and tools Establish appropriate evaluative criteria and cutoffs Select appropriate categories for reporting findings\n\nClaim:  tests  measure student achievement  of state standards  Claim:  tests  predict student performance  on [end of year summative test] Claim:  tests provide information that can be  directly used to inform instruction\n\nExample test purpose / claims - accountability: “annual measure of what students know and can do using the Common Core State Standards” “gauge how well students are mastering state standards” “show how learning compares to expectations” “academic  proficiency relative to state academic standards” Source: WCEPS/ WebbAlign , 2023\n\nExample test purpose / claims - instructional utility: “allow teachers to target and adjust instruction” “used to improve teaching and learning” “helps educators target instruction to students’ individual needs” “educators can use the results to inform instruction” “provide…immediate in-depth action data” “meaningful insights on student strengths and areas of need” Source: WCEPS/ WebbAlign , 2023\n\nIdentify appropriate scope, units of analysis, evaluative processes, etc In the context of accountability, emphasis is on documents related to expectations and assessment.  For claims of instructional utility, need to extend consideration of relationships to include all components of the system. Source: WCEPS/ WebbAlign , 2023\n\n“Creating assessments that produce fine-grained accounts of how students are learning the enacted curriculum— information teachers can use directly to inform instructional strategies —requires more than a revision of current state assessments. It requires designing entirely new assessments and  fundamentally shifting the way curriculum, instruction, and state assessments interact .”  (   (NCIEA, 2023)\n\nOverall, alignment expectations for purposes of peer review are similar across test type (interim + summative), but are likely to differ in what adequate alignment “looks like.” Alignment relationships between standards and assessment(s) depend on test design: e.g. comprehensive interim vs modular: Score aggregation  Score interpretation and reporting (e.g. how to interpret growth) Relationship of statewide curriculum with assessment components Grain size  Sufficient domain sampling (as relates to intended inferences) / test length\n\nSummative vs. Interim alignment of standards and assessment in the context of peer review -  EXAMPLE DIFFERENCE Assessment addresses  appropriate breadth of standards Test design E.g. summative or comprehensive interim E.g. interim modular\n\nContent Alignment and Test Purpose:  Considerations related to  predictive goals . EXAMPLE CLAIM:  Student scores on the interim test can predict performance on the state summative test. EXAMPLE ALIGNMENT CONSIDERATIONS:  To what extent do the test frameworks, format, blueprints, etc. concur?  To what extent do the structure, content, and cognitive demands of the interim test concur with  the structure, content, and cognitive demands  of the summative test?  OTHER EVIDENCE:  What is the relationship between test scores and a future outcome? (Requires data collection across time.)\n\nContent Alignment and Test Purpose:  EXAMPLE  considerations related to  predictive goals . What is the relationship between test blueprints? What is the relationship between assessments?\n\nContent Alignment and Interim Test Purpose: Compared with summative, key difference in claims relates to  instructional utility .  Credit: Tony Webster ( Creative Commons , 2012) “A key use of an interim assessment is their ability to  inform curriculum decisions and classroom practice ” “...should provide r esults that enable educators to adapt instruction and curriculum  to better meet student needs.”  “intended to provide educators the ability to…use results to  determine next steps for instruction. “...to provide educators with an even more  detailed understanding of student learning.”\n\n“ [ M]ost districts appear to want to use assessments for [instructional purposes], most vendors say their assessments can meet that purpose, and  we have more concerns about claims for instructional purposes then for evaluative and predictive purposes .”\n\n“Alignment refers to how well all policy elements in a system work together to guide instruction and, ultimately, student learning.”  (Webb, 1997)",
