  {
    "id": "aa40985e-a9ae-4edf-91e5-b4c01055ce96",
    "title": "NSCA 2023 Content Alignment - Interim Assessment_ANNOTATED SLIDES (Part 2/4)",
    "content": "Systemic Alignment (Webb, 1997) Content Focus Categorical Concurrence Depth of Knowledge (DOK) Consistency Range of Knowledge Structure of Knowledge Comparability Balance of Representation Dispositional Consonance Articulation across grades and ages Cognitive soundness determined by best research and understanding Cumulative growth in content knowledge during students’ schooling Equity and Fairness Pedagogical Implications Engagement of students and effective classroom practices Use of technology, materials, and tools System Applicability\n\nSystemic Alignment (Webb, 1997) Content Focus Categorical Concurrence Depth of Knowledge (DOK) Consistency Range of Knowledge Structure of Knowledge Comparability Balance of Representation Dispositional Consonance Articulation across grades and ages Cognitive soundness determined by best research and understanding Cumulative growth in content knowledge during students’ schooling Equity and Fairness Pedagogical Implications Engagement of students and effective classroom practices Use of technology, materials, and tools System Applicability\n\n“Alignment refers to how well all policy elements in a system work together to guide instruction and, ultimately, student learning.”  (Webb, 1997)\n\nContent Alignment and Interim Test Purpose:  Considerations related to  instructional utility . Content alignment evidence delimits test score interpretations and uses that can be considered plausible, and bears on test fairness.  What types of evidence would you expect to see to support a claim of instructional utility?\n\nInterim assessment alignment:  Overall, similar considerations as summative but difference in what acceptable alignment looks like. Derivative assessment targets Curriculum documents  Explicit inferences about student learning Score reports Resulting score-based decision rules  Instructional guidance Professional development Source: WCEPS/ WebbAlign , 2023\n\nMany System Components Underlie the Use of  Interim Assessments to Improve Student Outcomes UNDERLYING LEARNING MODEL Interim Assessments Improved Outcomes Teaching Strategies &amp; Instructional Practices Instructional Scope &amp; Sequence Curricular Materials Score Reports &amp; Decision Rules Source: WCEPS/ WebbAlign , 2023\n\nMany System Components Underlie the Use of  Interim Assessments to Improve Student Outcomes UNDERLYING LEARNING MODEL Interim Assessments “ helps educators know what students are ready to learn next ” ? Improved Outcomes ? Teaching Strategies &amp; Instructional Practices Instructional Scope &amp; Sequence Curricular Materials Score Reports &amp; Decision Rules OPPORTUNITY TO LEARN “data driven” guidance from score reports Source: WCEPS/ WebbAlign , 2023\n\nHow Do Alignment Considerations Differ for Interim  vs  Summative Assessments?   1.  Through the focused lens of Peer Review 2.  Through a broader lens of professional practice\n\nIs there coherence between/among expectations, assessments, inferences about student learning, instructional guidance based on student scores? How are test results used to make pedagogical decisions that are consequential to students’ opportunity to learn? Expectations Specific Inferences about Student Learning Interim Assessment Tasks Specific Instructional Guidance / Linked Resources Derivative targets Scope &amp; Sequence, Curriculum &amp; Instruction Score reports &amp; Decision rules UNDERLYING LEARNING MODEL\n\nHow are test results used to make pedagogical decisions that are consequential to students’ opportunity to learn? Expectations Specific Inferences about Student Learning Interim Assessment Tasks Specific Instructional Guidance / Linked Resources Derivative targets Scope &amp; Sequence, Curriculum &amp; Instruction Score reports &amp; Decision rules UNDERLYING LEARNING MODEL CASE STUDY (Goertz, et al. 2009): Content  alignme nt  analysis found broad disconnect between standards, items, inferences, instructional guidance.\n\nAre the instructional practices and materials specified by or implied by assessments consistent with those specified by or implied by expectations? How are test results used to make pedagogical decisions that are consequential to students’ opportunity to learn? Does instructional guidance concur with best research and understanding about how students learn within the specific domain, as reflected in the academic standards? Does instructional guidance for all students, including low scoring students, maintain the scope and complexity of the academic content standards for all students?\n\nContent alignment helps to ensure that interim assessments can be used to hold all students to the same high expectations.",
