  {
    "id": "3848cab0-39bc-479f-bfd6-14b570ce1115",
    "title": "NSCA 2023 Content Alignment - Interim Assessment_ANNOTATED SLIDES",
    "content": "How Do Alignment Considerations Differ for Interim  vs  Summative Assessments?  (And Why Should We Care?)\n\nPlanning considerations for alignment analysis: Define purpose of alignment analysis (e.g. peer review, to inform ongoing improvements,  etc ) Identify test purpose(s), including the  specific  intended claims and inferences Determine appropriate: scope of alignment analysis units of analysis evaluative processes and tools Establish appropriate evaluative criteria and cutoffs Select appropriate categories for reporting findings\n\nClaim:  tests  measure student achievement  of state standards  Claim:  tests  predict student performance  on [end of year summative test] Claim:  tests provide information that can be  directly used to inform instruction\n\nExample test purpose / claims - accountability: “annual measure of what students know and can do using the Common Core State Standards” “gauge how well students are mastering state standards” “show how learning compares to expectations”...",
