    "id": "48ae663c-d662-46e7-b867-185838d7e146",
    "title": "Debrief: Eden Prairie WebbAlign DOK PD December 8, 2022 (Part 1/2)",
    "content": "Debrief: Eden Prairie WebbAlign DOK PD December 8, 2022\n\nOn December 8, 2022, instructional coaches from Eden Prairie Schools participated in a 6-hr professional learning session facilitated by Sara Christopherson, Director of WebbAlign. The WebbAlign program is the home and steward of the DOK language system, originally developed by Dr. Norman Webb. The purpose of the session was to present DOK from the perspective of its development and intended use, and to work toward a common understanding of DOK in the context of an aligned educational system. In that context, DOK was presented as a tool that fosters intentionality in pedagogical decisions and in curriculum and assessment development and evaluation decisions – for example to provide learning and assessment opportunities with the intended task complexities, while differentiating complexity from other factors such as difficulty.\n\nEden Prairie educators have a range of background experience with DOK and have been using DOK in the context of a district-developed Authenticity Grid. In advance of the session, participants completed a baseline poll. After session completion, participants again completed a poll with similar questions. A total of 21 people completed the pre-session poll and 18 people completed the post-session poll. Because of the different total number of respondents, small shifts in response distribution (such as in the first question shown below, about the use of DOK in the interpretation of academic standards and other learning expectations) may be due to the difference in total responses and not reflective of an overall shift in participant perspective. Even with the different total number of respondents, results show that several individuals did report a shift toward greater understanding of the role of DOK in the design and evaluation of lessons and assessments. The greatest overall positive shift in understanding was in the use of DOK to promote alignment. This is a critical shift in understanding as it provides an overall context for the DOK tool itself. The vast majority of participants reported that their overall understanding of DOK was enhanced by their work in the December 8 session, with over 80% (13 people) ranking their enhanced understanding as a four or five on a scale of 0-5. Participants reported specific changes in their perspectives, including understanding DOK as an evaluative tool, reconsidering the idea of a linear progression of complexity as relates to learning, differentiating difficulty from complexity, and more. During the December 8 session, participants engaged actively, thoughtfully, and candidly. Within the leadership team, there was a very evident positive and supportive learning environment and culture, in which educators clearly felt comfortable expressing differences in opinion, sharing uncertainties, and pursuing questions and unknowns.\n\nPoll results for both the pre-session and post-session polls were shared with Lisa Birno and Robb Virgin via Google Forms. Participants left a range of narrative feedback. In general, respondents’ comments suggest that the time spent on the topics was productive, and generated rich thinking, discussion, and questioning.\n\nParticipants expressed many reasonable and common requests for additional support with DOK, including additional time for calibration, discussion, practice, and more examples. Several more detailed topics also came up. One important question raised by a participant during the session related to how ideas about DOK might be effectively brought to scale within the district. Others similarly let comments in the post-session poll related to interest in support for how to put DOK into practice with teachers. This general topic of implementation resonates with goals identified in conversations with Lisa and Robb about how to incorporate DOK into existing systems and processes within the district – e.g. curriculum review cycles, lesson planning / PLC work, teacher evaluation conversations (that may include consideration of planned vs enacted lessons), assessment planning and evaluation, etc. The strong leadership structure within Eden Prairie schools may allow for a productive collaborative approach to meet these goals.",
