    "id": "f3f49c78-34ee-4606-bf51-5760b7e3ffab",
    "title": "Assessment Planning",
    "content": "Title: Assessment Planning\n\nContent: Assessment Planning\n\nDefine purpose Specify assessment targets Write quality items Review assessment Use results and input to inform improvements TEST DESIGN is an iterative process\n\n“ Assessment  is the process of gathering evidence of student learning to inform education-related decisions. “ Source: National Task Force on Assessment Education for Teachers  ies.ed.gov\n\nDeveloping or selecting an assessment requires… starting with a clear purpose identifying clear and specific learning targets to be assessed establishing development processes that ensure high quality assessment items and tasks establishing development processes that ensure content alignment between the learning targets and the test communicating results in meaningful ways maintaining focus on students’ goals Source: National Task Force on Assessment Education for Teachers  ies.ed.gov\n\nAlignment: A powerful tool for focusing instruction, curricula, and assessment “ Alignment  is the degree to which expectations[, curricula,] and assessments are in agreement and serve in conjunction with one another to guide the system toward students learning what is expected.” (Webb, 1997)\n\nContent Alignment Standards Items / Tasks\n\nDegree of Alignment Standards Items / Tasks Standards Items / Tasks Standards Items / Tasks Standards Items / Tasks\n\nContent alignment is critical b ecause we use evidence from assessments to make inferences about students. “THE FEATURE THAT IS CLAIMED TO BE MEASURED IS ACTUALLY MEASURED.  The content and form of an assessment task must be congruent with what is supposed to be measured .” NRC, 1996 p. 84",
