  {
    "id": "567d8472-061a-4b69-98b9-e274b4c8caed",
    "title": "Administering WIDA MODEL Online: Grades 1–12 (Part 5/7)",
    "content": "Format of Writing Task The Writing Task has two parts: Part A:  Consists of a writing prompt that includes a picture. For Students in Grades 1-5: Students respond to simple questions about the picture. For Students in Grades 6-12: Students are given one minute to describe what they see in the picture. It is expected that students will produce  single words ,  phrases , or  simple sentences  in their written responses to Part A. WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nFormat of Writing Task Part B:  Consists of a constructed response task designed to allow students to demonstrate their ability to write beyond single words and short phrases.   Students in Grades 1-5 will handwrite their response Students in Grades 6-12 will type their response.  WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nSample Student pages: Grades 1-5 The left side of the screen includes a repeat of the prompts panels and information to help the student organize/plan his/her writing The right side of the screen displays the word box (Grade 1-2 only) and a picture of the Student Response Form. WIDA Consortium / MetriTech  Students write their response on the provided  Student  Response  Form\n\nSample Student pages: Grades 6-12 The left side of the screen includes information to help the student organize/plan his/her response WIDA Consortium / MetriTech  The right side of the screen is where the student types his/her response\n\nWriting Rubric of the WIDA Consortium Task Level Linguistic Complexity Vocabulary Usage Language Control 6 Reaching A variety of sentence lengths of linguistic complexity in a single tightly organized paragraph or in well-organized extended text; tight cohesion and organization Consistent use of just the right word in just the right place; precise Vocabulary Usage in general, specific, or technical language Has reached comparability to that of English proficient peers functioning at the  “ proficient ”  level in state-wide assessments 5 Bridging A variety of sentence lengths of varying linguistic complexity in a single organized paragraph or in extended text; cohesion and organization Usage of technical language related to the content area; evident facility with needed vocabulary Approaching comparability to that of English proficient peers; errors don ’ t impede comprehensibility 4 Expanding A variety of sentence lengths of varying linguistic complexity; emerging cohesion used to provide detail and clarity Usage of specific and some technical language related to the content area; lack of needed vocabulary may be occasionally evident Generally comprehensible at all times, errors don ’ t impede the overall meaning; such errors may reflect first language interference WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nWriting Rubric of the WIDA Consortium Task Level Linguistic Complexity Vocabulary Usage Language Control 3 Developing Simple and expanded sentences that show emerging complexity used to provide detail Usage of general and some specific language related to the content area; lack of needed vocabulary may be evident Generally comprehensible when writing in sentences; comprehensibility may from time to time be impeded by errors when attempting to produce more complex text 2 Emerging Phrases and short sentences, varying amount of text may be copied or adapted; some attempt at organization may be evidenced Usage of general language related to the content area; lack of vocabulary may be evident Generally comprehensible when text is adapted from model or source text, or when original text is limited to simple text; comprehensibility may be often impeded by errors 1 Entering Single words, set phrases, or chunks of simple language; varying amounts of text may be copied or adapted; adapted text contains original language Usage of highest frequency vocabulary from school setting and content areas Generally comprehensible when text is copied or adapted from model or source text; comprehensibility may be significantly impeded in original text WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nScoring Writing For all grade cluster tests, test administrators will provide two types of scores for the student ’ s response to the Writing Section. During  administration the TA quickly evaluates the student ’ s writing sample as Low, Mid or High. This is known as the  Quick Score . After  all sections of the test are complete and the student has been dismissed, the TA will evaluate the student ’ s writing using the full  Writing   Rubric of the WIDA   Consortium  to determine a language proficiency level.  All scoring is recorded in the Test Administrator Interface. WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nWIDA MODEL is available as a full-length summative assessment or a shorter screener assessment.  The version of the assessment you give is dependent upon the purpose for assessing.  Determining which form to use must be done  prior  to beginning the test administration. WIDA Consortium / MetriTech  General Information (3 of 3)",
