  {
    "id": "bd02f639-3987-4f6c-b31f-9b8c287bacf3",
    "title": "Administering WIDA MODEL Online: Grades 1–12 (Part 4/7)",
    "content": "Speaking Rubric of the WIDA Consortium (continued) Task Level Linguistic Complexity Vocabulary Usage Language Control 4 Expanding A variety of oral sentence lengths of varying linguistic complexity; responses show emerging cohesion used to provide detail and clarity Specific and some technical language related to the content area; groping for needed vocabulary may be occasionally evident At all times generally comprehensible and fluent, though phonological, syntactic, or semantic errors that don ’ t impede the overall meaning of the communication may appear at times; such errors may reflect first language interference 5 Bridging A variety of sentence lengths of varying linguistic complexity in extended oral discourse; responses show cohesion and organization used to support main ideas Technical language related to the content area; facility with needed vocabulary is evident Approaching comparability to that of English proficient peers in terms of comprehensibility and fluency; errors don ’ t impede communication and may be typical of those an English proficient peer might make WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nSpeaking Scoring Scale Meets (M) Meets all task level expectations in  quantity   and  quality ? Test Administrator is unsure if student ’ s response scores  meets  or  approaches .  This is a placeholder . The TA must make a judgment on the next task. Approaches (A) Approaches task level expectations, but falls short in quantity and/or quality; no response; response in a language other than English. WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nNavigating the Speaking Domain Test WIDA Consortium / MetriTech  WIDA   MODEL   Speaking Tasks Grades 1-5 Grades 6-12\n\nReinforcing Student Responses You may provide the student some limited reinforcement of their oral responses Remember to only give  nonjudgmental , concise encouragement such as,  “ Good, ”  or  “ O.K. ” Anything you say should be positive, though never evaluative Do not use phrases such as,  “ You answered that really well, ”  or  “ That ’ s right. ” WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nPractice Scoring Speaking Reference the following resources, located within the TAI: Scoring the WIDA MODEL   Speaking Section Training PowerPoint –  This includes benchmark samples for each task Extra Practice Scoring the MODEL Speaking Section PowerPoint –  This includes samples for independent practice Speaking Score Sheet  – Use this to record scores when completing the Extra Practice WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nWIDA MODEL   Listening Domain Test WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nListening Domain Test Overview Format:  Multiple Choice Group administered Student indicates responses by clicking on the correct answer Time:  Up to 30 minutes Scoring:  Student Test Platform captures student answers Required Materials: Student Test Platform, logged in for the student Headphones for each student Test Administrator Interface WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nListening Domain Administration Procedures Find the test session in the Test Administrator Interface Print the roster with student log in information Log each student in to the secure browser on their workstation After logging in, each student clicks on START TEST.  In the TAI, click on “Proctor” and then “Start Test” WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nSample Listening Item WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nScoring The Test Platform will automatically calculate the student scores and place the student appropriately.  WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nGeneral Information (2 of 3) Test items are aligned to the WIDA  English Language Development (ELD) Standards. Scores correlate to the WIDA  ELD levels in the Standards. Test items are organized into sets of 3 to 4 questions based on a topic or unifying theme Items within the set of questions progressively become more linguistically demanding WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nWIDA MODEL   Writing Domain Test WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nWriting Domain Test Overview Format:  Student-constructed responses Group administered  Students in grades 1-5 handwrite responses on a Student Response form Students in Grades 6-12 type in the Student Test Platform Time:  Up to 30 minutes Scoring:  Initial Quick Score for placement (during administration) and Writing Rubric (after administration) Required Materials: Student Test Platform Test Administrator Interface Headphones Student Response Form (for students in grades 1-5) WIDA Consortium / MetriTech\n\nWriting Task Selection There are 3 different Writing tasks Any task may be selected for a given student by the TA as they are selecting Summative vs. Screener Different writing tasks may be used in one testing session The district, school, or TA may want to use one task when the student is initially assessed and the second task to chart growth or collect more information at a later date.   WIDA Consortium / MetriTech",
