  {
    "id": "3dc48eeb-ddf0-4911-9c98-fc88fa6689e3",
    "title": "WebbAlign DOK PD for Fayette County CTE: March 20, 2023 Session Evaluation Responses (Part 1/2)",
    "content": "WebbAlign DOK PD for Fayette County CTE: March 20, 2023 Session Evaluation Responses\n\nTotal responses: 55\n\nParticipants reported that the sessions greatly enhanced their understanding of rubric development and use. They felt more confident in their ability to develop and appropriately use rubrics.\n\nWhich part of the March 20 PD session was most helpful for you? Explain why.\n\nTime to work on rubric\n\nSitting and brainstorming with my pathway colleagues\n\nWorking as a team\n\nThe focus on Rubric creation and assessing the utility of the rubric were helpful.\n\nGroup discussion\n\nIt was helpful to work with colleagues on the material at hand.\n\nBeing able to work on a rubric with my constant area members.\n\nSpecifics of wording rubrics\n\nThe Rubric development process helped me better understand what criteria my rubric should have as a foundation for structured well explained projects to my students.\n\nCollaboration with the other teachers in the county. Allowing us time to discuss common assessments since we do not get subject PLC\n\nBeing able to collaborate with peers and compare strategies\n\nContrasting complexity vs performance\n\nCollaboration with colleagues\n\nApplying the rubric theories to my class with the other teachers who teach the same content.\n\nCollaboration\n\nAs a singleton in my school PLC, the most valuable part of PD was time with my district level PLC.\n\nGroup discussions\n\nSara’s detailed breakdown of rubric development was helpful and made me re-think some of my assumptions about the rubrics I design.\n\nApplying to rubric\n\nRubric development\n\nRubrics are always adjusted to the way you teach.\n\nRubrics\n\nSharing with other school teachers of same subject to create common rubrics.\n\nRubric common assessment\n\nworking with team\n\nTalking to the team.\n\nI enjoyed learning how to create a stronger rubric\n\nThe opportunity to work with my counterparts\n\nGetting to share and talk with colleagues Is always a plus.\n\nAll\n\nExplanation of rubric construction\n\nReconnecting with the levels of rubrics and detail\n\nSpending time working on a rubric\n\nDeveloping rubrics.\n\nWorking with other teachers\n\nHow balance rubrics with expected student success and solid guidelines.\n\nRubric\n\nEvaluating existing rubric with template example\n\nPoints allocation and better task explanation\n\nI learned some helpful hints for creating rubrics, grading them and even explaining them to the students.\n\nCollaboration with other teachers in my pathway\n\nBeing able to work with another instructor and bounce ideas off him\n\nRubric development and improvement.\n\nActually I feel that all parts were helpful.\n\nProviding the info presented, to use as future reference.\n\nCreating Rubric, including the standards and DOK\n\nApplying DOK to our rubrics\n\n4. What aspects of the March 20 PD session helped affirm your existing understanding of rubric development and use?\n\nBrainstorming\n\nUnderstanding levels of knowledge\n\nThe rubric practice\n\nGaining a deeper understanding.\n\nAgain, brainstorming with colleagues was extremely helpful.\n\nTime to work with others\n\nThe review of the DOK levels\n\nThat I understand how well the assignment given should be detailed.\n\nNormalizing a rubric for all subject teachers\n\nPresentation on rubric alignment with course\n\nReviewing the \"bad\" rubric\n\nReassurance that other teachers were using the same methods as I am.\n\nThe rubric examples and rubric of the rubric.\n\nCollaborative discussion regarding approach to rubric use and existing use.\n\nSample rubric\n\ncontent analysis\n\nTalking with other teacher and understanding the difference teaching styles\n\nPreparing a group rubric\n\nMy performance criteria align with GADOE.\n\nGroup work and assignments.\n\nDOK Levels\n\nNeed for specificity and work focus not student focused\n\nThe examples that you have.\n\nMaking a rubric\n\nRubrics take time to develop, but can be very useful.\n\nAll\n\nCollaborating with colleagues\n\nMade it more concrete\n\nPracticing with a new template and discussing reasoning\n\nRevising current rubrics.\n\nReviewing what I have created with new ideas\n\nThe example and worksheet given\n\nStandards\n\nAs a new instructor of industry content this session has helped reaffirm my understanding of rubrics I am currently using.\n\nDescriptors\n\nThat students need feedback, understanding of requirements\n\nI was able to compare some early rubrics I made with a more recent one and saw the improvements\n\nSeeing the examples helped me see that I understand what I am doing\n\nTo focus in common areas with a different perspective.\n\nI was able to incorporate the knowledge during the much appreciated work time\n\nHaving a visual exposure definitely helped\n\nShould be clear to teacher and student\n\n6. Any additional questions, topics for future sessions, feedback or comments welcomed here!\n\nThanks!\n\nThank you for the review\n\nEnthusiasm and positivity of the presenter\n\nGreat presentation and presenter!\n\nThank you for your support\n\nI have learned a great deal in these sessions.\n\nThank you. I enjoyed learning more.\n\nThank you!",
