    "title": "Annotated NCSA 2019 Science Alignment  (Part 1/1)",
    "content": "■AIR @ WCEPS WebbAlign Alignment Analysis of a Framework-Based Statewide Summative Science Assessment with Corresponding Standards (such as NGSS): Results and Recommendations NCSA 2019  ■AIR @  M WCEPS @ wassaion]  Presenters and Contributors:  Timothy Butcher  West Virginia Department of  Education  Paul Dumas (in absentia)  Hawai'i Department of  Education  Sara Christopherson  Director of WebbAlign  Wisconsin Center for Education  Products and Services  Antonia Deoudes  Director of Client Operations  American Institutes for  Research  NCSA 2019  Topic Introduction and Landscape Alignment Analysis of a Framework-Based Statewide Summative Science Assessment with Corresponding Standards (such as NGSS): using a Multi-state Model -design decisions and discussion • Shift in original Multi-state Alignment Study timing from spring 2019 to summer 2019 • Opportunity to gather greater input to refine, focus design • Our presentation approach -• provide the context for the Multi-state effort, organization • Information about common item bank • Alignment Analysis design, logistics and recommendations [mAR Ł W WCEPS @ web6Anon]  Group of Sharing States • What is the relationship between participating states? • Group of states that decided to • develop items under a common item development system. • share a common bank of items. • Item development started December 2015. • All states had contracts with AIR when developing items. • Each state determines their blueprint. • Each state sets own cuts. [mAR Ł @ web6Anon]  Sharing States • Which states are in the MOU item sharing group? • Connecticut • Hawaii • Idaho • Oregon • Rhode Island • Utah • Vermont • West Virginia • Wyoming [\"AIR @ @ wessAton]  Commonalities  •  What are the commonalities within the item sharing group?  •  Item development activities (specifications, quality criteria, item reviews)  •  Alignment study  •  Created an initial draft of ALDs/PLDs to be ratified or customized by each state  •  Where do states have flexibility within the item sharing group?  •  Blueprints  •  Standard setting  •  Test administration grade levels  [AIR Ł @ web6Aon]  Test Development • Which grades are the tests administered? • Grades 3-5 • Some states administer a Grade 5 grade band test • Some states administer a Grade 4 grade level test • Grades 6-8 • To date, all states administer a Grade 8 grade band test • High School • Some states administer a high school test encompassing all HS PEs • Some states administer a life Science test [AIR Ł @ web6Aon]  Activities  •  What activities have occurred over the past four years,  •  Specifications developed for all NGSS and State Science standards  •  500+ Clusters were created  •  500+ Stand alone items were created  •  10 Rubric Validations were conducted (+4 scheduled)  •  9 Item writing workshops were held  •  37 Item review workshops were held  [AIR Ł @ web6Aon]  Item development • How are the items developed? • Item writing/phenomenon development at the state level by teachers within some states. • Items are developed by AIR. • Items undergo the traditional reviews. • Items are reviewed by each state prior to each state's approval. • Items are adjudicated by the owning state. • Items are field tested across states. • Items move through Rubric Validation and Data Review by owning state, using a cross-state sample. • Items are placed in the operational item bank. [AIR © Ł web6Aton]  Blueprint • What does the testing blueprint look like? • Each states determines the blueprint in use. • However, many states are following a similar blueprint. Content Min Clusters � Alignment Content Max Clusters Min Stand Max Stand Stand Alone + Stand Alone Grade 8, arranged by DCI Level Alignment Min Clusters Max Clusters Alone Items Alone Items Items Items Science I Discloline -Phvsical Science. PE Total = 19 Discipline PS 2 2 4 4 • • CI -Matterand Its Interactions DO PS1 • 1 • 2 • 3 I S-PS1-1 : Structure & Properties of Matter PE MS-PS1-1 • 1 • 1 • 1 IS-PS1-2 : Chemical Reactions PE MS-PS1-2 • 1 • 1 • 1 MS-PS1-3 : Structure & Properties of Matter PE MS-PS1-3 • 1 • 1 • 1 MS-PS1-4 : Structure & Properties of Matter PE MS-PS1-4 • 1 • 1 • 1 IS-PS1-5 : Chemical Reactions PE MS-PS1-5 • 1 • 1 • 1 S-PS1-6 : Chemical Reactions* PE MS-PS1-6 • 1 • 1 • 1 CI -Motionand Stability: Forces and Interactions DO PS2 • 1 • 2 • 3 MS-PS2-1 : Forces & Interactions* PE MS-PS2-1 • 1 • 1 • 1 MS-PS2-2 : Forces & Interactions PE MS-PS2-2 • 1 • 1 • 1 ■AIR � @ web6Anon] 10  Clusters Clusters Clusters Stand Stand Stand Student Life Science Physical Science Earth and Space Alone Alone Alone Items (LS) (PS) Science (ESS) Items LS Items PS ESS 1 2 2 2 4 4 4 2 2 2 2 4 4 4 3 2 2 2 4 4 4 [AIR Ł @ web6Aon] 11  Science Standards  •  What standards are states within the group using?  •  Many states have adopted NGSS completely.  •  Other states have varying degrees of modification.  •  State have individual item development plans inclusive of :  • Custom development for individual/adapted state standards.  •  MOU development for adopted/lightly adapted NGSS standards.  [AIR Ł @ web6Aon]  12  An item cluster is intended to target a single PE. The item cluster is composed of multiple parts. Not all parts are intended to require interweaving of all 3 dimensions of a PE but the entire item cluster overall is intended to require students to incorporate all three dimensions. In this example, Part A is intended to require a SEP with DCI interaction and Part B is intended to require a DCI/CCC interaction.  This is a released sample item. The left side of the slide shows the stimulus (phenomenon). The right side of the screen shows multiple interactions. E.g. part A has six separate interactions. The full set of items/tasks/interactions is called an “item cluster.” The entire item cluster, as a whole, is intended to target a single PE.  For each interaction, there is a corresponding “scoring assertion” that is intended as an explicit statement of what can be inferred about student knowledge, skills, and abilities, based on the successful response to an interaction.  Alignment Process  How did you get to where you are in the alignment process?  •  Gain efficiency by reducing redundancy across states  •  Share most of our standards and performance expectations.  •  Share most of our items.  •  Why pay to evaluate the same items aligned to the same standards?  •  Clearly articulate the types of thinking that we want students to do  •  What does \"depth of knowledge\" or \"cognitive complexity\" become in the context of framework-based science?  [AIR Ł @ web6Aon]  16  Alignment Process How did you get to where you are in the alignment process? • States meet every other week to discuss the how to align the multi­dimensional items regarding • Cognitive complexity. • Three dimensional standards. • Common TAC meeting held in March, 2019 • State TACs for input during design [AIR � @ web6Aon] 17  Study Design  •  Participation by the 10 States utilizing the shared Science Item Pool  •  Alignment Study in two parts:  •  Evaluate the alignment of items in the pool to the standards.  • Evaluate the match of state-specific forms to blueprint and the adequacy of the blueprint's coverage of the state standards.  •  Subject Matter Expert Meeting (July, 2019) will include participants from 10 states  •  Small Scale Trial on process, system, coding conducted June 4-5, 2019.  [AIR Ł @ web6Aon]  18  Vendor Selection  How did you select a vendor?  •  After review of many proposals through a competitive bid process, the group of states decided to award the alignment study project to WebbAlign.  •  One of the attractive components of the proposal was the concept of conducting a pilot with educators.  •  The states are working closely with WebbAlign to further refine the process.  [AIR Ł @ web6Aon]  19  The context for this discussion is grounded in states’ use of statewide summative assessments and the need for alignment, which is not only required by federal statute but is also a tenet of professional practice, per Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing.  The requirements here are that the assessments reflect the standards – that the assessments measure the knowledge and skills specified in the State’s standards.  Note one inherent tension: the language of the critical elements envisions academic content standards while NGSS are intended as performance expectations.  There are other critical elements that relate to alignment analysis but the focus is on these key ones for now – and 4.4 (scoring) will be discussed later.  This presentation walks through the study design and methodologies, which were informed by discussions with states about their intended claims related to student scores on these science assessments and was also informed by a small-scale pilot test of evaluative processes.  •  It can be helpful to really specify what is different about NGSS alignment and what is the same – compared with the alignment of summative assessments with other sets of standards. The overall goal is the same. But the approach needs some adjustments to address features of the NGSS.  •  The planned work is grounded in the assumption that the purpose of statewide summative NGSS assessment is to yield big-picture evidence of students’ 3D sense-making across subject areas but that these summative assessments are part of a larger system of assessments  •  The Fulmer et al article from JRST September 2018 , nicely summarizes the “aligned to what” question that we’ve revisited in many conferences and other spaces  •  Usually the referents for statewide summative assessments have been the standards. But with NGSS, we’ve struggled to define the referents, in large part because there is not consensus in the field about the intents and purposes of ”the standards.” Because the NGSS “vision” includes all possible intersections of all of the dimensions there have been some claims that NGSS assessments might evade the requirement that assessment tasks “match” any type of expectation. This is a risky proposition that could compromise the potential for NGSS (and other framework-based standards) to shift the system and to bring about the change that they’re intended to bring about.  •  We need to be able to identify our referents. If we are going to be able to know if assessments are meeting our expectations w/r/t alignment with standards then we need to name and define what our expectations are (as relates to assessment targets overall, the complexity of student engagement, as relates to dimensionality, etc).  We need a clear construct of what we are trying to measure. • Defining referents depends on the claims that states intend to make as relates to the standards -- this is true for ALL standards. (For example, common core math standards are two dimensional, but the dimensions aren’t integrated, and many states don’t identify the math practices as a referent for statewide summative assessment. But there’s more potential for variation in claims and interpretation with  NGSS than we’ve seen with other standards.)  •  After we have defined the referents, then we can consider the comparands. Comparands here include sample test forms, state blueprints, aggregate data for recently administered test events, and other test specifications and documentation. In this particular instance, the test program includes the scoring assertions that you saw earlier. These are also included in this study as a comparand –more about that later.  (Surely more than two – but here’s two broad categories.)  If using the exact language of the PEs – then the referent is 3 dimensional --it includes 3 detailed interwoven dimensions. The referent also specifies content – particular disciplinary core ideas. For example, 3-LS-4-1 DCI says “Fossils provide evidence about the types of organisms that lived long ago and also about the nature of their environments.” You could imagine many other disciplinary core ideas that relate to fossils – about the different ways that fossils form or about why fossils are more likely to show bones and other durable structures vs soft tissues, etc. The point is that a PE specifics content.  And the overall 3D expectation additionally describes and sometimes constrains expectations as relates to the complexity of engagement or extent of reasoning with evidence expected. These expectations for complexity emerge from the description of how students are expected to engage with the three dimensions – each PE specifies the roles of the SEP and the CCC as relates to the DCI.  In other words, a PE is a 3D expectation that specifies content as well as the complexity of cognitive engagement with the content.  It could also be argued that one cannot use the PEs – e.g. if the assessment target includes all possible intersections of all dimensions. Alternatively it could be claimed that we just shouldn’t use the PEs as a referent for statewide summative assessments, because they are intended just as examples of 3D combinations. In any case, If using broader vision, then assessment referents need to be defined based on external “rules.”  My opinion is that either of these are viable and defensible, depending on the arguments made. If there is a global use of external rules, these should be constant. Use of external rules that are defined on a case-by-case basis has the potential to become a slippery slope that undermines the intent of the standards and leads to inconsistency in evaluation of alignment.  There are three main reasons. First, the standards say that the PEs are the assessment targets.  We have people at all levels and in all roles of out education system who are reading these words even though not all folks close to the standards agree with this statement. While I understand and empathize with the reality of navigating the politics of new standards, these are the words we are left with. I’ll paraphrase Brian Gong of the Center for Assessment and say that we need to be able to trust the words of the standards. If using the PEs as the assessment targets is not appropriate, then this language needs to be removed from the NGSS.  As is acknowledged very directly in the Framework, the only way that a shift in standards (a shift in the expectations) can have the intended positive effect on a shift in the overall educational system, is if there is alignment or coherence between and among all parts of the system.  Within the language of the standards, there is differentiation between how NGSS is manifest in the classroom vs on summative assessments.  On a summative assessment, we NEED to have specific assessment targets, specific claims, a specific assessment construct – we have to know what we are measuring. We have some set of something that we should be able to pretty directly identify. (Again, if it is inappropriate to use the PEs as the assessment targets then the statement that PEs are intended to “…make clear the intent of the assessments” needs to be removed from the NGSS documentation.)  If we can identify WHAT we are assessing, we are then able to evaluate alignment – if and to what extent we are doing what we are intending to do.  Another reason to use the PE is because that’s what states are required to do per ESSA. ”The standards” are expected to be the referent for both content and complexity of engagement.  And, importantly, the assessment design is such that each item cluster and each stand-alone item is intended to target a particular PE. And, of course, that is the purpose of the alignment analysis --– to evaluate if and to what extent an assessment is doing what it is intended to do.  So once we PICK a referent, then we need to makes sure that we all agree on what that referent LOOKS LIKE. ALL of these images have some characteristics of a duck. Only one matches the referent, however.  There’s multiple factors that we need to consider when we’re comparing referents and comparands to determine match for NGSS. In this case we have a 3-dimensional complex duck as our referent. There’s only one other 3D complex duck. There’s also a 3D less complex duck and a 2D complex duck along with 2D less complex ducks. In this analogy, you can see that dimensionality is necessary to explain the match (or mismatch) but is not sufficient . We need to think not only about dimensionality but also about complexity.  So if we are using the PEs as a referent, then we are accounting for dimensionality if we find that an assessment item or cluster corresponds to a PE – if it corresponds to the PE then we are saying we’re fairly confident students are indeed interweaving all three dimensions to successfully complete the task.  We might also make decision rules about referents, and in this case of this alignment study, reflecting the assessment design and claims, Item clusters must reflect all three  dimensions but stand-alone items can reflect either 2 or 3 of the dimensions. Back to our analogy, in the case of a 2D task, we might find that the 2-D detailed-painting-duck (“task”) matches the referent, but that the other 2-D ducks (“tasks”) do not – and to differentiate between what is appropriate and what is not appropriate incorporates a consideration of complexity.  Of course when we are talking about an actual PE, not a duck, it’s a little more complicated and we need to calibrate on what a 3-dimensionai PE looks like – what does it look like / how do you know when a CCC is invoked in the context of a statewide summative assessment. What “counts” as analyzing data or modeling? Etc.  As previously noted, the language of the critical elements specifies the standards as the referent.  W/r/t cognitive engagement (complexity) you can see that critical element 3.2 specifies an expectation that the “intended cognitive processes” are ”as represented in the State’s academic content standards” – in other words there’s an expectation that the standards should communicate the intended cognitive complexity of student engagement.  And of course, this is an explicit GOAL of the Next Generation Science Standards –to shift the the expectations for student engagement with science to be more complex than what was included in previous sets of science standards and, as relates to summative assessment in particular, the NGSS specify that knowledge in isolation and recall of knowledge or facts should not be summative assessment targets.  •  Within the Framework and the resulting new standards, there’s many references to the intended shift away from standards that expected memorization that focused on “knowledge itself” or knowing “the facts” and instead to focus on final goals related to conceptual understanding and actual authentic engagement in scientific thinking and argumentation .  •  Some of these references are shown here.  •  The Framework called out previous science standards for being -- “long lists of detailed and disconnected facts” (Framework, p. 24). – in contrast with the goals for the new standards.  So alignment needs to compare the complexity of the expectations with the complexity of the corresponding tasks. If a task does not offer students the opportunity to engage at the same level of complexity as expressed in the standard, then a correct response doesn’t allow for an inference about a student’s ability to engage at that level of complexity / that type of cognitive engagement.  For evaluative purposes, we need a way of communicating consistently about complexity. DOK is an evaluative tool, a language system, that is used for this purpose, to interpret and evaluate the complexity of engagement required by a particular expectation or prompt or task. The original DOK definitions for science were written around 1997 and that original language has been out of date for a while now – in terms of reflecting the overall nature of how we are talking and thinking about science education and envisioning learning expectations.  With the release of NGSS we decided we really needed to make adjustments to the DOK definitions for science so that the tool would be more readily usable for interpretation of current standards. A couple of years ago we formally started into the information gathering process to inform changes to the definitions for science and also considered a complete reinvisioning of DOK for science. We wanted input from multiple perspectives in the field that would appropriately reflect the new standards. So last May we invited key stakeholders involved in the development and use of the new science standards to come from all around the country and sit in the same room for a couple of days to talk about complexity in NGSS.  Many amazing thought leaders shared and discussed their perspectives on how to  interpret the complexity of the NGSS in a 2-day meeting. Within this group, as with every other group of folks I’ve worked with on this subject, there were a number of different ideas about what constitutes cognitive complexity or complexity of student engagement in general. People also brought a variety of ideas about the meaning and interpretation of the standards themselves.  I’ve heard assertions that we don’t need to use a framework for cognitive complexity or complexity of student engagement to interpret the NGSS, because it is all clearly stated in the standards. This is empirically not true. Many of the exemplar NGSS assessment tasks that are circulating use an involved context or phenomenon but the student interactions require recall and knowledge in isolation. Understanding complexity is critical. Identifying the intended complexity can go a long way in CLARIFYING the intent of a PE as well as support assessment development –(it helps you know what a duck looks like). (Of course, this extends into the classroom as well – understanding complexity helps teachers as they design opportunities for students to make sense of phenomena – it supports intentionality of practice.)  We used input from the 2018 2-day stakeholder meeting, from follow-up from that meeting, from work with K-12 educators, and from a variety of other contexts and experiences, to rework the language. The intent is for the language to help cross-walk the relationship between ideas of task/performance complexity and the goals and features of Framework-based standards.  We have trialed use of the draft language with educators in three analyses. Feedback has been very positive so far and has helped identify a few places where we need to spend some time clarifying meaning  This revised tool will be made freely available when completed –draft copies are available now and feedback is welcomed.  Back to the PE as the unit of analysis….  When we’re thinking about the complexity of a task that involves developing or using a model or analyzing and interpreting data, the complexity of the task depends on multiple factors, including the type of question pursued, the sophistication of the model or data, the type and extent of explanation, evidence, or prediction, etc.  And that’s what the PEs do – they specify the relationship of a practice, DCI, and CCC.  For space, only the statements of the PEs are listed here, but the categories of engagement apply when considering the foundation boxes also. There are two examples of PEs that were considered Category 2, one example of Category 3, and one example of Category 4.  4-ESS2-2 is grounded in connecting patterns of Earth’s features with representations on maps. You can see the shift from previous expectations that might expect students to identify Earth’s features on maps. Here the emphasis is on a consideration of data and the patterns shown as relates to Earth features. (C2)  HS-PS-4: In this second PE there’s an expectation to develop a model that shows a specific relationship. Instead of requiring recitation of the DCIs, as we might have seen in previous standards, student are asked to think conceptually about the flow of energy in the overall system. (C2)  HS-ESS3-2: Here, there’s something more happening. This expectation is not just for students to demonstrate conceptual understanding of science ideas and concepts. This describes an evaluative task that would require students to weigh costs and  benefits, and presumably come up with a defensible conclusion grounded in scientific ideas, empirical evidence, and logical arguments as relates to constraints and parameters. (C3)  HS-ESS3-5: For this last one, educators thought that to really do justice to this expectation, students would need to spend extended time selecting appropriate data and models for analysis, engage in extended iterative work, trying out a variety of ways to approach the question, interpret the data and models, etc. and assigned it a Category 4. (C4)  Critical element 4.4 typically isn’t directly included in an alignment analysis of standards and assessments. Certainly it is related, but it is typically not part of a content alignment analysis.  For this alignment analysis (with scoring assertions as comparands), we have two referents – the PE and the actual student interactions – that we will compare with the scoring assertions.  The evaluation will be high-level, and not a fine-toothed editorial processes --that is expected to have been completed in the test design and development process.  NGSS Alignment Analysis Trial Run: Results and Recommendations • 10 educators; members of Wisconsin Science Education Leadership Association (WSELA) • Conducted in collaboration with Kevin Anderson, WI DPI + President of the Council of State Science Officers • Trialed abbreviated training, evaluative processes • Gathered limited alignment results but critical takeaways about evaluative approaches. • States observed via online meeting. [AIR Ł @ web6Aon]  Assessment design informs units of analysis. Remember that each item and item cluster is intended to target a single PE. This summarizes the evaluation process for an item cluster.  Panelists will work through the item cluster (all parts) as if they are the student.  Panelists will identify a PE, if any, that the item cluster assesses. Successful completion of the task should imply to a high level of certainty that the student has engaged with the specific three dimensions identified in the corresponding PE. If there are multiple bulleted DCIs for a PE, panelists will need to identify any DCIs that are NOT represented in the cluster.  •  First, each panelist will work independently to make a determination of the appropriate PE.  •  Next, each panelist will compare their selection against the PE given in the AIR metadata.  •  If different, the panelist will consider the internally assigned PE.  •  If the item/item cluster is appropriately coded, panelists will record this PE. If the item/item cluster is NOT appropriately coded, the item will be  identified. No additional content analysis will occur for item clusters for which the panel consensus is that the item cluster does not assess a PE. Students interact with very few item clusters, so it is critical that each item cluster \"does what it claims to do / measures what it is intended to measure\".  •  Does the task correspond to the discipline(s) and content expressed in the corresponding PE(s)?  •  Does successful completion of the task require students to engage with the dimensions of the standards that are identified as corresponding to the targeted PE?  This approach is different from the typical independent analysis,  Students interact with very few items/item clusters, so it is critical that each item/item cluster \"does what it claims to do\".  Because this is not a fixed form assessment, the accuracy of the internal (AIR) metadata is critical; appropriate selection of items for use in test events per test blueprints/algorithm is dependent on the internal metadata, including the coding for standards.  The evaluative judgement that needs to be made about multiple interactions and multiple scoring assertions depend on the specific three dimensions of the targeted PE. So we need to start with consensus on the targeted PE.  In grade-band panels, panelists practice using the Categories of engagement to interpret the PEs – this allows for calibration in interpretation of the standards and use of the tool. Then, they will review previously assigned CEs – flag any they want to discuss, and make any adjustments warranted if there’s consensus.  They will use the printed definitions of the Categories of Engagement along with the factors discussed in the initial training and the takeaways from that initial calibration work.  It is absolutely necessary to evaluate all subcomponents of an item cluster but data are recorded at the cluster-level.  Do the scoring assertions reflect  1)  the inferences that can be made from successful student interactions and  2)  the expectations in the corresponding PE?  As mentioned earlier, we typically do not conduct content alignment analysis for scoring with assessments and standards but the use of narrative scoring assertions necessitates a content alignment analysis because states are using these assertions to make claims about what students have demonstrated based on their responses to the assessment prompts  Additional data will be collected based on the states’ intended claims that the assessment is phenomena-based.  Panelists will also have the opportunity to record qualitative feedback about each item/cluster.  One thing that has come out of our discussions and trials is the critical importance of providing opportunities for panelists to calibrate on multiple components of the work. This requires facilitated interactive professional development that elicits educators’ ideas, challenges any dissonance in those ideas, provides example tasks that promote debate and discussion, and overall, helps move a group toward improved calibration (a shared understanding of different aspects of the work).  As for all contexts, not just NGSS, people need to calibrate on their understanding of complexity in general. We work with educators across the country in professional development settings to elicit ideas and grapple with ideas about what constitutes complexity in a task or expectation. We find that people – even experienced educators and assessment writers and editors – bring very different ideas about complexity to the table – they are all trying to talk about complexity of a task--and coming at it from different angles, looking through different lenses. People bring their own conceptual constructs of complexity, and their models depend on their underlying ideas about subject matter, about the nature of thinking in different disciplines, about the nature of learning, about student ability and capacity, and many other components of a person’s conceptual ecology and personal epistemology (ideas about the nature of knowledge).  So we know we need to calibrate on the use of this evaluative tool and on the use of the language system to differentiate between and among the different categories of engagement.  CCC: It’s acknowledged in the field (e.g. see linked proceedings given on slide) that the CCCs have also presented some acute interpretation challenges. For the context of this work, we will need to come to a shared understanding of what evidence we will ”see” of a CCC in an item or cluster.  (Summit for Examining the Potential for Crosscutting Concepts to Support Three-Dimensional Learning Conference Proceedings December 6th – 8th, 2018 University of Virginia – Darden Sands Family Grounds Arlington, VA Sarah J. Fick, Jeffrey Nordine, Kevin W. McElhaney (Editors))  We may refine some of this – but here’s where we are now. These are the alignment criteria and proposed cutoffs that will be used to evaluate alignment for this assessment.  We still care about the same general considerations as always – these are considerations that go into the design of a blueprint – e.g. how many opportunities should students have to interact with standards within each reporting category? What range of standards should be included as assessment targets? Etc.  For some of these categories, we need to redefine or specify what they each mean. And we need to add some additional criteria, depending on the intended claims based on student scores. If there’s a claim that students are making sense of phenomena, then we need to add a category to check that an external review agrees that items are grounded in making sense of a phenomenon vs a context or another type of stimulus – and also that students are actually using the information presented to figure out science problems vs being presented with items that do not require making sense of the phenomenon (which is a commonly observed issue for many released NGSS items).  To address 3-dimensionaity, could consider dimensionality to be part of Categorical Concurrence – as in this study, panelists will assign a PE to an item cluster only if all three dimensions of the PE are elicited by the task. be. Alternatively, could use an additional criterion of Structure of Knowledge, that compares the structure of knowledge represented in the standards with the structure of knowledge elicited by the assessment tasks.  These criteria and proposed cutoffs for acceptability are grounded in the idea that the purpose of statewide summative assessment is to yield big-picture evidence of students’ 3D sense-making across subject areas but that these summative assessments are just part of a larger system of assessments  We’ve focused on the content alignment analysis aspect of this study but we will incorporate the results of that work into analyses of other components, including a review of state blueprints and aggregate data from all administered test events. Although there are some differences between states’ blueprints and claims, there is much overlap. The shared alignment study can therefore be a cost-and time-efficient approach for the collective of states.  In-person alignment institute scheduled for next month: more news to come....  Contact:  Timothy Butcher tbutcher@k12.wv.us  Paul Dumas Paul Dumas/OSIP/HIDOE@notes.k12.hi.us  Toni Deoudes adeoudes@air.org  Sara Christopherson sara.christopherson@wceps.org  [AIR Ł @ web6Aon]",
