  {
    "id": "cc28e287-5991-48d5-837a-b63d348aa773",
    "title": "Sample Item-Writing Guidelines for Multiple Choice Questions",
    "content": "Title: Sample Item-Writing Guidelines for Multiple Choice Questions\n\nContent: Sample Item-Writing Guidelines for Multiple Choice Questions\n\n1. Identify the assessment target (element) and confirm the DOK. Discuss with colleagues:\n\nWhat exactly does this expectation indicate about what our students should know and be able to do?\n\nWhat evidence would help us know that a student had mastered this expectation?\n\nUse this discussion to guide the selection, writing, or revising of items.\n\n2. Consider using a stimulus. A stimulus could include graphics, data, narrative context, etc.\n\nMake sure the information provided is necessary for answering the question.\n\nUse plain language and clear presentation of data.\n\nCheck for accessibility.\n\n3. Ask a clear, direct, focused question (or state a clear, direct, focused task).\n\nRevise using plain language.\n\nCheck that DOK of prompt is as intended.\n\n4. Write a correct answer and at least three plausible distractors.\n\nMake sure there is only one correct answer option.\n\nStructure all answer choices similarly.\n\nEnsure all answer choices are grammatical matches for the question and do not include key words\n\nAs much as possible, write distractors that can help to identify common misunderstandings.\n\nCheck that all answer choices are approximately the same length.\n\nList answer choices in an appropriate order (for example, phrases from shortest to longest; terms in alphabetical order; numbers from low-to high or high-to-low; etc)\n\nCheck that DOK of overall task is as intended.\n\n5. For each item or set of items, check if instructions are needed. For example, if students will use a diagram to answer multiple questions, include an instruction such as “Use the diagram below to answer questions 3-6.”\n\nThings to avoid:\n\nStem structure of “Which of the following is true (or false)….?”\n\nAnswer choice of “All of the above” or “None of the above”\n\nQuestions or responses with negative wording (Ex: Which of these is not…)\n\nExcess difficulty and anything tricky\n\nWisconsin Center for Education Products &amp; Services\n\n1213 N. Sherman Ave., #322   Madison, WI 53704  877-272-5593   info@wceps.org   www.wceps.org\n\nWisconsin Center for Education Products &amp; Services\n\n1213 N. Sherman Ave., #322   Madison, WI 53704  877-272-5593   WebbAlign@wceps.org   www.webbalign.org",
