    "id": "3c433e34-f739-425f-9ee4-e8ebbc1496b8",
    "title": "NSCA 2023 Content Alignment - Interim Assessment_ANNOTATED SLIDES (Part 1/1)",
    "content": "These slides were presented at the 2023 National Conference on Student Assessment (NCSA) and developed in collaboration with Don Peasley, US Department of Education, Vince Verges, Florida Department of Education, and Art Thacker, HumRRO.  Slides assembled and annotations added by Sara Christopherson, Director of WebbAlign, a program of WCEPS. WCEPS is a non-profit organization affiliated with the University of Wisconsin – Madison with the mission to extend the reach of educational innovations developed at the Wisconsin Center for Education Research (WCER).  The ideas within this presentation build on these (and other) resources, focusing in on alignment considerations.  Specifically, we wanted to think about the ways in which alignment considerations for interim assessments might differ from considerations for summative assessments.  As we discussed this, two different tracks of conversation emerged – one as relates to the focused lens of peer review and another as relates to the broader lens outside of the accountability context.  Under Title I of the ESEA, each State is responsible for implementing a State assessment system that is coherent and consistent within the State. States can use multiple interim assessments for purposes of accountability but they need to meet the same technical criteria as summative assessments.  A major source of validity evidence is evidence based on test content.  Interim assessments are generally understood to be something “in between” summative and formative – administered multiple times a year, within specific time periods, and intended to be both evaluative AND be used to support either predictive or instructional goals.  An interim assessment is different from a summative assessment. Therefore, a validity argument will look slightly different for an interim vs a summative. Although general expectations remain the same -how these are met will look different. For example, there is an expectation for all tests to sample some appropriate breadth or range of knowledge but what that “looks like” depends on the test purpose, design, claims, etc.  NOTE: it is possible for an interim to meet peer review expectations; US ED has approved a through-year test.  Validation depends on test purpose and the inferences you want to make from test scores. Alignment studies provide validity evidence supporting those inferences. Instructional purposes may require stronger validity evidence than predictive or evaluative purposes.  “Content-oriented evidence of validation is at the heart of the process in the educational arena known as alignment….” (AERA/APA/NCME, 2014)  These considerations apply in all cases, but are more variable / more extensive in the context of interim assessments.  Claims related to accountability are fewer than claims related to instructional utility.  These are examples of actual state claims related to statewide summative assessments used for purposes of accountability.  For accountability/peer review, the emphasis is on the relationship between standards and assessments – which is broadly consistent with test purpose, claims, and score interpretation and use.  NOTE: specific states/programs were intentionally not cited b/c the purpose here is to look at the commonalities and generalize about the types of claims across states vs spotlight any particular claim or state.  These are examples of actual claims related to statewide interim assessments. Notice the difference in claims – both between summative vs instructional and also within the set of claims shown on this slide.  NOTE: specific states/programs were intentionally not cited b/c the purpose here is to look at the commonalities and generalize about the types of claims across states vs spotlight any particular claim or state.  This will be addressed in a later slide – but we recognize that diagram is a very simplified model - doesn’t show all parts explicitly - e.g, professional development is not explicit but could be considered as part of each component. Also doesn’t show the intricacies of the relationships between and among each of these broad system components.  In general, interim assessments have more claims than do summative assessments. Therefore, there is a greater scope of alignment considerations, more documents that warrant attention. Compared with common approaches in the context of summative assessments, these additional documents may require different evaluative processes, and different units of analysis are likely necessary to compare different documents.  This quote is from a recent publication from the Center for Assessment, which contrasts statewide summative assessments with assessments intended to inform instruction --noting that the inclusion of instructional purposes is “Fundamentally shifting the way curriculum, instruction, and state assessments interact.”  Overall, alignment expectations for purposes of peer review are similar across test type (interim+ summative), but are likely to differ in what adequate alignment \"looks like.\"  Alignment relationships between standards and assessment(s) depend on test design: e.g. comprehensive interim vs modular:  - Score aggregation  - Score interpretation and reporting (e.g. how to interpret growth)  - Relationship of statewide curriculum with assessment components  - Grain size  - Sufficient domain sampling (as relates to intended inferences) I test length  This gives one simple example of how differences in test purpose and claims may influence what adequate alignment “looks like.”  For any test, we have some intentional breadth of standards we want to address – but what is an “appropriate” breadth depends on the context. While we would expect a summative (or comprehensive interim) to sample from across a full set of standards, we would not expect that from a modular interim assessment. In both (all) cases, we want to consider breadth, but test purpose/claims affect what adequate breadth might look like.  One common goal of interims relates to predictive purposes– e.g. predictive of performance on the summative or of success on college readiness test, or the next interim, etc. Alignment evidence is important to support those claims.  Content alignment considerations would include, for example, relationships between test frameworks -to what extent are they the same or different? -as well as assessment-to-assessment.  There is a little more on this in the literature and we do not get too detailed here.  Framework to frameworks: to what extent are they the same or different? (domain of knowledge, depth, breath, balance / emphases, item type/format, etc) Assessment to assessments, etc.  (Of course also need evidence of statistical relationships; focus here is on content alignment considerations.)  Most interim assessments include a purpose related to instructional utility – “merging the lanes” of assessment with curriculum & instruction.  While interims tend to include fairly strong claims related to instructional utility (as shown on this slide and on slide 13), there’s some variation in the specifics of the claims.  These qualitative differences between/among claims have implications for alignment considerations because different types of evidence support different types of claims. While these are all strong claims that require evidentiary support, we found it was challenging to generalize due to the variation.  A common theme in the resources referenced (see slide 2) is the critical need to attend to claims about instructional utility.  Instructional claims require validation and content alignment is central to this process.  Alignment is a relatively new field. (And the term was not used in The Standards for Educational and Psychological Testing prior to the 2014 edition.)  The idea of alignment was originally conceptualized as holistic, systemic. The idea was that all of the parts of the system had to work together in order to move a standards-based system toward students learning what was intended.  And so (very broadly!) we can think about the standards, curriculum, and assessment as the key parts of the system that need to work together. As noted previously, this graphic is a very simplified model and the different parts of the system are interwoven and there are many components to each of these categories. Etc.  The ultimate goal for alignment is a fully functional SYSTEM.  Correspondingly, the original conceptualization of alignment included 12 criteria organized into 5 categories – including, for example, consideration of pedagogical implications.  Today, people tend to be most familiar with the four bulleted criteria under content focus (shown in bold font on this slide).  Many of the other criteria didn’t gain much traction initially in the context of summative assessments because assessment claims were fairly narrow and content-focused, such that some criteria were just not relevant to the assessment claims. For example, dispositional consonance was not relevant to tests that did not intend to assess disposition.  In the time since this was conceptualized, some of these ideas have been incorporated systemically (e.g. we now have vertical articulation of standards across grades)  Similarly, earlier versions of standards did not necessarily emphasize the structure of knowledge. More recently, however, academic standards across disciplines have incorporated epistemic practices with disciplinary content – NGSS and Framework-based standards are perhaps the most discussed, but this applies to math, literacy, and social studies standards as well. The disciplinary structure of knowledge has become an important part of our academic standards and also, correspondingly, many assessment claims.  In summary: Our system is anchored in high academic standards for all students, and the ultimate goal is for students to learn what is intended by these standards. Statewide assessments are based “on the premise that student outcomes will be improved through creating coherent systems” of all the different pieces of the puzzle. While alignment is sometimes discussed as if it is limited to the specific aspects that are explicit requirements in the context of federal peer review, it is important to remember the underlying rationale for a consideration of alignment relates to a wholistic and systemic perspective. The big idea is that within a standards-based system, we need all the different parts to work together to promote students learning what is intended.  Because an interim assessment has more claims than a summative assessment, and these claims either explicitly reference or at least implicitly invoke a broader scope of system components – including within curriculum and instruction -- there are many more alignment relationships that need to be considered.  The subtitle of this presentation is “And why should we care?” One reason we care about content alignment for interim assessments is because content alignment evidence helps to build an argument that test score interpretation as relates to a variety of instructional claims is plausible…which bears on fairness. For interim assessment contexts, important decisions about individual students depend on test performance.  For example, if a test is intended to “determine next steps for instruction” or to “target instruction to students’ individual needs” or to “identify specific student strengths and weaknesses” or as relates to instructional guidance, etc – these are very “ambitious” claims and it is important to verify that there is evidence based on test content, as relates to the other components of the system - standards, curriculum, instruction, etc. In this context, it is important to recognize that “evidence based on test content” is not delimited to just the assessment items or tasks, but also includes the score reports and resulting explicit inferences about student learning and corresponding instructional guidance, etc.  Again, the specifics of the types of evidence depend on the specifics of the claims, which vary quite a lot by program.  Overall, one key difference for the context of interim alignment considerations (compared with summative) is that instructional utility claims explicitly reference or at least implicitly invoke a broader scope of system components. A keystone component that merits thorough attention is any derivative assessment targets (e.g., ALDs or PLDs or skills statements or other set of targets that are derived from the standards). While this is absolutely true in the summative context, too, it is more directly linked to interim claims and is consequential to student opportunity to learn. This adds a critical link into the chain of reasoning – and it needs to be a connected link. If not, it is possible to have an assessment that is closely aligned to a set of derivative standards, but (at least some of) the derivative standards are unlinked from the grade level standards themselves. (And this indeed happens.)  Another part of why acceptable alignment “looks different” is because in the context of claims related to instructional utility, we want shorter inferential leaps. We may accept longer inferential leaps in the context of the more general claims made with summative assessments but we would expect shorter inferential leaps in the context of the fairly specific claims made with interims as relates to instructional utility. More ambitious claims require more evidence ….  Some additional documents for consideration in alignment analysis are listed on this slide but not intended as a comprehensive list.  The overall rationale / hypothesis is that interim assessments result in improved student outcomes. For this hypothesis to play out, we need tight connections between the assessment and the curriculum components and the standards. Importantly, research suggests that none of these should be assumed.  This graphic is intended to represent the need for connection between and among the many different “puzzle pieces” that are related to standards, curriculum, and assessment– and that all components need to be undergirded by a common learning model.  There are many additional important puzzle pieces that are outside of content alignment (e.g., school culture, teacher preparation, teacher beliefs, student attendance, etc. – and many, many more) but there are also many critical content alignment relationships between and among all of these different components from the perspective of systemic coherence in the service of positive student outcomes.  Back to why this matters – critically: The idea with many interim assessments is that test results can be used to make pedagogical decisions that are consequential to students’ opportunity to learn (OTL). Therefore, the degree of alignment within this system directly affects students’ opportunity to learn; the “improved outcomes” and instructional utility hinge on the pieces of the system working together in the service of students learning what they are intended to learn, as defined by a set of academic standards. It is possible that a system facilitates and/or accelerates learning. It is also possible that a system sets up obstacles and/or barriers that impede OTL.  As summarized on this slide: an interim assessment is intended to “help educators know what students are ready to learn next” and this is “data driven” based on scores that are used to make decision rules, and everything is grounded in explicit or implicit underlying ideas about the nature of learning. For this approach to have the potential to positively affect OTL, we need very tight alignment connections between and among the assessment and the curriculum components and the standards, and overall concurrence with the underlying learning model.  This graphic is an attempt to summarize key alignment relationships between the pieces of system that relate to the alignment question shown on the slide. Specifically, this graphic shows components of the system that are under editorial control. If alignment is inadequate, one or more of these components can be adjusted to improve alignment.  For example, the relationship between expectations (academic standards) and an interim assessment must concur with the scope and sequence of a grade/course and with the content of the curriculum. If a gap is identified, it could be resolved by an adjustment to the assessment—or to the scope and sequence and/or the content of the curriculum.  This graphic does NOT show what teachers actually do – we don’t have editorial control over how teachers actually use any guidance. But the components shown on the slide at least set up the capacity for a system to work as intended. For example, if we say “here’s what should happen next for this student” — this guidance should be defensible. There should be some underlying rationale, and that underlying rationale should be grounded in ideas about the nature of learning in the particular content area. All of this information should be documented and validated. We should be able to evaluate the degree of alignment between and among documentation that clearly  describes each component of the system.  There are additional important alignment relationships that are NOT represented on this diagram –for example, instructional guidance needs to have coherence with the instructional approaches and strategies used in the curriculum and these should all be consistent with the instructional approaches and strategies implied by the standards. (There should probably be many more arrows here connecting everything together.)  It is incumbent on us, as testing and education professionals, to interrogate claims of instructional utility and evaluate the coherence and content alignment of these puzzle pieces. Results of content alignment analyses can yield information that can be used to inform adjustments and help to ensure that our tests are doing what we intend them to do.  In one of the biggest case studies of interim assessments, Goertz, Oláh and Riggan (2009, then at the University of Pennsylvania; research conducted via the Consortium for Policy Research in Education) looked at two different interim tests for two different grades. They conducted content alignment analyses of these tests and found that there was a disconnect for the vast majority of items: the content of the items did not concur with the content of the corresponding inferences about student learning. Further, the instructional guidance for math was focused on procedural errors and very limited guidance or insights were related to conceptual understanding of math. Consequently, the content of the instructional guidance was disconnected in terms of the depth and structure of knowledge that was explicit within the academic standards. There are multiple layers of intertwined content alignment relationships that are consequential to the overall goal – for systemic coherence in support of student learning outcomes.  This example underscores the importance of a holistic perspective of alignment: there were content alignment disconnects between the different system components, including that the resulting instructional guidance did not concur with the teaching practices and strategies implied by the standards.  We know that statewide assessments influence what happens in the classroom (in both intended and unintended ways). In the context of interim assessments, the idea is to very purposefully affect instruction on a more detailed level. While we can likely agree that there needs to be some sort of connection between curriculum and assessment, it is important to clearly and specifically define the connections for each particular context. This slide shows a few examples of important and specific research questions related to interim assessments for which content alignment evidence is important –  *See slide 34 for additional comment on the last question on this slide.  Again, the premise of our standards-based system is that setting clear and high learning expectations for all students will lead to improved learning outcomes.  Our academic standards are grounded in underlying learning models appropriate to the content area. The importance of consistency in the underlying learning model is not a new idea and it applies to all assessments. However, in context of interim assessment and claims related to instructional utility, underlying ideas about learning are inherent to the claims, so it becomes more important to make ideas about the nature of learning explicit and demonstrate coherence between and among the parts.  For example, one key underlying idea about learning is that we can categorize student achievement by levels and / or we can categorize material and educational content by level. And we define what these levels are in ways that make lower levels qualitatively different from higher levels. They way we conceptualize these levels, and how they relate to learning, affects the types of assessment tasks we develop, the inferences we make based on student performance, and the resulting instructional guidance provided. An important content alignment question relates to how any identified levels relate to the academic expectations (standards) for all students (as well as how the instructional guidance provided relates back to all other parts of the system. The quotes on this slide are intended to represent important example topics for consideration:  First, it has been observed that instructional modifications for low-scoring students tend to be remedial. In contrast, evidence suggests that “A challenging curriculum is more effective in improving students’ learning than a low-level remedial curriculum” and that learning is better achieved by “placing low achievers in advanced programmes rather than lowering the expectations” (OECD, 2012 - Equity and Quality in Education: Supporting Disadvantaged Students and Schools). Strong alignment between the standards, assessment tasks, and instructional guidance –that maintains high expectations– can help to promote learning. Content alignment analysis can help to identify any instances of disconnect between expectations, tasks, and guidance that might limit OTL. Note, also, that this commonly observed issue relates to underlying ideas about learning. If stakeholders believe that low achieving students  are not ”ready” for complex learning opportunities, then these students may be tracked in ways that limit their OTL. Assessment systems may be developed that reinforce these underlying ideas, such that low scorers are tracked toward lowered learning expectations (e.g. via remedial instruction) and have limited opportunity to demonstrate learning (e.g. via lower-level assessments).  It is important that all students, including low-scoring students are provided access to learning as relates to the grade-level expectations within academic standards – which are intended for all students -- and the opportunity to demonstrate what they know and are able to do. Strong content alignment can help to ensure that all students, including low scoring students, are provided the opportunity to show what they know as relates to grade level expectations and receive instructional guidance that addresses the complexity and scope and structure of knowledge of the grade-level expectations.  While we know that the types of specific feedback provided via formative assessment supports learning (e.g. as found by Black and Wiliam’s analyses) we also know that tracking students as “lower level” vs “higher level” affects OTL and exacerbates existing inequities in education systems – e.g.:  “One robust and common finding is that early differentiation of students’ curricula tends to be associated with larger socio-economic inequalities, with no corresponding gains in average performance. Both cross-country and country-specific studies have highlighted the negative impact of ability tracking on educational socio-economic inequalities (for cross-country evidence, see OECD, 2004, 2007a, Schutz et al, 2005, Hanushek and Woessmann, 2005, Sutherland and Price, 2007, Duru-Bellat and Suchaut, 2005, Amermuller, 2005; for country-specific evidence, see e.g. Bauer and Riphahn, 2006, Pekkarinen et al., 2006, Holmlund, 2006, and Bratberg et al., 2005).” (OECD, 2010)  A critical component of content alignment for interim assessments, therefore, relates to the alignment relationship between the specific instructional guidance and the other parts of the system.  Overall, interim assessments have more claims (compared with summative assessments) – and more claims demand more evidence.  For purposes of peer review, evidence is focused on the use of scores for accountability.  Outside of peer review, claims for instructional use are much more extensive, and these claims still need to be supported with evidence, per professional standards. The validity argument starts to get more involved, because of the merging of lanes of statewide assessment with curriculum. Multiple types of evidence are important, but much evidence relates to content alignment relationships between and among the many components of the system.  References and Bibliography  Causa, 0. and Chapuis, C. (2010) Equity in Student Achievement Across OECD Countries: An Investigation of the Role of Policies. OECD Journal: Economic Studies Vol. 2010/1  Crane, E. (2010) Building an Interim Assessment System: A Workbook for School Districts. Council of Chief State School Officers. Available May 2023 at hues/Mes.eic.ea.govtuutexuED528642.pdf  Dadey, N. and Gong, B. (2017) Using Interim Assessments in Place of Summative Assessments? Consideration of an ESSA Option. Council of Chief State School Officers. Available May 2023 at hues/eessooasitesdetaultvtileszoi7-12ASR ESSA Interim Considerations-April pdf  Dadey, N., Evans, C.M., and Lorie, W (2023). Through-Year Assessment: Ten Key Considerations. Center for Assessment. Available May 2023 athuesww.naea.oawe­contanu'uploads/202303Ten-Key-Considerations-Through-Year-Assessment-Reporl-March2023-F pd[  Goertz, M.E., Olah, L.N., and Riggan, M. (2010) From Testing to Teaching: The Use of Interim Assessments in Classroom Instruction. Consortium for Policy Research in Education (CPRE) CPRE Research Report #RR-65  Herman, Joan (2017) Interim Assessments in Brief. National Center for Research on Evaluation, Standards, and Student Testing (CRESST). The Center on Standards & Assessment Implementation. WestEd. Available May 2023 at https://compcenternetwork.org/sites/default/files/archive/lnterimAssessmentsBrief.pdf  Kane, M. T. (2013) Validating the Interpretations and Uses of Test Scores. Journal of Educational Measurement 50(1), 1-73  Kannan, P. Bryant, A.D., Shao, S., and Wylie, E.C. (2021) Identifying Teachers' Needs for Results From Interim Unit Assessments. ETS Research Report 21-08  Marchitello, M. and Wilhelm, M. (2014) The Cognitive Science Behind the Common Core. Center for American Progress.  Perie, M;, Marion, S., Gong, B., and Wurtzel, J., (2007}The Role of Interim Assessments in a Comprehensive Assessment System. The Aspen Institute. Available May 2023 at https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED551318.pdf  Webb, N. L. (1997). Criteria for alignment of expectations and assessments In mathematics and mathematics education. Council of Chief State School Officers and National Institute for Mathematics Education Research Monograph No. 6. Madison: University of Wisconsin, Wisconsin Center for Education Research.  OECD (2012), Equity and Quality in Education: Supporting Disadvantaged Students and Schools, OECD Publishing. http://dx.doi.org/10.1787/9789264130852-en  WIDA (2018) Choosing an Interim Assessments: Guidelines for Stakeholders. Available May 2023 at https://wlda.wisc.edu/sites/default/files/resource/Choosing-Interim-Assessment-Guidelines­Stakeholders.pdf",
