  {
    "id": "8cb9765f-edbb-40d0-8a8e-4e0604eeabb3",
    "title": "A directory of the Proposal Submission Requirements is given below. (Part 6/7)",
    "content": "Dr. Ryan Rumpf, Ed.D Educational Leadership, is Lead CARE Coach at WCEPS. Dr. Rumpf is an educational leader whose work focuses on comprehensive and effective structures, systems and strategies for multilingual learners. He currently serves as the principal of an elementary newcomer program in the Midwest and as an adjunct professor for Missouri Western State University where he teaches courses on laws, policies and programs for multilingual learners.\n\nDr. Jessica Costa, Ph.D. is CARE Coach at WCEPS. Dr. Costa has worked for over 2 decades teaching students a love of language and learning or designing and facilitating professional learning and coaching nationally and internationally. She brings a wealth of knowledge from her experiences as a National Board Certified teacher, a leader at the state, district and school levels, a data coach and from her research focused on school leadership preparation.\n\nTamisha Sampson, M.A. Educational Leadership, is a CARE Coach at WCEPS. Mrs. Sampson has over 20 years of education leadership experience having served as building principal and Director of Multilingual Learner programs at Montgomery County Public Schools outside Washington DC in Maryland.\n\nMatt Messinger, Executive Director for WCEPS, along with WCEPS business office staff, will oversee all fiscal and business matters for administering the project, coordinate contracting, and provide support for logistics. WCEPS has successfully provided business and administrative support for program partnerships  since its inception in 2011.\n\nD. WCEPS’ WebbAlign and CARE Coaching offer innovative professional learning programs designed to equip school and district educators with the knowledge and skills to create meaningful educational opportunities for all students. The CARE Coaching approach fosters dynamic and engaging learning that is Collaborative, Actionable, Responsive, and Empowering. Through CARE Coaching, we offer a wide range of high-interest, interactive, and customizable continuous learning workshops, job-embedded coaching and consulting that provides tailored technical assistance and insightful program evaluations. WebbAlign brings decades of experience in content analyses of curricular materials and assessments in support of high-quality systems. Our professional learning topics include Webb’s DOK framework, content alignment analysis, assessment literacy, instructional design, lesson development, item writing considerations, classroom assessment, and evaluation of educational materials.\n\nE. Our programs have worked successfully with dozens of schools across the country and look forward to working with Denver Public Schools. References are provided as an attachment to this proposal.\n\nF. WCEPS is not involved in any current litigation, outstanding judgments, or liens.\n\nG. No licenses are required to perform the services listed in the statement of work.\n\nReferences\n\n​​American Educational Research Association, American Psychological Association, &amp; National Council on Measurement in Education (Eds.). (2014). Standards for educational and psychological testing.\n\nCausa, O. and Chapuis, C. (2010) Equity in Student Achievement Across OECD Countries: An Investigation of the Role of Policies. OECD Journal: Economic Studies Vol. 2010/1\n\nChristopherson, S. Peasley, D., Thacker, A., and Verges, V.  (2023) How Do Alignment Considerations Differ for Interim vs Summative Assessments? (And Why Should We Care?) Presented at National Conference for Student Assessment (NCSA) 2023\n\nChristopherson, S. (2023) Differentiating Difficulty from Complexity to Support Intended Uses of Learning Progressions. Paper presented at the 2023 Annual meeting of the National Council on Measurement in Education\n\nChristopherson, S. (2024) Complexity and Difficulty in a Coherent Standards-Based Education System. Paper presented at the National Conference on Student Assessment (NCSA) 2024\n\nCrane, E. (2010) Building an Interim Assessment System: A Workbook for School Districts. Council of Chief State School Officers. Available May 2023 at https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED528642.pdf\n\nDenver Public School (2022). DPS Thrives: A Strategic Roadmap to the Denver Public Schools Experience\n\nGoertz, M.E., Oláh, L.N., and Riggan, M. (2010) From Testing to Teaching: The Use of Interim Assessments in Classroom Instruction. Consortium for Policy Research in Education (CPRE) CPRE Research Report #RR-65\n\nHerman, Joan (2017) Interim Assessments in Brief. National Center for Research on Evaluation, Standards, and Student Testing (CRESST). The Center on Standards &amp; Assessment Implementation. WestEd. Available May 2023 at https://compcenternetwork.org/sites/default/files/archive/InterimAssessmentsBrief.pdf\n\nKane, M.T. (2013) Validating the Interpretations and Uses of Test Scores. Journal of Educational Measurement 50(1), 1-73",
