  {
    "id": "206c7780-1cd6-4b5c-958c-00f740a52809",
    "title": "A directory of the Proposal Submission Requirements is given below. (Part 3/7)",
    "content": "By equipping teachers with tools and strategies to differentiate difficulty from complexity–a key lever for positive shifts in teacher practice and a critical intervention to help disrupt remedial practices–our professional learning sessions help teachers to make appropriate and productive instructional decisions, focus and prioritize lesson time, and, overall, to better understand the different types of challenges students may face as learning unfolds. These high-leverage tools and strategies support advanced learners as well as populations identified in DPS’ Strategic Roadmap as needing additional support, evidenced by lagging 4-year graduation rates, including multilingual learners and students with disabilities.\n\nWith decades of experience working with educators across the country (and beyond!), we are attuned to common misconceptions and obstacles that can interfere with the attainment of shared goals. For example, when educators differentiate students’ ability to engage with the cognitive demands of the academic standards from the need for linguistic supports for instruction in English, they are better able to provide all students with the opportunity to learn. With extensive WIDA, ACCESS, and general bilingual learning expertise, our CARE Coaches and professional learning offerings provide key support to DPS, with multilingual learners comprising nearly 40% of the student population (DPS, 2022). Through supported interpretation of assessment data, professional development, and messaging, our program offerings help disrupt counterproductive but persistent patterns to help ensure that all students, including low-scoring students, are provided access to engaging learning opportunities aligned with the grade-level expectations within academic standards and receive instructional guidance that addresses the complexity, scope, and structure of knowledge of the grade-level expectations.\n\nWith DPS input, we will finalize professional learning and coaching schedules, participants, and topics. DPS anticipates the core group of teachers to include between 90-175 Kinder-8th grade educators.\n\nImplementation and Progress Monitoring\n\nProfessional learning and embedded coaching will be provided according to the agreed upon schedule and focused on specific prioritized areas of need. With input from the Collaborative Director and other DPS stakeholders, we will offer a range of services including traditional professional development workshops, job-embedded coaching, data analyses, curricular alignment, and lesson planning focused on the district’s curriculum and assessment resources. Our offerings include high-impact and culturally responsive instructional strategies that reach all students, including those acquiring English. With the Collaborative Director’s input and approval, we will customize our workshops, tools and resources based on the overall focused goals as well as the unique needs of individual buildings, administrators, and teachers to provide targeted learning opportunities.\n\nBased on currently available information, we anticipate around 10 professional learning touch-points over the course of the year for each school, the vast majority of which will be in-person. Following workshops, WCEPS will guide DPS leaders in Learning Walks, non-evaluative walkthroughs to monitor implementation and develop productive next steps. Table 2 includes a budget based on a proposed scope of touch-points. Section D includes a proposed schedule and implementation plan. Finalized plans require input from DPS. Upon contract award, the WCEPS team will engage with DPS to finalize the schedule and corresponding budget.\n\nOver the course of the 2025-2026 school year, our team will assist DPS in developing data-driven instructional strategies and execution plans, monitor progress of those plans, and make necessary data-informed adjustments as needed. WCEPS will guide five progress-monitoring data cycles per school focused on student outcomes and instructional effectiveness. Each data cycle will be explicitly tied to the targeted professional learning identified through the CALL survey and collaboration with the Collaborative Director and Instructional Leadership Team. An end-of-school-year re-administration of the CALL Survey will provide key insights about areas of growth and areas of growth in subsequent years.\n\nAs a non-profit organization affiliated with the University of Wisconsin-Madison, a world-class research institution, WCEPS is committed to high-quality, transparent research.\n\nSection C – Pricing\n\nTable 2 provides overall pricing estimates for Denver Public Schools, inclusive of project management, weekly online meetings, and travel costs. WCEPS welcomes DPS input on the finalized scope of work and the resulting total budget will be adjusted to reflect the agreed upon collaborative work for the 2025–2026 school year .\n\nTable 2. DPS / WCEPS Collaboration: Estimated Pricing for SY2025–2026\n\nCore Focus\n\nDescription\n\nCost",
