  {
    "id": "e4af9975-976c-4e81-822d-387075bb9fe5",
    "title": "A directory of the Proposal Submission Requirements is given below. (Part 1/7)",
    "content": "The Wisconsin Center for Education Products and Services (WCEPS) is pleased to provide a proposal in response to Denver Public Schools’ (DPS) Request for Proposal BD 2573. Our programs provide a constellation of critical expertise of great value for advancing DPS’ goals. This includes expertise with systemic analysis of needs, content alignment analysis, appropriate interpretation of assessment data to inform productive and constructive uses, and professional learning for K-12 audiences focused on fostering coherent systems that promote rigorous learning opportunities for all students. Our programs bring decades of experience in key areas that resonate strongly with DPS’ Strategic Roadmap and Instructional Framework.\n\nA directory of the Proposal Submission Requirements is given below.\n\n1. Organization Overview – Provided in Section E: Supplier Profile\n\n2. Approach &amp; Methodology – Provided in Section B: Management Summary\n\n3. Staffing Plan – Provided in Section E: Supplier Profile\n\n4. Work Plan &amp; Timeline – Provided in Section D: Project Schedule and Implementation Plan\n\n5. Budget Proposal – Provided in Section C: Pricing\n\n6. References – Attached to this proposal\n\nSection B – Management Summary\n\nThe Wisconsin Center for Education Products and Services (WCEPS), a non-profit organization affiliated with the University of Wisconsin-Madison, will support Denver Public Schools (DPS) with the goals outlined in RFP BD2573 toward accelerating student outcomes in 7–9 underperforming schools under the direction of the Collaborative Director. The first year of work will include the following main components and associated deliverables (Table 1):\n\nTable 1. DPS / WCEPS Collaboration: High-Level Summary of Components and Deliverables\n\nComponent\n\nDeliverable\n\nInitial needs assessment and goal-setting in collaboration with Instructional Leadership Teams and Principals\n\nCritical analyses of assessment data with consideration of alignment evidence\n\nReport summarizing key findings from discussions with DPS, CALL survey,  and/or analyses of assessments and assessment data.\n\nStrategic selection of professional development and CARE coaching to meet identified needs\n\nFinalized implementation plan, including coaching schedules and PD calendars\n\nImplementation of ongoing professional learning and CARE coaching support for key DPS teams\n\nOngoing monitoring of progress and identification of emergent needs.\n\nDocumentation of coaching sessions, progress monitoring reports, and instructional recommendations\n\nReport summarizing key findings from progress monitoring and recommendations for continuous improvement.\n\nWCEPS’ core expertise with alignment, Depth of Knowledge (DOK), and multilingual learners in K-12 education is unparalleled and provides critical strategic support for DPS’goals for educators to plan, internalize, and implement high-quality instruction that is culturally and linguistically sustaining and aligned with rigorous expectations. As the home of the concept of educational alignment and the home and steward of the DOK language system, we can help educators build a shared understanding of the meaning of district assessment data along with its appropriate (and inappropriate) uses. Equipped with a shared language, educators and district stakeholders are empowered to support all learners in engaging, rigorous learning opportunities. Our team also has deep experience with WIDA and WIDA ACCESS and with asset-based approaches to supporting multilingual learners. The CARE Coaching approach fosters dynamic and engaging learning that is Collaborative, Actionable, Responsive, and Empowering. To best support DPS with continuous improvement, our team will engage DPS leadership to reflect on the outcomes of the first year of collaboration and to determine the best next steps to advance DPS goals.\n\nInitial Needs Assessment and Goal-Setting\n\nOur leadership team will work closely with the Collaborative Director on initial needs assessment and goal-setting, grounded in the anchors, beliefs, and key drivers of the DPS Instructional Framework (2024). Goals and priorities will be identified using existing DPS data and expertise. If helpful, we can administer a relevant version of the Comprehensive Assessment of Leadership for Learning (CALL) survey to support district leadership in the identification of key goals and priorities. The CALL tool helps school and district leaders to identify key strengths and areas of growth in individual buildings and allows leaders to create and download various reports that summarize the findings and recommendations for improvement. Overall, our team will provide focused support informed by multiple sources of data and input that may include:\n\nDPS’ existing identified priorities and discussions with district leadership\n\nResults of a customized CALL survey\n\nDenver Public Schools’ interim and unit assessment data\n\nFigure 1. Alignment supports student learning.",
