  {
    "id": "337e428c-00b9-414c-bd32-fa4c364167a4",
    "title": "A directory of the Proposal Submission Requirements is given below. (Part 2/7)",
    "content": "The use of assessment data to make meaningful decisions that are consequential to students’ opportunity to learn depends critically on alignment between the content of the assessment and the content of Colorado’s Academic Standards as well as overall system coherence (Figures 1 and 2). DPS’ successful use of interim and unit assessment data to support improved student outcomes must attend to coherence: improved student outcomes hinge on the pieces of the DPS system working together in the service of students learning what they are intended to learn, as defined by Colorado’s rigorous college- and career-ready expectations.  It is additionally important to extend alignment considerations to the resulting pedagogical decisions implemented, as well as all other components of the DPS system, including the professional learning provided. Findings from one of the biggest studies of interim assessments (Goertz, Oláh and Riggan, 2009; research conducted via the Consortium for Policy Research in Education) indicates that evidence of content alignment should be interrogated and not assumed.\n\nUse of assessment data to inform instructional priorities and practice requires consideration of multiple “puzzle pieces” as shown in Figure 2. WCEPS leadership will discuss current use(s) of interim and unit assessment data with DPS to determine next steps for the district, including determining if alignment analyses of district materials and practices are warranted. Content alignment evidence delimits test score interpretations and uses that can be considered plausible, and bears on test fairness (Traynor and Christopherson, 2024; AERA, APA, &amp; NCME, 2014). Depending on available information, DPS may wish to pursue content validation studies to help inform the district’s use of assessment data. If collaborative discussions identify a need for alignment analyses, our team will discuss pathways for consideration. If possible, we recommend including DPS educators and other stakeholders in the alignment analyses as these are rich sources of professional learning.\n\nFigure 2. Underlying System Components that Support Use of Interim Assessments to Improve Student Outcomes (Slide presented at National Conference on Student Assessment, 2024)\n\nThe initial needs assessment and goal-setting work will also include an inventory of existing DPS systems and tools to identify those that are working as DPS expects as well as those that might need adjustment or replacement. The WCEPS team is glad to support continued use of DPS tools and practices, incorporating new tools and practices as needed.\n\nStrategic and Data-Driven Professional Development and Coaching Planning\n\nBased on the takeaways from our initial discussions and data analyses, WCEPS leadership and CARE Coaching leads will work with the Collaborative Director to focus plans for the 2025-2026 school year for identified schools that address clearly defined goals. Plans will emphasize strengthening system coherence, including thoughtful, appropriate, and meaningful use of interim and unit assessment data, curriculum implementation and instruction, and associated district leadership support.\n\nOur programs will help to operationalize DPS’ commitment to on-grade learning for all students, including students with low scores on interim and unit assessments. This DPS commitment is consistent with evidence-based approaches to accelerating student learning: providing challenging learning opportunities is more effective in improving students’ learning than “low-level” remediation or the lowering of expectations (OECD, 2012). However, research suggests that teachers tend to provide remedial instruction in response to low interim scores (ibid). Our professional learning offerings help to disrupt ideas that low-scoring students need remedial instruction and instead reinforces DPS’ evidence-based perspective that access to engaging, grade-level work supports accelerated learning (e.g., OECD, 2012 - Equity and Quality in Education: Supporting Disadvantaged Students and Schools).\n\nDifferentiating difficulty and complexity helps promote coherence in a standards-based system. How teachers and other stakeholders envision academic expectations, learning, and performance as relates to difficulty and complexity influences how teachers interpret and use the results of assessments. While all students are to be provided access to the full complexity of cognitive engagement as expressed in the standards, some aspects of these expectations may be more difficult for some individuals than for others. Attention to complexity as distinct from difficulty can help ensure all students, across the full range of interim and unit assessment scores, are provided access to learning opportunities consistent with the complexity of engagement specified in the academic standards for all students.",
