    "id": "99d6f801-41e4-45a4-ba3c-538f521af63a",
    "title": "Evaluation Form: Initial WebbAlign PD Session Jan 8 2020:",
    "content": "Title: Evaluation Form: Initial WebbAlign PD Session Jan 8 2020:\n\nContent: Evaluation Form: Initial WebbAlign PD Session Jan 8 2020:\n\nComplexity and DOK (40 Responses)\n\n2What was the most useful or meaningful part of today's work for you? Why?\n\nRealizing that DOK is an alignment and the complexity is critical.\n\nUnderstanding that DOK is not a progression\n\nGiving levels to classroom tasks, discussion of reasoning behind placement allowed for a greater understanding and analysis of levels\n\nsorting the learning tasks\n\nshowing how if students don't have basic skills they still can still think critically\n\nThe card sort.\n\nThe sorting activity. It opened my eyes and deepened my understanding.\n\nDOK is a language system not an instructional strategy.\n\nthe definition of DOK and the exercise about identifying the DOK levels- eye opening that we need to be consistent moving forward. Also how to focus on how to look for different DOK levels in the class.\n\nGoing through the sorting activity. It gave us the opportunity to think about what each level could look like.\n\nDistinguishing the levels of DOK\n\nThe explanation of DOK and the examples of DOK levels sort\n\nReviewing DOK to clear misconceptions and receive new ideas for application\n\nAll receiving the same message\n\nGroup work that allowed us to sort out the sample cards into the different DOK levels.\n\nActivities as they were engaging and allowed us to collaborate with peers.\n\nClarification of what DOK is and is not. Activity of levels was a great learning experience.\n\nClarification on DOK. I have been using DOK for years, but mostly replied on the wheel with verbs. I now know that is not the correct way to evaluate the complexity.\n\nPeer discussions because we get to process the information, together.\n\nSee above... it got to the heard of what level and why it was considered that.\n\nThe idea that DOK is a tool and not a new initiative, and that all levels are important and relevant to learning.\n\nThe definition-understanding the difference between DOK and Blooms.\n\nall\n\nThat DOK isn't a progression. The standards are the basis for DOK.\n\nSorting the tasks into categories really helped me to think through what makes a task complex.\n\nThe card activity because it was interactive\n\nGoing through examples and having discussions on the examples\n\nDiscussing/sharing out ideas with staff members\n\nKnowing the differences between DOK and the Blooms construct.\n\nLooking at the DOK Levels differently than the former DOK Wheel that I was used to using.\n\nLooking at examples in relation to standards\n\nWorking in groups to categorize the activities into DOK levels.\n\nHearing that DOK is not a progression, hearing that DOK is not an instructional strategy, hearing that DOK is really a categorization of what we do already and that we should try to at least have some tasks be Level 3 or 4\n\nSorting the Content Complexity\n\nthe overall presentation was very good, the most meaningful was understanding the alignment concept\n\nThe DOK level sorting activity\n\nThe sorting activity because we were able to put real experiences to our own knowledge",
