    "title": "Defining depth, breadth, and cognitive complexity: What do they mean and why does it matter? (Part 1/1)",
    "content": "Defining depth, breadth, and cognitive complexity: What do they mean and why does it matter?  Sara Christopherson, Director of WebbAlign  June, 2025  WebbAlign is a program of the Wisconsin Center for Education Products and Services (WCEPS). WCEPS is a non-profit organization affiliated with the University of Wisconsin – Madison.  www.webbalign.org  A perennial debate within education relates to the “well-worn topic” (Rhees, 1902) of the appropriate balance between the “two most elastic-sided metaphors” (Stewart, 1956) of depth and breadth. Typically, these concepts are considered in relation to one another: depth vs breadth. For example, in 1902, Rush Rhees, President of the University of Rochester, spoke about this topic to an audience of school principals in New York State and lamented an educational approach that would include “a smattering of many things with scant appreciation of their significance.” Over 100 years later, the development of the Common Core State Standards promised “fewer, clearer, higher” expectations—i.e., less breadth, greater depth. A Framework for K12 Science Education (2012) argued similarly, referencing the “oft-mentioned mile wide and inch deep” curriculum. In a standards-based education system, the academic standards operationally define the appropriate depth and breadth for each subject area, based on the thinking of the times.  In general, “breadth” refers to scope of content. There are no natural boundaries to broad educational topics such as mathematics or fine arts. One purpose of academic standards is to define and delimit the breadth of content included in a school year or course. Purposeful decisions are made to select what content is included and what is omitted.  In general, “depth” is used as a synonym for “cognitive complexity.” The term cognitive complexity is used to describe the type of domain-specific thinking or degree of processing of concepts and skills that is required by students’ interactions with academic content. This qualitative attribute of curricular materials is also referred to as “rigor,” “cognitive demand,” or just “complexity” (Polikoff, Porter, & Smithson, 2011, CCSSO, 2014). A core purpose of academic standards is to specify expectations for particular types of cognitive engagement with academic content. For example, instead of a system in which students learn “isolated facts and skills” a standards-based system was intended to set  expectations for specific types of valued cognitive engagement, such as those requiring “depth of knowledge [and] complex thinking” (Smith and O’Day, 1988). Although slightly different language has been used over the years, each iteration of standards frameworks asserts the value of the “depth,” “rigor,” “high-level cognitive demands,” etc. of the expectations. Today, academic standards generally incorporate a situative perspective on learning in which the expectations for complex cognitive engagement emphasize participation in aspects of epistemic practices and authentic ways of domain-specific knowing.  A coherent standards-based education system requires a common interpretation of the standards. Inconsistent interpretations hamper the goals of a standards-based system (Dickinson, et. al., 2020). Breadth may be communicated through the standards’ structure. Content topics are organized into strands, clusters, domains, and other conceptual categories. Although the field of education has long agreed on the importance of “depth,” interpretations of what this means can vary, depending on many factors, including the underlying perspective on learning, including whether learning is envisioned as a linear or nonlinear process, and how student ability / capacity is envisioned. Different interpretations may also focus on different units of analysis. For example, instead of a focus on the type of thinking or degree of processing of concepts and skills, some interpretations focus on the complexity of a topic or problem itself, the complexity of the related learning processes, or the complexity of the student response. Some interpretations differentiate complexity from difficulty (and from other factors such as sophistication of thinking) while other interpretations do not.  A premise of our standards-based education system is that we can specify—through the language of the standards—the types of cognitive engagement with academic content that we want students to have the opportunity to learn. Then, by interpreting the language of the standards, educators and other stakeholders can all “unlock” the same information about the intended complexity of engagement expected. In practice, however, perceptions of complexity are based on conceptual constructs that vary, and even expert educators bring different ideas to the table. To operationalize the intended cognitive engagement as expressed in the standards, educators, content developers, and other stakeholders benefit from the use of practical frameworks and tools (Stein et al., 2016). A variety of tools have been developed to help guide interpretations of cognitive demands for different purposes across different contexts. Some of these tools separate cognitive demands into different categories while others use a taxonomic or hierarchical structure. Some tools are content agnostic while others recognize inherent differences in what complexity “looks  like” between and among disciplines. Tools vary in the extent that they are perceived to be useful and the extent to which they can be used reliably. They vary in their conceptual underpinnings and the extent to which those conceptual underpinnings resonate with modern learning theory.  Assessment  In a standards-based education system, the purpose of educational assessment is to determine if the expectations within the academic content standards have been attained. A single assessment is not likely to address the full depth and breadth of the content standards. Test developers and decisionmakers use a variety of processes to decide what constitutes sufficiency for a particular context. Decisions about appropriate breadth are influenced by multiple factors, including the political and technological landscape as well as practical constraints. The resulting blueprints and other assessment framework materials reflect purposeful decisions about the breadth of content that will be included on any particular assessment.  In a coherent standards-based system, an assessment of student proficiency is expected to elicit work that is as cognitively demanding as the expectations in the corresponding standards or other assessment targets. In other words, the set of academic standards that are carefully selected for inclusion on an assessment are generally expected to provide the referent for the “depth” as represented on an assessment. Alternatively, a rule that is external to the standards could be applied to define the “depth” addressed on an assessment. In general, however, states intend for their academic standards to guide teaching and learning. If an assessment uses a rule that is external to the standards, which are intended to guide teaching and learning, careful consideration of the implications should be made and a thorough rationale should be provided.  Federal expectations for alignment between statewide assessments and academic standards are often distilled into the phrase “depth and breadth.” For purposes of assessment peer review, states are expected to show how the ideas of depth and breadth were considered in test design and development as well as provide evidence to show the extent to which administered assessments used for accountability addressed the depth and breadth of the standards as defined by the assessment program. Alignment is a relatively new field and was first included in the 2014 edition of the Joint Standards. However, related ideas had long existed within the field of measurement and were addressed within the spheres of “content validity” and “consequential validity” (Traynor  and Christopherson, 2024). Modern validity theory structures assessment system validation as an argument consisting of a series of logical chains supported by theory and evidence (Kane, 1992; 2013). Evidence based on test content, including evidence of alignment between an assessment and corresponding standards (or other assessment targets), critically supports a validity argument. A core reason federal peer review requires evidence related to depth and breadth is because this evidence based on test content supports a validity argument for an assessment program. It is important for each state to have a strong rationale for their conceptualizations of depth and breadth. However, the implications of how a state interprets and operationalizes depth and breadth extend beyond the current requirements of federal peer review.  Implications  A reasoned, clear, and shared understanding of the terms breadth and depth helps inform decisions about how to operationalize these concepts in the context of a state’s education system, including for assessments. A state’s interpretation of the concept of complexity signals implicit if not explicit ideas about teaching and learning. Educators and others involved in K-12 education take cues from the state assessment system. The shared goal of leveraging assessment to guide student learning is advanced when the underlying ideas that drive a state’s assessment design, development, and interpretation resonate with the underlying ideas within the state’s academic standards as well as our best understanding of teaching and learning.  Student achievement is generally understood to represent learning. How we define complexity–and how we understand complexity to relate to academic expectations, learning, and performance–influences how we measure student learning and how we interpret and use the results of standards-based assessments. In other words, the interpretation and use of test results by teachers (and others) is influenced by how an assessment program signals that complexity relates to learning. Thus, discrepancies in how decisionmakers conceptualize ideas of complexity may pose a threat to validity in terms of how student scores are interpreted. For example, when concepts of difficulty and complexity are not differentiated, items that are tricky or very difficult may be misclassified as complex, and false positive determinations may be made about content alignment between assessment tasks and academic standards.  The importance of a state’s interpretation of breadth and depth extends to a consideration of the consequences of testing, which falls outside of federal assessment peer review but is an important source of validity evidence (AERA, APA, & NCME, 2014). Indeed, the theory  of action for a standards-based assessment and accountability system hinges on the influence of testing. In an aligned system, testing student achievement of carefully defined depth and breadth of academic content will positively influence teaching practices and student learning. It is broadly agreed that high-stakes assessments strongly influence classroom instruction (Kane, 2013) and Shepard, et al. (2009) asserted that assessments have an even greater influence than do the academic standards.  Opportunity to learn can also be influenced, positively or negatively, depending on the way the field of education conceptualizes ideas of breadth as well as depth / complexity. A common concern about breadth as represented on statewide assessments relates to the unintended consequences of a resulting narrowing of the curriculum. A common concern about depth relates to teachers’ interpretation and use of test scores, particularly for low achieving students. A measurement perspective on achievement often connects low achievement with the ability to complete low complexity tasks and high achievement with the ability to complete high complexity tasks (e.g., Messick, 1996). This perspective does not necessarily extend to the realm of curriculum and instruction.  In Education and Learning to Think (1987), Lauren Resnick pointed out that the term ‘higher order’ skills is misleading, because it implies that a ‘lower order’ set of skills needs to come first. She posits that this idea that students must move along a sequence from lower level to higher level activities justifies long years of drill on the ‘basics’ before thinking and problem solving are demanded. Similarly, the term ‘depth’ is misleading as it implies a relative position. Similarly, instructional modifications for students with low test scores tend to be remedial; they are given “low-level” tasks to master, based on the idea that low-complexity tasks are necessary preparation for higher complexity work (Goertz, et al., 2009). In contrast, “postponing more complex reasoning about subject matter would be antithetical to the intentions of both the CCSS and learning progressions research” (Shepard, et al., 2013).  Extensive research indicates more positive learning outcomes for students who are provided the opportunity to engage in productive struggle through complex academic work and problem solving compared with students in classrooms that used more traditional and lower complexity tasks (e.g. Kiefer, 1993; Smith, 1996; Stein and Lane, 1996; Schmidt, et al., 1997; Roth, et al., 2006; NRC, 2012[SC1] ). Beyond depth and breadth, a holistic perspective on alignment considers how well all policy elements in a system work together to guide instruction and, ultimately, student learning. This holistic perspective includes consideration of our best understanding of teaching and learning and the pedagogical implications of our assessment designs (Webb, 1997).  References  Craik and Lockhart (1972) Levels of Processing Theory  Claim: The greater the “depth” of processing, the more something becomes embedded in one’s memory.  Dickenson, E.R, Thacker, A.A, and Michaels, H.R. (2020) NGSS Alignment: Where Have We Been and Where Are We Going? Educational Measurement: Issues and Practice 39(2), 31–34  https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.112.3.527  Kane, M. T. (1992). An argument-based approach to validity. Psychological Bulletin, 112(3), 527– 535.  McLaughlin, M.W., Shepard, L., and O’Day, J.A. (1995) Improving Education through Standards-Based Reform. A Report by the National Academy of Education Panel on Standards-Based Education Reform  Polikoff, M. (2020). The present and future of alignment. Educational Measurement: Issues and Practice, 39(2), 18–20. (<https://doi.org/10.1111/emip.12333>)https://doi.org/10.1111/emip.12333  Rhees, R. (1903). Thoroughness vs. Breadth. The School Review, 11(3), 200-210. Discussion and tension related to the broadening of high-school work, including moving away from classical education to “practical education.”  http://www.jstor.org/stable/27795566  Stewart, W. A. C. (1957). UNIVERSITY EDUCATION IN BREADTH AND DEPTH. The Journal of General Education, 10(4), 223–227.  Smith and O’Day (1988). A standards-based system was (and is) intended to set expectations for specific types of valued cognitive engagement, such as those requiring “depth of knowledge [and] complex thinking”  Shaw, S., & Crisp, V. (2011). Tracing the evolution of validity in educational measurement: past issues and contemporary challenges. Research Matters, 11(1), 14-9.  Traynor, A., & Christopherson, S. C. (2024). Using content relevance and representativeness indices in instrument revision. Applied Measurement in Education, 37(2), 132-147. (<https://doi.org/10.1080/08957347.2024.2347518>)https://doi.org/10.1080/08957347.2024.2347518  Webb, N. L. (1997). Criteria for alignment of expectations and assessments in mathematics and mathematics education (NISE Research Monograph No. 6). Council of Chief State School Officers;. Madison: University of Wisconsin, Wisconsin Center for Education Research.",
