    "id": "1ebdff2a-0387-42dc-aa60-e3197d24c524",
    "title": "SUBMITTED_Educational Side Effect (Part 1/2)",
    "content": "This is a proposal to investigate potential side effects of different interpretations of “rigor” as applied to interpretation and enactment of existing K-12 academic standards.\n\nIn the last decade-plus, new academic standards documents for Science (NGSS Lead States, 2013), Social Studies (National Council for the Social Studies, 2013), Mathematics, and English Language Arts (National Governors Association Center for Best Practices &amp; Council of Chief State School Officers, 2010) have been released. Each new framework or new set of standards differentiates itself from previous ones in relation to the “rigor” of the expectations and connects this attribute to college and career readiness. For example, in the 19 pages of NGSS’ Appendix C, which details the “importance of rigorous content for college and career readiness in science,” rigor is referenced 22 times. Similarly, the C3 framework states a core objective to “enhance the rigor of the social studies disciplines;” and the CCSS are grounded in “rigorous content and application of knowledge through higher-order thinking skills” (ibid). Overall rationales for the need for these shifts toward more rigorous expectations are anchored in relatively poor achievement for US students compared with other nations on international assessments, the identification of excessive academic remediation necessary for students entering colleges, and the identification of a need/lack of workforce preparedness for US graduates in the new Global Economy (ibid).\n\nThe Every Students Succeeds Act (ESEA, 2015) similarly fore-fronted the link between rigorous expectations and college and career readiness. The ESEA included a rigor-related provision that differentiated the new law from previous ones by expecting all students to be instructed based on “challenging State academic standards” that were demonstrably linked to credit-bearing tertiary coursework as well as post-secondary career and technical education standards 20 USC § 6311(b)(1)(A). To meet the expectations within ESEA, states must adopt “rigorous” academic content standards and statewide assessments are expected to reflect the “depth” or “cognitive complexity” explicit in those standards (US ED, 2018).\n\nThe nature of “rigor” has been variously conceptualized as related to task difficulty (e.g. Wise and Soneral, 2018), complexity (e.g. Webb, 1997, Christopherson and Webb, 2018), cognitive load (e.g. Sweller, 1988), sophistication of response structure (Biggs and Collis, 1982), degree of independence or student-teacher dynamics related to guidance (e.g. Carter, et al 2013, Tekkumru-Kisa, et al, 2015), and other attributes. Many additional examples exist.\n\nDifferent interpretations of rigor may also be influenced by different units of analysis. For example, some approaches use standards, tasks, or other academic content as the unit of analysis (e.g. Webb, 1997, Tekkumru-Kisa, et al, 2015, Christopherson and Webb, 2018), others use student responses as the unit of analysis (Biggs and Collis, 1982), and yet others are focused on the student themselves, in which case the conceptualization is such that “rigor” of an expectation or task could vary, depending on the person in question. Some definitions include multiple (and potentially conflicting) units of analysis, such as the idea that complexity is both an inherent property of a mathematics task that can be determined by the number of steps necessary to find a solution but also that it complexity is a relative task attribute such that “what is complex to one student may be less complex to his or her classmate” (NCES, 2003) A shared understanding of these attributes is consequential as translation of new “rigorous” academic standards into curriculum and assessments requires domain analyses that appropriately differentiate between and among these concepts. This lack of consensus on the idea of rigor has been recognized as affecting tertiary education as well (e.g. Schwegler, 2019). While the lack of consensus has been widely acknowledged, the issue warrants an examination of the discrepant definitions and how differences in interpretation might affect teaching and learning and, ultimately, students’ experiences and outcomes.",
