  {
    "id": "86895dd4-63f1-4bfb-8e86-76d1ec73f8ef",
    "title": "SUBMITTED_Educational Side Effect (Part 2/2)",
    "content": "The potential side effects of different interpretations may be consequential as opportunity to learn can be influenced - both positively and negatively - depending on the way the field of education conceptualizes ideas of “rigor.” Consider, for example, how decisions are made about who has access to advanced coursework. Evidence suggests positive outcomes for students provided access to an algebra course before high school (Smith, 1996). Similarly, if students are determined to be “low-performing”, providing “lower level” coursework has been observed to negatively influence academic success (Kifer, 1993, and many others). Access to advanced coursework depends on how decision-makers conceptualize ideas of rigor and how it relates to learning. More generally, discrepancies in ideas about “rigor” may threaten the validity of many decisions about students based on test scores. Standardized assessments typically determine student proficiency as relates to rigorous standards based on a student’s ability to answer questions differentiated by statistical metrics related to item difficulty (Smith, et. al 2014). For example, many statewide summative assessments claim to measure “different levels of understanding” by using computer adaptive tests in which “higher performance is followed by more difficult items and lower performance is followed by less difficult items.” In this case, the underlying learning model assumes that success with more difficult items corresponds to a higher level of understanding—an assumption that merits further examination as item difficulty has been found to favor different groups of test-takers in different ways (e.g. ACT, Inc, 2020) and is influenced by construct-irrelevant factors. If we use measurement tools defined by statistical metrics of difficulty, to what extent does that also measure the idea of “rigor” as conceptualized within the corresponding standards?\n\nWe propose to explore the various overlapping rigor-related terms and conceptualizations in use by the field, unpack their definitions and applications, and review the implications of these choices of terminology and interpretations when applied to curriculum and to assessments. Our purpose is to provide a critical view of what these terms mean conceptually and empirically, highlighting potential side effects.",
    "document_type": "docx",
    "file_name": "SUBMITTED_Educational Side Effect.docx",
