    "id": "fc338455-6613-4223-86a5-7c7858f7c963",
    "title": "SUBMITTED_Educational Side Effect",
    "content": "This is a proposal to investigate potential side effects of different interpretations of “rigor” as applied to interpretation and enactment of existing K-12 academic standards.\n\nIn the last decade-plus, new academic standards documents for Science (NGSS Lead States, 2013), Social Studies (National Council for the Social Studies, 2013), Mathematics, and English Language Arts (National Governors Association Center for Best Practices &amp; Council of Chief State School Officers, 2010) have been released. Each new framework or new set of standards differentiates itself from previous ones in relation to the “rigor” of the expectations and connects this attribute to college and career readiness. For example, in the 19 pages of NGSS’ Appendix C, which details the “importance of rigorous content for college and career readiness in science,” rigor is referenced 22 times. Similarly, the C3 framework states a core objective to “enhance the rigor of the social studies disciplines;” and the CCSS ar...",
