    "id": "fd1e7247-7815-4c51-9d17-64fb1203073e",
    "title": "NW AR ESC Jan 31 2018 WebbAlign PD Outline",
    "content": "Title: NW AR ESC Jan 31 2018 WebbAlign PD Outline\n\nContent: Proposal: WebbAlign DOK Professional Learning for Administrators and Teachers within the NW AR ESC region, January 31, 2017 Proposal below. Adjustments, additions, clarifications, etc. are welcome for all steps. Date: December 11, 2017 Introductory DOK Session 35 people (maximum) Up to 50 participants* Goals: Introduce/review DOK; work toward calibration of participant understanding and application of DOK; explore/expand conceptual understanding of alignment; practice using DOK to evaluate standards along with assessment items Sample Schedule: 9-10:30: This first segment of the morning session provides context for DOK, introducing it as a language system to talk about content complexity. Through activities, we elicit participants’ ideas about what factors influence content complexity and compare and contrast perspectives. Additional context is provided for DOK, including its origin as a tool to evaluate alignment and (if of interest) why Blooms Taxonomy (and others) were not used for this purpose. Participants will evaluate the degree of alignment of some examples, focusing on topic match (Categorical Concurrence) and complexity match (DOK Consistency). We will start into calibration of participants’ thinking about complexity, looking at item examples that are designed to elicit discussion. 10:30-10:45: Break 10:45-12:00: In the second part of the morning session, we will consider the relationship between difficulty and complexity, building off of earlier topics and examples. We will directly address several common misinterpretations or alternate interpretations of DOK. Participants will work in groups to complete a sorting activity that helps individuals to differentiate between and among the levels of DOK as well as helps to calibrate between and among team understanding. Participants will dive into the DOK definitions for reading, mathematics, science, and social studies (depending on subject area/interest). 12:00-1:00: Lunch 1:00-2:30: In the afternoon, we will practice using DOK to evaluate the complexity of state standards, assigning DOK, and building group consensus. This process helps participants to calibrate their interpretation and use of DOK (as well as the standards!).  We will consider application to classroom use, and look at some assessment item examples from released AR assessments and ACT Aspire.  2:30-3:30: Wrap up and consideration of distribution of DOK in CCSS (applies to AR standards). Consideration of tomorrow and beyond. Participants can request clarification as needed, introduce topics for discussion related to current work needs and plans, and discuss action items for moving forward.  *NOTE that depending on final group size, some of the small-group/large-group work in the afternoon may be abbreviated.  Follow-up sessions can focus on:  Alignment  Higher complexity tasks/items  Subject-specific considerations  Facilitated work time for PLCs and others who are trying to use DOK to inform their instructional design.  Assessment literacy considerations  (And many other facets – we can discuss! ) ",
