    "id": "77e5a435-06e6-4690-a04c-dc93b7a15b63",
    "title": "Proposal: WebbAlign DOK Professional Learning for (Part 2/2)",
    "content": "Sara and James will discuss and agree upon plans via calls/emails as needed. Teachers will prepare for the March session by selecting a particular existing or future task for which they will develop a rubric.\n\nAll CTE educators (~70), split into a morning and afternoon group\n\nMarch 20, 2023\n\nGroup 1: 8 – 11 am ET\n\nGroup 2: 12:30-3:30 pm ET\n\nGoals: Continue to work toward calibration of participant understanding and application of DOK, building on past work using DOK to evaluate standards and assessment items with a focus on coherence/alignment. Session will provide refresher and practice followed by a focus on rubrics. During small group work time, teachers will make progress on a rubric that they can apply in their assessment.\n\nPlan: Conduct an in-person interactive workshop that includes supported small group work time for teachers to discuss and develop a rubric.\n\nDebriefing phone call\n\nAfter March sessions\n\nDebrief of SY2022-2023 work, plan for next steps\n\nTeachers should bring a device they can use to access online materials, e.g. their standards, scope and sequence documents, any existing unit assessment materials, PD reference materials (from past sessions and new, to be provided), etc. Screen/projector and audio required for in-person sessions with IT support available if needed. Seating at tables in 4-5 person groups to facilitate small group discussions during the interactive presentation.\n\nTwo 3-hour in-person sessions on March 20, 2023 will be held at:\n\nCenter of Innovation\n\n440 Hood Avenue\n\nFayetteville, GA, 30214\n\nContext:\n\nThe overarching goals of this work are grounded in continuous improvement and fostering of teamwork + rich conversations across the district for CTE teachers, similar to the types of collaborative work that occurs in PLCs. Recent CTE team PD work included considerations for item-writing with a focus on multiple choice and other similar on-demand item types. Some teachers currently use project-based or problem-based assessment components. In past sessions, teachers have asked for more information about the use of rubrics to evaluate student work on project-or problem-based tasks. CTE teachers could benefit from having common rubrics for use across similar areas or contexts. Teachers could also benefit from building common rubric practices or components, for example, having students self-evaluate using the same rubric as the teacher uses (both teacher-eval and self-eval components). A current district-wide emphasis includes a focus on writing across the curriculum; CTE teachers are thinking about technical literacy and career tech literacy, including graphic and schematic components and not just texts. Writing-related and tech literacy components could be a helpful common component within rubrics to support this overarching goal. Overall, emphasis is on the use of rubrics to promote meaningful reflection on work vs as a checklist. Teachers will be split into two groups for the March 20 work. One group will attend the morning session and the other group will attend the afternoon session.\n\nWisconsin Center for Education Products &amp; Services\n\n1213 N. Sherman Ave., #322   Madison, WI 53704  877-272-5593   WebbAlign@wceps.org   www.webbalign.org\n\nWisconsin Center for Education Products &amp; Services\n\n1213 N. Sherman Ave., #322   Madison, WI 53704  877-272-5593   info@wceps.org   www.wceps.org",
