  {
    "id": "878b3175-3bf4-40e4-b106-20026d2960f4",
    "title": "2022 _WIDA MODEL Paper for Licensing-FINAL-PWC (Part 3/9)",
    "content": "Task Level 2 Expectations Linguistic Complexity Vocabulary Usage Language Control Phrases, short oral sentences General language related to the content area; searching for vocabulary when going beyond the highly familiar is evident When using simple discourse, is generally comprehensible and fluent; communication may be impeded by searching for language structures or by phonological, syntactic, or semantic errors when going beyond phrases and short, simple sentences\n\nLevel 2 Task – Grades 6-8 (Point to GOALIE)  Elsa is the goalie for the red team. (Point to NET)  Elsa must stand in  front of the net and stop the ball  from going in . Q1:  What is Elsa doing? Q2: (Point to BOY WHO KICKED BALL)  What is he doing? Q3:  What are the other students doing? Meets\n\nRationale Response meets expectations in both quantity and quality. The response consists of simple phrases that are automatic and easily comprehensible. The student generates original language not included in the script, such as \"catching,\" \"kicking,\" and \"running,\" all of which are general vocabulary related to the content area. Response meets all T2 Task Level Expectations.  Meets\n\nTask Level 3 Expectations Linguistic Complexity Vocabulary Usage Language Control Simple and expanded oral sentences; responses show emerging complexity used to add detail General and some specific language related to the content area; may search for needed vocabulary at times When communicating in sentences, is generally comprehensible and fluent; communication may from time to time be impeded by searching for language structures or by phonological, syntactic, or semantic errors, especially when attempting more complex oral discourse\n\nLevel 3 Task – Grades 6-8 Q1:  If Mrs. Han wants to point to something on the board from far away, what should she use? Q2:  Why do you say this? Q3:  How are laser pointers and flashlights similar? Q4:  How are they different? Q5: (If necessary)  Tell me more.  (OR)  Can you tell me anything else? (OR)  Can you give me more details?  (OR)  Can you be more specific? (OR)  Can you give me an example? (Point to PICTURE 1)  You saw how Ricardo used a flashlight to help him see in the dark. (Point to PICTURE 2)  You also saw how Mrs. Han used a laser pointer to point to the board from far away. Lasers are very accurate because they are focused and concentrated beams of light.\n\nRationale Response meets expectations in both quantity and quality. The student uses both simple and expanded sentences. The student uses mostly general vocabulary (\"some stuff,\" \"a lot of stuff,\" etc.) and repeats specific vocabulary used in the script, such as \"laser pointer,\" \"focus.” The response is mostly automatic, generally comprehensible and fluent. Response meets all T3 Task Level Expectations.  Meets\n\nTask Level 4 Expectations Linguistic Complexity Vocabulary Usage Language Control A variety of oral sentence lengths of varying linguistic complexity; responses show emerging cohesion used to provide detail and clarity Specific and some technical language related to the content area; searching for needed vocabulary may be occasionally evident At all times generally comprehensible and fluent, although phonological, syntactic, or semantic errors that don’t impede the overall meaning of the communication may appear at times; such errors may reflect first language interference\n\nLevel 4 Task – Grades 6-8 (Point to PICTURE 1)  Ralph wrote books and papers about equal rights. (Point to PICTURE 2)  He also organized marches and gave speeches to help solve this problem. Now think about all you’ve learned about Ralph Bunche. Q1:  What problem did Ralph want to make better? Q2:  How did Ralph work to make this problem better? Q3:  Why was Ralph’s work important? Q4: (If necessary)  Tell me more.  (OR)  Can you tell me anything else?  (OR)   Can you give me more details?  (OR)  Can you be more specific?   (OR)  Can you give me an example? Ralph Bunche saw a problem in the United States. Not all people had the same rights by law. For example, African Americans had to attend separate schools from other Americans. Throughout his life, Ralph worked hard to help African Americans gain the same rights as other Americans.  Response 1 :\n\nRationale Response approaches expectations. The response is deficient in both quantity and quality. Student answers question with simple and expanded sentences but the expanded sentences do not provide much additional detail. The response also lacks the technical language expected at the T4 Level. Response falls short of meeting T4 Task Level Expectations in the areas of linguistic complexity and vocabulary usage.    Approaches",
