  {
    "id": "fc6a9ba4-2f5c-4eaf-9a72-bab5f8986e82",
    "title": "2022 _WIDA MODEL Paper for Licensing-FINAL-PWC (Part 2/9)",
    "content": "Level 4 Task – Grades 1-2 Q1: (Point to the BABY BIRDS)  What are the babies doing in the nest? Q2:  How do you think the babies get their food? Q3:  What will help the babies get bigger and stronger? Q4:  Why do you say that? Q5:  What do you think will happen after the baby birds grow bigger? Q6:  Why do you say that? Over the next week, Sister Bear and Brother Bear watch the other eggs hatch. The baby birds are in the nest. The mother bird must feed all of them. The babies do not know how to fly. They must stay in the nest while they grow bigger and stronger.\n\nRationale Response meets expectations in both quantity and quality. Response shows a variety of sentence lengths, which show detail, clarity, and emerging cohesion. The student uses specific language (\"worm,\" \"pick,\" \"catch\") and some technical language (\"pick which one,\" \"live their own life,\" \"it's all part of nature\"). Although the student commits slight syntactic errors, such as \"She's  gonna  pick which one to give it,\" and \"Not to just put in the ground,\" the sentence is still comprehensible. The response meets all T4 Task Level Expectations.  Meets\n\nTask Level 5 Expectations Linguistic Complexity Vocabulary Usage Language Control A variety of sentence lengths of varying linguistic complexity in extended oral discourse; responses show cohesion and organization used to support main ideas Technical language related to the content area; facility with needed vocabulary is evident Approaching comparability to that of English proficient peers in terms of comprehensibility and fluency; errors don’t impede communication and may be typical of those an English proficient peer might make\n\nSo, after some time, the baby birds grow bigger. They become too big for the nest. Soon the babies are ready to fly on their own. Level 5 Task – Grades 1-2 113\n\nLevel 5 Task-Grades 1-2 Q1: (Point to PICTURE OF WEEK 4)  In week four, how many birds are in the nest in all? Q2: (Point to PICTURE OF WEEK 6)  In week  six, three of the birds start to fly. How many  birds are left in the nest? Q3:  Tell me how you figured that out.  (OR)  Tell me why you say that. Q4: (Point to BIRDS IN PICTURE OF WEEK 6)  Why did these birds stay in the nest? Q5: (If necessary)  Can you give me more details? Q6:  Now tell me the story of the five baby birds starting from when they were eggs. Q7: (If necessary)  Tell me more.  (OR)  Can you tell me anything else?     Response 1:\n\nRationale Response 1: Response meets expectations in both quantity and quality. The response uses a variety of sentence lengths and varying levels of linguistic complexity. Although the student grasped for details when retelling the story, it was at the same rate as a native English speaker in Grades 1-2. The student also committed semantic errors typical of those an English proficient peer would make (i.e., \"there is five,\" \"there was five\"). The response meets the vocabulary usage expectation because the student is able to appropriately sequence events and use mathematical terminology, such as \"five minus three is two.\" Response meets all T5 Task Level Expectations.  Meets\n\nLevel 5 Task-Grades 1-2 Q1: (Point to PICTURE OF WEEK 4)  In week four, how many birds are in the nest in all? Q2: (Point to PICTURE OF WEEK 6)  In week  six, three of the birds start to fly. How many  birds are left in the nest? Q3:  Tell me how you figured that out.  (OR)  Tell me why you say that. Q4: (Point to BIRDS IN PICTURE OF WEEK 6)  Why did these birds stay in the nest? Q5: (If necessary)  Can you give me more details? Q6:  Now tell me the story of the five baby birds starting from when they were eggs. Q7: (If necessary)  Tell me more.  (OR)  Can you tell me anything else?     Response 2:\n\nRationale Response meets expectations in both quantity and quality. The response includes a variety of sentence lengths of varying linguistic complexity that provide detailed events of the story. The student organizes and retells the events of the story fluently and automatically, much like an English proficient peer. The response meets vocabulary usage expectations through the use of technical language in the form of mathematical terminology, such as \"minus\" and \"equals\" and sequential phrases (\"on the first week,\" \"on week four,\" etc.). Response meets all T5 Task Level Expectations.  Meets\n\nGrades 6-8 Speaking Samples 118\n\nTask Level 1 Expectations Linguistic Complexity Vocabulary Usage Language Control Single words, set phrases, or chunks of memorized oral language  Highest frequency vocabulary from school setting and content areas  When using memorized language, is generally comprehensible; communication may be significantly impeded when going beyond the highly familiar\n\nStandards Abbreviations Page 3",
