    "id": "831ee1a6-5edf-460b-9975-50067de1a4a6",
    "title": "2022 _WIDA MODEL Paper for Licensing-FINAL-PWC (Part 4/9)",
    "content": "Level 4 Task – Grades 6-8 (Point to PICTURE 1)  Ralph wrote books and papers about equal rights. (Point to PICTURE 2)  He also organized marches and gave speeches to help solve this problem. Now think about all you’ve learned about Ralph Bunche. Q1:  What problem did Ralph want to make better? Q2:  How did Ralph work to make this problem better? Q3:  Why was Ralph’s work important? Q4: (If necessary)  Tell me more.  (OR)  Can you tell me anything else?  (OR)   Can you give me more details?  (OR)  Can you be more specific?   (OR)  Can you give me an example? Ralph Bunche saw a problem in the United States. Not all people had the same rights by law. For example, African Americans had to attend separate schools from other Americans. Throughout his life, Ralph worked hard to help African Americans gain the same rights as other Americans.  Response 2:\n\nFour Language Domains Interpretive Language Expressive Language Comprehension Communication Oral Language Written Language\n\nRationale Response meets expectations in both quantity and quality. The student's response includes three sentences of varying linguistic complexity. The amount of detail provided in each sentence gradually increases with each response. The student repeats some of the specific vocabulary in the script and generates her own language (\"protests\"). The response is automatic and fluent. Response meets all T4 Task Level Expectations.  Meets\n\nTask Level 5 Expectations Linguistic Complexity Vocabulary Usage Language Control A variety of sentence lengths of varying linguistic complexity in extended oral discourse; responses show cohesion and organization used to support main ideas Technical language related to the content area; facility with needed vocabulary is evident Approaching comparability to that of English proficient peers in terms of comprehensibility and fluency; errors don’t impede communication and may be typical of those an English proficient peer might make\n\nLevel 5 Task – Grades 6-8 Q1:  What is a problem that you think needs to be made better? Q2:  What do you think Ralph would do about that if he were here today? Q3: (If necessary)  Tell me more.  (OR)  Can you tell me anything else? (OR)  Can you give me more details?  (OR)  Can you be more specific?  (OR)  Can you give me an example? There’s no picture here. We have talked about how Ralph Bunche worked to make things better. Now we are going to talk about a problem Ralph might work to make better if he were alive today. Think about a problem in your school, community, or even the world that you want to make better.  Response 1 :\n\nRationale Response approaches expectations. The response falls short in both quantity and quality. The response is automatic and fluent but lacks the technical vocabulary expected at the T5 Task level. The response includes extended sentences, but the student does not provide enough oral discourse to meet the requirements of a T5 Task Level response. Response falls short of meeting T5 Task Level Expectations in the areas of: linguistic complexity and vocabulary usage.  Approaches\n\nLevel 5 Task – Grades 6-8 Q1:  What is a problem that you think needs to be made better? Q2:  What do you think Ralph would do about that if he were here today? Q3: (If necessary)  Tell me more.  (OR)  Can you tell me anything else? (OR)  Can you give me more details?  (OR)  Can you be more specific?  (OR)  Can you give me an example? There’s no picture here. We have talked about how Ralph Bunche worked to make things better. Now we are going to talk about a problem Ralph might work to make better if he were alive today. Think about a problem in your school, community, or even the world that you want to make better.  Response 2:\n\nRationale Response meets expectations in both quantity and quality. The student uses a variety of sentence lengths and structures to produce a cohesive response. She provides an example to support her idea and generates her own technical vocabulary related to the content area (\"immigrants,\" \"legal,\" \"Hispanic,\" \"opportunity\") for added detail. Her fluency is approaching comparability to that of English proficient peers. Response meets all T5 Task Level Expectations Meets\n\nScoring Writing\n\nGuidance for Scoring  Refer to the Test Administrator Manual: Writing Section tab Scored Writing Samples tab for further assistance in scoring. Practice PPTs are on the flash drive. Page 5\n\nPart A: Grades 1-2, “Dog Bath” Page 13\n\nACTIVITY: To Move On or Not? Rationale:  This student writes short phrases with invented spelling. Move on to Part B\n\nLevels of English Proficiency Page 4\n\nACTIVITY: To Move On or Not? Rationale:  This student writes short phrases and simple sentences. Though there are errors in mechanics and syntax at times, the meaning is clear.  Move on to Part B\n\nACTIVITY: To Move On or Not? Rationale:  This student is unable to produce comprehensible words or phrases in English (“ nino ” may show an attempt to write in the native language). Do Not Move On",
