  {
    "id": "3e6cf7fa-4a18-4e0b-ade6-3c819fa53555",
    "title": "2022 _WIDA MODEL Paper for Licensing-FINAL-PWC (Part 1/9)",
    "content": "WIDA MODEL Paper  for Test Administrators Prince William County Schools October 10th, 2022 Ellen Parkhurst, WIDA Licensed Facilitator\n\nSecure the consumable materials. Test Content should not be shared with students or their families prior to or after testing.  No information about the test can be shared outside the testing situation. Test Security 10\n\nRationale Response meets expectations in both quantity and quality. The student uses simple and expanded sentences (\"They all have the same color and they're all for winter.\" \"Maybe they want to play outside when it's snowing.\"). Although the student commits a slight syntactic error, \"Sister Bear is putting her coat,\" it is still comprehensible and does not impede the meaning of the sentence. The response also includes both general (\"winter,\" \"coat,\" etc.) and specific vocabulary (\"going to,\" \"gloves,\" \"scarf\"). Response meets all T3 Task Level Expectations.  Meets\n\nLevel 3 Task – Grades 1-2 (Clearly point out the HAT, the SCARF, and the GLOVES.) Now look at the hat, the scarf, and the gloves. Q1:  Do these things go together?  (Pause) (If “Yes”)  How do they go together? (If “No”)  Why don’t they go together? Q2: (If necessary)  How are they different? Q3: (If necessary)  Can you tell me anything else?   (OR)  Can you give me more details? Response 2 :\n\nRationale Response approaches expectations. The response falls short in both quantity and quality. The response includes comprehensible simple sentences but does not provide expanded sentences used to add detail. The student's ability to differentiate the three clothing items (\"hats,\" \"scarves,\" \"mittens\") demonstrates his use of specific language. The student's response is comprehensible and fluent. Response falls short of meeting T3 Task Level Expectations in the area of: Linguistic Complexity.  Approaches\n\nLevel 3 Task – Grades 1-2 (Clearly point out the HAT, the SCARF, and the GLOVES.) Now look at the hat, the scarf, and the gloves. Q1:  Do these things go together?  (Pause) (If “Yes”)  How do they go together? (If “No”)  Why don’t they go together? Q2: (If necessary)  How are they different? Q3: (If necessary)  Can you tell me anything else?   (OR)  Can you give me more details? Response 3:\n\nRationale Meets Response meets expectations in both quantity and quality. Response is a fluent expanded sentence that shows emerging complexity and detail. The student uses both general (\"color\") vocabulary and specific vocabulary (\"they all,\" \"dots\"), expected at a T3 Task Level. Response meets all T3 Task Level Expectations.\n\nLevel 3 Task – Grades 1-2 (Clearly point out the HAT, the SCARF, and the GLOVES.) Now look at the hat, the scarf, and the gloves. Q1:  Do these things go together?  (Pause) (If “Yes”)  How do they go together? (If “No”)  Why don’t they go together? Q2: (If necessary)  How are they different? Q3: (If necessary)  Can you tell me anything else?   (OR)  Can you give me more details? Response 4:\n\nRationale Response meets expectations in both quantity and quality. The response consists of an expanded sentence with both general and specific language. The student hesitates slightly as she adds detail to the sentence (\"your hands and your neck and your head can get cold\") but the response is still easily comprehensible. Response meets all T3 Task Level Expectations.  Meets\n\nLevel 3 Task – Grades 1-2 Sister Bear and Brother Bear look up at  the bird’s nest. The first week they see  that one egg has hatched. A baby bird is coming out of its shell. Q1: (Point to NEST)  How many more eggs will hatch? Q2: (Point to BABY BIRD)  How does a baby bird get out of its shell?  (If student says, “I don’t know”)  Tell me what you think happens. Q3:  Why do you think baby birds live in nests? Q4: (If necessary)  Can you tell me anything else?  (OR)  Can you give me more details?\n\nRationale Response meets expectations in both quantity and quality. The student's last response (\"So they don't get eaten by predators like wolves and fox.\") demonstrates emerging complexity used to add detail. The student uses some advanced technical vocabulary (\"predator\"). The linguistic complexity and language control meet the T3 Task Level Expectations. The response is also fluent and automatic. Therefore, this response is scored as meeting the T3 Task Level.  Meets\n\nTask Level 4 Expectations Linguistic Complexity Vocabulary Usage Language Control A variety of oral sentence lengths of varying linguistic complexity; responses show emerging cohesion used to provide detail and clarity Specific and some technical language related to the content area; searching for needed vocabulary may be occasionally evident At all times generally comprehensible and fluent, although phonological, syntactic, or semantic errors that don’t impede the overall meaning of the communication may appear at times; such errors may reflect first language interference\n\nWIDA MODEL  Components",
