    "id": "355f1370-046c-4262-bd64-fa12505f318e",
    "title": "2022 _WIDA MODEL Paper for Licensing-FINAL-PWC (Part 5/9)",
    "content": "Part A: Grades 6-8, “Class Field Trip” Page 14\n\nACTIVITY: To Move On or Not? Rationale:  This student is able to write comprehensible single words (sign, slick, birds, etc.). Move on to Part B\n\nACTIVITY: To Move On or Not? Rationale:  This student is able to write single words and phrases in English. Even though the writing contains errors, the text is comprehensible.  Move on to Part B\n\nACTIVITY: To Move On or Not? Rationale:  This sample contains copied test only.  Do Not Move On\n\nScoring Writing: Two Types of Scores Quick Score: High, Mid, Low During Test Administration Using  Expect Box Language Proficiency Level Score:  After Test Administration is Complete Writing   Rubric of the WIDA   Consortium  Pages 11 &amp; 27\n\nACTIVITY: Quick Score?  Page 11\n\nACTIVITY: Quick Score? (Part B) Grades 1-2: Dog Bath Grades 6-8: Class Field Trip PROMPTS: Pages 13 &amp; 14 Mid ANSWER KEY:  Page 24 SAMPLES: Pages 11 &amp; 12\n\nAdjustments &amp; Calibration: Plus or Minus?\n\nExpectations for Language Use Level  5 Bridging  Level 4 Expanding Level 3 Developing Level 2 Emerging Level 1 Entering Linguistic Complexity Discourse  Dimension Vocabulary Usage Word/Phrase Dimension Language Forms &amp; Conventions Sentence  Dimension\n\nScoring Writing Part B is scored first using the Writing Rubric. Remember, you will score Part A (using the Writing Rubric) if, and only if: Student does not advance to Part B. The response on Part B is not ratable.  Response in Part B receives a score of less than three.\n\nScoring Writing: Final Writing Score Page 27 Score: 4\n\nMaking Score Adjustments  based on Strengths Score: 4+LC Page 27\n\nMaking Score Adjustments  based on Weaknesses Score: 2-LX Page 27\n\nNot-Ratable Score No Response Entirely incomprehensible/illegible Off-Task: Shows now understanding or interaction with prompt Pictures or scribbles only Refusal  Language other than English  154\n\nACTIVITY: What’s that Score Point? In a small group:  Individually read the student writing sample As a group decide on a “solid score” and decide is an adjustment is appropriate. Check the rationale and discuss further.  Pages 15-21 Grades 1-2: Dog Bath Grades 6-8: Class Field Trip\n\nCalculating Results &amp;  Accessing Score Reports\n\n157 Summary Score Sheet\n\n158 Online Score Calculator https://bit.ly/MODEL-Score-Calculator\n\n159 Explore the Online Calculator\n\nOnline Score Calculator Page 26\n\nWIDA MODEL Interpretive Guide  for Score Reports, Grades K-12 Introduction WIDA MODEL Scores Score Reports Speaking and Writing Rubrics 161 161 Page 26\n\nWIDA Focus Bulletin Using WIDA MODEL to Support Instructional Planning for Multilingual Learners 162\n\nClosing\n\nWIDA MODEL:  wida.wisc.edu Features Test Administration Scoring\n\nWIDA MODEL Paper: Questions  Resources: Test Administration Manual WIDA MODEL DVDs WIDA Help Desk:    1-866-276-7735  or  help@wida.us\n\n166 Workshop Feedback – Exit Ticket Please take a moment to provide feedback on a sticky note for us before you leave today. Thank you for being here!\n\nPerformance Definition Criteria\n\nTest Items Adapt to  Student Proficiency  Page 3\n\n19 Review your notes. Discuss with your table English Language Development Standards Grade Level Clusters Dimensions of Language English Proficiency Levels ACTIVITY: Share Your Understanding Page 3\n\nWIDA Website:  wida.wisc.edu Features Purpose and Use Scores Assessments in the WIDA MODEL Suite\n\nWIDA MODEL  Uses\n\nPossible Uses for WIDA MODEL  Screener Interim Progress Monitoring 21\n\nPurpose MODEL Screener Identification of  a student needing ELL services Yes Yes Determine  a student’s ELP level on the WIDA scale Yes Yes Provide guidance on the amount and type of English language  support that may be needed   Yes No Serve  as an interim benchmark assessment Yes No Exit  a student from and ELL program* Yes No *Information obtained from the WIDA MODEL may be used as part of the decision‐making process to exit a student from ELL services. It should not be the sole factor in making this important decision .  MODEL v. MODEL Screener\n\n*Information about a student’s English proficiency level obtained from taking the WIDA MODEL summative assessment may be used as part of the decision‐making process to exit a student from ELL services. It should not be the sole factor in making this important decision .    MODEL vs. MODEL Screener   Purpose MODEL Screener Identify whether  a student needs ELL services Yes Yes Determine  a student’s ELP level on the WIDA scale Yes Yes Provide guidance on the amount and type of English language  support that may be needed   Yes No Serve  as an interim benchmark assessment Yes No Decide whether to exit  a student from an ELL program* Yes No\n\nAdministration of  WIDA MODEL Paper\n\nTest Administration Overview\n\nWhich Grade Cluster? If administering during the first semester: \t1 st  Grade \t3 rd  Grade \t6 th  Grade \t9 th  Grade   Kindergarten   Grade 1-2   Grade 3-5   Grade 6-8 Page 4",
