    "id": "e985b918-3241-4e99-844c-3922e402b584",
    "title": "2022 _WIDA MODEL Paper for Licensing-FINAL-PWC (Part 8/9)",
    "content": "Student: Records answers in the Reading Section of the Student Response Booklet Test Administrator: Follows Script exactly Does NOT read any passage, question, or response option to a student Reading Section Procedures\n\nLow Reading Item Grades 1-2  Students respond to a series of reading items using cards.\n\nMid Reading Item Grades 1-2\n\nKindergarten MODEL 8\n\nCard Sets for Reading\n\nThis initial rating of High, Mid, or Low, along with the number correct in Reading Step 1 establish the appropriate placement for Reading Step 2. Reading Section Quick Score Page 9\n\nAfter the student is finished with the Reading Section, the Test Administrator: Scoring Reading Step 2 Reviews the responses given by the student and scores them as correct or incorrect. Records the placement and total number of correct items in Reading Step 2. Page 9\n\nScoring MODEL  Domain Tests\n\nScoring Domains Tests All Domains: Scored by the Test Administrator Speaking  &amp;  Listening : Scored in real time Writing : 2 Scores Quick Score Final Writing Score Reading: 2 Scores Step 1 Step 2 84\n\nScoring Speaking\n\nLocated on the CD or flash drive in your kit Scoring the Speaking Domain Test Practice Scoring the Speaking Domain Test Speaking Domain Test Practice Score Sheet PPTs to Practice  Scoring Speaking\n\nSpeaking Scoring Scale 87 Definitions Meets Meets all task level expectations in quantity and quality Approaches Approaches task level expectations but falls short in quantity and/or quality; no response; response in a language other than English. ? Unsure if  response meets or approaches; Move on to the next task , and them go back to designate “Meets” or “Approaches”\n\nScoring the WIDA MODEL   Speaking Section Training PowerPoint  Extra Practice Scoring the MODEL Speaking Section PowerPoint  Speaking Score Sheet Practice Scoring Speaking Page 10\n\nSpeaking Rubric  Note the differences across: English Language Proficiency Levels Language Dimensions Page 28\n\nMODEL Online Available for grades 1–2, 3–5, 6–8 and 9–12. (Kindergarten is only in a paper-based format.) Assesses students   in the domains of Speaking, Listening, Writing, and Reading. Refreshed with new items in 2020. 9\n\nACTIVITY: Practice Scoring Speaking Find a partner.  Look at the Speaking Rubric for the task level. Listen to the student response.  Take notes of evidence related to that task level.  Decide if it is meets or approaches.   90 Page 28\n\nApproaches Listen and evaluate the speaking samples Raise the card that indicates your rating. ACTIVITY: Practice Scoring Speaking Page 28   Meets Do you have any questions before we begin rating Speaking Responses? ?\n\nTask Level 1 Expectations Linguistic Complexity Vocabulary Usage Language Control Single words, set phrases, or chunks of memorized oral language  Highest frequency vocabulary from school setting and content areas  When using memorized language, is generally comprehensible; communication may be significantly impeded when going beyond the highly familiar\n\nLevel 1 Task – Grades 1-2  (Point to SISTER BEAR)  This is a picture of Sister Bear getting ready to go outside to play. Now I’m going to ask you some questions about this picture. Q1: (Point to the HAT)  What is this? Q2: (Point to the COAT)  What is this? Q3: (Point to the SHOES)  What are these? Q4: (Point to STUDENT’S DESK)  What is this? Q5: (If necessary)  What other things do you see in this picture?\n\nRationale Response meets expectations in both quantity and quality. Response contains single word answers and high frequency vocabulary for the content area of social and instructional language, which is expected for the T1 Task Level. Response meets all T1 Task Level Expectations.  Meets\n\nTask Level 2 Expectations Linguistic Complexity Vocabulary Usage Language Control Phrases, short oral sentences General language related to the content area; searching for vocabulary when going beyond the highly familiar is evident When using simple discourse, is generally comprehensible and fluent; communication may be impeded by searching for language structures or by phonological, syntactic, or semantic errors when going beyond phrases and short, simple sentences\n\nLevel 2 Task – Grades 1-2 Now I am going to ask you different questions about Sister Bear’s classroom. Q1: (Point to the BOOK)  This is a book. What do we use a book for? Q2: (Point to the CLOCK)  This is a clock. What do we use a clock for? Q3: (Point to the CHAIR)  This is a chair. What do we use a chair for?\n\nRationale Response meets expectations in both quantity and quality. Student generates fluent phrases, such as \"to read stories in it,\" \"to see the time,\" \"to sit on it.\" The general vocabulary used is expected at the T2 Task Level. Response meets all T2 Task Level Expectations.  Meets",
