  {
    "id": "432fc8ca-6b6c-48f2-a3a9-577e6a8dde1e",
    "title": "March 15, 2022Fayette County CTE",
    "content": "Title: March 15, 2022Fayette County CTE\n\nContent: March 15, 2022Fayette County CTE\n\nITEM-LEVEL CONSIDERATIONS\n\nDoes the assessment item or task measure what it intends to measure?\n\nIs the item/task aligned with content from the course and the intended content standards?\n\nDoes the item/task require student engagement at a DOK level appropriate to the assessment target (learning expectation)?\n\nIs the assessment item/task relevant to student life or authentic?\n\nDoes the assessment item or task respect the diversity of the assessment population?\n\nIs the item/task sensitive to test taker characteristics (consider gender, age, ethnicity, socio-economic level, disability, language, etc.) and experiences\n\nDoes the item/task avoid content that might unfairly advantage or disadvantage any student subgroup?\n\nDoes the item/task represent all people respectfully?\n\nDoes the assessment item or task have clear visuals (when essential to an item) and clear formatting?\n\nAre the visuals labeled and do they have clearly defined features?\n\nDoes the item/task have sufficient blank space and clear text?\n\nIs the overall appearance clean and organized?\n\nDoes the assessment have concise and readable text?\n\nDoes text consist of commonly used words (except when vocabulary is being tested)? Are unnecessary words minimized?\n\nAre vocabulary and sentence complexity appropriate for the grade level used?\n\nAre technical terms and abbreviations avoided if not related to the content being measured?\n\nIs the question to be answered clearly identifiable?\n\nIs there anything included that might distract test-takers from their work?\n\nIs the assessment reliable?\n\nIs the grading scheme consistent? Would a student receive the same grade for the same work on multiple evaluations?\n\nCan multiple people use the scoring mechanism and come up with the same general score?\n\nDoes the rubric or other scoring tool allow for appropriate inferences as relates to the learning expectation and as relates to student work?\n\nHave colleagues reviewed the items/tasks and provided feedback?\n\nASSESSMENT-LEVEL CONSIDERATIONS\n\nDoes the assessment adequately meet the criteria of the intended use? (What do you want to learn from students’ scores?)\n\nIf formative does it generate diagnostic feedback to improve learning?\n\nIf summative, is it built upon multiple sources of data?\n\nWill the information derived from the assessment help to improve teaching and learning?\n\nDoes the assessment address an appropriate depth, breadth, and balance of the corresponding learning targets?\n\nDo the DOK levels of the assessment items/task correspond, overall, to the DOK levels of the assessment targets?\n\nAre any learning targets overemphasized on the assessment?\n\nDo the items/tasks on the assessment sample from the full range or breadth of assessment targets?\n\nReferences\n\nAmerican Educational Research Association, American Psychological Association, &amp; National Council on Measurement in Education. (2014). Standards for educational and psychological testing. Washington DC: American Psychological Association.\n\nJoint Committee on Testing Practices (2004). Code of Fair Testing Practices in Education. Washington, DC\n\nNational Research Council (2001). Knowing what students know: The science and design of educational assessment. National Academy Press: Washington DC.\n\nPellegrino, J. W. (2016). 21st Century Science Assessment: The Future Is Now. (SRI Education White Paper). Menlo Park, CA: SRI International.\n\nThompson, et. al (2005) Considerations for the Development and Review of Universally Designed Assessments; NCEO Technical Report 42. National Center on Educational Outcomes\n\nWestEd, CCSSO, et. al (2015) Item Specification Guidelines for the Next Generation Science Standards\n\n1\n\nwebbalign.org",
