  {
    "id": "f4e1e3bf-436a-4e1c-a82b-69869ae5e642",
    "title": "WebbAlign DOK PD for Fayette County CTE: September 7, 2021 Session Evaluation Responses (Part 2/2)",
    "content": "Speaking with our cohorts and concentrating on the task at hand. Listening to other views and reconsidering what the question is really asking or what the statement is really saying.\n\nCollaborating with my group,\n\nDefinition and then the breakouts\n\nUnderstanding what each DOK level represents.\n\nParticipants reported the ways that they viewed DOK differently after participating in the September 7 PD?\n\nI understand it better and can summarize what each level represents.\n\nI am looking at my activities with DOK in mind.\n\nThey are not progressive. They are not stair stepping levels. Each one serves a roll.\n\nClearing understanding of how to apply DOK to CTE standards.\n\nApplicable\n\nLearning about the various levels.\n\nBig take away - DOK does not represent a progression. Instead, it is categorization tool.\n\nDOK is a language that can enhance efficiancy and effectiveness when there is a shared understanding of how to distinguish the types of engagement.\n\nApply them to every standard\n\nDOK is not about difficulty, but rather it is about complexity. Also, DOK is not\n\ndeeper understanding\n\nThey're more important that realized. Most importantly, they enable teachers across the county to be on the same page and allow for consistency between schools within the same subject/class.\n\nClearer picture of how DOK works and how to use it in the classroom\n\nOriginally, I had solely concentrated on the verb to determine what the DOK level is. I'm now more aware to not be influenced by the verb or the wording of the content. It is important to think about  how students need to engage rather than just the verb. Verbs can be interpreted in many different ways.\n\nIt is a different way to view standards to better be able to create lessons to reflect DOK level.\n\nDOK does Not represent a learning progression\n\nDOK will help me guide my future presentation of lessons and assessments.\n\nI feel that every teacher viewed the standards a little different. Using DOK helped us to calibrate our understanding of the standards.\n\nWe analyzed what the definitions were and that we can’t just focus on the verb, so that opened up my eyes to more understanding.\n\nThey help reflect on my questions for my students.\n\nDistinguishing between complexity from  difficulty was key for me. It is easy to get caught up in that and automatically assume they are the same.\n\nI think DOK planning helps us think about our standards in a more intentional way.\n\nRealizing it is not just key words that determine the level\n\nJust a clearer understanding of how to weed through the verbage of standards\n\nBetter understanding\n\nEssential for cross-county assessing\n\nI understand it a lot better\n\nHaving an understanding/conversation about the depth of the standard helps teachers across the district provide the same quality of instruction.\n\nI understand more clearly, the importance and the differentiation between the levels.\n\nI may have to read and re-read and analyze the standard before making a decision about how to teach it. It would help to ask my cohorts to share some ideas on the topic.\n\nLooking carefully at the context of the standard and be clear on the wording.\n\ninterpretation of the standards\n\n11. Any other feedback or comments are welcomed here!\n\nThank you so much!\n\nVery informative. Thank you.\n\nThank you\n\nGood exercise in discussion Standards by relating to the DOK level\n\nGreat session on DOKs and I am better from it\n\nThank you. I would love to meet again to do the 7th and 8th grade standards and perhaps write curriculum too with these DOK elements in mind.\n\nThank you for a great job today. Together we are stronger and can provide a better experience for our students. It was certainly interesting having been on the MS standards writing committee and doing this with the team. Very interesting perspective.\n\nWas a good experience.\n\nThank you for your time.\n\nWisconsin Center for Education Products &amp; Services\n\n1213 N. Sherman Ave., #322   Madison, WI 53704  877-272-5593   WebbAlign@wceps.org   www.webbalign.org\n\nWisconsin Center for Education Products &amp; Services\n\n1213 N. Sherman Ave., #322   Madison, WI 53704  877-272-5593   info@wceps.org   www.wceps.org",
