    "id": "ed936957-2992-47bf-bbc5-a7f2385276d0",
    "title": "[Type text][Type text][Type text] (Part 2/2)",
    "content": "Learning about DOK – I had no experience with it; Comparing ELA to math standards; Difference of DOK between math and ELA; difficulty vs complexity was a huge eye opener + group discussions; difficulty vs complexity; information on difficulty vs complexity; DOK levels and practicing; activities to see how there is different interpretations, discussing tasks with DOK levels; getting a working definition of DOK – makes it easier to grasp; The alignment between DOK levels and standards and getting everyone on the same page. Clarity because it helps with student engagement; Difficulty vs complexity – it ensures I can work on engagement with all levels of text; the practice and calibration around standards to “calibrate”; getting clarity on complexity vs difficulty and then discussing samples to get a better idea\n\n5. What would you do differently if you were to facilitate this type of session? Why?\n\nStart with brief description of DOK and how used; I thought it was run well, I enjoyed the time to talk with colleagues and share ideas; None, well done; N/A; nothing; have all teachers from the school involved – then it would be easier to bring back to our teams; Thought it was great; Multiple days; What does DOK look like in a classroom – maybe some sort of video? I would include a video because most of the time a visual helps. Make teams attend together! This was amazing – thank you, Sara!! I hope our district utilizes you much more; have admin representative; Have more admin + staff representation so the whole building/district participates",
