  {
    "id": "cda58d49-5ac6-4522-be97-8b771857e511",
    "title": "ITEM-LEVEL CONSIDERATIONS",
    "content": "Title: ITEM-LEVEL CONSIDERATIONS\n\nContent: ITEM-LEVEL CONSIDERATIONS  Does the assessment item or task measure what it intends to measure?  •  Is the item/task aligned with content from the course and the intended content standards?  •  Does the item/task require student engagement at a DOK level appropriate to the assessment target (learning expectation)?  •  Is the assessment item/task relevant to student life or authentic?  Does the assessment item or task respect the diversity of the assessment population?  •  Is the item/task sensitive to test taker characteristics (consider gender, age, ethnicity, socio-economic level, disability, language, etc.) and experiences  •  Does the item/task avoid content that might unfairly advantage or disadvantage any student subgroup?  •  Does the item/task represent all people respectfully?  Does the assessment item or task have clear visuals (when essential to an item) and clear formatting?  •  Are the visuals labeled and do they have clearly defined features?  •  Does the item/task have sufficient blank space and clear text?  •  Is the overall appearance clean and organized?  Does the assessment have concise and readable text?  •  Does text consist of commonly used words (except when vocabulary is being tested)? Are unnecessary words minimized?  •  Are vocabulary and sentence complexity appropriate for the grade level used?  •  Are technical terms and abbreviations avoided if not related to the content being measured?  •  Is the question to be answered clearly identifiable?  •  Is there anything included that might distract test-takers from their work?  Is the assessment reliable?  •  Is the grading scheme consistent? Would a student receive the same grade for the same work on multiple evaluations?  •  Can multiple people use the scoring mechanism and come up with the same general score?  •  Does the rubric or other scoring tool allow for appropriate inferences as relates to the learning expectation and as relates to student work?  •  Have colleagues reviewed the items/tasks and provided feedback?  ASSESSMENT-LEVEL CONSIDERATIONS  Does the assessment adequately meet the criteria of the intended use? (What do you want to learn from students’ scores?)  •  If formative does it generate diagnostic feedback to improve learning?  •  If summative, is it built upon multiple sources of data?  •  Will the information derived from the assessment help to improve teaching and learning?  Does the assessment address an appropriate depth, breadth, and balance of the corresponding learning targets?  •  Do the DOK levels of the assessment items/task correspond, overall, to the DOK levels of the assessment targets?  •  Are any learning targets overemphasized on the assessment?  •  Do the items/tasks on the assessment sample from the full range or breadth of assessment targets?  References  American Educational Research Association, American Psychological Association, & National Council on Measurement in Education. (2014). Standards for educational and psychological testing. Washington DC: American Psychological Association.  Joint Committee on Testing Practices (2004). Code of Fair Testing Practices in Education. Washington, DC  National Research Council (2001). Knowing what students know: The science and design of educational assessment. National Academy Press: Washington DC.  Pellegrino, J. W. (2016). 21st Century Science Assessment: The Future Is Now. (SRI Education White Paper). Menlo Park, CA: SRI International.  Thompson, et. al (2005) Considerations for the Development and Review of Universally Designed Assessments; NCEO Technical Report 42. National Center on Educational Outcomes  WestEd, CCSSO, et. al (2015) Item Specification Guidelines for the Next Generation Science Standards ",
