  {
    "id": "36f65807-f967-46a8-94be-bbf43bc33b34",
    "title": "Aldine CTE Debrief_scc_edits",
    "content": "Title: Aldine CTE Debrief_scc_edits\n\nContent: Debrief: Aldine CTE DOK training 6/5/17 – 6/8/17 From June 5 through June 8, a total of 91 Aldine ISD Career and Technical Education teachers participated in a four-day curriculum development workshop focusing on alignment through Depth of Knowledge. The purpose of the sessions was to help the teams develop a deep shared understanding of the DOK language system and to work toward a common understanding of how DOK can inform curriculum and assessment choices in their fields.  The four days were facilitated by Linda McQuillen and Erin Quast and were structured as follows:  •  Day 1—The group was split into two almost equal sized groups that included a mix of teachers new to the concepts of DOK and teachers who had participated in the workshops that Erin conducted in June 2016. An introductory DOK training was provided addressing the key concepts related to content complexity and the DOK language system.  •  Day 2—Subject teams completed a mini-alignment study. Teams first applied DOK levels to their course TEKS, a process that, in many instances, required that they revise their previous analysis and DOK determinations. Using the DOK lens, teams identified areas for revisions in their existing Scope and Sequence documents for their courses.  •  Day 3—Subject teams continued the mini-alignment study applying DOK to existing course assessments. The DOK levels of assessment items were compared with the DOK levels the teams had assigned to the corresponding TEKS. For many teams, this process illuminated the significance of assessments matching the complexity as well as the content of the TEKS. A small, selected group met separately to draft the DOK definitions for CTE, using all of their work from last summer as well as the generic definitions created by WebbAlign.  •  Day 4—Linda and Erin supported the teachers in a whole group as they worked on the following activities:  • Distribute and discuss the draft document with the DOK definitions for Aldine CTE and the process for on-going editing.  • Revise assessment items to mirror the complexity of the corresponding TEKS.  • Revise/develop Scope and Sequence documents with consideration of DOK and alignment of breadth, complexity, and content in the TEKS.  • Identify DOK 3 and 4 in their TEKS and develop opportunities in their curriculum and assessments to address these levels.  As facilitators, we made a number of key observations in teachers’ learning about DOK, including:  •  Differentiating between difficulty and complexity was significant to participants’ understanding of rigor.  •  Participants recognized that a majority of their existing assessment items were at a DOK 1 level, meaning that there was misalignment of complexity with many of the TEKS.  •  Writing DOK 2 multiple-choice assessment items and generating ideas for DOK level 3 items required extensive support from us.  •  Teachers made large advances in deepening understanding of the initiative around cohesion in Aldine’s TLP and CTE teachers’ roles in attaining it.  Recommended next steps for the CTE team in regard to DOK include:  •  Support the CTE Program Directors and teacher leaders in their plans for rolling out DOK in the fall 2017 Aldine staff orientation.  •  Structure time for CTE staff to continue to apply DOK to curriculum and assessment.  •  Provide further professional learning in assessment writing and in developing items aligned to DOK levels.  •  Address the need for teachers who have not received DOK training to receive it.  •  Gather and incorporate revisions of the DOK definitions for CTE and release the final document to Aldine. ",
