  {
    "id": "3254c6eb-3ba1-4a7c-b2fe-1b9a10dfed42",
    "title": "Enhancing Writing Proficiency through a Literacy Learning Cycle (Part 1/1)",
    "content": "Enhancing Writing Proficiency through a Literacy Learning Cycle  Day 1  Workshop Description  In this interactive workshop, participants explore an apprenticeship approach to writing instruction known as the Literacy Learning Cycle, a pedagogical framework designed to scaffold academic writing instruction through interactive reading, academic discussions, critical thinking tasks, and language development. This approach can be adapted to any content area, enabling multilingual learners to become effective and informed writers across genres and disciplines.  Intended Outcomes  After this workshop, participants will be able to:  ▪  Engage students in tasks that build deep knowledge on a topic  ▪  Support students in reading a variety of genre-specific texts  ▪  Use mentor texts to explore the purposes, organizational structures, and language features of specific genres  ▪  Support students as they apply learning in writing tasks  ▪  Examine student work with a focus on genre-specific features  Introductions  Nací en Chihuahua Chihuahua México, mi papa y mi mamá también nacieron en Chihuahua . Llegue a los EEUU a las 5 años  Mi bisabuela nos contaba historias de Pancho Villa (José Doroteo Arango Arámbula) y como llego a conocerlo. .  Started teaching in 1995 in Las Vegas NV when Bilingual Program started, taught: K, 1st , 3rd Middle School 6th English & 7th Science. ELL specialist for secondary schools Instructional coach for 25 schools. Family coordinator for HS lastly, Family Engagement Specialist for Pre-K to 12th grade districtwide.  Left to work for WIDA in 2014  Ivonne Margarita Oronoz Rosas  First time my young men make tamales, want them to start experiencing their culture.  Getting to Know You  Linguistic Support  1.  Middle of tent: your first name (front fold)Bottom left: Your position  2.  Bottom right: years in education  3.  Bottom left: role  4.  Middle top: response to prompt  1 3 5 7 9 ¿Ayúdame? ¿Cuándo comemnos?¡Necesito café!¡Contenta/Contento!¡Estoy bien!  I am ok!  Help?  Happy  I need coffee!  When’s lunch?  2 64 8 10 Kind of sad*Sigh*Eager to learn! I am not here. No estoy aqui. *Suspirando* Un poco triste ¡Deseoso/a de aprender¡******* *******  Ivonne  Working Agreements What do you need to be a successful learner? How do you prefer to collaborate?What is most important for you in a workshop?  When Do We Write?  Considering Everyday Writing  1.  List the different ways you interacted with written communication in the last two days.  2.  Consider how this interaction might have been different 10 years ago.  3.  Be prepared to share.  Everyday Writing Examples  Exploring Written Communication  1.  Explore the examples of writing as a table.  2.  Discuss the purpose and mode of the communication and determine whether or not it was the most appropriate way to communicate the message.  3.  Be prepared to share.  Why Do We Write?  1.  Consider:  ◦  examples of writing you just explored  ◦  your reflection on how you have recently used writing in your daily life.  2.  Brainstorm a list of the reasons people write.  3.  Enter your responses: bit.ly/WritingPurpose  Purposes for Writing  •  Inform  •  Entertain  •  Explain  •  Persuade  •  Argue  •  Evaluate  •  Express  Sadlier School  People use writing as a tool to communicate thought, ideas, histories, stories, opinions, and knowledge to a broad array of audiences.  Describing Effective Writers  1.  Consider the characteristics that make a piece of writing effective.  2.  Now think about effective writers.  3.  Work together to create a chart in response to the following prompt:  What do they typically know, do, or understand about writing?  Characteristics of Effective Writers  1.  Read through the set of cards.  2.  Compare them to your chart.  3.  Discuss whether you agree or disagree with each.  Baby Boomer  •  Born 1946-1964  •  Prefers face-to-face communication  •  Starting to embrace modern digital technology  •  Social Media Preferences: Facebook & Linkedln  Generation X  •  Born 1965-1979  •  Prefers email communication  •  Comfortable with technology  •  Social Media Preferences: Facebook & Twitter  Millennial  •  Born 1980-1994  •  Prefers email or text  •  First \"digitally native\" generation  •  Social Media Preferences: Not loyal to any particular social media outlet  ŁŁŁŁŁŁŁŁŁŁŁ ŁŁ  •  Born 1995-2012  •  Likes written communication, but in-person meetings in the workplace  •  Expects quick responses  •  Social Media Preferences: Instagram & Snapchat  Four Generations of Communicators  Gen Z Communication Preferences  New Rules for Writing?  1.  Now consider your Gen Z students.  2.  Discuss the ways your students use writing in their personal lives?  3.  Work together to add to your chart.  Classroom Writing Practices  1.  Think about your current practice.  2.  Compare your practices to those listed.  3.  Record ideas for implementation.  4.  Consider this generation of learners.  Connections: Effective Writers  •  What connections are you making to the ideas we have discussed so far?  •  How have they affirmed your thinking about  writers and writing? Challenged it? Expanded it?  Believing In Students  “One of the greatest gifts we can give others is to see in them what they cannot yet see in themselves. To fuel that until they own it, because when you help people realize they are much stronger than they think, it can give them a self confidence that can last the rest of their lives.”  The Power of Believing in Others  Paul Castagno TEDx Talk  1 • 1 1 ' ... • .. . . . .  What Lies Ahead . © 2024 CARE Coaching  p7  Page 3  “Believing in your students' ability to develop starts with knowing what they do, what they like, and how to listen to them as learners!”  Connections: Starting with Your Students  •  What connections are you making to the ideas we have discussed so far?  •  How have they affirmed your thinking about  writers and writing? Challenged it? Expanded it?  Learning Cycle  Learning Cycle  Building Knowledge Supporting Reading Exploring a Genre Supporting Writing Using a Genre  Adapted from: Derewianka, B. (n.d.). A teaching and learning cycle. https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf  Building Knowledge Supporting Reading Exploring a Genre Supporting Writing Using a Genre Gradual Release of ResponsibilityIncreasing Student Independence  Adapted from: Derewianka, B. (n.d.). A teaching and learning cycle. https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf  An Introduction  Exploring the Cycle  1.  Read about each component of the Teaching and Learning Cycle.  2.  Engage with the text by:  •  Underlining key ideas,  •  Annotating connections, and  •  Recording questions.  3.  When prompted, share your thoughts with a partner during a “Walk and Talk.”  Composite of 20 student brains taking the same test After sitting quietly After 20 minute walk Research/Scan compliments of Dr. Chuck Hillman University of Illinois  Learning Walks  One-Pager  One-pagers are all the rage lately around teacher circles.  Have fun watching your students represent their thoughts and ideas in this beautiful form.  This simple one pager is an engaging tool for critical thinking that you can use.  One-Pager  •  A border which represents key ideas from what you read  •  An image in the upper left-hand corner to somehow represent what you consider to be the most important in the text so far.  •  Images and/or doodled words in the upper right-hand corner that represent your key understanding from the text.  •  Write a phrase that stood out in the lower left-hand corner  •  Images and/or words in the bottom right-hand corner that show connections between the cycles  •  Middle: Three important points from the text  Connections: Learning Cycle  •  What connections are you making to the ideas we have discussed so far?  •  How have they affirmed your thinking about  writers and writing? Challenged it? Expanded it?  1 Hour  Activity: About You  Sharing a Little about Yourself  1.  Go to the slides: bit.ly/Aboutyoutoday  2.  Number off.  3.  Find the slide with your number.  4.  Complete the information.  Building Knowledge  Building Knowledge  •  Engaging students in language-rich experiences  •  Finding out what students know about a topic  •  Developing shared understandings  Building Knowledge Supporting Reading Exploring a Genre Supporting Writing Using a Genre  Adapted from: Derewianka, B. (n.d.). A teaching and learning cycle. https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf  1.  Record ways you currently “build the field.”  2.  Consider how instructional tasks enable students to become experts on a topic.  3.  Add some new ideas.  4.  Be prepared to share.  Building Context Activities  1.  Choose an instructional activity cards. 2. Summarize your activity for your table teams. 3. Stand up and find someone not at your table and exchange activities.  4.  What would you add?  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp - content/uploads/A - Teaching - and - Learning - Cycle.pdf  https://www.wested.org/resources/scaffolding - writing - through - the - teaching - and - learning - cycle/  Adapted from: Derewianka, B. (n.d.). A teaching and learning cycle. Speycher, P. (2017). Scaffolding writing through the teaching and learning cycle.WestEd.  Start Writing?  1.  Read the text.  2.  Respond to the reflection questions.  3.  Share with someone sitting near you.  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp content/uploads/A Teaching and Learning Cycle.pdf  Adapted from: Derewianka, B. (n.d.). A teaching and learning cycle. ----- Gathering What Students Know  1.  Discuss what you currently do for lessons that may involve difficult concepts.  2.  What questions do you ask?  3.  How do you prepare once you gather information?  4.  How do you support students at different proficiency levels?  5.  Be prepared to share out ideas.  Connections: Building Knowledge  •  What connections are you making to the ideas we have discusses so far?  •  How have they affirmed your thinking? Challenged it? Expanded it?  Supporting Reading  Supporting Reading  In order to write, students need to gather information from texts. Most students, however, will need support in how to read the increasingly complex texts of school.  Building Knowledge Supporting Reading Exploring a Genre Supporting Writing Using a Genre  Adapted from: Derewianka, B. (n.d.). A teaching and learning cycle. https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf  Building Knowledge Supporting Reading Exploring a Genre Supporting Writing Using a Genre Shared Reading Modeled Reading Independent Reading Guided Reading Collaborative Reading Approaches  Modeled Reading  “When I start reading a text, I skim it to see what it will be about.” Explanation: it is usually easier to understand a text if its purpose can be predicted. “I wonder why the natives were in canoes?” Explanation: Active readers interact with the text by asking questions and being curious.  The whole country about the bay is agreeable beyond description and, if properly cultivated, would be a most fertile spot. The hills are covered with beautiful flowering shrubs, which perfume the air, making it odiferous.  The natives, in canoes, invited them hither, and behaved very amicably.  A chief entertained us military exercises, which are performed with the patoo-patoo and the lance.  “I wonder which one is the patoo-patoo?”  Explanation: Good readers look for the relationship between the written text and the accompanying images.  “If I close my eyes, I can picture the scenery in my mind.”  Explanation: Visualization can aid comprehension.  “How can I understand the meaning of this word?”  Explanation: Clues from the text can help define unfamiliar words.  Shared Reading  Teachers read text to the class, engaging students by:  •  asking questions  •  defining vocabulary in context  •  explaining the relationship between written text and images  •  interpreting key messages  •  demonstrating comprehension strategies (e.g., previewing, predicting, questioning, visualizing, etc.)  •  drawing attention to relevant language features (e.g., simple sentences for effect, tightly crafted sentence to express complex ideas, nominalization, etc.)  Guided Reading  Teachers group students based on ability (e.g., reading levels, English language proficiency) to provide differentiated support and engage students in specific aspects of reading texts.  Certain students might be having difficulty following cohesive relationships in a text, so the teacher would work with that group to identify how language creates links across the text.  Some students might need support in decoding words in a mentor texts while others might need help understanding how ideas are elaborated or condensed in texts.  Collaborative Reading  Collaborative reading is a blend of shared reading – students read a text together, guided by the teacher – and close reading – students in small groups to analyze a text.  One five-phase approach emphasizes repeated exposure to the text to deepen comprehension by:  1.  understanding the main idea  2.  annotating the text  3.  identifying key ideas and details  4.  analyzing author’s craft, and  5.  summarizing and drawing conclusions  https://www.edutopia.org/article/driving deep reading comprehension k 5  Adapted from: Driving Deep Reading Comprehension in K–5. (n.d.). Edutopia. ----- Independent Reading  To support the development of academic writing, students choose from a range of texts intentionally selected by the teacher to: 1) continue to “build the field,” and 2) exemplify characteristics of the focus genre.  Independent reading can be accompanied by activities that reinforce skills that have already been introduced, such as:  •  determining purpose  •  drawing conclusions  •  making connections between text and accompanying visuals  https://www.edutopia.org/article/driving deep reading comprehension k 5  Adapted from: Driving Deep Reading Comprehension in K–5. (n.d.). Edutopia. ----- Supporting Literacy  1.  Independently, read the activities for supporting literacy across the curriculum.  2.  As a team, create an anchor chart that visually represents these.  Comprehension across Curriculum  1.  Explore “Habits Of Five Generations” bit.ly/5GenerationsReading  2.  Discuss ways that you engage in all five.  3.  Create an anchor chart that illustrates how you support comprehension across the curriculum  1Ł Ł-a' 111 5t -. -111 , 1!11 --..... -• , -. --Ł _,,A-.\"  Connections: Support Reading  •  What connections are you making to the ideas we have discusses so far?  •  How have they affirmed your thinking? Challenged it? Expanded it?  Why We Writing  Starting with Your Students  Teaching and Learning Cycle  Building Knowledge  Supporting Reading  Review Day 1  H.L.O.  Home Learning Opportunity:  Read and Review the document of your choosing for professional learning  bit.ly/WritingHLO  Ekcl of 1 bo.Ł 1  Previewing Day 2  •  Independent Construction  •  Success Criteria  •  Framing Questions  •  Scaffolding Writing  •  Types of Writing  ▪  Modeled Writing  ▪  Shared Writing  ▪  Guided Writing  ▪  Collaborative Writing  ▪  Joint Construction  •  Facilitating Joint Construction  Building Knowledge Supporting Reading Exploring a Genre Supporting Writing Using a Genre  •  Why Explore Genre?  •  Mentor Texts  •  Characteristics of Genre  •  Sorting Characteristics  •  Identifying Purpose  •  Exploring Structure  •  Examining Language Features",
