    "content": "Enhancing Writing Proficiency through a Literacy Learning  Cycle Day 2  Intended Outcomes  After this workshop, you will be able to:  ▪  build shared understanding of content so students are equipped to write knowledgeably  ▪  use mentor texts to identify explore the purposes, organizational structures and language features of specific genres  ▪  jointly construct a text with students based on what was learned from various mentor texts  ▪  support students as they apply their learning to their independent writing  ▪  engage students in drafting, reviewing, and revising to increase the effectiveness of their writing  Why We Write  Starting with Your Students  Teaching and Learning Cycle  Building Knowledge  Supporting Reading  Review Day 1  Everyday Writing  Characteristics of Effective Writers  Believing In = Knowing  “Believing in your students' ability to develop starts with knowing what they do, what they like, and how to listen to them as learners!”  Learning Cycle  Building Knowledge  Supporting Reading  Shared Reading Modeled Reading Independent Reading Guided Reading Collaborative Reading Approaches  Getting to Know You  1.  Middle of tent: your first name  2.  Top left: oracy and literacy connection?  3.  Bottom right: years in education  4.  Bottom left: role or grade level  How Are You Feeling?  1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9  Exploring a Genre  Exploring a Genre  •  Highlight the purpose, organization, and key language features of a particular genre  •  Use mentor texts to engage in a close analysis of the language characteristics of a given genre  •  Facilitate language-focused conversations to support mentor text analysis  Building Knowledge Supporting Reading Exploring a Genre Supporting Writing Using a Genre  Adapted from: Derewianka, B. (n.d.). A teaching and learning cycle. https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf  Why Explore Genre?  •  Since students will be expected to write in ways that are characteristic of given genres, close analysis of mentor texts and accompanying language-focused discussions provide critical support for all students.  •  Use of mentor texts enables student to learn from exemplary writing that effectively achieves the purpose of a given genre and provides them with an expanded repertoire of language choices that they can then draw from as writers.  Adapted from: Derewianka, B. (n.d.). A teaching and learning cycle. https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf  “Contextualized language use means speakers and writers make lexical choices that vary according to purpose and audience.”  Khote, 2018  Context Matters  Sharing your information as a letter to the editor  Sharing information with a grandparent  p2  Exploring Genre?  1.  Read the text.  2.  Respond to the reflection questions.  3.  Share with someone sitting near you.  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp content/uploads/A Teaching and Learning Cycle.pdf  Adapted from: Derewianka, B. (n.d.). A teaching and learning cycle. ----- Essentials for Exploring a Genre  ▪  Students understand what genres are and why people use a specific genre for a particular purpose.  ▪  Students engage in activities that allow them to discover organizational structures of genres.  ▪  Students learn to “notice and name” critical language features and how to “lift and borrow” from mentor texts.  (p3)  JIGSAW: Considering Mentor Texts  1. Pick a card from the table. 2. Independently read your card. 3. Find someone with the same card. 4. Share your ideas.  Adapted from Dorfman & Cappelli (2007)  VIDEO: Using Mentor Texts  1.  Watch the video.  2.  Record responses to the questions.  3.  Be prepared to share.  Using Mentor Texts in the Classroom  HIGHLIGHT: Guiding Selection  1.  Read through the guiding questions for selecting mentor texts.  2.  Highlight questions you think are critical when identifying mentor texts.  JIGSAW: Examining Texts  1.  Word off: Paragraph-Sentence-Word  2.  Read the activities described in your section:  •  Text- and Paragraph-Level Analysis (p6)  •  Sentence-Level Analysis (p7)  •  Word-Level Analysis (p8)  3.  Share highlights.  https://www.wested.org/resources/scaffolding writing through the teaching and learning cycle/  Taken from: Speycher, P. (2017). Scaffolding writing through the teaching and learning cycle. WestEd. ------- Distinct Characteristics of Genre  Purpose  Structure  Features  Motive  Organization  Components  https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp - content/uploads/A - Teaching - and - Learning - Cycle.pdf  Adapted from: Derewianka, B. (n.d.). A teaching and learning cycle.  Sorting Genres  1.  Look at the sample genres.  2.  Read the characteristics cards.  3.  Sort them by purpose, organization and features for each of the six genres.  Adapted from: Gibbons, P. (2009). English learners, academic literacy, and thinking: learning in the challenge zone. Heinemann.  Personal Genres  Adapted from: Derewianka, B., & Jones, P. (2016). Teaching language in context. Oxford University Press.  Factual Genres  Adapted from: Derewianka, B., & Jones, P. (2016). Teaching language in context. Oxford University Press.  Analytical Genres  Adapted from: Gibbons, P. (2009). English learners, academic literacy, and thinking: learning in the challenge zone. Heinemann.  Debrief  Share your understanding of purpose, organization and features for each of the six genres you reviewed.  Adapted from: Gibbons, P. (2009). English learners, academic literacy, and thinking: learning in the challenge zone. Heinemann.  =-45 -15--_ 40  Purpose  Identifying Purpose  1.  Choose one of the text cards and read it.  2.  Stand up and find someone with the same text.  3.  Discuss the purpose.  4.  Be as precise as you can.  Adapted from: Gibbons, P. (2009). English learners, academic literacy, and thinking: learning in the challenge zone. Heinemann.  “Remember to provide students with a variety of exemplar genre-specific texts and multiple opportunities to understand the purposes of each.”  Anchoring Learning  1.  Return to tables.  2.  Create an anchor chart with three categories:  •  Purpose  •  Organization  •  Language Features  1.  As a team, record key ideas about the purposes of texts and how you might share this with students as you explore genres.  2.  Be prepared to share.  Structure  Identifying Organization Structure  Event Event Event CauseEvent CauseEvent CauseEvent Event Event Event Event Event Outcome Factor Factor Factor Consequence Consequence Consequence InputSequential Explanation Cyclical Explanation Consequential Explanation Causal ExplanationFactorial Explanation  Adapted from: Gibbons, P. (2009). English learners, academic literacy, and thinking: learning in the challenge zone. Heinemann.  INPUT TITLE Definition Cause CONSEQUENCES Consequence 1 Consequence 2 Impacts Consequence 3  The Effects of Global Warming  Global warming is the gradual increase in air temperature around the globe. It is caused by air pollutants, such as carbon dioxide and methane. The rise in air temperatures can lead to higher extinction rates and an increased risk of disease. It may also have political repercussions, such as wars over available resources.  Extinction of Important Species  The biggest risk of global warming comes from habitat change. For example, polar bears rely on cold, snowy regions. As these regions shrink, polar bears will no longer have a place to live. Extinction affects the food chain and puts stress on the surviving animals to find adequate food.  Health Issues  As temperatures rise, so do heat-related illnesses such as heatstroke. Increased air pollution correlates with the number of lung-related illnesses, such as asthma and lung cancer. Rising temperatures and unstable climates can also lead to rapid spread of disease.  Political Conflict  As sea levels and temperatures rise, access to resources such as clean water and land decreases. This could raise the threat of political conflicts. Extreme drought can lead to fighting over the limited resources of water and food.  Exploring Structure  1.  Preview the text.  2.  Choose one dry erase marker for each of the colors of the organizational components of your text.  3.  Individually, use your colored marker to identify all the text that comprises your particular component.  4.  Discuss as a group.  “Routinely engage students in activities that provide them opportunities to examine various organizational structures across a range of genre-specific mentor texts.”  Anchoring Learning  1.  As a team, record notes about the organization of texts and how you might share this with students as you explore genres.  2.  Be prepared to share.  Features  Developing Language Detectives  “ As students embark on language exploration tasks, teachers might encourage them to view themselves as “language detectives” working to find the hidden content meanings revealed in the clues of language. Language exploration tasks should be interactive, engaging, and enticing. “  https://www.wested.org/resources/scaffolding writing through the teaching and learning cycle/  Speycher, P. (2017). Scaffolding writing through the teaching and learning cycle.WestEd. ------- Further Consideration  “Often, students are told to write in their own words rather than to copy from books. However, if children do not learn the language of books, they will never acquire academic language. Therefore, the task is to support students in learning the language used in books.”  Brisk, 2015  How does this quote affirm or expand your thinking regarding how to apprentice students into academic genres of writing?  Two-Step Detective Process  1)  Noticing and Naming – Reading like writers to identify and describe specific features that authors use in a particular genre.  2)  Lifting and Borrowing – Highlighting specific language features so they can be appropriated and incorporated into student writing.  Examining Language Features  Explanation Texts  Language Features Cards  Notice and Name  1.  Match the language features cards to the texts.  2.  Notice and name as many examples of the sample language features as you can.  3.  Find someone who worked with a different text.  4.  Share.  Lift and Borrow  1.  Look at the texts again.  2.  Consider what you might lift and borrow.  3.  Create a chart to share.  Additional Consideration  “Nobody is born with a style or a voice. … We learn by copying. We’re talking about practice, not plagiarism. Plagiarism is trying to present the work of someone else as your own. Copying is about reverse-engineering. It’s like a mechanic taking apart a car to see how it works.”  -Steal Like an Artist, Austin Kleon  How would you explain the difference between “lifting and borrowing” as an instructional practice, separate and distinct from the concept of plagiarism?  Anchoring Learning  1.  As a team, record notes about the language features of texts and how you might share this with students as you explore genres.  2.  Be prepared to share.  Notice, Name, Lift & Borrow  .l gEngูlshpeple \\ ·as�salnn เ์B6hun's ls Rcacn coleoe ) �ๆ� �� � st Huc wdad Lo lRe/sasGst คี่งปีฐี-85ht 'o-katiPT it าhrs ม.ไว5:-ัะ:·ws '68a (ะ77ว2ค่อืแ+mw, 8 � 2: hidh Soตd ยua la A  Cyclical Explanation  Purpose  Explain a Cyclical Phenomenon  HOW? WHY? BOTH?  Organizational Structure  Water is essential to life on Earth. In its three states – solid, liquid, and gas – water links components of the climate system on Earth, including air, clouds, salty and fresh bodies of water, and vegetation. The water cycle outlines the continuous movement of water within the Earth and atmosphere, namely the journey water takes as it circulates from the land and water to the sky and back again.  Phenomenon Identification  The water cycle goes through four main stages: evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection.  Explanation Sequence  When the sun heats water in oceans, lakes, rivers, and on the ground, it causes the water to change from a liquid to a gas and to rise up into the sky. This is called evaporation.  Phase 1  As the evaporating water reaches colder temperatures, it cools, turning back into tiny water droplets, which in turn form clouds. We refer to this as condensation.  Phase 2  Eventually, clouds become too full of water droplets. As they become too full, the water droplets fall, and we have rain or snow or some other type of precipitation.  Phase 3  At the collection stage, some of the water stays on the earth’s surface in reservoirs, lakes, and oceans. Other water seeps down into the ground. When the water reaches the ground, it is heated again by the sun and the cycle begins again.  Phase 4  It takes about nine days to complete the water cycle.  Conclusion  Reflection  1.  Consider the organizational structure and the text that corresponds to each.  2.  Discuss changes you might make to this mentor text to make it more exemplary.  Language Features  Reflection  1.  Look at the water cycle text.  2.  Consider what you might notice and name. 3. Identify what you might lift and borrow.  4.  Discuss at your table.  Examining Student Work  1.  Look at the two student writing samples.  2.  Identify language features associated with cyclical explanations.  3.  Find a “sole mate.”  4.  Share.  he اماer cyg/e s مد,irporan! هar{ ٧١ rre '0e T pu�بوء،١حن, ب F«t, ,\"مبلاهاa ي،و'ماd, اke اوام إ، »م,}خرء،مزةهأء,أ{ومهمالل: tghl, anndtHeصsu'مهاوmonمadلhaa مote ا 4he, 'codes&tion o.مeyapr risesuأaلا (aه tke' بوابا vمor is جنا. iأ sماما to{ iآفo ي وشائبا{بإوويماءodl 4عتهhe clods {lease ااle اateث'ب«le#ك, uch ك cم! بجله،ي،وآوأمء[ًءlلأً.بمk/ً.بازم{ (hك's و}{0انا uلaler,عomes ouدn {o ee./'heمموهمtg»نوومبeإصg, u,لإdهط٥ heلkeeuجدناًبهodا\"eoaak{ما{\"ولما اصناsسsة  p10  Cyliaa! Explanateم Eمmpke tهnguهge 6eب ٣٤\"\"'منميدهم؟ وبو يesي٣• وبمكرر»ي،متت تجؤي يونوم» Tet: mNe ٤إنا cyrkeofa RAم  lhe vapour nuen then into a m Ł Jura  p11  Supporting Writing  Three Approaches  •  Modelled  •  Collaborative  •  Joint Construction  What is Modeled Writing?  ▪  Modelled writing provides an opportunity for students to observe a proficient turning ideas into a written form as teachers make their own thinking and writing processes visible, so students can clearly see how the text is constructed.  ▪  Students do not offer ideas or suggestions during the modelling, rather they observe strategies they will eventually incorporate into their own independent writing.  https://www.education.vic.gov.au/school/teachers/teachingresources/discipline/english/literacy/writing/P  ages/teachingpracmodelled.aspx  Adapted from: Literacy Teaching Toolkit. Victoria State Government Education and Training.  The Role of the Teacher  ▪  Teacher is in complete control.  ▪  Teacher makes explicit exactly what is being modelled and why.  ▪  Teacher may engage students in discussion after the modelling to clarify the expectation that the modelled actions and strategies will be incorporated into the students’ own independent writing.  https://www.education.vic.gov.au/school/teachers/teachingresources/discipline/english/literacy/writing/P  ages/teachingpracmodelled.aspx  Adapted from: Literacy Teaching Toolkit. Victoria State Government Education and Training.  The Role of Students  ▪  Students observe the actions, strategies and decisions modelled during the writing process.  ▪  Students are actively engaged through questioning by the teacher that encourages them to:  •  identify the strategies being employed  •  consider why they are being used, and  •  determining whether they are effective  ▪  Students are expected to incorporating the actions and strategies in their own writing.  https://www.education.vic.gov.au/school/teachers/teachingresources/discipline/english/literacy/writing/P  ages/teachingpracmodelled.aspx  Adapted from: Literacy Teaching Toolkit. Victoria State Government Education and Training.  What is Collaborative Writing?  Collaborative writing is a process for co-constructing text in which all members contribute. It involves:  ▪  Interaction among group members throughout the process (e.g., brainstorming, drafting, and revising)  ▪  Consensus-building so that all members are actively engaged in decisions related to the text (e.g., organizational structure, word choice, etc.)  ▪  Cooperation in the development of a single text  Why Collaborate?  Collaboration helps students:  •  understand writing as a process  •  develop a sense of audience  •  better understand the conventions of academic discourse  •  recognize that academic writing conventions are not simply arbitrary rules, but reflect readers’ expectations  •  explain and sometimes defend their writing choices  https://www.vanderbilt.edu/bold/tools/collaborative writing/#:~:text=Collaborative%20or%20team%20writing%20is,how%20the%20group%20will%20function  Adapted from: Vanderbilt University. -.  What is Joint Construction?  ▪  Joint construction involves the teacher and students working together to collaboratively construct a text.  ▪  Teachers scaffold the co-construction of text through questions, think-alouds, and explanations, etc.  ▪  This approach is best supported when student have:  •  already build knowledge of a topic  •  been explicitly taught purpose, organization and language features of a particular genre  Benefits for MLLs  ▪  Provides a context for talking and learning about organizational structures and language features  ▪  Supports students in understanding how purpose and audience impact language choices  ▪  Scaffolds the students through the writing process  ▪  Allows students to write a text that they might not be able to write independently in a supportive context  ▪  Offers a model students can use during independent construction of genre-specific writing tasks  Steps in Joint Construction  Ideas for Differentiating  VIDEO: Joint Construction  So what we're going to Ł do now is write our Ł own explanation, L-LI  Using a Genre  Using a Genre  •  Engaging students in language-rich experiences  •  Finding out what students know about a topic  •  Developing shared understandings  Building Knowledge Supporting Reading Exploring a Genre Supporting Writing Using a Genre  Adapted from: Derewianka, B. (n.d.). A teaching and learning cycle. https://victesol.vic.edu.au/wp-content/uploads/A-Teaching-and-Learning-Cycle.pdf  Independent Construction  Review, Reflect, Revise  Framing Questions  Pulling it All Together  Learning Cycle  Building Knowledge Supporting Reading Exploring a Genre Supporting Writing Using a Genre",
