    "title": "Unlocking Rigor: Practical Applications of Webb’s Depth of Knowledge (DOK) (Part 1/1)",
    "content": "Unlocking Rigor: Practical Applications of Webb’s Depth of Knowledge (DOK)  Sara Christopherson, Director of WebbAlign sara@wceps.org  Unlocking Rigor: Practical Applications of Webb’s Depth of Knowledge (DOK)  Please sign in using the QR code of the link below! bit.ly/CARESIGN  Today’s Schedule  •  Our meeting is from 9:00 am – 4:30 pm  •  Lunch break at noonish.  What is rigor?  Introductions  Select a spokesperson for your table.  1.  Names, home bases, professional role(s)  2.  Six degrees of separation  3.  Any particular questions about DOK?  Purpose  Purposes of Today’s Meeting:  •  Contextualize DOK within an aligned (coherent) education system  •  Work toward a common understanding of cognitive complexity  •  Differentiate difficulty from complexity (and from other factors)  •  Review common misinterpretations of DOK  •  Practice using DOK to promote calibration within our team  •  Identify specific applications for use of DOK within schools and districts  Goals  Here are the goals we advertised:  Participants will:  • Differentiate DOK levels, addressing common misconceptions.  • Apply the DOK framework to curriculum, instruction, and assessment contexts.  • Develop an action plan for implementation or improvement.  Self-Reflection Stoplight  Hold up, I need a better map I need to work on this a little more This extended or affirmed my thinking  We have many important tools and considerations that all work together to support students’ achievement of educational goals.  � -,_ �� . H, • iJiii/F)Jr*w 1/?:_./·..if / ·..__ 1 ,..,_ ·--1 -. ----: -:--.•;-..._ ���-�··. •• .J\\ ► •••• !!a�;;;;..� . 1 • � ----' -. ' �_...., . -J .....) Q� ---'1\\ti • .,..0r�..... . \\, --,--:-.: --\\ , 't);�� . ') ->-�� � --�-. _- What is (are) the purpose(s) of academic content standards? Talk with your table. Then, share out.  Academic standards guide…  •  equitable educational opportunity  •  assessment development  •  curriculum development  •  teachers’ practices  •  development of learning objectives  •  unit/lesson development  •  teacher professional development  •  teacher education  •  and more  Source: WCEPS/WebbAlign, 2023  “Alignment refers to how well all policy elements in a system work together to guide instruction and, ultimately, student learning.”  Alignment: A powerful tool for focusing instruction, curricula, and assessment  Alignment is the degree to which expectations, curriculum, and assessments are in agreement and serve in conjunction with one another to guide the system toward students learning what is expected.  (Webb, 1997)  There were (and are) multiple goals of standards-based reform.  Cognitive Social and Emotional Dispositional Collaborative Behavioral  For example…“developing students’ self-worth and promoting collective responsibility”…  A core purpose is to set “higher-level cognitive goals” for all students.  “…depth of understanding, and complex thinking” …“knowledge construction through analysis and synthesis of real life problems” …“production of knowledge, rigor in thinking, and sustained intellectual effort” vs “isolated facts and skills” and “broad coverage of unconnected facts.”  (Smith and O’Day, 1988)  RIGOR  “…alignment and cognitive rigor…based on rigorous standards…aligned with rigorous standards…access to rigorous coursework…rigorous and of higher quality…”  From: https://oese.ed.gov/files/2020/10/implementing_the_every_student_succeeds_act-1.pdf  What is RIGOR?  Strictness and hard work  Rigor is used in various ways, to mean various things, as relates to various topics.  Qualifier of the evidentiary basis in educational research and evaluation  General environment Academic Content Evaluative determination  Within standards-based education, rigor is typically closely linked with cognitive complexity.  Rigorous standards are those that “ include high-level cognitive demands by asking students to demonstrate deep conceptual understanding through the application of content knowledge and skills to new situations.”  These types of high-level cognitive demands involve thinking skills like “reasoning, justification, synthesis, analysis, and problem-solving.”  (CCSS development criteria)  Standards explicate not just what students are expected to know but also specify expectations for the complexity of cognitive engagement with disciplinary concepts and ideas.  But wait…what is complexity?Okay, so rigor is the complexity of cognitive engagement.  COMPLEXITY as relates to the PROCESS OF LEARNING?  COMPLEXITY as relates to the PROCESS OF TEACHING?  COMPLEXITY as relates to the PROCESS OF SOLVING A PROBLEM?  COMPLEXITY as relates to STUDENT PERFORMANCE?  COMPLEXITY as relates to ACADEMIC TOPICS?  COMPLEXITY as relates to TEXTS - text complexity?  COMPLEXITY as relates to LANGUAGE and ELP Development - linguistic complexity?  COMPLEXITY as relates to an EXPECTATION or TASK?  Warm Up: Which expectation is most complex?  A.  Interpret symptoms of common car problems to suggest likely repair needs.  B.  Correctly install a car battery.  C.  Propose defensible systemic changes to improve automobile safety regulations.  Self-Reflection Stoplight  Hold up, I need a better map I need to work on this a little more This extended or affirmed my thinking  Sort the cards by task complexity  Our perceptions of complexity are based on conceptual constructs that may vary.  A grounding assumption of a standards-based system is that the language of the standards communicates the expected complexity of cognitive engagement with disciplinary content – for all students.  We must be able to use a content analysis…  …to make an inference…  …about the cognitive complexity required to successfully meet an expectation or for successful task completion.  A coherent standards-based system requires consistency in interpretation, communication, and evaluation of expectations.  \"If teachers are inconsistent in the way they interpret standards, then students’ opportunity to learn will be varied according to their teachers’ understanding.\" (Dickenson, et al., 2020)  Inconsistent interpretations also affect curriculum and assessment development and evaluation.  Distinguishing between and among DOK 1, 2, 3, and 4 requires using the DOK definitions.  DOK 1  •  Solve the linear equation.  •  Correctly spell 10 terms.  •  Define the word “liminal.”  •  Describe the characteristics of a right triangle.  •  Draw a circle, square, rectangle, and triangle.  •  Match the carnivore with its prey.  DOK 2  •  Summarize the main idea of the article.  •  Compare and contrast the roles of a president and the roles of a prime minister.  •  Draw a floor plan of this room (to scale).  •  Define the difference between a short story and an essay.  DOK 3  •  Provide a rationale for your solution to the problem.  •  Describe how the story would be different if it were set in the year 2015 instead of 1915.  •  Discuss what you think the author of Story A would think about Story B.  •  Draw a 5-10 panel cartoon that presents an alternative ending to the story you just read.  •  Define the terms and conditions under which children less than 15 years old should be allowed to work.  DOK 4  •  Develop a research-based argument for whether or not the U.S. government should provide money to fund space exploration.  Does the verb tell you how complex the task is?  Define the term “applet.”  Define your personal philosophy of education.  1. Evaluate the shapes. Which ones are circles?  Evaluate the relative success of reading programs A, B, and C, using qualitative and quantitative data from sampled schools.  3. Evaluate the expression x + 2 for x = 5  Connect Synthesize Apply Concepts Memorize Who, What, When, Where, Why peat State Name peat Tabulate Tell Recognize Cause/Effect Estimate Construct Modify Predict Interpret Distinguish Use Context Cues Make Observations Develop aL vgument Constru Use Concepts to Solve Non-Routine Problems Compare Explain Phenomena n Terms of Concepts Formulate Investigate Draw Conclusions Hypothesize Cite Evidence  One way to define cognitive complexity in the context of a standards-based system is by categorizing the types of cognitive demands within the academic standards themselves.  Conduct a content analysis of the academic standards documents.  Identify patterns, themes, categories (requires input from subject area experts).  Derive underlying ideas about the factors that influence complexity of engagement, as represented by the standards for each subject area.  This is how the Depth of Knowledge (DOK) framework was developed.  G GENERAL AUDIENCESŁ ALL AGES ADMITTED J  PG PARENTAL GUIDANCE SUGGESTED SOME MATERIAL MAY NOT BE SUITABLE FOR CHILDREN]  PG-13[PARENTS STRONGLY CAUTIONED« SOME MATERIAL MAY BE INAPPROPRIATE FOR CHILDREN UNDER 13 ---------------® RRESTRICTED UNDER 17 REQUIRES ACCOMPANYING PARENT OR ADULT GUARDIAN _J  Credit: Motion Pictures Association  e Ł  International Scale of River Difficulty  EASY  ♦  Easy difficulty with low consequences. Smooth water with a clear passage through slight current or ruffles; can have gentle curves. No gradient loss. No hazards present or maneuvering required.  NOVICE  ♦  Moderate difficulty with low consequences. Moving water with identifi­able rapids and regular waves; clear passages exist between rocks and hazards. Minimal gradient loss. Some maneuvering is required.  INTERMEDIATE  ♦  Moderate difficulty and some consequences. Fast water with standing waves; rocks, eddies and hazards exist with clear passages through narrower channels. Some gradient loss. Maneuvering is required.  ADVANCED  ♦  Hard, with major consequences. Long and/or powerful rapids with dynamic features; rocks, recirculating hydraulics, boiling eddies and other hazards. Significant gradient loss. Precise maneuvering required.  EXPERT  ♦  Very hard, with major consequences. Long and powerful rapids with dynam­ic features; rocks, recirculating hydraulics, boiling eddies and other hazards. Substantial and continuous gradient loss. Exact maneuvering required.  EXPERT+  ♦  Previously considered un-navigable rapids. Unavoidable hazards and extreme consequence. Substantial gradient loss.  Academic expectations can be categorized by type.  DOK 1: Requires recall and reproduction of taught facts, concepts, procedures, protocols, etc; Routine tasks; fluency.  DOK 3: Requires engagement with hypothetical or abstract constructs, evaluative tasks, heavy reasoning.  This QR Code goes to Edutopia article. Could go to the DOK 2: Involves website /definitions understandinginstead? I have done applicationboth, depending onand connections ideas. context. Will bring printouts of some materials, too.  DOK 4: Applies to high complexity, iterative tasks that require extended time to complete with greater metacognitive demands.  What do we mean by DOK levels?  •  They ARE levels of increasing complexity.  •  They ARE NOT levels of importance.  •  They ARE NOT levels of progression or a prescribed sequence.  DOK 1 DOK 2 DOK 3 DOK 4  Learning does not progress strictly from low to high complexity.  DOK 1 DOK 2 DOK 3 DOK 4  Learning involves an interconnected network of ideas that get restructured and elaborated as needed.  Self-Reflection Stoplight  Hold up, I need a better map I need to work on this a little more This extended or affirmed my thinking  DOK Calibration with Test Items  Depth of Knowledge (Webb, 1997)  DOK 1  requires recall of information, use of a taught procedure or protocol, routine tasks, etc.  DOK 2  involves use of underlying conceptual understanding, connections between and among ideas and relationships, etc.  DOK 3  requires heavy reasoning, abstract thinking, may involve novel solutions, etc.  DOK 4  applies to high complexity tasks that require extended time to complete, for example a research-based argument or an authentic science investigation, etc.  Work through the problem as a whole group.  On your own, assign a DOK.  Discuss at your table.  Discuss as whole group.  Grade 4  Which of these means about the same as the word gauge?  a.balance  b.measure  c.select  a.warn  Grade 8  A car odometer registered 41,256.9 miles when a highway sign warned of a detour 1,200 feet ahead. What will the odometer read when the car reaches the detour? (5,280 feet = 1 mile)  (a)  42,456.9  (b)  41,279.9  (c)  41,261.3  (d)  41,259.2  (e)  41,257.1  Did you use the calculator on this question?    Yes No  Which of these conclusions is best supported by information from the passage?  a.  If a candidate meets the personal and educational qualifications and is in fair physical shape, his or her chances of becoming an agent are very good.  b.  Compared with other law enforcement agencies in the country, the F.B.I. has a low success rate for tracking down and apprehending suspected offenders.  c.  The job of an agent is not for everyone; it takes someone with special training who is not afraid of danger and doesn’t mind being socially isolated at times.  d.  The life of a federal investigator is not as interesting as most people think; agents spend most of their time working at desks. Grade 8  Grade 5 Sample Item Cluster: Lights Out!  I  Grade 5 Science Question 2 Ł Lights Out! (Part 1) Students are learning about eyes in science class. During their class discussion, power outage occurs and the lights go out in the classroom. While the teacher looks for a flashlight, one student exclaims, \"I can't see anything!\" The teacher tuns on a flashlight and points it across the classroom to a pla table. The teacher says, \"This makes me wonder how we are able to see plant.\" Ł Review/End Test I age Training Student lect information about the world i Which statement best describes how th formation is processed? n Ł ght from the eyes is sent directly to the brain to be processed. Ł Light is processed in the eyes, allowing the Ł object to be seen immediately. © The eyes retied light bad< to the object as the information about the 