ne/typical problem type ------------------------------------------------------------Non-routine/novel problem type  Straightforward/literal thinking ---------------------------------------------------------------Inferential/figurative thinking  No transfer --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------Far transfer  No metacognitive demands---------------------------------------------------------- Extensive metacognitive demands  Definitions in a Nutshell: Difficulty (that is inherent to a task)  Learn theWhich is more difficult? Play the game.  What makes this question difficult?  0.82 X 1.34 If learning goal is to use standard algorithm to multiply two three-digit numbers, then excess difficulty – no need for decimals. If learning goal is to use standard algorithm to multiply two three-digit decimals, then necessary difficulty – there is a need for decimals.  Alas, all the good intentions that I had had had had  no effect on the intended outcomes.  Source: [common “English is hard” memes]  What makes this question difficult?  Which of the following is least likely to not be partially incorrect?  What does that even mean??  Is one of these items more difficult than the other?  Item C What is the name of this plant?  a.  American sycamore  b.  Box elder  c.  Swamp maple  d.  Sweet gum  Item D What is the name of this plant?  a.  Carrot  b.  Corn  c.  Potato  d.  Tomato  Is one of these more difficult than the other?  Item E What brands are shown here? Item F What brands are shown here?  y y  We can be intentional about factors within the realm of curriculum and instructional design.  Target: Compare similarities and differences in the setting of two versions of the same story.Difficulty & Complexity  Romeo and Juliet  Read the two texts. What is the difference in setting?  Romeo and Juliet Source: https://www.mangashakespeare.com/titles/romeo_and_juliet.html Source: https://www.classicalcomics.com/produc