xity of cognitive engagement.  Takeaways:  DOK is an evaluative tool that helps us talk effectively and consistently about complexity of cognitive engagement.  Difficulty and complexity are related but different.  There are several common misunderstandings and misrepresentations of DOK.  Successful application of the language of DOK within a team is an ongoing process.  DOK can inform standards interpretation, lesson and assessment developnent and evaluation practices.  DOK informs alignment efforts; alignment is a necessary component of a standards based education system.  Don’t think the remaining slides will be part of the June session.  It can be helpful to have the Blooms taxonomy slides on hand at the end of the deck – just in case participants are stuck on that topic….  _J  WebbAlign Lesson Plan Considerations: Webb's DOK  What information in the lesson plan indicates that there is potential for alignment with learning E DOK consistency? How well do the instructional decisions and questions provide students with th at the desired level of DOK?  Standard(s):  What is the DOK of the standard(s) addressed in this lesson?  Learning Objective(s):  Check the translation of standards into learning objectives -Is the complexity expressed in the standa the learning goals? (Note: multiple learning objectives with varying DOKs may be necessary to fully m essential DOK level of the standard should still be met through the course of the objectives.)  Activities/Tasks  How will students engage with the topics/content/material in this lesson? Do these activities/tasks pro to engage at the intended levels of complexity?  Instructional Questions:  What questions will be used to prompt students? Do these questions aim to involve students at the int  Difficulty/Complexity  Is the amount of difficulty involved in the tasks/materials necessary for meeting the intended comp/exit students to access materials at the intended level?  Where do you anticipate students may struggle with