    "content": "Engaging English Learners in Math Classrooms  CARE Coach Name, CARE COACH Date School or District Supporting  Overview  Agenda  ▪  Overview  ▪  Introductions  ▪  Framing OUR Language  ▪  Standards for Mathematical Practices  ▪  Break  ▪  Connecting SMPs to ELD Standards  ▪  Lunch  ▪  Amplifying Language in a Math Lesson  ▪  Break  ▪  Present Amplified Math Lessons  ▪  Closing / Evaluation  Logistics  Objectives  ▪  Analyze Standards for Mathematical Practice (SMPs)  ▪  Connect ELD Standards to Standards for  Mathematical Practice  ▪  Identify opportunities for language amplifications in a math content lesson  ▪  Apply language amplifications to an upcoming math content lesson  Resources  Notetaker  ▪  Download a copy  ▪  Includes links, note taking areas  https://bit.ly/49vMnQE  Identities and Roles  ▪ Collaborators  ▪ Learners  ▪ Reflective Practitioners  ▪ Language Analysts  We all have unique expertise that will enhance the conversation.  Why So Much Note-Taking?  How Long after Learning?  Forgotten?  Sixty Minutes  50%  One Day  70%  One Week  90%  https://www.shponline.co.uk/memory-like-goldfish-get-90-improvement-safety-training/  ) \"\\f you can't explain it simply, you don't understand it well enough.\" Albert Einstein  © 2024 CARE Coaching  Introductions  Who’s in the Room?  Getting to Know You  Linguistic Support  1.  Middle of tent: your first name  2.  Top left: Why Math?  3.  Bottom right: years in education  4.  Bottom left: role or grade level  Ground Rules  Take responsibility for your own learning.  Participate by sharing your own experiences. Be willing to experiment with the ideas and techniques  presented.  Honor time limits.  Listen to learn.  Take risks.  Be respectful of those speaking.  Be digitally responsible.  Enjoy yourself!  Framing OUR Language  Asset-Based Philosophy  ▪  Reflects the belief that everyone has valuable resources they can use to support their own and others’ learning  ▪  Asserts that linguistically and culturally diverse learners bring a unique set of assets that enrich any learning community  Multilingual Learners  ▪  Supports an asset-based belief system  ▪  Describes students who use, understand, or engage with languages in addition to English on a regular basis.  Growth Mindset  ▪  “People believe that their most basic abilities can be developed through dedication and hard work.”  ▪  Embracing the power of “not yet” rather than “right or wrong”  - --Carol Dweck, 2015  Fixed to Growth Mindset  Ł Ł Ł Ł Ł Ł Ł Ł  Teacher-Centered. Student strengths enrich the classroom.  Student-Centered. Student strengths are identified to enrich the classroom. Students are doing the thinking, speaking, reading, writing and listening.  Teacher-Centered. Some cultures are implicitly valued over others.  Student-Centered. Some cultures are implicitly valued over others.  © 2024 CARE Coaching  Ł CARE Coaching 7 a\"\"5 a.« ea.so6siobos«okza  Is Math a Universal Language?  Ten divided by 2 <-> 10:2 (Germany)  1,234,567.89 <-> 123,4567.89 (China, powers of 10,000)  Roman: XIV + XXVIII = ?  26 <-> “sechsundzwanzig” (German)= six and twenty 70 <-> Soixante-dix (French) = sixty ten  Pamean languages in Mexico have octal (base 8) systems because the speakers count using the spaces between their fingers rather than the fingers themselves.  $63,578.50 in the USA <-> $63.578,50 in Brazil  English: Peter’s Gross Tables factory creates an array of rational products that will not seem odd to even your most obtuse operations.  What are implications for American English math instruction for Multilingual Learners?  But what about in classrooms?  Read and annotate the article:  Surprising ?@ Questions Important  Take a moment to reflect on the following questions and take notes on your Note-Taker.  Section Recap 1.  What have you learned about mathematics and language?  2.  How does asset-based mindset influence our assumptions about what students know?  BREAK!  Standards for Mathematical Practice (SMPs)  b0 C 4 e •e  2.  Reason abstractly and quantitatively  3.  Construct viable arguments and critique the reasoning of others  4.  Model with mathematics  S.  Use appropriate tools strategically  □  7.  Look for and make use of structure.  8.  Look for and express regularity in repeated reasoning.  Adapted from Bill McCallum, 20 7 0: http://ime.math.arizona.edu/20 7 0-17120 71_04_01_/ME_Pradices.pdf  Reasoning and explaining  Modeling and using tools  Seeing structure and generalizing  Overarching habits of mind of a productive mathematical thinker.  Gallery Walk Exploration Activity  1.  Read the SMP  2.  Watch a video from a grade level of your choice  3.  On the anchor chart  •  Summarize your SMP  •  Summarize the Lesson  •  Identify Student and Teacher behaviors that demonstrate SMP  1.  Gallery Walk and Rotate to each poster  2.  Consider the lesson  3.  Discuss  •  The extent to which the SMP is captured in the lesson  •  Confusions and clarifications with your group •  Connect to real-life examples you have experienced  Objectives  ▪  Analyze Standards for Mathematical Practice (SMPs)  ▪  Connect ELD Standards to Standards for  Mathematical Practice  ▪  Identify opportunities for language amplifications in a math content lesson  ▪  Apply language amplifications to an upcoming math content lesson  Section Recap  Take a moment to reflect on the following questions and take notes on your Note-Taker. 2. Which SMP would you like to focus more on?  1.  How do the SMPs guide what your lessons might look like across a year?  3.  What behaviors would you need to see your students do?  Connecting SMPs to ELD  Standards  Objectives  ▪  Analyze Standards for Mathematical Practice (SMPs)  ▪  Connect ELD Standards to Standards for Mathematical Practice  ▪  Identify opportunities for language amplifications in a math content lesson  ▪  Apply language amplifications to an upcoming math content lesson  Why Language in Math?  1. Read 2. 3.  # a key finding 250 characters or less on the back of your name tent  Modes of Communication  Interpretive/Receptive  Expressive/Productive  Listening  Speaking  Reading  Writing  Viewing  Representing  But WHY are you Communicating?  Exemplify Functional Approach  •  Bring focus and coherence to the language of school  •  Represent prominent language use across content standards  •  Help educators make choices to prioritize and organize content and language integration  •  Serve as an organizing principle for Language Expectations  WIDA. (2020). WIDA English language development standards framework, 2020 edition: Kindergarten– grade 12. Board of Regents of the University of Wisconsin System  Key Language Uses  NARRATE: Highlights language to convey real or imaginary experiences through stories and histories, including instruct, entertain, teach, or support argumentation.  ARGUE: Highlights language to develop claims, counterclaims, and provide convincing evidence to substantiate them in order to advance or defend an idea or solution, change the audience’s point of view, bring about action, or accept a position or evaluation of an issue.  EXPLAIN: Highlights language to give account for how things work or why things happen as students substantiate the inner workings of natural, man-made, and social phenomena.  INFORM: Highlights language to provide factual information, such as define, describe, compare, contrast, organize, categorize, or classify concepts, ideas, or phenomena.  Most Common Math KLUs in Each Grade Cluster  What does this look like?  Listen to an Explanation  Write to Argue  Represent to Explain Read a Narration View an Argument  Speak to Argue  But what language is involved?  Key Language Uses or KLUs Grades K, 1, 2-3, 4-5, 6-8, 9-12 Language Features for:  •  Narrate  •  Argue •  Explain •  Inform  … when Interpreting (Listening/Reading/Viewing)  and  Expressing (Speaking/Writing/Representing)  Explain in 6-8 Math  Pg. 152 of WIDA Framework 2020 Edition  Sample Language  Pg. 152 of WIDA Framework 2020 Edition  Pg. 153 of WIDA Framework 2020 Edition  Exploring KLUs  Grade K: p. 44 Grade 1: p. 70 Grades 2-3: p. 92 Grades 4-5: p. 118 Grades 6-8: p. 152 Grades 9-12: p. 190  Objectives  ▪  Analyze Standards for Mathematical Practice (SMPs)  ▪  Connect ELD Standards to Standards for Mathematical Practice  ▪  Identify opportunities for language amplifications in a math content lesson  ▪  Apply language amplifications to an upcoming math content lesson  Take a moment to reflect on the following questions and take notes on your Note-Taker.  Section Recap  1.  The 4 Key Language Uses are N___, A____, E____, I____ which can be used in…  2.  …the 6 Modes of Communication: L_____, S_____, R_____, W_____, V______ and Re_______.  3.  The Mode of Communication I’d like to work on using more is ___.  Lunch!  Identifying Opportunities for Language Amplifications in a Math Content Lesson  Objectives  ▪  Analyze Standards for Mathematical Practice (SMPs)  ▪  Connect ELD Standards to Standards for Mathematical Practice  ▪  Identify opportunities for language amplifications in a math content lesson  ▪  Apply language amplifications to an upcoming math content lesson  Consider the Open-Middled Question  Compare the following questions:  Use standard algorithm to solve 475 ÷ 25.  Do Hybrid cars pay for themselves?  How much does a 100x100 In-N-Out Cheeseburger cost?  End  Only one right answer.  Multiple right answers depending on cost of gas, mileage, price of car, etc.  Only one right answer.  Middle  Only one way to solve.  Multiple ways to solve.  Multiple ways to solve.  https://www.openmiddle.com/  SOURCE: http://robertkaplinsky.com/the-difference-between-open-ended-and-open-middled-problems/  Sample Math Lesson, Grade 6  Mathematical Practice 1: Make sense of problems and persevere in solving them.  Students will need to look for various entry points into this problem and persevere through difficulty. There are many equations to consider and many possible values that can work for a single equation, but students must find the value that works for all instances of each fruit.  Mathematical Practice 3: Construct viable arguments and critique the reasoning of others.  Students’ primary task in this problem is to explain their pairings. To construct a cohesive argument, they need to make conjectures and follow the logic to verify the truth of their conjectures.  Mathematical Practice 7: Look for and make use of structure.  Students look for the pattern and structure in the equations and likely need to step back and shift perspectives to realize that a fruit could be paired with a different value.  Adapted from OERCommons.org  Receptive and Productive Language in SMPs  1.  Revisit the SMP Lesson you analyzed on the Anchor Chart  2.  Match with the corresponding Language Use table from the HO-SMP w RP Language.  3.  Identify Analytical Tasks, Receptive Language, and Productive Language using the sticky notes.  4.  Rotate to the next SMP.  Objectives  ▪  Analyze Standards for Mathematical Practice (SMPs)  ▪  Connect ELD Standards to Standards for  Mathematical Practice  ▪  Identify opportunities for language amplifications in a math content lesson  ▪  Apply language amplifications to an upcoming math content lesson  Section Recap  Take a moment to reflect on the following questions and take notes on your Note-Taker.  1.  Why is it important to identify EXEMPLAR language (receptive and productive) involved with solving a math problem? 2. Which SMP do you feel you’d need support with identifying language in?  BREAK!  Amplifying Language in a Math  Lesson  Objectives  ▪  Analyze Standards for Mathematical Practice  (SMPs) ▪ Connect ELD Standards to Standards for  Mathematical Practice  ▪  Identify opportunities for language amplifications in a math content lesson  ▪  Apply language amplifications to an upcoming math content lesson  Application Task  1.  Identify an upcoming math lesson.  2.  Identify SMPs (1-8)  3.  Identify Analytical Tasks, Receptive Language, and Productive Language.  4.  Identify Key Language Uses (KLUs) and related Language Features.  5.  Amplify Receptive Language or Productive Language ensuring they meet the Analytical Task demands.  6.  Present in your choice of format!  ▪  Analyze Standards for Mathematical Practice  (SMPs) ▪ Connect ELD Standards to Standards for  Mathematical Practice ▪ Identify opportunities for language amplifications in a math content lesson  ▪  Apply language amplifications to an upcoming math content lesson  Closing  1. Evaluation  C 0 • ■ � � � G > I  Ł CARE Coaching ŁŁ \"%asevoesioeoou  0 � ii ■ • ■r -� ■ 1 ■ .,",
