    "content": "Practical Considerations for Supporting English Learners in Science  Objectives  ▪  Explore the role of collaboration in science  ▪  Consider student assets for learning  ▪  Examine the impact of race, culture and language in the learning process  ▪  Recognize the connection between content knowledge and academic language  ▪  Explore how a focus on genres can support academic discourse  Objectives Will Cover 4 Focus Areas  Focus 1 - Collaboration Focus 2 - Asset Orientation Focus 3 - Race, Culture & Language Focus 4 - Academic Language  Focus 4 Academic Language  Resources  https://bit.ly/ScienceWCEPS  0 0 0 \\ , :o; I \\ 0 0 0 Mir\\d Łull, or MindŁul?  © 2024 CARE Coaching  Checklist  Tracking Learning  1.  Work as a table team.  2.  Synthesize your understanding in 3-5 words.  3.  Add to chart.  1  2  3  4  Ask It Basket  Question?  Getting to Know You  Linguistic Support  1.  Middle of tent: your first name  2.  Top left: why science?  3.  Bottom right: years in education  4.  Bottom left: role or grade level  How Are You Feeling?  1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9  Getting to Know You  Why So Much Note-Taking?  How Long after Learning?  Forgotten?  Sixty minutes  50%  One Day  70%  One Week  90%  https://www.shponline.co.uk/memory-like-goldfish-get-90-improvement-safety-training/  ENGAGE: String Me Along!  1.  Choose a piece of string and find someone who is not sitting at your table.  2.  Introduce yourself and tell your partner what you hope to learn more about during this workshop.  3.  Speak for as long as it takes to wrap the piece of yarn around your index finger.  REFLECT: String Me Along!  •  What did you notice?  •  How might you apply this in your context?  •  What variations might you make?  Identities and Roles  •  Collaborators  •  Learners  •  Reflective Practitioners  •  Language Analysts  We all have unique expertise that will enhance the conversation.  งก it simpปู s·wุe\"',ย••ลืไอดีา�· 'uR แงุo่ยอ%สูกoug!่ © 2024 CARE Coaching ·งCARE.S9echins Focus 1 Collaboration  Considering Collaboration  1.  Individually, respond to the questions.  2.  Share your responses with your table.  3.  Record responses to the last question on sentence strips.  Debrief  •  One attribute of a meaningful collaborative discussion is ___.  •  Collaborative discussions are ___.  •  One benefit of collaborative discussions is ___.  Collaborative Thinking  To enable students to engage in meaningful collaborative discussions:  ▪  Specific language support may be needed.  ▪  New ways of interacting may need to be introduced.  ▪  Practice in sharing ideas, clarifying ideas, and building on the ideas of others may be required.2  ) 2024 CARE Coaching  SCARE Coaching Advancing Equitable Educational Opportunities  Disclaimer  This activity is meant to be a shared experience to consider collaboration and an experiential introduction to the engineering design process and NOT a formal engineering project.  Tower of Babel?  Materials  20 pieces  1 yard  1 piece  1 yard  Rules for Building the Tower  1.  Marshmallow must be intact on the top.  2.  Structure must be freestanding! (no hands)  3.  Use any of the materials provided.  4.  Structure must stand for 5 seconds or longer.  5.  Highest structure wins!  Team of Builders + One Observer  ENGAGE: String Me Along!  ▪  How did teams approach the challenge?  ▪  When did teams begin to build the structure?  ▪  What roles were assumed?  ▪  How did team members problem solve?  ▪  What other observations were made?  Rules  1.  Your marshmallow needs to be intact on the top of your structure.  2.  The marshmallow must be on the top of the freestanding structure!  3.  You can use all the materials that were given to your team.  4.  You cannot be touching the structure.  5.  Structure must stand for 5 seconds or longer.  6.  Highest structure wins!  ) 2024 CARE Coaching SCARE Coaching Advancing Equitable Educational Opportunities  Leaning Tower of Pisa?  Eiffel Tower?  © 2024 CARE Coaching  Statistics  Build a tower, build a team  Which Practices?  1.  Asking questions and defining problems  2.  Developing and using models  3.  Planning and carrying out investigations  4.  Analyzing and interpreting data  5.  Using mathematics and computational thinking  6.  Constructing explanations and designing solutions  7.  Engaging in argument from evidence  8.  Obtaining, evaluating, and communicating information  Which Cross Cutting Concepts?  1.  Patterns  2.  Cause and effect  3.  Scale, proportion, and quantity  4.  Systems and system models  5.  Energy and matter  6.  Structure and function  7.  Stability and change  Focus 1: Collaboration  o  How do you currently engage students in collaborative discussions?  oWhat supports do you (might you) provide?  oWhat changes might you implement to make language expectations more explicit?  https://bit.ly/ScienceSynthesis  BREAK  2024 CARE Coaching  SCARE Coaching Advancing Equitable Educational Opportunities  Focus 2 Asset Orientation  Student Portrait: Familiar?  ▪  Child of an immigrant.  ▪  Struggling with learning English.  ▪  Reads below grade level.  ▪  Can be a behavior problem.  ▪  Talks too much in class.  ▪  Distracts others.  ▪  Receives free or reduced lunch.  ▪  Speaks another language at home.  ▪  Parents only have an elementary education.  That Same Student Today  ▪  Director of WIDA Licensing  ▪  National and International Presenter  ▪  Masters in Education  ▪  Taught K-12 across 20+ Years  ▪  Data Coach  ▪  Consultant  Deficit Mindset Asset Mindset  ▪  Child of an immigrant.  ▪  Student with a diverse background.  ▪  Struggling with learning English.  ▪  Beginning to learn English.  ▪  Reads below grade level.  ▪  Decodes fluently, needs support in developing comprehension strategies.  ▪  Talks too much in class  ▪  Eager to share opinions.  ▪  Distracts others.  ▪  Is trying to connect with peers.  ▪  Receives free or reduced lunch.  ▪  Receives free or reduced lunch.  ▪  Speaks another language at home.  ▪  Comes from multilingual home.  ▪  Parents have an elementary education.  ▪  Parents demonstrate perseverance.  Asset Focus  ▪  Linguistic  ▪  Cultural  ▪  Experiential  ▪  Social and Emotional  What assets and contributions do multilingual learners bring to your science classroom?  REFLECT: Apply to Your Context  1.  Read the chart.  2.  Record ways your currently leverage students’ assets or ideas for how you might.  3.  Be prepared to share with a partner.  ENGAGE: Walk and Talk  Learning Walks  Some Additional Ideas  Linguistic  Cultural  •  Literate in Spanish  •  Knows how to use resources to support her learning  •  Translanguaging: Makes connections between languages  •  Critical language awareness: Identifies cognates  •  Developing identity across multiple layers (student/friend in a US school, child in Latino home)  •  Bi-cultural/Cultural flexibility  •  Engaged in culturally relevant traditions  Experiential  Social and Emotional  •  Has lived in different environments  •  Increasing social skills and teamwork skills through soccer  •  Acquiring knowledge of procedures (e.g., sequencing, cause and effect) by  engaging in “chores” at home  •  Learning brings joy  •  Responsible  •  Emotional intelligence: aware of others’  feelings  •  Perfectionist  •  Helps peers  •  Takes on different perspectives  Social and Emotional Assets Personal interests and needs, awareness of/empathy for diverse experiences, knowledge and enrichment of community resources Linguistic Assets Knowledge of multiple languages, varying representation of ideas, metalinguistic and metacognitive awareness, diverse strategies for language learning Cultural Assets Different perspectives, practices, beliefs, social norms, ways of thinking Experiential Assets Varied life and educational experiences, exposure to unique topics, diverse approaches to learning and expressing content knowledge  Class Portrait  Ł  \\ r  Listen best when...  Birth Country:  Languages Spoken:  Content: Class Period:  Literacy Skills:  Learning Preferences:  Pair work and small group work with interaction  Modeling or demonstrating instructions  Movement  Favorite Topics: s  I  Speak best when...  Ł  [write best when...  Ł  Entering  Emerging  Develop-ing  Expanding  Bridging  L  s  R  w  Read best when...  l  I Am From Poems…  My name is…..  I am from [ familiar places ]  I am from [ familiar foods you eat with your family ]  I am from [ familiar sayings or expressions you hear at home ]  I am from [ people you love ]  Adapted from Cahnmann as cited in Reading, Writing and Talk (2016) by Souto-Manning and Martell  https://flipgrid.com/00161e54  Student Voices  Thought to Ponder  Your beliefs become your thoughts,  Your thoughts become your words,  Your words become your actions,  Your actions become your habits,  Your habits become your values,  Your values become your destiny.  Mahatma Gandhi  Goal 2: Asset Mindset  oHow do you currently set the stage for embracing an asset orientation?  oWhat can help you keep this philosophy present?  oHow do you currently leverage your students’ strengths?  https://bit.ly/ScienceSynthesis  How Do We Plan for Scaffolds?  Robert Wood, Johnson Foundation  Equality is everyone receiving the same exact bike, even though only one person of the four can ride it comfortably. Equity, on the other hand, is everyone receiving something catered to their individual needs for a result of all four people being able to comfortably ride their own bikes  Process Content Product Interests Readiness Learning Profile Environment Why Blue? Why Green? Orange?  My Espirimint  Write about your Science experiment.  Some leafs really, really, really no like da wader. Do you no about leafs dat dona’t like wader? Well I do. I did a espirimint where I put wader on da leaf and dat wader bounce off.  Some leafs really, really, really no like da wader. Do you no about leafs dat dona’t like wader? Well I do. I did a espirimint where I put wader on da leaf and dat wader bounce off.  Feedback  1.  You really understood the concept  2.  I love how you wanted to emphasize “really” what other word would you use to emphasize this?  3.  Do you know that “bounced off” is related to our Science term: Hydrophobic?  4.  Quickly mention why bounce would be bounced.  5.  Rewrite: using word bank and feedback  Word bank: Leaves Experiment Water  Cognates  Taken from Language Matters Conference PPT, Ingrid Colon  Connected Cognates  •  absorber  • ocurrir  •  afectar  • petróleo  •  atmósfera  • planeta  • carbono (C)  • contaminación, polución  • clima global  • radiación  •  conservación de energía  • recurso energético no renovable •  contaminación, polución  •  dióxido de carbono (CO2)  • responsible  •  efecto  • respirar/respiración  • energía solar  • solución  • energía térmica  • transferencia  •  gas  • temperatura  •  negativo  Differentiating Presentation of Content  Language of Inquiry & Prediction  What happens if you wring out a wet towel in space?  The words ring, wring sound the same but have different meanings and spellings. Why do ring, wring sound the same even though they are completely different words? The answer is simple: ring, wring are homophones of the English language  Wring means: To twist, squeeze, or compress, especially to extract liquid. Often used with out: wring out a wet towel.  Focus 2: Asset Oriented  oHow do you currently get to know your students assets?  oWhat ideas have you gathered to help you learn about your students?  oWhat changes might you implement to ensure you are using the information to scaffold?  https://bit.ly/ScienceSynthesis  LUNCH  Focus 3 Race, Culture & Language  ENGAGE: Jigsaw Reading  1.  Letter off A-D.  2.  Move to the appropriate table.  3.  Read your card.  4.  Share with your table.  5.  Number off 1-4.  6.  Move to the appropriate table.  7.  Share with table.  8.  Share with whole group.  A B C D  The Impact of Culture  Cultural identity impacts how students…  ▪  define success  ▪  understand school rules and procedures  ▪  work with others in small groups  ▪  interact with others (e.g., peers, adults, genders)  ▪  use and handle school materials  ▪  participate in class  ▪  ask for help  …and the list goes on  Mirrors, Window & Doors  Sims Bishop, R. (1990). Mirrors, windows, and sliding glass doors.  Getting to Know your Students  ▪ Student interests hold so much power.  ▪ When a topic connects to what students like, engagement deepens, as they willingly spend time thinking, dialoging, and creating ideas in meaningful ways.  ▪ Making learning contextual to real-world experiences is a key learning technique when differentiating based on students’ interests.  Connecting to Students’ Interests  Focus 3: Race, Culture & Language  oHow do you currently ensure that concepts connected to race, culture and language are represented in your lessons?  oHow could you be more intentional about being culturally responsive within your lessons?  oWhat changes can you make that embrace diversity?  https://bit.ly/ScienceSynthesis  BREAK  Focus 4 Academic Language  ENGAGE: Considering Language  1.  Individually respond to the questions.  2.  Share at your table.  3.  Be prepared to share with the whole group.  Content vs. Language  © 2024 CARE Coaching  lt's literally: THE HIDDEN TIGER!  © 2024 CARE Coaching  Language Development Standards  Give Me Five?  © 2024 CARE Coaching  #1: Describe this image to a friend.  © 2024 CARE Coaching  #2: Describe thls image as a poet laureate.  #3: Describe this image as a mathematics professor.  #4: Describe this image as a materials engineer.  © 2024 CARE Coaching  #5: Describe this image as a tour guide.  © 2024 CARE Coaching  Reflection  Think about the language you produced.  What did you notice?  Did you….  ▪  speak in words and phrases? sentences? simple, compound, or complex?  ▪  notice any specialized structures or language patterns?  ▪  hear any general, specific, and technical vocabulary?  The Bridge  Language Instruction through Content ELD Standards  Science Instruction through Language Academic Language Academic State Standards Science Practices  Interdependence  Provide Access to Grade-Level Concepts  ELLs Learning a New Language Learning IN a New Language Language Content  Develop Academic Language Skills  Academic Language Defined  Academic language is the language that students need to process and produce to be successful in a school setting. We as educators often are oblivious to the language embedded within our tasks because it is so second nature to us– it’s automatic, we know English and don’t have attend to the language. However, when planning for ELLs, it is not enough to think about the content concepts we need to teach within our lessons, we also need to make ourselves aware of the language students will be expected to process and produce in the lesson. We need to make ourselves aware of the language expectations embedded within our lessons.  “The fish does not recognize the water in which it swims.”  - Pauline Gibbons  Dimensions of Academic Language  GRADES 4-5  WIDA ELD STANDARD 4  Language for Science  I Argue  I  Language Expectations: Multilingual learners will...  ELD-SC.4-5.Argue.Interpretive Interpret scientific arguments by  •  Identifying relevant evidence from data, models, and/or information from investigations of phenomena or design solutions  •  Comparing reasoning and claims based on evidence  •  Distinguishing among facts, reasoned judgment based on research findings, and speculation in an explanation  ELD-SC.4-5.Argue.Expressive  Construct scientific arguments that  •  Introduce topic/phenomenon in issues related to the natural and designed world(s)  •  Make and define a claim based on evidence, data, and/or model  •  Establish a neutral tone or an objective stance  •  Signal logical relationships among reasoning, relevant evidence, data, and/or a model when making a claim  Language Functions and Sample Language Features  Make and define 