    "title": "Building Community with Multilingual Learners through Family Engagement (Part 1/1)",
    "content": "Building Community with Multilingual Learners through Family Engagement  Yvonne Williams  Director of ML Educator Engagement  WCEPS  Overview  This session explores the value and components of effective family partnerships across the school community. Allowing educators to reflect on the definition of engagement, our own experiences, and how to consider who defines engagement. Creating an environment that leverages families as a reliable resource to engage students, support families, and honor culture creates a successful school vision. After completing this workshop, participants will be able to:  Define Family Engagement• Consider research that supports family engagement • Create a vision/mission for family engagement for class/school•  © 2024 CARE Coaching  CARE Coaching  A Brief History of the Wisconsin Center  for Education Products and Services  Who’s in the room?  Elementary teachers PK-5 Middle School teachers 6-8 High School teachers 9-12 Elementary Admin Middle School Admin High School Admin District  Logistics  https://bit.ly/familyYW  ❑Padlet link:  https://bit.ly/notefamilyPA  ❑Note taker:  Record Your Learning  Introduction  Gardener planting seeds. You decide how you cultivate those seeds, fertilize it and help it grow  Today Support Implementation Student Success Individual It’s hard to imagine that a mighty oak tree can come from a tiny acorn, but with enough patience, you can grow one yourself! Look for an acorn to plant in early fall, germinate it, and start it in a container. Then, transplant your acorn in a prepared site. Care for your growing oak tree so it grows.  Collaborative  WCEPS  Ivonne  ▪  BA Business Administration  ▪  MA Bilingual/TESL Education  ▪  Reading Specialist License  ▪  Computer Science License  ▪  Instructional Coach Endorsement  ▪  ELL Coordinator  ▪  MLL Director  30+  BA in Elementary Education MA in Instructional Technology MA in Education, K-12 ESL Ed.S. in Education Leadership MI School Administrator Certificate  4th grade ESL Elementary Teacher ELD Specialist, Grades 4-8 District K-12 EL Teacher Leader  25+  Nadra  Getting to Know You  I am ok! ¡Estoy bien!  Help? ¿Ayúdame?  What time is it? ¿Que hora es?  I need caffeine! ¡Necesito cafeina!  *sigh* *suspirando*  Kind of sad Un poco triste  Eager to learn! ¡Deseoso/a de aprender¡  No estoy aqui I am not here  Happy! ¡Contenta/Contento!  bit.ly/YODAIAM  Why is Today Important?  Why Family Engagement?  Digital Divide:  With increased reliance on technology in education, families can play a crucial role in ensuring equitable access to technology and supporting students with digital literacy skills.  Complex Social Landscape:  Today's students face diverse social challenges, and strong family support can help them navigate these complexities and build resilience.  Personalized Learning:  Family engagement can provide valuable insights into a child's individual needs and learning styles, allowing for more personalized educational approaches.  National and State Data Summary  As of Fall 2021, about 5.3 million students were identified as English learners (ELs) in U.S. public schools, representing approximately 10.6% of total enrollment (NCES, 2024).  •  Pennsylvania reported 98,278 ELs in 2023-24 (PDE, 2024).  •  Many PA districts now report 5% or more of students as ELs for the 2024-25 school year (Rural PA Datagram, 2025).  •  Pennsylvania’s EL population is growing steadily, reflecting linguistic and cultural diversity that requires intentional instructional planning.  Implications:  –  Prioritize equitable writing and assessment practices for multilingual learners.  –  Strengthen family engagement and culturally responsive pedagogy.  ”After the pandemic, family engagement is no longer a ‘nice-to-have’ it is essential. Strong partnerships between schools and families are the key to addressing learning loss, supporting mental health, and ensuring every child thrives.\"  adapted from Karen L. Mapp  What the Research Says  Thirty years of national achievement research shows a direct correlation between families engaged in their child’s education to higher achievement.1  Active participation of parents in the schools their children attend is beneficial to their children’s educational success. 2  Family disengagement is one of the multiple risk factors of dropout a lack of communication between families and the school and the family feeling “unwelcomed” are the triggers that predict future dropout.  1 ((Harvard Family Research Project, 2017)  2 (Auerbach, 2009 & Noguera, 2001)  3(Dropout Risk Factors and Exemplary Programs, n.d.)  1 ' I P!j , lŁ lll ' , ------f/j ŁŁi-:  Hand Framework  It’s at Your Fingertips!  Trace Your Hand  Thumb: \"We all understand\" and have a common definition on engagement, for families, culture and communities  Index: \"Not just pointing the finger at families\" consider yourself, students, colleagues & district leaders should participate in dialogue  Middle: ”Meaningful\" when we ask for families to be part of the education journey, it must be meaningful & relevant  Ring: \"Commitment\" to having an asset mindset as well as evaluating engagement/needs & reflecting on what is working or not working  Pinky: ?  The pinky is up, was a sign of courtesy or etiquette. Thus, it was done with “intention”. Understanding families, revisiting what engagement looks like and honoring families' needs to be done with intention. This also ties the cultural piece to our families.  5 Framework  Key Message  PTA Standard Alignment  Thumb  “We All Understand” – shared definition of engagement  Standard 1 – Welcoming All Families  Index  Shared responsibility across educators and families  Standards 2 & 5 – Communicating Effectively, Sharing Power  Middle  “Meaningful” – authentic, relevant engagement  Standards 3 & 6 – Supporting Student Success, Collaborating with Community  Ring  “Commitment” – reflection and asset mindset  Standards 4 & 5 – Speaking Up for Every Child, Sharing Power  Pinky  “Intention & Promise” – continual reflection  Standards 1 & 6 – Welcoming All Families, Collaborating with Community  If kids come to us from strong, healthy functioning families, it makes our job easier. If they do not come to us from strong, healthy, functioning families, it makes our job more important. Ł Barbara Coloroso  © 2024 CARE Coaching  Consider Your Family  Why Vulnerability Is Important • Greater strength Stronger relationships Improved self-acceptance  Inclusion: Your Family  What image, words, memories, would best represents how involved your parent/family was during your K-12 education what would that look like? Where are you now vs. then?  Inclusion: Your Family  CHIHUAHUA Started teaching in 1995 in Las Vegas NV when Bilingual Program started, taught: K, 1st , 3rd Middle School 6th English & 7th Science. ELL specialist for secondary schools Instructional coach for 25 schools. Family coordinator for HS lastly, Family Engagement Specialist for Pre-K to 12th grade districtwide. Left to work for WIDA in 2014 then WCEPS in 2018 CULTURE NOW THEN  GREAT GRANDMA STORIES  Ivonne Margarita Oronoz Rosas  Nadra  Started teaching in 2001 in Dearborn MI (childhood school): 4th grade ESL, ELD Specialist for Intermediate bldg (4-8).  2009: District EL Teacher Leader 2017: WIDA Featured Educator 2017-19: WIDA LEA Advisory  NOW  THEN  Share Your Family Portrait  1.  Discuss your family's engagement  2.  What are some similarities or differences  3.  How engaged was your family?  4.  If you are a parent of a k-12 student, discuss how you engage with your son/daughter school.  Student families  Involve students in sharing their families, their journey  Your Family 1 minute  15 minutes  Defining  Family Engagement  Involvement vs. Engagement  ⮚ Multicultural night  ⮚ Mutual goal setting  ⮚ Parent/Teacher  ⮚ Focus groups  conferences  ⮚ Variety in PTA  ⮚ Award night  ⮚ Daily or Weekly  ⮚ Festival day  Communication  ⮚ Open House  ⮚ Cultural connections  TASK: Considering Current State  1)  As a table, share how you or your school currently engage families.  1)  Discuss similarities between each other and your family’s engagement.  1)  Make note of a new idea.  1)  Be prepared to share.  A Definition: Family Engagement  Family engagement refers to the systematic integration of families in activities, mentorship and programs that promote student’s  •  development,  •  learning, and  •  wellness,  including in the planning, creation, and evaluation of such activities, programs, and systems.  a  Adapted from ED-HHS Policy Statement on Family Engagement: From the Early Years to the Early Grades  Characteristics  Effective partnerships are characterized by:  •  Mutual trust and respect  •  Two-way collaboration and support  •  Equality in the relationship  Success can be defined by:  •  Ongoing participation (home or school)  •  Two-way communication  •  Consistent evaluation of family needs  Multilingual Learner Journey  Speaking with families about the journey of being an English Language Learner (ELL) is essential for building trust and understanding. When families know how language development unfolds, they can better support their child’s learning and celebrate progress along the way. These conversations also affirm the value of students’ home languages and cultures, promote realistic expectations, and strengthen the partnership between home and school. By sharing the ELL journey openly, educators help families feel empowered, informed, and included in their child’s educational growth.  Communicating about Language  Emphasize Assets and Growth -Multilingualism is an asset; students bring valuable linguistic and cultural knowledge. -Language development is a journey that looks different for every learner. -Celebrate progress in both the home language and English.  Explain the Language Learning Trajectory -Language develops over time and across contexts — listening and speaking often emerge before reading and writing. -Growth occurs through meaningful interaction with peers, content, and teachers. -Academic English can take 5–7 years to fully develop, even with strong instruction.  Connect Language and Content Learning -Students learn language and content simultaneously. -Teachers provide scaffolds so students can engage with grade-level material while developing English. -Families can support learning by encouraging talk, reading, and storytelling in any language.  Use Family-Friendly Language -Avoid jargon (e.g., say “your child is beginning to express ideas in English” instead of “WIDA Level 2”). -Share specific examples of what the student can do in English and in their home language. -Provide translations or interpretation to ensure equitable communication.  Two-Partners  1.  Decide who is partner A and Partner B  2.  Partner A be prepared to respond to a question  3.  Partner B be prepared to listen attentively  4.  Partner A please respond in complete sentences  5.  You will have exactly one minute to respond  Partner A Respond:  Strategy Share  1.  Partner B, please summarize what Partner A said.  2.  Please respond in complete sentences.  3.  You will have one minute to share what Partner A said; however,  You may not use the letter “r” in your response.  Debrief  ▪  How did that feel?  ▪  What are the implications for instruction?  ▪  What connections can you make viewing, representing and providing language guidance?  ▪  Why is this important for families to understand?  Padlet Ideas for Engagement  bit.ly/EngageFamilia  TASK: Defining Your Vision/Mission  1)  As a class, school, district, it is important to co-create a vision of effective family engagement with your community.  2)  Consider :  ✔  How is effective engagement defined?  ✔  What does it look and feel like?  ✔  How can effective engagement be measured?  ✔  How does a language barrier influence engagement?  3)  Post the vision and revisit it often.  DEEP CULTURE THE CULTURAL ICEBERG Food FQ&'J NISCMb F,nh!Gt HoUcb� Mllllc SIMMMneff 0-G.IMH ArU & Cnrfb lltlr.rtwc un� &, S. li◄113\"4 Concepts of: 1.,,,..,1.....................-...... Falmenald.Mtk9 .._,_....,o.._..Sn. �F-'¥, «c lldln Adol«-Depe,•cb••o ... � Wort,. A&ldloftry IPP(OKhH 10: IWpi,, c-atllp� .........w_..  Where Cultures Meet  Culture Intersects with School Culture  Visual: Create Two overlapping circles (Venn diagram style):  Left circle: Family & Community Culture  Right circle: School Culture  Overlap area: Shared Understanding / Student Success  Text prompts:  Family & Community Culture:  Values, traditions, language, communication styles, faith, family roles  School Culture:  Expectations, policies, routines, language of instruction, norms of behavior  Intersection:  Relationships, shared goals for learning, respect, collaboration  Discussion Prompt: “Where do our school’s practices align with or clash against the cultures our families bring?  What can we do to expand the overlap between the two circles?”  Bridging the Cultures  Reflect on the intersections between family and school culture.  Steps:  Divide into pairs or groups.  Use the chart to create two columns: Family/Community Culture and School Culture.  Brainstorm:  In each column, list typical behaviors, values, or norms (e.g., communication style, role of authority, expectations around homework).  Highlight:  Circle areas where the two cultures might intersect or conflict.  Discuss:  What adjustments can we make in classrooms or communication to bridge these gaps?  How can we honor and integrate family culture into our daily practices?  Include visuals of your families that tie to your vision of family engagement  Respect Quality Commitment Advocacy High Expectations Communication Trusting Partnerships  Your Family 1 minute  Record: Defining Family Engagement  Committed to an Asset Mindset  Two First Grade Students  Student A  Student B  • Parents are immigrants  • Dad is a retired doctor  •  Only speaks Spanish  •  Dad works nights  •  Does not S, R, or W English  •  Learning to read in English  •  Two grade levels behind  • Mom a university professor  • Mom volunteers often  • Reads in his/her home language  •  Motivated to learn English  •  Showing progress toward goals  Be willing to challenge your own assumptions and biases  Consider a Kindergarten Newcomer  “It’s hard to know if they understand directions or what’s being said in class.”  “I worry they feel lost or left out when they can’t communicate with peers.”  “It’s tough to tell what they actually know versus what they can’t express yet.”  “Routines take a lot longer to stick when we can’t use language to explain.”  “Connecting with families is tricky when we don’t share a common language.”  Ł CARE Coaching 4#aa\"cine eaŁse eao-stonai o6po«onios  Talking about Families  Parents may not have a formal education, they can’t … What talents, skills & knowledge are families sharing with their students and perhaps willing to help integrate into the curriculum in order to support student learning? Parents might have been in legal trouble, so they… What support and resources does the family want? Parents utilize the free and reduced program, so…? Are there other resources the family/families would like to know more about or have the school explore? Parents never come to…so… How are and how would families like to be involved in their student’s education? We not They  Developing an Asset Mindset  •  Shift from 'What families lack' → 'What families contribute.'  •  Recognize home language maintenance as engagement.  •  Ask: How do we validate families’ funds of knowledge?  Asset-Based Philosophy  Education systems with an asset orientation empower…  •  Linguistically diverse students and their peers  •  Families  •  Communities  •  Educators  •  Administrators  •  Researchers  •  Policymakers  Considerations  •  Relationships must be built  •  Schools must be supportive and responsive  •  Emphasis placed on the reciprocal relationships between families and schools  •  Families must guide conversations and action  •  Provide a space to evaluate what is working and what families need  •  Actively co-create with families rather than seek input  Asset-Based Philosophy  Support education systems where an asset-based Philosophy empowers…  •  Linguistically diverse students and their peers  •  Families  •  Communities  •  Educators  •  Administrators  •  Researchers and Policymakers  Cultural and Linguistic Humility  •  Affirm family identities and stories.  •  Provide multilingual communication channels.  •  Use bilingual liaisons or community connectors.  Family Engagement & Cultural Differences: What Matters Most for Learning  1. Relationships First  Build trust through respect, curiosity, and consistency.  Learn about families’ cultural values before making assumptions.  2. Communication with Care  Use clear, translated, and inclusive communication.  Be flexible with timing (e.g., holidays) and formats for contact.  3. Inclusive Learning Environment  Acknowledge and include students’ cultural identities in class content.  4. Equity in Access & Participation.  Create multiple entry points for families to support student learning.  5. Reflective Practice  Examine personal biases and assumptions.  Seek continuous learning about culturally responsive and faith-inclusive teaching.  Discussion Prompt:  “How do these considerations shape our approaches to instruction and family partnerships with culturally diverse families?”  Toward Authentic Partnerships  Six Pillars of Engagement: 1. Relational Trust 3. Multilingual Communication  2.  Accessibility & Safety  4.  Collaborative Learning  5.  Community Partnership  6.  Student Voice  Committed to an Asset Mindset 1 minute  Record: Consider an Asset Mindset  Lunch Break: 11:30-12:15  Intentional Inclusion of Families  Focus on Community Engagement  • Community Leaders  • Libraries  • Museums  • Mental Health Facilities  • Community Schools  • Other?  Discuss  Do you currently have a needs assessment that will provide the information you need on your family’s needs or preferences for attendance?  If not, in what ways do you currently gather this information?  Compliant 🡪 Authentic  Compliant  Making it More Authentic  Newsletters  ???  Conferences  ???  Programs for Parents  ???  Score Reporting  ???  Literacy Nights, Multicultural Fair, etc.  ???  Toward Authentic Partnerships  Leadership that Prevents Partnerships Leadership for Nominal Partnerships Leadership for Traditional Partnerships Leadership for Authentic Partnerships  Auerbach, S. (2010). Beyond Coffee with the Principal: Toward Leadership for Authentic School-Family Partnerships. Journal of School Leadership 20 (6): 728-757.  bit.ly/EngageFamilia  Compliant Family Involvement  Effective Authentic Family Engagement  Families are informed about school events and policies.  Families are actively involved in decision-making and school improvement efforts.  Communication is one-way (newsletters, emails, announcements).  Communication is two-way, with opportunities for dialogue and feedback.  Families are invited to school events but have limited roles.  Families co-plan and co-lead events that reflect their interests and cultures.  Schools set the agenda, and families are expected to comply.  Schools and families collaborate to create shared goals.  Parent-teacher conferences are focused on student performance.  Parent-teacher partnerships focus on student strengths, needs, and aspirations.  Engagement is primarily during school hours, making it difficult for working families to participate.  Engagement is flexible, with opportunities available in various formats and times.  Support is limited to fundraising or volunteering in classrooms.  Families contribute in diverse ways, including curriculum input, mentoring, and advocacy.  Cultural differences are acknowledged but not deeply integrated.  Schools embrace and incorporate diverse cultural perspectives into learning.  Schools provide resources for families but do not seek their input.  Schools and families share knowledge and learn from each other.  Efforts are school-centered, with families expected to adjust to the system.  Efforts are student-centered, with schools adapting to family and community needs.  Continuum of impact on student achievement for family engagement strategies (the way they are typically done)  Ł\\ l(/§1: . ,..,,  Lower Impact  Higher Impact  Celebrations  Parent help on administrative tasks  Parent  training  events  Goal-setting talks  Weekly data-sharing folders  Fundraisers  Parent resource  rooms  Back to  school night  Regular, personalized communication  Academic  partnering  Home visits  Potlucks  Family support  services  Parent- teacher  conferences  Positive phone calls home  Ongoing communications  Performances &  showcases  Generic school  newsletters  Interactive  homework  Parent help on learning projects  Modeling of learning support strategies  1eJ  FLAMBOYAN 1ouaaŁiios  https://flamboyanfoundation.org/our-work/family-engagement/ Why Move from Involvement to Core Engagement?  •  Surface = Attendance and logistics; one-way communication.  •  Core = Shared decision-making, co-created goals, and trust.  •  Question: Where are we now—surface, emerging, or core?  Framework Foundations  •  Dual Capacity-Building Framework (Mapp & Kuttner, 2013): Builds educator and family capacity together.  •  Epstein’s Six Types of Involvement (2011): Expands how families engage beyond events.  •  Culturally and Linguistically Responsive Engagement (Gay, 2010; Moll et al., 1992): Treats culture and language as assets.  Defining School Engagement  Involvement vs. Engagement:  •  Families attend events vs. Families co-create goals  •  One-way vs. Two-way communication  •  Success = attendance vs. Success = learning & partnership  Prompt: Which column best represents your current practice?  Parental Engagement  Key Idea:  Building strong, culturally responsive partnerships with families is essential for supporting emergent bilingual learners.  Highlights:  Culturally Connected Learning: Engage parents by linking cultural and linguistic practices to classroom learning.  Mutual Partnerships: Ongoing, goal-directed relationships between educators and families that are reciprocal and strengths-based (Noguera, 2006).  Collaborative Approach: Effective engagement requires shared responsibility among schools, communities, and families (Rockett Sullivan, 2013).  Impact on Achievement: Active parental involvement—such as supporting homework— boosts confidence, self-regulation, and academic success (Rogers et al., 2009).  Takeaway:  Empower educators to view families as co-educators and leverage their cultural knowledge to enhance learning outcomes.  Research Highlights  •  Family engagement predicts achievement more than SES (Jeynes, 2012)  •  Trust is essential to collaboration (Bryk & Schneider, 2002)  •  Authentic partnerships build equity (Ishimaru, 2019)  Guiding Questions for “You Are Welcome Here”  A Colorín Colorado Video (approx. 20 minutes)  Purpose  Use these questions to guide viewing, reflection, or group discussion as you explore how schools can create welcoming environments for immigrant and multilingual families.  Before Watching  What does a truly welcoming school look and feel like to you?  How do new families currently experience your school’s culture and communication systems?  What personal assumptions or barriers might influence your approach to engaging families?  During the Video  What examples of welcome practices stand out?  How do educators in the video build trust and show respect for students’ and families’ backgrounds?  What do you notice about language access—how schools address interpretation, translation, or multilingual communication?  How do students and families describe feeling included or supported?  What strategies or ideas could transfer directly to your setting?  https://www.colorincolorado.org/you are welcome here  --- conference.  After Watching  Reflect  Which messages from the video resonate most strongly with your school or classroom experience?  What small changes could you make immediately to improve how families are greeted, informed, or involved?  What long-term systems (e.g., staff training, scheduling, communication structures) might need to change?  Collaborate  Who in your building or community can help strengthen family engagement efforts?  How can school staff, families, and community partners share responsibility for creating a welcoming culture?  Broader Reflection  What does “welcome” mean in a multilingual context?  How can schools move from involving families to partnering with them?  How does culturally responsive practice connect to academic success and belonging?  Would you like me to format this into a printable handout or editable document (e.g., Word or Google Doc)?  Check for Understanding  Surface / Involvement  bit.ly/PAFAMILIA  One-way communication, attendance-based activities, or surface-level participation. Families are informed but not empowered.  Transition / Capacity-Building  Educators and families develop mutual trust, shared skills, and understanding. The mindset shifts from service delivery to shared capacity.  Core / Engagement  Families are equal partners and co-leaders in learning. Engagement focuses on equity, shared decision-making, and impact on learning.  Questions to Ponder…  •  How do you plan to communicate with your families?  •  What resources and support do you make available to parents?  •  How do you help parents ensure student learning continues outside of school?  •  How do you involve your families in school decision-making?  •  How do you regularly ask for parent feedback and insights?  Intentional Inclusion of Families 1 minute  Record: Authentic Partnerships  Resources on  Family Engagement  Resources  bit.ly/EngageFamilia  What are the ABCs by WIDA?  •  Includes six key considerations  •  Tool to facilitate reflection and dialogue  •  Not a step-by-step approach  •  Research-based  •  Language focused  •  Home Language  Visit WIDA’s website for publicly available resources wida.wisc.edu/resources  What are the ABCs?  •  Six key considerations  •  Research-based  •  Not a step-by-step approach  •  Use to facilitate reflection and dialogue  TASK: Exploring the ABCs  1)  Label yourselves A-B-C  2)  Find your letter group – number off 1-8  3)  Review your assigned letter  4)  Record on chart, responses to the following:  •  Summary of your letter  •  What kinds of family engagement are most effective?  •  What additional thoughts do you have in relation to your multilingual families?  •  How do you currently include families as educational partners?  5)  Be prepared to share.  The ABCs of Family Engagement  •  Awareness  •  Advocacy  •  Brokering  •  Building Trust  •  Communication  • Connect to Learning  WIDA Focus Bulletin –wida.us  TASK: Discussing the ABCs  1)  Go back to your tables and gallery walk  2)  As you go to each designated letter chart  3)  Write the key ideas discussed  4)  Move to the next letter  5)  Once done go back and discuss  Resources on  Family Engagement 1 minute  Planning for Change  Guiding Questions  Five Goals, Five Fingers, 5 Standards  1.  GOAL 1 – Academic:  Engage families in supporting academic learning such as reading, writing, and math.  2.  GOAL 2 – Behavioral:  Engage families in the behavioral expectations and procedures of the school, as reflected in the School Improvement Plan and Parent Involvement Policy and Compact.  3.  GOAL 3 – Relationship Building:  Welcome, honor, and connect with families to strengthen relationships and create a sense of belonging within the school community.  4.  GOAL 4 – Professional Development:  Provide professional development on family engagement for staff to build capacity and foster an empowering culture.  5.  (Implicit Fifth Area – Family & Community Collaboration):  Although not labeled as a separate goal in your version, the plan’s structure and alignment with PTA Standards include Collaborating with the Community, emphasizing shared responsibility and partnership with community organizations to support family engagement.  Use your notes: Plan for Success  Guiding Questions to Build Upon  • How do you ensure you are building authentic  relationships?  • How do you plan for two-way communication?  • How do you include non-traditional forms of  engagement?  • How can family partnerships and engagement  be sustained?  •  Create a mini-vision/mission with the factors we have explored today.  Create a Plan  TASK: Plan for your Context  1)  Make connections to your context by identifying:  •  an idea to implement  •  an engaging practice to revise  •  an idea on how to measure success  •  a resource to explore or challenge to overcome  2)  Create an anchor chart, including resources needed to bring your plan to action  Planning for Change and Intentional Engagement  Action Planning Tool  Steps to Move from Surface to Core:  1.  Identify one area at surface level.  2.  Define what 'core' would look like.  3.  Develop 2–3 strategies.  4.  Assign responsibilities.  5.  Monitor with feedback.  Reflection Rubric  Rubric Dimensions:  Communication | Decision-Making | Learning Connection | Relationships | Cultural Responsiveness  Rate your school: 1 = Surface, 2 = Emerging, 3 = Core.  Guiding Questions  (Questions you can ask in your setting as you analyze your current language-focused family engagement practices)  •  Is the focus on authentic relationships?  •  Is communication two-way?  •  Is participation equitable?  •  Is it inclusive of non-traditional forms of engagement?  •  Is information about language learning and development shared?  Gallery Walk  Extending Learning  •  Guiding families to teach child through everyday activities and routines  •  Making home visits  •  Create a family lounge or welcoming resource center  •  Advocating for preferred informational, emotional, and logistical support  •  Addressing behavioral challenges in home and community settings  Extending Learning (cont’d)  •  Helping to connect children and families to neighborhood and community peers  •  Supporting children to be included in community recreational activities  •  Supporting children and families to attend community venues and events • Building community partnerships to address school, student, and family needs.  Reflect and Respond  •  How does your teacher/leader evaluation or professional development include ideas and actions related to cultural proficiency, if at all?  •  How should/could they?  •  What needs to happen for our school to get to culturally proficient and authentic family engagement?  •  How can family partnerships and engagement be sustained with a change in leaders?  Bringing It All Together  •  Core Engagement Framework → Defines Depth  •  Building Community → Reflection Tools  •  Family Engagement Framework → Equity & Trust  Prompt: What’s one step your team will take toward core engagement?  Record: Reflection  Survey  bit.ly/FamilyEngageSurvey  References  ▪  Auerbach, S. (2010). Beyond Coffee with the Principal: Toward Leadership for Authentic School-Family Partnerships. Journal of School Leadership 20 (6): 728-757.  ▪  Auerbach, S. (2009). Walking the walk: Portraits in leadership in family engagement in urban schools. The School Community Journal, 19(1), 9–31.  ▪  https://www.lawyerscommittee.org/debunking - the - myth - of - uncaring - parents - in - ell - classrooms/  Augustin, S. (2018, April 17). Debunking The Myth Of “Uncaring Parents” in ELL Classrooms. Lawyers’ Committee for Civil Rights under Law.  ▪  http://dropoutprevention.org/wp - content/uploads/2015/05/DropoutRiskFactorsandExemplaryProgramsFINAL5 - 16 - 07.pdf  Dropout Risk Factors and Exemplary Programs. (n.d.). Retrieved September 25, 2021, from  ▪  https://archive.globalfrp.org/  Harvard Family Research Project. (2017). HFRP Home /