    "title": "Designing Multi-Modal Instruction to Meet the Needs of Multilingual Learners (Part 1/1)",
    "content": "Designing Multi-Modal Instruction to Meet the Needs of Multilingual Learners  CARE Coaching  A Brief History of the Wisconsin Center for Education Products and Services  Overview  To meet the needs of multilingual learners, educators must provide multimodal instruction using Universal Design for Learning principles and scaffolding techniques to make grade-level learning accessible to students at all levels of English language proficiency.  After engaging in this learning opportunity, participants will be able to:  ▪  Describe the principles of UDL and their connection to scaffolding techniques  ▪  Develop and deliver multimodal instruction  ▪  Design learning opportunities that leverage learners’ strengths, interests and preferences  Reflect and Plan  People forget 40% of what they have learned after 20 minutes and 77% of what they learned after six days.  (http://www.festo-didactic.co.uk/gb-en/news/forgetting-curve-its-up-to-you.htm?fbid=Z2IuZW4uNTUwLjE3LjE2LjM0Mzc).  People forget 50-80% of what they have learned after one day and 97-98% after one month.  (http://www.adm.uwaterloo.ca/infocs/study/curve.html)  Logistics  Please sign in!  Sign-In:  https://form.jotform.com/WCEPS/signindiffer  Logistics  ▪  Documents needed:  ▪  Padlet to help organize and guide the workshop  ▪  Note taker for quick access to make notes  Padlet link:  bit.ly/ CARE Multimodal  https://form.jotform.com/WCEPS/signindiffer  Sign-In Sheet:  Track Learning  Planting Seeds  Gardener planting seeds; You decide how you cultivate those seeds, fertilize it and help it grow.  It’s hard to imagine that a mighty oak tree can come from a tiny acorn, but with enough patience, you can grow one yourself! Look for an acorn to plant in early autumn, germinate it, and start it in a container. Then, transplant your acorn in a prepared site. Care for your growing oak tree so that future generations can enjoy it for years to come!  Introductions  Ivonne Margarita Oronoz Rosas Experience & Language Timeline  Born in Chihuahua Chihuahua Mexico, both my parents also born in Chihuahua. Came to the US when I was 5.  My great grandmother was a story taller, she often shared stores about Pancho Villa (Jose Doroteo Arango Arambula) and many other historical events  Started K only speaking Spanish. In 3Ł grade I was still reading at a K-1 level. In HS I was placed in JROTC, my teacher thought I was a born leader. I was pushed to be better than what I thought I was capable of.  Started teaching in 1995 in Las Vegas NV when Bilingual Program started, taught: K, 1, 3r Middle School 6t English & 7t Science. ELL specialist for secondary schools Instructional coach for 25 schools. Family coordinator for HS lastly, Family Engagement Specialist for Pre-K to 12Ł grade districtwide. Left to work for WIDA in 2014 then WCEPS in 2018  First time my young men make tamales, want them to start experiencing their culture  © 2024 CARE Coaching  Linguistic Support  ✓  Middle: First Name (in favorite color)  ✓  Upper Left: Years in Education  ✓  Upper Right: Rate your understanding of scaffolding  ✓  Lower Left: Role  ✓  Lower Right: A word that describes scaffolding  Gauging Expertise  Rate your familiarity on a scale of 1-5.  1.  Not too sure how to scaffold for MLs, YET  2.  I have tried scaffolding, but it is difficult with large classes  3.  I have planned to scaffold and have had some success  4.  I have planned and facilitated scaffolded lessons well  5.  I am very successful in scaffolding lessons  Getting to Know You  I am ok! ¡Estoy bien!  Help? ¿Ayúdame?  When’s lunch? ¿Cuándo es el almuerzo?  I need coffee! ¡Necesito café!  *sigh* *suspirando*  Kind of sad Un poco triste  ¡Deseoso/a de aprender¡  No estoy aqui I am not here  Happy! ¡Contenta/Contento!  Eager to learn!  bit.ly/YODAIAM  Collaboration  A single conversation across the table with a wise man is better than ten years mere study of books.  -  Henry Wadsworth Longfellow  Be a Marigold  Marigolds are one of the best “companion” plants for gardens. Experienced gardeners utilize the marigold to help other plants nearby flourish and improve the overall health of the garden. The connection for teachers is to seek out and surround yourself with marigold teachers who will help you improve and thrive.  In contrast, Gonzalez talks about the marigold’s anthesis – the walnut tree. The walnut mindset or walnut tree “…give off a negativity that can inhibit growth, wilt, and ultimately prevent the growth of nearby vegetable plants.”  Find Your Marigold: The  Adapted from “Be A Marigold” is a corollary post to  One Essential Rule for New Teachers  by Jennifer Gonzalez.  Who is in Your Class?  Look Familiar?  ▪  Is an immigrant  ▪  Parents have an elementary education  ▪  Speaks another language at home  ▪  Receives free or reduced lunch  ▪  Struggling with learning English  ▪  Reads below grade level  ▪  Talks too much in class  ▪  Distracts others  Look at Her Now!  ▪  Earned a Master’s degree in education  ▪  Taught multiple subjects and grades (25+ years)  ▪  Developed family engagement program  ▪  Provided instructional coaching  ▪  Directs national Educator Engagement Program  ▪  Presents at national and international conferences  ▪  Facilitates professional learning for K-12 teachers  ▪  Has been a keynote for regional conferences  - Possible Labels + Mind shifts  ▪  Child of immigrants.  ▪  Student with a diverse background.  ▪  Struggling with learning English.  ▪  Beginning to learn English.  ▪  Reads below grade level.  ▪  Decodes fluently, needs support in developing comprehension strategies.  ▪  Talks too much in class.  ▪  Eager to share opinions.  ▪  Distracts others.  ▪  Is trying to connect with peers.  ▪  Receives free or reduced lunch.  ▪  Receives free or reduced lunch.  ▪  Speaks another language at home.  ▪  Comes from multilingual home.  ▪  Parents have an elementary education.  ▪  Parents demonstrate perseverance.  Knowledge is Empowering  The more you know students’ readiness levels, learning preferences, interests, linguistic, backgrounds, and life experiences and skills, the more effective your instruction will be.  You will find so many different personalities stuck together in one classroom. By getting to know your students, you have a better understanding of how to plan, engage and support their learning.  In Your Context  Share how you currently gather information about your students:  ▪  Interests  ▪  Experiences  ▪  Languages  ▪  Cultures  ▪  Learning preferences  ▪  Families  One Minute Reflection  1.  Consider how you might change, enhance or incorporate ideas from this section.  2.  Share with your table group.  3.  Record additional ideas.  Who is in Your Class?  What is Multi-Modal?  Redefining Texts as Multimodal  Texts can be…  ▪  defined as a unit of meaning that has an intended purpose for a specific audience in a particular context  ▪  multimodal, incorporating a wide variety of meaning-making systems  ▪  written, oral (conversations or essays), digital (websites or broadcasts), visual (diagrams, art, posters, advertisements), or a combination  Multiple Modes of Communication  Exploring Scaffolding  Everyone is a genius. But if you judge a fish by its ability to climb a tree, it will live its whole life believing that it can’t swim.  ─ Adapted from Albert Einstein  A Scaffolding Definition  ▪  Learners complete small, manageable steps in order to reach the goal. Working in collaboration with a skilled instructor or more knowledgeable peers help students make connections between concepts.  ▪  As learners grow within their zone of proximal development and become more confident, they practice new tasks with the social support that surrounds them.  Vygotsky defined scaffolding instruction as the “role of teachers and others in supporting the learner's development and providing support structures to get to that next stage or level” (Raymond, 2000)  A Scaffolding Definition (cont’d)  ▪  Learning occurs through purposeful, meaningful interactions with others and the teacher is central to learning; society and culture influence the attitudes and beliefs of a student towards learning and education; language is the primary tool used in the development of learning in children, including the transfer of sociocultural influences; and students benefit greatly in programs that are student-led, as they can use the social interaction to grow towards their potential level of development.  Vygotsky defined scaffolding instruction as the “role of teachers and others in supporting the learner's development and providing support structures to get to that next stage or level” (Raymond, 2000)  A Scaffolding Definition (cont’d)  ▪  Society and culture influence the attitudes and beliefs of a student towards learning and education. Creating relationships that provide a safe environment in using language as the primary tool used in the development of learning in children, including the transfer of sociocultural influences; and students benefit greatly in programs that are student-led, as they can use the social interaction to grow towards their potential level of development.  Vygotsky defined scaffolding instruction as the “role of teachers and others in supporting the learner's development and providing support structures to get to that next stage or level” (Raymond, 2000)  Concepts from Scaffolding  Relationships Concept Goals Beliefs Zone Interaction Sociocultural Why Blue? Why Green?Why Orange?  Zone of Proximal Development  1.  Review the image on the following slide.  2.  Make notes of key understanding.  3.  Work together to create a one-minute summary.  4.  Volunteers to share their summary  Connections Making  What is UDL?  Universal Design for Learning provides the opportunity for all students to access, participate in, and by reducing barriers to instruction, including language barrier.  The UDL framework values diversity through proactive design of an inclusive instructional practices.  Principles of UDL  WHY: Engagement  WHAT:  Representation  HOW: Action & Expression  Connected to Concept  Goal: Expert learners who are…  Access  Support  Function  Design Options for Building Knowledge (3) Ł • Connect prior knowledge to new learning ( 3.1) > • Highlight and explore patterns, critical features, big ideas, and relationships (3.2) > • Cultivate multiple ways of knowing and making meaning (3.3) > • Maximize transfer and generalization (3.4) >  © 2024 CARE Coaching  Connected to Relationships  Goal: Expert learners who are…  Access  Support  Function  Design Options for  Emotional Capacity (9) C)  •  Recognize expectations, beliefs, and motivations (9.1) >  •  Develop awareness of self and others (9.2) >  •  Promote individual and collective reflection (9.3) >  •  Cultivate empathy and restorative practices (9.4) >  Connected to Goals  Goal: Expert learners who are…  Access  © 2024 CARE Coaching  Support  Function  Design Options for  Strategy Development (6) C)  •  Set meaningful goals (6.1) >  •  Anticipate and plan for challenges (6.2) >  •  Organize information and resources (6.3) >  •  Enhance capacity for monitoring progress (  6.4)  >  •  Challenge exclusionary practices (6.5) >  Explore  :Padlet Yvonne + 8 • 19h CARE Coaching: Designing Multi-Modal Instruction to Meet the Needs of English Learners Principle 2: Engage in Effective Pedagogical Practices � + 1) Workshop Materials\\ I Session Note Taker : z a MultiModal-K-12 FINAL Guide-FILLABLE 9o • Example Lesson Planning Template DOCX Lesson Planning Template (MM) Oo 2) Universal Design: for Leaming + -□ About Universal Design for Learr ing-CAST 9° • UDL Guidelines Overview Oo ne uDL Guidelines [ As----z � � .u The UDL Guidelines Oo ' 3) ELD Framework 2020 Resources + I I . PDF WIDA ELD Standards Framework 2020 91 • Kindergarten O o 4) Resources for Leaming about Students Culture Cards : 5) Resources to Support Student Success SIOP Language Objectives Cheat Sheet Oo 99 7)Po 6) Classroom 