ou Understand?  At a party, everyone shook hands with everyone else. There were sixty-six handshakes. How many people were at the party?  Does This Help?  n= number of people  n  …  5  4  3  2  T=  total  handshakes  6  …  10  6  3  1  Difference between total # of handshakes (based on # of people  -4 -3 -2  n2-n/2=66 n2-n=132  T = n(n-1)/2  n2-n-132=0  n=12  With two people, there is one handshake: A-B (1)  With three, three handshakes: A-B B-C A-C (3)  With four, six handshakes: A-B A-C A-D B-C B-D C-D (6)  With five, 10 handshakes: A-B A-C A-D A-E B-C B-D B-E C-D C-E D-E (10)  With n people, there are n-1 handshakes!  If n equals the number of people, and n-1 equals the number of handshakes, the number of people is the product of the number of people and the number of handshakes, divided by two, since there can only be one handshake for every two people. n2-n/2=66 n2-n=132  n2-n-132=0  n=12  In a Language You Understand?  At a party, everyone shook hands with everyone else. There were sixty-six handshakes. How many people were at the party?  There were 12 people at the party!  Writing & Scaffolds  ▪  Encourage translanguaging  ▪  Encourage them to make meaning first  ▪  Encourage asking for clarification  ▪  Encourage the use of sentence frames or word choices created by your students  Ł CARE Coaching Ł Ł an»o tau.vie taosvont opportuaes © 2024 CARE Coaching  Building and Assessing Oral Academic Language  Ł CARE Coaching Ł Ł an»o tau.vie taosvont opportuaes SAVANAH BALLENTINE GRADE9 © 2024 CARE Coaching  One Minute Reflection  1.  Consider how you might change, enhance or incorporate ideas from this section.  2.  Share with your table group.  3.  Record additional ideas.  Understanding Language & Content  Lunch Break 60 minutes  Shared Experience  Strategy Share: Two-Partners  1.  Partner up  2.  Decide who is partner A and Partner B  3.  Partner A be prepared to respond to a question  4.  Partner B be prepared to listen attentively  5.  Partner A please respond 