    "content": "Please sign in using the QR code of the link below! bit.ly/CARESIGN  Building Capacity to Bridge Content Learning and Language Development  Facilitator Name  CARE Coach  Date  Location  A Brief History  Wisconsin Center for Education Products and Services  Session Objectives  •  Describe the four components of the framework  •  Use functions and features for instructional planning  •  Analyze student writing using proficiency level descriptors  Ł One mark of a great educator is the ability to lead students out to new places where even the educator has never been. -a  © 2024 CARE Coaching  Getting to Know Me  24+ years in education  • Elementary teacher  • Middle and high school counselor  • Elementary principal  • K-5 Science, Social Studies, Grading and Reporting Supervisor  • K-12 Supervisor of Assessment  • K-12 Supervisor of ESOL & Title III  Getting to Know You  1.  Middle: Name  2.  Bottom left: Years in Education  3.  Bottom right: Position  4.  Top right: How familiar with the Framework  1.  Huh? | 2. Heard of them | 3. Explored | 4. Used | 5. Give me that clicker!  25 5 Facilitator  C aching � CARE Coa-*::: �� □.os.ca.sons �� a»ncnataus 4 CARE Coaching ©202  Gauging Expertise  Rate your familiarity on a scale of 1-5.  1.  Never heard of the 2020 Standards Framework  2.  I have heard of the 2020 Standards Framework  3.  Have planned with the 2020 Standards Framework  4.  Have implemented the 2020 Standards Framework  5.  I can facilitate this workshop  I am ok! ¡Estoy bien!  Help? ¿Ayúdame?  When’s lunch? ¿Cuándo es el almuerzo?  I need coffee! ¡Necesito café!  *sigh* *suspirando*  Kind of sad Un poco triste  ¡Deseoso/a de aprender¡  No estoy aqui I am not here  Happy! ¡Contenta/Contento!  Eager to learn!  Foundational Concepts  Asset-Based Philosophy  ▪  Reflects the belief that everyone has valuable resources they can use to support their own and others’ learning  ▪  Asserts that linguistically and culturally diverse learners bring a unique set of assets that enrich any learning community  Multilingual Learners  •  Supports an asset-based belief system  •  Describes students who use, understand, or engage with languages in addition to English on a regular basis.  The 2020 Edition  2020 Edition  WIDA. (2020). WIDA English language development standards framework, 2020 edition: Kindergarten–grade 12. Board of Regents of the University of Wisconsin System.  Session Objectives  bit.ly/ELDRollout PW: CARECoaching  Big Ideas  Four Big Ideas  TASK: Big Ideas Jigsaw  p17  A B C D  TASK: Big Ideas Jigsaw  1.  Letter off A through D.  2.  Read your assigned idea.  3.  Gather in like-lettered groups.  4.  Share your key ideas.  5.  Create a representation of your learning.  6.  Choose someone to share.  p18  Equity  A  Opportunity and Access  ▪  Set high expectations for all students  ▪  Provide access to grade-level content  ▪  Enact sustainable pedagogies  ▪  Create opportunities for learning:  o  engage actively in deep learning  o  use multiple resources, such as translanguaging  pp18-19  Integration  B  Benefits of Integration  Integration of content and language promotes:  ▪  Understanding the connections between content and language  ▪  Making meaning within and across disciplines  ▪  Interaction of students with each other in challenging content activities  ▪  Coordination of design and delivery of curriculum, instruction, and assessment  Multiple Means  Modes of Communication  pp19-20  Collaboration  C  Opportunities to Collaborate  Content and language teachers can work collaboratively to:  ▪  Design and deliver grade-level standards-based instruction  ▪  Plan for systematic, explicit, and sustained language development alongside the academic demands of the content areas  ▪  Support one another  ▪  Share unique fields of expertise  Shared Responsibility  ▪  Language specialists, content teachers, and administrators need to see themselves as responsible for academic achievement  ▪  Systems must reject the approach that language specialists alone should assume responsibility language development  Collaboration among Stakeholders  Stakeholders in educational systems include:  ▪  District, school and community leaders  ▪  Content and classroom teachers  ▪  Specialists  ▪  Support staff  ▪  Families  ▪  Students  Functional Approach  D  ▪  Use of particular linguistic tools to achieve certain purposes  ▪  Making language choices based on  o  Topic  o  Task  o  Roles  o  Identities  o  Status  o  Audience  Key Concepts  ŁMinl Ł_.:_::: ;ounial Ł Golf Łance ln nm room .,, Ł Ł Hula • Hoop Ł Ł'RtAKis Ł lff'\\Ł A Oraw 1 1) C,1 V hlindf olde( Go 01Łtdoors lndoor he1wling Meditate . -f1 . Ł d Ł Scretch Jb. walk__ Pet a og J\\ p[ay an i;,1<t.,.-, 1'fUPl-1t' ------------l \\ \"\"' ... Ł.,.-.......... -- --Ł .... -... , ' t 1 .. Ł Bftc:tih 8ftBClk8 ' I ' , --... -... .,,.---... -._ .. _..,,. 1 1  2024 CARE Coaching  The WIDA  Standards Framework  2020 Edition  WIDA. (2020). WIDA English language development standards framework, 2020 edition: Kindergarten–grade 12. Board of Regents of the University of Wisconsin System.  Four Components  (WIDA, 2020)  Organization  Grades Standard Key Language Use Language Expectations Proficiency Level Descriptors K | 1 | 2-3 | 4-5 | 6-8 | 9-12 S & I | LA | MA | SC | SS Narrate| Inform | Argue | Explain Stem + Function + (Features) PL1 →PL6  What’s New? Different?  Support our Planning  Standards Statements  Standards Statements  (WIDA, 2020)  •  Provide the broadest conceptual framing of language development  •  Illustrate the integration of content and language  •  Show language use in the service of learning  •  Address the language of schooling  Standards Abbreviations  →  of for  Academic language is the gatekeeper  to success in our schools; it is the  means by which students access  knowledge, express their  understanding, and find their voice  in the world.  — Unknown  TASK: A Thousand Words?  TASK: A Thousand Words?  1.  Number off 1 to