ea or solution, change the audience’s point of view, bring about action, or accept a position or evaluation of an issue.  Anie Ian E.  Genre =  Genre =  Category of Literature  Purpose for Communication  Genre by Any Other Name?  Key Language Use  =  Genre-Family  One Concept, Two Phrases  Families–Examples-Applications  Key Language Uses  Sixth Column Third Document  Grade-Level Discussions  1)  Organize into grade-level groups.  2)  Read the KLUs Two-Pager  3)  Make connections to KLU Snapshot Tables  4)  Share your understanding of KLUs:  •  Chart paper summary  •  Visual representation  •  Skit  •  Song  •  Other?  Share  NARRATE Represent experiences  Family Members  Intention  Curriculum Connections  Short stories  Entertain, encourage reflection or teach a lesson  Woman Hollering Creek  Anecdotes  Share a short and amusing episode about a real person  You won’t believe what  happened!  Biographies  Recount episodes from  another person’s life  A Biography of Nelson Mandela  Historical Accounts  Recount historical eras  The Ming Dynasty  News Articles  Inform about current events  Safety in School  Content Connections  ELA.Literacy.W.6.3  Write narratives to develop real or imagined  experiences or events using effective technique, relevant descriptive details, and  well-structured event sequences.  INFORM Communicate generalized information  Family  Members  Intention  Curriculum  Connections  Descriptive Report  Describe attributes and features of an entity  Magnets  Comparative Report  Identify similarities and differences between two or more things  Stars and planets  Compositional Report  Describe parts & wholes  Layers of the rainforest  Lab Report  Describe experiments based on the scientific method  Techniques to separate mixtures  Design Report  Design and create a product, service, performance, or artwork  Constructing multimedia compositions  D2.Geo.1.6-8 (C3 Framework)  Construct maps to represent … special patterns of cultural and environmental ch