  {
    "id": "a6b5b9d0-3a5c-4bae-a8f4-00285b5bc29d",
    "title": "Preparing Students for WIDA ACCESS Online (Part 1/1)",
    "content": "Preparing Students for WIDA ACCESS Online  Test Demo  Watch the test demo before you go through the sample items. The test demo takes about 15 minutes to introduce the features of the test platform and introduce the item types students will see on the test. Review the demo at least once before showing it to students. Students do not need to interact with the platform as the test demo plays, so you can have students watch the demos on their own, or you can show the demo to a group of students all at once. There are two modules of the test demo that don’t play automatically. These modules cover handwriting responses to the Writing test and using test accommodations. If these topics are applicable to your students, select and play them from the menu.  Allow students to re-watch the test demo as needed. Many schools schedule a time for students to watch the demo and then immediately try out what they’ve learned by completing the sample or practice items.  To access the Test Demo:  1.  wbte.drcedirect.com/WIDA Go to (<https://wbte.drcedirect.com/WIDA/portals/wida>).  2.  Select Test Demo.  3.  Select ACCESS for ELLs.  4.  Select the demo appropriate to the grade level of your students.  ©2025 Board of Regents of the University of Wisconsin System Last revised September 2025  Student Support  Talk with all students about using universal tools during testing just as they may in the classroom. Tools that are built into the test platform for any student to use at any time are detailed in the table below.  Icon  Icon Name  Purpose/Function  Help  Provides information on tool usage, test navigation, accommodations, and other topics.  Options  Allows the student to access the Color Preferences options, which change the background and text colors.  Highlighter  Allows the student to highlight a portion of the text.  Notepad  Allows the student to create a small note in which they can type a short message for later reference. The Notepad is only available for Writing.  Magnifier  Allows the student to access the magnifier options: x1.5, x2.0, x3.0, or x4.0. Once the tool is activated, an “Off” option will also appear when this icon is clicked again.  Line Guide  Allows the student to follow along with each line of text. The student can drag the line up, down, left, or right on the screen as an aid in reading an item or passage.  Students with an Individualized Education Program (IEP) or 504 Plan may be able to use a variety of additional accommodations when testing. For example, you can give students the ability to manually play or replay audio that would otherwise play automatically and only once. All students need opportunities to familiarize themselves with any accessibility support they’ll use during testing.  Accessibility and Accommodations page  Find details on allowed testing tools in the Accessibility and Accommodations Manual, available on the of the WIDA website.  Sample Items  Sample items are representative of the format and structure of actual test items and tasks. Students need headphones for the Listening and Writing items, and headsets with microphones for the Speaking items. The sample items are similar in difficulty and depth to the items and tasks students complete on testing day, and they are a helpful preparation tool even for students who have taken the test in prior years. However, sample items do not simulate a complete test experience. They do not cover the full range of content or performance level expectations students encounter on the test.  It is especially important that students practice the Speaking test. The Speaking domain test is a unique, and for some students, challenging test experience. To complete the Speaking tasks, students record their voices using the recording tools built into the test platform. The more opportunities students have to interact with the test platform and become familiar with speaking into a headset, the more comfortable they will be on testing day.  sample scripting  Review each sample item at least once before students complete it. Unlike items in the test practice, sample items do not have embedded test directions, so have students watch the test demo before going through the sample items. Use to guide students through sample items and test platform features.  When students are completing sample items:  •  Answer questions about test content, testing tools and procedures, and technology.  •  Rephrase test directions. If allowed under your state’s testing policy, it’s okay to translate test directions into the student’s home language.  •  Help students log in, navigate, adjust volume, and adjust headphones and headsets.  •  Help students use the recording tools in the Speaking test.  •  Don’t interrupt those who are working independently.  •  Don’t confirm or correct students’ responses.  To access Sample Items:  1.  wbte.drcedirect.com/WIDA Go to (<https://wbte.drcedirect.com/WIDA/portals/wida>).  2.  On the Select An Administration screen, select Sample Items.  3.  On the Select an Assessment screen, select Sample Items.  4.  On the Sample Items screen, select the appropriate grade-level cluster.  5.  On the Assessment Selection screen, select the appropriate domain.  6.  On the Grade Selection screen, select the appropriate tier (and accommodation, if applicable).  7.  On the Verify Student Information screen, select Continue in the blue bar to proceed.  8.  On the Test Modules screen, select the domain name.  9.  On the Test Directions screen, select Begin The Test at the bottom of the screen.  Test Practice  The Listening, Reading, and Speaking domain tests begin with an opportunity for students to practice responding to practice items.  Test Practice items are not necessarily representative of the difficulty of scored test items – they are simply a chance for students to become familiar with the structure of the test and try out the process of responding to items and tasks.  The test platform guides students through these practice items before presenting the scored items. However, completing the Test Practice items prior to testing day as well can be helpful for students, particularly those new to testing, such as grade 1 students or newcomers. To access Test Practice items outside of the actual test session, follow these steps:  1.  wbte.drcedirect.com/WIDA Go to (<https://wbte.drcedirect.com/WIDA/portals/wida>).  2.  On the Select An Administration screen, select WIDA ACCESS.  3.  On the Select An Assessment screen, select WIDA ACCESS Test Practice.  4.  On the next screen, select the appropriate domain test.  5.  On the Grade Selection (Listening, Reading, and Writing) or Assessment Selection (Speaking) screen, select the appropriate grade-level cluster (and tier/accommodation , if applicable).  6.  On the Verify Student Information screen, select Continue in the blue bar to proceed.  7.  On the Test Modules screen, select the domain name. For the Speaking domain Test Practice, the student will be prompted to complete the microphone check next.  8.  On the Test Directions screen, select Begin the Test at the bottom of the screen.",
