    "id": "920be3e4-be3f-4802-ac5f-48e6ddabebc6",
    "title": "Top Challenges Faced by ELD Specialists, Instructional Leaders, and Content Teachers",
    "content": "Title: Top Challenges Faced by ELD Specialists, Instructional Leaders, and Content Teachers\n\nContent: Top Challenges Faced by ELD Specialists, Instructional Leaders, and Content Teachers  This one-pager synthesizes the most common and recurring challenges identified across all uploaded meeting notes, planning documents, and professional learning session summaries. These themes are present across K–12 settings, various districts, and both in-person and virtual PD planning contexts.  ELD Specialists  ●  Integrating the WIDA 2020 Framework into daily planning and instructional coaching.  ●  Supporting dually-identified students (MLLs with IEPs) without clear protocols or aligned practices.  ●  Navigating push-in and co-teaching models with unclear roles and limited co-planning time.  ●  Operating with limited differentiated PD materials (especially for K–12 splits).  ●  Lack of visibility in decision-making and need for greater advocacy for MLs.  Instructional Leaders  ●  Building capacity in content teachers to deliver language-integrated instruction.  ●  Establishing consistency across grade levels and content areas for ML strategies.  ●  Managing instructional decisions for dually-identified students across buildings.  ●  Measuring implementation success without clear benchmarks or walkthrough norms.  ●  Scaling high-quality PD to large, diverse educator groups without losing depth.  Content Teachers  ●  Difficulty identifying the language demands embedded in content instruction.  ●  Challenges differentiating for newcomers and long-term ELs in one classroom.  ●  Limited understanding of how to embed language objectives meaningfully.  ●  Uncertainty around how to foster academic discourse with MLs.  ●  Need for concrete UDL/multimodal strategies that connect to both language and content goals. ",
