    "id": "45ae4965-2b7c-4072-90bc-aebd28b08e8d",
    "title": "GMT20241001-215804_Recording.cc (Part 1/1)",
    "content": "WEBVTT\r\n\r\n00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:30.000\r\nThanks for joining Rosemary.\r\n\r\n00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:43.000\r\nHave about 2 min before we begin. We want to make sure we start on time. So we're gonna wait for all the colleagues across the Us.\r\n\r\n00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.000\r\nTo join us. In the meantime, please keep.\r\n\r\n00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.000\r\nAdding, where you're from, what you.\r\n\r\n00:00:49.000 --> 00:01:19.000\r\nAnything you'd like to share in the chat while we wait.\r\n\r\n00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.000\r\nI'm happy to see Nevada. We hardly see you all, Leslie. Thank you for joining us.\r\n\r\n00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.000\r\nThere's 1 from North Dakota.\r\n\r\n00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.000\r\nOh, wonderful!\r\n\r\n00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.000\r\nYeah.\r\n\r\n00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.000\r\nNew York.\r\n\r\n00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.000\r\nSan Francisco, couple from Texas.\r\n\r\n00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.000\r\nWonderful welcome Texas.\r\n\r\n00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:51.000\r\nIowa.\r\n\r\n00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.000\r\nAny from California yet.\r\n\r\n00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.000\r\nYeah. One from San Francisco.\r\n\r\n00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.000\r\nOh, that's right. San Francisco.\r\n\r\n00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:04.000\r\nI was in Reno, Nevada, so I feel San Francisco is like part of Nevada. I spent so much time there.\r\n\r\n00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.000\r\nHi erin nice to see you.\r\n\r\n00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.000\r\nAnd we got a principal in here.\r\n\r\n00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.000\r\nWonderful. That's a plus.\r\n\r\n00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:17.000\r\nTo those of you who are administrators. Jamie, I think.\r\n\r\n00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:25.000\r\nMargie. I'm not sure if you have the link. We have a free webinar specific for administrators that is coming up.\r\n\r\n00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.000\r\nAmy, I mean Margie, if you want to send it to Jamie.\r\n\r\n00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:34.000\r\nSure. Yeah, let me look for that. Yes, I'll look for that, and I can share it.\r\n\r\n00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.000\r\nYes.\r\n\r\n00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:41.000\r\nMight be good to have one on newcomers. Yeah, you're welcome.\r\n\r\n00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:47.000\r\nOh, there's Pasadena! Yay, Gloria!\r\n\r\n00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.000\r\nAlright! It's 5 o'clock.\r\n\r\n00:02:50.000 --> 00:03:05.000\r\nSo I think we'll go ahead and get started. It's nice to see such a variety of folks here, different roles from across the country. All joining us for the for us tips and dips on newcomers.\r\n\r\n00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.000\r\nI think, from what we're hearing and seeing.\r\n\r\n00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:14.000\r\nA lot of districts are seeing an increase in the student population.\r\n\r\n00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.000\r\nAnd a lot of interest in.\r\n\r\n00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.000\r\nDesigning, you know, an effective system.\r\n\r\n00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.000\r\nAnd strategies for helping them.\r\n\r\n00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:34.000\r\nMy name's Ryan. I'm the lead newcomer coach at Yipsips.\r\n\r\n00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:48.000\r\nFor those of you who don't know. Yeps began about 14 years ago.\r\n\r\n00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:49.000\r\nBrian, you're muted.\r\n\r\n00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.000\r\nOh, you got muted for some reason, right?\r\n\r\n00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.000\r\nAnd gradually.\r\n\r\n00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.000\r\nThis time it wasn't me.\r\n\r\n00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.000\r\nI did into that, either. I don't know what happened.\r\n\r\n00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.000\r\nI don't know who that.\r\n\r\n00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:03.000\r\nAnyway, care coaching started in about in 2,021.\r\n\r\n00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.000\r\nAnd there's a range of offerings for.\r\n\r\n00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:15.000\r\nIn person, and webinars that yeps can do to support your district. It's very customized work to meet the needs of.\r\n\r\n00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.000\r\nOf your specific situation.\r\n\r\n00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:24.000\r\nIn including this one. So this newcomer series is, we do offer as a full day session.\r\n\r\n00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:32.000\r\nToday, we're gonna do 3 of the sections that we generally do in the full day workshop, but just are more.\r\n\r\n00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:39.000\r\nAnd today we'll be talking about who are our newcomers creating a safe and thriving environment.\r\n\r\n00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.000\r\nAnd instructional strategies.\r\n\r\n00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.000\r\nAnd so let's just dive right in.\r\n\r\n00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.000\r\nOne thing to remember. I think we all probably know this, but.\r\n\r\n00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.000\r\nThis is. It's a very big puzzle.\r\n\r\n00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:55.000\r\nWorking with newcomers, working with any student.\r\n\r\n00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.000\r\nThere's a lot of things that go into it. There are no magic bullets with working.\r\n\r\n00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.000\r\nWith newcomers. So this is just one piece of an overall.\r\n\r\n00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:08.000\r\nOf a much larger puzzle.\r\n\r\n00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:24.000\r\nA little bit about me. My name's Ryan, I said. I've been in education for 21 years. I've worked as a teacher and coordinator in public schools in Missouri and Colorado, and I also worked in an international school in Vietnam.\r\n\r\n00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:32.000\r\nI was a State Director of English Development in Missouri, and most recently I served as the principal of a newcomer in Kansas City.\r\n\r\n00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.000\r\nCalled the Global Academy.\r\n\r\n00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.000\r\nAnd now I work with yep.\r\n\r\n00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:42.000\r\nI am bilingual although I don't think I speak any language.\r\n\r\n00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.000\r\nThat well,\r\n\r\n00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:50.000\r\nI have. I do have my my doctorate, and.\r\n\r\n00:05:50.000 --> 00:06:02.000\r\nAnd educational leadership, and something a little different or not different. But unique about me is that I did grow up in rural Missouri where there wasn't much diversity.\r\n\r\n00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.000\r\nAnd\r\n\r\n00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.000\r\nI think that's 1 of the reasons why I'm so.\r\n\r\n00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:14.000\r\nEngaged and fascinated by all of the different cultures around the world. Now, because I didn't see that much growing up.\r\n\r\n00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:21.000\r\nThis is an example of a trading card. A trading cards are great ways to get to know your students.\r\n\r\n00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:28.000\r\nAnd you can use these in a variety of ways in the classroom, so they can get to know each other.\r\n\r\n00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:33.000\r\nOne idea that that you may share is\r\n\r\n00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.000\r\nThis is a welcoming ritual.\r\n\r\n00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:42.000\r\nRituals are very important for students as you, as they start their journey in your school.\r\n\r\n00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:49.000\r\nIn this example. There's a large map right in the middle of the hallway, where every new student, as they come in.\r\n\r\n00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:53.000\r\nCan put their trading card up on the wall.\r\n\r\n00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:59.000\r\nAnd they can make connections to other students who may be from that area, or just see.\r\n\r\n00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.000\r\nThe diversity that's in your school.\r\n\r\n00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:11.000\r\nIn this particular example. This student who's putting the card there now made a quick connection with someone in her class found another student from Puerto Rico.\r\n\r\n00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:16.000\r\nAnd she could make an immediate connection to that student.\r\n\r\n00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:21.000\r\nThese. You only have one time to make a 1st impression. So.\r\n\r\n00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:26.000\r\nWhat you do whenever the students 1st arrive in your building.\r\n\r\n00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:32.000\r\nReally matters and and help create this sense of community.\r\n\r\n00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:41.000\r\nOther ideas to get to know your newcomers. Th! There's an IM from that we generally do in the full day workshop.\r\n\r\n00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:45.000\r\nTick, flip grid, although.\r\n\r\n00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:50.000\r\nI hear Flipgrid has gone away for anybody that doesn't use Microsoft or Microsoft teams.\r\n\r\n00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.000\r\nBut there's some other cool online things you can do.\r\n\r\n00:07:53.000 --> 00:08:00.000\r\nThe trading cards. As I mentioned before, social media type post.\r\n\r\n00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:08.000\r\nOr even a story. A dual language. My journey story.\r\n\r\n00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:11.000\r\nAs a very powerful way for students to.\r\n\r\n00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:18.000\r\nIntroduce themselves to to you, to the community, but also to help them process. Maybe.\r\n\r\n00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:22.000\r\nSome of the things that happened on their journey.\r\n\r\n00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:26.000\r\nBut getting to know your newcomers. So who.\r\n\r\n00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:28.000\r\nWho are our newcomers.\r\n\r\n00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.000\r\nI think there's a range of.\r\n\r\n00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:35.000\r\nRange of people in this webinar.\r\n\r\n00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:41.000\r\nSo the the term newcomer, which I've come to realize means different things in different.\r\n\r\n00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:44.000\r\nDifferent school districts.\r\n\r\n00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:52.000\r\nIt's an umbrella term that includes a a wide variety of of students. It's a very diverse mix.\r\n\r\n00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:56.000\r\nI know one newcomer is alike.\r\n\r\n00:08:56.000 --> 00:09:02.000\r\nSo grouping them all together is is interesting. Sometimes newcomers.\r\n\r\n00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:05.000\r\nAre any students who.\r\n\r\n00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:08.000\r\nRecently immigrated to the United States.\r\n\r\n00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:13.000\r\nBut there are a lot of countries around the world where English is the\r\n\r\n00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:16.000\r\nIs the is the official language.\r\n\r\n00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:18.000\r\nFor example.\r\n\r\n00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:20.000\r\nWe had a student from Guyana.\r\n\r\n00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:24.000\r\nTeacher really wanted them to come to a new center.\r\n\r\n00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:26.000\r\nBut the student.\r\n\r\n00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:31.000\r\nEnglish was their native language, so.\r\n\r\n00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:33.000\r\nMost.\r\n\r\n00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:42.000\r\nWell, how do how do I say that? Right? Some multilingual students may need help integrating into life and school in the United States? So school looks differently.\r\n\r\n00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:45.000\r\nIn different parts of the world.\r\n\r\n00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:49.000\r\nBig yellow school buses aren't always the the norm.\r\n\r\n00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:57.000\r\nSo helping them adjust to life in school, in in your area is important.\r\n\r\n00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:00.000\r\nSome students may have access to a newcomer center.\r\n\r\n00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.000\r\nAnd some students may be mainstreamed with the Ld support.\r\n\r\n00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:15.000\r\nAnd so it's very important that you also think about the structures available to help students in in their journey.\r\n\r\n00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:19.000\r\nAnd that's very blurry. But hopefully we can still see.\r\n\r\n00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:22.000\r\nIs that? Alright, Margie!\r\n\r\n00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:27.000\r\nYou can see it. This chart showed growth patterns.\r\n\r\n00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:34.000\r\nOkay, so there's some. There was some research around when Essa was coming out.\r\n\r\n00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:38.000\r\nJust seemed like a few years ago, but it's been a while now.\r\n\r\n00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.000\r\nAround growth rates and.\r\n\r\n00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:49.000\r\nNewcomers in particular. Here was they studied 3rd and 8th graders.\r\n\r\n00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:52.000\r\nNewcomers had tremendous growth.\r\n\r\n00:10:52.000 --> 00:10:54.000\r\nIn their 1st few years.\r\n\r\n00:10:54.000 --> 00:10:59.000\r\nWhile in the you know, while in the States.\r\n\r\n00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:03.000\r\nCompared to students who started higher on the scale.\r\n\r\n00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:12.000\r\nAnd so newcomers tend to come, and they grow very rapidly and then start to level off.\r\n\r\n00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:15.000\r\nThey say that? What is it.\r\n\r\n00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:18.000\r\nLower, is faster, higher.\r\n\r\n00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:23.000\r\nAnd that is true for grade, and proficiency level.\r\n\r\n00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:26.000\r\nSo students who are lower in lower grades.\r\n\r\n00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:31.000\r\nAnd lower proficiency level grow faster than students who are.\r\n\r\n00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:34.000\r\nAnd the higher grades and the higher proficiency levels.\r\n\r\n00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:45.000\r\nNow I will. I will tell you that the range for newcomers is much bigger than the other the other grade levels and other proficiency levels.\r\n\r\n00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:48.000\r\nWhich means that what schools do.\r\n\r\n00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:54.000\r\nReally matters like how they address the needs of newcomers really matters. So if you do.\r\n\r\n00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:57.000\r\nIf you do a good job at addressing their needs.\r\n\r\n00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:01.000\r\nYou're gonna give them a nice jump. Start in their English journey.\r\n\r\n00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:04.000\r\nRyan. There's a question in the chat that says.\r\n\r\n00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:11.000\r\nIt seems like the older newcomers in the scraph actually went down. Is that right?\r\n\r\n00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:20.000\r\nWell, that would be year 1, 2, 3, 4, so it looks like from year 3 to 4 the newcomers. Scores went down.\r\n\r\n00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:25.000\r\nThis would be an 8th grade student, and that would have happened at the high school age.\r\n\r\n00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:28.000\r\nYes, but generally what?\r\n\r\n00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:32.000\r\nWhat we should look in this 1st year.\r\n\r\n00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:35.000\r\nYou see how it just shoots up there.\r\n\r\n00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:45.000\r\nAs compared to students who started, say, at a level 2, a level, 3, or a level, 4.\r\n\r\n00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.000\r\nCan you all hear me? Okay.\r\n\r\n00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:48.000\r\nYes.\r\n\r\n00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:54.000\r\nAlright!\r\n\r\n00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.000\r\nOkay.\r\n\r\n00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:03.000\r\nSo again, the growth rates for newcomers can be very high, and they can grow very quickly. But we need to do.\r\n\r\n00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:06.000\r\nYou know, pay attention to the need.\r\n\r\n00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:12.000\r\nBut also a reminder about having labels. So labels learning.\r\n\r\n00:13:12.000 --> 00:13:19.000\r\nIf we lump them all together into one group. You know we're.\r\n\r\n00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:24.000\r\nIt's we're. We're simplifying the complexity of each of each child.\r\n\r\n00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:28.000\r\nSo it's really important to get to know the child or the student.\r\n\r\n00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:32.000\r\nTheir family experiences, schooling, history.\r\n\r\n00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:42.000\r\nAnd know them on an individual level rather than lumping them all together under the term newcomer.\r\n\r\n00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:47.000\r\nSo that leads us to creating a safe and thriving environment.\r\n\r\n00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.000\r\nThis is a.\r\n\r\n00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:52.000\r\nPlace. Attachment theory has been around for a while.\r\n\r\n00:13:52.000 --> 00:13:58.000\r\nBut this Ppp framework I'm very intrigued by it.\r\n\r\n00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:06.000\r\nIf you're not familiar with it. Brian, out of the University of Kansas, put together this framework, as it relates to.\r\n\r\n00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:08.000\r\nImmigrants and refugees.\r\n\r\n00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:13.000\r\nAnd what ha! What's their experiences like in school?\r\n\r\n00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:17.000\r\nAnd in the in the community.\r\n\r\n00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:22.000\r\nAnd the the 3 P's are the person, the psychological processes.\r\n\r\n00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:24.000\r\nAnd the place.\r\n\r\n00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:26.000\r\nSo.\r\n\r\n00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:29.000\r\nThis framework gives us kind of.\r\n\r\n00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:31.000\r\nGuidance or a background.\r\n\r\n00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:36.000\r\nTo think about all the different ways that folks make an attachment.\r\n\r\n00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:39.000\r\nTo where they are and to where they they're being.\r\n\r\n00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:42.000\r\nAnd I think about you know the.\r\n\r\n00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:45.000\r\nSchool being like a second home.\r\n\r\n00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:53.000\r\nOur school, feeling like a second home, or even individual classrooms within that school, or even the school library.\r\n\r\n00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:56.000\r\nBeing a place that feels like.\r\n\r\n00:14:56.000 --> 00:14:59.000\r\nThey they belong.\r\n\r\n00:14:59.000 --> 00:15:03.000\r\nAnd there's things that we can do to.\r\n\r\n00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:06.000\r\nTo welcome that to\r\n\r\n00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:10.000\r\nYou take advantage of that feeling, and there's also things that we can do to.\r\n\r\n00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:13.000\r\nTo push students away.\r\n\r\n00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:20.000\r\nSo let's look at the 1st scenario or the 1st component of the framework. The person.\r\n\r\n00:15:20.000 --> 00:15:22.000\r\nThe person.\r\n\r\n00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:27.000\r\nIs really reflecting the cultural and historical groups.\r\n\r\n00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:29.000\r\nAnd individual care.\r\n\r\n00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:32.000\r\nOr personal development.\r\n\r\n00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:35.000\r\nTake a look at this poster.\r\n\r\n00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:44.000\r\nThis is just one example. This is something that you can buy on Amazon, or what have you? That shows the greatest mathematicians of all time, and here we have.\r\n\r\n00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:49.000\r\nA list of French and Greek.\r\n\r\n00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:51.000\r\nBritish.\r\n\r\n00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:55.000\r\nMathematicians that really made an impact on the world.\r\n\r\n00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:59.000\r\nBut if you have students from\r\n\r\n00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:01.000\r\nSyria, for example.\r\n\r\n00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:03.000\r\nWhere\r\n\r\n00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:11.000\r\nThey, they may have a different set of historical figures that really made contributions to math.\r\n\r\n00:16:11.000 --> 00:16:13.000\r\nFor example.\r\n\r\n00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:19.000\r\nAnd I'm gonna butcher the names because I can't ask if it's if I'm saying it right. But I'll query.\r\n\r\n00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:25.000\r\nMaurice me was the father of algebra. We can thank this man for the classes we had to take in school.\r\n\r\n00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:28.000\r\nAnd.\r\n\r\n00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:33.000\r\nHe made very, very important contributions to the world of math.\r\n\r\n00:16:33.000 --> 00:16:39.000\r\nAl Biruni. We can thank him for trigonometry and spherical geometry.\r\n\r\n00:16:39.000 --> 00:16:44.000\r\nAl Karaji. Polynomials and factorization.\r\n\r\n00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:55.000\r\nAll this to say that you know the the images, the things that we put up, the the cultural groups that we recognize in schools.\r\n\r\n00:16:55.000 --> 00:17:05.000\r\nMatters. So if you have students that they may have a connection to to some people who are not.\r\n\r\n00:17:05.000 --> 00:17:07.000\r\nGenerally represented.\r\n\r\n00:17:07.000 --> 00:17:11.000\r\nIn the posters and and things we can find here.\r\n\r\n00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:15.000\r\nThat that stuff matters.\r\n\r\n00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:17.000\r\nSo some strategies for individual care.\r\n\r\n00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:22.000\r\nBefore the 1st day. If you can have a.\r\n\r\n00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:25.000\r\nAn enrollment center or a welcome center.\r\n\r\n00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:29.000\r\nTo collect information to give a school tour.\r\n\r\n00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:36.000\r\nThat helps prepare the students and the staff for their time for their the beginning of their journey.\r\n\r\n00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:42.000\r\nEspecially how to pronounce a student's name understanding their English proficiency level.\r\n\r\n00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:47.000\r\nSchool tours are very important.\r\n\r\n00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:50.000\r\nThe the kids are generally pretty nervous to start school.\r\n\r\n00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:57.000\r\nShow them the important places show them where their classroom is, where the office is.\r\n\r\n00:17:57.000 --> 00:18:01.000\r\nSome important people, some especially those that speak their language.\r\n\r\n00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:04.000\r\nIntroduce them to their teacher.\r\n\r\n00:18:04.000 --> 00:18:10.000\r\nThose are really good ways to start the year offright, or to start their time off in your school.\r\n\r\n00:18:10.000 --> 00:18:17.000\r\nAnd when they get there maybe have a peer mentor, one that speaks the language of the student, if possible.\r\n\r\n00:18:17.000 --> 00:18:22.000\r\nBut if not, find some, you know, find a willing.\r\n\r\n00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:27.000\r\nPeer Mentor, an old friend of mine, who's familiar with the work that I do.\r\n\r\n00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:31.000\r\nHer daughter found out that.\r\n\r\n00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:36.000\r\nShe was in 1st grade at the time, found out that a new student would be joining the class.\r\n\r\n00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:41.000\r\nAnd they were just beginning their journey in English. A child spoke Spanish.\r\n\r\n00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:46.000\r\nWhenever I went over to her house she was on Duolingo, learning some Spanish words.\r\n\r\n00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:50.000\r\nBecause he was gonna have a new classmate wanted to be able to help.\r\n\r\n00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:58.000\r\nI also recommend giving the gift of time. So give the student some time to adjust\r\n\r\n00:18:58.000 --> 00:19:00.000\r\nWe don't have to give them\r\n\r\n00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.000\r\nYou know, just because a test is scheduled doesn't mean they have to take it on the 1st day.\r\n\r\n00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:10.000\r\nAnd some students need time for breaks, you know.\r\n\r\n00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:29.000\r\nThat spending all day a full day in a classroom where English is the language of instruction. And you're just beginning. Your journey is is tiring. So give some time for breaks, especially if there is someone who's there. That's that speaks the language.\r\n\r\n00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:38.000\r\nAnd set small goals. Goal setting can happen with newcomers as far as language participation.\r\n\r\n00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:44.000\r\nAnd these things are all super important. Because.\r\n\r\n00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:47.000\r\nYou've you've got to give.\r\n\r\n00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:51.000\r\nGive attention to these things, otherwise the rest won't really matter.\r\n\r\n00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:53.000\r\nSo\r\n\r\n00:19:53.000 --> 00:19:58.000\r\nIn the chat. What are some things that you all do to welcome students.\r\n\r\n00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:06.000\r\nAnd to get to know them.\r\n\r\n00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:15.000\r\nThat's awesome, Luce. You're on call all day in case they need something that's taxing on you.\r\n\r\n00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:23.000\r\nMeet them a week before, and give them a tour.\r\n\r\n00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:27.000\r\nConnect the students with other school tours and a buddy student.\r\n\r\n00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:33.000\r\nIt's great picture books. I love that idea. Students.\r\n\r\n00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:37.000\r\nOf important places in the in the room.\r\n\r\n00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:41.000\r\nThey're in the school.\r\n\r\n00:20:41.000 --> 00:20:46.000\r\nFinding books with their cultural background and language more on that later. Yes.\r\n\r\n00:20:46.000 --> 00:20:50.000\r\nSomeone's also saying a snack social, or like a lunchtime, you know, like a particular gathering.\r\n\r\n00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:57.000\r\nYeah, it's a great idea. Home country projects.\r\n\r\n00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:03.000\r\nBy a board. Yeah, it's more on some of these later.\r\n\r\n00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:10.000\r\nAll about Mihoot. Oh, man, I haven't played a cahoot in a while.\r\n\r\n00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:14.000\r\nA school tour for parents. That's great.\r\n\r\n00:21:14.000 --> 00:21:22.000\r\nAlright. Thank you all.\r\n\r\n00:21:22.000 --> 00:21:32.000\r\nSo the next part of the framework is the psychological processes. These are intended to foster happiness, pride, and a love of the place.\r\n\r\n00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:37.000\r\nAs far as cognition. We're talking about building memories, schemas.\r\n\r\n00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:45.000\r\nAnd specifically around behavioring the feelings of displacement and homesickness, because those are very real.\r\n\r\n00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:48.000\r\nWe don't know.\r\n\r\n00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:54.000\r\nHow the students are feeling about their transition to life in school, in the Us. Until we've.\r\n\r\n00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:56.000\r\nWe've sat down and talked with them.\r\n\r\n00:21:56.000 --> 00:22:00.000\r\nSome of the students are thrilled and couldn't be happier to be here.\r\n\r\n00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:03.000\r\nSome students didn't want to leave.\r\n\r\n00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:06.000\r\nAnd you have a range of things.\r\n\r\n00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:08.000\r\nIn between, those 2.\r\n\r\n00:22:08.000 --> 00:22:17.000\r\nSo it's it's really important that we help the students process those because that leads to other things.\r\n\r\n00:22:17.000 --> 00:22:21.000\r\nHaving a counselor and a trauma.\r\n\r\n00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:34.000\r\nTrauma therapist trained trauma therapists to be available, and we didn't need them for everyone. But we, the ones that we did need them for was very, very valuable to help with their transition to school. Here.\r\n\r\n00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:38.000\r\nLet's take a quick moment to just read through this vignette.\r\n\r\n00:22:38.000 --> 00:22:44.000\r\nIt's about a Syrian student who fled Syria during the Civil War.\r\n\r\n00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:47.000\r\nAnd this is from a soon to be published book.\r\n\r\n00:22:47.000 --> 00:22:51.000\r\nBy Brian Mann. As I was talking about.\r\n\r\n00:22:51.000 --> 00:23:21.000\r\nJust take a minute and read through this.\r\n\r\n00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:27.000\r\nAlright. So this it's a controversial topic in schools. Right?\r\n\r\n00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:32.000\r\nNo students are, of course, allowed to pray. The school just can't mandate it.\r\n\r\n00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:39.000\r\nAny student from any religious background. can do\r\n\r\n00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:43.000\r\nYou know what they, if they want to do it on their own.\r\n\r\n00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:50.000\r\nThey're they're able to. Yusuf is a 15 year old, and in his religion he's required to pray 5 times a day.\r\n\r\n00:23:50.000 --> 00:23:54.000\r\nI've seen schools handle this in so many different ways.\r\n\r\n00:23:54.000 --> 00:24:02.000\r\nBut if we're talking about place making and finding and and making a connection with school.\r\n\r\n00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:05.000\r\nThis is one very hot button.\r\n\r\n00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:09.000\r\nIssue, or it could be\r\n\r\n00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:12.000\r\nThat could really make her break this connection.\r\n\r\n00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:15.000\r\nSo let's take a look through.\r\n\r\n00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:20.000\r\nThe 1st in the 1st scenario. He didn't. He did not know who he could trust.\r\n\r\n00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:27.000\r\nTo to ask the question. He knew he didn't. He hadn't thought about it before, because in his school.\r\n\r\n00:24:27.000 --> 00:24:30.000\r\nIn his former school. He didn't have to think about it.\r\n\r\n00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:35.000\r\nThey all prayed together, and it just wasn't something he had to think of before.\r\n\r\n00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:40.000\r\nBut he didn't know who to ask, so he should have a go to person or people.\r\n\r\n00:24:40.000 --> 00:24:44.000\r\nIn situations like these.\r\n\r\n00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:52.000\r\nSo in Syria he and his classmates prayed together in one space it suddenly occurred to him that he did not know where to pray.\r\n\r\n00:24:52.000 --> 00:24:58.000\r\nIs there some way we could capitalize on this, knowing what his former experience was.\r\n\r\n00:24:58.000 --> 00:25:02.000\r\nIs there something we could do?\r\n\r\n00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:04.000\r\nYou know. Perhaps.\r\n\r\n00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:07.000\r\nHe and any other Muslim student.\r\n\r\n00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:14.000\r\nCould help design an area in the school where they could go. Pray.\r\n\r\n00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:18.000\r\nHelp them know what's yes, help them know which direction to face.\r\n\r\n00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:23.000\r\nAnd there. Yes.\r\n\r\n00:25:23.000 --> 00:25:30.000\r\nAnd how does praying by a staircase make use of, feel.\r\n\r\n00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:37.000\r\nDoes it make him feel like he's part of the school, or is he being pushed to the side to take care of his business, while everybody else does their thing.\r\n\r\n00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:40.000\r\nAnd so.\r\n\r\n00:25:40.000 --> 00:25:43.000\r\nI love. Yeah. Humiliated.\r\n\r\n00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:47.000\r\nHe knew that kids were whispering. Everybody knows what a whisper is.\r\n\r\n00:25:47.000 --> 00:25:54.000\r\nYou know you may not understand the words they were saying, but everybody understands what a what a whisper is.\r\n\r\n00:25:54.000 --> 00:25:59.000\r\nSo things like this.\r\n\r\n00:25:59.000 --> 00:26:08.000\r\nMelan here when he was finished praying he was tardy to his class, and the teacher called him out in front of everybody.\r\n\r\n00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:11.000\r\nAnd so you know, policies do matter.\r\n\r\n00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:19.000\r\nAnd as we're thinking about this, you know what c