eryone is a genius. But if you judge a fish by its ability to climb a tree.\r\n\r\n00:39:11.000 --> 00:39:14.000\r\nIt will live its whole life, believing that it can't swim.\r\n\r\n00:39:14.000 --> 00:39:20.000\r\nAnd I'm you know, coming from a newcomer. I'm very aware of the.\r\n\r\n00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:30.000\r\nThe assessment culture that we have in the United States, and so many decisions are based on the results of an assessment.\r\n\r\n00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:33.000\r\nBut just know that, you know.\r\n\r\n00:39:33.000 --> 00:39:36.000\r\nIf we're wanting\r\n\r\n00:39:36.000 --> 00:39:38.000\r\nThe language of the test.\r\n\r\n00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:42.000\r\nReally makes a difference, and the purpose of the test really matters.\r\n\r\n00:39:42.000 --> 00:39:48.000\r\nIf we're gauging English proficiency or gauging\r\n\r\n00:39:48.000 --> 00:39:52.000\r\nHow much English they've acquired. We're going to. That's what we're going to assess.\r\n\r\n00:39:52.000 --> 00:40:00.000\r\nBut for newcomers who are just beginning their journey in English, you know, taking a reading or a math test in English right away.\r\n\r\n00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:04.000\r\nReally isn't. Gonna tell you much about.\r\n\r\n00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:10.000\r\nTheir knowledge of language, arts, or math. It's going to tell you that they really don't understand the test questions yet.\r\n\r\n00:40:10.000 --> 00:40:12.000\r\nBut they will.\r\n\r\n00:40:12.000 --> 00:40:24.000\r\nEventually? As far as what does those? What are those 1st stages for? For Weeda?\r\n\r\n00:40:24.000 --> 00:40:30.000\r\nThose are generally, or those are descriptions of the end of a level.\r\n\r\n00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:35.000\r\nAnd so for newcomers. What does it look like before that.\r\n\r\n00:40:35.000 --> 00:40:38.000\r\nWell would they come, or the students come in.\r\n\r\n00:40:38.000 --> 00:40:44.000\r\nAnd they're they're in. What's what the research community calls the silent period.\r\n\r\n00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:46.000\r\nThere are\r\n\r\n00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:50.000\r\nYou know different opinions around the use of that term, but.\r\n\r\n00:40:50.000 --> 00:40:56.000\r\nThere's a reluctant use of in our case. English. If anybody's been in.\r\n\r\n00:40:56.000 --> 00:41:05.000\r\nWorld language course before, and your 1st experience, you know, having to speak to your teacher, maybe for a test.\r\n\r\n00:41:05.000 --> 00:41:09.000\r\nThere's some anxiety. There's some effective variables. You may not have wanted.\r\n\r\n00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:12.000\r\nTalk in front of your friends.\r\n\r\n00:41:12.000 --> 00:41:16.000\r\nBecause the the confidence just wasn't there.\r\n\r\n00:41:16.000 --> 00:41:21.000\r\nThe good news about the silent period is it's it can be relatively quick.\r\n\r\n00:41:21.000 --> 00:41:26.000\r\nDepending on personality, and how we work with the students in that level.\r\n\r\n00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:30.000\r\nWe don't want to to force it.\r\n\r\n00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:32.000\r\nBut we wanna build confidence.\r\n\r\n00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:36.000\r\nWe want to give them words. We want to use repetition.\r\n\r\n00:41:36.000 --> 00:41:46.000\r\nImmediately after the silent period. It's generally chunks of language. This is language that we give to them things that they hear all all day.\r\n\r\n00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:48.000\r\nThey hear frequently.\r\n\r\n00:41:48.000 --> 00:42:07.000\r\nAnd you know common words that daily interactions in class and experiences in class 9 times out of 10. The 1st words are the students are using are meant to convey, like a a need, a survival situation like needing to go to the bathroom or some water or.\r\n\r\n00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:11.000\r\nThat there's a challenge, but.\r\n\r\n00:42:11.000 --> 00:42:17.000\r\nThose are generally the first, st and you never know when a student's ready to come out of the silent period, I had a.\r\n\r\n00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:25.000\r\n1st grader, who seemed like she was in the silent period for 4 or 5 months, and we were wondering what's going on. Nobody.\r\n\r\n00:42:25.000 --> 00:42:27.000\r\nTakes this long to.\r\n\r\n00:42:27.000 --> 00:42:36.000\r\nTo speak before, and we were walking out to the bus one day, and she just darts back inside, and I'm like, Where are you going? She turned around and said, I gotta go to the bathroom.\r\n\r\n00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:39.000\r\nYeah. Full sentence. Just like that.\r\n\r\n00:42:39.000 --> 00:42:42.000\r\nSo you you never know\r\n\r\n00:42:42.000 --> 00:42:48.000\r\nBut with the with the students who are coming out of this, the affective side of that.\r\n\r\n00:42:48.000 --> 00:42:52.000\r\nIs very real, the the confidence, the.\r\n\r\n00:42:52.000 --> 00:42:57.000\r\nThe self-esteem of embarrassment.\r\n\r\n00:42:57.000 --> 00:43:04.000\r\nThings like that really matter. So it's up to us to build them up.\r\n\r\n00:43:04.000 --> 00:43:15.000\r\nAnd and and help them feel like they're that they're in a safe space to, to try to use the the language that they have.\r\n\r\n00:43:15.000 --> 00:43:33.000\r\nAnd here's another slide about the development. Just as recently as 20. There was a study showing that newcomers advanced more quickly over time than non newcomer English learners, despite having lower initial English proficiency levels.\r\n\r\n00:43:33.000 --> 00:43:47.000\r\nThe 4 years that was impacted the most was with students who are entering schools at the higher grades showed slower growth over the 1st 4 years than by students in the younger grade.\r\n\r\n00:43:47.000 --> 00:43:50.000\r\nSo how can we leverage this rapid growth.\r\n\r\n00:43:50.000 --> 00:43:59.000\r\nWe should build on their language skills to to support this growth. Be ready to jump on those teachable moments.\r\n\r\n00:43:59.000 --> 00:44:05.000\r\nPraise them or give them praise whenever they've stretching themselves.\r\n\r\n00:44:05.000 --> 00:44:14.000\r\nAnd there are promising programs to support this. This development bilingual programs.\r\n\r\n00:44:14.000 --> 00:44:22.000\r\nIdeas and strategies to help them interact with the with the grade level.\r\n\r\n00:44:22.000 --> 00:44:25.000\r\nCourse placement policies.\r\n\r\n00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:36.000\r\nYou know, I've seen schools where every Ela or English Language arts class was substituted with an Esl class. So a student would.\r\n\r\n00:44:36.000 --> 00:44:41.000\r\nTheoretically graduate without ever having taken language, arts.\r\n\r\n00:44:41.000 --> 00:44:44.000\r\nAnd then, of course.\r\n\r\n00:44:44.000 --> 00:44:53.000\r\nIf we're gonna the more students have opportunities to engage with the language to use the language among their peers, the better.\r\n\r\n00:44:53.000 --> 00:44:58.000\r\nAs much as we pride ourselves in the work that we do. The kids are going to.\r\n\r\n00:44:58.000 --> 00:45:01.000\r\nLearn more language with each other.\r\n\r\n00:45:01.000 --> 00:45:06.000\r\nSo we need to give them opportunities to do that.\r\n\r\n00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:13.000\r\nMoving out of the silent period again, attention to that effective side.\r\n\r\n00:45:13.000 --> 00:45:21.000\r\nLots of repetition. So we should be working with the same text from Monday to Friday. Gradually.\r\n\r\n00:45:21.000 --> 00:45:27.000\r\nVery teacher led on Monday, and gradually moving it to more student, led on Friday.\r\n\r\n00:45:27.000 --> 00:45:29.000\r\nThe point is, we get a lot of repetition.\r\n\r\n00:45:29.000 --> 00:45:32.000\r\nMemorize chunks of words.\r\n\r\n00:45:32.000 --> 00:45:36.000\r\nAnd then this idea of total physical response.\r\n\r\n00:45:36.000 --> 00:45:40.000\r\nTotal physical response has been around, I think, since the 60 s.\r\n\r\n00:45:40.000 --> 00:45:42.000\r\nThat's a very.\r\n\r\n00:45:42.000 --> 00:45:47.000\r\nPopular technique in the world, languages in the.\r\n\r\n00:45:47.000 --> 00:45:54.000\r\nLearning a language like many of us, did Spanish or French in in school.\r\n\r\n00:45:54.000 --> 00:46:01.000\r\nWhere there's a an oral prompt from the teacher, followed by motion of some kind.\r\n\r\n00:46:01.000 --> 00:46:12.000\r\nWith the students. So what are some strategies that you do already? As you're working with students in the silent period.\r\n\r\n00:46:12.000 --> 00:46:28.000\r\nSo, Ryan, while we wait for some of those responses to come through. There was a comment. And I know that translating can be a a concept for a full day, but I think it was new to some people. So they somebody was asking if you could just elaborate.\r\n\r\n00:46:28.000 --> 00:46:33.000\r\nA little bit more. That's possible, because I know it's a complex.\r\n\r\n00:46:33.000 --> 00:46:37.000\r\nConcept, to just to explain that a little bit more.\r\n\r\n00:46:37.000 --> 00:46:41.000\r\nYeah. And we are developing a translation workshop, too. So that's another.\r\n\r\n00:46:41.000 --> 00:46:42.000\r\nYeah.\r\n\r\n00:46:42.000 --> 00:46:51.000\r\nBut in essence translating is giving the students the opportunity to use their full linguistic repertoire.\r\n\r\n00:46:51.000 --> 00:46:55.000\r\nAs they interact with the content and interact with each other.\r\n\r\n00:46:55.000 --> 00:47:00.000\r\nWhat I immediately think of is, whenever I would walk.\r\n\r\n00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:10.000\r\nA front office and had a couple of bilingual folks working up there, and if you heard them talking it's just going back and forth between English and Spanish.\r\n\r\n00:47:10.000 --> 00:47:13.000\r\nEffortlessly,\r\n\r\n00:47:13.000 --> 00:47:22.000\r\nIs, and it was just common, and it it used to be looked down upon right like they were. This type of code switching was.\r\n\r\n00:47:22.000 --> 00:47:26.000\r\nWas frowned upon and.\r\n\r\n00:47:26.000 --> 00:47:28.000\r\nFor many that went to school, you know.\r\n\r\n00:47:28.000 --> 00:47:35.000\r\n1020 years ago. There they may have stumbled upon an English only policy or so.\r\n\r\n00:47:35.000 --> 00:47:37.000\r\nBut we found that.\r\n\r\n00:47:37.000 --> 00:47:43.000\r\nThat's normal. And children, adults.\r\n\r\n00:47:43.000 --> 00:47:48.000\r\nDo that. And it's it's beneficial. Not only.\r\n\r\n00:47:48.000 --> 00:47:52.000\r\nTo their their place and their their sense of belonging, but also their.\r\n\r\n00:47:52.000 --> 00:47:54.000\r\nAcademic growth and language growth.\r\n\r\n00:47:54.000 --> 00:48:04.000\r\nYeah. And I think, like someone mentioned on the chat, too, they learn faster because they're not kind of inhibited to only try to say words in a certain way. They're allowed to use.\r\n\r\n00:48:04.000 --> 00:48:07.000\r\nLike you said. They're full repertoire.\r\n\r\n00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:10.000\r\nAnd I saw some people talking about songs on here.\r\n\r\n00:48:10.000 --> 00:48:14.000\r\nMusic chance. I think.\r\n\r\n00:48:14.000 --> 00:48:21.000\r\nSomewhere around 4, th 5th grade. We generally tend to move from music to chance.\r\n\r\n00:48:21.000 --> 00:48:24.000\r\nI don't know what it is about kids not wanting to sing along with.\r\n\r\n00:48:24.000 --> 00:48:27.000\r\nTheir peers at that age, but.\r\n\r\n00:48:27.000 --> 00:48:33.000\r\nWhen this, when you start getting some reluctance to sing.\r\n\r\n00:48:33.000 --> 00:48:36.000\r\nChance would be a great way.\r\n\r\n00:48:36.000 --> 00:48:39.000\r\nGosh! I've had.\r\n\r\n00:48:39.000 --> 00:48:42.000\r\nIf anybody's listened to the doors. I don't know if.\r\n\r\n00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:46.000\r\nI'm gonna date any of us here. But.\r\n\r\n00:48:46.000 --> 00:48:50.000\r\nThere's a song out there. That's Hello! I love you! Won't you tell me your name?\r\n\r\n00:48:50.000 --> 00:48:55.000\r\nBy. Just change the words to Hello! How are you? What is your name?\r\n\r\n00:48:55.000 --> 00:49:04.000\r\nAnd that was just play that along with the kids. And right away they learned they learned how to introduce themselves to each other.\r\n\r\n00:49:04.000 --> 00:49:07.000\r\nBut songs.\r\n\r\n00:49:07.000 --> 00:49:10.000\r\nVery powerful.\r\n\r\n00:49:10.000 --> 00:49:17.000\r\nIn fact, sometimes the songs they sang him so much. Then you're singing them all weekend, too. You can't get them out of your head.\r\n\r\n00:49:17.000 --> 00:49:19.000\r\nWhich is kind of the point.\r\n\r\n00:49:19.000 --> 00:49:22.000\r\nGood stuff.\r\n\r\n00:49:22.000 --> 00:49:26.000\r\nThere are some variations to total physical response.\r\n\r\n00:49:26.000 --> 00:49:32.000\r\nTpr circles. So after practicing the movements, you can all get in the big circle.\r\n\r\n00:49:32.000 --> 00:49:36.000\r\nLast one to do the movements has to sit down.\r\n\r\n00:49:36.000 --> 00:49:38.000\r\nLast one standing wins.\r\n\r\n00:49:38.000 --> 00:49:43.000\r\nAlthough the person who sat down 1st should probably get more practice. But.\r\n\r\n00:49:43.000 --> 00:49:50.000\r\nThere's a Simon says, is is Tpr, that is, someone giving an oral prompt, and the students responding.\r\n\r\n00:49:50.000 --> 00:49:57.000\r\nThrough movement. Tpr sounds, there's slap vocabulary. I've seen\r\n\r\n00:49:57.000 --> 00:50:03.000\r\nIt's a picture laying down flashcards on the ground or on the table.\r\n\r\n00:50:03.000 --> 00:50:06.000\r\nSomebody says the word, and the 1st one to slap it.\r\n\r\n00:50:06.000 --> 00:50:10.000\r\nGets the card. Whoever has the most cards at the end wins.\r\n\r\n00:50:10.000 --> 00:50:14.000\r\nNow after trial and error.\r\n\r\n00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:16.000\r\nFlyers.\r\n\r\n00:50:16.000 --> 00:50:18.000\r\nWork a lot better than the hands.\r\n\r\n00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:21.000\r\nBecause then you won't. The kids won't be hitting each other.\r\n\r\n00:50:21.000 --> 00:50:29.000\r\nAnd even Bingo arguably, is a type of total physical response. You have a oral prompt.\r\n\r\n00:50:29.000 --> 00:50:31.000\r\nStudents have to make a connection in their brain.\r\n\r\n00:50:31.000 --> 00:50:35.000\r\nThey? They have a movement afterwards.\r\n\r\n00:50:35.000 --> 00:50:40.000\r\nRyan can. Do you mind if I quickly answer a question.\r\n\r\n00:50:40.000 --> 00:50:41.000\r\nYeah.\r\n\r\n00:50:41.000 --> 00:50:46.000\r\nThere's a question, how do we know when a newcomer is relying on translating to a detrimental point.\r\n\r\n00:50:46.000 --> 00:50:54.000\r\nOnly seeking out bilingual staff members and to interact with monolingual staffs and students.\r\n\r\n00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:56.000\r\nBefore I give my answer. Do you have one.\r\n\r\n00:50:56.000 --> 00:51:03.000\r\nWell, I would say, translating and translating are 2 different things.\r\n\r\n00:51:03.000 --> 00:51:09.000\r\nSo translating, everything is.\r\n\r\n00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:14.000\r\nReally reluctance to engage in.\r\n\r\n00:51:14.000 --> 00:51:21.000\r\nAnd the target language. Right? It's a reluctant but translating.\r\n\r\n00:51:21.000 --> 00:51:25.000\r\nIs using both.\r\n\r\n00:51:25.000 --> 00:51:29.000\r\nSo this there is some confusion around.\r\n\r\n00:51:29.000 --> 00:51:34.000\r\nTranslation versus translating.\r\n\r\n00:51:34.000 --> 00:51:38.000\r\nSo. My my response is, of course, what Ryan mentioned that.\r\n\r\n00:51:38.000 --> 00:51:45.000\r\nTranslating doesn't mean just translating Spanish is now an acceptable.\r\n\r\n00:51:45.000 --> 00:51:53.000\r\nWay for students to make meaning of content and engage in making meaning in conversations with other students.\r\n\r\n00:51:53.000 --> 00:52:12.000\r\nThe idea is that they're seeing it as a scaffold or support, so that they know when and how it's necessary. And it's not something that they are going to do consistently all the time. We do want them to be exposed to the use of English, and or how they would write in the in the content area.\r\n\r\n00:52:12.000 --> 00:52:15.000\r\nThe practice of translanging is allowing them.\r\n\r\n00:52:15.000 --> 00:52:18.000\r\nTo use it when they need.\r\n\r\n00:52:18.000 --> 00:52:30.000\r\nTo engage in understanding or clarifications, so that they know that it isn't something in their toolbox, and not necessarily. That's all they're goin