  {
    "id": "14a2a84f-d765-4fcf-93a2-3cad0c857c39",
    "title": "Grade 1",
    "content": "Title: Grade 1\n\nContent: Grade 1  Level 1  Level 2  Level 3  Level 4  Level 5  Level 6 (exited)  Interpretive  (Listening/Reading)  D- organized around topics with repetition, rhyming, and common language patterns; frequently used single noun groups (dinosaurs)  S- words, pictures, phrases, and chunks (turtle eggs)  W- repeated words and phrases in familiar contexts and topics (classroom helpers)  Interpretive  (Listening/Reading)  D- organized around topics with short sentences; repetitive chunks; frequently used multi word noun groups (big tall dinosaurs)  S- chunks of language (turtles swimming)  W- frequently used words and phrases in familiar topics (Would you like to share?)  Interpretive  (Listening/Reading)  D- organized around topics through multiple related simple sentences; demonstratives; multi word noun groups with connectors (a big and hungry dinosaur)  S- simple sentences (Turtles swim in the ocean.)  W- situation specific words and phrases (Plant the seeds in this pot.)  Interpretive  (Listening/Reading)  D-organized to meet a purpose in a series of extended sentences; repetition, pronoun referencing; expanded noun groups with classifiers (the biggest meat eating dinosaurs)  S-related simple sentences (Turtles are reptiles. They like warm water.)  W- an increasing number of words and phrases (Tell me about your picture on the left.)  Interpretive  (Listening/Reading)  D-organized to inform, narrate, or entertain; synonyms, antonyms; expanded noun groups and prepositional phrases (the meat eating dinosaurs in the jungle)  S- multiple related simple sentences  W- a growing number of words and phrases in a variety of contexts (How many red triangles are there?)  Interpretive (Listening/Reading)  Grade Appropriate  Expressive  (Speaking/Writing)  D- organized in single words, phrases, or chunks; patterned with repetitive phrases and sentences; limited elaboration (single words)  S- words, pictures, phrases, chunks (flowers and trees)  W- emerging use of words and phrases (over there, line up in a row)  Expressive  (Speaking/Writing)  D-organized in phrases or short sentences to represent ideas to describe, narrate, or share opinion; repetition; simple elaborations (familiar single nouns)  S-sentence fragments (grow taller)  W-few frequently used words and phrases (lunch time, clean up my desk)  Expressive  (Speaking/Writing)  D- organized in short sentences linked together; pronouns; adding adjectives to nouns  S-sentence fragments and emerging use of simple sentences (had no water so died)  W-some frequently used words and phrases  (have a nice day, I’m finished)  Expressive  (Speaking/Writing)  D-organized in sentences that convey purpose with organizational pattern (first, next); pronouns (my, her); adding adjectives to nouns  S-simple sentences (Plants need water.)  W- a small repertoire of words and phrases with developing precision (best friend, the red ball)  Expressive  (Speaking/Writing)  D- organized in short texts that convey intended purpose with organizational patterns; emerging use of articles to refer to the same word. Substitution/omission (that one, so did I); adding articles and demonstratives to nouns  S- sentences with emerging use of clauses (Plants need water but… They need sun. Those ones died.)  W- a growing repertoire of words and phrases with growing precision (preschool friends, math time)  Expressive (Speaking/Writing)  Grade Appropriate  D-Discourse Level/linguistic complexity  S – Sentence Dimension/Language forms and conventions  W – Word Phrase/Vocabulary  WIDA 2020  Social &  Instructiona l  WIDA 2020  (<https://wida.wisc.edu/sites/default/files/resource/WIDA-ELD-Standards-Framework-2020.pdf#page=70>)Language Arts  WIDA 2020  (<https://wida.wisc.edu/sites/default/files/resource/WIDA-ELD-Standards-Framework-2020.pdf#page=72>)Mathematics  WIDA 2020  (<https://wida.wisc.edu/sites/default/files/resource/WIDA-ELD-Standards-Framework-2020.pdf#page=73>)Science  WIDA 2020 