    "content": "Title: Teachers:\n\nContent: Teachers:  Kindergarten  Level 1  Level 2  Level 3  Level 4  Level 5  Level 6 (exited)  Interpretive  (Listening/Reading)  D- organize around topics with words, pictures, phrases, chunks, repetition, and single nouns (ball)  S- words, pictures, and phrases (Anna’s chair)  W- a few words and phrases in familiar contexts and topics (hello, desk)  Interpretive (Listening/Reading)  D- organize around topics with repetition, rhyming, and common language patterns; frequently used single noun groups (my toys)  S-words, pictures, chunks of language (ran to her room)  W- repeated phrases in familiar context (sound it out)  Interpretive (Listening/Reading)  D- organize around topics with repetition, rhyming, and other language patterns with short sentences; frequently used noun groups (my favorite book)  S- chunks of language (sat in her chair)  W-frequently used words and phrases in familiar context (time to clean up)  Interpretive (Listening/Reading)  D- inform, narrate, entertain through multiple related sentences; use of demonstratives; multi-word noun groups (a shiny truck and red ball)  S- simple sentences (She picked it up.)  W- situation specific words and phrases (What sounds do we hear?)  Interpretive (Listening/Reading)  D- to meet a purpose in a series of extended sentences; pronoun referencing; expanded noun groups with classification (the red fire truck)  S- related simple sentences (She picked it up. She carried it to her room.)  W- increasing number of words and phrases (We need four different colors to make a pattern.)  Interpretive (Listening/Reading)  Grade Appropriate  Expressive  (Speaking/Writing)  D- organized in single words, phrases, or chunks; single words related to topic (water, leaf); limited elaboration (single words)  S- words, pictures, and phrases (cats and dogs)  W- frequently reoccurring words and phrases (Good morning, let’s play)  Expressive  (Speaking/Writing)  D- organized phrases or short sentences to describe, narrate or share opinion; repetition; simple elaboration (familiar single nouns)  S- words, pictures, phrases, and chunks (cats meow and dogs bark)  W- emerging use of words and phrases with attempted precision (Today is Friday, September 15.)  Expressive  (Speaking/Writing)  D- organized in short sentences linked together (and, then); repetition (this leaf is red, this lead is yellow); newly learned single nouns  S- sentence fragments (cats and dogs)  W-few frequently used words and phrases with emerging precision (lunch time, morning meeting)  Expressive  (Speaking/Writing)  D-organized in short sentences that convey an intended purpose; demonstratives; adding an adjective to noun  S-sentence fragments and emerging use of simple sentences (Dogs sleep.)  W- some frequently used words and phrases with some precision (my pattern is red, blue, red)  Expressive  (Speaking/Writing)  D- sentences linked together to inform, narrate, entertain (The parrot eats nuts and seeds.); pronouns; newly learned adjectives and nouns  S- simple sentences (Cats like to climb. Dogs like to run.)  W-a small repertoire of words and phrases with developing precision (beautiful butterfly)  Expressive (Speaking/Writing)  Grade Appropriate  D-Discourse Level/linguistic complexity  S – Sentence Dimension/Language forms and conventions  W – Word Phrase/Vocabulary  WIDA 2020  Social &  Instructional  WIDA 2020  (<https://wida.wisc.edu/sites/default/files/resource/WIDA-ELD-Standards-Framework-2020.pdf#page=50>)Language Arts  WIDA 2020  (<https://wida.wisc.edu/sites/default/files/resource/WIDA-ELD-Standards-Framework-2020.pdf#page=52>)Mathematics  WIDA 2020  (<https://wida.wisc.edu/sites/default/files/resource/WIDA-ELD-Standards-Framework-2020.pdf#page=53>)Science  WIDA 2020  (<https://wida.wisc.edu/sites/default/files/resource/WIDA-ELD-Standards-Framework-2020.pdf#page=55>)Social Studies  € ELD-SI.K-3  .Narrate  € ELD-SI.K-3  .Inform  € ELD-SI.K-3  .Explain  € ELD-SI.K-3  .Arg