    "id": "b55cf152-a16b-4c50-9ce7-1ab779ae904d",
    "title": "Rapid Reference: WIDA's ELD Standards Framework, 2020 Edition",
    "content": "Title: Rapid Reference: WIDA's ELD Standards Framework, 2020 Edition\n\nContent: Rapid Reference: WIDA's ELD Standards Framework, 2020 Edition  Content & Brief Description  Graphic(s)  Big Ideas  The 2020 Edition of the WIDA ELD Standards Framework is anchored by four Big Ideas that are interwoven throughout the document. Like the Can Do Philosophy, they support the design of standards-based educational experiences that are student-centered, culturally and linguistically sustaining, and responsive to multilingual learners’ strengths and needs. [pages 17-20]  Components of the Framework: Overview  These four components are like building blocks of language development and range from broad too narrow in scope. They work together to take a comprehensive picture of language development. [pages 23-35]  ELD Standards Statements  The five standards statements, introduced in Section 1, Table 1-1, represent the language of schooling, and provide the broadest conceptual framing of content and language integration…ELD Standard 1 applies across a range of educational settings, and works both independently from and in conjunction with ELD Standards 2-5. Students communicate to learn but also to convey personal needs and wants, to affirm their own identities, and to form and maintain relationships. [pages 24-25]  Key Language Uses  WIDA has identified four Key Language Uses—Narrate, Inform, Explain, and Argue— that can be used to prioritize and organize the integration of content and language… They emphasize language use for particular purposes, with particular audiences, and in particular sociocultural contexts…They help educators make choices to prioritize and coordinate content and language integration…Key Language Uses share some common aspects across disciplines, and yet each discipline also has unique ways of applying each. [pages 26-27 and 217-233]  Language Expectations  The four individual language domains (listening, speaking, reading, and writing) are consolidated into two more inclusive modes of communication: interpretive and expressive.  Language Expectations are built around a set of Language Functions:  Language Functions & Features  Language Functions are common patterns of language use that showcase particular ways students might use language to meet the purposes of schooling.  Language Features  In order to carry out particular Language Functions, language users rely on various language resources, including Language Features (e.g., types of sentences, clauses, phrases, and words).  Language Expectations offer goals for how all students might use language to meet academic content standards.  [pages 28-30 and throughout the grade cluster materials]  Proficiency Level Descriptors  Proficiency Level Descriptors describe how multilingual learners might develop language across levels of English language proficiency as they move toward meeting Language Expectations… At any given point along their continua of language development, multilingual learners may demonstrate a range of abilities within and across each proficiency level.  PLDs maintain consistency with the K-12 Performance Definitions of the 2012 edition of the WIDA ELD Standards. As such, the PLDs continue to be written according to the  three dimensions of language use:  discourse, sentence, and word/phrase.  [pages 31-34 and throughout the grade cluster materials]  Other Helpful Resources within the 2020 Edition  •  Setting Unit Level Goals- Steps and Guiding Questions [page 237]*  •  Honing a Language Focus [page 246]  •  Lesson Planning Considerations [pages 249-250]*  *from An Overview of Collaborative Planning [pages 234-250]  •  Comparison on Editions for the WIDA Language Standards [pages 352-353]  •  Annotated Language Samples [throughout the grade cluster materials]  •  Glossary [pages 251-259] ",
