    "id": "1acd5b91-d6c4-49d8-abda-c2831ab4b137",
    "title": "Rapid Reference Guide and Notetaker:",
    "content": "Rapid Reference Guide and Notetaker:  WIDA's ELD Standards Framework, 2020 Edition  Content & Brief Description  Graphic(s)  Big Ideas  The 2020 Edition of the WIDA ELD Standards Framework is anchored by four Big Ideas that are interwoven throughout the document. Like the Can Do Philosophy, they support the design of standards-based educational experiences that are student-centered, culturally and linguistically sustaining, and responsive to multilingual learners’ strengths and needs. [pages 17-20]  Your Notes:  Components of the Framework: Overview  These four components are like building blocks of language development and range from broad too narrow in scope. They work together to take a comprehensive picture of language development. [pages 23-35]  Your Notes:  ELD Standards Statements  The five standards statements, introduced in Section 1, Table 1-1, represent the language of schooling, and provide the broadest conceptual framing of content and language integration…ELD Standard 1 applies across a range of educational settings, and works both independently from and in conjunction with ELD Standards 2-5. Students communicate to learn but also to convey personal needs and wants, to affirm their own identities, and to form and maintain relationships. [pages 24-25]  Your Notes:  Key Language Uses  WIDA has identified four Key Language Uses—Narrate, Inform, Explain, and Argue— that can be used to prioritize and organize the integration of content and language… They emphasize language use for particular purposes, with particular audiences, and in particular sociocultural contexts…They help educators make choices to prioritize and coordinate content and language integration…Key Language Uses share some common aspects across disciplines, and yet each discipline also has unique ways of applying each. [pages 26-27 and 217-233]  Your Notes:  Language Expectations  The four individual language domains (listening, speaking, reading, and writing) are consolidated into two more inclusive modes of communication: interpretive and expressive.  Language Expectations are built around a set of Language Functions:  Language Functions  Language Functions are common patterns of language use that showcase particular ways students might use language to meet the purposes of schooling.  Language Features  In order to carry out particular Language Functions, language users rely on various language resources, including Language Features (e.g., types of sentences, clauses, phrases, and words).  Language Expectations offer goals for how all students might use language to meet academic content standards.  [pages 28-30 and throughout the grade cluster materials]  Your Notes:  p.33 p.246  Proficiency Level Descriptors  Proficiency Level Descriptors describe how multilingual learners might develop language across levels of English language proficiency as they move toward meeting Language Expectations… At any given point along their continua of language development, multilingual learners may demonstrate a range of abilities within and across each proficiency level.  PLDs maintain consistency with the K-12 Performance Definitions of the 2012 edition of the WIDA ELD Standards. As such, the PLDs continue to be written according to the  three dimensions of language use:  discourse, sentence, and word/phrase.  [pages 31-34 and throughout the grade cluster materials]  Your Notes:  p.33  Other Helpful Resources within the 2020 Edition • Setting Unit Level Goals- Steps and Guiding Questions [page 237]*  •  Honing a Language Focus [page 246]  •  Lesson Planning Considerations [pages 249-250]*  *from An Overview of Collaborative Planning [pages 234-250]  •  Comparison on Editions for the WIDA Language Standards [pages 352-353]  •  Annotated Language Samples [throughout the grade cluster materials]  •  Glossary [pages 251-259]  And resources beyond the 2020 Edition  Finding the Language in Content Area Instruction: Guiding Questions  Listening  Speaking  Reading  Writing  Additional  Considerations  •  What listening tasks will there be?  •  What kinds of listening do they involve: One way or two way? Interpersonal or informational?  •  What spoken language activities will occur?  •  Are there sufficient opportunities for students to use language for learning, and to use literate spoken language?  •  What texts will students be reading?  •  What are the possible cultural, conceptual and linguistic difficulties that learners might encounter?  •  How will you develop learners’ schema prior to reading the text?  •  What other “bridges” to the text will be provided (e.g., during-, or after- reading activities)?  •  What writing tasks are there?  •  What is the key genre you will focus on or revisit?  •  What is the purpose of the genre, its overall organization, the types of connectives that are important to the genre, and the grammatical structures that are likely to occur?  •  What specific vocabulary do children need to understand and use in this unit of work?  •  What key aspects of grammar will naturally occur (e.g., past tense, generalizations, interpersonal language for collaborative work, asking questions, language of comparison)?  Primary Student Scholar Sheet: Connecting Ideas and Using Academic Language  Connectors are one of the sample language features that support development of all dimensions of language: discourse (organization), sentence (grammatical) complexity, and Word/Phrase (precision). Educators should select connectors for students to practice from class texts that support the language function and key language use of the lesson. Educators and students can co-create similar charts using class texts. The chart below provides some examples.  Type of Connector  Example Connectors/Expressions and Related Sentence Structures and Language Frames  • Compare and Contrast  •  ___ and ___ are the same/different.  •  Both and are/have/can . Additional expressions: alike, also, and, as well as, but, like, similar  •  Sequence  •  Order  •  Arrange  •  First, . / Next, . / Then, / After that, _____ / Finally, ___.  •  ___came before . / ____ happened after __. Additional expressions: afterward, as soon as, at the same time, awhile, before, begin, during, last, later  •  Classify  •  Sort  •  Categorize  •  I organized these objects by ___.  •  ___ belongs to category ___. Additional expressions: according to, arrange, attribute(s), group  • [Identify] Cause and Effect  •  If , then .  •  ___ is a cause/effect/result of ____. Additional expressions: based on, because, caused by, effect/affect, since, so, therefore, this is why, ___ will happen if ___  •  Predict  •  Make Inferences  •  Draw Conclusions  • I infer . I predict that ___ will happen. / My prediction is ___. Additional expressions: guess, think  •  Justify  •  Persuade  •  Explain Reasoning  •  In my opinion___.  •  I chose this because ___. Additional expressions: reason(s) why  •  Evaluate  •  Critique  •  I agree/disagree because _____.  •  ____ is better than ___ because ___. Additional expressions: agree/disagree, bad, best, better  Academic Language Function Toolkit  Main Sources: VDOE EL Teacher Toolkit; (Sweetwater Union High School District from Kinsella, 2010)  Upper Elementary and Secondary Student Scholar Sheet: Connecting Ideas and Using Academic Language  Connectors are one of the sample language features that support development of all dimensions of language: discourse (organization), sentence (grammatical complexity_, and Word/Phrase (precision). Educators should select connectors for students to practice from class texts that support the language function and key language use of the lesson. Educators and students can co-create similar charts using class texts. The chart below provides some examples.  Type of Connector  Example Connectors and Related Expressions  Related Sentence Structures and Frames  • Compare and Contrast  alike, also, although, alternatively, although, and, as well as, but, connects to, contrary to, conversely, despite, differ/difference/different, difference/similarity between, equally, even though, however, in common, instead, like, likewise, neither/nor, nevertheless, on the other hand, opposed to, rather, same, similarly to, unlike, versus, while, whereas  •  One similarity/difference between and  •  Both and are/have/can .  •  and differ in a variety of ways.  •  Sequence  •  Order  •  Arrange  afterward, as soon as, at last, at that time, at the same time as, at which point, awhile, before, begin, concurrently, daily, during, eventually, finally, first, following that, immediately, in anticipation, initially, last, later, meanwhile, next, ongoing, over time, preceding, precipitate, previously, prior to, simultaneously, sometime later, sometimes, subsequently, then, the onset of, to begin, to start, ultimately, when, while  •  In the time between and .  •  First, . / Next, . /Then, / After that,  •  came before . / happened just after  •  Classify  •  Sort  •  Categorize  according to, arrange, attribute(s), behavior(s), belongs to/in, category, characteristic(s), correlate(s) to, features(s), fits into, group, organize/organized by, properties, quality (qualities), trait(s)  •  The classification is based on _____.  •  I sorted these objects by .  •  belongs to category .  • [Identify] Cause and Effect  accordingly, as a result, based on, because, caused by, consequently, correlates to, effect/affect, factors contributed to the outcome, for that reason, if/then, in order to, led to, makes, one reason for, resulted in, since, so, therefore, this is why, thus  •  If , then .  •  in order to .  •  is a cause/effect/result of .  •  Predict  •  Make Inferences  •  Draw Conclusions  assume, based on, conclude, determine, due to, estimate, expect, guess, implies, infer, in light of, predict/prediction, speculate, suppose, surmise, suspect, therefore, thus  •  I infer ___ based on ___.  •  Due to the fact that __, I conclude .  •  Given , I surmise that .  •  Justify/Defend Position/ Explain Reasoning  •  Persuade/Argue  •  Also see Evaluate  according to, advantages outweigh disadvantages, appeal to, argue/argument, based on, belief, claim, clearly, convince, definitely, disadvantage, due to, evidence, feel, following careful scrutiny, for this reason, for example, furthermore, influence, obligation to, obvious that, in my opinion, my interpretation is, opponents would argue, outweigh, must, perception, perspective, prefer, proposition, should, shows, support, the benefits are obvious, the facts suggest that, think, urge  •  The evidence points to .  •  suggest , while argue(s) that .  •  The advantages of clearly outweigh the disadvantages.  •  Evaluate  •  Critique  advantage/disadvantage, after analysis or inspection, agree, apparent, assessment, bad, benefit, best, better, challenge, consideration, criteria, disagree, estimation, favorable, further, greater than, inspection, interpretation, judgment, less than, negative, obvious, positive, unfavorable, valuable, value, worse than, worst, worth  •  After analyzing , it is apparent that .  •  Considering , it is obvious that .  •  is more valuable than because___.  Sample Language Functions and Features Overview  Sample Language Functions*  (Collected and modified for simplicity from WIDA’s ELD Standards 2020)  *Students also interpret texts (spoken and written) related to the expressive functions below.  Sample Language Features (forms)  (Modified from WIDA’s Sample Language Features) pg. 33  Narrate  •  Reflect on lived experiences and/or give personal narratives  •  Create stories (using narrative structures and elements; e.g., details about characters and setting, plot, dialogue, varied tones and themes)  •  Reflect and connect to narrative texts (e.g., share personal reactions)  •  Describe people, objects, and scenes  •  Identify perspectives  Inform  •  Share factual information  •  Describe observations and concepts/topic  •  Identify and describe various relationships among ideas and information  •  Compare and contrast information  •  Categorize/classify information  •  Construct research reports that require multiple sources of factual information  Explain  •  Ask and answer questions about natural phenomena, how things work, or why things happen  •  Identify/express/analyze causal, sequential, and/or cyclical relationships  •  Share interpretations of multimodal representations (e.g., drawings, diagrams) about how and why things work  •  Construct explanations (including using models and representations, evidence from science) [possibly from multiple sources, depending on grade level]  •  Analyze and evaluate data in explanations  Argue  •  State opinions (including likes/dislikes)  •  Give reasons/justify opinions and claims (including using observational data, textual evidence)  •  Recognize the difference between claims with and without support  •  Consider alternative perspectives and arguments; adjust arguments  •  Construct claims and/or respond to counterclaims  •  Analyze literary techniques  •  Discern what types of arguments are needed, when they are needed, and what purposes they meet in different content areas  Organization:  [how ideas are organized to meet a purpose; e.g., narrate, argue]  organization patterns such as introduction, body, conclusion; claim and evidence to support  Cohesion:  [how ideas are connected within and across sentences]  devices such as repeated words for emphasis, pronouns usage, connectors (transitional and signal words and phrases)  Density:  [how information is expanded; adding to a single noun]  added adjectives, prepositional phrases, etc.  Grammatical Complexity:  [simple, compound, and complex sentence types]  added complexity through creating and combining clauses in different ways (different kinds of conjunctions)  Note: Support for variation in cohesion, density and precision all support the development of grammatical complexity  Precision:  [how everyday, cross-content, and technical language more effectively convey precise meaning]  using content-specific terminology, adding details with adverbs (time, place, and manner)  Blank PLD Template  Modified Proficiency Level Descriptors (PLDs) for the __________ Communication Mode  Toward the end of each proficiency level, when scaffolded appropriately, multilingual learners will…  Criteria  End of Level 1  End of Level 2  End of Level 3  End of Level 4  End of Level 5  Level 6  DISCOURSE  Organization of language  Understand how coherent texts (spoken, written, multimodal) are created…  DISCOURSE  Cohesion of language  Understand how ideas are connected across a whole text through…  DISCOURSE  Density of  language  Understand how ideas are elaborated or condensed through…  SENTENCE  Grammatical complexity  Understand how meanings are extended or enhanced through…  WORD,  PHRASE  Precision of language  Understand how precise meanings are created through everyday, cross-disciplinary, and technical language through… ",
