    "id": "cd0938f1-6838-40af-8e39-46f3da9f3bae",
    "title": "Applying the Framework to Instructional Planning Learning Walk Observation Tool",
    "content": "Applying the Framework to Instructional Planning Learning Walk Observation Tool  Teacher Name  Date of Observation  Observer Name  Step 1: Identify the Relevant Standards  As evidenced by instructional planning and classroom artifacts, the teacher has…  Comments  Created content objectives based on identified content standard(s)  ☐  Communicated learning expectations to students by clarifying content objectives  ☐  Created language objectives based on identified ELD standards  ☐  Communicated language use expectations by clarifying language objectives  ☐  Identified the concepts and content-specific practices associated with the standards  ☐  Recorded ways to activate prior knowledge and/or leverage students’ experiences, interests, and cultural and linguistic backgrounds  ☐  Step 2: Identify the Key Language Uses Associated with the “Demonstration of Learning” Task  As evidenced by instructional planning and classroom instruction, the teacher has…  Comments  Identified the key language use aligned to the “demonstration of learning” task  ☐  Designed instructional tasks designed for meaning-making  ☐  Determined how instructional tasks will be used to monitor both content learning and language development  ☐  Developed formative assessment tasks to monitor content and language  ☐  Step 3: Use Language Expectations to Create a Unit Learning Goal, Learning Intentions, and Success Indicators  As evidenced by instructional planning and classroom instruction, the teacher has…  Comments  Used language expectation stems and content standards to develop a learning goal  ☐  Prioritized language functions  ☐  Used prioritized language functions to develop learning intentions  ☐  Scaffolded instruction based on learning intentions  ☐  Prioritized language features aligned to prioritized language functions  ☐  Clarified expectations using mentor texts  ☐  Students will: (construct) (standard) (key language use) that (language function(s)) using (language feature(s)).  Step 4: Identify Appropriate Supports*  As evidenced by instructional planning and classroom instruction, the teacher has identified supports…  Sensory Supports  (circle as identified)  General  ●  Real-life objects (realia)  ●  Manipulatives  ●  Pictures & photographs  ●  Illustrations, diagrams & drawings  ●  Magazines & newspapers  ●  Physical activities  ●  Videos & Films  ●  Broadcasts  ●  Models & figures  Language Arts  ●  Illustrated word/phrase walls  ●  Cards with story elements  ●  Sequence blocks  ●  Environmental print  ●  Posters or displays  ●  Bulletin boards  ●  Photographs  ●  Cartoons  ●  Audiobooks  Math  ●  Blocks/Cubes  ●  Clocks, sundials, and other timekeepers  ●  Number lines  ●  Models of geometric figures  ●  Calculators  ●  Protractors  ●  Rulers  ●  Yard/meter sticks  ●  Geoboards  ●  Counters  ●  Compasses  ●  Calendars  ●  Coins  Science  ●  Scientific instruments  ●  Measurement tools  ●  Physical models  ●  Natural materials  ●  Actual substances, organisms, or objects of investigation  ●  Posters/Illustrations of processes or cycles  Social Studies  ●  Maps  ●  Globes  ●  Atlases  ●  Compasses  ●  Timelines  ●  Multicultural artifacts  ●  Arial & satellite photographs  ●  Video Clips  Graphic Supports  (circle as identified)  ●  Charts  ●  Graphic organizers  ●  Tables  ●  Graphs  ●  Timelines  ●  Number lines  Interactive Supports  (circle as identified)  ●  In pairs or partners  ●  In triads or small groups  ●  In a whole group  ●  Using cooperative group structures  ●  Using the Internet or software programs  ●  In the native language (L1)  ●  With mentors  *Taken from: The WIDA English Language Proficiency Standards and Resource Guide, 2007 Edition, Pre-Kindergarten through Grade 12, page RG-21.  Students will: (construct) (standard) (key language use) that (language function(s)) using (language feature(s)) reinforced by ((a) (support(s)). ",
