    "id": "bca1579d-9ecf-4850-b7f2-2210f461e06f",
    "title": "A new partnership with Nanyang Technological University - The Discussion Project",
    "content": "Description: The Discussion Project team traveled to Nanyang Technological University to implement a three day course focusing on discussion instruction.\n\nSelect Page A new partnership with Nanyang Technological University Written by The Discussion Project  August 29, 2024  Higher Education i Read Time: A new partnership with Nanyang Technological University In July 2024, Lynn Glueck (Director of The Discussion Project) and John Zola (curriculum writer and instructor for The Discussion Project) traveled to Nanyang Technological University (NTU) to implement the three day course with faculty members who teach NTU’s Interdisciplinary Collaborative Core (ICC) courses. The ICC program at NTU is designed to encourage innovation and cross-disciplinary collaboration among students. By incorporating diverse fields of study, the ICC program helps students tackle complex, real-world problems from many different perspectives. Through a series of interdisciplinary courses, students engage in collaborative projects that bridge different academic domains, equipping them with the skills to creatively and effectively address global challenges. These courses have a flipped classroom structure and focus on team-based learning during class. Because NTU believes strongly in developing speaking, listening, and critical thinking skills for their students, their leadership reached out to The Discussion Project to help their faculty grow and refine their discussion pedagogy. Dr. Ho Shen Yong from NTU and John Zola from The Discussion Project Highlights: Structured Academic Controversy and Flowchart Discussion In The Discussion Project, participants learn over 20 discussion protocols that help them structure and implement high quality discussion in their classroom. Participants see these protocols modeled and experience their implementation. A couple of highlights from the three day course were the Structured Academic Controversy and the Flowchart discussion. Structured Academic Controversy Always a favorite among The Discussion Project’s participants is the Structured Academic Controversy (SAC), a protocol developed over 50 years ago at University of Minnesota. SAC is a highly engaging, but challenging model to learn and implement into a classroom environment. NTU’s participants found this protocol to be effective because it helps students dig deeper into complex issues while maintaining civility. Flowchart discussion The second highlight from the course was the flowchart discussion. In this activity participants are given key concepts, events, and facts and a variety of arrows. They must work together and arrange all the pieces of paper to tell a coherent story. This protocol encourages collaborative inquiry and critical thinking. Flowchart discussion with team participants. At the heart of both these protocols (SAC and the flowchart discussion), students are engaging in collaborative learning and they dig deeper in concepts and synthesize ideas together. The lessons learned as part of these sessions will continue to support the growth of NTU’s Interdisciplinary Collaborative Core courses. “It was a course that gave us many practical tools for our teaching toolbox and would help directly with our teaching practices.It was really well-organized and structured. The pacing was excellent! NTU recognizes the importance of The Discussion Project’s core tenets: instructional design that shifts the cognitive load on to their students with a focus on building community in the classroom and the importance of planning for discussion. The participants appreciated the opportunity to further hone and develop their discussion pedagogy skills. While some of the discussion protocols were already known and widely used in the ICC courses, some practices were completely new and provided an excellent opportunity for participants to see them modeled and implemented. The Discussion Project team appreciated that the instructors at NTU were eager to improve learning for their students. What’s next for NTU educators: Faculty + student engagement The Discussion Project’s approach across these three days is rooted in creating an environment of collaboration and high-quality discussion. This dynamic is not only important for our three days, but also when NTU instructors return to their Interdisciplinary Collaborative Core (ICC) courses and begin applying these lessons in the classroom. Both teachers and students stand to benefit from the process and learnings. These lessons learned are foundational to improving student engagement and learning. We are excited to see the impact in the coming academic year. Ready to have a discussion? Submit your information for a personalized course walk-through with The Discussion Project team to better understand how it can support the growth of your instructors, departments, and institution. Schedule a consult today Interested in learning more about the Structured Academic Controversy strategy? > Learn more today × Manage Cookie Consent To provide the best experiences, we use technologies like cookies to store and/or access device information. Consenting to these technologies will allow us to process data such as browsing behavior or unique IDs on this site. Not consenting or withdrawing consent, may adversely affect certain features and functions. Functional Functional Always active The technical storage or access is strictly necessary for the legitimate purpose of enabling the use of a specific service explicitly requested by the subscriber or user, or for the sole purpose of carrying out the transmission of a communication over an electronic communications network. Preferences Preferences The technical storage or access is necessary for the legitimate purpose of storing preferences that are not requested by the subscriber or user. Statistics Statistics The technical storage or access that is used exclusively for statistical purposes. The technical storage or access that is used exc