  {
    "id": "a28dfc41-e41d-46ad-9e9f-35e5cfea4c38",
    "title": "Discussion-based Professional Development at Morgan State University - The Discussion Project",
    "content": "Select Page Discussion-based Professional Development at Morgan State University Written by The Discussion Project  February 28, 2025  Featured Post, Higher Education i Read Time: The Discussion Project team interviewed Dr. Laura Dorsey-Elson as part of the most recent (and fourth) implementation of The Discussion Project professional development course with Morgan State University (MSU) faculty and staff. Dr. Dorsey-Elson currently serves as the Director of MSU’s Teaching Excellence Center, which is also called the Center for Innovative Instruction and Scholarship (CIIS). Dr. Dorsey-Elson is an experienced leader in higher education, having spent the last 23 years as a faculty member at Morgan St University. Dr. Dorsey-Elson and other leaders at MSU have partnered with The Discussion Project over the last four years to bring discussion-based professional development to faculty and graduate school teams. This interview is edited for brevity and clarity. The Discussion Project Can you share more about your expertise and experience in instructional leadership and higher education? Dr. Laura Dorsey-ElsonMy path to this role has been both traditional and unique to me. I came into instructional leadership through instructional development, faculty development and eventually a leadership role. I followed the traditional path of a long-term faculty member. This is my 23rd year at Morgan State University and my 29th year in higher education. My passion for instructional excellence as a faculty member evolved into a passion for continuous improvement—always seeking ways to be better. Many of the strategies I used were self-created, which is quite common in faculty development. People who take on this role often have a drive to innovate in the classroom because the rewards are so fulfilling. At some point, leadership recognized my work and said, “You can help other faculty be amazing.” And then, suddenly, new opportunities emerged. My background in instructional excellence and my discipline—communication—have both played significant roles in my journey. My PhD is in organizational communication, which has shaped my career in profound ways. This expertise has allowed me to engage in organizational development and professional training. I have extensive experience facilitating and working with groups, which is another passion of mine. Faculty development is very similar to organizational development, and I see my role as Director of CIIS as an internal organizational development position—working systemically within the university. My years of experience have prepared me well for this role, and it truly feels like the best time of my career. Every aspect of my work aligns with the skills and passions I enjoy most. And, of course, I love faculty as much as I love the work itself. It’s a perfect fit. The Power of process and intentional facilitation The Discussion ProjectGiven your rich experience and the way you’ve blended different roles to create your own “special sauce,” what initially drew you to The Discussion Project? Dr. Laura Dorsey-ElsonWhat initially drew me in was the structure of The Discussion Project. Your dedication to the process signaled to me that this was a group that truly understands human dynamics and instructional professional development. When I recognized that, I knew I had found my people. The foundation of everything I do is built on understanding the value of process—both in planning and execution. Your team gets that, and that’s not something I find everywhere. It takes a certain mindset and expertise to appreciate that level of intentionality, and I saw it in your approach right away. Discussion is one of the most powerful ways to facilitate learning across all levels. That’s why I don’t limit this training to classrooms alone. It enhances discussion in classrooms, labs, advisement sessions, and even informal gatherings.The protocols are formal, but elements of them can be adapted to facilitate learning anywhere, anytime. I also admire your commitment to in-person engagement. While online formats can work, you understand the unique power of being in the room together—of dedicating time, at least two full days, to immersive learning. You’ve also allowed for flexibility, which I appreciate. I always tell my team that no training is truly beneficial in the long term unless there is time for reflection. You can gain short-term benefits from single-day events, but real transformation happens over time. I believe in the “night of sleep” rule—there must be time for passive reflection, where people can metabolize what they’ve learned. That element is embedded in your approach and it solidified my belief in our partnership. That’s why I will always speak highly of our collaboration. It has been incredibly fruitful because we share a language, an understanding and a commitment to a thoughtful process. The Discussion ProjectI like how you touched on how The Discussion Project is different from other professional development programs. Many programs focus primarily on how to teach course content as the end goal, but The Discussion Project emphasizes learning of course content and discussion skills. Dr. Laura Dorsey-Elson…and if I may add, while I was drawn to the process, the content is also incredible. It’s action-centered, immediately implementable, and rooted in an instructor’s lived experience—navigating the highs and lows of working with diverse classrooms. The content itself often comes from the group. While the strategies and protocols are clearly defined, they evolve based on the participants, which makes each session unique. That’s what makes it special. A well-trained facilitation team is essential to recognizing and working with this dynamic year after year. This is our fourth year hosting The Discussion Project on our campus, and the feedback has been consistently positive. The Discussion ProjectOur facilitators, like Anissa Butler, have decades of instructional experience. They don’t just teach the course—they come with authority, but they also listen and problem-solve. For example, if someone in the group says, “I have a challenge implementing this in my math course,” our facilitators can dive in and provide practical guidance. As you mentioned, the course adapts to the participants. The content remains the same, but because we actively engage in discussion, every session has its own unique magic. So, how has The Discussion Project helped build instructional skills at Morgan State University? You mentioned it aligns with your philosophy, but what other impacts have you seen? Dr. Laura Dorsey-ElsonThe answer is quite simple: discussion is fundamental to learning. If we want to foster engagement and deeper understanding, discussion must be present. It’s essential to creating meaningful learning experiences. Think about Bloom’s Taxonomy—discussion enables students not only to remember and understand but also to apply, analyze and create. That said, discussion is one of the most powerful ways to facilitate learning across all levels. That’s why I don’t limit this training to classrooms alone. It enhances discussion in classrooms, labs, advisement sessions, and even informal gatherings. The protocols are formal, but elements of them can be adapted to facilitate learning anywhere, anytime. Another key takeaway is this: “The one doing the talking is doing the learning.” If the instructor is always talking, they’re the one processing and metabolizing the content—not the students. For real learning to occur, students need to engage in discussion themselves. Discussion across academic departments: strengthening instruction The Discussion ProjectLet’s move into how the course supports different disciplines. You mentioned earlier that discussion is valuable across STEM, humanities, and even advisory roles. How does The Discussion Project apply across various fields? Dr. Laura Dorsey-ElsonInstructors across disciplines have told me that The Discussion Project works universally. The protocols are not discipline-specific—they layer onto whatever course content is being taught. Think of them like Legos. You bring your subject matter—architecture, for example—and apply the discussion protocols to it. The content remains your own, but the structure facilitates deeper engagement. The beauty of these protocols is that they encourage students to engage in discussion without requiring them to be experts. Instead, they create space for meaningful exchanges of ideas, allowing students to build understanding collaboratively. The evidence is in the feedback. Instructors from all disciplines find The Discussion Project valuable, which speaks to the program’s broad applicability and effectiveness. Transformative training: How faculty embrace discussion-based teaching The Discussion ProjectWhen describing the Discussion Project course to faculty and staff at MSU or outside institutions, how do you describe it to them? Dr. Laura Dorsey-ElsonIt’s transformative. Instructors—even those who are cautiously resistant—come out of this three-day experience with a renewed energy and a willingness to try new approaches. It’s transformative on an individual level. It also builds community, which we’re seeing at Morgan State University. Suddenly, instructors realize they are not alone in their struggles. It’s powerful to sit in a room and hear others say, “Oh, wow. It’s not just me.” It reassures them that they are not bad instructors—there are just difficult moments in teaching. What matters is staying in a mindset of “Let me try this.” Discussion is fundamental to learning. If we want to foster engagement and deeper understanding, discussion must be present. It’s essential to creating meaningful learning experiences. The Discussion Project is transformative because it provides tangible strategies. It fosters a sense of community and has systemic effects. Now that we’re in year four at Morgan, I’m seeing its impact on a larger scale. As more people are exposed to this model, try it, and experience success—whether that success is as simple as getting students to talk more—it shifts the culture. They also gain strategies to handle inevitable silent moments in discussion, understanding them rather than shutting down or ending class early. The systemic part is crucial. When more instructors feel confident about facilitating discussions, we create a more optimistic environment around student engagement. Many faculty members express concerns about Gen Z students being “stuck in their phones.” But they’re not stuck because they want to be—they’re stuck because we need to find ways to engage them. The Discussion Project helps “unstick” them. The more faculty who use these strategies, the more we shift the culture of classroom engagement for the better. The Discussion ProjectHave any faculty members who participated in the Discussion Project received feedback from their students saying, Something has changed here. The way you’re facilitating, the way you’re engaging us—it’s different? At first, you described the instructor experience, how some were hesitant but eventually embraced it. But what about students? Have you heard anything from them that validates what you’re saying? Dr. Laura Dorsey-ElsonI’m hearing more and more faculty say that their students are noticing a shift. Would I love to see concrete course evaluation data? 100%. And actually, we’re working on that. We recently redesigned our course evaluations at Morgan State, and one of the key questions now is: Did your instructor build rapport with you? Did you feel like you were in an inclusive learning community? I fully expect those numbers to rise. Anecdotally, I’ve had professors who took the Discussion Project training, applied it for a semester, and came back saying their students told them things like: “I really got to know my peers in this course.” “This was great—I actually felt like my opinion mattered.” “I never knew I’d talk this much in a chemistry class.” That last one really sticks with me—I’ve had chemistry instructors tell me that! And honestly, STEM faculty often face the biggest challenges with discussion-based learning. Their work tends to be introspective, and they naturally bring that mindset into the classroom. But students thrive on interaction, and those instructors need tools to switch into a more extroverted teaching style. That’s where the Discussion Project makes a real impact. I’ve had STEM faculty say, “I never knew this could work in my class. I never knew I could do this.” The Discussion ProjectWell, one hard data point we do have is that this is now the fourth time implementing the program. Do you want to speak to that? Dr. Laura Dorsey-ElsonOur first engagement was when we partnered with you to pilot and research this initiative. Thankfully, I was in a position at the time to influence the decision and say, Yes, we want to bring this here. The feedback was so strong that I made the call to run it a second time. The feedback was so strong that the Office of the Provost authorized me to make the call to run it a second time. And, the response was incredible—people loved it. Some even wanted to repeat the training. That gave us a new challenge: balancing repeat participants with those who had never taken it before. So, we made adjustments. Now, in year four, we’re seeing something amazing: entire groups of faculty who do frontline instruction—our orientation instructors—are getting on board. A few attended in past years, and now all 40 of them want this training. That’s huge! A group of them will attend in January, and we’ll see what happens from there. But I can already imagine future faculty meetings—when instructional challenges come up, there will be 15 trained instructors saying, But this works. And from there? Who knows? I expect my office will be getting a lot more requests for support and training. Another group embracing this is our graduate teaching assistants. They’re attending, too, and that’s another exciting shift. Now our graduate school is getting involved. They’re considering this training for all of their teaching assistants because, as we know in higher education, many of us start teaching without formal training in instruction. We’re the only field where professionals earn degrees in their subject areas but not necessarily in how to teach that subject. We enter the classroom with deep disciplinary knowledge, but no structured preparation in how to facilitate learning or inspire passion in others. That’s why training like this is so important. Our graduate school sees the value in reaching teaching assistants early in their careers. Imagine—this training alone puts them miles ahead of many PhD candidates who will one day be handed a syllabus and told, Go teach. Building a sustainable teaching culture of engagement The Discussion ProjectWhat are your goals moving forward? And since you’ve already started taking action, could you talk about the Review & Reflect Zoom sessions and your efforts to build a community of practice at MSU? Dr. Laura Dorsey-ElsonMy primary goal is to continue listening to faculty and instructors who’ve taken the course.We’re reviewing participant feedback, and it’s clear that they want more opportunities to engage. So now, with nearly 100 faculty trained on our campus, we’re thinking about how to deepen and expand this work. MSU has a faculty of around 500, plus another 400 adjunct instructors—many of whom haven’t been introduced to these methods yet. There’s a whole pocket of faculty who could benefit from this training. The question is: How do we reach them? One of our key goals now is maintaining engagement among those who have already completed the training. How do we keep them connected and learning from one another? This year, we launched a Community of Practice with a series called R&R: Review & Reflect. We’ve had one meeting so far, and it went well. Our hope is to recruit participants from our newest cohort in January, so they can transition directly into this ongoing reflection process. Beyond that, we’re exploring ways to shift expertise among faculty who have been practicing these skills for three or four years now. Could we introduce peer coaching? Peer observation? Could we establish shared protocols for discussion-based teaching? We also want to continue learning from you all at The Discussion Project. This partnership has been invaluable, and I see it evolving. And that’s what I love about this collaboration—you all bring in ideas that I wouldn’t have on my own. Another goal is to keep brainstorming with you all about what’s next. I don’t want to call it the next stage yet, but I do feel like we’re in a transition period. And I think we’ll figure it out together. Ready to have a discussion? Submit your information for a personalized course walk-through with The Discussion Project team to better understand how it can support the growth of your instructors, departments, and institution. Schedule a consult today Interested in learning more about the Structured Academic Controversy strategy? > Learn more today × Manage Cookie Consent To provide the best experiences, we use technologies like cookies to store and/or access device information. Consenting to these technologies will allow us to process data such as browsing behavior or unique IDs on this site. Not consenting or withdrawing consent, may adversely affect certain features and functions. Functional Functional Always active The technical storage or access is strictly necessary for the legitimate purpose of enabling the use of a specific service explicitly requested by the subscriber or user, or for the sole purpose of carrying out the transmission of a communication over an electronic communications network. Preferences Preferences The technical storage or access is necessary for the legitimate purpose of storing preferences that are not requested by the subscriber